window.QUIZ_DATA = {"exams":[{"examNumber":1,"sdls":[{"sdlNumber":1,"title":"MSK Embryology & Limb Development","questions":[{"id":"sdl1_q01","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the derivation of musculoskeletal tissues from mesodermal layers, focusing on somite differentiation and the roles of the apical ectodermal ridge (AER) and zone of polarizing activity (ZPA) in upper and lower limb development.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A researcher removes the apical ectodermal ridge (AER) from a developing chick limb bud at week 5 of embryogenesis. Which of the following best describes the expected consequence?","choices":{"A":"Digit fusion develops along the hand plate, because apoptosis between the digital rays is suppressed when AER-derived FGF8 signals are absent from the distal limb mesenchyme.","B":"An ectopic limb bud forms on the contralateral side, because FGF10 released from the lateral plate mesoderm is redirected toward the opposite limb field without AER-mediated inhibition.","C":"The limb ceases outgrowth and fails to form structures distal to the level of removal, because the AER secretes FGF8 required for continued mesoderm proliferation in the progress zone.","D":"Mirror-image digit duplication occurs on the anterior limb border, because anterior mesenchyme is exposed to unopposed Sonic Hedgehog from the zone of polarizing activity.","E":"The limb develops normally with intact proximal structures but shows shortened metacarpals, because only distal ossification depends on AER-derived FGF8 while proximal endochondral centers are autonomous."},"correct":"C","explanation":"The AER is located at the lateral-most rim of the limb bud and secretes FGF8 (and FGF4), which sustains undifferentiated mesenchymal proliferation in the underlying progress zone. Without the AER, outgrowth stops and only the proximal structures that had already formed are retained. Digit fusion (syndactyly) results from failed apoptosis between digital rays, not AER removal. Ectopic limb formation requires FGF10/FGF8 signaling to initiate a new bud, not a consequence of AER loss. Mirror-image digit duplication results from transplanting an extra ZPA anteriorly, not from AER removal. Isolated metacarpal shortening describes achondroplasia, not AER absence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AER = FGF8 source; if AER removed, limb truncated at that developmental stage — classic experimental manipulation tested in developmental biology. COMLEX: AER removal is a limb defect mechanism; connects to SDL 1 Obj 4 (amelia = no AER/no FGF8 expressed).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — paraxial mesoderm somites give rise to sclerotome, myotome, and dermatome; AER (FGF8) drives distal limb outgrowth; ZPA (Shh) patterns the A/P axis"},{"id":"sdl1_q02","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the derivation of musculoskeletal tissues from mesodermal layers, focusing on somite differentiation and the roles of the apical ectodermal ridge (AER) and zone of polarizing activity (ZPA) in upper and lower limb development.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"The zone of polarizing activity (ZPA) occupies the posterior mesenchyme of the developing limb bud and controls digit identity along the anterior-to-posterior axis. Which signaling molecule mediates this patterning function?","choices":{"A":"Fibroblast growth factor 8 (FGF8), which is secreted from the AER at the distal rim of the limb bud and maintains undifferentiated mesenchymal cells in the progress zone for continued outgrowth.","B":"Sonic Hedgehog (Shh), which is most highly concentrated near the posterior mesenchyme where the fifth digit forms and least concentrated anteriorly near the first digit.","C":"Fibroblast growth factor 10 (FGF10), which is released by lateral plate mesoderm to induce the overlying ectoderm to form the AER and initiate the initial phases of limb bud outgrowth.","D":"Wnt signaling protein, which is released by the dorsal neural tube and overlying ectoderm to induce dermomyotome formation from the paraxial mesoderm somites bilaterally.","E":"Indian Hedgehog (IHH), which is secreted by hypertrophic chondrocytes in the epiphyseal growth plate to coordinate the rate of chondrocyte proliferation and longitudinal bone elongation."},"correct":"B","explanation":"The ZPA expresses Sonic Hedgehog (Shh), creating a posterior-to-anterior concentration gradient that assigns digit identity: highest Shh = fifth digit, lowest = first digit. FGF8 is the AER signal maintaining proximal-to-distal outgrowth. FGF10 is secreted by lateral plate mesoderm to initiate AER formation and limb bud outgrowth. Wnt induces dermomyotome differentiation from somites. Indian Hedgehog (IHH) is a growth plate regulator, not a limb patterning molecule.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ZPA = Shh gradient = digit identity (A-P axis); AER = FGF8 = proximal-to-distal outgrowth. These two structures are the key patterning signals tested. COMLEX: Connect to SDL 1 Obj 4 — transplanting ZPA anteriorly causes mirror-image polydactyly.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — paraxial mesoderm somites give rise to sclerotome, myotome, and dermatome; AER (FGF8) drives distal limb outgrowth; ZPA (Shh) patterns the A/P axis"},{"id":"sdl1_q03","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the derivation of musculoskeletal tissues from mesodermal layers, focusing on somite differentiation and the roles of the apical ectodermal ridge (AER) and zone of polarizing activity (ZPA) in upper and lower limb development.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"During week 4 of embryogenesis, the paraxial mesoderm segments into somites. Sonic Hedgehog released by the notochord and ventral neural tube induces differentiation of the ventral portion of each somite into a specific structure. What does this ventral somite component become?","choices":{"A":"The sclerotome, which migrates to surround the notochord and neural tube and differentiates into the vertebral column, ribs, and other axial skeletal elements.","B":"The dermomyotome, which is induced by Wnt signaling from the dorsal neural tube and overlying ectoderm and gives rise to both the dermis of the skin and the skeletal muscle precursors.","C":"The myotome, which subdivides into epaxial and hypaxial portions under spinal nerve innervation and generates the intrinsic back muscles and the muscles of the trunk and limbs.","D":"The meningotome, which proliferates medial to the sclerotome under notochordal signaling and eventually differentiates into the dura, arachnoid, and pia mater surrounding the spinal cord.","E":"The lateral plate mesoderm, which lies peripheral to the paraxial mesoderm and splits into somatic and splanchnic layers to form the pelvic bones, limb bones, and body wall cavities."},"correct":"A","explanation":"The ventral portion of each somite becomes the sclerotome under Sonic Hedgehog signaling from the notochord and ventral neural tube. The sclerotome forms the vertebral bodies, neural arches, ribs, and tendons. The dorsal portion becomes the dermomyotome under Wnt signaling, which further subdivides into the myotome (skeletal muscles) and dermatome (dermis). The meningotome is a subdivision of the sclerotome that forms the meninges, not the ventral somite itself. Lateral plate mesoderm is a separate compartment from paraxial mesoderm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Paraxial mesoderm → somites → sclerotome (ventral, Shh) + dermomyotome (dorsal, Wnt). Sclerotome → vertebrae and ribs. Dermomyotome → myotome (muscle) and dermatome (dermis). COMLEX: Three MSK sources — paraxial mesoderm, neural crest cells (facial bones), and lateral plate mesoderm (limb/pelvic bones).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — paraxial mesoderm somites give rise to sclerotome, myotome, and dermatome; AER (FGF8) drives distal limb outgrowth; ZPA (Shh) patterns the A/P axis"},{"id":"sdl1_q04","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the stages of limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation, including the processes of segmentation, re-segmentation.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"During vertebral column development, each sclerotome somite subdivides into cranial and caudal sub-segments that then undergo re-segmentation. Which of the following correctly describes this process and its anatomical result?","choices":{"A":"The cranial sub-segment of each somite fuses with the cranial sub-segment of the adjacent inferior somite to form paired vertebral bodies per spinal level, with sclerotome remnant forming the annulus fibrosus.","B":"The entire sclerotome ossifies as a single unit per segment, and the notochord degenerates completely without contributing to the intervertebral disc, leaving only loose mesenchyme between vertebrae.","C":"The caudal sub-segment of each somite fuses with the cranial sub-segment of the adjacent inferior somite to form a vertebral body, leaving the notochord remnant between segments to become the nucleus pulposus.","D":"The caudal sub-segment fuses with the ipsilateral dermomyotome to form the posterior elements of the vertebra, while the cranial sub-segment becomes the nucleus pulposus under FGF10 signaling.","E":"Adjacent sclerotome somites remain intact and separate without fusion, with the intervertebral disc cavity enlarging spontaneously under Sonic Hedgehog signaling from the persisting notochord."},"correct":"C","explanation":"Re-segmentation is critical for vertebral column formation: the caudal portion of one sclerotome somite fuses with the cranial portion of the next inferior somite to build each vertebral body. This creates an offset so that each vertebra is derived from two adjacent somites. The notochord remnant trapped between the newly formed vertebral bodies persists as the nucleus pulposus of the intervertebral disc. The annulus fibrosus is derived from the sclerotome, not from the cranial sub-segment directly. Re-segmentation also creates the intervertebral foramina, through which spinal nerve roots exit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Notochord → nucleus pulposus (FA: \"Notochord becomes nucleus pulposus of intervertebral disc in adults\"). Annulus fibrosus → from sclerotome. This is a classic embryology board question. COMLEX: Re-segmentation also creates the intervertebral foramina, allowing spinal nerve exit — relevant to somatic dysfunction and radiculopathy discussions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — lateral plate mesoderm forms limb buds; vertebrae form by sclerotome re-segmentation; notochord remnant becomes the nucleus pulposus"},{"id":"sdl1_q05","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the stages of limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation, including the processes of segmentation, re-segmentation.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"In the developing embryo, TBX genes play a key role in distinguishing which type of limb a limb bud will produce. Which of the following correctly matches the TBX gene to the limb field it specifies?","choices":{"A":"HOX genes establish whether a limb bud becomes an upper or lower extremity by controlling TBX5 and TBX4 expression at the level of the limb field within the lateral plate mesoderm.","B":"TBX5 is expressed in the upper limb field of lateral plate mesoderm specifying upper extremity identity, while TBX4 is expressed in the lower limb field specifying lower extremity identity.","C":"TBX4 is expressed in the upper limb field specifying shoulder and arm identity, while TBX5 is expressed in the lower limb field specifying hip and leg identity, with FGF10 as the common downstream effector.","D":"TBX genes specify anterior versus posterior limb identity within a single limb bud, working in concert with Sonic Hedgehog from the ZPA to assign digit positions along the hand or foot plate.","E":"Both TBX4 and TBX5 are co-expressed in both limb fields and only distinguish upper from lower limb identity through differential binding to the FGF10 promoter in the lateral plate mesoderm."},"correct":"B","explanation":"TBX5 is expressed in the upper limb lateral plate mesoderm and specifies upper extremity identity. TBX4 is expressed in the lower limb field and specifies lower extremity identity. HOX genes determine position along the craniocaudal axis (where a limb forms), not which type of limb forms. The lecture slides confirm: HOX = \"where along the column,\" TBX = \"upper vs lower,\" with FGF10 as the shared primary growth stimulant. TBX genes do not control A/P digit patterning, which is the ZPA/Shh role.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Limb bud gene hierarchy — HOX genes determine craniocaudal position; TBX5/TBX4 specify upper/lower limb identity; FGF10 initiates AER induction; AER (FGF8) drives outgrowth; ZPA (Shh) patterns A/P axis. COMLEX: Upper limb bud forms at C5-T1; lower limb bud at L2-S3 levels.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — lateral plate mesoderm forms limb buds; vertebrae form by sclerotome re-segmentation; notochord remnant becomes the nucleus pulposus"},{"id":"sdl1_q06","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the stages of limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation, including the processes of segmentation, re-segmentation.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A student is reviewing vertebral ossification for an upcoming exam. Which of the following correctly identifies the primary ossification centers of a typical vertebra and the secondary ossification centers that appear at puberty?","choices":{"A":"Primary centers: one in the centrum (vertebral body) and two in the neural arch (one on each side); secondary centers: tip of spinous process, tips of two transverse processes, and two annular epiphyses at the superior and inferior vertebral body rim.","B":"Primary centers: three in the centrum, two in the pedicles, and two in the laminae; secondary centers: one in the spinous process appearing at puberty, completing ossification by age 25.","C":"Primary centers: one in the centrum and one in the neural arch fused midline; secondary centers: one annular epiphysis at the disc surface appearing at age 5 and fusing by early adulthood.","D":"Primary centers: one in the spinous process and two annular epiphyses; secondary centers: one in the centrum and two in the neural arch appearing at puberty after the posterior elements are complete.","E":"Primary centers: two in the centrum (anterior and posterior) and one in the neural arch; secondary centers: two in the transverse processes and one in the tip of the spinous process, all appearing before birth."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Each typical vertebra has three primary ossification centers: one in the centrum (future vertebral body) and one on each side of the neural arch (two total). At puberty, five secondary ossification centers appear: one at the tip of the spinous process, one at each tip of the two transverse processes, and two annular epiphyses (one superior, one inferior) at the rim of the vertebral body. The secondary centers fuse with the vertebral body and complete ossification during early adulthood. This pattern is important for understanding Scheuermann disease and stress-related vertebral injuries in adolescents.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vertebral ossification — 3 primary centers (centrum + 2 neural arch). Secondary centers appear at puberty. The cervical ossification centers appear at weeks 8-9 in utero; first primary centers are in centrum and neural arch. COMLEX: Spina bifida occurs when the neural arch centers fail to fuse posteriorly — connects to SDL 1 Obj 4.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — lateral plate mesoderm forms limb buds; vertebrae form by sclerotome re-segmentation; notochord remnant becomes the nucleus pulposus"},{"id":"sdl1_q07","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific muscle types and the development of muscular tissues, including the critical periods in this process.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"The myotome subdivides into epaxial and hypaxial muscle groups during embryogenesis. Which of the following correctly distinguishes epaxial from hypaxial muscles and their innervation?","choices":{"A":"Epaxial muscles form the flexors of the upper and lower limbs and are innervated by anterior divisions of the brachial and lumbosacral plexuses, while hypaxial muscles form the intrinsic back extensors innervated by posterior rami.","B":"Epaxial muscles form the intrinsic muscles of the back and are innervated by posterior rami of spinal nerves, while hypaxial muscles form muscles of the anterior neck, trunk, limbs, and perineum and are innervated by anterior rami.","C":"Epaxial muscles derive from the dermomyotome under Wnt signaling and form the intercostal muscles, while hypaxial muscles derive from the sclerotome under Sonic Hedgehog signaling and form the paraspinal muscles.","D":"Hypaxial muscles form the intrinsic muscles of the back and are innervated by posterior rami, while epaxial muscles migrate into the limb buds to form flexor and extensor compartments innervated by the plexuses.","E":"Both epaxial and hypaxial muscles derive from the lateral plate mesoderm and are equally innervated by both anterior and posterior rami, depending on their final anatomical position in the body wall."},"correct":"B","explanation":"Epaxial muscles arise from the medial part of the myotome, remain dorsal, and form the intrinsic (erector spinae and deep back) muscles. They are innervated by posterior rami of spinal nerves. Hypaxial muscles arise from the lateral myotome, migrate ventrally and into limb buds, and form the muscles of the anterior neck, thorax, abdomen, limbs, and perineum. They are innervated by anterior rami, which become the cervical, brachial, intercostal, lumbar, and lumbosacral plexuses. Both groups arise from the myotome (paraxial mesoderm), not lateral plate mesoderm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Epaxial = intrinsic back muscles, posterior rami; hypaxial = everything else (limbs, trunk, perineum), anterior rami. This explains why the intrinsic back muscles are supplied at every segmental level by posterior rami — each myotome level contributes one epaxial segment. COMLEX: Posterior rami somatic dysfunction affects epaxial muscles and is the basis for paraspinal muscle tender points and restricted spinal motion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — myoblasts fuse into myotubes at 4 weeks; epaxial (posterior rami) muscles form the intrinsic back; hypaxial (anterior rami) muscles form trunk, limbs, and perineum"},{"id":"sdl1_q08","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific muscle types and the development of muscular tissues, including the critical periods in this process.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"During limb development, hypaxial myotome cells migrate into the limb bud and split into dorsal and ventral muscle masses. Which of the following correctly identifies the muscles derived from the dorsal muscle mass of the upper limb and their nerve supply?","choices":{"A":"The dorsal muscle mass forms the flexors and adductors of the upper limb and is innervated by branches of the anterior division of the brachial plexus, including the median and ulnar nerves.","B":"The dorsal muscle mass forms the extensor muscles of the upper limb but is innervated by the anterior division of the brachial plexus, specifically the musculocutaneous and median nerve branches.","C":"The dorsal muscle mass of the upper limb forms extensors innervated by the tibial and obturator nerves, which branch from the lumbosacral plexus to supply the posterior compartments of the lower limb.","D":"The dorsal muscle mass forms both flexor and extensor muscles, with each group innervated by opposite divisions of the brachial plexus based on their final anterior or posterior position in the limb.","E":"The dorsal muscle mass of the upper limb forms the extensor muscles and is innervated by branches of the posterior division of the brachial plexus, primarily the radial and axillary nerves."},"correct":"E","explanation":"During upper limb development, the dorsal muscle mass migrates to the posterior side of the developing limb and forms the extensor compartment (triceps, wrist extensors, finger extensors, intrinsic extensors). It is innervated by the posterior division of the brachial plexus — specifically the radial nerve (C5-T1) and axillary nerve (C5-C6). The ventral muscle mass forms the flexors and adductors, innervated by the anterior division via the median, ulnar, and musculocutaneous nerves. The tibial and obturator nerves are lower limb nerves and have no role in upper limb innervation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dorsal muscle mass = extensors = posterior division brachial plexus (radial + axillary). Ventral muscle mass = flexors/adductors = anterior division (median + ulnar + musculocutaneous). Lower limb reversal: dorsal mass = extensors, anterior division (femoral, fibular); ventral mass = flexors, posterior division (tibial, obturator). COMLEX: Radial nerve palsy (wrist drop) from midshaft humerus fracture disrupts the dorsal muscle mass innervation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — myoblasts fuse into myotubes at 4 weeks; epaxial (posterior rami) muscles form the intrinsic back; hypaxial (anterior rami) muscles form trunk, limbs, and perineum"},{"id":"sdl1_q09","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific muscle types and the development of muscular tissues, including the critical periods in this process.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Skeletal muscle fibers develop through a defined sequence of cellular stages. At which developmental stage do the nuclei of skeletal muscle fibers complete their shift from a central position to the periphery of the cell?","choices":{"A":"At four weeks of embryogenesis, when mesenchymal cells first differentiate into myoblasts and begin aggregating and aligning into myotubes, causing immediate peripheral repositioning of nuclei.","B":"At nine weeks of embryogenesis, when myofilaments first appear within the developing myotubes and the fibers begin to acquire a cross-striated pattern as actin and myosin organize into sarcomeres.","C":"At birth, when myofibrils have fully formed and centrally located nuclei migrate to the peripheral margin of each muscle fiber, establishing the histological appearance of mature skeletal muscle.","D":"At twenty weeks of embryogenesis, when developing muscle fibers acquire their fully cross-striated appearance as nuclei migrate peripherally during the rapid proliferation of myofibrils.","E":"At five years of postnatal life, when satellite cells complete the final maturation of skeletal muscle and permanently reposition nuclei to the cell periphery during the last phase of myofibrillogenesis."},"correct":"C","explanation":"Skeletal muscle fiber development proceeds through the following sequence: mesenchymal cells differentiate into myoblasts at 4 weeks, which fuse into multinucleated myotubes; myofilaments appear at 9 weeks with nuclei still centrally located; by 20 weeks, fibers are cross-striated with nuclei beginning to move peripherally; and by birth, myofibrils are fully formed and nuclei have completed peripheral migration. This is clinically important because centrally placed nuclei in postnatal muscle (as seen on biopsy) indicate myopathic disease. Satellite cells persist as adult muscle stem cells but are not responsible for this initial nuclear repositioning.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Centrally placed nuclei = myopathy (abnormal postnatal finding). In normal development, nuclei are central in myotubes and shift to periphery at birth. COMLEX: Critical period for muscle development is weeks 4-20; myopathies such as myotonic dystrophy and congenital myopathies show persistent central nuclei on biopsy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — myoblasts fuse into myotubes at 4 weeks; epaxial (posterior rami) muscles form the intrinsic back; hypaxial (anterior rami) muscles form trunk, limbs, and perineum"},{"id":"sdl1_q10","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline and explain common abnormal musculoskeletal development and discuss the role of teratogens during critical periods of embryogenesis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy has short stature with shortened limb bones relative to a normal-length trunk, a large cranium, and limited elbow extension. Spinal radiographs show a narrow interpedicular distance in the lumbar region. Genetic testing identifies a gain-of-function mutation that impairs chondrocyte proliferation at growth plates. Which gene is most likely affected?","choices":{"A":"COMP (cartilage oligomeric matrix protein), whose loss-of-function destabilizes chondrocytes during endochondral ossification and causes pseudoachondroplasia with more pronounced joint laxity and no foreshortening of facial bones.","B":"GHR (growth hormone receptor), whose loss-of-function reduces IGF-1 production and causes proportionate dwarfism affecting limb length, trunk height, and cranial dimensions equally without facial bone involvement.","C":"IHH (Indian Hedgehog), whose gain-of-function would overstimulate chondrocyte proliferation in growth plates, causing disproportionate elongation of limb bones relative to the trunk rather than shortening.","D":"COL1A1 (type I collagen alpha-1), whose loss-of-function disrupts bone matrix production and causes osteogenesis imperfecta with blue sclerae, frequent fractures, and normal limb-to-trunk proportions.","E":"FGFR3 (fibroblast growth factor receptor 3), whose constitutive activation inhibits chondrocyte proliferation at all growth plates during endochondral ossification, causing short limbs with a relatively normal trunk and large head."},"correct":"E","explanation":"Achondroplasia is caused by a gain-of-function mutation in FGFR3, which constitutively inhibits chondrocyte proliferation in growth plates (endochondral ossification). Because long bones grow via endochondral ossification, they are shortened. The skull, however, grows via intramembranous ossification and is unaffected, giving a characteristically large cranium relative to the limbs. More than 85% of cases are sporadic and associated with advanced paternal age. COMP mutations cause pseudoachondroplasia, which is clinically similar but has greater joint laxity and no facial bone foreshortening. GHR mutations cause proportionate dwarfism. IHH gain-of-function would increase, not decrease, bone length. COL1A1 mutations cause osteogenesis imperfecta, not disproportionate dwarfism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Achondroplasia = FGFR3 gain-of-function; autosomal dominant, full penetrance; homozygosity lethal; most common cause of short-limbed dwarfism; >85% sporadic; associated with advanced paternal age (FA). Endochondral ossification impaired, membranous ossification intact → big head, short limbs. COMLEX: Spinal stenosis is a common complication of achondroplasia (impaired neural arch endochondral growth narrows canal).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — FGFR3 gain-of-function causes achondroplasia; failed AER causes amelia; HOX disruption causes phocomelia; caudal neuropore failure causes spina bifida"},{"id":"sdl1_q11","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline and explain common abnormal musculoskeletal development and discuss the role of teratogens during critical periods of embryogenesis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A newborn is noted on physical examination to have fusion of the second and third digits bilaterally, with soft tissue and bony elements joined from the metacarpophalangeal to the distal interphalangeal joints. The remainder of the examination is normal. Which embryologic process failed to occur?","choices":{"A":"Apoptosis between the digital rays of the hand plate, which normally eliminates the interdigital mesenchyme and carves out each individual finger as a distinct structure.","B":"Migration of lateral plate mesoderm cells into the limb bud to form the skeletal precursors of each digit, resulting in absent mesenchymal condensations between the affected digital rays.","C":"Re-segmentation of sclerotome somites to create the intervertebral foramina, a homologous process to digit separation that fails when HOX gene expression is disrupted during vertebral column formation.","D":"Expression of FGF8 by the apical ectodermal ridge, which normally induces apoptosis in the interdigital mesenchyme by withdrawing growth factor support from non-digital ray tissue.","E":"Sonic Hedgehog signaling from the zone of polarizing activity, which normally assigns unique digit identities by establishing an anterior-to-posterior Shh concentration gradient across the hand plate."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Syndactyly results from failure of apoptosis (programmed cell death) in the interdigital mesenchyme between the digital rays. Normally, the cells of the hand plate between each digital ray undergo apoptosis, separating the fingers by approximately day 52. When this apoptosis fails or is incomplete, the digits remain fused — a spectrum ranging from simple skin webbing to complete bony fusion. The AER supports growth zone proliferation but does not directly induce interdigital apoptosis. The ZPA assigns digit identity via Shh but is not responsible for digital separation. Polydactyly, not syndactyly, results from aberrant molecular signals causing extra digital ray formation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Syndactyly = failed apoptosis between digital rays. Polydactyly = aberrant ZPA signals or excess digit ray formation. In bats, the absence of interdigital apoptosis is what gives them wings — a useful memory hook. COMLEX: Digital anomalies may accompany chromosomal syndromes (e.g., trisomy 21, VACTERL); examining digits is part of the newborn screen for syndromic malformations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — FGFR3 gain-of-function causes achondroplasia; failed AER causes amelia; HOX disruption causes phocomelia; caudal neuropore failure causes spina bifida"},{"id":"sdl1_q12","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline and explain common abnormal musculoskeletal development and discuss the role of teratogens during critical periods of embryogenesis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"An infant born in the 1960s has five-fingered hands attached directly to the shoulder girdles, with complete absence of the humerus, radius, and ulna bilaterally. The fingers are functional with mild motor deficit. The mother had taken a sedative during the first trimester. Which mechanism best explains the pattern of limb malformation?","choices":{"A":"Complete absence of FGF8 from the AER caused total failure of limb bud outgrowth, with the hands spared because digital ray condensations had formed before the teratogen was introduced.","B":"A gain-of-function FGFR3 mutation selectively impaired chondrocyte proliferation in the long bone growth plates of the arm and forearm while sparing the hand bones, which undergo intramembranous ossification.","C":"Failure of the ZPA to express Sonic Hedgehog during A/P patterning caused loss of all proximal limb segments, with only the most distal Shh-independent digit structures surviving teratogen exposure.","D":"Disruption of HOX gene expression during the critical limb elongation period caused failure of proximal and intermediate limb segment formation, while the AER-maintained distal bud continued producing functional hand structures.","E":"Premature apoptosis of the dorsal muscle mass in the upper limb bud caused secondary regression of the humeral and forearm skeletal elements, which require mechanical stimulation from muscle contraction for normal development."},"correct":"D","explanation":"Phocomelia — hands and feet projecting directly from the trunk — is the classic presentation of thalidomide embryopathy, which was used as a sedative/antiemetic in the late 1950s and early 1960s. The mechanism involves disruption of HOX gene expression during the critical window of limb elongation (approximately days 30-56). Because HOX genes pattern the proximal-to-intermediate limb segments, their disruption prevents humerus, radius, and ulna from forming, while the more distal AER-driven hand bud continues developing. Total absence of the AER would cause complete amelia with no limb at all. FGFR3 mutations cause achondroplasia, not proximal limb segment aplasia. ZPA disruption affects digit identity, not proximal segment formation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Phocomelia = thalidomide; mechanism = HOX gene disruption during days 30-56 (critical limb period). Amelia = no AER = no FGF8 = no limb at all. Meromelia = partial limb aplasia. COMLEX: Thalidomide is teratogenic during organogenesis (weeks 3-8); limb buds are most vulnerable between days 28-56 — the window of sensitivity coincides with when women often do not yet know they are pregnant.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — FGFR3 gain-of-function causes achondroplasia; failed AER causes amelia; HOX disruption causes phocomelia; caudal neuropore failure causes spina bifida"},{"id":"sdl1_q13","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the derivation of musculoskeletal tissues from mesodermal layers, focusing on somite differentiation and the roles of the apical ectodermal ridge (AER) and zone of polarizing activity (ZPA) in upper and lower limb development.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A developmental biology researcher transplants the zone of polarizing activity (ZPA) from the posterior mesenchyme of a chick limb bud to the anterior mesenchyme of the same limb bud at week 5. The developing limb subsequently forms six digits instead of five, with the extra digits arranged in a mirror-image pattern on the anterior side. Which of the following best explains the mechanism underlying this finding?","choices":{"A":"The transplanted ZPA secretes FGF8 into the anterior mesenchyme, overriding the AER signal and causing progress zone cells to produce additional digit condensations proportional to local FGF8 concentration.","B":"The transplanted ZPA secretes FGF10 into the anterior mesenchyme, initiating a new limb bud outgrowth event that recruits additional lateral plate mesoderm cells to form a supernumerary complete digit column.","C":"The transplanted ZPA releases Wnt signaling proteins into the anterior limb field, which redirect dermomyotome cells from the paraxial mesoderm into the limb bud to form extra mesenchymal digit condensations.","D":"The transplanted ZPA releases Indian Hedgehog, which normally functions in growth plate chondrocyte regulation but ectopically activates HOX gene expression in the anterior limb field causing polydactyly.","E":"The transplanted ZPA expresses Sonic Hedgehog in the anterior mesenchyme, creating a second posterior-to-anterior Shh gradient in opposition to the original gradient, which induces mirror-image digit duplication in the anterior limb field."},"correct":"E","explanation":"The ZPA normally sits in the posterior mesenchyme and expresses Sonic Hedgehog (Shh) at high concentration near the fifth digit and low concentration near the first digit. When a second ZPA is placed anteriorly, it creates an opposing Shh gradient, so both the posterior and anterior ends of the limb now interpret themselves as \"posterior.\" The result is mirror-image digit duplication: the normal 1-2-3-4-5 pattern becomes 5-4-3-4-5. This classic experiment established that Shh is the key digit-patterning signal. The ZPA does not express FGF8, FGF10, Wnt, or IHH as its primary signaling output. FGF10 originates from lateral plate mesoderm, and AER expresses FGF8.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ZPA transplant to anterior = mirror-image polydactyly = classic experimental proof that Shh controls A/P digit patterning. Removal of ZPA from medial hand = thumbs on both sides. High Shh concentration = digit 5 identity; low = digit 1. COMLEX: Polydactyly can result from ZPA signal overstimulation or aberrant molecular signals during hand plate formation (weeks 6-8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — polydactyly from aberrant ZPA/Shh signals; amelia from absent AER/FGF8; phocomelia from HOX gene disruption during limb elongation"},{"id":"sdl1_q14","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the derivation of musculoskeletal tissues from mesodermal layers, focusing on somite differentiation and the roles of the apical ectodermal ridge (AER) and zone of polarizing activity (ZPA) in upper and lower limb development.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 3-week-old male infant is evaluated for bilateral limb anomalies. Physical examination reveals complete absence of the scapulae and clavicles with severely hypoplastic upper extremities, while the lower extremities appear normal. Genetic workup reveals a deletion of a transcription factor specifically expressed in the upper limb field of the lateral plate mesoderm. Which gene is most likely disrupted?","choices":{"A":"TBX4, which is specifically expressed in the lower limb lateral plate mesoderm; deletion of TBX4 would cause isolated lower limb aplasia with preservation of upper limb development under intact TBX5 signaling.","B":"HOX9, which establishes the craniocaudal position along the embryonic axis where the upper limb bud forms rather than determining which limb type develops, so its deletion would shift limb position rather than eliminate upper limb identity.","C":"FGF10, which is produced by both upper and lower limb lateral plate mesoderm to induce AER formation and initiate limb bud outgrowth; its deletion would symmetrically affect both upper and lower limb formation.","D":"TBX5, which is specifically expressed in the upper limb lateral plate mesoderm and is required for upper extremity identity; its deletion causes failure of upper limb specification while lower limb development proceeds normally via intact TBX4.","E":"Sonic Hedgehog (Shh), which controls anterior-to-posterior digit patterning via the ZPA and does not specify whether a given limb field will produce an arm versus a leg structure."},"correct":"D","explanation":"TBX5 is the key transcription factor for upper limb identity, expressed in the lateral plate mesoderm at the C5-T1 level. Its deletion prevents the upper limb from forming. TBX4 performs the analogous function in the lower limb field (L2-S3). HOX genes determine where along the body axis a limb bud will form, not which type it becomes — their disruption shifts limb position. FGF10 is a downstream effector of both TBX5 and TBX4, initiating AER induction in both limb fields; its deletion affects both limbs. Shh is the ZPA A/P patterning signal and does not specify limb identity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TBX5 deletion = upper limb aplasia (seen in Holt-Oram syndrome: TBX5 haploinsufficiency causes upper limb defects + cardiac septal defects). TBX4 deletion = lower limb aplasia. COMLEX: Holt-Oram syndrome (TBX5) = radial ray defects + ASD/VSD — a classic board presentation linking limb and cardiac anomalies.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — lateral plate mesoderm forms limb buds; HOX genes determine position (where); TBX genes determine identity (which type)"},{"id":"sdl1_q15","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the derivation of musculoskeletal tissues from mesodermal layers, focusing on somite differentiation and the roles of the apical ectodermal ridge (AER) and zone of polarizing activity (ZPA) in upper and lower limb development.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman presents with acute low back pain after lifting a heavy box at work. MRI shows a herniated intervertebral disc at L4-L5 compressing the L5 nerve root. A first-year medical student reviewing the case asks their attending which embryonic structure gives rise to the nucleus pulposus that is compressing the nerve root. Which of the following is the correct answer?","choices":{"A":"The sclerotome, which forms the fibrous outer ring of the intervertebral disc (the annulus fibrosus) and whose partial failure of migration creates the gelatinous herniated material seen on MRI.","B":"The notochord, which serves as a midline signaling structure during embryogenesis and whose remnant trapped between re-segmented vertebral bodies persists as the gelatinous nucleus pulposus of each intervertebral disc.","C":"The myotome, which gives rise to the paraspinal and intrinsic back muscles innervated by posterior rami and does not contribute structural material to the intervertebral disc or nucleus pulposus.","D":"The meningotome, which forms the dura, arachnoid, and pia mater surrounding the spinal cord and nerve roots; its remnant does not persist within the nucleus pulposus or the disc space.","E":"The lateral plate mesoderm, which forms the pelvic and lower extremity bones and the body wall cavities and does not contribute to midline axial structures such as the vertebral bodies or intervertebral discs."},"correct":"B","explanation":"The notochord is the embryonic midline structure that induces the overlying ectoderm to form the neural plate and signals the surrounding sclerotomes to form the vertebral column. During re-segmentation, the caudal sub-segment of one sclerotome fuses with the cranial sub-segment of the adjacent inferior somite, trapping a remnant of the notochord between the newly formed vertebral bodies. This notochord remnant differentiates into the nucleus pulposus — the gelatinous central portion of the intervertebral disc. The sclerotome forms the annulus fibrosus (the outer ring), not the nucleus pulposus. First Aid explicitly states: \"Notochord becomes nucleus pulposus of intervertebral disc in adults.\"","boardPrep":"Step 1: Notochord → nucleus pulposus (FA direct quote, very high yield). Annulus fibrosus → sclerotome. In disc herniation, the nucleus pulposus (notochord remnant) ruptures through the annulus fibrosus (sclerotome-derived). COMLEX: Disc herniation at L4-L5 affects the L5 nerve root (exits below L5 pedicle); this root innervates the hypaxial muscles of the big toe dorsiflexion and lateral leg sensation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — vertebral column re-segmentation creates nucleus pulposus from notochord remnant; annulus fibrosus from sclerotome"},{"id":"sdl1_q16","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the stages of limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation, including the processes of segmentation, re-segmentation.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"An embryology professor explains that each vertebra is derived from two adjacent somites rather than a single somite. A student asks why this re-segmentation arrangement exists. Which of the following best explains the functional significance of the re-segmentation process?","choices":{"A":"Re-segmentation distributes the notochord remnant evenly between each vertebral body, preventing compression of the spinal cord by ensuring the gelatinous disc material is centered in the intervertebral space.","B":"Re-segmentation allows Sonic Hedgehog from the notochord to diffuse uniformly across all vertebral segments, inducing symmetric ossification of the centrum, pedicles, and neural arches simultaneously.","C":"Re-segmentation is required for the primary ossification centers to appear concurrently in both the centrum and neural arch, because the two sub-segments provide the separate vascular territories needed for each center.","D":"Re-segmentation creates a space at the junction between the original somite units, which becomes the intervertebral foramen allowing spinal nerve roots to exit the vertebral canal and reach their corresponding myotomes and dermatomes.","E":"Re-segmentation prevents the somites from fusing into a continuous bony column around the spinal cord by maintaining individual segmental identities through differential HOX gene expression gradients."},"correct":"D","explanation":"Re-segmentation is functionally critical because it ensures that the intervertebral foramen (the exit point for spinal nerves) sits adjacent to the middle of the original somite — not at the somite boundary. If vertebrae were simply derived from single somites without re-segmentation, spinal nerves would exit directly at the sclerotome-to-sclerotome boundary, which would be mechanically problematic. By re-segmenting, each vertebra straddles the junction of two somites, and the intersomitic space becomes the foramen through which the segmental nerve exits. This is why each spinal nerve serves the same-numbered myotome and dermatome. The notochord remnant distribution and ossification center vascularization are separate processes not dependent on re-segmentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Re-segmentation purpose = creates intervertebral foramina for spinal nerve exit. Each vertebra = caudal half of superior somite + cranial half of inferior somite. This is why each spinal nerve exits BELOW its named vertebra (except C nerves). COMLEX: Intervertebral foramina are the sites of nerve root compression in radiculopathy; somatic dysfunction of a vertebral unit affects the same segment that innervates that myotome and dermatome.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — somite segmentation and HOX gene patterning establish vertebral identities before re-segmentation reorganizes them into the final vertebral column"},{"id":"sdl1_q17","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the stages of limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation, including the processes of segmentation, re-segmentation.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman at 20 weeks gestation has an ultrasound revealing a fluid-filled sac protruding from the lumbar spine. The sac contains meninges filled with cerebrospinal fluid, but the spinal cord and nerve roots are not displaced and no neurologic deficits are identified. Maternal serum AFP was elevated on first-trimester screening. Which of the following correctly identifies this defect and its embryologic basis?","choices":{"A":"Myelomeningocele, in which both CSF-filled meninges and spinal cord or nerve roots herniate through the posterior bony defect; neurologic deficits below the level of the lesion are invariably present due to cord involvement.","B":"Spina bifida occulta, a closed defect with no herniation of any meningeal or neural tissue; discovered incidentally on imaging and typically marked only by a tuft of hair or skin dimple with no sac formation.","C":"Meningocele, resulting from failure of the spinous processes and laminae to close over the lumbar spinal cord; the sac contains only CSF-filled meninges because the neural tube itself closed normally and the cord remains intact.","D":"Rachischisis, the most severe form of spina bifida, in which neurulation completely fails and exposed neural tissue lies on the dorsal surface without any meningeal covering, sac, or skin closure.","E":"Encephalocele, occurring when the cranial neuropore fails to close by day 24 and allows brain parenchyma and meninges to herniate through an opening in the calvarium rather than the lumbar vertebral column."},"correct":"C","explanation":"Meningocele is an open NTD in which the meninges (but not the neural tissue) herniate through a posterior vertebral defect. Because the spinal cord itself remains within the vertebral canal, neurologic function is typically preserved. This differs from myelomeningocele, where both meninges and spinal cord or cauda equina herniate, causing neurologic deficits below the lesion. Spina bifida occulta has no external sac; it is a subclinical bony defect. Rachischisis involves complete neurulation failure with no meningeal covering at all. Encephalocele involves the cranial, not lumbar, neuropore. All open NTDs are associated with elevated maternal serum AFP and reduced fetal swallowing of AFP-rich amniotic fluid.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NTD classification — occulta (no herniation) → meningocele (meninges only, no deficits) → myelomeningocele (cord + meninges, has deficits) → rachischisis (most severe, no closure). AFP elevated in open NTDs. Folic acid supplementation before conception reduces NTD risk. COMLEX: SDL 8 covers spinal dysraphism in the pediatric context; caudal neuropore closes by day 27 — a day earlier than the cranial neuropore closes at day 24 is wrong; cranial closes first (day 24), caudal closes later (day 27).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — neural tube defects from failed caudal neuropore closure; folic acid prevents NTDs by supporting rapid cell division during neurulation"},{"id":"sdl1_q18","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the stages of limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation, including the processes of segmentation, re-segmentation.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A second-year medical student is reviewing the embryologic basis for the anatomical organization of limb muscles. She notes that the upper limb undergoes a 90-degree rotation by day 56 so that the elbow points posteriorly, while the lower limb rotates 90 degrees in the opposite direction. Which of the following correctly describes a consequence of the lower limb rotating opposite to the upper limb?","choices":{"A":"Both upper and lower limbs rotate in the same direction, which is why both the elbow and the knee point posteriorly in anatomical position and are both innervated by the posterior divisions of their respective plexuses.","B":"The lower limb rotation causes the dorsal muscle mass to remain posterior, keeping the knee extensors in the same relative position as the elbow extensors, making the lower limb innervation pattern identical to the upper limb.","C":"The opposite rotation of the lower limb causes the knee to point posteriorly just like the elbow, explaining why the quadriceps are innervated by the posterior division of the lumbosacral plexus rather than the femoral nerve.","D":"The lower limb rotation has no effect on muscle compartment arrangement because hypaxial muscle mass migration from the myotome determines final compartment position independently of any limb bud torsion.","E":"The lower limb extensors, which arise from the dorsal muscle mass, come to lie anteriorly after rotation, while the flexors lie posteriorly — the opposite of the upper limb, where dorsal muscle mass extensors remain posterior."},"correct":"E","explanation":"The upper limb rotates so that the thumb points laterally and the elbow points posteriorly, placing the dorsal muscle mass (extensors) posteriorly — innervated by posterior brachial plexus division (radial/axillary). The lower limb rotates in the opposite direction so that the big toe is medial and the knee points anteriorly. This places the dorsal muscle mass (extensors) anteriorly — the quadriceps and pretibial muscles. This is why the \"extensors\" of the lower limb are on the front and innervated by the femoral nerve (anterior division of lumbosacral plexus), opposite to upper limb extensors. The lower limb flexors (hamstrings, calf) remain posterior, innervated by the tibial nerve (posterior division).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Upper limb: dorsal mass = posterior extensors = radial nerve (posterior division). Lower limb: dorsal mass = anterior extensors = femoral nerve (anterior division). Opposite rotation = inverted compartment positions. COMLEX: This explains dermatome \"barber pole\" arrangement on the lower limb — skin innervation twists due to limb rotation, producing spiral dermatomal patterns.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — dorsal and ventral muscle mass innervation by posterior vs anterior plexus divisions; limb rotation determines final compartment position"},{"id":"sdl1_q19","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific muscle types and the development of muscular tissues, including the critical periods in this process.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 22-year-old male is brought to the emergency department after a motorcycle collision. Imaging reveals a midshaft humerus fracture. On examination he cannot extend the wrist, cannot extend the fingers, and has diminished sensation over the dorsum of the hand and lateral forearm. Which embryologic muscle mass is denervated by this injury and what is its developmental origin?","choices":{"A":"The ventral muscle mass of the upper limb, which migrates anteriorly to form the flexor and adductor compartment and is innervated by the median and ulnar nerves, branches of the anterior division of the brachial plexus.","B":"The epaxial muscle compartment, which derives from the medial myotome and forms the intrinsic back muscles innervated by posterior rami; its injury causes paraspinal atrophy rather than wrist drop or dorsal hand sensory loss.","C":"The hypaxial prevertebral division, which forms muscles along the anterior vertebral column and is innervated by anterior rami; injury to this group causes neck flexion weakness rather than distal upper extremity motor deficit.","D":"The dorsal muscle mass of the upper limb, which migrates posteriorly to form the extensor compartment and is innervated by the radial nerve, a branch of the posterior division of the brachial plexus.","E":"The dorsal muscle mass of the lower limb, which forms the extensor compartment of the thigh and leg innervated by the femoral and common fibular nerves; this group is unrelated to upper extremity function."},"correct":"D","explanation":"Radial nerve palsy from a midshaft humerus fracture is the classic presentation of \"wrist drop\" with associated loss of finger extension and diminished dorsal hand sensation. The radial nerve is a branch of the posterior division of the brachial plexus, which innervates all muscles derived from the dorsal muscle mass of the upper limb. During embryogenesis, the dorsal muscle mass migrates to the posterior compartment as the limb rotates and differentiates into all extensor muscles of the arm, forearm, and hand. The ventral muscle mass forms flexors and adductors, innervated by the anterior division (median, ulnar, musculocutaneous nerves). Epaxial muscles are intrinsic back muscles and are not affected by a peripheral nerve injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Midshaft humerus fracture = radial nerve injury = wrist drop + loss of finger extension + diminished lateral forearm/dorsal hand sensation (from superficial radial nerve). Radial nerve = posterior division = dorsal muscle mass embryologic origin. COMLEX: \"Saturday night palsy\" = radial nerve compression at the spiral groove (same nerve, different mechanism); somatic dysfunction at C7-C8 vertebral levels may reproduce radial nerve symptoms.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — dorsal muscle mass = posterior division brachial plexus; AER and somite myotome migration together establish limb muscle compartments"},{"id":"sdl1_q20","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific muscle types and the development of muscular tissues, including the critical periods in this process.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 52-year-old male presents with progressive bilateral paraspinal muscle weakness and atrophy over 8 months. Electromyography shows denervation at multiple spinal levels bilaterally in the deep back muscles. The findings are confined to the muscles immediately adjacent to the spinous processes. Which embryologic compartment and innervation pattern are disrupted?","choices":{"A":"The hypaxial muscle compartment, derived from the lateral myotome and forming trunk and limb muscles innervated by anterior rami that branch into the cervical, brachial, intercostal, lumbar, and lumbosacral plexuses.","B":"The epaxial muscle compartment, derived from the medial portion of the myotome under posterior neural tube signaling, forming the intrinsic back muscles and innervated by the posterior rami of spinal nerves at each segmental level.","C":"The dorsal muscle mass of the upper limb, which is derived from hypaxial myotome and forms the extensor compartment of the arm and forearm, innervated by the posterior division of the brachial plexus.","D":"The ventral muscle mass of the lower limb, derived from the hypaxial myotome and forming the adductor and flexor compartment of the thigh and leg, innervated by the obturator and tibial nerves.","E":"The sclerotome-derived paraspinal tendons, which form the posterior ligamentous complex of the spine and are not innervated by posterior rami but instead receive nociceptive input from dorsal root ganglion collaterals."},"correct":"B","explanation":"The intrinsic (deep) back muscles — erector spinae, multifidus, rotatores — are derived from the epaxial portion of the myotome, the medial compartment that remains dorsal and is innervated by posterior rami of every spinal segment. Because posterior rami innervate the epaxial muscles at each level, multisegmental bilateral paraspinal denervation points to a process affecting posterior rami or the epaxial compartment specifically. The hypaxial compartment forms all other trunk and limb muscles via anterior rami. The dorsal and ventral muscle masses are specifically limb-related subdivisions of the hypaxial compartment after migration into the limb bud. Tendons are derived from sclerotome but do not contain motor end plates.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Epaxial muscles (deep back/erector spinae/multifidus) = posterior rami only. Hypaxial muscles (everything else) = anterior rami. This is why EMG of paraspinal muscles tests posterior rami, and their denervation helps localize radiculopathy versus plexopathy versus mononeuropathy. COMLEX: OMM treatment of somatic dysfunction targets the epaxial muscles at restricted vertebral segments; trigger points in paraspinal muscles are treated by direct inhibition and muscle energy techniques.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — somite myotome gives rise to epaxial (posterior rami) and hypaxial (anterior rami) compartments; paraxial mesoderm is the common origin"},{"id":"sdl1_q21","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific muscle types and the development of muscular tissues, including the critical periods in this process.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A researcher studying fetal skeletal muscle development collects biopsies at different gestational ages. She observes that at 9 weeks myofilaments have appeared but nuclei remain centrally located. By 20 weeks, fibers show cross-striations. At birth, nuclei have fully shifted to the cell periphery. She further notes that fetal movement begins around the third month of development. Which developmental event is most closely associated with the onset of fetal movement at this time?","choices":{"A":"Completion of synovial fluid secretion by the synovial membrane of fetal joint capsules by approximately the third month of development, allowing myotubes with forming myofilaments to actively mobilize the joints for the first time.","B":"The appearance of myofilaments at nine weeks of embryogenesis, which immediately generates sufficient contractile force for fetal movement before synovial joint cavities have fully formed or filled with fluid.","C":"The peripheral migration of muscle fiber nuclei at birth, which is a histological marker of final muscle maturation occurring too late in development to account for fetal movement beginning in the third month.","D":"The innervation of the myotome by anterior rami of the spinal cord at four weeks of development, which enables immediate coordinated muscle contractions upon completion of neurulation and axon outgrowth.","E":"The formation of secondary ossification centers in the epiphyses during the first two years of postnatal life, which allows load-bearing and initiates the muscle contractions required for coordinated movement."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Fetal movement begins around the third month of intrauterine life (approximately 12 weeks), which correlates with the completion of synovial fluid secretion by the synovial membrane of joint capsules — an event that occurs by the end of the third month. Before this, joint cavities exist anatomically but the synovial layer has not yet begun secreting adequate fluid for smooth joint movement. Myofilaments appear at 9 weeks, but the ability to actually mobilize joints requires the mechanical prerequisite of functional synovial joints. Nuclear migration to the cell periphery occurs at birth, well after fetal movement begins. Motor axons reach the myotome early, but coordinated movement requires both mature neuromuscular junctions and functional joints.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Synovial joint development is complete by the third month, enabling fetal movement. Oligohydramnios limits fetal movement and can cause contractures — e.g., talipes equinovarus (clubfoot) from restricted joint mobilization. COMLEX: Restricted fetal movement is a documented cause of positional deformities; this connects to SDL 1 Obj 4 (clubfoot from oligohydramnios) and SDL 8 (DDH from restricted hip movement).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — synovial joint development from loose mesenchyme by the 3rd month enables the functional use of muscles that were formed from myotome differentiation"},{"id":"sdl1_q22","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline and explain common abnormal musculoskeletal development and discuss the role of teratogens during critical periods of embryogenesis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy is brought to the pediatrician for short stature. He has shortened extremities relative to a normal-length trunk, a prominent forehead, midface hypoplasia, and trident-shaped hands. His parents are of normal stature. Lumbar spine radiographs show decreased interpedicular distance. Neurological examination is normal. Which of the following best explains why this child is at risk for spinal cord compression?","choices":{"A":"Increased growth hormone secretion from the anterior pituitary stimulates IGF-1-driven vertebral body growth asymmetrically, causing anterior osteophyte formation that narrows the spinal canal from the front.","B":"A COMP gene mutation disrupts chondrocyte survival during endochondral ossification, causing early disc degeneration and progressive posterior disc herniation that compresses the cord at multiple levels.","C":"Premature estrogen rise relative to testosterone during early childhood accelerates closure of vertebral growth plates, shortening vertebral bodies and narrowing the spinal canal before age five.","D":"The gain-of-function FGFR3 mutation inhibits chondrocyte proliferation at all growth plates, including those that contribute to vertebral body height and the growth of the neural arches, producing a small spinal canal that can compress the cord.","E":"Failure of secondary ossification centers to appear in the annular epiphyses at puberty results in incomplete vertebral height and a congenitally narrow canal that worsens progressively during adolescence."},"correct":"D","explanation":"Achondroplasia from FGFR3 gain-of-function inhibits endochondral ossification at all growth plates, including those of the vertebral bodies and neural arches. In the lumbar spine, this produces a characteristically narrow interpedicular distance (the distance between the pedicles on AP imaging), creating a congenitally small spinal canal. Even minor disc pathology or osteophyte formation in adulthood can cause cord or cauda equina compression in these patients. This explains why spinal stenosis is a significant adult complication of achondroplasia. Growth hormone excess (gigantism/acromegaly) causes increased bone size via IGF-1 and would not cause a congenitally narrow canal. COMP mutations produce pseudoachondroplasia with joint laxity as the prominent feature.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Achondroplasia complications: (1) spinal stenosis from narrow canal; (2) sleep apnea from foramen magnum narrowing; (3) intelligence normal (membranous ossification unaffected). FGFR3 = constitutive activation = inhibits chondrocytes (gain-of-function paradox). COMLEX: Foramen magnum stenosis in achondroplasia can cause sudden death in infancy from cervical cord compression — an urgent pediatric complication requiring decompression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — endochondral ossification in growth plates driven by chondrocyte proliferation; FGFR3 normally limits growth plate activity, so gain-of-function causes premature closure"},{"id":"sdl1_q23","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline and explain common abnormal musculoskeletal development and discuss the role of teratogens during critical periods of embryogenesis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman in her first trimester takes a medication without knowing it is a known teratogen. Her infant is born with bilateral absence of the humerus, radius, and ulna, with five-fingered hands attached directly to both shoulder girdles. Sensation and motor function of the fingers are near-normal. Which of the following most accurately explains the embryologic mechanism of this malformation?","choices":{"A":"Disruption of HOX gene expression during the critical window of limb elongation (approximately days 30-52) impaired proximal and intermediate limb segment formation while the AER-driven distal hand bud continued developing functional digits.","B":"Absent FGF8 expression from the apical ectodermal ridge during the early limb bud stage caused total limb outgrowth failure, but the hands were spared because digital ray condensations had already formed before teratogen exposure began.","C":"A gain-of-function FGFR3 mutation selectively impaired chondrocyte proliferation in the long bone growth plates of the arm and forearm, sparing the hand bones because they undergo intramembranous rather than endochondral ossification.","D":"Failure of the ZPA to express Sonic Hedgehog during the anterior-to-posterior patterning window resulted in loss of all proximal limb segments, with only the most distal Shh-independent digit structures surviving.","E":"Premature apoptosis of the dorsal muscle mass in the upper limb bud caused secondary bony regression of the humeral and forearm skeletal elements, which require mechanical stimulation from contracting muscles for normal ossification."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Phocomelia — the direct attachment of hands to the trunk with absent intervening segments — is classically caused by thalidomide embryopathy. The mechanism is disruption of HOX gene expression, which patterns the proximal-to-distal identity of limb segments. Because HOX genes establish which part of the limb is \"arm,\" \"forearm,\" or \"hand,\" their disruption at the right stage prevents the proximal and intermediate bones from forming, while the distal AER-maintained hand bud continues to develop functional digits. Total AER absence causes amelia (no limb at all), not phocomelia. FGFR3 gain-of-function causes achondroplasia (short but present bones), not segment aplasia. Shh/ZPA disruption causes digit identity errors, not proximal segment loss. Muscle contraction is not required for bone formation in early embryogenesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Phocomelia = thalidomide; mechanism = HOX gene disruption during critical window days 30-56. Amelia = no AER = no outgrowth at all. Meromelia = partial limb reduction. Teratogen sensitive period = organogenesis (weeks 3-8); limb buds most vulnerable days 28-56. COMLEX: Critical period concept — teratogens produce malformations only when exposure overlaps with the vulnerable developmental window for each organ system.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — HOX genes pattern limb segment identity; AER (FGF8) drives outgrowth — both are necessary for normal limb formation and both are targets of teratogens"},{"id":"sdl1_q24","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline and explain common abnormal musculoskeletal development and discuss the role of teratogens during critical periods of embryogenesis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A newborn male has a fluid-filled sac protruding from the lumbar spine at the L3-L4 level. The sac contains meninges, CSF, and displaced spinal cord with nerve roots. Lower extremity motor and sensory deficits are present below L3, and bladder and bowel function are absent. The mother had taken no prenatal vitamins. Maternal AFP was elevated on first-trimester screening. Which of the following best describes the embryologic event that failed and the preventable risk factor?","choices":{"A":"Failure of cranial neuropore closure by day 24 caused the neural tube defect; folate deficiency is the risk factor, but the resulting defect is anencephaly with absent forebrain rather than a lumbar spinal cord anomaly.","B":"Failed re-segmentation of the lumbar sclerotome somites produced fused vertebrae that compressed the spinal cord; iron deficiency anemia is the preventable risk factor associated with this form of spinal dysraphism.","C":"Failure of caudal neuropore closure by day 27 allowed sclerotomal mesenchyme to be excluded from the posterior arch, producing a myelomeningocele; folate deficiency is the preventable maternal risk factor.","D":"Failure of apoptosis in the lumbar interdigital mesenchyme caused soft tissue bridging over the developing neural tube, preventing normal spinous process formation and creating the fluid-filled posterior sac.","E":"Failed secondary ossification center development in the lumbar vertebral annular epiphyses allowed spinal cord herniation during the pubertal growth spurt; vitamin D deficiency is the relevant preventable risk factor."},"correct":"C","explanation":"Myelomeningocele is the most severe form of spinal dysraphism, occurring when both meninges and spinal cord or nerve roots herniate through a posterior vertebral defect. The embryologic failure is incomplete closure of the caudal neuropore by approximately day 27 of development, which prevents the sclerotomal mesenchyme from migrating posterior to the neural tube to form the spinous processes and laminae. The exposed, unfused neural tissue and meninges herniate posteriorly. Maternal folate deficiency during the periconception period is the primary preventable risk factor; supplementation with 400-800 mcg/day starting at least one month before conception reduces NTD risk significantly. Cranial neuropore closure failure causes anencephaly. Re-segmentation failure causes vertebral fusion, not NTD.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Myelomeningocele = meninges + neural tissue herniate; neurologic deficits below the level. Caudal neuropore closes day 27; cranial closes day 24 (cranial closes first). AFP elevated in open NTDs. Folic acid: supplement 1 month prior to conception (FA). COMLEX: Myelomeningocele at L3-L4 → paraplegia, neurogenic bladder/bowel; management includes early surgical closure within 48 hours of birth to prevent infection and further neural damage.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — caudal neuropore closure by day 27 is required for sclerotomal mesenchyme to form posterior vertebral elements; failure prevents arch formation and exposes neural tube"}]},{"sdlNumber":4,"title":"Knee Pain: Diagnostic Workflow & Pathologies","questions":[{"id":"sdl4_q01","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physician evaluating knee pain uses a 5-step sequential diagnostic workflow. The first step is to determine whether pain is related to trauma. Which of the following correctly identifies the FIFTH and final step in this knee pain diagnostic workflow?","choices":{"A":"Determining whether the pain is related to acute trauma or a recent sports-related mechanism","B":"Ruling out a dangerous medical condition such as septic arthritis, malignancy, or fracture","C":"Assessing whether osteoarthritis or another type of arthritis is the underlying cause","D":"Evaluating whether nerve pathology such as radiculopathy or referred pain is responsible","E":"Determining whether the pain is localized and, if so, identifying the specific anatomic pain generator"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The 5-step knee pain diagnostic workflow proceeds: (1) Is pain trauma-related? (2) Is a dangerous condition the cause? (3) Is OA or arthritis responsible? (4) Is nerve pathology such as radiculopathy responsible? (5) Is pain localized and, if so, what structure is the pain generator? Identifying the specific anatomic pain generator is the final step that guides targeted treatment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A structured diagnostic workflow is essential — always rule out dangerous causes (infection, fracture, tumor) before assuming OA. COMLEX: Osteopathic palpatory exam can help localize the pain generator at step 5.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — knee pain workflow: 5 sequential questions from trauma to localized pain generator identification"},{"id":"sdl4_q02","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old male presents with acute knee pain and swelling after falling from a ladder at work. He has point tenderness over the tibial plateau and cannot bear full weight. Applying the knee pain diagnostic workflow, which step should the physician prioritize FIRST?","choices":{"A":"Proceeding directly to the arthritis differential and ordering an X-ray series for OA staging","B":"Evaluating for nerve pathology such as radiculopathy as the most likely cause of acute swelling","C":"Ruling out dangerous medical conditions including fracture, dislocation, or vascular injury","D":"Identifying the localized pain generator by palpating individual knee compartments systematically","E":"Initiating conservative management with PRICE therapy before completing the diagnostic workflow"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Step 2 of the knee pain workflow — ruling out dangerous medical conditions — takes priority when trauma is identified in step 1. Fracture, dislocation, vascular injury, and compartment syndrome must be excluded before advancing to the arthritis or nerve pathology steps. Proceeding to OA or localized pain generator assessment before ruling out these conditions is an error.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ottawa Knee Rules guide X-ray ordering after knee trauma (age >55, inability to bear weight, isolated patella tenderness, or fibular head tenderness). COMLEX: Always document neurovascular status distal to a suspected fracture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — knee pain workflow: 5 sequential questions from trauma to localized pain generator identification"},{"id":"sdl4_q03","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman presents with chronic bilateral knee pain worsening with activity and stiffness lasting 15 minutes in the morning. She has no trauma, no fever, and normal neurological exam. The physician has excluded dangerous conditions and nerve pathology. Which step in the knee pain diagnostic workflow should be evaluated NEXT?","choices":{"A":"Returning to step 1 to re-evaluate for an unrecognized traumatic mechanism of injury","B":"Evaluating whether osteoarthritis or another type of arthritis is the underlying cause","C":"Skipping to step 5 and identifying the localized pain generator without further workup","D":"Immediately ordering MRI to characterize the meniscal and ligamentous anatomy in detail","E":"Referring to neurology to rule out lumbar radiculopathy as the source of bilateral knee pain"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After excluding dangerous conditions (step 2) and nerve pathology (step 4), the 5-step workflow directs the clinician to step 3: evaluating for OA or another arthritis. The clinical features — bilateral distribution, activity-related worsening, morning stiffness under 30 minutes, and no trauma — are classic for OA and fit perfectly within this step.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA clinical criteria include age ≥45, usage-related pain in one or few joints, and morning stiffness ≤30 minutes. COMLEX: Somatic dysfunction of the knee may accompany OA and can be treated with OMT as adjunctive therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — knee pain workflow: 5 sequential questions from trauma to localized pain generator identification"},{"id":"sdl4_q04","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ACL/PCL/MCL/LCL injuries and the unhappy triad.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 20-year-old female soccer player plants her foot and receives a direct lateral blow to her knee, creating a valgus force. She immediately collapses with a \"pop,\" develops hemarthrosis within hours, and has medial joint line tenderness, positive anterior drawer, and positive valgus stress test with laxity at 30 degrees. This presentation is MOST consistent with which combination of injuries?","choices":{"A":"PCL, LCL, and lateral meniscus — the unhappy triad variant from a posterior hyperflexion mechanism","B":"ACL, PCL, and lateral meniscus — multiligamentous injury from a combined valgus and rotational force","C":"ACL, MCL, and medial meniscus — the classic unhappy triad from lateral valgus force on a planted foot","D":"MCL, LCL, and popliteal ligament — posterolateral corner injury from combined varus and hyperextension","E":"Patellar tendon, ACL, and medial collateral ligament — extensor mechanism disruption from deceleration"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The unhappy triad consists of ACL, MCL, and medial meniscus injury caused by a lateral valgus force with the foot planted — the exact mechanism described. The anterior drawer sign indicates ACL disruption, valgus stress laxity confirms MCL injury, and medial joint line tenderness suggests medial meniscal involvement. First Aid notes that lateral meniscus involvement is actually more common than medial in conjunction with ACL+MCL injury, but the classic teaching triad remains ACL+MCL+medial meniscus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Unhappy triad = ACL + MCL + medial meniscus (classic); First Aid notes lateral meniscus is more frequently co-injured. Lachman test (knee at 30°) is more sensitive than anterior drawer for ACL. COMLEX: Valgus force is the classic mechanism for this CP8 knee injury pattern.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — ACL: Lachman/anterior drawer; PCL: posterior sag/posterior drawer; MCL: valgus stress; LCL: varus stress; unhappy triad = ACL + MCL + medial meniscus"},{"id":"sdl4_q05","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ACL/PCL/MCL/LCL injuries and the unhappy triad.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old rugby player falls directly onto his flexed right knee with his ankle plantarflexed. He develops posterolateral knee pain and swelling. Examination reveals a positive posterior sag sign and 8mm of posterior tibial glide on the posterior drawer test at 90 degrees of knee flexion. Which ligament is most likely injured and what is its primary function?","choices":{"A":"Anterior cruciate ligament, which resists anterior translation of the tibia on the femur","B":"Posterior cruciate ligament, which resists posterior translation of the tibia on the femur","C":"Medial collateral ligament, which resists valgus stress and medial compartment widening","D":"Lateral collateral ligament, which resists varus stress and posterolateral corner instability","E":"Popliteofibular ligament, which resists external tibial rotation and posterior subluxation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The PCL resists posterior tibial translation on the femur. The classic PCL injury mechanism is a fall onto a flexed knee with a plantarflexed ankle, or a dashboard injury (anterior force to the proximal tibia with the knee flexed). Positive posterior sag sign and posterior drawer test confirm PCL disruption. The mechanism and findings rule out ACL (which would show anterior drawer/Lachman), MCL (valgus stress), and LCL (varus stress).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PCL mnemonic — medial femoral condyle to POSTERIOR tibia. PCL injury: posterior sag + posterior drawer at 90°. MOI: fall on flexed knee with plantarflexed ankle OR dashboard injury (MVA). COMLEX: PCL rarely injured in isolation; assess for posterolateral corner co-injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — ACL: Lachman/anterior drawer; PCL: posterior sag/posterior drawer; MCL: valgus stress; LCL: varus stress; unhappy triad = ACL + MCL + medial meniscus"},{"id":"sdl4_q06","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ACL/PCL/MCL/LCL injuries and the unhappy triad.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 32-year-old football player sustains a valgus blow to the knee. Examination shows medial joint tenderness, laxity of 7mm on valgus stress at 30 degrees of flexion, and a firm endpoint. There is no laxity at 0 degrees. X-ray is negative. Which MCL injury grade does this represent, and what structures are most likely intact?","choices":{"A":"Grade 1 injury with laxity under 5mm and a solid endpoint, indicating microscopic fiber disruption only","B":"Grade 3 injury with laxity over 10mm and no perceptible endpoint, indicating complete MCL rupture","C":"Grade 4 injury with complete bony avulsion from the medial femoral condyle requiring urgent surgery","D":"Grade 2 injury with 5-9mm laxity at 30 degrees and a firm endpoint, indicating partial MCL disruption with the deep capsular layer largely intact","E":"Grade 3 injury with laxity at both 0 and 30 degrees, indicating complete MCL and ACL co-disruption"},"correct":"D","explanation":"MCL grading: Grade 1 = <5mm laxity, solid endpoint; Grade 2 = 5-9mm laxity, firm endpoint (partial tear); Grade 3 = ≥10mm laxity, no endpoint (complete rupture). Laxity at 30° isolates the superficial MCL. Laxity at 0° indicates deep capsular and ACL involvement as well. This patient has 7mm laxity with a firm endpoint = Grade 2.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MCL valgus stress test at 30° = superficial MCL; laxity at 0° adds deep MCL and possibly ACL involvement. Grade 2 treated conservatively. Pellegrini-Stieda lesion = chronic MCL calcification on X-ray from prior injury. COMLEX: MCL is the most commonly injured knee ligament overall.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — ACL: Lachman/anterior drawer; PCL: posterior sag/posterior drawer; MCL: valgus stress; LCL: varus stress; unhappy triad = ACL + MCL + medial meniscus"},{"id":"sdl4_q07","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of meniscus injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 26-year-old male basketball player twists his planted knee during a game, immediately feeling pain at the medial joint line followed by intermittent locking and giving-out episodes. McMurray test performed with external rotation and valgus force at 30 degrees of flexion produces a palpable click and medial pain. Which meniscus is injured and how does the McMurray test confirm this?","choices":{"A":"Lateral meniscus, because internal rotation with varus force stresses the lateral compartment reproducing lateral symptoms","B":"Posterior horn of the lateral meniscus, because external rotation with flexion compresses the posterolateral space","C":"Both menisci equally, because the McMurray test is non-specific and stresses medial and lateral simultaneously","D":"Anterior horn of the medial meniscus, because valgus stress preferentially loads the anterior attachment site","E":"Medial meniscus, because external rotation with valgus force stresses the medial compartment and reproduces medial joint line pain and clicking"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The McMurray test mnemonic: LIME — Internal rotation + varus force = Lateral meniscus; External rotation + valgus = Medial meniscus. External rotation stresses the medial compartment; a click or pain with this maneuver implicates the medial meniscus. Medial meniscal tears are more common than lateral (medial > lateral), occurring at 61/100,000 in the general population.","boardPrep":"Step 1: McMurray LIME mnemonic — Internal+varus=Lateral, External+valgus=Medial. Locking = bucket-handle tear. Mechanical symptoms (locking, giving-out) are indications for surgical referral. COMLEX: Meniscal tears are a named COMLEX CP8 musculoskeletal condition.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — McMurray: external rotation + valgus = medial meniscus; internal rotation + varus = lateral meniscus; medial > lateral incidence; 61/100,000"},{"id":"sdl4_q08","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of meniscus injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following patient profiles is MOST consistent with a degenerative meniscal tear rather than a traumatic meniscal tear?","choices":{"A":"A 16-year-old female soccer player who develops acute swelling and locking after a contact pivot injury","B":"A 55-year-old male with known knee osteoarthritis who develops medial pain after minimal twisting while rising from a chair","C":"A 22-year-old wrestler who sustains acute medial joint line pain after a takedown with rotational force","D":"A 30-year-old marathon runner who develops sudden lateral pain after a varus twisting fall on a trail","E":"A 19-year-old basketball player with prior ACL reconstruction who develops locking with pivoting drills"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Degenerative meniscal tears occur in older patients (typically over 40) with pre-existing arthritis, often with minimal or no significant trauma. Risk factors include male gender, age >40, prior ACL injury, and presence of arthritis. Traumatic tears occur in younger individuals through acute twisting mechanisms. The 55-year-old with OA and minimal-trauma onset fits the degenerative profile exactly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Degenerative tears = horizontal cleavage pattern, older patients, OA background, minimal trauma. Traumatic tears = vertical/longitudinal (bucket-handle), younger athletes, acute mechanism. COMLEX: OMT is not appropriate for acute bucket-handle tears causing mechanical locking.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — McMurray: external rotation + valgus = medial meniscus; internal rotation + varus = lateral meniscus; medial > lateral incidence; 61/100,000"},{"id":"sdl4_q09","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of meniscus injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old male has been managed conservatively for 8 weeks for a meniscal tear with PRICE therapy and physical therapy. He continues to experience his knee locking during daily activities, cannot fully extend the joint, and reports recurrent giving-out episodes. Which of the following findings BEST justifies surgical referral at this point?","choices":{"A":"A positive Thessaly maneuver in the clinic without any subjective sense of instability or locking during activities","B":"Mild medial joint line tenderness reproducible with deep squatting but no loss of full range of motion","C":"Persistent mechanical symptoms including true locking and giving-out that have not responded to an adequate trial of conservative treatment","D":"MRI showing a Grade 1 signal change within the posterior horn without surface disruption or extension","E":"Occasional clicking with knee flexion beyond 90 degrees without effusion or functional limitation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Surgical referral for meniscal tears is indicated when: (1) mechanical symptoms persist (locking, giving-out), (2) multiligamentous pathology is present, or (3) conservative measures have failed. True locking — inability to fully extend the knee — typically indicates a displaced bucket-handle tear requiring arthroscopic intervention. A positive provocative test without mechanical symptoms alone does not meet the threshold.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Surgical indications = mechanical symptoms (locking/giving-out), multiligamentous instability, or failure of conservative care. True locking = bucket-handle tear. Initial management = PRICE + PT + avoidance of deep squatting and pivoting. COMLEX: Role of regenerative injections (PRP) is being studied for degenerative meniscal tears.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — McMurray: external rotation + valgus = medial meniscus; internal rotation + varus = lateral meniscus; medial > lateral incidence; 61/100,000"},{"id":"sdl4_q10","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 23-year-old soccer player sustains a valgus force injury with tibial rotation and develops acute hemarthrosis. AP X-ray shows a small avulsed bone fragment at the anterolateral margin of the proximal tibia, just below the lateral tibial plateau. Which fracture does this represent and what associated injury is strongly implied?","choices":{"A":"Pellegrini-Stieda lesion, a calcification at the medial femoral condyle indicating prior chronic MCL injury","B":"Arcuate sign, a small fibular head avulsion indicating injury to the posterolateral corner structures","C":"Osgood-Schlatter lesion, fragmentation at the tibial tubercle from patellar tendon traction in adolescents","D":"Segond fracture, an avulsion of the anterolateral ligament from its tibial attachment, strongly implying a concurrent ACL tear","E":"Tibial plateau impaction fracture, indicating articular surface depression from axial loading of the lateral condyle"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A Segond fracture is an avulsion of the anterolateral ligament (ALL) from the lateral tibial plateau. It appears as a small osseous fragment lateral to the tibial plateau on AP X-ray and is highly specific for concurrent ACL tear — so much so that a Segond fracture is considered pathognomonic for ACL disruption until proven otherwise. The arcuate sign is a posterolateral corner fracture of the fibular head. Pellegrini-Stieda = chronic MCL calcification.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Segond fracture = lateral tibial rim avulsion = ACL tear until proven otherwise. X-ray finding: small bony fragment lateral to proximal tibia on AP view. COMLEX: All traumatic knee injuries require neurovascular assessment distal to the injury before imaging workup.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — Segond fracture = anterolateral ligament avulsion = strong ACL tear marker; MRI is gold standard for concurrent soft tissue injury evaluation"},{"id":"sdl4_q11","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"According to the Ottawa Knee Rules, which of the following patient presentations mandates knee radiography after acute trauma?","choices":{"A":"Inability to bear weight for four steps, age over 55 years, or isolated tenderness at the fibular head or patella","B":"Any knee pain occurring after a sports injury regardless of the ability to ambulate after the event","C":"Presence of joint effusion or hemarthrosis regardless of weight-bearing status at the time of presentation","D":"Positive Lachman test suggesting ligamentous laxity in the absence of bony point tenderness","E":"Prior history of knee surgery or previous fracture regardless of current mechanism of injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ottawa Knee Rules indicate X-ray if: (1) age ≥55, (2) isolated tenderness at the fibular head, (3) isolated patellar tenderness with no other bony tenderness, (4) inability to flex to 90°, or (5) inability to bear weight (4 steps) both immediately and in the ED. Effusion alone, prior surgery, and ligamentous laxity without the above criteria do not mandate imaging per the Ottawa Rules.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ottawa Knee Rules — validated decision rule to reduce unnecessary knee X-rays after trauma. Sensitivity approaches 100% for fracture. COMLEX: These rules apply to adults; pediatric knee trauma generally warrants lower threshold for imaging given growth plate vulnerability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — Segond fracture = anterolateral ligament avulsion = strong ACL tear marker; MRI is gold standard for concurrent soft tissue injury evaluation"},{"id":"sdl4_q12","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old male sustains a tibial plateau fracture during a football game. X-ray confirms the fracture but cannot characterize potential soft tissue injuries. Which imaging modality is the GOLD STANDARD for evaluating concurrent meniscal tears, ligamentous disruption, and chondral injury in this setting?","choices":{"A":"CT scan of the knee, which provides the best characterization of articular cartilage and synovial tissue","B":"Bone scan, which detects areas of increased metabolic activity corresponding to ligamentous microtrauma","C":"Diagnostic arthroscopy, which is the definitive assessment but carries procedural risk and is not first-line","D":"Weight-bearing X-ray series, which adequately characterizes all soft tissue injuries around the fracture site","E":"MRI, which provides superior soft tissue contrast and characterizes meniscal, ligamentous, and cartilage pathology not visible on plain radiographs"},"correct":"E","explanation":"MRI is the gold standard for evaluating soft tissue injuries — meniscal tears, ligament disruption, and cartilage damage — that accompany knee fractures. CT excels at defining bony architecture and articular depression but is inferior to MRI for soft tissue. Bone scan is not used for acute soft tissue evaluation. Arthroscopy remains definitive but invasive; MRI guides surgical planning first.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MRI = sensitive and specific for meniscal tears (>90%) and ligament injuries. After tibial plateau fracture, MRI changes surgical plan in a high proportion of cases by revealing meniscal and ligamentous co-injuries. COMLEX: Always image before manipulating a post-fracture knee.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — Segond fracture = anterolateral ligament avulsion = strong ACL tear marker; MRI is gold standard for concurrent soft tissue injury evaluation"},{"id":"sdl4_q13","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of patellofemoral syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Patellofemoral syndrome (PFS), commonly called \"runner's knee,\" is one of the most prevalent knee complaints in outpatient practice. Which of the following correctly describes the proportion of all knee problems that PFS represents in a typical outpatient clinic setting?","choices":{"A":"Fewer than 5% of all knee problems, primarily seen in elderly sedentary patients with significant comorbidities","B":"Approximately 8-10% of all knee problems, similar in incidence to lateral meniscal tears in athletes","C":"More than 60% of all knee problems, making it the most common musculoskeletal complaint overall","D":"Between 25 and 40% of all knee problems seen in outpatient clinical settings","E":"Exactly 15% of all knee problems, documented exclusively in female distance runners under age 30"},"correct":"D","explanation":"PFS accounts for 25-40% of all knee problems seen in clinic, making it one of the most common knee complaints in outpatient practice. Its true incidence is unknown due to under-reporting and variable diagnostic criteria. It can be traumatic (fall on flexed knee) or atraumatic (overuse). It is NOT exclusively seen in runners and affects all activity levels and ages.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PFS is extremely common; also called \"chondromalacia patella.\" Key clinical finding: pain with patellofemoral compression test and single-leg squat. Biomechanical drivers = dynamic valgus, quad weakness, hip weakness. COMLEX: PFS often has a component of somatic dysfunction of the patellofemoral articulation amenable to soft tissue OMT.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — patellofemoral syndrome = 25-40% of knee problems; PT with quad and hip strengthening is first-line; dynamic valgus is key biomechanical driver"},{"id":"sdl4_q14","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of patellofemoral syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 16-year-old female cross-country runner presents with bilateral anterior knee pain worsening with stairs, prolonged sitting with knees bent, and single-leg squatting. Patellofemoral compression test is positive bilaterally. There is no effusion or joint line tenderness. Dynamic valgus is observed on single-leg squat. Which is the MOST appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":{"A":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injection into the patellofemoral joint to reduce inflammatory-driven cartilage pain","B":"Immediate surgical referral for arthroscopic debridement of patellar articular cartilage surface changes","C":"Complete activity restriction for 3 months with cast immobilization to allow cartilage surface recovery","D":"Oral opioid analgesics as a bridge therapy while awaiting specialist evaluation for surgical management","E":"Physical therapy targeting quadriceps strengthening, posterior hip strengthening, and hamstring flexibility to correct biomechanical contributors to patellar maltracking"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Physical therapy is the cornerstone first-line treatment for PFS. The program targets: (1) quadriceps strengthening for patellar stabilization, (2) posterior hip strengthening (gluteus medius/maximus) to reduce dynamic valgus, and (3) hamstring flexibility. Dynamic valgus is a key biomechanical driver of patellofemoral maltracking and increased contact pressure. Surgery and injections are not first-line for PFS.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PFS first-line = PT with quad + hip strengthening. No role for surgery without prior PT failure. Dynamic valgus from hip abductor weakness is the dominant biomechanical driver. COMLEX: Patellar taping (McConnell technique) may provide short-term symptom relief as an adjunct to PT.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — patellofemoral syndrome = 25-40% of knee problems; PT with quad and hip strengthening is first-line; dynamic valgus is key biomechanical driver"},{"id":"sdl4_q15","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of patellofemoral syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old male recreational cyclist develops anterior knee pain reproducible with the patellofemoral compression test and aggravated by prolonged knee flexion (the \"movie sign\"). Which mechanism BEST explains the pathophysiology of pain in patellofemoral syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Synovitis from repetitive friction between the iliotibial band and lateral femoral epicondyle during cycling","B":"Abnormal contact pressure between the patellar articular cartilage and the anterior femoral trochlear groove due to patellar maltracking","C":"Posterior knee capsule inflammation from excessive tibial internal rotation during the power phase of cycling","D":"Tibial stress fracture from repetitive axial load producing referred anterior knee pain from periosteal irritation","E":"Avascular necrosis of the patellar apex from stress-related disruption of blood supply during high-cadence cycling"},"correct":"B","explanation":"PFS results from abnormal patellofemoral contact pressure caused by patellar maltracking — the patella does not glide smoothly through the trochlear groove. Increased contact pressure on articular cartilage produces pain, particularly with activities that increase patellofemoral loading (stairs, squatting, prolonged sitting = \"movie sign\"). The other choices describe separate entities: IT band syndrome, popliteal pathology, tibial stress fracture, and AVN.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"Movie sign\" = pain after prolonged sitting with knees flexed (patellofemoral contact pressure increases with knee flexion). Dynamic valgus increases lateral patellar tracking and amplifies contact pressure. COMLEX: PFS in runners often presents with bilateral symptoms unlike meniscal or ligamentous injuries.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — patellofemoral syndrome = 25-40% of knee problems; PT with quad and hip strengthening is first-line; dynamic valgus is key biomechanical driver"},{"id":"sdl4_q16","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of prepatellar bursitis and patellar subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 50-year-old tile installer presents with fluctuant, erythematous swelling directly overlying the patella after years of kneeling on hard surfaces. He has low-grade fever. Aspiration reveals cloudy, purulent fluid and the Gram stain shows gram-positive cocci in clusters. Which is the MOST appropriate immediate management?","choices":{"A":"Compression wrap and elevation alone without aspiration or antibiotics, since bursitis resolves with PRICE therapy","B":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injection into the prepatellar bursa to reduce acute inflammation and prevent recurrence","C":"Colchicine therapy for presumed gout since urate crystals can cause purulent-appearing fluid and elevated WBC","D":"Immediate surgical bursectomy without prior antibiotic therapy given the occupational exposure and purulent aspirate","E":"Aspiration of the bursal fluid combined with systemic antibiotics targeting Staphylococcus aureus for septic prepatellar bursitis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Septic prepatellar bursitis (positive Gram stain, fever, purulent aspirate) requires aspiration plus systemic antibiotics. Gram-positive cocci in clusters = Staphylococcus aureus, the most common causative organism. Corticosteroid injection into a septic bursa is contraindicated. Colchicine is for gout (urate crystals on aspirate, no organisms). Bursectomy is reserved for recurrent or refractory cases, not first-line. PRICE alone is insufficient for septic bursitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Prepatellar bursitis ddx — septic (fever, erythema, organisms) vs. gout (urate crystals) vs. mechanical (kneeling exposure, no fever). Septic = aspirate + antibiotics. \"Housemaid's knee\" = classic name for prepatellar bursitis from chronic kneeling. COMLEX: Distinguish intra-articular from extra-articular fluid collection before injecting.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 6 — prepatellar bursitis: septic = aspirate + antibiotics, gout = aspirate + colchicine; patellar subluxation: MPFL is primary lateral restraint"},{"id":"sdl4_q17","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of prepatellar bursitis and patellar subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 15-year-old female volleyball player sustains her first lateral patellar dislocation after a non-contact planting and pivoting maneuver. Reduction is achieved in the emergency department. MRI confirms disruption of the medial patellofemoral ligament without osteochondral fracture. She has no prior history of patellar instability. What is the MOST appropriate initial management?","choices":{"A":"Immediate surgical MPFL reconstruction regardless of dislocation episode history or recurrence risk","B":"Long-leg cast immobilization for 10 weeks before any rehabilitation or weightbearing is permitted","C":"PRICE therapy, physical therapy focusing on quadriceps and hip strengthening, and a patellar stabilizing brace as first-line conservative management for a first-episode dislocation","D":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injection to reduce hemarthrosis followed by early return to sport","E":"Lateral retinacular release as the primary surgical intervention for first-episode lateral dislocation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"First-episode patellar dislocation without osteochondral fracture or predisposing anatomic factors is managed conservatively with PRICE, PT (quad/hip strengthening), and patellar bracing. Surgical MPFL reconstruction is reserved for recurrent dislocations or when predisposing anatomic factors (trochlear dysplasia, high TT-TG distance) are present. Lateral retinacular release alone does not address the MPFL deficiency and is not standard first-line care.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MPFL = primary medial restraint to lateral patellar displacement. MRI is the key imaging study post-reduction. Surgery is considered for recurrent dislocation or anatomic predisposition. COMLEX: Patellar instability is a CP8 joint dislocation condition; ensure documentation of ligamentous laxity as a predisposing factor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 6 — prepatellar bursitis: septic = aspirate + antibiotics, gout = aspirate + colchicine; patellar subluxation: MPFL is primary lateral restraint"},{"id":"sdl4_q18","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of prepatellar bursitis and patellar subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"When evaluating a patient with recurrent patellar subluxation using MRI, which structure is the PRIMARY imaging target for assessing the integrity of the medial patellar restraint system?","choices":{"A":"Posterior cruciate ligament, which acts as the primary medial stabilizer of the patellofemoral articulation","B":"Quadriceps tendon, which is assessed for longitudinal splitting and partial tearing in recurrent instability","C":"Lateral collateral ligament, which when torn allows unrestricted medial-to-lateral patellar displacement","D":"Medial patellofemoral ligament, which is the primary passive restraint preventing lateral displacement of the patella","E":"Anterior cruciate ligament, which indirectly stabilizes the patella by controlling tibial rotation at the knee"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The medial patellofemoral ligament (MPFL) is the primary passive restraint against lateral patellar displacement, providing approximately 50-60% of total medial restraining force. MPFL disruption occurs in virtually all patellar dislocations. On MRI, MPFL injury appears as signal change or discontinuity along its course from the medial femoral condyle to the superomedial patella. PCL, ACL, LCL, and quadriceps tendon do not serve as primary medial patellar restraints.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MPFL runs from medial femoral epicondyle to superomedial patella. Torn in nearly 100% of patellar dislocations. Merchant view (X-ray) = sunrise view for patellofemoral alignment. COMLEX: Ligamentous laxity as a systemic condition predisposes to recurrent patellar instability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 6 — prepatellar bursitis: septic = aspirate + antibiotics, gout = aspirate + colchicine; patellar subluxation: MPFL is primary lateral restraint"},{"id":"sdl4_q19","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, histopathology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of popliteal cyst.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A popliteal cyst (Baker's cyst) is best described pathophysiologically as which of the following structures?","choices":{"A":"A true synovial cyst arising from a congenital remnant of the embryonic knee joint capsule in adults","B":"A ganglion cyst forming from periarticular connective tissue without communication to the joint space","C":"A hematoma that organizes into a posterior fluid collection after posterior cruciate ligament rupture","D":"A lipoma within the popliteal fossa that compresses neurovascular structures during knee flexion","E":"A gastrocnemius-semimembranosus bursitis that frequently communicates with the synovial cavity of the knee joint"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A popliteal cyst (Baker's cyst) is a gastrocnemius-semimembranosus bursitis — specifically a fluid collection within the bursa located between the medial gastrocnemius and semimembranosus tendons. In adults it typically communicates with the synovial cavity, allowing joint fluid to accumulate in the bursa when intra-articular pressure increases. This distinguishes it from a true ganglion cyst (no synovial communication) or a hematoma (post-traumatic).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Baker's cyst = popliteal fluid in gastrocnemius-semimembranosus bursa, communicating with synovial space in adults. Causes include OA, RA, and meniscal tears. COMLEX: Rupture of a Baker's cyst can mimic DVT (\"pseudothrombophlebitis\") — ultrasound differentiates.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 7 — Baker's cyst = gastrocnemius-semimembranosus bursitis communicating with synovial space; bimodal peaks at ages 4-7 and 35-70"},{"id":"sdl4_q20","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, histopathology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of popliteal cyst.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Popliteal cysts demonstrate a bimodal age distribution with two distinct incidence peaks. Which of the following correctly identifies BOTH peak age ranges and their corresponding pathophysiological contexts?","choices":{"A":"Ages 1-3 years in infants due to congenital joint laxity, and ages 50-60 due to post-arthroplasty bursitis","B":"Ages 12-18 in adolescent athletes due to meniscal overuse, and ages 40-55 due to early inflammatory arthritis","C":"Ages 20-35 in young adults from acute knee ligament injuries, and ages 65-80 from post-surgical adhesions","D":"Ages 4-7 years in children with different non-communicating pathoanatomy, and ages 35-70 in adults with underlying degenerative joint disease","E":"Ages 8-12 in school-age children from sports overuse, and ages 60-75 from end-stage knee osteoarthritis only"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Popliteal cysts have two incidence peaks: (1) ages 4-7, where the cyst typically does NOT communicate with the joint space (different pathoanatomy than adults) and frequently resolves spontaneously; (2) ages 35-70, where the cyst is associated with underlying intra-articular pathology (OA, RA, meniscal tears) and communicates with the synovial space. Treatment differs significantly between the two groups.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Children (4-7) — non-communicating, often resolve spontaneously with watchful waiting or aspiration. Adults (35-70) — communicating, address underlying pathology. Baker's cyst rupture mimics DVT with calf pseudothrombophlebitis. COMLEX: Ultrasound is useful to guide aspiration and confirm communication with joint.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 7 — Baker's cyst = gastrocnemius-semimembranosus bursitis communicating with synovial space; bimodal peaks at ages 4-7 and 35-70"},{"id":"sdl4_q21","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, histopathology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of popliteal cyst.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old male with advanced knee osteoarthritis presents with a large posterior knee mass and restricted flexion. Ultrasound confirms a popliteal cyst communicating with the synovial space. He has undergone two prior aspirations that resolved the cyst temporarily, but it returned within 3 months each time. Which represents the MOST effective long-term treatment strategy?","choices":{"A":"Treating the underlying knee osteoarthritis to reduce excess synovial fluid production, since cyst recurrence reflects persistent intra-articular pathology","B":"Surgically excising the cyst wall through a posterior popliteal approach as the definitive long-term treatment","C":"Performing repeated aspiration every 3 months indefinitely to prevent symptomatic recurrence of the cyst","D":"Prescribing custom orthotics to reduce tibial loading and allow the cyst to reabsorb through offloading","E":"Aspirating the cyst with concurrent corticosteroid injection as the definitive non-surgical management"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Popliteal cysts in adults recur after aspiration because the underlying cause — excess synovial fluid production from OA, RA, or meniscal tears — is not addressed. The cyst is a consequence, not a primary pathology. Effective treatment requires addressing the root cause. If OA is the driver, management follows OA guidelines (exercise, NSAIDs, IACS, eventually TKA). Aspirating the cyst without treating the source leads to predictable recurrence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Baker's cyst = manifestation of intra-articular pathology. Treat the cause (OA, RA, meniscal tear). Aspiration alone = temporizing. COMLEX: This principle mirrors osteopathic philosophy — treat the primary dysfunction, not the compensatory finding.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 7 — Baker's cyst = gastrocnemius-semimembranosus bursitis communicating with synovial space; bimodal peaks at ages 4-7 and 35-70"},{"id":"sdl4_q22","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"According to the simplest clinical diagnostic criteria for knee osteoarthritis, a diagnosis can be established when a patient has persistent usage-related joint pain in one or a few joints, age of 45 years or older, and which additional criterion?","choices":{"A":"Elevated ESR above 40 mm/hr with synovial biopsy showing fibrocartilaginous pannus formation","B":"Morning stiffness lasting 30 minutes or less, without features of systemic inflammatory arthritis","C":"Positive rheumatoid factor with symmetric small joint involvement and periarticular osteopenia","D":"Positive ANA titer with malar rash, photosensitivity, and constitutional symptoms","E":"X-ray showing periarticular erosions with uniform joint space loss and cortical destruction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The simplest OA diagnostic criteria are: (1) persistent usage-related joint pain in one or a few joints, (2) age ≥45 years, and (3) morning stiffness ≤30 minutes. This triad establishes the clinical diagnosis without requiring imaging or laboratory studies. Inflammatory arthritis (RA, SLE, vasculitis) is distinguished by morning stiffness >1 hour, positive serology, systemic features, and erosive changes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA is a CLINICAL diagnosis — morning stiffness ≤30 min vs. RA (>1 hour). Usage-related pain = mechanical (worse with activity, better with rest). COMLEX: OA is the most common form of arthritis; CP8 named musculoskeletal condition for COMLEX.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — knee OA: clinical diagnosis; OARSI core = structured exercise + arthritis education; weight-bearing X-ray is gold standard imaging"},{"id":"sdl4_q23","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"According to OARSI guidelines for non-surgical management of knee osteoarthritis, which of the following is classified as the CORE recommendation with the highest consensus strength across ALL patient comorbidity profiles?","choices":{"A":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injection as monotherapy without a concurrent structured exercise program","B":"Non-selective oral NSAIDs as the primary first-line treatment across all patients regardless of GI or CV history","C":"COX-2 selective inhibitors combined with intra-articular hyaluronic acid as universal first-line pharmacotherapy","D":"Duloxetine as the first-line agent in patients with bilateral knee OA given its central sensitization mechanism","E":"Structured land-based exercise programs combined with arthritis patient education as the foundation of OA management"},"correct":"E","explanation":"OARSI guidelines designate structured land-based exercise (strengthening, cardio, balance/neuromuscular training, OR mind-body exercise such as Tai Chi or yoga) combined with arthritis education as the CORE recommendation with the highest consensus and strength of evidence. This applies regardless of comorbidities. Pharmacologic options are layered based on comorbidity profile (GI, CV, frailty, widespread pain/depression).","boardPrep":"Step 1: OARSI core = exercise + education. Topical NSAIDs preferred for GI comorbidity. IACS for symptomatic flares. IAHA conditionally recommended. COMLEX: Exercise is the most universally recommended intervention — OMT can complement structured exercise programs for OA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — knee OA: clinical diagnosis; OARSI core = structured exercise + arthritis education; weight-bearing X-ray is gold standard imaging"},{"id":"sdl4_q24","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which imaging modality is considered the GOLD STANDARD for the initial diagnosis and staging of knee osteoarthritis, and which radiographic features are most diagnostically useful?","choices":{"A":"Weight-bearing X-ray, showing joint space narrowing, osteophyte formation, subchondral sclerosis, and subchondral cysts under physiological load","B":"MRI of the knee, showing synovial thickening and early cartilage signal changes on T2-weighted sequences","C":"Ultrasound of the knee, showing hypoechoic effusion and osteophyte shadowing with dynamic joint assessment","D":"CT arthrogram, showing articular cartilage thinning and full-thickness chondral defects with bony detail","E":"DEXA scan, showing periarticular bone density reduction and cortical thinning in the affected compartments"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Weight-bearing X-ray is the gold standard for knee OA diagnosis and staging. Key findings: joint space narrowing (most important — reflects cartilage loss), osteophytes (bony growths at joint margins), subchondral sclerosis (increased bone density beneath cartilage), and subchondral cysts. Weight-bearing is essential — non-weight-bearing X-rays can miss joint space narrowing. MRI is reserved for complex cases or when soft tissue evaluation is needed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA X-ray triad = joint space narrowing + osteophytes + subchondral sclerosis/cysts. Weight-bearing is critical. MRI is NOT first-line for OA. Merchant view assesses patellofemoral compartment alignment. COMLEX: X-ray finding of bone-on-bone OA indicates TKA may be appropriate when conservative measures fail.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — knee OA: clinical diagnosis; OARSI core = structured exercise + arthritis education; weight-bearing X-ray is gold standard imaging"},{"id":"sdl4_q25","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 67-year-old male presents with chronic knee pain. The physician begins the knee arthritis diagnostic workflow by obtaining a thorough history including pain characteristics, prior knee injuries, and family history. What is the CORRECT next step in the knee arthritis diagnostic workflow?","choices":{"A":"Immediately ordering MRI to characterize cartilage and synovial pathology before completing physical examination","B":"Performing a directed physical examination including range of motion assessment, palpation for bony prominences, effusion, tenderness, and crepitus","C":"Obtaining synovial fluid analysis to differentiate inflammatory from non-inflammatory arthritis before imaging","D":"Prescribing a therapeutic NSAID trial to confirm an inflammatory pain pattern before further workup","E":"Ordering nuclear medicine bone scan to detect early periarticular metabolic activity before X-ray"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The knee arthritis diagnostic workflow proceeds: history → physical examination → imaging. After history, the next step is physical examination including ROM, palpation for bony prominences and effusion, assessment of tenderness, and crepitus on motion. Only after completing history and exam does imaging follow, beginning with weight-bearing X-ray. MRI is not the first imaging study ordered in the arthritis workflow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Knee arthritis workflow = history → PE → weight-bearing X-ray → MRI (only if complex). Physical exam hallmarks of OA: crepitus, bony enlargement, restricted ROM, effusion. COMLEX: Osteopathic structural exam can identify somatic dysfunction contributing to painful gait adaptation in OA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 9 — knee arthritis workflow: history → exam → weight-bearing X-ray; MRI only for complex cases; OA is a clinical diagnosis not requiring biopsy"},{"id":"sdl4_q26","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old female presents with medial knee pain worse with activity, morning stiffness of 20 minutes, and bony enlargement of the medial joint line. She has no fever or systemic features. Which imaging finding on the knee arthritis diagnostic workup MOST confirms the diagnosis of knee osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"MRI showing T2 signal changes in the meniscal body and synovial thickening without cartilage loss on imaging","B":"Ultrasound showing a hypoechoic effusion and a posterior popliteal cyst without bony changes on assessment","C":"Weight-bearing X-ray showing medial compartment joint space narrowing with osteophytes and subchondral sclerosis","D":"Nuclear medicine bone scan showing diffusely increased periarticular uptake bilaterally in the knee joints","E":"CT arthrogram showing full-thickness chondral defects throughout all three knee compartments uniformly"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In the knee arthritis diagnostic workflow, weight-bearing X-ray is the gold standard initial imaging study. The classic OA findings — medial compartment joint space narrowing, osteophytes, and subchondral sclerosis — directly confirm the diagnosis in the correct clinical context. MRI, ultrasound, bone scan, and CT arthrogram are not first-line in this workflow; MRI is reserved for complex presentations needing soft tissue characterization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Weight-bearing AP view reveals medial > lateral compartment narrowing in typical varus knee OA. Lateral compartment OA is less common and associated with prior trauma or valgus alignment. COMLEX: Clinical diagnosis of OA does not require imaging confirmation when the full triad (age ≥45, usage pain, stiffness ≤30 min) is present.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 9 — knee arthritis workflow: history → exam → weight-bearing X-ray; MRI only for complex cases; OA is a clinical diagnosis not requiring biopsy"},{"id":"sdl4_q27","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"According to the knee arthritis diagnostic workflow, in which clinical scenarios is MRI specifically indicated beyond the standard weight-bearing X-ray evaluation?","choices":{"A":"When the clinical case is complex, when soft tissue injuries need to be excluded, or when cartilage integrity evaluation will change clinical management","B":"As the routine initial imaging study for all patients over 60 presenting with knee pain and morning stiffness","C":"When the patient has classic OA risk factors and X-ray already demonstrates joint space narrowing bilaterally","D":"For annual monitoring of disease progression in all patients with established knee OA on stable treatment","E":"Only in patients under 40 presenting with unexplained monoarticular arthritis without X-ray changes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the knee arthritis diagnostic workflow, MRI is indicated when: (1) the case is complex and X-ray is insufficient, (2) soft tissue injuries (meniscal, ligamentous) need to be ruled out, or (3) cartilage integrity assessment will change the clinical management plan. MRI is NOT the routine first imaging study — weight-bearing X-ray serves that role. It is also not indicated for routine monitoring of stable OA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MRI is powerful but not cost-effective for routine OA monitoring. Reserve for complexity, pre-surgical planning, or when diagnosis is uncertain. Ultrasound can assess effusion and guide injection but is not the gold standard for OA diagnosis. COMLEX: Imaging should guide — not replace — the clinical examination and history in the OA workflow.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 9 — knee arthritis workflow: history → exam → weight-bearing X-ray; MRI only for complex cases; OA is a clinical diagnosis not requiring biopsy"},{"id":"sdl4_q28","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old male with BMI of 34 presents with 4 months of right knee pain that is diffuse, worsening with prolonged standing, and associated with morning stiffness lasting 15 minutes. He denies trauma, fever, or radicular leg pain. Neurovascular exam is normal. X-ray shows medial compartment joint space narrowing. Applying the knee pain diagnostic workflow, the physician determines that trauma, dangerous conditions, and nerve pathology have been excluded. Which of the following BEST represents the appropriate next diagnostic conclusion?","choices":{"A":"Peroneal nerve palsy should be evaluated given the occupational exposure to prolonged standing positions","B":"ACL tear should be considered given the diffuse knee pain and positive biomechanical stress on the medial compartment","C":"Osteoarthritis is the most likely cause; the physician should proceed with OA-directed clinical assessment and OARSI-guided management","D":"Femoral nerve entrapment should be ruled out given the anterior thigh and medial knee pain distribution pattern","E":"Popliteal artery entrapment should be considered given the BMI-related vascular risk and posterior knee symptoms"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After excluding trauma (step 1), dangerous conditions (step 2), and nerve pathology (step 4), step 3 of the workflow — OA or arthritis — becomes the focus. The clinical presentation (age, BMI, diffuse pain worse with activity, stiffness ≤30 min) combined with X-ray showing medial compartment narrowing is diagnostic of knee OA. Proceeding with OARSI-guided management (exercise, education, pharmacotherapy) is appropriate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The knee pain workflow is a structured clinical heuristic — use it to prevent premature closure. OA is the most common cause of chronic knee pain in patients over 45. COMLEX: Somatic dysfunction often accompanies OA and may be an adjunctive treatment target.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — ACL/PCL/collateral injuries present after trauma; the workflow step 2 dangerous-condition screen must precede advancing to OA assessment in atraumatic chronic knee pain"},{"id":"sdl4_q29","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 27-year-old female marathon runner presents with left anterior knee pain that worsens with downhill running and stairs but resolves with rest. She has no trauma history, no joint line tenderness, no effusion, and no neurological deficits. The physician excludes dangerous conditions and nerve pathology. On single-leg squat, dynamic valgus collapse is observed. Applying the knee pain diagnostic workflow, which anatomic pain generator is identified in step 5?","choices":{"A":"The patellofemoral joint, where biomechanical overload from dynamic valgus increases patellofemoral contact pressure during downhill impact","B":"The medial collateral ligament, which sustains chronic valgus stress from pronation during forefoot strike","C":"The posterior cruciate ligament, which bears eccentric load during downhill deceleration maneuvers","D":"The medial tibial plateau, which accumulates stress fracture from high-mileage cumulative impact loading","E":"The popliteal fossa bursa, which becomes inflamed from repetitive posterior compression during running"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Step 5 of the knee pain workflow identifies the specific pain generator. Dynamic valgus, anterior pain worse with patellofemoral loading (stairs, downhill), and positive patellofemoral compression test localize the pain to the patellofemoral joint. Downhill running increases patellofemoral contact pressure due to greater knee flexion angle during impact, amplifying pain in PFS. This finding guides targeted PT (hip and quad strengthening to correct valgus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Downhill running = greatest patellofemoral contact pressure (greater knee flexion angle). Single-leg squat reveals dynamic valgus as the biomechanical driver. COMLEX: Patellofemoral syndrome is a named CP8 musculoskeletal condition amenable to structured rehabilitation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — patellofemoral syndrome identified as pain generator at step 5; downhill running increases patellofemoral contact pressure proportionally with knee flexion angle"},{"id":"sdl4_q30","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old male with poorly controlled type 2 diabetes presents with right knee pain, erythema, and fever for 3 days. He has marked warmth and swelling of the entire joint. CRP is 85 mg/L and WBC is 16,000/mL. He denies trauma. Using the knee pain diagnostic workflow, which step is MOST critical to prioritize immediately in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Step 3 — evaluating for osteoarthritis as the cause given his age and the presence of joint swelling","B":"Step 4 — evaluating for nerve pathology since diabetic peripheral neuropathy can mimic septic arthritis","C":"Step 5 — identifying the localized pain generator to determine whether OA or bursitis is responsible","D":"Step 1 — returning to the trauma assessment since a missed fall in a diabetic patient could explain findings","E":"Step 2 — determining whether a dangerous medical condition such as septic arthritis is responsible for the presentation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The fever, erythema, elevated inflammatory markers, and monoarticular swelling in an immunocompromised (diabetic) patient demand immediate step 2 evaluation for a dangerous medical condition — specifically septic arthritis, which requires urgent arthrocentesis, culture, and antibiotics. Proceeding to OA or localized pain generator steps without ruling out septic arthritis would be a critical clinical error in this patient.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Septic arthritis is a medical emergency — joint destruction occurs within days. Synovial fluid WBC >50,000/mL with >90% PMNs is highly suggestive. Never inject a joint with suspected infection. COMLEX: Diabetes is a major risk factor for septic arthritis and septic bursitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 9 — septic arthritis is a dangerous condition excluded in step 2 of the workflow before attributing knee pain to OA or structural causes"},{"id":"sdl4_q31","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ACL/PCL/MCL/LCL injuries and the unhappy triad.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 16-year-old male soccer player is tackled with a direct lateral valgus blow to his planted left knee. He hears a pop and immediately develops hemarthrosis. On exam, Lachman test is positive, valgus stress at 30 degrees shows laxity with a soft endpoint, and McMurray test reproduces medial joint line pain with external rotation and valgus force. Which combination of injuries MOST likely explains the examination findings?","choices":{"A":"PCL, LCL, and lateral meniscus — because posterior sag and varus laxity are the expected findings here","B":"ACL, MCL, and medial meniscus — the unhappy triad produced by lateral valgus force on a planted foot with hemarthrosis confirming capsular disruption","C":"ACL, PCL, and popliteal ligament — because bicruciate injuries produce complete rotational instability","D":"Lateral capsule, LCL, and popliteal tendon — because posterolateral corner injuries follow valgus forces","E":"Patellar tendon, ACL, and tibial plateau — because inability to bear weight implies extensor disruption"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The mechanism (lateral valgus blow to the planted knee) + Lachman (ACL) + valgus stress laxity at 30° (MCL) + McMurray external rotation/valgus pain (medial meniscus) = classic unhappy triad. Hemarthrosis within hours of injury implies significant capsular or ligamentous disruption, consistent with ACL and MCL involvement. First Aid confirms that lateral meniscus involvement is actually more common in practice, but the classic teaching triad remains ACL+MCL+medial meniscus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Unhappy triad exam pattern = Lachman + valgus stress test + McMurray. Hemarthrosis after knee injury requires MRI workup. Acute ACL tears = hemorrhagic aspirate in >70%. COMLEX: ACL/MCL/meniscus triad is a named CP8 knee condition.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — medial meniscus is attached to MCL; valgus force producing ACL+MCL tear simultaneously stresses the medial meniscal attachment creating the unhappy triad"},{"id":"sdl4_q32","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ACL/PCL/MCL/LCL injuries and the unhappy triad.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 30-year-old male rugby player falls onto his flexed knee with ankle plantarflexed during a tackle, developing posterolateral knee pain. Posterior sag sign is positive. Posterior drawer test shows 8mm of posterior glide at 90 degrees. MRI confirms a mid-substance PCL tear without bony avulsion and no associated ligamentous injury. Which of the following represents the MOST appropriate initial management?","choices":{"A":"Immediate surgical PCL repair regardless of grade or associated injury status given the acute hemarthrosis","B":"Arthroscopic PCL reconstruction within 48 hours to prevent posterior instability and tibial sag progression","C":"Lateral collateral ligament reconstruction since LCL co-injury invariably accompanies PCL disruption patterns","D":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injection to reduce acute hemarthrosis before initiating surgical planning","E":"Non-operative management with bracing and physical therapy, given an isolated mid-substance PCL tear without multiligamentous involvement or bony avulsion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Isolated mid-substance PCL tears (without bony avulsion, multiligamentous injury, or severe instability >Grade 3) are managed non-operatively with bracing and PT. The literature supports good outcomes with conservative care for isolated PCL tears. Surgical indications include: bony avulsion fragment amenable to fixation, multiligamentous instability (combined with ACL/posterolateral corner), or Grade 3 tears with functional limitations not responding to PT.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PCL isolated tears = non-operative (brace + PT). Surgical indications = bony avulsion, multiligamentous injury, or Grade 3 with functional limitation. PCL grade: Grade 1 (<5mm), Grade 2 (5-10mm), Grade 3 (>10mm). COMLEX: PCL injury is less common than ACL (PCL is the strongest knee ligament).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — PCL avulsion from posterior tibia is the bony avulsion pattern that converts a non-operative PCL tear into a surgical candidate; mid-substance tears without avulsion are managed conservatively"},{"id":"sdl4_q33","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ACL/PCL/MCL/LCL injuries and the unhappy triad.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 26-year-old female presents after a varus stress injury to her right knee during recreational martial arts. Exam shows tenderness along the lateral knee, varus thrust gait, and laxity at 30 degrees of knee flexion with a solid endpoint at 0 degrees. X-ray is negative. MRI reveals a Grade 2 LCL tear without posterolateral corner injury or other ligamentous disruption. Which of the following represents the MOST appropriate management for this isolated injury?","choices":{"A":"Conservative management with a hinged knee brace and physical therapy, appropriate for an isolated Grade 2 LCL tear with preserved endpoint and no posterolateral corner involvement","B":"Immediate surgical LCL reconstruction because all LCL injuries above Grade 1 require operative stabilization","C":"Arthroscopic lateral retinacular release to reduce varus alignment forces on the lateral compartment ligaments","D":"Total knee arthroplasty consultation given the severity of lateral compartment laxity demonstrated at 30 degrees","E":"Aspiration of the lateral joint and corticosteroid injection to reduce inflammation and prevent chronic laxity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Isolated LCL Grade 2 tears (5-10mm laxity, solid endpoint, no posterolateral corner involvement) are managed conservatively with bracing and PT. Surgical referral for LCL injury is indicated for isolated but unstable injuries, mid-portion tears, complete tears, persistent injury at 4-6 weeks, or associated posterolateral corner injuries. Laxity only at 30° with solid endpoint at 0° and no PLC co-injury = conservative management appropriate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LCL is the 2nd least common knee ligament injury (8% of knee injuries); 87% involve multiligamentous co-injuries. LCL surgical indications = isolated unstable, complete, mid-portion, persistent, or associated injuries. LCL runs from lateral femoral epicondyle to fibular head. COMLEX: Varus thrust gait is a key clinical sign of LCL/PLC insufficiency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — LCL injury assessment uses varus stress at 0° and 30°; laxity at 0° implies combined ACL or PCL involvement and elevates surgical threshold beyond isolated Grade 2 LCL"},{"id":"sdl4_q34","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of meniscus injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old male with known knee osteoarthritis develops acute medial joint line pain after minimal twisting while rising from a chair. He describes no significant trauma. MRI reveals a horizontal cleavage tear of the posterior horn of the medial meniscus. Which type of meniscal tear does this most likely represent, and what is the key distinguishing feature?","choices":{"A":"A traumatic bucket-handle tear requiring urgent arthroscopic repair given the mechanical symptoms on exam","B":"A lateral meniscal root tear from rotational stress with tibial external rotation during the rising motion","C":"An osteochondritis dissecans fragment causing a loose body that mimics a meniscal tear on MRI signal","D":"A degenerative meniscal tear, characterized by horizontal cleavage pattern in an older patient with pre-existing OA and minimal trauma mechanism","E":"A parameniscal cyst arising from a traumatic vertical meniscal tear in a young athlete with acute pivot injury"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Degenerative meniscal tears occur in older patients (>40) with pre-existing arthritis, typically presenting with minimal or no significant trauma. The horizontal cleavage pattern on MRI is characteristic of degenerative tears. They differ from traumatic tears (vertical, longitudinal, bucket-handle patterns in younger athletes with acute mechanisms). Risk factors include OA, male gender, and age. Treatment is typically conservative initially unless mechanical symptoms develop.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Degenerative tear = horizontal cleavage, older patient, OA background. Traumatic tear = vertical/bucket-handle, younger athlete, acute twist. MRI tear grades: 1 = intrasubstance, 2 = reaching surface, 3 = full-thickness. COMLEX: Degenerative tears in OA may not benefit from surgery beyond conservative care.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — degenerative meniscal tears occur in the context of OA; risk factors include age >40, male gender, prior ACL injury, and presence of arthritis; minimal trauma is the distinguishing historical feature"},{"id":"sdl4_q35","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of meniscus injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 20-year-old female basketball player twists her planted knee, hears a \"pop,\" and develops lateral joint line pain and swelling. McMurray test performed with internal rotation and a varus force applied at 30 degrees of flexion produces a palpable click and lateral pain. Which meniscus is most likely injured, and what confirms this finding?","choices":{"A":"The medial meniscus, confirmed by the positive external rotation with valgus force reproducing medial pain","B":"The lateral meniscus, confirmed by the positive internal rotation with varus force McMurray test reproducing lateral compartment clicking and pain","C":"Both menisci, since internal rotation stresses both compartments equally during the McMurray maneuver","D":"The posterior cruciate ligament, producing pain from posterior capsular impingement during knee flexion","E":"The anterior cruciate ligament, with referred lateral pain from the joint capsule and synovial irritation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"McMurray test mnemonic LIME: Internal rotation + varus force = Lateral meniscus; External rotation + valgus force = Medial meniscus. A click with internal rotation and varus stress implicates the lateral meniscus. Lateral meniscal tears are less common than medial (medial > lateral), but occur with rotational and pivot mechanisms. The lateral meniscus is less firmly attached to the capsule than the medial meniscus, making it more mobile but still susceptible to traumatic tears.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LIME mnemonic — lateral meniscus = internal + varus; medial = external + valgus. Lateral meniscal tears are less common but often associated with ACL tears (lateral meniscus is more commonly co-injured with ACL than medial). COMLEX: Meniscal tears are a named COMLEX CP8 knee condition.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — lateral meniscus involvement is actually more common than medial when ACL tears occur; the classic unhappy triad teaching (medial meniscus) reflects historical teaching rather than current evidence"},{"id":"sdl4_q36","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of meniscus injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 40-year-old male soccer player underwent conservative management with 10 weeks of PRICE therapy and physical therapy for a medial meniscal tear. He now presents with worsening knee pain, persistent true locking that prevents full knee extension, and recurrent giving-out episodes during daily activities. Which finding MOST clearly justifies surgical referral at this stage?","choices":{"A":"Persistent mechanical symptoms including true locking and giving-out that have failed an adequate course of conservative management","B":"Mild discomfort with deep squatting and occasional clicking during pivoting without joint line effusion","C":"Positive Thessaly maneuver in clinic without any subjective locking or instability during daily activities","D":"MRI showing Grade 1 intrasubstance meniscal signal change without disruption of the articular surface","E":"Intermittent mild medial knee aching reproducible with prolonged standing after two weeks of physical therapy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"True locking — inability to fully extend the knee — combined with giving-out after failed conservative treatment are the primary surgical referral indications for meniscal tears. True locking indicates a displaced bucket-handle fragment mechanically blocking joint motion. After 10 weeks of adequate conservative care, persistent mechanical symptoms justify arthroscopic evaluation. Mild clicking, positive provocative tests without functional impairment, or Grade 1 MRI signals alone do not meet this threshold.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Surgical referral criteria = (1) mechanical symptoms (true locking, giving-out), (2) multiligamentous pathology, (3) failure of conservative care. True locking = bucket-handle displacement. COMLEX: Conservative management = PRICE + PT + avoidance of pivoting/deep squatting; surgical referral for refractory cases.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — multiligamentous pathology (co-existing ACL/MCL injury) is an independent surgical referral indication for meniscal tears; isolated meniscal tears without mechanical symptoms are managed conservatively first"},{"id":"sdl4_q37","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 17-year-old male soccer player sustains a valgus-rotation knee injury. AP X-ray shows a small osseous avulsion fragment at the anterolateral aspect of the lateral tibial plateau just below the joint line. The radiologist describes this as a \"Segond fracture.\" Which structure is avulsed, and what associated ligamentous injury does this finding most strongly imply?","choices":{"A":"The posterior cruciate ligament is avulsed from its tibial attachment, implying a hyperflexion mechanism","B":"The medial collateral ligament is avulsed from the medial femoral condyle, implying a valgus loading injury","C":"The patellar tendon is avulsed from the tibial tubercle, implying a quadriceps contraction deceleration injury","D":"The anterolateral ligament is avulsed from its lateral tibial plateau attachment, strongly implying a concurrent ACL tear","E":"The lateral meniscal root is avulsed from its tibial plateau attachment, implying a rotational root tear"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A Segond fracture is an avulsion of the anterolateral ligament (ALL) from the lateral tibial plateau. It is highly pathognomonic for ACL tear — studies show ACL rupture in >90% of Segond fractures. The mechanism involves combined valgus and internal tibial rotation. It appears as a small cortical avulsion fragment lateral to the tibial plateau on AP X-ray. This finding should always prompt MRI to evaluate for ACL and associated soft tissue injuries.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Segond fracture = lateral tibial rim avulsion = ACL tear. X-ray finding: small fragment just below the lateral joint line on AP view. Contrast with arcuate sign (fibular head = posterolateral corner). COMLEX: Recognize Segond fracture on X-ray as a subtle but critical finding that demands MRI follow-up.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — Segond fracture implies ACL disruption; the ALL stabilizes rotational forces that also stress the ACL, explaining the high co-injury rate between Segond fracture and ACL rupture"},{"id":"sdl4_q38","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 75-year-old female with severe osteoporosis falls from standing height and presents with acute knee pain and inability to bear weight. X-ray is equivocal. CT confirms a displaced lateral tibial plateau fracture with articular depression. To complete the fracture assessment and guide surgical planning, which additional imaging study is MOST appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Nuclear medicine bone scan to detect additional occult lower extremity fractures missed on CT scanning","B":"DEXA scan to quantify bone mineral density and urgently guide osteoporosis pharmacotherapy initiation","C":"MRI of the knee to evaluate for concurrent meniscal tears, ligamentous disruption, and chondral injury that will affect the surgical management plan","D":"Fluoroscopic stress views to demonstrate the degree of articular depression under valgus load application","E":"Ultrasound of the popliteal fossa to assess hemarthrosis volume and patellar tendon integrity dynamically"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After CT characterizes the tibial plateau fracture bony architecture, MRI is the most appropriate next study to evaluate concurrent soft tissue injuries — meniscal tears, ligamentous disruption (MCL, ACL, PCL), and chondral damage. These injuries frequently accompany tibial plateau fractures (meniscal tears in 40-70% of cases) and directly influence surgical approach and outcomes. MRI findings change the operative plan in a high proportion of plateau fractures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tibial plateau fractures = high association with meniscal (40-70%) and ligamentous co-injuries. CT = bony detail; MRI = soft tissue detail; both needed for complex fractures. COMLEX: Assess neurovascular status (peroneal nerve, popliteal artery) before any imaging in high-energy fractures.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 9 — MRI is indicated when soft tissue injuries need evaluation beyond what X-ray or CT provides; the knee arthritis and fracture workflows share this MRI decision-point principle"},{"id":"sdl4_q39","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 18-year-old male basketball player sustains a direct blow to his flexed knee with his ankle in plantarflexion during a fall. He cannot ambulate. X-ray shows a small posterior tibial avulsion fracture. Which structure is responsible for this avulsion pattern, and which clinical test corresponds to disruption of this structure?","choices":{"A":"PCL avulsion from its posterior tibial attachment; positive posterior drawer test and posterior sag sign confirm posterior cruciate ligament disruption","B":"ACL avulsion from the anterior tibial spine; positive Lachman test and anterior drawer confirm anterior cruciate disruption","C":"LCL avulsion from the fibular head; positive varus stress test at 30 degrees confirms lateral collateral ligament disruption","D":"Patellar tendon avulsion from the tibial tubercle; inability to actively extend the knee confirms extensor mechanism disruption","E":"Medial meniscal root avulsion from the tibial plateau; positive McMurray with external rotation confirms medial meniscal disruption"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The classic PCL injury mechanism is a fall directly onto a flexed knee with plantarflexed ankle (or MVA dashboard injury). PCL inserts on the posterior tibia; avulsion fractures occur at this insertion. The posterior sag sign (tibia sags posteriorly relative to femur with knee at 90°) and posterior drawer test (posterior tibial glide at 90° flexion) are the diagnostic exam findings. An anterior tibial spine avulsion would implicate the ACL with positive Lachman/anterior drawer.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PCL avulsion fracture = small bony fragment posterior to the tibia on lateral X-ray. Mechanism: flexed knee + plantarflexed ankle impact (fall or dashboard). PCL = medial femoral condyle to posterior tibia (LAMP mnemonic). COMLEX: Bony avulsion PCL injuries are surgically repaired; mid-substance tears are typically managed non-operatively.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — PCL resists posterior tibial translation; the fall-on-flexed-knee with plantarflexed ankle is the prototypical PCL mechanism producing posterior sag and posterior drawer positive findings"},{"id":"sdl4_q40","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of patellofemoral syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 22-year-old female cross-country runner presents with bilateral anterior knee pain worsening with stairs, single-leg squatting, and prolonged sitting. Patellofemoral compression test is positive bilaterally. There is no effusion or joint line tenderness. Video gait analysis shows dynamic valgus collapse during the stance phase of running. Which biomechanical abnormality MOST directly drives patellofemoral maltracking in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Tibial torsion causing excessive internal tibial rotation during heel strike and increasing patellofemoral compressive force","B":"Weakness of hip abductors and external rotators causing dynamic knee valgus that increases lateral patellar tracking forces","C":"Shortened Achilles tendon reducing ankle dorsiflexion and causing forefoot strike mechanics with knee flexion compensation","D":"Isolated quadriceps atrophy causing reduced extensor mechanism tension and inferior patellar tilt during loading","E":"Overpronation of the subtalar joint causing lateral compartment loading and increasing lateral tibial plateau stress"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hip abductor and external rotator weakness is the primary biomechanical driver of dynamic valgus in runners. Weakness of the gluteus medius causes the hip to adduct and internally rotate during single-leg stance, which forces the knee into valgus alignment. This valgus position increases the lateral force on the patella, causing maltracking and elevated patellofemoral contact pressure. PT targeting posterior hip strengthening directly addresses this mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PFS biomechanical chain — hip abductor weakness → dynamic valgus → lateral patellar tracking → elevated patellofemoral contact pressure. PT must include gluteus medius and external rotator strengthening, not just quad work. COMLEX: OMT targeting hip external rotator dysfunction can complement PFS rehabilitation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — hip abductor/external rotator weakness drives dynamic valgus; physical therapy must address hip strengthening as the root cause, not only quadriceps strengthening around the knee"},{"id":"sdl4_q41","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of patellofemoral syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 17-year-old male soccer player is diagnosed with patellofemoral syndrome after 6 weeks of anterior knee pain. His coach suggests he undergo arthroscopic debridement of the patella. Which of the following represents the LEAST appropriate treatment strategy for this patient at this stage?","choices":{"A":"Physical therapy targeting quadriceps strengthening and hamstring flexibility to improve patellar tracking mechanics","B":"Hip strengthening exercises focusing on the gluteus medius and external rotators to reduce dynamic valgus forces","C":"Immediate arthroscopic surgical debridement of patellar articular cartilage without completing a trial of supervised physical therapy","D":"Activity modification to reduce high-impact loading while maintaining fitness with low-impact cross-training","E":"Patellar taping and stabilizing bracing techniques to improve patellofemoral alignment during rehabilitation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Surgery for PFS is not indicated without an adequate prior trial of conservative management (physical therapy, activity modification, bracing). At 6 weeks without PT, the patient has not exhausted first-line treatments. PT targeting quadriceps and hip strengthening with biomechanical correction is the evidence-based first step. Surgical debridement is reserved for cases with documented chondromalacia refractory to extensive conservative care.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PFS treatment algorithm = PT first (quad + hip strengthening + biomechanics) → bracing/taping → activity modification → role of injections uncertain → surgery only for refractory documented chondromalacia. COMLEX: Conservative management must be exhausted before surgical referral in PFS.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 9 — the diagnostic workflow for knee arthritis and knee pain both reinforce conservative-first management before imaging-guided or surgical interventions"},{"id":"sdl4_q42","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of patellofemoral syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 25-year-old female runner has patellofemoral syndrome confirmed by positive patellofemoral compression test. On single-leg squat assessment, moderate dynamic valgus collapse is observed. Which mechanism BEST explains why single-leg squatting reproducibly worsens her anterior knee pain?","choices":{"A":"Patellar tendon elongation from eccentric quadriceps loading concentrates stress at the distal patellar pole","B":"Medial meniscal compression from tibial internal rotation during the deep phase of the single-leg squat","C":"Femoral nerve stretch from hip flexion causing referred anterior thigh and anterior knee discomfort","D":"Lateral retinacular tightness pulling the patella medially during varus knee alignment at the squat midpoint","E":"Dynamic valgus during single-leg squat increases lateral patellar tracking and elevates patellofemoral contact pressure against the lateral trochlear groove"},"correct":"E","explanation":"During single-leg squatting, dynamic valgus collapse — from hip abductor weakness — causes the knee to move medially over the foot, creating a valgus moment that lateralizes the pull on the patella. This increases lateral tracking of the patella against the trochlear groove, amplifying patellofemoral contact pressure. Single-leg squat is the most sensitive clinical movement for detecting dynamic valgus and reproducing PFS symptoms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Single-leg squat = the clinical test of choice for detecting dynamic valgus in PFS. Patellofemoral contact pressure increases with knee flexion angle — greatest at 90° flexion. COMLEX: Clinical video analysis during single-leg squat is a practical screening tool for biomechanical PFS contributors.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — dynamic valgus increases lateral patellar tracking and patellofemoral contact pressure; single-leg squat is the functional test that makes this biomechanical driver clinically visible"},{"id":"sdl4_q43","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of prepatellar bursitis and patellar subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old male presents with painful, fluctuant swelling directly over the patella, fever of 38.5°C, and skin erythema. He is a professional housekeeper. Aspiration reveals 85,000 WBC/mL with 92% PMNs and gram-positive cocci on Gram stain. Which of the following BEST represents the appropriate management of this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Immediate surgical bursectomy without antibiotics as the definitive first-line treatment for purulent bursitis","B":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injection into the prepatellar bursa to reduce the acute inflammatory response","C":"Compression wrap and elevation alone since prepatellar bursitis resolves with conservative PRICE therapy","D":"Aspiration of purulent bursal fluid combined with systemic antibiotics targeting Staphylococcus aureus as the most common causative organism of septic bursitis","E":"Colchicine therapy for presumed gouty bursitis since urate crystals can generate WBC counts above 50,000/mL"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Septic prepatellar bursitis requires aspiration plus systemic antibiotics. Gram-positive cocci in clusters = Staphylococcus aureus (most common causative organism). Corticosteroid injection into a septic bursa is absolutely contraindicated — it risks spreading infection and delaying appropriate treatment. Surgical bursectomy is reserved for refractory cases. Colchicine is for gout (requires urate crystals on polarized microscopy, not organisms). PRICE alone is insufficient.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Septic bursitis vs. gout vs. mechanical — differentiated by aspiration analysis (Gram stain/culture vs. crystal analysis). Gram-positive cocci = S. aureus. Always aspirate suspected septic bursitis before treating. COMLEX: Prepatellar bursitis from chronic kneeling = \"housemaid's knee\" — occupational red flag.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 6 — septic prepatellar bursitis is a dangerous medical condition requiring aspiration and antibiotics; failure to treat promptly risks osteomyelitis and joint invasion in the adjacent patellofemoral joint"},{"id":"sdl4_q44","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of prepatellar bursitis and patellar subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old female volleyball player sustains an acute first-episode lateral patellar dislocation during a non-contact cutting maneuver. After closed reduction in the ED, MRI confirms a complete MPFL tear without osteochondral fracture. She has no prior dislocation history and no predisposing anatomic risk factors on MRI. Which of the following is the MOST appropriate initial management?","choices":{"A":"Immediate MPFL surgical reconstruction given the complete tear severity regardless of episode number or anatomy","B":"Conservative management with PRICE therapy, physical therapy for quadriceps and hip strengthening, and a patellar stabilizing brace for the first episode without predisposing anatomic factors","C":"Open lateral retinacular release as the primary surgical intervention to reduce lateral patellar traction forces","D":"Total knee arthroplasty consultation given the severity of acute MPFL disruption and instability risk","E":"Long-leg casting for 12 weeks with strict non-weight-bearing before initiating any rehabilitation protocol"},"correct":"B","explanation":"First-episode patellar dislocation in the absence of osteochondral fracture or predisposing anatomic factors (trochlear dysplasia, high TT-TG distance, patella alta) is managed conservatively. PRICE + PT (quad and hip strengthening) + patellar stabilizing brace is the evidence-based first-line approach. MPFL reconstruction is reserved for recurrent dislocations (typically after 2+ episodes) or when significant anatomic predisposing factors are present on MRI.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Patellar dislocation management — first episode without predisposing factors = conservative (PRICE + PT + brace). Recurrent or with anatomic factors = consider MPFL reconstruction. COMLEX: Patellar dislocation is a CP8 joint dislocation condition; MRI post-reduction assesses MPFL and chondral status.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 6 — MPFL is disrupted in virtually all patellar dislocations; surgical reconstruction is reserved for recurrent instability or when predisposing anatomic abnormalities are identified on imaging"},{"id":"sdl4_q45","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of prepatellar bursitis and patellar subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"In the evaluation of a patient with suspected recurrent patellar subluxation, which plain radiographic view is MOST appropriate for assessing patellofemoral alignment and lateral patellar tilt?","choices":{"A":"Merchant view (also called sunrise or subpatellar view), which images the patellofemoral joint tangentially to assess patellar tilt, trochlear morphology, and alignment","B":"Weight-bearing AP view, which demonstrates tibiofemoral joint space and is the gold standard for OA staging","C":"Oblique lateral view, which demonstrates the arcuate sign indicating posterolateral corner injury pattern","D":"Standard lateral view, which shows the Pellegrini-Stieda lesion at the medial femoral condyle attachment","E":"Tunnel view (notch view), which is used to evaluate the intercondylar notch for osteochondritis dissecans"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Merchant view (sunrise or subpatellar view) is the dedicated radiographic view for patellofemoral assessment. The X-ray beam is directed inferosuperiorly along the axis of the femur, providing a tangential view of the patellofemoral joint. It demonstrates patellar tilt, trochlear morphology, and patellar subluxation. First Aid also mentions this view for detecting bone-on-bone patellofemoral arthritis. The AP and lateral views do not adequately assess the patellofemoral compartment in this way.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Merchant view = sunrise view = subpatellar view. Used for: patellar subluxation, PFS assessment, patellofemoral OA (bone-on-bone on Merchant = patellofemoral arthritis). COMLEX: X-ray alignment views guide surgical planning for tibial tubercle osteotomy in recurrent patellar instability.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 9 — Merchant view is part of the knee arthritis imaging protocol for assessing the patellofemoral compartment; the knee arthritis workflow includes standard AP, lateral, and Merchant views"},{"id":"sdl4_q46","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, histopathology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of popliteal cyst.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 60-year-old male with long-standing knee osteoarthritis presents with increasing posterior knee swelling and restricted flexion. Ultrasound confirms a 4cm popliteal cyst communicating with the joint. He has undergone two ultrasound-guided aspirations in the past year, each providing 3 months of relief before recurrence. Which of the following represents the MOST effective long-term treatment strategy?","choices":{"A":"Surgical excision of the popliteal cyst wall through a posterior incision as the definitive long-term treatment","B":"Aspiration followed by corticosteroid injection into the cyst space as the definitive non-surgical management","C":"Prescribing custom orthotics and a patellar stabilizing brace to reduce knee loading and fluid production","D":"Addressing the underlying knee osteoarthritis with OARSI-guided management to reduce synovial fluid overproduction that feeds the cyst","E":"Immobilizing the knee in full extension for 8 weeks to allow the cyst to collapse and adhere permanently"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Popliteal cysts in adults are driven by intra-articular pathology — excess synovial fluid production from OA, RA, or meniscal tears fills the bursa. Aspiration without treating the underlying cause leads to predictable recurrence (as demonstrated in this patient). Effective long-term management requires treating the underlying OA (exercise, NSAIDs, IACS, IAHA, eventually TKA). Once the source of fluid overproduction is controlled, the cyst regresses.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Baker's cyst = symptom, not disease. Treat the cause, not the cyst. Recurrence after aspiration = signal to escalate OA management. COMLEX: This principle echoes osteopathic philosophy — identify and treat the primary dysfunction driving the compensatory finding.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 8 — knee OA management follows OARSI guidelines; treating OA addresses the root cause of synovial overproduction driving popliteal cyst formation and recurrence after aspiration"},{"id":"sdl4_q47","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, histopathology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of popliteal cyst.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy is incidentally found to have a 2cm popliteal cyst on knee ultrasound during evaluation of mild knee stiffness after a minor fall. He has no constitutional symptoms, no fever, and no prior knee pathology. Ultrasound shows no communication with the synovial space. Which is the MOST appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Immediate surgical excision because pediatric popliteal cysts always carry risk of malignant transformation","B":"Aspiration combined with corticosteroid injection to permanently collapse the cyst in young children","C":"Watchful waiting or aspiration as appropriate, since pediatric popliteal cysts typically do not communicate with the joint and frequently resolve spontaneously","D":"MRI with gadolinium contrast to rule out a vascular malformation or synovial sarcoma in this age group","E":"Total knee arthroscopy to assess the posterior compartment and cauterize the cyst at its point of origin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pediatric popliteal cysts (peak incidence ages 4-7) have different pathoanatomy from adult cysts — they typically do NOT communicate with the synovial space, arise from the gastrocnemius-semimembranosus bursa independently, and frequently resolve spontaneously with watchful waiting or simple aspiration. They are not associated with intra-articular pathology the way adult cysts are, and there is no indication for aggressive surgical or injection treatment in the absence of concerning features.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pediatric (4-7) vs. adult (35-70) Baker's cysts have different pathoanatomy and treatment. Pediatric = non-communicating, benign, often resolve spontaneously. Adult = communicating, treat underlying joint pathology. COMLEX: Watchful waiting is appropriate for asymptomatic pediatric popliteal cysts.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 7 — pediatric popliteal cysts arise from different pathoanatomy than adult cysts; they typically lack synovial communication and resolve spontaneously, in contrast to adult cysts driven by intra-articular disease"},{"id":"sdl4_q48","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, histopathology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of popliteal cyst.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 67-year-old female with rheumatoid arthritis develops acute calf pain and swelling mimicking a deep vein thrombosis. Doppler ultrasound shows no venous thrombus but reveals a ruptured popliteal cyst with free fluid tracking into the posterior calf. Which term BEST describes this complication and its underlying pathophysiology?","choices":{"A":"True DVT, because popliteal cyst compression of the popliteal vein causes secondary venous thrombosis formation","B":"Pseudothrombophlebitis, caused by rupture of the popliteal cyst releasing synovial fluid into the calf musculature, mimicking the presentation of deep venous thrombosis","C":"Compartment syndrome, caused by arterial embolization from synovial shedding producing distal calf ischemia","D":"Septic thrombophlebitis, caused by bacterial seeding from the ruptured cyst resulting in polymicrobial cellulitis","E":"Gastrocnemius muscle avulsion, caused by sudden knee hyperextension during the rupture event itself"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Rupture of a popliteal cyst causes \"pseudothrombophlebitis\" — synovial fluid tracks inferiorly into the calf, producing acute pain, swelling, and erythema that mimics DVT. Doppler ultrasound demonstrates the cyst rupture and absence of venous thrombus, distinguishing it from true DVT. This is a recognized complication of Baker's cysts in patients with RA or OA who have large fluid collections. Management involves treating the underlying joint disease rather than anticoagulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ruptured Baker's cyst = pseudothrombophlebitis. Ultrasound is the diagnostic test of choice — shows free fluid tracking into calf with no venous thrombus. D-dimer may be falsely elevated. COMLEX: RA and OA are the most common underlying conditions driving large popliteal cysts that can rupture.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 7 — popliteal cyst rupture causes pseudothrombophlebitis; ultrasound differentiates this from true DVT and guides the decision against anticoagulation in this setting"},{"id":"sdl4_q49","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 73-year-old female presents with bilateral knee pain rated 6/10, worsening with activity and partially relieved with rest. Morning stiffness lasts 20 minutes. X-ray shows medial compartment joint space narrowing, osteophytes, and subchondral sclerosis bilaterally. She has no cardiovascular or GI comorbidities. According to OARSI guidelines, which treatment represents the CORE first-line foundation of her management?","choices":{"A":"Structured land-based exercise program combined with arthritis patient education as the universal core recommendation across all comorbidity profiles","B":"Immediate TKA consultation given the bilateral radiographic OA severity and functional limitation","C":"Tramadol as first-line opioid analgesic given the severity of pain and the absence of NSAID candidacy","D":"Duloxetine as the primary pharmacologic therapy given the bilateral symmetric pain suggesting central sensitization","E":"Intra-articular hyaluronic acid injection as monotherapy without concurrent exercise programming initiation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"OARSI designates structured land-based exercise (strengthening/cardio/balance OR mind-body exercise such as Tai Chi) combined with arthritis education as the CORE recommendation with the highest consensus and evidence strength — applicable to ALL patients regardless of comorbidity profile. Pharmacotherapy is layered on top based on specific comorbidities (GI, CV, frailty). Surgery is reserved for failure of comprehensive conservative management.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OARSI hierarchy — CORE = exercise + education (all patients). Pharmacologic add-ons by comorbidity: GI comorbidity → topical NSAIDs; CV comorbidity → topical NSAIDs; no comorbidities → non-selective NSAIDs or COX-2. COMLEX: Exercise is the most strongly supported intervention for OA across all guidelines.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 9 — the knee arthritis diagnostic workflow culminates in treatment guided by OARSI guidelines; core management (exercise + education) precedes pharmacologic and interventional options"},{"id":"sdl4_q50","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old male with knee osteoarthritis and a history of peptic ulcer disease controlled on PPI therapy requires pharmacologic treatment for moderate pain. He also has stable hypertension. According to OARSI guidelines, which pharmacologic option is MOST appropriate as the preferred initial choice for this patient?","choices":{"A":"Non-selective systemic NSAIDs as monotherapy given the anti-inflammatory benefit and cost-effectiveness","B":"COX-2 selective inhibitors alone without gastroprotection since reduced GI ulcer risk versus non-selectivesNSAIDs","C":"Oral opioid analgesics as first-line therapy given the contraindication to both selective and non-selective NSAIDs","D":"Topical NSAIDs, which are the preferred initial pharmacologic agent for patients with gastrointestinal comorbidities such as peptic ulcer disease","E":"Aspirin monotherapy given its combined anti-inflammatory and cardiovascular protective dual mechanism of action"},"correct":"D","explanation":"OARSI guidelines specifically recommend topical NSAIDs as the preferred pharmacologic option for patients with GI comorbidities (peptic ulcer disease). Topical NSAIDs provide local anti-inflammatory effect with minimal systemic absorption, significantly reducing GI risk compared to oral NSAIDs. Systemic NSAIDs (selective or non-selective) carry GI bleeding risk even with PPI use. Opioids are not first-line for OA per OARSI. Aspirin is not recommended as primary OA pharmacotherapy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OARSI GI comorbidity → topical NSAIDs first. CV comorbidity → topical NSAIDs first (avoid systemic non-selective). Topical NSAIDs: diclofenac gel/patch has strong evidence for knee OA. COMLEX: Avoid systemic NSAIDs in patients with peptic ulcer disease even with PPI coverage when topical options are available.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 8 — OARSI pharmacologic stratification by comorbidity: GI comorbidity = topical NSAIDs preferred; this reflects the risk-benefit calculation of minimizing systemic drug exposure in high-risk patients"},{"id":"sdl4_q51","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old female with knee OA meeting clinical diagnostic criteria asks her physician about the role of MRI in her care. Her weight-bearing X-ray shows mild medial compartment narrowing and osteophytes consistent with OA. Her pain is stable and no new symptoms have developed. According to the knee OA diagnostic workflow, MRI is MOST appropriately indicated in which of the following circumstances?","choices":{"A":"As a routine annual imaging study to monitor OA progression and adjust conservative treatment accordingly","B":"For all patients with confirmed OA diagnosis to assess baseline cartilage thickness before starting treatment","C":"When the clinical case is complex, when soft tissue pathology needs to be excluded, or when cartilage integrity assessment will directly change the management plan","D":"When the patient has bilateral symptoms and X-ray shows osteophytes in more than one compartment","E":"Only for patients under 50 years with unexplained knee pain and negative weight-bearing X-ray results"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In the knee OA workflow, MRI is NOT a routine study. It is reserved for: (1) complex clinical presentations where X-ray is insufficient, (2) suspected soft tissue co-pathology (meniscal tears, ligamentous injury) that would change management, or (3) situations where cartilage mapping will affect the treatment decision (e.g., pre-surgical planning). For a stable OA patient with consistent symptoms and expected X-ray findings, MRI adds cost without changing management.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MRI in OA — reserved for complexity, soft tissue evaluation, or surgical planning. Not for routine OA monitoring. Weight-bearing X-ray = gold standard for OA diagnosis and progression monitoring. COMLEX: Cost-effective imaging decision-making is tested on COMLEX clinical reasoning questions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 9 — the knee arthritis diagnostic workflow positions MRI as a conditional study based on complexity; OA is a clinical and radiographic diagnosis that does not require routine MRI confirmation"},{"id":"sdl4_q52","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 74-year-old female presents with progressive bilateral knee pain, bony enlargement, and crepitus over 3 years. Applying the knee arthritis diagnostic workflow, which of the following correctly describes the SEQUENCE of steps?","choices":{"A":"Weight-bearing X-ray first, then physical examination, then history, then laboratory studies if imaging is abnormal","B":"Laboratory studies first (RF, ANA, ESR) to differentiate OA from inflammatory arthritis before imaging","C":"MRI first to characterize cartilage and synovial pathology, then X-ray, then history and physical examination","D":"History first (pain onset, prior injuries, family history), then physical examination (ROM, palpation, effusion), then weight-bearing X-ray; MRI is reserved for complex cases","E":"Physical examination first, then history, then MRI as the initial imaging study to rule out soft tissue injury"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The knee arthritis diagnostic workflow follows: (1) History — pain characteristics, prior knee trauma/fracture/surgery, family history; (2) Physical exam — ROM, bony prominences, tenderness, crepitus, effusion, swelling; (3) Imaging — weight-bearing X-ray as the gold standard; MRI only when complex, soft tissue injury must be excluded, or cartilage integrity assessment changes management. This sequence mirrors sound clinical reasoning — history and exam guide appropriate imaging selection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Knee arthritis workflow = H&P before imaging. Weight-bearing X-ray is the gold standard initial imaging. OARSI criteria do NOT require imaging for diagnosis when clinical triad is present. COMLEX: Structural exam and ROM assessment are foundational osteopathic clinical skills applied to this workflow.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — the knee pain diagnostic workflow and the knee arthritis workflow both prioritize history and physical examination before imaging, emphasizing clinical reasoning over test-driven diagnosis"},{"id":"sdl4_q53","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 67-year-old female presents with 2 years of progressive right medial knee pain worsening with activity and 15 minutes of morning stiffness. Physical exam shows bony enlargement, crepitus, and a small effusion at the medial joint line. Weight-bearing AP X-ray of the knee is ordered. Which X-ray finding would MOST directly confirm the diagnosis of knee osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Medial compartment joint space narrowing with marginal osteophytes and subchondral sclerosis on the weight-bearing AP view","B":"Uniform joint space narrowing across all three compartments with periarticular osteopenia bilaterally","C":"Erosive changes at the joint margins with cortical destruction and soft tissue swelling pattern","D":"Calcification of the meniscal fibrocartilage (chondrocalcinosis) with periarticular calcium deposits","E":"Complete joint space obliteration with bony ankylosis and loss of joint architecture throughout the knee"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The classic OA triad on weight-bearing X-ray: (1) joint space narrowing in the affected compartment (medial in varus knee OA), (2) marginal osteophytes at the joint margins, and (3) subchondral sclerosis. Subchondral cysts may also be seen. Uniform joint space narrowing with periarticular osteopenia is characteristic of RA, not OA. Erosive changes with cortical destruction suggest psoriatic or erosive OA. Chondrocalcinosis (CPPD) causes calcium deposits, not sclerosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA X-ray triad = joint space narrowing + osteophytes + subchondral sclerosis. Medial compartment narrowing with varus deformity = typical OA pattern. RA = symmetric narrowing + periarticular osteopenia + erosions. COMLEX: X-ray interpretation is a testable COMLEX clinical skill — know OA vs. RA patterns.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 8 — weight-bearing X-ray demonstrating the OA triad (joint space narrowing, osteophytes, subchondral sclerosis) is the gold standard confirming OA diagnosis in the arthritis workflow"},{"id":"sdl4_q54","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physician completing the knee arthritis diagnostic workflow reaches the imaging step. The patient is a 65-year-old male with classic clinical OA features and pain that is moderate in severity. According to the knee arthritis workflow, which imaging modality is the GOLD STANDARD for initial evaluation of knee arthritis?","choices":{"A":"MRI, because it provides the most sensitive evaluation of articular cartilage and synovial tissue integrity","B":"Ultrasound, because it provides real-time dynamic assessment of effusion and allows immediate guided aspiration","C":"Weight-bearing X-ray, which demonstrates joint space narrowing under physiological load and is the recommended gold standard for OA diagnosis and staging","D":"CT arthrogram, because it provides superior cartilage mapping and bony architectural detail for OA staging","E":"Radionuclide bone scan, because it identifies early periarticular metabolic changes preceding X-ray abnormalities"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Weight-bearing X-ray is the gold standard for initial knee OA imaging. It is essential that imaging be performed weight-bearing — non-weight-bearing X-rays can miss joint space narrowing by allowing the femur and tibia to separate. Standard knee OA X-ray series includes AP weight-bearing (for tibiofemoral joint space), lateral, and Merchant view (for patellofemoral compartment). MRI is not the gold standard for OA; it is reserved for complex presentations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Weight-bearing X-ray = OA gold standard. Always specify \"weight-bearing\" when ordering — non-weight-bearing AP is inadequate for OA assessment. Merchant view adds patellofemoral compartment evaluation. COMLEX: Appropriate imaging selection is a core clinical reasoning skill tested in COMLEX Level 1 and Level 2.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 8 — weight-bearing X-ray is the gold standard for knee OA diagnosis and staging; it is the definitive imaging step in the knee arthritis diagnostic workflow before MRI or other advanced imaging is considered"}]},{"sdlNumber":5,"title":"Hip Pain: Diagnostic Workflow & Pathologies","questions":[{"id":"sdl5_q01","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for hip pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physician evaluating a patient with hip pain applies the 5-step diagnostic workflow. After confirming no trauma in step 1, what does step 2 direct the physician to assess?","choices":{"A":"Whether osteoarthritis or another inflammatory arthritis is the most likely cause of the pain","B":"Whether nerve pathology such as radiculopathy or the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve is responsible","C":"Whether the pain is localized and, if so, identifying the specific anatomic hip pain generator","D":"Whether a dangerous medical condition such as infection, malignancy, or fracture is the underlying cause of the hip pain","E":"Whether the pain is related to overuse activities such as running or repetitive hip flexion exercises"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The hip pain diagnostic workflow mirrors the knee pain framework: (1) Is pain trauma-related? (2) Is a dangerous medical condition the cause? (3) Is OA or arthritis responsible? (4) Is nerve pathology such as radiculopathy responsible? (5) Is pain localized — what is the pain generator? Step 2 always screens for dangerous conditions — septic hip, fracture, psoas abscess, or malignancy — before advancing to the arthritis or nerve steps.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip pain diagnostic workflow = same 5-step structure as knee pain. Critical point: hip X-rays are almost always indicated to augment H&P for hip pain. COMLEX: In children presenting with knee pain, always include hip X-ray — hip pathology (LCP, SCFE, septic hip) commonly refers to the knee.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — hip pain workflow: same 5-step framework as knee; X-ray almost always used to augment H&P; include hip X-ray in children presenting with knee pain"},{"id":"sdl5_q02","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for hip pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 10-year-old male presents with medial knee pain and a slight limp but no knee trauma. The physician completes the knee pain diagnostic workflow and finds no knee pathology. Which additional imaging step is MOST critical before concluding the evaluation?","choices":{"A":"Ordering knee MRI to rule out occult growth plate injury in the medial femoral condyle region","B":"Ordering a bone scan to rule out malignancy at the distal femoral epiphysis and proximal tibia","C":"Obtaining hip X-rays, since hip pathology frequently refers pain to the thigh and knee in children and hip X-ray is indicated for any child presenting with knee pain","D":"Performing nerve conduction studies to evaluate for saphenous nerve entrapment at the medial knee","E":"Ordering bilateral knee X-rays with comparison views to assess for physeal growth plate abnormality"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The hip pain workflow explicitly states that hip X-rays should almost always be used to augment history and physical, including in children presenting with knee pain. Hip pathology (Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease, SCFE, septic hip) frequently manifests as ipsilateral thigh or knee pain rather than hip pain in children, making this a critical clinical pearl. Missing hip pathology by focusing only on the knee is a recognized diagnostic error.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic boards trap — child with knee pain without knee pathology = always X-ray the hip. SCFE and LCP both present with knee or thigh pain. SCFE: obese adolescent, \"ice cream scoop\" X-ray appearance. LCP: age 4-9, insidious limp. COMLEX: Hip disease in children is a CP8 named musculoskeletal presentation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — hip pain workflow: same 5-step framework as knee; X-ray almost always used to augment H&P; include hip X-ray in children presenting with knee pain"},{"id":"sdl5_q03","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for hip pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"In the hip pain diagnostic workflow, which of the following BEST distinguishes the approach to hip pain from that of knee pain?","choices":{"A":"Hip X-rays are almost always used to augment the history and physical, even when the presentation does not clearly localize to the hip joint itself","B":"The hip pain workflow uses only 3 steps, while the knee pain workflow requires a 5-step sequential process","C":"Nerve pathology is assessed before dangerous conditions in the hip workflow, unlike the knee workflow order","D":"The hip workflow begins with imaging before the history because X-rays are always required for hip pain","E":"Arthritis is evaluated in the final step of the hip workflow, whereas it is assessed in step 3 for knee pain"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The hip pain workflow uses the same 5-step sequential framework as the knee pain workflow. The key distinguishing feature is that hip X-rays are almost always used to augment the history and physical in hip evaluations — a stronger imaging recommendation than for knee pain. This is because hip pathology (fracture, OA, AVN, SCFE, septic joint) frequently requires radiographic confirmation even when the history and exam are incomplete or atypical.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Both knee and hip workflows use the same 5-step structure. Hip-specific addition: X-ray is almost always ordered as a routine augment — not just when Ottawa-like criteria are met. COMLEX: Osteopathic structural exam complements the hip diagnostic workflow by identifying somatic dysfunction patterns consistent with specific hip pathologies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — hip pain workflow: same 5-step framework as knee; X-ray almost always used to augment H&P; include hip X-ray in children presenting with knee pain"},{"id":"sdl5_q04","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 65-year-old male presents with a deep aching pain around the hip socket, worsening with both active and passive motion, and progressive restriction of internal rotation followed by external rotation. He has no radicular pain. Which of the following risk factors is MOST associated with hip osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Male gender over age 40, NSAID use, and sedentary lifestyle are the primary risk factors for hip OA","B":"Autoimmune disease, positive rheumatoid factor, and prolonged corticosteroid use are the key risk factors","C":"Prior hip trauma, high-impact activity history, obesity, and family history of arthritis are all associated risk factors for hip osteoarthritis","D":"Tobacco use, hypertension, and hyperlipidemia are the most strongly associated risk factors for hip OA","E":"History of gout, hyperuricemia, and metabolic syndrome are the dominant risk factors for hip OA development"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hip OA risk factors are multifactorial and include: prior trauma or fracture or dislocation, history of high-impact activities, obesity, family history, and metabolic conditions. The clinical presentation features a deep aching pain around the hip socket, stiffness, and restricted range of motion — internal rotation is lost first, followed by external rotation in end-stage disease. Both active and passive motion reproduce pain, distinguishing hip OA from extra-articular causes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip OA = deep ache around socket; IR lost first (then ER). Active and passive motion both painful = intra-articular source. Hip OA can refer to thigh or knee without hip pain. COMLEX: Hip OA is a named COMLEX CP8 musculoskeletal condition; OMT can address somatic dysfunction of the hip as an adjunct.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — hip OA: deep ache around socket, restricted IR then ER, usually unilateral; active and passive motion painful; can refer to thigh or knee without groin pain"},{"id":"sdl5_q05","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old female with hip osteoarthritis presents with predominantly thigh and knee pain with no groin complaint. Knee examination is normal. Which of the following BEST explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Hip pathology frequently refers pain to the thigh and knee in the absence of groin pain, requiring hip evaluation even when the knee exam is normal","B":"The knee pain is from a separate degenerative meniscal tear that has developed simultaneously with the hip OA","C":"The thigh pain represents L2-L3 radiculopathy, which should be investigated with lumbar MRI before hip imaging","D":"Obturator nerve entrapment is the most likely cause of medial thigh pain in patients with hip osteoarthritis","E":"The patient has patellofemoral syndrome bilaterally causing knee pain secondary to the altered hip mechanics"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hip OA can present with thigh and/or knee pain without actual hip or groin pain — a clinically important pitfall. The hip joint refers pain through the obturator nerve and capsular innervation to the medial thigh and knee, which can mislead clinicians into focusing on the knee rather than the hip. This is why the hip diagnostic workflow recommends X-raying the hip in children with knee pain, and why a normal knee exam should prompt hip evaluation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip OA referred pain pattern = medial thigh and knee. Clinical pearl: knee pain with normal knee exam = X-ray the hip. COMLEX: Hip OA is a common cause of knee pain misdiagnosis; osteopathic structural exam should include hip mobility assessment in all patients with knee complaints.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — hip OA: deep ache around socket, restricted IR then ER, usually unilateral; active and passive motion painful; can refer to thigh or knee without groin pain"},{"id":"sdl5_q06","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old male with hip OA is found to have progressive loss of range of motion. Which pattern of motion restriction is MOST characteristic of advanced hip osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"External rotation is the first motion restricted, followed by internal rotation loss as a late finding in OA","B":"Internal rotation is lost first with disease progression, followed by external rotation restriction as the disease advances to end stage","C":"Flexion is the first and predominant motion loss, while rotation is preserved until the very late stages","D":"Abduction is preferentially restricted early and is always the first clinical sign of hip OA progression","E":"Extension loss occurs earliest and is the most reliable indicator of early hip OA on clinical examination"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In hip OA, internal rotation (IR) is characteristically the first motion restricted as the joint capsule and surrounding tissues contract. As disease progresses, external rotation (ER) also becomes restricted, particularly in end-stage OA. Both active and passive hip motion provoke pain in intra-articular pathology — this distinguishes hip OA from extra-articular causes such as greater trochanteric tendinopathy, where passive motion is usually preserved.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip OA motion loss pattern: IR first → then ER (end stage). Active AND passive motion painful = intra-articular. Passive motion preserved = extra-articular. COMLEX: Restricted hip IR on passive exam is a somatic dysfunction finding amenable to OMT (muscle energy, articulatory technique).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — hip OA: deep ache around socket, restricted IR then ER, usually unilateral; active and passive motion painful; can refer to thigh or knee without groin pain"},{"id":"sdl5_q07","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip overuse injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 32-year-old female marathon runner presents with lateral hip pain over the greater trochanter, worsening with single-leg squat and associated with dynamic knee valgus. She was referred with a diagnosis of \"greater trochanteric bursitis.\" What does current evidence suggest is the ACTUAL underlying pathology in most cases?","choices":{"A":"Gluteus medius tendinopathy is the primary pathology in most cases of so-called greater trochanteric bursitis","B":"Iliotibial band friction syndrome is the primary cause of lateral hip pain in runners, not tendinopathy","C":"Hip labral tear is the underlying cause of lateral hip pain in runners who present with dynamic valgus","D":"Snapping hip syndrome from iliopsoas impingement is the correct diagnosis in most cases of lateral hip pain","E":"True bursitis of the trochanteric bursa from direct pressure is the diagnosis in the majority of runners"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Current evidence demonstrates that \"greater trochanteric bursitis\" is a misnomer — the primary pathology is gluteus medius tendinopathy in the vast majority of cases. The presentation is lateral greater trochanter pain worsening with single-leg squat, running, and activities requiring repetitive hip flexion/extension. Dynamic knee valgus (from gluteus medius weakness) is both a biomechanical driver and a clinical finding. Treatment targets the tendinopathy and underlying biomechanics, not bursitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"Greater trochanteric bursitis\" = gluteus medius tendinopathy. Lateral GT pain + single-leg squat pain + dynamic valgus = tendinopathy pattern. COMLEX: Gluteus medius weakness causes Trendelenburg gait and is a key somatic dysfunction pattern in the hip-pelvis region.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 3 — greater trochanteric \"bursitis\" is usually gluteus medius tendinopathy; lateral GT pain, single-leg squat pain, dynamic valgus; biomechanics correction is first-line"},{"id":"sdl5_q08","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip overuse injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 40-year-old female runner with confirmed gluteus medius tendinopathy fails conservative treatment with rest and physical therapy. Which of the following represents a RECOGNIZED escalation treatment option for refractory gluteus medius tendinopathy?","choices":{"A":"Surgical bursectomy is always the next step after physical therapy fails in any form of hip tendinopathy","B":"Oral opioid analgesics combined with long-leg immobilization is the recommended escalation for tendinopathy","C":"Total hip arthroplasty is the only definitive treatment for refractory gluteus medius tendinopathy at the hip","D":"Corticosteroid injection, tenotomy, regenerative injection (such as PRP), or nitroglycerin patches are recognized treatment escalation options for refractory tendinopathy","E":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injection into the hip joint capsule is the preferred escalation for tendinopathy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"After first-line biomechanical adjustment (PT, gait retraining, strengthening), refractory gluteus medius tendinopathy can be treated with: corticosteroid injection (for acute flare), tenotomy (for recalcitrant cases), regenerative injection (PRP), or nitroglycerin patches (shown to improve tendinopathy healing). These options address the tendinopathic tissue directly. Surgical bursectomy is not indicated when the pathology is tendinopathic rather than true bursitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tendinopathy treatment ladder = biomechanics → CSI → regeneratives/NTG patches → tenotomy. NTG patches (nitroglycerin) have evidence for tendinopathy healing via nitric oxide signaling. COMLEX: OMT addressing hip-pelvis somatic dysfunction can reduce the biomechanical load driving tendinopathy recurrence.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 3 — greater trochanteric \"bursitis\" is usually gluteus medius tendinopathy; lateral GT pain, single-leg squat pain, dynamic valgus; biomechanics correction is first-line"},{"id":"sdl5_q09","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip overuse injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old female dancer describes an audible \"snapping\" sensation at the hip with certain movements. She has no pain with the snapping at rest but mild discomfort during repetitive activity. Which is the MOST common anatomic source of snapping hip syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Avulsion of the gluteus medius from the greater trochanter producing an audible click during hip rotation","B":"Femoral head subluxation from a torn hip labrum producing a reproducible snap during hip flexion maneuvers","C":"Greater trochanteric bursitis producing a palpable fluid wave as the bursa shifts over the trochanteric ridge","D":"Osteochondral loose body impinging within the joint space producing a mechanical snap during hip extension","E":"The iliotibial band snapping over the greater trochanter (external snapping hip) or the iliopsoas tendon snapping over the iliopectineal eminence (internal snapping hip)"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Snapping hip syndrome (coxa saltans) has two main anatomic sources: (1) external snapping — the iliotibial band snapping over the greater trochanter with hip flexion/extension; (2) internal snapping — the iliopsoas tendon snapping over the iliopectineal eminence with hip flexion. A third form involves intra-articular loose bodies, but this is less common. External snapping is the most common and is often painless; treatment focuses on IT band and hip flexor flexibility.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Snapping hip = coxa saltans. External = IT band over greater trochanter. Internal = iliopsoas over iliopectineal eminence. Most snapping is benign and painless. COMLEX: Snapping hip from IT band tightness responds to hip-pelvis OMT addressing somatic dysfunction of the iliotibial band insertion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 3 — greater trochanteric \"bursitis\" is usually gluteus medius tendinopathy; lateral GT pain, single-leg squat pain, dynamic valgus; biomechanics correction is first-line"},{"id":"sdl5_q10","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip fracture and dislocation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 78-year-old female falls from standing height and presents with right groin pain, inability to bear weight, and the leg appearing shortened and internally rotated. Which hip fracture pattern is MOST consistent with this presentation, and what blood supply concern is associated with it?","choices":{"A":"Femoral neck fracture, which carries the highest risk of avascular necrosis due to poor blood supply to the femoral head from disruption of the medial circumflex femoral artery","B":"Intertrochanteric fracture, which carries the highest AVN risk due to complete disruption of the femoral neck vasculature","C":"Subtrochanteric fracture, which is most commonly seen in elderly women and carries a low AVN risk overall","D":"Acetabular fracture, which disrupts the joint capsule but rarely affects blood supply to the femoral head","E":"Femoral shaft fracture, which presents with internal rotation and shortening but carries minimal AVN risk"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Femoral neck fractures carry the highest risk of AVN because the blood supply to the femoral head — primarily via the medial circumflex femoral artery — traverses the femoral neck and is disrupted by the fracture. Intertrochanteric fractures have better blood flow (below the neck) but greater displacement risk. The classic presentation of hip fracture is groin pain, shortened leg, and external rotation (though the leg can be internally rotated depending on fracture type and position).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Femoral neck = poor blood supply → high AVN risk. Medial circumflex femoral artery = primary blood supply to femoral head. 1/3 of hip fractures progress to THA. FA: hip fractures can lead to AVN and nonunion (femoral neck). COMLEX: Hip fracture in elderly female = high COMLEX yield with osteoporosis management component.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — femoral neck: poor blood flow, high AVN risk; intertrochanteric: better flow; presentation = groin pain, shortened and internally rotated leg; most require surgery"},{"id":"sdl5_q11","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip fracture and dislocation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 25-year-old male motorcyclist presents after a high-speed MVC with the right hip flexed and internally rotated, with the femoral head palpable posteriorly. Sciatic nerve function should be immediately assessed. Which type of hip dislocation is MOST common, and which nerve is MOST at risk?","choices":{"A":"Anterior hip dislocation is most common; the femoral nerve is most at risk causing quadriceps weakness and knee extension deficit","B":"Posterior hip dislocation is most common; the obturator nerve is most at risk causing medial thigh adductor weakness","C":"Central fracture-dislocation is most common; the pudendal nerve is most at risk from acetabular fragment displacement","D":"Posterior hip dislocation is most common; the sciatic nerve is most at risk and can cause foot drop and posterior thigh sensory loss if injured","E":"Superior hip dislocation is most common; the superior gluteal nerve is most at risk causing abductor weakness and Trendelenburg gait"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Posterior hip dislocation is the most common type (>85% of all hip dislocations), typically caused by high-energy trauma (MVA dashboard mechanism). The femoral head dislocates posteriorly and superiorly, putting the sciatic nerve at direct risk of injury. Sciatic nerve injury causes foot drop (peroneal division) and hamstring weakness, along with posterior thigh and below-knee sensory loss. Urgent reduction is essential to reduce sciatic nerve and femoral head ischemia risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior hip dislocation — leg flexed, adducted, internally rotated. Sciatic nerve at risk (foot drop = peroneal division). FA: inferior gluteal nerve injury = posterior hip dislocation = difficulty rising from seated/climbing stairs. COMLEX: Posterior dislocation = CP8 named joint dislocation; requires urgent reduction within 6 hours to minimize AVN risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — femoral neck: poor blood flow, high AVN risk; intertrochanteric: better flow; presentation = groin pain, shortened and internally rotated leg; most require surgery"},{"id":"sdl5_q12","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip fracture and dislocation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Regarding hip fracture epidemiology, which of the following correctly identifies the fracture type MOST common in women aged 65 and older, and the fracture subtype with a BIMODAL age distribution?","choices":{"A":"Femoral shaft fractures are most common in women over 65; femoral neck fractures have a bimodal distribution","B":"Acetabular fractures are most common in elderly women; intertrochanteric fractures have a bimodal distribution","C":"Femoral neck fractures are most common in elderly women; acetabular fractures have a bimodal distribution","D":"Subtrochanteric fractures are most common in elderly women; femoral neck fractures have a bimodal distribution","E":"Intertrochanteric and femoral neck fractures are most common in women over 65; subtrochanteric fractures have a bimodal distribution peaking at ages 20-40 and over 60"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In women aged 65 and older, intertrochanteric and femoral neck fractures are the most common hip fracture types. Femoral neck fractures occur 3x more commonly in women than men. Subtrochanteric fractures have a bimodal age distribution — a younger peak at ages 20-40 (high-energy trauma) and an older peak above age 60 (low-energy osteoporotic fractures). Overall hip fracture incidence is 100-315 per 100,000 population.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip fracture types by age — elderly women: intertrochanteric + femoral neck. Subtrochanteric: bimodal (20-40 trauma, >60 osteoporosis). Femoral neck: 3x more common in women. Risk factors: age, female, osteoporosis, low BMI <22, endocrine disease. COMLEX: Hip fracture prevention via osteoporosis screening and fall prevention is a key preventive medicine objective.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — femoral neck: poor blood flow, high AVN risk; intertrochanteric: better flow; presentation = groin pain, shortened and internally rotated leg; most require surgery"},{"id":"sdl5_q13","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of avascular necrosis of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old male with SLE who has been on long-term corticosteroids presents with left groin pain at rest and with weight bearing. X-ray is normal. MRI shows characteristic signal changes in the femoral head. Which of the following BEST describes the ARCO stage classification for this patient?","choices":{"A":"ARCO Stage II — subchondral fracture with crescent sign visible on plain X-ray indicating early collapse","B":"ARCO Stage III — femoral head depression greater than 2mm on X-ray representing established collapse","C":"ARCO Stage IV — joint space narrowing and degenerative acetabular changes consistent with end-stage OA","D":"ARCO Stage I — normal radiograph with abnormal MRI findings, representing the pre-collapse stage with ischemic changes visible only on MRI","E":"ARCO Stage II — sclerosis and osteolysis visible on X-ray without crescent sign or femoral head collapse"},"correct":"D","explanation":"ARCO staging for AVN: Stage I = normal X-ray with abnormal MRI (bone marrow edema, early ischemic signal); Stage II = no crescent sign, X-ray shows sclerosis/osteolysis/osteoporosis; Stage III = subchondral fracture with crescent sign and femoral head flattening (IIIA ≤2mm, IIIB >2mm depression); Stage IV = OA changes with joint space narrowing and acetabular degeneration. This patient has normal X-ray + abnormal MRI = Stage I.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MRI sensitivity for AVN = 100% (Stage I detectable on MRI when X-ray is normal). ARCO Stage I = pre-collapse = opportunity for joint-preserving treatment. FA: AVN mnemonic CASTS Bend LEGS. Most common site = femoral head (watershed from medial circumflex femoral artery). COMLEX: AVN is a CP8 named hip condition.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — AVN mnemonic CASTS Bend LEGS (Corticosteroids, Alcohol, Sickle cell, Trauma, SLE, Bends, LCP, SCFE); MRI sensitivity 100%; ARCO staging I-IV"},{"id":"sdl5_q14","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of avascular necrosis of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following correctly lists the risk factors associated with avascular necrosis of the femoral head using the First Aid mnemonic?","choices":{"A":"Calcium deficiency, Anemia, Smoking, Tuberculosis, Statin use, Bisphosphonate use, Lupus, Exercise excess","B":"Corticosteroids, Alcohol, Sickle cell disease, Trauma, SLE, the Bends (caisson disease), Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease, and SCFE — remembered as CASTS Bend LEGS","C":"Crohn disease, ACE inhibitors, Sepsis, Thrombus, Spondylosis, Bariatric surgery, Long-term NSAID use","D":"Corticosteroids, Aspirin, Septic arthritis, Thyroid disease, Systemic sclerosis, Bisphosphonate use, Lactose intolerance, Edema","E":"Calcium oxalate, Alcohol, Smoking, Thrombocytopenia, Septicemia, Beta-blocker use, Liver cirrhosis, Embolism"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The First Aid mnemonic CASTS Bend LEGS captures the key AVN associations: glucoCorticoids (most common drug-related cause), chronic Alcohol overuse, Sickle cell disease, Trauma, SLE, \"the Bends\" (caisson/decompression disease), Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease (idiopathic childhood AVN), and SCFE (slipped capital femoral epiphysis). Corticosteroid use and alcohol are the two most common reversible risk factors in adults.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CASTS Bend LEGS = canonical AVN mnemonic (First Aid). Corticosteroids + alcohol = most common drug-related causes. Sickle cell = AVN in multiple sites. Medial circumflex femoral artery = blood supply at risk. COMLEX: AVN occurs in all joints but femoral head is most clinically tested for boards.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — AVN mnemonic CASTS Bend LEGS (Corticosteroids, Alcohol, Sickle cell, Trauma, SLE, Bends, LCP, SCFE); MRI sensitivity 100%; ARCO staging I-IV"},{"id":"sdl5_q15","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of avascular necrosis of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 42-year-old male with chronic alcohol use disorder presents with bilateral groin pain that is worse at night and at rest. X-ray shows flattening of the right femoral head and subchondral fracture with a crescent sign. Left femoral head shows sclerosis without collapse. Which imaging modality has 100% sensitivity for detecting early AVN before X-ray changes appear?","choices":{"A":"CT scan of the pelvis, which is superior to MRI for detecting early bone marrow ischemia in AVN staging","B":"Bone scan with technetium, which is the gold standard imaging for early AVN detection before collapse occurs","C":"Ultrasound of the hip joint, which shows early joint effusion and subchondral signal change in pre-collapse AVN","D":"Plain X-ray with comparison views, which is reliable for detecting Stage I and Stage II AVN with high sensitivity","E":"MRI, which has near 100% sensitivity for detecting femoral head AVN even at Stage I when plain X-rays are completely normal"},"correct":"E","explanation":"MRI has near 100% sensitivity for detecting AVN even in Stage I, when X-rays are completely normal. The characteristic finding is a \"double line sign\" on T2-weighted images — a low-signal outer band and high-signal inner band at the necrotic zone interface. CT is better than X-ray but inferior to MRI. Bone scan has moderate sensitivity (60-80%) for early AVN. Plain X-ray only becomes positive at Stage II or later. Early detection is critical because joint-preserving interventions are most effective before collapse.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MRI = gold standard for early AVN (Stage I — normal X-ray). X-ray: crescent sign = subchondral fracture = Stage III. ARCO Stage IV = OA + joint space narrowing. FA: AVN of femoral head = watershed area (medial circumflex femoral artery). COMLEX: Early detection enables core decompression before Stage III collapse.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — AVN mnemonic CASTS Bend LEGS (Corticosteroids, Alcohol, Sickle cell, Trauma, SLE, Bends, LCP, SCFE); MRI sensitivity 100%; ARCO staging I-IV"},{"id":"sdl5_q16","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of a psoas abscess and septic hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old male with Crohn disease presents with right hip pain, fever, and pain that worsens with passive hip extension. Lab shows WBC 15,000 and ESR 88. CT reveals a focal hypodense lesion within the iliopsoas muscle plane. Which organism is MOST commonly implicated in psoas abscess?","choices":{"A":"Staphylococcus aureus is the most commonly isolated organism in psoas abscess, particularly in primary (hematogenous) cases","B":"Escherichia coli is the most common organism since psoas abscess is predominantly a GI-origin infection","C":"Candida albicans is the most common organism in immunocompromised patients with psoas abscess overall","D":"Streptococcus pyogenes is the most common organism in psoas abscess from Crohn disease-related spread","E":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis is the most common organism causing psoas abscess in all geographic settings"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Staphylococcus aureus is the most commonly isolated organism in psoas abscess, especially in primary (hematogenous) cases. Secondary psoas abscess from Crohn disease may also involve S. aureus or mixed flora (including gram-negative organisms from the GI tract). Tuberculosis causes psoas abscess in endemic regions (Pott disease spreading from vertebral osteomyelitis), but is not the global leading cause. Key diagnostic features: psoas sign — pain with passive hip extension — is a classic finding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Psoas abscess = infection in iliopsoas compartment; S. aureus most common. FA: also associated with vertebral osteomyelitis (Pott disease), Crohn disease, pyelonephritis. Psoas sign = hip extension exacerbates lower abdominal/hip pain. CT = 90% sensitive (lower if <6 days of symptoms). TX: drainage + IV antibiotics. COMLEX: Psoas abscess is a CP8 dangerous hip condition that mimics hip OA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — psoas abscess: Staph aureus most common, psoas sign; septic hip: modified Kocher 4 criteria = 99.6% risk; distinguish from transient synovitis (afebrile, normal labs)"},{"id":"sdl5_q17","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of a psoas abscess and septic hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy presents with fever of 38.9°C, refusal to bear weight on the left leg, ESR of 52 mm/h, and WBC of 13,500. CRP is 2.8 mg/dL. Using the modified Kocher criteria, what is the estimated probability of septic hip arthritis?","choices":{"A":"A score of 4 criteria gives approximately 40% probability, which requires watchful waiting with repeat labs","B":"A score of 4 criteria gives approximately 3% probability, which is managed with outpatient antibiotics alone","C":"A score of 4 criteria gives approximately 93% probability, requiring urgent surgical lavage consultation","D":"A score of 4 out of 4 criteria met (non-weight bearing, fever, elevated ESR, elevated WBC, elevated CRP) corresponds to approximately 99.6% probability of septic arthritis","E":"A score of 3 criteria gives approximately 93% probability since CRP is not included in the Kocher criteria"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Modified Kocher criteria include: (1) non-weight bearing, (2) ESR >40 mm/h, (3) temperature >101.3°F (38.5°C), (4) WBC >12,000, and (5) CRP >2.0 mg/dL. Risk stratification: 0 criteria = <0.2%, 1 = 3%, 2 = 40%, 3 = 93%, 4 = 99.6%. This patient meets all criteria (non-weight bearing, fever 38.9°C, ESR 52, WBC 13,500, CRP 2.8) — 4-5 criteria = 99.6% probability. Urgent surgical aspiration/lavage and IV antibiotics are required.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Modified Kocher criteria = 5 criteria, 4+ = 99.6% septic arthritis risk. Treatment = surgical aspiration/lavage + IV antibiotics. Key pitfall: distinguish from transient synovitis (afebrile, normal ESR/WBC, post-viral). COMLEX: Septic hip is a medical emergency in children — delay in treatment leads to femoral head destruction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — psoas abscess: Staph aureus most common, psoas sign; septic hip: modified Kocher 4 criteria = 99.6% risk; distinguish from transient synovitis (afebrile, normal labs)"},{"id":"sdl5_q18","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of a psoas abscess and septic hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 4-year-old girl presents with a 3-day history of left hip pain and reluctance to walk. Temperature is 37.1°C (afebrile), ESR is 22 mm/h, WBC is 8,200. She had a URI 2 weeks ago. Hip ultrasound shows a small effusion. Modified Kocher score is 0. Which diagnosis is MOST likely and what is the appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Septic arthritis, which must always be excluded with surgical aspiration regardless of the Kocher score result","B":"Transient synovitis, which is managed conservatively with NSAIDs and activity restriction since Kocher score 0 gives <0.2% probability of septic arthritis","C":"Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease, which presents acutely with fever and joint effusion in children after a viral illness","D":"Psoas abscess, which should be excluded with CT imaging given the hip pain and recent URI history in a child","E":"SCFE, which presents acutely with effusion and restricted hip motion in young children after viral illness onset"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Transient synovitis is the most common cause of acute hip pain in children aged 3-10, often following a viral illness 1-4 weeks earlier. It is distinguished from septic arthritis by the absence of fever, normal or mildly elevated inflammatory markers, and a low Kocher score. Kocher score 0 = <0.2% probability of septic arthritis. Management is conservative: NSAIDs + activity restriction + close follow-up. Symptoms typically resolve within 1-2 weeks.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Transient synovitis vs. septic hip — key differentiator = Kocher criteria. Transient synovitis: afebrile, normal WBC/ESR, post-viral. Septic hip: fever, elevated inflammatory markers, ill-appearing child. Both can cause limp and hip effusion. COMLEX: Transient synovitis is the most common cause of acute hip pain in school-age children.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — psoas abscess: Staph aureus most common, psoas sign; septic hip: modified Kocher 4 criteria = 99.6% risk; distinguish from transient synovitis (afebrile, normal labs)"},{"id":"sdl5_q19","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of meralgia paresthetica.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which nerve is compressed in meralgia paresthetica, and what is the characteristic distribution of symptoms?","choices":{"A":"The femoral nerve is compressed, producing anterior thigh pain with weakness of knee extension and absent patellar reflex","B":"The obturator nerve is compressed, producing medial thigh pain and weakness of hip adduction bilaterally","C":"The sciatic nerve is compressed, producing posterior thigh pain with foot drop and diminished Achilles reflex","D":"The lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (LFCN) is compressed, producing pain, tingling, and burning paresthesias over the anterolateral thigh","E":"The ilioinguinal nerve is compressed, producing medial inguinal and scrotal pain without any thigh paresthesias"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Meralgia paresthetica is caused by compression of the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (LFCN), a purely sensory nerve that exits the pelvis near the anterior superior iliac spine and passes beneath or through the inguinal ligament. It produces tingling, numbness, and burning pain in the anterolateral thigh — no motor deficit (it is purely sensory). The LFCN does not supply the medial thigh, groin, or below the knee, distinguishing it from femoral, obturator, and sciatic nerve pathology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meralgia paresthetica = LFCN compression = anterolateral thigh paresthesias only, NO motor deficit. FA: causes = tight clothing, obesity, pregnancy; tingling/numbness/burning in anterolateral thigh. Median age = 50. COMLEX: LFCN runs beneath/through inguinal ligament near ASIS; palpation at this point may reproduce symptoms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 7 — meralgia paresthetica: LFCN compression at inguinal ligament; anterolateral thigh paresthesias/pain; risk factors = obesity, diabetes, pregnancy, tight belts"},{"id":"sdl5_q20","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of meralgia paresthetica.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old male with BMI of 38 and poorly controlled type 2 diabetes presents with burning and tingling over the anterior and lateral right thigh for 6 weeks. Neurological exam shows no motor weakness. Sensation is diminished over the anterolateral thigh only. Deep tendon reflexes are intact bilaterally. Which risk factor MOST significantly increases his risk for meralgia paresthetica?","choices":{"A":"Male gender is the primary risk factor since meralgia paresthetica predominantly affects males over age 45","B":"Diabetes mellitus, which increases the risk of meralgia paresthetica approximately 7-fold compared to non-diabetics","C":"Hypertension is the most significant risk factor through microvascular disease affecting nerve conduction velocity","D":"Hyperlipidemia is the primary risk factor through nerve sheath lipid deposition causing external compression","E":"Chronic NSAID use is the most significant risk factor through peripheral nerve demyelination over time"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Diabetes mellitus increases the risk of meralgia paresthetica approximately 7-fold compared to non-diabetics — making it the most significant listed risk factor in this patient. The mechanism involves peripheral nerve susceptibility to compression from metabolic neuropathy. Other risk factors include obesity, older age (median age 50), pregnancy (increased abdominal pressure), ascites, tight belts/waistbands, prolonged pressure, and long-distance activities.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meralgia paresthetica risk factors = obesity, older age (median 50), diabetes (7x risk), pregnancy, ascites, tight clothing. Clinical diagnosis — no EMG required unless diagnosis is uncertain. COMLEX: Anterior ASIS-area palpation may reproduce LFCN symptoms; OMT can address iliac/inguinal somatic dysfunction contributing to nerve compression.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 7 — meralgia paresthetica: LFCN compression at inguinal ligament; anterolateral thigh paresthesias/pain; risk factors = obesity, diabetes, pregnancy, tight belts"},{"id":"sdl5_q21","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of meralgia paresthetica.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old pregnant woman in her third trimester develops burning pain and numbness over the anterolateral right thigh. Exam confirms LFCN distribution sensory loss without motor deficit. Which is the MOST appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":{"A":"Treating the underlying cause — in this case, reassuring the patient that symptoms typically resolve postpartum as increased abdominal pressure subsides","B":"Prescribing anticonvulsants such as gabapentin or pregabalin as the primary first-line pharmacologic treatment","C":"Referring to anesthesia for nerve block injection at the anterior superior iliac spine immediately","D":"Ordering EMG and nerve conduction studies to confirm the diagnosis before initiating any treatment","E":"Placing the patient in a corset brace to decompress the inguinal ligament and reduce LFCN compression"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Meralgia paresthetica treatment begins by addressing the underlying cause. In pregnancy, increased abdominal pressure compresses the LFCN at the inguinal ligament. Symptoms typically resolve spontaneously after delivery as pressure decreases — expectant management and reassurance are appropriate first-line treatment. For recalcitrant cases (e.g., non-pregnant patients not responding to conservative measures), anticonvulsants (gabapentin, pregabalin) or anesthesia consultation for nerve block are escalation options.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meralgia paresthetica treatment = treat the cause first (weight loss, pregnancy delivery, remove tight clothing). Recalcitrant = anticonvulsants or anesthesia consult. Clinical diagnosis — treatment does not require imaging or EMG. COMLEX: Pregnancy-associated meralgia paresthetica resolves postpartum; OMT is safe in pregnancy and may address contributing pelvic somatic dysfunction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 7 — meralgia paresthetica: LFCN compression at inguinal ligament; anterolateral thigh paresthesias/pain; risk factors = obesity, diabetes, pregnancy, tight belts"},{"id":"sdl5_q22","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for hip pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 72-year-old female with type 2 diabetes presents with 3 days of right hip pain, fever of 38.7°C, and inability to bear weight. She appears ill. Applying the hip pain diagnostic workflow, which step should be prioritized FIRST and what immediate action follows?","choices":{"A":"Step 3 — evaluating for hip osteoarthritis, since elderly diabetic patients with hip pain most likely have OA","B":"Step 5 — identifying the localized pain generator to differentiate greater trochanteric tendinopathy from joint disease","C":"Step 4 — evaluating for nerve pathology since diabetic peripheral neuropathy can mimic septic joint in elderly patients","D":"Step 2 — ruling out a dangerous medical condition; immediate workup includes CBC, ESR, CRP, and hip X-ray with ultrasound-guided aspiration if effusion is confirmed","E":"Step 1 — returning to the trauma screen since a fall that was not reported is the most common cause of hip pain in elderly diabetics"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Fever, inability to bear weight, and elevated inflammatory markers in an immunocompromised (diabetic) patient mandate immediate step 2 evaluation for a dangerous condition — specifically septic hip arthritis. The modified Kocher criteria should be applied, and hip aspiration performed urgently if the score is elevated. The hip diagnostic workflow explicitly requires dangerous conditions to be excluded before proceeding to OA or nerve pathology steps.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip pain + fever + non-weight-bearing + elevated labs = septic hip until proven otherwise. Modified Kocher criteria apply to children and adults. COMLEX: Diabetes is a major immunocompromising risk factor for septic arthritis; septic hip requires surgical lavage + IV antibiotics.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 6 — septic hip must be ruled out in step 2 of the hip pain workflow; modified Kocher criteria guide the urgency of aspiration and surgical consultation in febrile patients with hip pain"},{"id":"sdl5_q23","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for hip pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 9-year-old male presents with his mother reporting medial knee pain and a limp for 2 weeks. He denies trauma and has a normal knee examination. Which step in the hip pain diagnostic workflow is MOST critical to apply for this patient, and what imaging must be obtained?","choices":{"A":"The hip pain workflow must be applied; hip X-rays are essential since hip pathology including LCP and septic hip commonly refer pain to the thigh and knee in children","B":"The knee pain workflow should be completed first with MRI of the knee before considering any hip evaluation","C":"Lumbar spine MRI should be ordered first since L2-L3 radiculopathy is the most common cause of knee pain in children","D":"No additional imaging is needed since a normal knee exam in a child confirms there is no significant pathology","E":"Bilateral knee X-rays should be obtained to compare growth plates before expanding the evaluation to the hip"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A child with knee pain and a normal knee examination demands hip evaluation — this is a high-yield clinical pearl explicitly stated in the SDL 5 hip pain workflow. Hip pathology (LCP, SCFE, septic hip, hip OA) frequently refers pain to the medial thigh and knee in children via obturator nerve distribution. Hip X-rays are almost always indicated. Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease (ages 4-9, more common in males) and SCFE (obese adolescents) must both be excluded.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Child with knee pain + normal knee exam = ALWAYS X-ray the hip. LCP = age 4-9, male predominance 4:1, insidious limp. SCFE = obese adolescent, ice cream scoop appearance on frog-leg lateral X-ray. Both are CP8 named COMLEX pediatric hip conditions. COMLEX: Hip disease in children is a classic board trap with knee as the presenting complaint.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — hip fractures and pediatric hip pathologies (LCP, SCFE) frequently refer pain to the knee; the hip workflow's emphasis on hip X-ray in children with knee pain is driven by this referral pattern"},{"id":"sdl5_q24","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for hip pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old male presents with right hip pain for 4 months. History reveals no trauma. He denies fever. Neurological exam is normal. Hip X-ray shows mild joint space narrowing and osteophyte formation. Applying the hip pain diagnostic workflow, which step has the physician reached in determining the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Step 5 — the specific pain generator has been confirmed as the hip labrum based on the X-ray findings","B":"Step 3 — osteoarthritis or arthritis has been identified as the most likely cause after trauma and dangerous conditions were excluded in steps 1 and 2","C":"Step 4 — nerve pathology must now be ruled out before the OA diagnosis can be confirmed on X-ray","D":"Step 2 — a dangerous condition such as septic arthritis must be ruled out before X-ray findings are interpreted","E":"Step 1 — the history of possible missed trauma must be re-explored before attributing the findings to OA"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After excluding trauma (step 1) and dangerous conditions (step 2), and with a normal neurological exam excluding nerve pathology (step 4), finding classic X-ray features of OA — joint space narrowing and osteophytes — places this physician at step 3 of the hip pain workflow: determining that OA is the most likely cause. Importantly, hip X-ray is almost always used to augment the H&P in the hip workflow, as it was here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip workflow integrates X-ray as a routine diagnostic augment — not just ordered after all H&P steps are complete. OA X-ray findings: joint space narrowing, osteophytes, subchondral sclerosis. COMLEX: The hip pain workflow is identical in structure to the knee pain workflow; both follow the 5-step framework.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — hip OA presenting with joint space narrowing and osteophytes represents step 3 of the workflow; X-ray is almost always used to augment the hip pain diagnostic evaluation at this stage"},{"id":"sdl5_q25","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 72-year-old female presents with progressive right hip pain that she describes as a \"deep ache\" around the hip socket. She also reports right medial knee aching over the past year without trauma. Hip examination reveals markedly restricted internal rotation and moderate restriction of external rotation. Knee exam is entirely normal. What BEST explains the knee pain in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Concurrent medial compartment knee OA, which commonly develops simultaneously with ipsilateral hip OA in older women","B":"Degenerative medial meniscal tear, which is a predictable secondary complication of altered gait from hip OA","C":"Saphenous nerve entrapment at the medial knee from altered biomechanics secondary to hip abductor dysfunction","D":"Hip OA referring pain to the medial thigh and knee through obturator nerve distribution, without intrinsic knee pathology","E":"Patellar subluxation from hip external rotator tightness causing lateral patellar tracking during ambulation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hip OA can cause thigh and knee pain without any hip or groin pain — a recognized clinical pitfall. The hip joint's capsular innervation includes the obturator nerve (L2-L4), which also supplies the medial knee, explaining the referral pattern. The findings of restricted IR (first lost) and restricted ER (more advanced) confirm intra-articular hip pathology. A completely normal knee exam in the context of progressive hip OA strongly supports referred pain rather than intrinsic knee disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip OA referred pain = thigh and medial knee via obturator nerve. Always evaluate the hip when knee exam is normal in older patients with knee pain. COMLEX: In osteopathic practice, restricted hip IR on structural exam is a key finding that guides hip OMT and differentiates hip from knee pathology.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — the hip pain workflow specifically flags knee pain in children and thigh pain as potential hip referral patterns; this applies to adults with hip OA referring through obturator nerve distribution"},{"id":"sdl5_q26","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 65-year-old male with obesity, prior right hip labral tear, and family history of arthritis presents with progressive right hip OA. On examination, both active and passive hip motion reproduce groin pain. Which combination of risk factors MOST likely contributed to his hip OA development?","choices":{"A":"Sedentary lifestyle alone is sufficient to explain hip OA development in any male patient over age 60","B":"Obesity is the only risk factor that matters since BMI independently predicts hip OA with a linear relationship","C":"Prior labral tear (joint injury/trauma), obesity, and family history are all multifactorial risk factors for hip OA consistent with the described presentation","D":"Prior labral tear is irrelevant since labral pathology does not progress to hip OA in males over 60 years","E":"Family history is the sole determining factor since hip OA is exclusively a genetic condition without modifiable contributors"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hip OA risk factors are multifactorial: prior trauma/fracture/dislocation (the labral tear represents prior joint injury), obesity, family history, history of high-impact activities, and metabolic conditions all contribute. The combination of joint trauma + obesity + genetic predisposition exemplifies the multifactorial nature of hip OA. Active AND passive motion reproducing pain confirms intra-articular pathology, distinguishing this from extra-articular hip pain generators.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip OA = multifactorial — trauma + obesity + genetics + high-impact activities + metabolic disease. Active AND passive motion painful = intra-articular. Passive only = extra-articular. COMLEX: Hip OA is a CP8 named condition; OMT for hip OA targets restricted IR/ER and hip-pelvis somatic dysfunction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — hip overuse injuries (bursitis/tendinopathy) produce pain primarily with active motion; hip OA produces pain with both active and passive motion, distinguishing intra-articular from extra-articular pathology"},{"id":"sdl5_q27","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old female is newly diagnosed with hip OA based on clinical criteria and X-ray findings. She asks what initial motion restriction to expect. Which pattern of range-of-motion loss MOST accurately describes the progression of hip OA?","choices":{"A":"Abduction is invariably the first motion restricted in hip OA, with internal and external rotation preserved until late stages","B":"Internal rotation is restricted first, reflecting early capsular and periarticular contracture; external rotation restriction follows in advanced disease","C":"Flexion is the predominant early loss in hip OA, and extension loss occurs as the final stage before end-stage disease","D":"All planes of motion are equally and simultaneously restricted from the onset of hip OA without a predictable sequence","E":"External rotation is the earliest and most sensitive clinical sign of hip OA, preceding internal rotation loss by years"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The characteristic motion loss sequence in hip OA: internal rotation (IR) is lost first as early capsular contracture develops; as the disease progresses, external rotation (ER) is additionally restricted, particularly in end-stage disease. This pattern is clinically useful — restricted passive IR on examination is an early and sensitive sign of hip OA, and its quantification helps track disease progression. Both active and passive motion are painful in intra-articular OA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip OA motion loss = IR first, then ER. Restricted passive IR = early intra-articular disease. COMLEX: Passive restricted IR is a somatic dysfunction finding amenable to muscle energy or articulatory OMT; it is also a clinical exam sign used to grade hip OA severity. IR loss on passive testing distinguishes hip OA from extra-articular pain.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — internal rotation restriction is the earliest motion loss in hip OA; this is the clinical examination finding that most reliably confirms intra-articular hip pathology when combined with activity-related groin pain"},{"id":"sdl5_q28","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip overuse injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old female long-distance runner presents with lateral hip pain over the greater trochanter for 3 months, worsening with running, single-leg squat, and stair climbing. Passive hip range of motion is fully preserved and pain-free. MRI shows increased signal at the gluteus medius tendon insertion on the greater trochanter. What is the MOST accurate diagnosis and first-line treatment?","choices":{"A":"True greater trochanteric bursitis; first-line treatment is intra-articular hip joint corticosteroid injection for bursitis","B":"Hip labral tear; first-line treatment is arthroscopic labral repair given the lateral hip pain in a runner","C":"Gluteus medius tendinopathy; first-line treatment is biomechanical correction including gait retraining and hip abductor strengthening to address the underlying load","D":"IT band friction syndrome; first-line treatment is foam rolling and stretching of the iliotibial band insertionally","E":"Hip OA; first-line treatment follows OARSI guidelines with structured land-based exercise and education as core care"},"correct":"C","explanation":"MRI showing signal change at the gluteus medius tendon insertion — not the bursa — confirms gluteus medius tendinopathy, the actual pathology underlying most cases of so-called \"greater trochanteric bursitis.\" Preserved passive hip ROM distinguishes this extra-articular tendinopathy from intra-articular hip OA (which restricts passive motion). First-line treatment is biomechanical correction: gait retraining (reduce hip adduction during stance), hip abductor strengthening, and load management.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Greater trochanteric \"bursitis\" = gluteus medius tendinopathy in most cases. MRI confirms tendon pathology. Passive ROM preserved = extra-articular. Treatment: biomechanics first → CSI → NTG patches → tenotomy → regenerative injection. COMLEX: Gluteus medius weakness = Trendelenburg pattern; OMT addresses hip-pelvis somatic dysfunction driving tendinopathy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — lateral hip pain with preserved passive ROM represents step 5 of the hip pain workflow: the localized pain generator is the gluteus medius tendon, not the intra-articular hip joint"},{"id":"sdl5_q29","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip overuse injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old male runner with refractory gluteus medius tendinopathy has failed biomechanical therapy and a single corticosteroid injection. He continues to have lateral hip pain at 6/10 severity limiting running. Which of the following represents an APPROPRIATE escalation treatment option?","choices":{"A":"Immediate total hip arthroplasty since refractory tendinopathy eventually progresses to femoral head destruction","B":"Systemic corticosteroid oral taper for 6 weeks given that local injection alone was insufficient for resolution","C":"Arthroscopic bursectomy and greater trochanter bursa excision as the definitive surgical treatment for tendinopathy","D":"Immobilization in a hip abduction brace for 8 weeks with complete non-weight-bearing to allow tendon recovery","E":"Regenerative injection therapy such as platelet-rich plasma (PRP) or a topical nitroglycerin patch, which have evidence for promoting tendon healing in refractory tendinopathy"},"correct":"E","explanation":"For refractory gluteus medius tendinopathy after failed PT and CSI, recognized escalation options include: regenerative injections (PRP — promotes tendon healing via growth factors), nitroglycerin patches (topical NTG produces nitric oxide, which improves tendinopathic tissue remodeling), tenotomy (for calcific tendinopathy), or ultrasound-guided procedures. Surgical bursectomy is not indicated because the pathology is tendinopathic, not true bursitis. THA is for end-stage intra-articular disease, not tendinopathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tendinopathy escalation ladder: biomechanics → CSI → NTG patches/regeneratives (PRP) → tenotomy. Topical nitroglycerin = evidence-based for tendinopathy. COMLEX: OMT targeting hip-pelvis somatic dysfunction should be considered at each step of tendinopathy management as an adjunct.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — hip overuse injuries including gluteus medius tendinopathy are managed with a stepwise approach; escalation to regenerative therapy is appropriate after biomechanical correction and a single CSI have failed"},{"id":"sdl5_q30","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip overuse injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 29-year-old female ballet dancer describes an audible, painless snap at the left hip that occurs reproducibly when she swings her leg forward during pliés. She has no pain at rest and no mechanical locking. Which structure MOST likely causes this external snapping hip syndrome?","choices":{"A":"The iliotibial band snapping over the greater trochanter as the hip moves from extension to flexion, producing an external audible snap","B":"The iliopsoas tendon flipping over the iliopectineal eminence, producing an internal deep groin snap during hip flexion","C":"A hip labral tear causing intermittent femoral head subluxation with an audible click during hip rotation maneuvers","D":"A loose osteochondral body within the hip joint impinging during range of motion with reproducible snapping","E":"Greater trochanteric bursitis causing fluid displacement over the trochanteric prominence with hip motion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"External snapping hip syndrome occurs when the iliotibial band (or posterior edge of the gluteus maximus aponeurosis) snaps over the greater trochanter with hip flexion and extension. It produces a reproducible lateral snap or click that is audible and often palpable over the greater trochanter. Internal snapping hip involves the iliopsoas tendon over the iliopectineal eminence (anterior/deep groin snap). External snapping is the most common form; it is usually painless and benign, managed conservatively with IT band stretching and hip flexor flexibility.","boardPrep":"Step 1: External snapping hip = IT band over greater trochanter = lateral audible snap. Internal snapping hip = iliopsoas over iliopectineal eminence = anterior deep groin snap. Intra-articular snapping = loose body or labral tear = painful mechanical click. COMLEX: Painless snapping hip without impingement is managed conservatively; OMT can address IT band and hip flexor somatic dysfunction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — snapping hip (coxa saltans) is classified as external (IT band over greater trochanter) or internal (iliopsoas over iliopectineal eminence); the lateral location of snapping distinguishes external from internal types"},{"id":"sdl5_q31","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip fracture and dislocation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 77-year-old female with osteoporosis is brought to the ED after a ground-level fall. She has right groin pain, a shortened right leg, and the right foot appears externally rotated. X-ray confirms a displaced femoral neck fracture. Which complication is MOST specifically associated with femoral neck fractures due to their anatomy, and what surgical option addresses this definitively?","choices":{"A":"Compartment syndrome of the thigh, which is the primary complication of femoral neck fractures in elderly patients","B":"Nonunion of the intertrochanteric region, due to poor blood supply to the greater trochanter in elderly women","C":"Avascular necrosis of the femoral head, due to disruption of the medial circumflex femoral artery; total hip arthroplasty addresses end-stage AVN when the femoral head cannot be preserved","D":"Peroneal nerve palsy, which is the most common neurological complication of femoral neck fractures after surgery","E":"Tibial stress fracture, which commonly occurs contralaterally from overloading during femoral neck fracture recovery"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Femoral neck fractures carry the highest AVN risk because the medial circumflex femoral artery — the primary blood supply to the femoral head — traverses the femoral neck and is frequently disrupted. Approximately 1/3 of hip fractures ultimately progress to THA. Intertrochanteric fractures have better blood supply but greater displacement risk. The classic presentation of hip fracture is groin pain, shortened leg, and externally rotated foot (loss of muscle tension allowing external rotation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Femoral neck fracture = highest AVN risk (disrupts medial circumflex femoral artery). FA: hip fracture complications = AVN and nonunion (femoral neck specific). 1/3 progress to THA. COMLEX: Hip fracture in elderly with osteoporosis = CP8 fracture condition; post-fracture osteoporosis management is key to preventing contralateral fracture.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — AVN is a direct complication of femoral neck fracture from medial circumflex femoral artery disruption; the same pathophysiology underlies both traumatic and non-traumatic AVN of the femoral head"},{"id":"sdl5_q32","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip fracture and dislocation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 23-year-old male motorcycle rider is brought to the ED after a collision. He is found to have his right hip held in flexion, adduction, and internal rotation. Neurovascular assessment shows diminished sensation over the posterior thigh and lateral leg. Which type of hip dislocation is MOST likely, and what is the urgency of reduction?","choices":{"A":"Anterior hip dislocation with femoral nerve injury; reduction can be delayed up to 24 hours safely without AVN risk","B":"Central fracture-dislocation with pudendal nerve injury; emergent CT must be completed before any reduction attempt","C":"Superior dislocation with superior gluteal nerve injury; urgent arthroscopic reduction is the preferred technique","D":"Posterior hip dislocation with peroneal nerve injury only; sciatic involvement is not possible in posterior dislocations","E":"Posterior hip dislocation with sciatic nerve injury; reduction must be performed urgently within 6 hours to minimize avascular necrosis risk and limit further nerve ischemia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Posterior hip dislocation — the most common type — presents with the hip in flexion, adduction, and internal rotation. The sciatic nerve lies posterior to the joint and is at risk of stretch injury, causing posterior thigh sensory loss (semitendinosus, semimembranosus territory) and lateral leg sensory loss (common peroneal component producing risk of foot drop). Reduction must occur within 6 hours to minimize both AVN risk (from femoral head ischemia) and sciatic nerve stretch injury. Emergent closed reduction under sedation is standard.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior hip dislocation = hip flexed, adducted, internally rotated. Sciatic nerve at risk (foot drop from peroneal division). Reduce within 6 hours to prevent AVN. FA: inferior gluteal nerve at risk in posterior dislocation = difficulty rising from chair/climbing stairs. COMLEX: Hip dislocation is a CP8 named joint dislocation requiring urgent reduction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — posterior hip dislocation is a traumatic cause of femoral head AVN; urgency of reduction (within 6 hours) is driven by the same vascular disruption mechanism as femoral neck fracture-related AVN"},{"id":"sdl5_q33","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip fracture and dislocation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 30-year-old male with a high-velocity skiing injury presents with right hip pain and X-ray showing a subtrochanteric femur fracture. Which of the following BEST describes the epidemiology of subtrochanteric fractures?","choices":{"A":"Subtrochanteric fractures have a bimodal distribution peaking at ages 20-40 from high-energy trauma and again over age 60 from osteoporotic low-energy falls","B":"Subtrochanteric fractures are seen exclusively in elderly women over age 75 and are always associated with osteoporosis","C":"Subtrochanteric fractures are the most common hip fracture type in women over 65 and have no bimodal distribution","D":"Subtrochanteric fractures occur predominantly in male athletes aged 15-25 from high-impact sports participation","E":"Subtrochanteric fractures are a pediatric injury seen exclusively in Salter-Harris physeal injury patterns under age 15"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Subtrochanteric fractures have a bimodal age distribution: a younger peak at ages 20-40 caused by high-energy trauma (MVA, falls from height, sports injuries) and an older peak above age 60 caused by osteoporotic low-energy falls. This is in contrast to intertrochanteric and femoral neck fractures, which predominantly occur in elderly women. Intertrochanteric and femoral neck fractures are the most common types in women over 65, with femoral neck occurring 3x more often in women than men.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip fracture epidemiology by type — elderly women: intertrochanteric + femoral neck (most common). Subtrochanteric: bimodal (20-40 trauma, >60 osteoporosis). Femoral neck: 3x more common in women. COMLEX: Hip fracture in elderly = CP8 fracture; prevention requires fall risk assessment and osteoporosis treatment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — subtrochanteric fractures have a bimodal distribution reflecting two distinct injury mechanisms: high-energy trauma in young adults and osteoporotic low-energy falls in the elderly population"},{"id":"sdl5_q34","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of avascular necrosis of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old male with SLE on chronic high-dose prednisone develops bilateral groin pain that is worst at night and at rest. X-ray of both hips is normal. MRI shows bilateral femoral head signal changes consistent with early avascular necrosis. Which ARCO stage best describes this patient, and what is the clinical significance?","choices":{"A":"ARCO Stage II — sclerosis visible on X-ray with no crescent sign; this is the last stage where arthroplasty can prevent progression","B":"ARCO Stage III — crescent sign and femoral head depression on X-ray; this is the earliest stage detectable by MRI alone","C":"ARCO Stage I — normal X-ray with abnormal MRI; this pre-collapse stage is the critical window for joint-preserving interventions such as core decompression before femoral head collapse","D":"ARCO Stage IV — OA changes and joint space narrowing on X-ray; this is reversible with bisphosphonate therapy","E":"ARCO Stage I — normal X-ray with normal MRI; this is the asymptomatic stage where treatment is never indicated"},"correct":"C","explanation":"ARCO Stage I is defined by a normal X-ray with abnormal MRI findings (bone marrow edema, ischemic signal changes). MRI sensitivity for AVN is nearly 100%, allowing detection before X-ray abnormalities develop. This pre-collapse stage is clinically critical — joint-preserving procedures (core decompression, vascularized fibular grafting) are most effective at Stage I before femoral head collapse. Once collapse occurs (Stage III) or OA develops (Stage IV), arthroplasty becomes the primary option.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ARCO Stage I = normal X-ray + abnormal MRI = pre-collapse. Core decompression is most effective at Stage I. MRI sensitivity = ~100%. FA: CASTS Bend LEGS = AVN mnemonic; corticosteroids = most common drug-related cause. COMLEX: Early detection of AVN in high-risk patients (SLE on steroids, sickle cell) with MRI is cost-effective.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — corticosteroid use (in SLE or other conditions) is a leading cause of AVN of the femoral head; bilateral involvement is characteristic of steroid-induced and sickle cell-related AVN"},{"id":"sdl5_q35","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of avascular necrosis of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old male with sickle cell disease presents with severe bilateral groin pain, worse at night and with weight bearing. X-ray shows sclerosis in the right femoral head without crescent sign and a crescent sign with 3mm femoral head depression in the left. Which ARCO stages correspond to each hip, and which hip requires more urgent surgical consultation?","choices":{"A":"Right hip = Stage I (normal X-ray); left hip = Stage II (sclerosis); neither requires urgent surgical evaluation","B":"Right hip = Stage III (crescent sign); left hip = Stage IV (OA changes); both require total hip arthroplasty now","C":"Right hip = Stage IV (OA changes); left hip = Stage IIIA (depression ≤2mm); right hip is more urgent for arthroplasty","D":"Right hip = Stage II (sclerosis); left hip = Stage I (MRI only); surgical consultation is not yet warranted for either hip","E":"Right hip = ARCO Stage II (sclerosis without collapse); left hip = ARCO Stage IIIB (depression >2mm); the left hip requires more urgent surgical evaluation given established collapse"},"correct":"E","explanation":"ARCO staging: Stage II = X-ray shows sclerosis, osteolysis, or osteoporosis without crescent sign or collapse (right hip here); Stage IIIA = femoral head depression ≤2mm; Stage IIIB = depression >2mm (left hip with 3mm depression). The left hip at Stage IIIB has established structural collapse and requires urgent surgical evaluation for core decompression, osteotomy, or arthroplasty depending on extent. The right hip at Stage II is pre-collapse and eligible for joint-preserving procedures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ARCO staging key cutpoints — Stage II = X-ray changes without crescent/collapse. Stage IIIA = ≤2mm depression. Stage IIIB = >2mm depression. Stage IV = OA + joint space narrowing. Sickle cell disease is a major cause of bilateral AVN. COMLEX: Pain at rest and nocturnal pain are characteristic of AVN, distinguishing it from OA (activity-related pain).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — ARCO staging guides treatment decisions in AVN; Stage I-II = joint-preserving (core decompression); Stage III-IV = reconstruction/arthroplasty; sickle cell disease causes bilateral AVN through vascular sludging in the femoral head watershed"},{"id":"sdl5_q36","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of avascular necrosis of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 40-year-old female is diagnosed with ARCO Stage II AVN of the femoral head secondary to chronic alcohol use. No femoral head collapse has occurred. Which of the following represents the MOST appropriate conservative management options for this patient?","choices":{"A":"Immediate total hip arthroplasty since Stage II AVN always progresses to collapse and arthroplasty is the only cure","B":"Activity reduction, analgesics or NSAIDs, bisphosphonates, and physical therapy with the goal of preserving joint function before collapse occurs","C":"Systemic corticosteroid therapy to reduce inflammation in the femoral head and halt ischemic progression","D":"Intra-articular hyaluronic acid injection into the hip as the primary treatment for Stage II femoral head AVN","E":"Complete hip immobilization with non-weight-bearing cast for 12 weeks as the definitive conservative treatment"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Conservative management of AVN at Stage II (pre-collapse) focuses on: (1) activity reduction to minimize femoral head loading, (2) analgesics/NSAIDs for pain, (3) bisphosphonates (reduce osteoclast activity and may slow progression), and (4) physical therapy to maintain function. No clearly superior treatment plan exists, but the goal is joint preservation. Investigational options include anticoagulants, statins, ESWT, and hyperbaric oxygen. Surgery (core decompression, osteotomy, arthroplasty) is reserved for progressive or end-stage disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AVN conservative management = activity reduction + NSAIDs + bisphosphonates + PT. No treatment reverses established necrosis — goal is joint preservation. Core decompression = surgical option for Stage I-II (pre-collapse). Stage III-IV = osteotomy or arthroplasty. COMLEX: Alcohol use disorder is a major modifiable AVN risk factor; counseling and cessation are part of management.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — hip fracture and dislocation are traumatic causes of femoral head AVN; alcohol-induced AVN shares the same endpoint of progressive femoral head ischemia and collapse requiring staged joint-preserving or reconstructive interventions"},{"id":"sdl5_q37","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of a psoas abscess and septic hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 50-year-old male with a history of Crohn disease and recent ileal resection presents with left hip pain, fever, and difficulty extending the left hip. On exam, passive left hip extension exacerbates lower abdominal pain (positive psoas sign). CBC shows WBC 18,000. CT shows a focal hypodense collection within the left iliopsoas muscle. Which is the MOST appropriate treatment?","choices":{"A":"Oral antibiotics alone as an outpatient, since psoas abscess from Crohn disease is always polymicrobial and responds to broad-spectrum coverage","B":"Intra-articular hip joint corticosteroid injection to reduce the periarticular inflammation from psoas abscess extension","C":"Watchful waiting with repeat CT in 2 weeks, since primary psoas abscesses in Crohn disease resolve spontaneously","D":"Surgical hip replacement as the initial treatment to definitively address the source of iliopsoas infection in Crohn disease","E":"Incision and drainage of the abscess combined with intravenous antibiotics, targeting Staphylococcus aureus as the most commonly isolated organism"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Psoas abscess treatment requires drainage (incision/drainage or CT-guided percutaneous drainage) plus IV antibiotics. Staphylococcus aureus is the most commonly isolated organism in both primary and secondary cases, though secondary cases from Crohn disease may include mixed enteric flora. CT diagnosis is 90% sensitive when symptoms have been present more than 6 days (only 33% sensitive if <6 days). The positive psoas sign — hip extension exacerbating lower abdominal/hip pain — is a classic exam finding pointing to the iliopsoas compartment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Psoas abscess = I&D + IV antibiotics. FA: S. aureus most common; also TB (Pott disease), Crohn disease, pyelonephritis. Psoas sign = hip extension exacerbates pain. CT = 90% sensitive (>6 days). COMLEX: Psoas abscess in Crohn disease is a secondary abscess — treat Crohn disease concurrently to prevent recurrence.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 6 — psoas abscess secondary to Crohn disease involves direct spread from the adjacent GI tract; treatment addresses both the abscess itself (drainage + antibiotics) and the underlying Crohn disease to prevent recurrence"},{"id":"sdl5_q38","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of a psoas abscess and septic hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 7-year-old male presents with 4 days of right hip pain, fever of 39.1°C, and refusal to bear weight. ESR is 65 mm/h, WBC is 14,200, and CRP is 3.2 mg/dL. He is ill-appearing. Modified Kocher score is 4-5. Which of the following is the MOST appropriate next step in management?","choices":{"A":"Outpatient NSAID therapy and repeat Kocher score assessment in 48 hours to monitor for improvement or decline","B":"Urgent surgical aspiration and lavage of the hip joint combined with intravenous antibiotics for septic hip arthritis given the 99.6% probability from the modified Kocher criteria","C":"Ultrasound-guided aspiration for fluid analysis without lavage, followed by oral antibiotics if WBC >100,000","D":"Watchful waiting with IV hydration and bed rest while awaiting blood culture results before starting antibiotics","E":"MRI of the hip to confirm effusion before any aspiration or antibiotic therapy is initiated in this child"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Modified Kocher score 4-5 = 99.6% probability of septic hip arthritis. This is a surgical emergency — delay in treatment leads to rapid femoral head cartilage destruction and permanent disability. Management requires: (1) surgical aspiration/lavage of the joint, and (2) IV antibiotics (anti-staphylococcal coverage first-line). Delayed aspiration or conservative treatment alone is inappropriate at this Kocher score. The key pitfall is distinguishing this from transient synovitis (afebrile, normal WBC/ESR, Kocher score 0-1).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kocher score 4 = 99.6% septic arthritis = surgical emergency. Treatment = surgical lavage + IV antibiotics. Contrast with transient synovitis (Kocher score 0 = <0.2% risk). COMLEX: Septic hip is a named CP8 pediatric musculoskeletal emergency; joint destruction occurs within days without surgical intervention.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 6 — modified Kocher criteria are the validated decision tool for septic hip vs. transient synovitis in children; score of 4-5 mandates urgent surgical aspiration/lavage regardless of imaging findings"},{"id":"sdl5_q39","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of a psoas abscess and septic hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 5-year-old female presents with left hip pain and a limp for 3 days. Temperature is 37.0°C. ESR is 18 mm/h and WBC is 7,800. She had a mild upper respiratory infection 10 days ago. Hip ultrasound shows a small joint effusion. Modified Kocher score is 0. Which diagnosis is MOST likely, and how does it differ from septic hip?","choices":{"A":"Septic hip arthritis, which should always be treated surgically regardless of Kocher score in any child with an effusion","B":"Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease, which presents acutely with fever and effusion after a viral illness in young children","C":"Transient synovitis, which is distinguished from septic hip by being afebrile with normal inflammatory markers and often following a viral illness; Kocher score 0 = <0.2% septic arthritis probability","D":"Psoas abscess, which causes hip effusion and limp in young children after viral respiratory illness exposure","E":"Acute osteomyelitis of the femoral head, which presents with effusion and low-grade fever from bone marrow infection"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Transient synovitis is the most common cause of acute hip pain and limp in children ages 3-10. Key distinguishing features from septic hip: afebrile (or low-grade fever <38.5°C), normal or mildly elevated ESR and WBC, often follows a viral illness by 1-4 weeks, and Kocher score 0-1. Management is conservative: NSAIDs + activity restriction. Symptoms resolve within 1-2 weeks. The pitfall is that both transient synovitis and septic hip can cause hip effusion — the Kocher criteria guide clinical decision-making.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Transient synovitis vs. septic hip — Kocher criteria are the key. Transient synovitis: post-viral, afebrile, normal labs, Kocher 0-1. Septic hip: fever >38.5°C, elevated ESR/WBC/CRP, ill-appearing, Kocher 3-5. COMLEX: Transient synovitis resolves spontaneously; follow-up hip X-ray in 4-6 weeks is prudent to exclude early LCP developing in the same age group.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 6 — transient synovitis is the primary pitfall diagnosis in the differential for septic hip in children; the modified Kocher criteria provide the validated framework to distinguish these two conditions and determine urgency of intervention"},{"id":"sdl5_q40","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of meralgia paresthetica.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 54-year-old male with BMI of 42 and type 2 diabetes presents with a 3-month history of burning pain and numbness over the right anterior and lateral thigh. He works as a long-haul truck driver and wears a tight tool belt. Neurological exam shows no motor weakness, intact patellar reflex, and decreased sensation over the anterolateral thigh only. Which diagnosis BEST explains his symptoms?","choices":{"A":"L3 radiculopathy from lumbar disc herniation, producing anterior thigh pain with quadriceps weakness and reduced patellar reflex","B":"Meralgia paresthetica from compression of the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve by the tool belt and obesity, producing pure sensory symptoms in the LFCN distribution","C":"Femoral neuropathy from diabetic amyotrophy, producing proximal leg pain and weakness with reduced patellar reflex","D":"Obturator nerve entrapment producing medial thigh adductor weakness and medial thigh sensory loss","E":"Sciatic nerve compression producing posterior thigh pain with foot drop and reduced Achilles tendon reflex"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Meralgia paresthetica (LFCN compression) presents with pure sensory symptoms — burning, tingling, numbness over the anterolateral thigh — without any motor deficit or reflex change. The LFCN is a purely sensory nerve. Key distinguishing features: no motor weakness (eliminates radiculopathy and femoral/obturator neuropathy), intact reflexes, and sensory loss confined to the anterolateral thigh. Risk factors include obesity, DM (7x risk), tight belts, and prolonged sitting/pressure — all present here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meralgia paresthetica = LFCN = anterolateral thigh sensory only, NO motor, intact reflexes. Distinguish from L3 radiculopathy (quadriceps weakness, reduced patellar reflex), femoral neuropathy (motor deficit + reflex loss). FA: LFCN = anterolateral thigh sensation; causes = obesity, pregnancy, tight clothing. COMLEX: LFCN passes under/through inguinal ligament at ASIS; local palpation at ASIS may reproduce symptoms.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 7 — meralgia paresthetica is differentiated from lumbar radiculopathy by the absence of motor deficit and preserved deep tendon reflexes; sensory loss confined to the anterolateral thigh confirms LFCN rather than L2-L3 root involvement"},{"id":"sdl5_q41","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of meralgia paresthetica.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 30-year-old female in her 32nd week of pregnancy develops burning and tingling over the right anterolateral thigh that began 3 weeks ago. Motor exam is normal. Patellar reflex is intact. She finds the symptoms most bothersome at night. Which is the MOST accurate explanation for this presentation?","choices":{"A":"L3-L4 disc herniation from pregnancy-related lumbar hyperlordosis causing radiculopathy into the anterior thigh","B":"Femoral nerve stretch from increased pelvic tilt during pregnancy causing quadriceps weakness and anterior thigh pain","C":"Obturator nerve entrapment from pelvic widening during pregnancy causing medial thigh burning and adductor weakness","D":"Piriformis syndrome from pelvic floor changes during pregnancy compressing the sciatic nerve in the gluteal region","E":"Increased abdominal pressure from the gravid uterus compresses the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve at the inguinal ligament, causing meralgia paresthetica that typically resolves postpartum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pregnancy-associated meralgia paresthetica results from increased abdominal pressure (gravid uterus) and altered pelvic mechanics compressing the LFCN at the inguinal ligament. It presents with burning and tingling in the anterolateral thigh without motor deficit — characteristic of pure LFCN sensory compression. It typically resolves spontaneously within weeks to months after delivery as abdominal pressure decreases. Management is expectant with reassurance; positioning changes (avoiding prolonged sitting) may help.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pregnancy = major risk factor for meralgia paresthetica via increased abdominal pressure. Pure sensory (anterolateral thigh), NO motor deficit. Management = reassurance + symptom modification; resolves postpartum. COMLEX: OMT is safe in pregnancy and can address iliac/inguinal somatic dysfunction contributing to LFCN compression during pregnancy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 7 — pregnancy causes meralgia paresthetica through increased abdominal pressure compressing the LFCN at the inguinal ligament; this is the same mechanism as obesity and ascites, both of which increase intra-abdominal pressure"},{"id":"sdl5_q42","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of meralgia paresthetica.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old male with obesity-related meralgia paresthetica fails to improve after weight loss counseling over 3 months and lifestyle modification. He has persistent 7/10 burning pain over the anterolateral thigh limiting work and sleep. Which is the MOST appropriate escalation of treatment for refractory meralgia paresthetica?","choices":{"A":"Lumbar epidural steroid injection targeting the L2-L3 level to reduce radicular inflammation around the LFCN origin","B":"Intra-articular hip joint corticosteroid injection to reduce local inflammation compressing the LFCN at the joint margin","C":"Anticonvulsant medications such as gabapentin or pregabalin, or referral to anesthesia for nerve block at the inguinal ligament level, for refractory meralgia paresthetica","D":"Surgical hip arthroplasty to decompress the LFCN by relieving hip joint capsule tension on the surrounding nerves","E":"Systemic oral corticosteroid taper for 6 weeks to reduce perineural inflammation around the compressed LFCN"},"correct":"C","explanation":"For refractory meralgia paresthetica (failed conservative management including treating the underlying cause), escalation options include: (1) anticonvulsants — gabapentin or pregabalin — for neuropathic pain management, and (2) anesthesia consultation for nerve block injection at the inguinal ligament level (LFCN block). Surgical decompression or neurectomy is rarely needed. Lumbar epidural and hip injections target the wrong anatomic structure. The SDL explicitly lists anticonvulsants and anesthesia consultation as the treatment options for recalcitrant cases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meralgia paresthetica treatment = treat cause first (weight loss, remove compression). Recalcitrant = anticonvulsants (gabapentin/pregabalin) or anesthesia consult for LFCN block. Surgical decompression or neurectomy is rare. COMLEX: LFCN block at ASIS is a targeted intervention that confirms diagnosis and provides therapeutic benefit.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 7 — anticonvulsant medications (gabapentin, pregabalin) are the recognized pharmacologic escalation for refractory meralgia paresthetica when treating the underlying cause fails to resolve the neuropathic pain"}]},{"sdlNumber":7,"title":"Leg, Ankle & Foot Pathologies","questions":[{"id":"sdl7_q01","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial tibial stress syndrome (MTSS).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which structure is the primary site of pathology in medial tibial stress syndrome (MTSS)?","choices":{"A":"Posteromedial tibial periosteum, subjected to repetitive traction from posterior compartment muscles including the tibialis posterior, soleus, and flexor digitorum longus","B":"Anterior tibial cortex, stressed by axial compressive loading from repetitive heel strike impact forces during high-mileage training programs in competitive distance runners","C":"Lateral tibial cortex, subjected to shear stress from excessive ankle pronation and supination cycling forces during push-off in runners with biomechanical malalignment","D":"Medial tibial endosteum, injured by elevated intramedullary pressure from increased bone marrow vascularity during rapid progression of aerobic training load in athletes","E":"Posterior tibial diaphysis, compressed by increased quadriceps and hamstring co-activation forces generating axial tibial stress during high-intensity interval training"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The primary pathological site in MTSS is the posteromedial tibial periosteum, which is subjected to repetitive traction from the origins of the posterior compartment muscles (tibialis posterior, soleus, FDL). The anterior tibial cortex is more associated with anterior tibial stress fractures, while endosteal pathology suggests deeper marrow injury. Identifying the periosteum as the site explains why overpronation — which increases traction on the posteromedial periosteum — is the strongest modifiable risk factor.","boardPrep":"MTSS = traction periostitis at origins of posterior compartment muscles (tibialis posterior, soleus, FDL) on posteromedial tibia. Steps: repetitive activity → posterior compartment overuse → traction periostitis → bone remodeling → fracture if no rest.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 1 — MTSS: diffuse posteromedial tibial periostitis from posterior compartment traction; X-ray often negative; overpronation is the confirmed risk factor"},{"id":"sdl7_q02","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial tibial stress syndrome (MTSS).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which bone scan finding best differentiates medial tibial stress syndrome from a tibial stress fracture?","choices":{"A":"Focal, intense, well-demarcated area of increased uptake at a single point on the tibial cortex, indicating a stress fracture at that specific discrete cortical location","B":"Completely normal bone scan bilaterally with no areas of increased uptake despite significant clinical symptoms and prolonged posteromedial tibial tenderness on examination","C":"Patchy, diffuse areas of increased uptake spanning several centimeters along the posteromedial tibial cortex without a single focal hot spot on imaging","D":"Increased uptake in the anterior tibial compartment bilaterally reflecting tibialis anterior muscle inflammation from overuse training in a distance runner during peak mileage","E":"Focal uptake at the tibial metaphysis bilaterally suggesting growth plate stress injury rather than periosteal or cortical bone involvement in the skeletally immature athlete"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The key differentiator between MTSS and tibial stress fracture on bone scan is the distribution of tracer uptake: MTSS produces patchy, diffuse uptake spanning centimeters along the posteromedial cortex, while a stress fracture shows a focal, intense single-point hot spot at the fracture site. Both conditions can be bilateral, so symmetry is not the distinguishing feature. MTSS does produce an abnormal bone scan, ruling out a completely normal result as the distinguishing answer.","boardPrep":"MTSS bone scan: patchy diffuse uptake. Stress fracture: focal intense hot spot at one site. MRI: periosteal fluid, bone marrow signal in MTSS. X-ray often negative in MTSS; may show periosteal scalloping or cortical thickening late.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 1 — MTSS: diffuse posteromedial tibial periostitis from posterior compartment traction; X-ray often negative; overpronation is the confirmed risk factor"},{"id":"sdl7_q03","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial tibial stress syndrome (MTSS).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following is the most consistently confirmed modifiable risk factor for medial tibial stress syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Increased external hip rotation beyond 15 degrees measured during gait analysis in female distance runners with a history of recurrent lower extremity overuse injuries","B":"Decreased lumbar spine bone mineral density measured by DEXA scan in collegiate athletes with amenorrhea from relative energy deficiency in sport during competitive season","C":"Use of minimalist footwear with a heel-to-toe drop below 4mm in athletes who transition abruptly from traditional cushioned running shoes during high-mileage training","D":"Increased tibial torsion angle above 20 degrees measured on CT scanogram of the lower extremity in military recruits during the first weeks of intensive basic training","E":"Overpronation of the subtalar joint, increasing traction forces on the posteromedial tibial periosteum from the posterior compartment muscles during the loading phase of gait"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Overpronation of the subtalar joint is the most consistently confirmed modifiable risk factor for MTSS, increasing traction forces on the posteromedial periosteum from posterior compartment muscle origins during the loading phase of gait. External hip rotation, bone mineral density, footwear style, and tibial torsion are proposed risk factors but have weaker or less consistent evidence than subtalar overpronation in the published literature on MTSS.","boardPrep":"MTSS confirmed risk factors: overpronation (strongest), training errors (rapid mileage increase), low bone mineral density. In males: increased hip internal and external rotation, less calf girth. Females 3x more likely to get MTSS than males.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 1 — MTSS: diffuse posteromedial tibial periostitis from posterior compartment traction; X-ray often negative; overpronation is the confirmed risk factor"},{"id":"sdl7_q04","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ankle sprain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement most accurately describes the epidemiology of ankle sprains?","choices":{"A":"Lateral ankle sprains account for 70-90% of all ankle sprains, with the ATFL injured in isolation in approximately 73% of lateral sprain cases seen clinically","B":"Medial ankle sprains are the most common type, occurring at 2.7 per 1000 athlete exposures and frequently requiring surgical repair of the deltoid ligament complex","C":"Syndesmotic high ankle sprains account for 40-60% of all ankle injuries and most cases require orthopedic surgical fixation to restore distal tibiofibular joint stability","D":"Ankle sprains affect males more than females with an overall incidence of 7 per 1000 person-years in male athletes compared to female counterparts in all age groups","E":"Lateral ankle sprains carry a 45% recurrence rate and invariably progress to chronic ankle instability requiring surgical ligament reconstruction within 2 years of injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lateral ankle sprains account for 70-90% of all ankle sprains, with the ATFL injured in isolation in approximately 73% of lateral cases. Medial sprains are rare (0.06/1000 athlete exposures), not the most common type. Syndesmotic sprains account for only 0.38/1000 — not 40-60%. Ankle sprains occur more frequently in females than males, and the recurrence rate is approximately 11.9%, not 45%.","boardPrep":"Ankle sprains: 2 million/year in US. Lateral 70-90%; ATFL injured in 73% of lateral cases. Females > males. Children/adolescents > adults. Medial: 0.06/1000 athlete exposures. Syndesmotic: 0.38/1000. Recurrence rate 11.9%.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: lateral 70-90% (ATFL in 73%); inversion with plantarflexion; Ottawa rules guide imaging; grades 1-3 by ligament integrity"},{"id":"sdl7_q05","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ankle sprain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which mechanism of injury most commonly produces a lateral ankle sprain with isolated ATFL injury?","choices":{"A":"Dorsiflexion combined with internal rotation of the tibia over a fixed foot, stressing the anterior talofibular ligament during a rapid deceleration maneuver in sport","B":"Eversion combined with external rotation of the ankle while planting the foot, stressing the calcaneofibular and deltoid ligament complex in a multiligamentous injury","C":"Plantarflexion combined with tibial external rotation stressing the anterior tibiofibular ligament and the interosseous membrane of the distal tibiofibular syndesmosis","D":"Inversion while the foot is in plantarflexion, placing the ATFL perpendicular to the fibula and making it the first lateral structure to fail under tensile loading","E":"Dorsiflexion combined with external rotation stressing the deltoid ligament medially and the anterior tibiofibular ligament of the syndesmosis simultaneously"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lateral ankle sprain most commonly occurs with inversion while the foot is in plantarflexion, which orients the ATFL perpendicular to the fibula and makes it the first lateral structure to fail under tensile loading. Dorsiflexion with internal rotation and dorsiflexion with external rotation both stress the syndesmosis rather than the lateral ligaments. Eversion with external rotation injures the deltoid complex, not the lateral ligaments.","boardPrep":"Lateral ankle sprain mechanism: inversion with plantarflexion. Order of injury: ATFL first (73% isolated) → CFL → PTFL. Medial sprain: eversion and dorsiflexion. Syndesmotic: dorsiflexion + external rotation. Anterior drawer tests ATFL; talar tilt tests CFL; squeeze and external rotation tests syndesmosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: lateral 70-90% (ATFL in 73%); inversion with plantarflexion; Ottawa rules guide imaging; grades 1-3 by ligament integrity"},{"id":"sdl7_q06","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ankle sprain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"According to the Ottawa Ankle Rules, which criterion mandates ankle radiograph in a patient presenting with ankle pain?","choices":{"A":"Anterior ankle tenderness at the ATFL with visible swelling and ecchymosis, regardless of whether the patient can bear weight on the injured extremity at time of evaluation","B":"Midfoot tenderness at the cuboid or base of the 5th metatarsal with inability to bear weight, which triggers foot radiographs rather than standard ankle view series","C":"Posterior medial malleolus tenderness over the distal 6cm, posterior lateral malleolus tenderness, or inability to bear weight for 4 steps immediately after injury","D":"Lateral ankle tenderness anterior to the ATFL with a positive anterior drawer test indicating ligament laxity but no point tenderness over the malleoli on palpation","E":"Visible ankle swelling appearing within 2 hours of injury with complete inability to complete active range of motion in plantarflexion or dorsiflexion upon examination"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ottawa Ankle Rules mandate radiographs when there is posterior medial malleolus tenderness (distal 6cm), posterior lateral malleolus tenderness, or inability to bear weight for 4 steps. Anterior ATFL tenderness and visible swelling alone do NOT trigger ankle X-rays under the Ottawa rules. Midfoot tenderness at the navicular or 5th metatarsal base triggers foot radiographs, not ankle views. Positive anterior drawer without malleolar tenderness does not mandate imaging.","boardPrep":"Ottawa Ankle Rules — ankle X-ray if: (1) posterior lateral malleolus tenderness (distal 6cm), (2) posterior medial malleolus tenderness, OR (3) inability to bear weight for 4 steps. Foot X-ray if: navicular tenderness OR 5th metatarsal base tenderness. Sensitivity near 100% for clinically significant fracture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: lateral 70-90% (ATFL in 73%); inversion with plantarflexion; Ottawa rules guide imaging; grades 1-3 by ligament integrity"},{"id":"sdl7_q07","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of gastrocnemius strain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which mechanism of injury most commonly produces an acute gastrocnemius strain at the medial musculotendinous junction?","choices":{"A":"Sustained plantarflexion against resistance during repetitive uphill running causing concentric overload and progressive degenerative change at the medial gastrocnemius origin","B":"Rapid deceleration with the knee extended causing simultaneous hamstring and gastrocnemius strain with diffuse posterior knee pain in middle-aged recreational athletes","C":"Repetitive eccentric loading during prolonged downhill running causing gradual degenerative microtrauma at the Achilles tendon myotendinous junction proximal to insertion","D":"Sudden acceleration from a stationary position with the ankle in dorsiflexion, causing eccentric overstretch of the medial gastrocnemius musculotendinous unit during push-off","E":"Direct blunt contusion to the posterior calf from contact during a team sport causing a localized intramuscular hematoma within the lateral gastrocnemius muscle belly"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The gastrocnemius medial head strain (tennis leg) classically occurs with sudden acceleration from a stationary position with the ankle in dorsiflexion, producing eccentric overstretch at the musculotendinous junction during push-off. Sustained plantarflexion against resistance causes concentric, not eccentric, overload. Rapid deceleration with the knee extended more commonly stresses the hamstrings. Direct blunt contusion causes intramuscular hematoma at the point of impact, not at the medial MTJ.","boardPrep":"Gastrocnemius strain (tennis leg): sudden acceleration from stationary position with ankle dorsiflexed (lunging in tennis or squash). Eccentric overstretch; injury at transition from eccentric to isometric contraction. Medial head most commonly injured. Classic: sudden pop, \"hit in the calf\" sensation, tenderness at medial MTJ.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — Gastrocnemius strain: medial belly injury from eccentric overstretch; dorsiflexion reproduces pain; Grade III has palpable defect and positive Thompson test"},{"id":"sdl7_q08","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of gastrocnemius strain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which clinical finding distinguishes a Grade III gastrocnemius strain from a Grade II injury?","choices":{"A":"Severe posterior calf pain during passive dorsiflexion that prevents the patient from completing full range of motion assessment during the clinical physical examination","B":"Positive Thompson test with a palpable defect at the musculotendinous junction indicating complete rupture of the medial gastrocnemius with surgical repair required","C":"Significant ecchymosis tracking distally to the heel within 24 hours of injury due to gravitational spread of blood along the posterior leg fascial planes after trauma","D":"Inability to perform a single-leg calf raise on the affected side within the first 72 hours of the injury due to pain and weakness of the posterior calf compartment","E":"Loss of active plantarflexion strength measured against gravity with the ankle held at 20 degrees of dorsiflexion, distinguishing Grade II from Grade I partial tear"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Grade III gastrocnemius strain is defined by complete rupture of the medial head, producing a positive Thompson test and a palpable defect at the musculotendinous junction. Grade II has active plantarflexion pain and loss of dorsiflexion ROM but Thompson is negative and no palpable defect exists. Ecchymosis and inability to calf raise occur in both Grade II and III and therefore do not distinguish the grades from each other.","boardPrep":"Gastrocnemius strain grades: Grade I — sharp pain, can calf raise, returns in 10-12 days. Grade II — unable to continue activity, active plantarflexion pain, loss of dorsiflexion, returns in 16-21 days. Grade III — immediate severe pain, positive Thompson test, palpable defect at MTJ, surgical repair, returns in 6 months.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — Gastrocnemius strain: medial belly injury from eccentric overstretch; dorsiflexion reproduces pain; Grade III has palpable defect and positive Thompson test"},{"id":"sdl7_q09","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of gastrocnemius strain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which progressive rehabilitation sequence is most appropriate within the first 24 hours of a gastrocnemius strain?","choices":{"A":"Begin concentric calf strengthening exercises, progressing to weighted calf raises and then eccentric calf lowering exercises as pain allows for full functional restoration","B":"Apply a full leg cast in equinus for 6 weeks with strict non-weight bearing to protect the myotendinous junction from any mechanical loading during the acute healing phase","C":"Administer a corticosteroid injection directly into the musculotendinous junction under ultrasound guidance to reduce the acute inflammatory response within 24 hours","D":"Proceed with surgical exploration and primary repair of the medial gastrocnemius head regardless of clinical grade determination on initial physical examination findings","E":"Apply deep tissue massage and myofascial release techniques directly over the site of the muscle tear within the first 24 hours to limit early scar tissue formation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Gastrocnemius strain rehabilitation begins within 24 hours with concentric calf strengthening, then progressive loading with weight, then eccentric calf lowering as pain allows. Full equinus casting is reserved only for Grade III complete rupture after surgical repair. Corticosteroid injection and surgery are not indicated for Grade I or Grade II strains. The progression from concentric to eccentric loading is the established rehabilitation sequence for calf injuries.","boardPrep":"Gastrocnemius strain rehabilitation: ice, TENS, crutches if needed, heel lift, gentle stretching. Strengthening within 24h: (1) concentric, (2) add weight, (3) eccentric calf lowering. Low-impact cardio maintained throughout. Myofascial techniques when pain-free. Surgery only for Grade III complete rupture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — Gastrocnemius strain: medial belly injury from eccentric overstretch; dorsiflexion reproduces pain; Grade III has palpable defect and positive Thompson test"},{"id":"sdl7_q10","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of posterior tibial tendon dysfunction/tendinitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which combination of clinical findings is most consistent with Grade 2 posterior tibial tendon dysfunction?","choices":{"A":"Normal medial longitudinal arch with pain and swelling of the tendon; patient is able to complete a single-leg heel raise without significant difficulty or pain on examination","B":"Fixed subtalar joint deformity with rigid pes planus that cannot be passively corrected to neutral; requires orthopedic referral for surgical bony reconstruction procedure","C":"Bilateral posterior tibial tendon swelling with preserved normal arch height; patient completes heel raise without weakness or pain on repeat standardized physical exam","D":"Inability to perform a single-leg heel raise with flexible pes planus and forefoot abduction deformity that is passively correctable to neutral hindfoot alignment","E":"Complete tendon rupture confirmed on MRI with total collapse of medial arch requiring urgent orthopedic consultation for posterior tibial tendon surgical reconstruction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Grade 2 PTTD is defined by the inability to perform a single-leg heel raise combined with flexible pes planus and forefoot abduction that is passively correctable to neutral hindfoot alignment. Grade 1 has tendon pain and swelling but preserved heel raise ability. Grade 3 has a fixed subtalar deformity that cannot be passively corrected to neutral. Grade 4 involves valgus tibiotalar tilt within the mortise, requiring surgical reconstruction.","boardPrep":"PTTD grading: Grade 1 — pain/swelling, able to single-leg heel raise, no deformity, conservative care. Grade 2 — CANNOT single-leg heel raise, flexible pes planus + forefoot abduction, same conservative treatment as Grade 1. Grade 3 — Grade 2 + fixed subtalar joint → ortho referral for reconstruction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — PTTD: hallmark is failed single-leg heel raise; Grade 1 normal arch, Grade 2 flexible pes planus, Grade 3 fixed subtalar joint"},{"id":"sdl7_q11","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of posterior tibial tendon dysfunction/tendinitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which physical examination test is the hallmark clinical assessment for posterior tibial tendon dysfunction across all grades?","choices":{"A":"Weakness or inability to perform a single-leg heel raise on the affected side, indicating insufficient posterior tibial tendon function to supinate the hindfoot during rise","B":"Positive anterior drawer sign at the ankle indicating concurrent anterior talofibular ligament laxity contributing to chronic lateral ankle instability in the affected patient","C":"Tenderness over the lateral calcaneus worsening with inversion stress testing, indicating peroneal tendon subluxation or coexisting lateral ankle ligament pathology","D":"Decreased passive subtalar inversion range of motion below 10 degrees indicating early degenerative arthritic changes in the subtalar joint complex on examination","E":"Medial ankle ecchymosis with a palpable gap at the posterior tibial musculotendinous junction indicating acute complete rupture of the posterior tibial tendon recently"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The single-leg heel raise is the hallmark clinical test for PTTD across all grades, directly assessing the posterior tibial tendon ability to supinate the hindfoot and maintain the medial arch during heel rise. The anterior drawer tests the ATFL, not the posterior tibial tendon. Subtalar inversion ROM reflects joint mobility, not tendon function specifically. A palpable gap with medial ecchymosis would indicate acute complete tendon rupture, not the chronic progressive course of typical PTTD.","boardPrep":"PTTD hallmark: single-leg heel raise failure. The posterior tibial tendon inverts the hindfoot and maintains the medial arch during heel rise. Too-many-toes sign (hindfoot valgus, forefoot abduction viewed from behind) is classic for Grade 2+. Prevalence 3.3-10%; increases after age 40; usually unilateral.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — PTTD: hallmark is failed single-leg heel raise; Grade 1 normal arch, Grade 2 flexible pes planus, Grade 3 fixed subtalar joint"},{"id":"sdl7_q12","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of posterior tibial tendon dysfunction/tendinitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which management strategy is most appropriate as the primary initial treatment for Grade 1 posterior tibial tendon dysfunction?","choices":{"A":"Platelet-rich plasma injection directly into the posterior tibial tendon sheath under ultrasound guidance as the preferred first-line regenerative intervention for Grade 1","B":"Corticosteroid injection into the posterior tibial tendon sheath as the primary evidence-based first-line management approach for Grade 1 posterior tibial tendon dysfunction","C":"Nitroglycerin patch applied over the medial ankle as the first-line adjunctive treatment for all grades of posterior tibial tendinopathy based on strong evidence","D":"Extracorporeal shockwave therapy targeting the tendon insertion as the evidence-based preferred non-surgical option for Grade 1 posterior tibial tendon dysfunction management","E":"Activity modification, medial arch support orthotics, analgesics for pain control, and supervised physical therapy with eccentric tibialis posterior strengthening exercises"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Grade 1 PTTD is managed conservatively with activity modification, medial arch support orthotics, analgesics, and supervised physical therapy including eccentric tibialis posterior strengthening. PRP and corticosteroid injections are adjunctive options with limited evidence and are not first-line for Grade 1. Casting is reserved for more advanced cases. Nitroglycerin patches have limited and inconsistent evidence and are not the primary recommended treatment.","boardPrep":"PTTD management: Grade 1 and Grade 2 — activity modification, medial arch support orthotics, analgesics, physical therapy. Grade 3 — orthopedic referral for surgical reconstruction. Adjunctive options: corticosteroid injections (caution, rupture risk), regenerative injections, nitroglycerin patches (all limited evidence). Eccentric PT is cornerstone.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — PTTD: hallmark is failed single-leg heel raise; Grade 1 normal arch, Grade 2 flexible pes planus, Grade 3 fixed subtalar joint"},{"id":"sdl7_q13","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Achilles tendinopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement best distinguishes insertional from mid-portion Achilles tendinopathy based on anatomic location?","choices":{"A":"Mid-portion tendinopathy involves the paratenon sheath exclusively and responds to eccentric loading programs regardless of the stage or chronicity of symptoms in the patient","B":"Insertional tendinopathy occurs exclusively in older sedentary patients and is entirely unrelated to activity level, sport participation, or mechanical loading forces","C":"Insertional tendinopathy affects the tendon-bone junction at the calcaneal enthesis, while mid-portion tendinopathy occurs in the hypovascular zone 2-6cm proximal to the insertion","D":"Mid-portion tendinopathy causes calcaneal bone spurring at the insertion requiring surgical excision, while insertional disease has no associated bony calcaneal changes","E":"Insertional tendinopathy exclusively affects competitive athletes while mid-portion tendinopathy is limited to recreational runners with poor foot and ankle biomechanics"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Insertional Achilles tendinopathy occurs at the calcaneal enthesis where the tendon attaches to bone, while mid-portion tendinopathy occurs in the hypovascular zone 2-6cm proximal to the insertion — the most common site of tendinopathy. The mid-portion involves the tendon substance and paratenon, not the paratenon exclusively. Insertional tendinopathy is not limited to older sedentary patients, and bone spurring (Haglund deformity) is associated with the insertional type, not mid-portion disease.","boardPrep":"Achilles tendinopathy: insertional = at calcaneal enthesis (tendon-bone junction), associated with bone spur or Haglund deformity. Mid-portion = 2-6cm proximal to insertion in the hypovascular zone (most common site). Both worsen with increased activity. Tendinopathy umbrella includes tendinitis, tendinosis, paratendinitis, enthesopathy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: insertional at calcaneal enthesis vs mid-portion 2-6cm proximal; eccentric calf loading is first-line PT rehabilitation"},{"id":"sdl7_q14","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Achilles tendinopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which combination of factors is confirmed as independently associated with increased risk of Achilles tendinopathy?","choices":{"A":"Female sex with estrogen-related collagen laxity, vegetarian diet with reduced collagen precursors, and hyperpronation of the subtalar joint as the primary risk factors","B":"High weekly mileage above 50 miles per week, running exclusively on hard concrete surfaces, and consuming a high-fat diet as the primary confirmed risk factors identified","C":"Reduced ankle dorsiflexion ROM below 10 degrees measured non-weight bearing as the single most important confirmed primary risk factor for Achilles tendinopathy in athletes","D":"Male sex, increasing age, prior history of tendinopathy, overweight status, and poor running mechanics including excessive supination as confirmed risk factors","E":"Leg-length discrepancy above 2cm measured by scanogram combined with arch height index below 0.30 as the most strongly confirmed combination of risk factors for tendinopathy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Confirmed risk factors for Achilles tendinopathy include male sex, increasing age, prior tendinopathy history, overweight status, and poor running mechanics (excessive supination, inadequate dorsiflexion). Female sex and estrogen-related laxity are not the primary confirmed risk factors — males have higher incidence. High mileage and hard surfaces are proposed risk factors but are less consistently confirmed than the biomechanical and demographic factors listed.","boardPrep":"Achilles tendinopathy risk factors: prior tendinopathy, poor running mechanics (excessive supination, inadequate dorsiflexion), worn footwear, weak plantarflexion, leg-length discrepancy, increased age, male sex, overweight. Incidence: competitive athletes 24%, recreational runners 6-17% of injuries.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: insertional at calcaneal enthesis vs mid-portion 2-6cm proximal; eccentric calf loading is first-line PT rehabilitation"},{"id":"sdl7_q15","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Achilles tendinopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which physical therapy intervention is considered the first-line rehabilitation for mid-portion Achilles tendinopathy?","choices":{"A":"Concentric calf strengthening only in a completely pain-free range with no eccentric component permitted until 12 full weeks after symptom onset has been reached","B":"Eccentric calf-muscle loading protocol with progressive resistance increase, establishing controlled tendon loading to promote collagen remodeling and pain reduction","C":"Isokinetic strengthening using a dynamometer at 60 degrees per second as the preferred physical therapy approach validated for mid-portion Achilles tendinopathy treatment","D":"Ultrasound-guided corticosteroid injection into the paratenon sheath followed by 6 weeks of complete relative rest and passive gentle Achilles tendon stretching exercises","E":"Static gastrosoleus stretching for 30 seconds three times daily combined with daily nitroglycerin patch application as the current evidence-based standard protocol for tendinopathy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Eccentric calf-muscle loading (Alfredson protocol) is first-line PT for mid-portion Achilles tendinopathy, applying controlled tensile load that stimulates tenocyte mechanotransduction and promotes collagen remodeling. Concentric-only exercises lack the specific mechanical stimulus that drives matrix remodeling in tendinopathy. Corticosteroid injection carries significant tendon rupture risk and has uncertain long-term benefit. Static stretching and nitroglycerin patches are adjunctive at best and are not first-line evidence-based treatments.","boardPrep":"Achilles tendinopathy PT: eccentric calf-muscle loading (Alfredson protocol) is first-line. Perform heel drop off a step: rise on two, lower on one. Gastrosoleus mobilization and activity modification are complementary. Corticosteroid injection: uncertain benefit, rupture risk. Percutaneous tenotomy and nitroglycerin patches are adjunctive options.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: insertional at calcaneal enthesis vs mid-portion 2-6cm proximal; eccentric calf loading is first-line PT rehabilitation"},{"id":"sdl7_q16","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of retrocalcaneal bursitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which anatomic description correctly identifies the location of the retrocalcaneal bursa?","choices":{"A":"The superficial subcutaneous bursa located posterior to the Achilles tendon between the skin and the tendon at the heel level, irritated by shoe counter pressure on activity","B":"The medial calcaneal bursa located at the plantar-medial calcaneal prominence adjacent to the plantar fascia attachment on the inferior calcaneal surface with weight bearing","C":"The retrocalcaneal bursa is positioned between the posterior calcaneus and the deep anterior surface of the Achilles tendon, just proximal to the calcaneal insertion","D":"The subtalar bursa located within the tarsal sinus between the talus and calcaneus adjacent to the interosseous talocalcaneal ligament on the lateral aspect of the foot","E":"The tibiotalar bursa located anteriorly between the tibial plafond and the talar dome within the ankle mortise, irritated by repetitive dorsiflexion impingement in athletes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The retrocalcaneal bursa is positioned between the posterior calcaneus and the deep anterior surface of the Achilles tendon, just proximal to the calcaneal insertion. The superficial calcaneal bursa lies between the Achilles and overlying skin and is irritated by shoe counter pressure. The medial calcaneal and subtalar bursae are distinct structures at different anatomic locations. The tibiotalar bursa is anterior within the ankle mortise and is not the retrocalcaneal bursa.","boardPrep":"Retrocalcaneal bursa: between the calcaneus and deep surface of Achilles tendon. Distinct from superficial calcaneal bursa (between Achilles and skin). Often coexists with Achilles tendinosis and calcaneal enthesopathy. One of 3 most common bursitis locations. More common in adults; males = females.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 6 — Retrocalcaneal bursitis: bursa between calcaneus and Achilles tendon; worse with dorsiflexion; highly associated with Achilles tendinosis and enthesopathy"},{"id":"sdl7_q17","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of retrocalcaneal bursitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which physical examination finding is most characteristic of retrocalcaneal bursitis?","choices":{"A":"Pain with resisted plantarflexion and maximum tenderness at the mid-portion of the Achilles tendon 4cm proximal to the calcaneal insertion on direct palpation of the tendon","B":"Posterior heel pain with tenderness at the posterosuperior calcaneus just anterior to the Achilles tendon insertion, worsening with passive ankle dorsiflexion examination","C":"Inferomedial heel pain that is most severe with the first morning steps and improves significantly after walking for approximately 10 minutes of activity on the affected foot","D":"Lateral heel tenderness with swelling that worsens with ankle inversion stress testing and resolves completely when the ankle is held in passive dorsiflexion by the examiner","E":"Diffuse posterior ankle swelling with pain on forced plantarflexion at end range indicating posterior impingement between the calcaneus and the posterior tibial plafond"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis characteristically produces posterior calcaneal tenderness at the posterosuperior calcaneus just deep to the Achilles tendon, worsened by passive dorsiflexion which compresses the bursa. Mid-tendon tenderness 4cm proximal suggests mid-portion Achilles tendinopathy. Inferomedial heel pain worst with first morning steps is the classic presentation of plantar fasciitis, not bursitis. Lateral heel pain with inversion stress indicates lateral ankle ligament or peroneal tendon pathology.","boardPrep":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis: posterior calcaneal pain deep to Achilles, worse with dorsiflexion (bursa compressed). Often confused with plantar fasciitis (inferior heel, worse first steps). Highly associated with Achilles tendinosis. Treatment: cold compresses, analgesics, footwear modification, Achilles stretching, corticosteroid injection, surgery rarely.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 6 — Retrocalcaneal bursitis: bursa between calcaneus and Achilles tendon; worse with dorsiflexion; highly associated with Achilles tendinosis and enthesopathy"},{"id":"sdl7_q18","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of retrocalcaneal bursitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which combination of interventions is most appropriate as the initial conservative treatment for retrocalcaneal bursitis?","choices":{"A":"Cold compresses to reduce swelling, analgesics for pain control, footwear modification to reduce heel counter pressure, and Achilles tendon stretching program","B":"Immediate corticosteroid injection into the retrocalcaneal bursa under ultrasound guidance followed by 2 weeks of strict non-weight bearing on crutches for the patient","C":"Surgical bursectomy with calcaneal exostectomy as the primary treatment given the poor long-term outcomes reported with conservative management for retrocalcaneal bursitis","D":"Platelet-rich plasma injection combined with prolotherapy injections into the bursa and surrounding tendon as the initial regenerative treatment approach at first visit","E":"Oral corticosteroid taper for 10 days combined with iontophoresis applied directly over the posterior heel as the standard evidence-based initial management protocol"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Initial conservative treatment for retrocalcaneal bursitis includes cold compresses to reduce swelling, analgesics for pain, footwear modification to reduce heel counter pressure on the bursa, and Achilles tendon stretching. Immediate corticosteroid injection is not first-line — it is reserved for cases refractory to conservative care. Surgery is rarely needed. PRP and prolotherapy have insufficient evidence to serve as initial treatment. Oral corticosteroid taper is not standard management for localized bursitis.","boardPrep":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis treatment: cold compresses, analgesics, footwear modification (reduce heel counter pressure), Achilles stretching. If refractory: corticosteroid injection into the bursa (not into Achilles tendon). Surgery rarely indicated. Distinguish from superficial calcaneal bursitis (between skin and Achilles — caused by shoe rubbing).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 6 — Retrocalcaneal bursitis: bursa between calcaneus and Achilles tendon; worse with dorsiflexion; highly associated with Achilles tendinosis and enthesopathy"},{"id":"sdl7_q19","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of calcaneal apophysitis (Sever Disease).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement most accurately describes the epidemiology of calcaneal apophysitis (Sever disease)?","choices":{"A":"Sever disease affects females at age 8 years and males at age 6 years, with unilateral involvement accounting for approximately 90% of all affected pediatric patients","B":"The incidence is 10 per 1000 pediatric patients, and unilateral presentation occurs in approximately 90% of all cases presenting in skeletally immature young athletes","C":"Sever disease predominantly affects adolescents over age 14 years and resolves spontaneously within 6 weeks without any specific treatment or activity restriction","D":"Males and females are equally affected with a peak onset between ages 12 and 14 years and predominantly unilateral involvement in the dominant extremity in most cases","E":"Males develop at a peak age of 8 years, females at 6 years, with bilateral involvement in approximately two-thirds of cases and an incidence of 3.7 per 1000 patients"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sever disease peaks in males at age 8 years and females at age 6 years, with bilateral involvement in approximately 2/3 of cases and an incidence of 3.7 per 1000 patients. Females develop earlier due to earlier skeletal maturation, not later. The incidence is not 10/1000. Sever disease does NOT predominantly affect adolescents over 14 — it resolves as the calcaneal apophysis fuses at skeletal maturity, which occurs by the early teenage years.","boardPrep":"Sever disease: males peak at 8yr, females peak at 6yr (females develop earlier due to earlier skeletal maturation). Males > females. Bilateral in 2/3. Incidence 3.7/1000. Duration: 3-4 years until skeletal maturity. Posteroinferior calcaneus growth plate at Achilles insertion is the site of injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 7 — Sever disease: calcaneal apophysitis from Achilles traction on growth plate; males at 8yr, females at 6yr; bilateral in 2/3; self-limited 3-4 years"},{"id":"sdl7_q20","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of calcaneal apophysitis (Sever Disease).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which pathophysiologic mechanism is responsible for calcaneal apophysitis (Sever disease)?","choices":{"A":"Avascular necrosis of the calcaneal epiphysis from disruption of the nutrient artery blood supply to the posteroinferior calcaneal growth plate cartilage during athletic activity","B":"Repetitive traction stress from the Achilles tendon on the calcaneal apophysis growth plate during running and jumping in skeletally immature athletes during development","C":"Acute complete fracture through the calcaneal apophyseal growth plate from a single episode of forceful ground impact during competitive athletic activity in youth athletes","D":"Inflammatory bursitis of the retrocalcaneal bursa causing secondary irritation of the adjacent calcaneal growth plate cartilage in young skeletally immature pediatric athletes","E":"Repetitive compressive stress fracture of the calcaneal body from excessive impact loading during running causing subchondral bone insufficiency injury in growing athletes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sever disease is caused by repetitive traction from the Achilles tendon on the posteroinferior calcaneal growth plate (apophysis) during running and jumping in skeletally immature athletes. It is NOT avascular necrosis — that term applies to Legg-Calve-Perthes disease of the femoral head. It is not an acute fracture through the apophysis, nor isolated bursitis. The diagnosis is traction apophysitis, mechanically analogous to Osgood-Schlatter disease at the tibial tubercle but occurring at the calcaneal apophysis.","boardPrep":"Sever disease pathophysiology: calcaneal apophysis is the posteroinferior calcaneal growth plate at the Achilles insertion. Repetitive Achilles traction on the immature apophysis during running and jumping → apophysitis (traction injury, NOT avascular necrosis — that is Legg-Calve-Perthes). Clinical diagnosis; X-ray not required but may show fragmentation of apophysis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 7 — Sever disease: calcaneal apophysitis from Achilles traction on growth plate; males at 8yr, females at 6yr; bilateral in 2/3; self-limited 3-4 years"},{"id":"sdl7_q21","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of calcaneal apophysitis (Sever Disease).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which combination of interventions represents appropriate initial management of Sever disease in a skeletally immature athlete?","choices":{"A":"Activity reduction, heel cushioning inserts, Achilles tendon stretching, NSAIDs for acute pain control, and footwear modification with adequate heel support","B":"Immediate casting in equinus position for 6 weeks followed by a gradual return to sport over 3 months under supervised physical therapist rehabilitation program","C":"Surgical removal of the apophyseal ossification center when conservative measures fail to improve symptoms within 2 weeks of initial conservative treatment initiation","D":"Corticosteroid injection into the calcaneal apophysis under ultrasound guidance combined with complete cessation of all athletic activity for 3 months minimum","E":"Platelet-rich plasma injection into the apophyseal growth plate combined with extracorporeal shockwave therapy as the preferred first-line treatment for all cases"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sever disease management begins with activity reduction (not complete cessation), heel cushioning inserts, Achilles tendon stretching, NSAIDs for acute pain, and footwear modification. Surgery is NEVER indicated for Sever disease, which is self-limited. Casting in equinus is not the initial approach. Corticosteroid injection is contraindicated at a pediatric growth plate. PRP is not an appropriate first-line therapy in pediatric growth plate conditions. The goal is symptom control with maintenance of activity as tolerated.","boardPrep":"Sever disease treatment: activity reduction (not complete cessation), heel cushioning inserts, Achilles stretching, cold compresses, NSAIDs, PT. Recalcitrant cases: short-term immobilization. Self-limited 3-4 years. Surgery NOT indicated. Corticosteroid injection NOT appropriate in pediatric growth plate conditions. Return to sport as symptoms allow with activity modification.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 7 — Sever disease: calcaneal apophysitis from Achilles traction on growth plate; males at 8yr, females at 6yr; bilateral in 2/3; self-limited 3-4 years"},{"id":"sdl7_q22","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which clinical presentation is most specific for common peroneal nerve injury at the level of the fibular head?","choices":{"A":"Burning pain along the medial calf with numbness at the medial knee and no identifiable motor deficits, consistent with saphenous nerve entrapment at the fascia level","B":"Decreased sensation over the dorsolateral foot and sinus tarsi with tenderness to percussion at the crural fascia proximal to the lateral malleolus without any weakness","C":"Burning superficial pain with decreased sensation at the anterolateral shin and a palpable fascial defect at the crural fascia just lateral to the fibula distally","D":"Medial knee pain with numbness extending to the medial calf without any foot weakness, from saphenous nerve compression at the medial knee fascial level on examination","E":"Burning and shooting pain radiating from the lateral knee to the dorsum of the foot and toe web spaces, with possible ankle dorsiflexion weakness or foot drop"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Common peroneal nerve injury at the fibular head produces burning and shooting pain from the lateral knee to the dorsal foot and web spaces, with possible foot drop from motor involvement (ankle dorsiflexion and toe extension weakness). Saphenous nerve entrapment causes medial knee and calf pain without any foot weakness. Superficial peroneal entrapment at the crural fascia produces only sensory symptoms (dorsolateral foot numbness) without motor deficit. Deep peroneal compression causes first web space numbness only.","boardPrep":"Common peroneal nerve: wraps around fibular neck at the fibular head. Injury from: cycling (hip flexion pressure), leg crossing, braces, casts, direct trauma. Motor: foot drop (ankle dorsiflexion, toe extension). Sensory: dorsum of foot, first through fourth web spaces, lateral lower leg. Tinel at fibular head. Most common cause of foot drop.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injuries: common peroneal (fibular head, foot drop), superficial peroneal (crural fascia, dorsolateral foot), saphenous (medial knee, no motor deficit)"},{"id":"sdl7_q23","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Where is the superficial peroneal nerve most commonly entrapped, and which symptom pattern does it produce?","choices":{"A":"At the crural fascia proximal to the lateral malleolus where the nerve exits the fascia, producing burning pain and decreased sensation at the sinus tarsi and dorsolateral foot","B":"At the fibular head where the nerve wraps around the lateral fibular neck, producing foot drop and toe web space numbness from direct pressure at the lateral knee level","C":"At the medial knee where the nerve passes through the fascia between the sartorius and gracilis tendons, producing medial lower leg paresthesias and medial knee pain","D":"At the tarsal tunnel posterior to the medial malleolus beneath the flexor retinaculum, producing plantar foot burning, numbness, and arch pain with prolonged standing","E":"At the anterior ankle beneath the extensor retinaculum, producing first web space numbness and pain with active dorsiflexion testing of the anterior compartment musculature"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The superficial peroneal nerve exits the crural fascia proximal to the lateral malleolus and is most commonly entrapped at this fascial exit point, producing burning pain and decreased sensation at the sinus tarsi and dorsolateral foot. It does NOT wrap around the fibular head — that is the common peroneal nerve. Superficial peroneal entrapment is PURELY SENSORY distally, distinguishing it from common peroneal injury which causes foot drop. Tarsal tunnel involves the tibial nerve posterior to the medial malleolus.","boardPrep":"Superficial peroneal nerve: entrapment at crural fascia proximal to lateral malleolus. Sports: bodybuilding, horse racing, running, soccer, tennis. Symptoms: burning superficial pain, decreased sensation at sinus tarsi and dorsolateral foot. Signs: Tinel at fascia, palpable fascial defect. Purely sensory distally — no foot drop (distinguishes from common peroneal).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injuries: common peroneal (fibular head, foot drop), superficial peroneal (crural fascia, dorsolateral foot), saphenous (medial knee, no motor deficit)"},{"id":"sdl7_q24","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which diagnostic study is most appropriate to confirm the diagnosis and localize the level and severity of lower extremity peripheral nerve entrapment?","choices":{"A":"MRI of the lower extremity with dedicated nerve protocol to evaluate for intraneural ganglion cysts or perineural fibrosis mechanically compressing the nerve trunk along its course","B":"Nerve conduction studies and electromyography to localize the level of nerve dysfunction and quantify whether the injury is primarily axonal or demyelinating in nature","C":"Ultrasound-guided injection of local anesthetic adjacent to the suspected nerve as the gold standard confirmatory test for definitively confirming peripheral nerve entrapment","D":"CT myelogram of the lumbar spine to exclude a radicular origin at the L4-S1 nerve roots as the source of lower extremity sensory and motor symptoms and changes","E":"Plain radiograph of the fibula to identify bony cortical abnormality at the fibular head contributing to common peroneal nerve compression and foot drop in the patient"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Nerve conduction studies and electromyography (NCS/EMG) are the definitive tests for peripheral nerve entrapment, localizing the level of dysfunction and characterizing whether injury is primarily axonal (amplitude reduction) or demyelinating (conduction velocity slowing). MRI with nerve protocol detects structural causes but does not provide electrophysiological characterization. Ultrasound-guided local anesthetic injection is diagnostic-therapeutic but not the gold standard for nerve injury characterization. CT myelogram evaluates the spinal canal for radicular pathology.","boardPrep":"Lower extremity nerve injuries: confirm with NCS and EMG to localize level and characterize injury (axonal vs demyelinating). Saphenous nerve: purely sensory — pain/numbness medial knee and calf, NO motor deficits. Common peroneal: motor (foot drop) + sensory. Superficial peroneal: mainly sensory (dorsolateral foot). Conservative: gait analysis, neuromodulators, TENS, PT, anti-inflammatories. Invasive: steroid injection, hydrodissection, surgical release.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injuries: common peroneal (fibular head, foot drop), superficial peroneal (crural fascia, dorsolateral foot), saphenous (medial knee, no motor deficit)"},{"id":"sdl7_q25","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial tibial stress syndrome (MTSS).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old female cross-country runner has 3 weeks of bilateral lower leg pain. Pain is diffuse along the posteromedial tibia, worsens with activity, and is present the following morning. She increased mileage from 30 to 50 miles/week over 2 weeks. Exam shows diffuse posteromedial tibial tenderness without focal point tenderness. X-rays are negative. Which diagnosis is most consistent?","choices":{"A":"Bilateral tibial stress fractures from acute mileage increase, requiring immediate non-weight bearing and orthopedic referral for management with focal point tenderness expected","B":"Bilateral exertional compartment syndrome causing elevated posterior compartment pressures with exercise, requiring compartment pressure measurement at rest and with exertion","C":"Bilateral posterior tibial vein deep venous thrombosis presenting as bilateral lower extremity pain in a young female athlete with increased training demands and activity level","D":"Bilateral periosteal hematomas from direct contact injuries during team cross-country training sessions causing localized posteromedial tibial tenderness and soft tissue swelling","E":"Medial tibial stress syndrome from repetitive posterior compartment traction periostitis triggered by rapid mileage increase and likely subtalar overpronation mechanics"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This clinical picture — diffuse posteromedial tibial pain bilaterally, worsening with activity and present the following morning, triggered by rapid mileage increase in a young female runner with negative X-rays — is classic MTSS. Bilateral tibial stress fractures would show focal point tenderness and require imaging confirmation. Exertional compartment syndrome symptoms occur during exercise and require compartment pressure measurement at rest and post-exercise. DVT and periosteal hematoma from contact do not fit this activity-related pattern.","boardPrep":"MTSS hallmark: diffuse (NOT focal) posteromedial tibial pain at junction of proximal 2/3 to distal 1/3. Pain decreases with warm-up, athlete can complete session, recurs after exercise and the following morning. Key differentiator from stress fracture: diffuse vs focal tenderness. Bone scan differentiates: MTSS = patchy diffuse vs stress fracture = focal hot spot.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — Hip overuse injuries: repetitive mechanical loading exceeding tissue adaptation capacity is the shared pathophysiologic basis of all overuse syndromes including MTSS; apply the 10% rule to prevent all overuse injuries"},{"id":"sdl7_q26","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial tibial stress syndrome (MTSS).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 22-year-old male Army recruit has bilateral lower leg pain after 4 weeks of basic training. Bone scan shows patchy, diffuse areas of increased uptake along the posteromedial tibia bilaterally without focal hot spots. The radiologist notes this differs from the expected appearance of a tibial stress fracture. Which finding most reliably differentiates MTSS from stress fracture on bone scan?","choices":{"A":"Diffuse patchy uptake spanning several centimeters in MTSS versus a focal, intense single-point hot spot at the fracture site in tibial stress fracture on nuclear scintigraphy","B":"Bilateral symmetric uptake in MTSS suggesting systemic metabolic disease, versus asymmetric unilateral single-bone uptake as the expected pattern in tibial stress fracture","C":"Posterior tibial cortex uptake in stress fracture versus anterior tibial cortex uptake in MTSS, distinguishing the two conditions by their anatomic cortical distribution","D":"Completely normal bone scan in MTSS with periosteal fluid on MRI only, while tibial stress fracture produces markedly abnormal bone scan with discrete focal uptake","E":"Metaphyseal uptake near the growth plate in MTSS versus diaphyseal uptake in stress fracture, distinguishing these conditions by the bony location of increased tracer uptake"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The distinguishing bone scan feature of MTSS versus tibial stress fracture is the distribution of uptake: MTSS shows patchy diffuse uptake spanning several centimeters along the periosteum, while stress fracture produces a focal intense single-point hot spot at the fracture site. Bilateral symmetry is not the distinguishing feature since both conditions can be bilateral. MTSS does produce an abnormal bone scan — a completely normal scan argues against MTSS.","boardPrep":"MTSS imaging: X-ray often negative; bone scan patchy diffuse uptake; MRI periosteal fluid and bone marrow signal. Stress fracture: X-ray may show cortical lucency/periosteal reaction late; bone scan = focal hot spot; MRI = discrete bone marrow edema band. Bone scan and MRI have similar sensitivity and specificity for MTSS diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 6 — Tibial shaft fractures: stress fractures are incomplete cortical fractures from repetitive submaximal loading; focal periosteal reaction on X-ray and focal hot spot on bone scan distinguish stress fracture from MTSS diffuse periostitis"},{"id":"sdl7_q27","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial tibial stress syndrome (MTSS).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 25-year-old female marathon runner recovers from MTSS after activity modification. Bilateral subtalar overpronation is confirmed on exam. She asks which modification most urgently reduces recurrence risk given the evidence base for MTSS risk factors. Which counseling point is best supported?","choices":{"A":"Transition entirely to barefoot running to eliminate heel strike and normalize posterior compartment loading mechanics during all future training and competition activities","B":"Increase training cadence above 180 steps per minute as the single most effective evidence-based intervention for reducing the risk of MTSS recurrence in distance runners","C":"Obtain custom orthotics proven by a randomized controlled trial to reduce MTSS recurrence by 60% in female runners with confirmed subtalar overpronation on physical exam","D":"Address subtalar overpronation with appropriate motion-control footwear or custom orthotics, as overpronation is the most consistently confirmed modifiable risk factor for MTSS","E":"Permanently reduce weekly mileage by 50% below the level at which symptoms developed, as mileage reduction is the only proven strategy for preventing future MTSS episodes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Overpronation is the most consistently confirmed modifiable risk factor for MTSS, so addressing it with motion-control footwear or custom orthotics is the best-supported prevention counseling given this runner's confirmed subtalar overpronation. Barefoot running is not evidence-based for MTSS prevention and may worsen the condition. Increased cadence has theoretical benefit but is not confirmed as the most effective intervention. Permanent 50% mileage reduction is unsupported — the 10% weekly increase rule is the appropriate training modification.","boardPrep":"MTSS prevention: address overpronation (strongest confirmed evidence). Apply 10% rule for mileage increase. Footwear modification for gait abnormalities. Cross training maintains fitness during recovery. Return to play: 2 weeks to 4 months with 10% weekly mileage rule. Surgery (superficial posterior compartment release): 70% success rate for refractory cases.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — MTSS: overpronation is the strongest confirmed modifiable risk factor; training errors such as rapid mileage increase and changes in terrain are probable contributors with less consistent evidence than biomechanical factors"},{"id":"sdl7_q28","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ankle sprain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 17-year-old male basketball player rolls his right ankle during practice. He has lateral ankle pain, ecchymosis, and is able to bear weight with pain. Anterior drawer test is mildly positive; talar tilt is negative. Ottawa ankle rules are negative. Which sprain grade and initial management plan is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Grade 2 lateral ankle sprain with incomplete ATFL tear; treat with PRICE therapy, brief non-weight bearing, short immobilization period, and early progressive rehabilitation","B":"Grade 1 lateral ankle sprain with microtears only; treat with PRICE and immediate full activity return without any immobilization or bracing because no instability is present","C":"Grade 3 lateral ankle sprain with complete ligament rupture; treat with surgery and 6 weeks of non-weight bearing in a full cast before initiating any rehabilitation program","D":"Syndesmotic ankle sprain requiring orthopedic referral for syndesmotic fixation and 8 weeks of non-weight bearing based on mechanism regardless of examination findings","E":"Medial ankle sprain of the deltoid complex requiring MRI confirmation and non-weight bearing before initiating any weight-bearing activity or rehabilitation for this patient"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A mildly positive anterior drawer (ATFL partially torn) with negative talar tilt (CFL intact), ecchymosis, and ability to bear weight with pain best fits Grade 2 lateral ankle sprain. Grade 1 has no instability on anterior drawer. Grade 3 has complete ligament rupture with strongly positive stress testing and often inability to bear weight. Ottawa rules were negative, excluding clinically significant fracture. Syndesmotic or medial sprains do not match the mechanism or this exam pattern.","boardPrep":"Ankle sprain grading: Grade 1 — stretching/microtears, minimal pain, no instability, no immobilization, early rehab. Grade 2 — incomplete tear, moderate swelling/ecchymosis, mild anterior drawer, brief NWB + immobilization, rehab. Grade 3 — complete tear, marked swelling in 1-2h, cannot bear weight, positive stress test, immobilization + rehab.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 7 — Ankle joint dislocations: severe ankle ligament disruption with dislocation requires immediate orthopedic evaluation; ankle sprains without dislocation are graded 1-3 based on ligament integrity and functional instability"},{"id":"sdl7_q29","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ankle sprain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old female soccer player sustains an ankle injury when her foot is planted and her leg externally rotates while the ankle dorsiflexes. Pain is above the ankle at the distal tibiofibular junction. The squeeze test is positive. No lateral ankle ligament tenderness is present. Which structure is most likely injured?","choices":{"A":"Anterior talofibular ligament and calcaneofibular ligament complex from classic inversion with plantarflexion mechanism causing the most common lateral ankle sprain pattern","B":"Anterior tibiofibular ligament of the syndesmosis, injured by dorsiflexion and external rotation mechanism producing a high ankle sprain above the ankle mortise","C":"Deltoid ligament complex from eversion and dorsiflexion mechanism causing medial ankle pain, often associated with avulsion fracture of the medial malleolus in athletes","D":"Posterior talofibular ligament from severe inversion at terminal plantarflexion causing posterior ankle pain and positive posterior drawer sign on physical examination testing","E":"Calcaneofibular ligament in isolation from pure inversion mechanism without plantarflexion, producing lateral ankle pain and a positive talar tilt test on examination"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Dorsiflexion combined with external rotation of the leg over a planted foot, producing pain at the distal tibiofibular junction with a positive squeeze test but no lateral ligament tenderness, is pathognomonic of a syndesmotic (high) ankle sprain of the anterior tibiofibular ligament. Lateral ligament injuries (ATFL/CFL) occur with inversion-plantarflexion. Deltoid injury occurs with eversion. PTFL injury from severe inversion causes posterior ankle pain, not proximal tibiofibular pain. Always palpate the proximal fibula to exclude Maisonneuve.","boardPrep":"Syndesmotic high ankle sprain: mechanism = dorsiflexion + external rotation. Structures: anterior tibiofibular ligament (AITFL), posterior tibiofibular ligament (PITFL), interosseous membrane. Tests: squeeze test (compress fibula at mid-calf, pain at distal tibiofibular joint), external rotation test. Harder to diagnose, longer recovery than lateral sprain. Always palpate proximal fibula to exclude Maisonneuve fracture.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 8 — Ankle fractures: Maisonneuve fracture = syndesmotic disruption + proximal fibula fracture from external rotation; always palpate proximal fibula when syndesmotic sprain is suspected on examination after ankle injury"},{"id":"sdl7_q30","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ankle sprain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 14-year-old female gymnast has medial ankle pain after landing from a dismount with her foot in eversion. X-ray shows an avulsion fracture of the medial malleolus. Which ligament injury is most likely responsible for the avulsion fracture mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Anterior talofibular ligament tear from inversion mechanism causing lateral ligament disruption and lateral ankle instability without medial ankle or malleolus involvement","B":"Calcaneofibular ligament rupture from combined inversion and plantarflexion during the landing, producing lateral ankle swelling and a positive talar tilt test on examination","C":"Anterior tibiofibular ligament of the syndesmosis torn from dorsiflexion and external rotation producing proximal pain above the ankle mortise after the gymnastics dismount","D":"Deltoid ligament complex injury from eversion and dorsiflexion mechanism, with the deltoid traction force avulsing the medial malleolus at its bony attachment","E":"Posterior talofibular ligament avulsion from severe inversion at terminal plantarflexion causing posterior lateral ankle pain and chronic instability after the gymnastics landing"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Eversion combined with dorsiflexion stresses the deltoid ligament complex, which can avulse the medial malleolus at its bony attachment. The deltoid is the medial stabilizer of the ankle and resists eversion forces. ATFL injuries from inversion and CFL injuries from inversion-plantarflexion do not produce medial malleolus avulsion. Syndesmotic injury from external rotation produces proximal pain at the tibiofibular junction, not medial malleolus avulsion fracture.","boardPrep":"Medial ankle sprain: mechanism = eversion and dorsiflexion. Deltoid complex injury: often with multiligamentous injury or medial malleolus avulsion fracture. Medial sprain incidence: 0.06/1000 athlete exposures (much rarer than lateral 0.93/1000). Treatment: PRICE, immobilization, non-weight bearing, orthopedic consult. Ottawa: medial malleolus posterior tenderness = X-ray required.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 8 — Ankle fractures: medial malleolus fracture from deltoid traction is a component of bimalleolar or trimalleolar fracture patterns in ankle injuries with eversion mechanism; Ottawa rules mandate imaging when medial malleolus tenderness is present"},{"id":"sdl7_q31","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of gastrocnemius strain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old male tennis player feels a sudden pop in his posterior left calf while lunging for a ball. He reports feeling \"hit in the leg.\" Tenderness is at the medial gastrocnemius musculotendinous junction. Dorsiflexion reproduces pain. No palpable defect is found. Thompson test is negative. Which grade and expected return-to-sport timeline is most consistent?","choices":{"A":"Grade 2 gastrocnemius strain with incomplete tear, active plantarflexion pain, loss of dorsiflexion ROM, and expected return to sport in 16 to 21 days with progressive rehabilitation","B":"Grade 3 gastrocnemius strain with complete rupture confirmed by Thompson positivity and palpable defect, requiring surgical repair with return 6 months after operative intervention","C":"Grade 1 gastrocnemius strain with microtears causing mild calf pain on unilateral calf raise only, with expected return to sport in 10 to 12 days with conservative management","D":"Achilles tendon rupture with positive Thompson sign and palpable gap at the tendon, requiring urgent orthopedic evaluation for either surgical repair or conservative casting","E":"Deep venous thrombosis of the posterior tibial vein mimicking gastrocnemius strain, requiring Doppler ultrasound and therapeutic anticoagulation rather than sport rehabilitation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Negative Thompson test and absent palpable defect with active plantarflexion pain and loss of dorsiflexion ROM confirm Grade 2 (incomplete tear), with expected return to sport in 16-21 days. Grade 3 requires a positive Thompson test and a palpable defect at the MTJ. Grade 1 produces only pain with single-leg calf raise with no loss of dorsiflexion. Achilles tendon rupture has a gap at the tendon proper (not the MTJ) with a positive Thompson test. DVT should be excluded but has a distinct clinical course.","boardPrep":"Gastrocnemius strain grade 2 vs 3: Grade 2 = incomplete tear, active plantarflexion pain, loss of dorsiflexion, Thompson negative, no palpable defect, RTS 16-21 days. Grade 3 = complete rupture, Thompson positive, palpable defect at MTJ, surgery, RTS 6 months. Achilles rupture: gap at tendon itself (not MTJ), Thompson positive.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: Thompson test distinguishes Achilles rupture (positive = no plantarflexion squeeze response) from gastrocnemius strain (Thompson negative); palpable defect location at the MTJ (gastrocnemius) vs tendon itself (Achilles) is the key anatomic distinguishing feature"},{"id":"sdl7_q32","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of gastrocnemius strain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old female masters runner has a sudden tearing sensation in her right calf while accelerating from a stop. Ultrasound shows a 2cm partial tear of the medial gastrocnemius head at the musculotendinous junction. She has pain with weight bearing and reduced dorsiflexion. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Immediate surgical exploration and primary repair of the medial gastrocnemius given the 2cm partial tear size confirmed on musculoskeletal ultrasound imaging assessment","B":"Ice, crutches for weight bearing assistance, heel lift to offload the calf, and progressive rehabilitation beginning with concentric then eccentric calf strengthening program","C":"Corticosteroid injection into the tear site under ultrasound guidance to reduce acute inflammation and facilitate earlier return to running activity in this masters athlete","D":"Equinus casting for 4 weeks in full plantarflexion to protect the healing musculotendinous junction from mechanical stretch forces during the acute tissue repair phase","E":"Platelet-rich plasma injection into the partial tear under ultrasound guidance as the current standard first-line treatment based on strong randomized controlled trial evidence"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Grade 2 (incomplete) gastrocnemius strain from a 2cm partial tear on ultrasound is managed conservatively with ice, crutches for weight-bearing assistance, heel lift, and progressive rehabilitation starting with concentric then eccentric calf strengthening. Surgery is not indicated for partial tears — only Grade III complete rupture warrants surgical exploration. Corticosteroid injection is contraindicated at an acute muscle tear site. Equinus casting is reserved for post-surgical Grade III repair management.","boardPrep":"Gastrocnemius strain Grade II conservative management: ice, TENS, crutches if needed, heel lift, gentle stretching, strengthening within 24h (concentric → add weight → eccentric calf lowering), low-impact cardio, myofascial techniques when pain-free, biomechanical correction. MSK ultrasound helpful for grading >Grade 1. Surgery reserved for Grade III only.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — Physical therapy: eccentric strengthening is a core modality for tendon and muscle injuries; progressive loading from concentric to eccentric is the standard rehabilitation sequence for calf injuries after gastrocnemius strain"},{"id":"sdl7_q33","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of gastrocnemius strain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old male soccer player has a Grade I gastrocnemius strain with pain on unilateral calf raise. He asks when he can return to full competitive soccer. Which return-to-sport timeline is most appropriate for Grade I gastrocnemius strain?","choices":{"A":"Return in 16 to 21 days following rehabilitation with progressive concentric and eccentric calf strengthening, as appropriate for Grade II gastrocnemius strain management","B":"Return in 6 months following surgical repair and sport-specific rehabilitation as appropriate for Grade III complete gastrocnemius rupture with complete defect at the MTJ","C":"Return in 10 to 12 days following ice, relative rest, heel lift, and progressive strengthening once pain on unilateral calf raise has fully resolved with exercise","D":"Return in 4 to 6 weeks contingent upon achieving a pain-free single-leg calf raise and full ankle dorsiflexion range of motion compared to the uninjured contralateral side","E":"Return in 3 to 5 days following acute swelling resolution because Grade I strains involve only microtears and do not require any significant time away from competitive sport"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Grade 1 gastrocnemius strain (microtears only, pain on single-leg calf raise) has an expected return-to-sport of 10-12 days once the athlete can perform a pain-free calf raise. The 16-21 day timeline applies to Grade 2. The 6-month timeline applies to Grade 3 post-surgery. A 4-6 week timeline is more consistent with Grade 2 criteria. A 3-5 day timeline is insufficient even for a Grade 1 strain and risks reinjury.","boardPrep":"Gastrocnemius strain return-to-sport timelines: Grade I = 10-12 days (pain-free calf raise), Grade II = 16-21 days (pain-free active plantarflexion, full dorsiflexion), Grade III = 6 months post-surgery. Always compare to contralateral side. Sport-specific activity progression before full return to competitive play.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — Gastrocnemius strain: Grade I returns in 10-12 days, Grade II in 16-21 days, Grade III in 6 months post-surgery; these timelines assume progressive pain-free rehabilitation with restoration of strength and ROM before sport clearance"},{"id":"sdl7_q34","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of posterior tibial tendon dysfunction/tendinitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old female nurse has 6 months of progressive medial ankle pain and visible flatfoot deformity. She cannot stand on her toes on the affected foot alone. Exam shows pes planus and forefoot abduction; the subtalar joint corrects passively to neutral. Which PTTD grade and management plan is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Grade 1 PTTD with tendon pain and swelling but intact function; treat with activity modification, analgesics, arch support orthotics, and supervised physical therapy program","B":"Grade 3 PTTD with fixed subtalar joint deformity; requires immediate orthopedic referral for calcaneal osteotomy and flexor digitorum longus tendon transfer procedure","C":"Grade 4 PTTD with valgus tibiotalar tilt within the ankle mortise requiring total ankle arthroplasty or tibiotalar arthrodesis as the definitive surgical management option","D":"Grade 2 PTTD with failed single-leg heel raise and flexible pes planus passively correctable; treat conservatively with activity modification, orthotics, and physical therapy","E":"Acute posterior tibial tendon rupture requiring urgent orthopedic surgical consultation for primary tendon repair and non-weight bearing immobilization in a boot or cast"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Failed single-leg heel raise combined with flexible pes planus (passively correctable to neutral subtalar alignment) = Grade 2 PTTD, which is managed conservatively with activity modification, orthotics, and physical therapy — the same as Grade 1. The key grade distinction is that Grade 2 has failed heel raise while Grade 1 preserves it. Grade 3 has a fixed (not passively correctable) subtalar deformity requiring orthopedic referral. Grade 4 involves valgus tibiotalar tilt.","boardPrep":"PTTD Grade 2: cannot single-leg heel raise + flexible pes planus + forefoot abduction (passively correctable). Treatment = same as Grade 1 (conservative). Grade 3 = fixed subtalar → ortho referral. Grade 4 = tibiotalar valgus → surgical. Too-many-toes sign from behind = classic Grade 2+.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — Hip osteoarthritis: progressive joint deformity from mechanical overload drives destruction; PTTD causes progressive flatfoot deformity from tendon insufficiency analogous to how hip OA causes progressive joint destruction from altered biomechanics"},{"id":"sdl7_q35","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of posterior tibial tendon dysfunction/tendinitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old male runner is diagnosed with Grade 1 PTTD. He has medial ankle tendon pain and swelling but can perform a single-leg heel raise. No foot deformity is present. Which management plan is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Refer immediately to orthopedic surgery for medial displacement calcaneal osteotomy and flexor digitorum longus tendon transfer as definitive Grade 1 PTTD management","B":"Administer a corticosteroid injection into the posterior tibial tendon sheath as the primary first-line treatment to reduce tendon inflammation in confirmed Grade 1 PTTD","C":"Activity modification, medial arch support orthotic, analgesics for pain control, and supervised physical therapy with progressive eccentric tibialis posterior strengthening","D":"Apply a short-leg non-weight-bearing cast for 8 weeks to allow complete posterior tibial tendon rest and healing before initiating any physical therapy or orthotics","E":"Order MRI of the ankle to confirm the diagnosis with imaging before initiating any activity modification or conservative treatment plan for this runner with Grade 1 PTTD"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Grade 1 PTTD is a clinical diagnosis requiring no MRI before starting treatment. Conservative management (activity modification, medial arch support, analgesics, PT) is the evidence-based first-line approach for Grades 1 and 2. Surgery is reserved for Grade 3 and 4 only. Corticosteroid injection carries tendon rupture risk and is not first-line. Long-leg non-weight-bearing casting is not appropriate for Grade 1 disease where the patient can still perform the heel raise.","boardPrep":"PTTD Grade 1: clinical diagnosis — no MRI required to initiate treatment. Conservative management (activity modification + orthotics + PT) is first-line for Grades 1 and 2. Corticosteroid injection: controversial, risk of tendon rupture. Surgery reserved for Grade 3-4 only. Physical therapy focuses on eccentric tibialis posterior strengthening and gait retraining.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — Physical therapy modalities: orthotic support, strengthening, and activity modification are evidence-based PT and rehabilitation interventions for tendon disorders including PTTD at all grades of severity"},{"id":"sdl7_q36","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of posterior tibial tendon dysfunction/tendinitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 42-year-old female with posterior tibial tendinitis asks about nitroglycerin patches after seeing them mentioned as a treatment option online. Which statement best reflects current evidence for nitroglycerin patches in tendinopathy management?","choices":{"A":"Nitroglycerin patches are proven in multiple high-quality randomized controlled trials to significantly accelerate tendon collagen synthesis and reduce pain scores reliably","B":"Nitroglycerin patches have been explored as an adjunctive therapy option for tendinopathy management but current evidence remains limited and results are inconsistent overall","C":"Nitroglycerin patches are strictly contraindicated in tendinopathy because nitric oxide increases local vasodilation and paradoxically worsens inflammatory pain over time","D":"Nitroglycerin patches are the recommended first-line adjunctive therapy in all major orthopedic and sports medicine tendinopathy clinical practice guidelines worldwide","E":"Nitroglycerin patches are reserved exclusively for post-surgical tendon repair failure and should not be used for any conservative tendinopathy management in any patient"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Nitroglycerin patches have been explored as an adjunctive option for tendinopathy but current evidence remains limited and results are inconsistent — they are listed in SDL 7 with acknowledged uncertainty, not as first-line therapy. They are not proven by multiple high-quality RCTs. They are not contraindicated (they have been studied in this indication). They are not endorsed as first-line by all major guidelines. The established first-line treatments for tendinopathy are eccentric physical therapy and orthotics.","boardPrep":"PTTD adjunctive therapies: corticosteroid injections (use caution, rupture risk), regenerative injections/PRP (investigational), nitroglycerin patches (limited/mixed evidence). SDL 7 lists all three as options without strong endorsement. For boards: eccentric PT and orthotics = established first-line. The adjunctive therapies have limited evidence and are used selectively.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 6 — Analgesics: topical agents including nitroglycerin patches, topical NSAIDs, and local anesthetics may have roles in musculoskeletal pain management; evidence quality varies significantly by agent and specific clinical indication"},{"id":"sdl7_q37","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Achilles tendinopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 33-year-old male recreational runner has 8 weeks of posterior heel pain specifically at the tendon-bone junction on the calcaneus. Pain is worst with first steps in the morning and during running. Tenderness is isolated to the insertion site with no mid-tendon tenderness. Which diagnosis is most consistent?","choices":{"A":"Mid-portion Achilles tendinopathy in the hypovascular zone 2-6cm proximal to the insertion with paratenon inflammation contributing to the morning stiffness and running pain","B":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis with inflammation of the bursa between the posterior calcaneus and the deep Achilles surface, worsened by dorsiflexion and confused with tendinopathy","C":"Plantar fasciitis from inflammation at the calcaneal origin of the plantar fascia, causing inferior heel pain and classic post-static dyskinesia on first steps in the morning","D":"Achilles tendon rupture at the insertion with a palpable gap and positive Thompson test distinguishing complete rupture from tendinopathy in a middle-aged recreational runner","E":"Insertional Achilles tendinopathy at the calcaneal enthesis where the tendon attaches to bone, presenting with insertion-site pain and stiffness worst at onset of activity"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tenderness specifically at the calcaneal enthesis (tendon-bone junction) with morning stiffness and worsening with activity = insertional Achilles tendinopathy. Mid-portion tendinopathy would be tender 2-6cm proximal to the insertion. Retrocalcaneal bursitis is deep to the Achilles, worsened by dorsiflexion, and not exclusively at the enthesis. Plantar fasciitis causes inferior heel pain worst with first morning steps. Achilles rupture would have a positive Thompson test or a palpable gap in the tendon.","boardPrep":"Insertional Achilles tendinopathy: at the calcaneal enthesis (tendon-bone junction). Morning stiffness, insertion tenderness, worse at onset of activity. Associated with Haglund deformity (posterosuperior calcaneal prominence). Mid-portion tendinopathy: 2-6cm proximal to insertion (hypovascular zone, most common site). Both worsen with increased activity. Distinguish from retrocalcaneal bursitis (dorsiflexion worsens, deep to tendon).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 6 — Retrocalcaneal bursitis: closely associated with insertional Achilles tendinopathy; distinguish by location of maximal tenderness (bursa = just anterior to Achilles at posterior calcaneus; insertional tendinopathy = at the tendon-bone junction at the calcaneal enthesis itself)"},{"id":"sdl7_q38","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Achilles tendinopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 29-year-old female distance runner with mid-portion Achilles tendinopathy is prescribed an eccentric loading protocol. She asks why eccentric exercises are specifically recommended over concentric-only exercises for tendinopathy rehabilitation. Which mechanism best explains the therapeutic benefit?","choices":{"A":"Eccentric loading acutely elevates local glucocorticoid levels within tendon tissue, suppressing inflammatory cytokine production and reducing tendon pain signaling pathways","B":"Eccentric loading applies mechanical tension that stimulates tenocyte mechanotransduction, promoting collagen synthesis, fiber reorganization, and tendon matrix remodeling","C":"Eccentric loading immediately relieves pain by activating descending inhibitory spinal pain control pathways activated by high-intensity mechanical input during the exercise","D":"Eccentric loading compresses and reduces the size of the retrocalcaneal bursa by mechanically displacing fluid, reducing bursitis-related pain components during activity","E":"Eccentric loading relaxes the paratenon sheath surrounding the Achilles tendon, reducing adhesion formation between the tendon and paratenon during plantarflexion movements"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Eccentric loading of the Achilles tendon stimulates tenocyte mechanotransduction, promoting collagen synthesis and reorganization of the disorganized tendon matrix in tendinopathy. This is the established mechanism underlying the Alfredson eccentric protocol. Eccentric loading does not acutely elevate local glucocorticoids. It does not work through descending pain inhibitory pathways. It does not compress the retrocalcaneal bursa or relax the paratenon sheath mechanically.","boardPrep":"Eccentric calf loading (Alfredson protocol): stand on step, raise on two feet, lower on single affected foot. Mechanism: mechanotransduction in tenocytes → collagen synthesis and fiber reorganization in the tendon matrix. Mid-portion responds better to eccentric loading; insertional may worsen with full ROM eccentric (avoid dorsiflexion past neutral in insertional disease). Multiple RCTs support eccentric loading as first-line PT.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — Physical therapy: eccentric exercise involves muscle-tendon unit contraction while lengthening; eccentric calf lowering places controlled tensile load on the Achilles, stimulating collagen remodeling in tendinopathy through tenocyte mechanotransduction"},{"id":"sdl7_q39","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Achilles tendinopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 50-year-old male with chronic mid-portion Achilles tendinopathy has had 3 months of eccentric exercises with incomplete improvement. He asks whether corticosteroid injection would speed his recovery. Which response best reflects current evidence regarding corticosteroid injection for Achilles tendinopathy?","choices":{"A":"Corticosteroid injection is the established gold standard first-line treatment for Achilles tendinopathy and should be offered before any physical therapy is attempted for this patient","B":"Corticosteroid injection directly into the Achilles tendon substance is safe and recommended before attempting any further physical therapy or rehabilitation program","C":"Corticosteroid injection for Achilles tendinopathy has uncertain long-term benefit and carries significant risk of tendon weakening and rupture with intratendinous injection","D":"Corticosteroid injection is beneficial only for insertional Achilles tendinopathy into the retrocalcaneal bursa and is absolutely contraindicated for mid-portion disease","E":"Corticosteroid injection is absolutely contraindicated for all forms of tendinopathy and may only be used after complete tendon rupture and surgical repair has fully healed"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Corticosteroid injection for Achilles tendinopathy has uncertain long-term benefit and carries significant risk of tendon weakening and rupture, particularly with intratendinous injection. SDL 7 explicitly notes \"Corticosteroid ??\" indicating uncertain evidence. It is not the gold standard first-line treatment — eccentric loading is. Intratendinous injection is specifically dangerous. Corticosteroid injection into the bursa (not the tendon) is appropriate for retrocalcaneal bursitis, but not for mid-portion tendinopathy.","boardPrep":"Corticosteroid injection in Achilles tendinopathy: NOT first-line. Risks include tendon rupture, especially intratendinous injection. Peritendinous injection (into paratenon) carries lower risk. Evidence: may provide short-term pain relief but inferior long-term outcomes vs eccentric loading. SDL 7 notes \"Corticosteroid ??\" indicating uncertainty. Contrast with retrocalcaneal bursitis: corticosteroid injection into the bursa is an appropriate adjunctive option.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 4 — Adverse effects of NSAIDs and corticosteroids: systemic and local corticosteroid effects include tendon collagen weakening and increased rupture risk; intratendinous injection carries the highest risk of subsequent Achilles tendon rupture"},{"id":"sdl7_q40","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of retrocalcaneal bursitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 44-year-old female runner has posterior heel pain worse with dorsiflexion. Tenderness is at the posterior calcaneus just anterior to the Achilles tendon with local swelling. No plantar heel tenderness is present. She was previously treated for plantar fasciitis without success. Which finding most reliably distinguishes retrocalcaneal bursitis from plantar fasciitis?","choices":{"A":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis produces lateral calcaneal tenderness worse with inversion stress while plantar fasciitis produces medial calcaneal tenderness at the plantar fascia enthesis","B":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis causes posterior calcaneal tenderness deep to the Achilles tendon worsened by dorsiflexion, while plantar fasciitis causes inferior heel pain worst with first steps","C":"Plantar fasciitis causes pain at the Achilles musculotendinous junction while retrocalcaneal bursitis causes pain exclusively at the plantar fascial calcaneal attachment point","D":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis causes pain exclusively at night while plantar fasciitis is exclusively a first-morning-step (post-static dyskinesia) symptom and not a nocturnal complaint","E":"Plantar fasciitis causes posterior ankle swelling anterior to the Achilles tendon while retrocalcaneal bursitis causes diffuse inferior heel pain with prolonged weight-bearing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis causes posterior calcaneal tenderness deep to the Achilles tendon worsened by dorsiflexion (which compresses the bursa between the calcaneus and the deep Achilles surface). Plantar fasciitis causes inferior/medial heel pain worst with first morning steps (post-static dyskinesia). These two conditions are frequently confused because both involve the heel, but the location of tenderness (posterior deep vs inferior) and the aggravating movement (dorsiflexion vs initial weight-bearing) clearly distinguish them.","boardPrep":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis vs plantar fasciitis: retrocalcaneal = posterior calcaneus deep to Achilles, dorsiflexion worsens (compresses bursa); plantar fasciitis = inferior/medial heel, worse first steps in morning (post-static dyskinesia), point tenderness at plantar fascia origin. SDL 7: retrocalcaneal bursitis \"often confused with plantar fasciitis.\" Dorsiflexion worsening = key distinguishing feature for bursitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: retrocalcaneal bursitis is highly associated with Achilles tendinosis and enthesopathy; distinguish from plantar fasciitis (inferior heel), Achilles tendinopathy (tendon mid-substance or insertion), and superficial calcaneal bursitis (skin-tendon interface irritated by shoe counter)"},{"id":"sdl7_q41","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of retrocalcaneal bursitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old male has retrocalcaneal bursitis confirmed on ultrasound. After 3 months of cold compresses, analgesics, footwear modification, and Achilles stretching, he has only partial improvement. Which is the most appropriate next management step?","choices":{"A":"Proceed directly to surgical bursectomy with calcaneal exostectomy as surgery is the definitive treatment and should be offered after any conservative management failure","B":"Begin a 2-week oral prednisone taper combined with 6 weeks of complete non-weight bearing immobilization to reduce residual bursal inflammation via systemic corticosteroid","C":"Administer platelet-rich plasma injection into the retrocalcaneal bursa as the current evidence-based second-line treatment endorsed by all major orthopaedic specialty societies","D":"Repeat diagnostic ultrasound to reassess the bursa size and vascularity before initiating any additional therapeutic intervention for this patient with refractory bursitis","E":"Offer corticosteroid injection into the retrocalcaneal bursa as an adjunctive treatment option for refractory cases that have failed initial conservative management"},"correct":"E","explanation":"After failure of initial conservative management (cold compresses, analgesics, footwear modification, Achilles stretching), the appropriate next step for retrocalcaneal bursitis is corticosteroid injection INTO the bursa — not into the Achilles tendon. Surgery is rarely indicated and not the next step after only 3 months of conservative management. Oral corticosteroid taper is not the standard approach for localized bursitis. PRP is not endorsed as the current second-line standard for retrocalcaneal bursitis.","boardPrep":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis management ladder: (1) Cold compresses, analgesics, footwear modification, Achilles stretching. (2) Corticosteroid injection INTO the bursa (appropriate, lower rupture risk than intratendinous). (3) Surgery — rarely indicated. Key: injection into the bursa is appropriate for refractory bursitis; injection INTO the Achilles tendon is contraindicated.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 6 — Retrocalcaneal bursitis: cold compresses, analgesics, footwear modification, and Achilles stretching are first-line; corticosteroid injection into the bursa is appropriate for refractory cases; surgery is rarely needed and reserved for truly refractory cases"},{"id":"sdl7_q42","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of retrocalcaneal bursitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 39-year-old female triathlete is diagnosed with retrocalcaneal bursitis. Ultrasound also shows concurrent insertional Achilles tendinopathy at the same site. She asks whether the two conditions are related. Which statement best reflects the pathophysiologic relationship between retrocalcaneal bursitis and Achilles tendinopathy?","choices":{"A":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis and insertional Achilles tendinopathy have entirely distinct and unrelated pathophysiologic mechanisms with no consistent clinical association between them","B":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis invariably develops as a late complication of insertional Achilles tendinopathy and only occurs after tendinopathy has been present for several years","C":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis is highly associated with Achilles tendinosis and enthesopathy of the calcaneus, as the bursa is mechanically stressed by the same disease process","D":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis always precedes Achilles tendinopathy and must be fully resolved before initiating any Achilles tendinopathy rehabilitation treatment in athletes","E":"Insertional Achilles tendinopathy exclusively affects athletes over age 60 while retrocalcaneal bursitis exclusively affects younger athletes under 30 years of age in sports"},"correct":"C","explanation":"SDL 7 explicitly states that retrocalcaneal bursitis is \"highly associated with Achilles tendinosis and enthesopathy of the calcaneus\" — the same mechanical forces that stress the Achilles tendon also compress the adjacent bursa between the thickened tendon and the calcaneus (or Haglund deformity). They do not have entirely unrelated mechanisms. The two conditions coexist frequently from early in the disease course and should be treated simultaneously with a unified rehabilitation approach.","boardPrep":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis: SDL 7 states \"inflammation highly associated with Achilles tendinosis, enthesopathy of the calcaneus.\" The bursa sits between calcaneus and Achilles — when the Achilles is thickened (tendinosis) or the calcaneus is irregular (enthesopathy/Haglund deformity), bursal compression increases. Treat both simultaneously. Haglund deformity = posterosuperior calcaneal prominence causing increased bursal compression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: insertional type involves the calcaneal enthesis; the retrocalcaneal bursa lies immediately adjacent to this enthesis and is compressed by the same Achilles forces, explaining their frequent coexistence on ultrasound or MRI"},{"id":"sdl7_q43","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of calcaneal apophysitis (Sever Disease).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An 8-year-old male soccer player has bilateral posterior heel pain worsening with running and jumping. He is skeletally immature. Exam reveals tenderness at the posteroinferior calcaneus bilaterally with pain on medial-lateral heel squeeze. X-ray shows fragmentation and sclerosis of the calcaneal apophysis. Which diagnosis is most consistent?","choices":{"A":"Plantar fasciitis at the calcaneal origin of the plantar fascia causing inferior heel pain in a skeletally immature athlete with high training volume in competitive soccer","B":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis between the calcaneus and Achilles tendon deep surface causing posterior heel pain worsened by ankle dorsiflexion in this young soccer player","C":"Iselin disease affecting the 5th metatarsal base apophysis from peroneus brevis traction causing lateral midfoot pain in an active skeletally immature pediatric athlete","D":"Calcaneal apophysitis (Sever disease) from repetitive Achilles tendon traction on the posteroinferior calcaneal growth plate in this skeletally immature male athlete","E":"Calcaneal stress fracture from repetitive impact loading causing posteroinferior calcaneal pain requiring immediate non-weight bearing and cast immobilization in a child"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bilateral posteroinferior calcaneal tenderness with apophyseal fragmentation on X-ray in an 8-year-old skeletally immature male athlete playing high-impact sport = Sever disease (calcaneal apophysitis). Plantar fasciitis causes inferior heel pain predominantly in adults. Retrocalcaneal bursitis occurs in adults posterior to the Achilles. Iselin disease affects the 5th metatarsal base apophysis (lateral midfoot). Calcaneal stress fracture would show a cortical lucency or fracture line, not apophyseal fragmentation.","boardPrep":"Sever disease diagnosis: clinical — skeletally immature child, posteroinferior calcaneal tenderness, pain with activity, bilateral in 2/3. Sever sign: medial-lateral heel squeeze is positive. X-ray NOT required but may show apophyseal fragmentation/sclerosis (can be normal variant). Distinguish from plantar fasciitis (older patients, inferior heel) and calcaneal stress fracture (imaging required, NWB).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — Osgood-Schlatter disease: apophysitis from repetitive traction on a growth plate is the shared mechanism; Osgood-Schlatter = tibial tubercle from patellar tendon traction; Sever disease = calcaneal apophysis from Achilles tendon traction in skeletally immature athletes"},{"id":"sdl7_q44","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of calcaneal apophysitis (Sever Disease).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 10-year-old female gymnast has bilateral Sever disease. Her parents ask about the expected course and long-term prognosis. Which statement is most accurate regarding the natural history of Sever disease?","choices":{"A":"Sever disease requires surgical resection of the apophyseal ossification center to achieve pain-free return to gymnastics and prevent long-term skeletal deformity of the calcaneus","B":"Sever disease resolves within 2 weeks with complete rest and will not recur upon returning to gymnastics if heel cushioning is consistently worn during all athletic activity","C":"Sever disease is a permanent condition that persists throughout adulthood and requires lifelong footwear modification and Achilles stretching to manage symptoms indefinitely","D":"Sever disease will inevitably progress to calcaneal apophyseal stress fracture requiring surgery if the child participates in any form of physical activity during treatment","E":"Sever disease is a self-limited condition that typically lasts 3 to 4 years until the calcaneal apophysis fuses with skeletal maturity, after which symptoms fully resolve"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sever disease is self-limited, resolving when the calcaneal apophysis fuses at skeletal maturity — a process that takes approximately 3-4 years after onset. Surgery is NEVER indicated because the condition resolves completely without intervention at maturity, leaving no permanent deformity or sequelae. It does not resolve in 2 weeks with rest, nor does it persist permanently into adulthood. Progression to stress fracture is not inevitable with appropriate activity modification and symptom management.","boardPrep":"Sever disease prognosis: self-limited, resolves when the calcaneal apophysis fuses at skeletal maturity (3-4 years). No permanent deformity or long-term sequelae. Surgery NOT indicated. Contrast: Legg-Calve-Perthes (AVN, can cause permanent hip deformity) vs Sever (resolves fully at maturity). Osgood-Schlatter is also self-limited at skeletal maturity. Sever disease = purely traction apophysitis with benign prognosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — Legg-Calve-Perthes disease: avascular necrosis of the femoral head in children is a distinct condition with potential for permanent deformity; unlike LCP, Sever disease resolves completely without sequelae once the calcaneal apophysis fuses at skeletal maturity"},{"id":"sdl7_q45","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of calcaneal apophysitis (Sever Disease).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 9-year-old male basketball player with Sever disease asks what management approach allows the safest return to basketball while controlling symptoms. His parents are concerned about his continued participation. Which management plan is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Immediate surgical referral for calcaneal apophyseal resection to achieve permanent pain relief and allow unrestricted immediate return to competitive basketball at full intensity","B":"Complete cessation of all athletic activity for 2 years until the calcaneal apophysis fuses, to prevent any further traction stress on the calcaneal growth plate cartilage","C":"Activity reduction, heel cushioning inserts, Achilles tendon stretching, NSAIDs for pain control, and footwear modification with progressive return to sport as symptoms allow","D":"Corticosteroid injection into the calcaneal apophysis under ultrasound guidance to eliminate growth plate inflammation and allow immediate return to competitive basketball","E":"Full below-knee walking cast immobilization for 6 weeks with strict non-weight bearing before any athletic activity of any kind is permitted for this pediatric patient"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Activity reduction, heel cushioning inserts, Achilles stretching, NSAIDs, and footwear modification represent the appropriate initial management of Sever disease allowing continued sport participation with modified load. Complete cessation for 2 years is excessive and not evidence-based. Surgery is never indicated. Corticosteroid injection is contraindicated in pediatric growth plate conditions. Full casting is reserved for recalcitrant cases unresponsive to first-line management.","boardPrep":"Sever disease management: activity reduction (not complete cessation), heel cushioning inserts, Achilles stretching, NSAIDs, footwear modification. Progressive return to sport as symptoms improve. Recalcitrant cases: short-term immobilization (walking boot). Corticosteroid injection and surgery are NOT appropriate for pediatric growth plate conditions. The goal is symptom control, not complete rest.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 7 — Sever disease: activity reduction, heel cushioning, Achilles stretching, cold compresses, NSAIDs, and PT are the cornerstones; recalcitrant cases may need short immobilization; surgery is never indicated and corticosteroid injection is contraindicated in growth plate conditions"},{"id":"sdl7_q46","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old male long-distance cyclist develops burning pain along the lateral lower leg and dorsum of his right foot over 3 months. He has numbness in the toe web spaces and occasional ankle dorsiflexion weakness. Tinel's sign is positive at the fibular head. Which nerve is most likely injured and what is the mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Superficial peroneal nerve compressed at the crural fascia proximal to the lateral malleolus, causing dorsolateral foot numbness without any ankle or toe motor weakness","B":"Saphenous nerve compressed at the medial knee fascia causing medial knee and medial calf pain with numbness but absolutely no ankle or foot motor weakness at all","C":"Deep peroneal nerve compressed at the anterior tarsal tunnel beneath the extensor retinaculum, causing first web space numbness between the first and second toe only","D":"Sural nerve compressed at the lateral ankle causing burning pain along the lateral foot and fifth toe with no motor involvement of any lower extremity musculature","E":"Common peroneal nerve injured at the fibular head from repetitive cycling pressure, causing dorsal foot and web space symptoms with possible foot drop from motor involvement"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Common peroneal nerve injury at the fibular head from repetitive cycling pressure causes burning pain radiating from the lateral knee to the dorsal foot and web spaces, with possible ankle dorsiflexion weakness or foot drop from motor involvement. Superficial peroneal entrapment at the crural fascia causes only sensory symptoms at the dorsolateral foot without any motor deficit. Saphenous nerve entrapment causes medial knee and calf pain without foot weakness. Sural nerve causes lateral heel and 5th toe symptoms.","boardPrep":"Common peroneal nerve: at fibular head, wraps around lateral fibular neck. Pressure from cycling (hip flexion position), leg crossing, braces, casts. Motor: foot drop (ankle dorsiflexion, toe extension). Sensory: dorsum of foot, web spaces 1-4, lateral lower leg. Tinel at fibular head. Most common cause of foot drop in lower extremity. SDL 7: \"repetitive inversion/eversion (running, cycling), braces, casts.\"","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — Carpal tunnel syndrome: median nerve entrapment at the wrist is the classic peripheral entrapment neuropathy paradigm; common peroneal nerve at the fibular head is the analogous high-yield lower extremity entrapment neuropathy causing foot drop"},{"id":"sdl7_q47","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 32-year-old female soccer player develops burning superficial pain along the lateral ankle and dorsolateral foot over 4 months. She has no foot drop and no weakness. Tenderness to percussion is proximal to the lateral malleolus. A small fascial defect is palpable at the same site. Which nerve injury is most consistent?","choices":{"A":"Common peroneal nerve injury at the fibular head causing lateral foot burning with variable ankle dorsiflexion weakness and positive Tinel sign at the fibular neck level","B":"Sural nerve entrapment at the lateral ankle causing posterolateral foot burning and numbness in the lateral heel and fifth toe without motor involvement in the leg","C":"Superficial peroneal nerve entrapment at the crural fascia proximal to the lateral malleolus, with palpable fascial defect at the nerve exit point through the fascia","D":"Saphenous nerve entrapment at the medial knee causing medial calf pain that radiates distally to the dorsal foot in a purely sensory distribution without weakness","E":"Deep peroneal nerve injury at the anterior tarsal tunnel causing first web space numbness and extensor hallucis longus weakness with anterior ankle tenderness on palpation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Superficial peroneal nerve entrapment at the crural fascia causes burning superficial pain along the lateral ankle and dorsolateral foot with no motor deficit (no foot drop), and a palpable fascial defect at the nerve exit point is a classic distinguishing finding. Common peroneal nerve injury at the fibular head would additionally cause ankle dorsiflexion weakness or foot drop. Sural nerve entrapment is posterior and lateral at the ankle. Saphenous nerve entrapment is medial with no foot weakness. Deep peroneal causes first web space numbness with EHL weakness.","boardPrep":"Superficial peroneal nerve: exits crural fascia proximal to lateral malleolus. Sports: bodybuilding, horse racing, running, soccer, tennis. Classic finding: palpable fascial defect where nerve herniates through fascia. Symptoms: burning superficial pain, decreased sensation at sinus tarsi and dorsolateral foot. Purely sensory distally — no foot drop (distinguishes from common peroneal). Tinel at crural fascia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow: peripheral nerve entrapment at fascial exit points is a common pattern; the superficial peroneal nerve exits the crural fascia just as the ulnar nerve exits at the cubital tunnel, and palpable fascial defects can be found at both sites"},{"id":"sdl7_q48","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 40-year-old male runner is diagnosed with saphenous nerve entrapment at the medial knee. He has pain and numbness in the medial knee region extending to the medial calf. He denies foot drop or ankle weakness. Which statement most accurately characterizes the saphenous nerve and its clinical features?","choices":{"A":"Saphenous nerve injury causes foot drop and decreased sensation over the lateral dorsum of the foot from compression of the nerve at the lateral fibular head on examination","B":"Saphenous nerve injury causes burning pain in the lateral ankle and dorsolateral foot without any motor deficits due to its purely sensory function along the lateral lower leg","C":"Saphenous nerve injury causes complete loss of ankle plantarflexion strength from gastrocnemius and soleus denervation as the nerve innervates the posterior calf compartment","D":"Saphenous nerve entrapment at the medial knee fascia causes pain and numbness at the medial knee and calf with no motor deficits because it is a purely sensory nerve branch","E":"Saphenous nerve injury causes wrist drop from compression at the medial elbow, as the saphenous nerve is the lower extremity equivalent of the radial nerve in the upper extremity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The saphenous nerve is the terminal sensory branch of the femoral nerve and is PURELY SENSORY — it carries no motor fibers. Entrapment at the medial knee fascia causes pain and numbness at the medial knee and medial calf without any motor deficit, distinguishing it from the common peroneal (motor + sensory, foot drop) and differentiating it from other lower extremity entrapment neuropathies. Foot drop, plantarflexion weakness, and lateral foot symptoms are all inconsistent with saphenous nerve pathology.","boardPrep":"Saphenous nerve: terminal sensory branch of the femoral nerve. Location: entrapment at medial knee fascia (between sartorius and gracilis). PURELY SENSORY — no motor deficit (key board fact). Symptoms: pain and numbness at medial knee and medial calf. Compare: common peroneal (motor + sensory, foot drop) vs superficial peroneal (sensory, dorsolateral foot) vs saphenous (purely sensory, medial knee/calf, NO motor).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — Upper extremity nerve injuries: axillary, radial, median, and ulnar nerves each carry both motor and sensory fibers; the saphenous nerve is purely sensory (femoral nerve branch) with no motor component, making it analogous to the medial cutaneous nerve of the forearm in the upper extremity"}]},{"sdlNumber":8,"title":"Pediatric MSK Conditions","questions":[{"id":"sdl8_q01","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of developmental dysplasia of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement most accurately describes the epidemiology and recommended screening strategy for developmental dysplasia of the hip (DDH)?","choices":{"A":"DDH occurs in 3-5 per 1000 live births; mild hip instability is found in 40%; bilateral in 37%; left > right; screening recommended at birth and every wellness visit until walking","B":"DDH occurs in 1 per 10,000 live births; mild instability in 10%; bilateral in 75%; right > left; screening recommended only once at birth by specialized pediatric orthopedics","C":"DDH occurs in 10-15 per 1000 live births; bilateral in 60%; right > left; screening with ultrasound is recommended for all infants between 2 and 4 months regardless of exam findings","D":"DDH occurs in 1 per 1000 live births; mild hip instability is found in 5%; unilateral in 90% of cases; screening is recommended only when clinical risk factors are identified at birth","E":"DDH occurs in 3-5 per 1000 live births; bilateral in 70% of cases; right > left side; screening is deferred until 6 months of age because physical exam before that age is unreliable"},"correct":"A","explanation":"DDH occurs in 3-5 per 1000 live births; mild hip instability is found in 40% of neonates (most resolve spontaneously); bilateral in 37%; left > right (due to left occiput anterior fetal positioning in most deliveries). Screening is recommended at birth and at every wellness visit until walking. The incidence is not 1 per 10,000 or 10-15 per 1000. Bilateral in only 37%, not 70%. Right is not more common than left.","boardPrep":"DDH epidemiology: 3-5/1000 live births. Mild instability: 40% (most resolve). Bilateral: 37%. Left > right (likely from left occiput anterior fetal position). Risk factors: female sex, breech position, family history, first-born, oligohydramnios. Screen at birth and every wellness visit until walking.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 1 — DDH: 3-5/1000; Ortolani (reduce), Barlow (dislocate); ultrasound <4mo, X-ray >4mo; Pavlik harness for <6mo; screen at birth and every wellness visit"},{"id":"sdl8_q02","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of developmental dysplasia of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement correctly differentiates the Ortolani maneuver from the Barlow maneuver in DDH examination?","choices":{"A":"Ortolani dislocates an unstable hip by applying posterior force with the hip adducted; Barlow reduces a dislocated hip by abducting while applying anterior force on the femoral head","B":"Ortolani reduces a dislocated hip (a clunk indicates the femoral head slipping back into the acetabulum); Barlow dislocates an unstable hip (posteriorly displaceable during the test)","C":"Ortolani tests hip abductor muscle strength in infants over 6 months; Barlow tests hip flexor tightness in infants under 3 months, both requiring radiographic confirmation of findings","D":"Ortolani is a dynamic ultrasound measurement of the acetabular angle; Barlow is a static clinical test that evaluates femoral head position relative to the acetabulum at rest","E":"Ortolani is performed with the hip in extension and external rotation; Barlow is performed with the hip in flexion and adduction to detect posterior hip joint laxity in infants"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ortolani is the REDUCTION maneuver: with the hip flexed and abducted while lifting the femoral head anteriorly, a palpable clunk indicates the dislocated femoral head slipping BACK INTO the acetabulum. Barlow is the DISLOCATION maneuver: adduction while applying posterior force causes the unstable femoral head to slip OUT. The descriptions are not reversed. Both are performed with the hip flexed at 90 degrees. Ortolani does not involve ultrasound measurement or hip extension.","boardPrep":"DDH physical exam: Ortolani = reduction maneuver (hip flexed 90°, abduct while lifting femoral head anteriorly; positive = clunk of femoral head reducing into acetabulum). Barlow = dislocation maneuver (hip flexed 90°, adduct while applying posterior force; positive = femoral head dislocating out posteriorly). Also: Galeazzi test (functional femoral shortening with hips flexed), Klisic test (>3 months), Trendelenburg gait (ambulatory).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 1 — DDH: 3-5/1000; Ortolani (reduce), Barlow (dislocate); ultrasound <4mo, X-ray >4mo; Pavlik harness for <6mo; screen at birth and every wellness visit"},{"id":"sdl8_q03","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of developmental dysplasia of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which imaging modality is recommended for the initial workup of developmental dysplasia of the hip, and what determines which modality is chosen?","choices":{"A":"MRI of the hip is the gold standard imaging modality for all ages regardless of the infant's age because it provides superior visualization of cartilaginous and bony hip anatomy","B":"Plain radiographs are preferred at all ages because they provide the most accurate measurement of the acetabular index and femoral head ossification center position in infants","C":"CT scan of the pelvis with radiation shielding is the preferred initial imaging modality to measure the acetabular index and confirm femoral head position in all ages","D":"Ultrasound is preferred before 4 months of age (cartilaginous femoral head not visible on X-ray); radiographs are preferred after 4 months when ossification centers appear on imaging","E":"Ultrasound should only be used after 6 months of age once the femoral head begins to ossify; radiographs with the hip in abduction are preferred for the first 6 months of life"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ultrasound is preferred before 4 months of age because the femoral head is cartilaginous and cannot be seen on plain X-ray. After 4 months, when the femoral head ossification center appears, plain radiographs become more informative and are preferred. MRI requires sedation and is reserved for complex cases, not first-line imaging. CT scan involves radiation and is not first-line. Ultrasound should NOT be deferred until 6 months — the 4-month threshold is the standard transition point.","boardPrep":"DDH imaging: ultrasound preferred before 4 months (femoral head is cartilaginous and not yet ossified — not visible on plain X-ray). Radiographs after 4 months when ossification centers appear. Hip ultrasound evaluates alpha angle (acetabular coverage) and femoral head position dynamically. Plain X-ray evaluates Shenton line, acetabular index, center-edge angle in older infants.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 1 — DDH: 3-5/1000; Ortolani (reduce), Barlow (dislocate); ultrasound <4mo, X-ray >4mo; Pavlik harness for <6mo; screen at birth and every wellness visit"},{"id":"sdl8_q04","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteogenesis imperfecta.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which molecular defect is responsible for the majority of osteogenesis imperfecta cases and what is the inheritance pattern of classical OI?","choices":{"A":"Defects in the production, processing, or use of Type 1 collagen account for approximately 85% of OI cases; classical OI (Sillence Types I-IV) is autosomal dominant","B":"Defects in Type 3 collagen synthesis account for 85% of OI cases; OI is exclusively autosomal recessive and the recessive form tends to be milder than the dominant form","C":"Defects in osteoclast function causing excessive bone resorption account for 85% of OI cases; OI is autosomal dominant with complete penetrance in all affected families","D":"Defects in bone matrix mineralization related to alkaline phosphatase deficiency account for 85% of OI cases; all forms of OI are autosomal recessive with variable expressivity","E":"Defects in fibrillin-1 causing abnormal connective tissue cross-linking account for 85% of OI cases; inheritance is X-linked recessive with severe phenotype in affected males"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Defects in the production, processing, or use of Type 1 collagen account for approximately 85% of OI cases (COL1A1 and COL1A2 mutations). Classical OI (Sillence Types I-IV) is autosomal dominant. Type 3 collagen defects cause Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, not OI. Osteoclast dysfunction underlies osteopetrosis. Alkaline phosphatase deficiency causes hypophosphatasia. Fibrillin-1 mutations cause Marfan syndrome. OI is not X-linked.","boardPrep":"OI molecular basis: Type 1 collagen defects in 85% (COL1A1, COL1A2 mutations). Classical OI = autosomal dominant. Autosomal recessive forms (collagen prolyl 3-hydroxylation complex: LEPRE1, CRTAP, PPIB) = more severe. Osteoblast function and bone matrix mineralization defects account for remaining cases. Type 1 collagen is the primary structural protein of bone, skin, and tendon.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — OI: Type 1 collagen defect (85%); classic triad = fragile bones + blue sclerae + early deafness; Sillence I-IV; bisphosphonates benefit trabecular bone most"},{"id":"sdl8_q05","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteogenesis imperfecta.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which Sillence type of osteogenesis imperfecta is associated with perinatal lethality and the most severe skeletal deformity?","choices":{"A":"Sillence Type I is the mildest form with blue sclerae, presenile hearing loss, and recurrent childhood fractures, but results in perinatal death in 30% of affected newborns","B":"Sillence Type IV is moderately severe with white sclerae, variable dentinogenesis imperfecta, and short stature, and causes perinatal death in 50% of affected newborns","C":"Sillence Type II is the perinatal lethal form, characterized by severe bone fragility with multiple fractures and deformities at birth that are incompatible with survival","D":"Sillence Type III is the progressive deforming form, always lethal in the perinatal period due to thoracic kyphoscoliosis and respiratory insufficiency from rib cage deformity","E":"Sillence Type V has calcifying interosseous membranes and hyperplastic callus formation after fractures, and is lethal in the perinatal period from cardiac involvement"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sillence Type II is perinatal lethal, with multiple fractures in utero, accordion rib deformities, and marked bone fragility incompatible with survival beyond the neonatal period. Type I is the mildest form — patients survive with recurrent childhood fractures and blue sclerae. Type III is the most severe non-lethal type — patients survive but with progressive deformity and often wheelchair dependence. Type IV is moderately severe with white sclerae. None of the other types are perinatal lethal as a rule.","boardPrep":"Sillence OI Classification: Type I (mild) — blue sclerae, recurrent fractures, presenile hearing loss (30-60%), least severe, normal stature. Type II (perinatal lethal) — multiple fractures in utero, accordion ribs, incompatible with life. Type III (progressive deforming) — most severe non-lethal; short stature; scleral hue varies; dentinogenesis imperfecta and hearing loss common. Type IV (moderate) — white sclerae, variable dentinogenesis imperfecta. Classic triad: fragile bones + blue sclerae + early deafness.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — OI: Type 1 collagen defect (85%); classic triad = fragile bones + blue sclerae + early deafness; Sillence I-IV; bisphosphonates benefit trabecular bone most"},{"id":"sdl8_q06","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteogenesis imperfecta.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which pharmacologic therapy is used in osteogenesis imperfecta and where in the skeleton does it exert the greatest benefit?","choices":{"A":"Teriparatide (PTH analog) is the primary bone-targeted therapy for OI and exerts its greatest benefit in cortical bone at long bone diaphyses rather than in trabecular bone","B":"Bisphosphonates (IV pamidronate) are used in OI treatment and are more beneficial for vertebrae (trabecular bone) than for cortical long bones in children with OI","C":"Denosumab is the preferred first-line agent for OI and exerts equal beneficial effects on cortical and trabecular bone, reducing fracture rates in all skeletal compartments equally","D":"High-dose calcium and vitamin D supplementation are the established first-line pharmacologic treatments for OI, exerting their greatest benefit at the cortical long bone level","E":"Romosozumab is the recommended bone-targeted therapy for OI and exerts its greatest benefit at cortical long bone diaphyses rather than at the vertebral trabecular bone level"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bisphosphonates (IV pamidronate or zoledronate) reduce fracture rates and improve vertebral morphology in OI, with greater benefit demonstrated for trabecular (vertebral) bone than for cortical long bone diaphyses. Prolonged use may paradoxically weaken long bone material properties and increase nonunion risk. Bisphosphonates do NOT correct the underlying collagen defect. They do not provide equal benefit to all skeletal compartments. They are not the only required therapy — multidisciplinary care is essential.","boardPrep":"OI management: multidisciplinary approach — PT, bone-targeted therapies, surgical intervention, dental care, hearing/growth/cardiac monitoring. Bisphosphonates (IV pamidronate or zoledronate): reduce fracture rate, improve vertebral shape (trabecular bone benefit), may weaken long bone material properties with prolonged use. Bisphosphonates more beneficial for vertebrae (trabecular) than cortical long bones. Bisphosphonates reduce scoliosis incidence.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — OI: Type 1 collagen defect (85%); classic triad = fragile bones + blue sclerae + early deafness; Sillence I-IV; bisphosphonates benefit trabecular bone most"},{"id":"sdl8_q07","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of talipes equinovarus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The CAVE mnemonic describes the four components of talipes equinovarus. Which answer correctly identifies all four components?","choices":{"A":"Calcaneus (hindfoot in excessive dorsiflexion), Adduction (forefoot angled laterally), Valgus (foot everted outward), Extension (ankle held in maximal dorsiflexion at rest)","B":"Contracture (of the Achilles tendon), Articular (joint deformity), Varus (foot inverted), Extension (limited plantarflexion caused by shortened anterior compartment muscles)","C":"Cavus (abnormally high lateral arch), Abduction (forefoot angled laterally), Valgus (foot everted), Equinus (ankle held in excessive plantarflexion from intrinsic muscle shortening)","D":"Cavus (abnormally high medial arch), Adduction (forefoot angled medially), Varus (foot inverted), Equinus (foot limited in dorsiflexion) — each component reflecting a specific deformity plane","E":"Curvature (of the spine), Adduction (hip adduction contracture), Varus (knee varus deformity), Extension (limited knee flexion from posterior capsular fibrosis and contracture)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The CAVE mnemonic for clubfoot describes: Cavus (high medial arch), Adduction (forefoot angled medially), Varus (hindfoot inverted), Equinus (limited ankle dorsiflexion). Calcaneus means excessive dorsiflexion — the opposite of equinus. Valgus means eversion — the opposite of varus. Abduction means lateral deviation — the opposite of adduction. The correction order in Ponseti casting follows the CAVE mnemonic from distal to proximal.","boardPrep":"Clubfoot (CAVE): Cavus = abnormally high medial arch (midfoot); Adduction = forefoot angled medially (forefoot/midfoot); Varus = foot inverted (hindfoot); Equinus = limited dorsiflexion (hindfoot/ankle). Mnemonic: CAVE = correction order from distal to proximal in Ponseti casting. The hindfoot is in varus and equinus; forefoot/midfoot in cavus and adductus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — Clubfoot (CAVE): Cavus, Adduction, Varus, Equinus; idiopathic 80% (rigid); Ponseti method = standard (5-10 serial casts, Achilles tenotomy 80%, FAB brace)"},{"id":"sdl8_q08","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of talipes equinovarus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement best describes the Ponseti method of clubfoot treatment and its expected outcomes?","choices":{"A":"The Ponseti method involves daily casting changes to achieve rapid correction of all four CAVE components simultaneously, followed by immediate full weight-bearing without bracing","B":"The Ponseti method involves a single-stage surgical correction of all four CAVE components in the operating room, followed by 6 weeks of casting and then functional rehabilitation","C":"The Ponseti method involves the French functional method using physiotherapy and taping; Achilles tenotomy is avoided; bracing is not required; outcomes equal surgical release","D":"The Ponseti method involves 2-3 serial casts for positional clubfoot only; idiopathic and syndromic clubfoot always require primary surgical release for acceptable long-term outcomes","E":"The Ponseti method involves weekly serial casting to correct deformities in CAVE order; 5-10 casts are required; Achilles tenotomy is performed in approximately 80% of cases; outcomes are excellent at up to 40 years of follow-up"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The Ponseti method uses weekly serial casting to correct deformities in CAVE order (cavus first, then adductus, then varus, then equinus), requiring 5-10 casts; Achilles tenotomy is performed in approximately 80% of cases to correct residual equinus; foot abduction brace (FAB) is worn for 4 years. The method does NOT use daily cast changes. It does NOT begin with a foot abduction brace. It has replaced extensive soft tissue release surgery as the gold standard with excellent 40-year follow-up outcomes.","boardPrep":"Ponseti method: standard treatment for idiopathic clubfoot. Weekly serial casts correcting CAVE order (cavus first, then adductus, then varus, then equinus). 5-10 casts typically required. Achilles tenotomy performed in ~80% of cases (to correct residual equinus). Foot abduction brace (FAB, Dennis-Brown bar) worn for 4 years. Excellent results at 40-year follow-up. Gold standard — preferred over extensive soft tissue release surgery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — Clubfoot (CAVE): Cavus, Adduction, Varus, Equinus; idiopathic 80% (rigid); Ponseti method = standard (5-10 serial casts, Achilles tenotomy 80%, FAB brace)"},{"id":"sdl8_q09","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of talipes equinovarus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which characteristic best differentiates positional (postural) clubfoot from idiopathic congenital clubfoot?","choices":{"A":"Positional clubfoot always requires surgical correction because it is rigid and cannot be corrected with serial casting; idiopathic clubfoot is flexible and resolves with stretching only","B":"Positional clubfoot is associated with chromosomal anomalies and neuromuscular syndromes such as arthrogryposis; idiopathic clubfoot has no genetic component and is always unilateral","C":"Positional clubfoot is flexible (passively correctable to neutral), caused by intrauterine crowding; idiopathic clubfoot is rigid (not passively correctable) due to intrinsic structural abnormalities","D":"Positional clubfoot shows all four CAVE components on examination; idiopathic clubfoot shows only equinus and varus without cavus or adductus as the deformity is less complete","E":"Positional clubfoot is more common in males by a 4:1 ratio; idiopathic clubfoot is more common in females and requires immediate surgical repair in the first week of life"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Positional (postural) clubfoot is FLEXIBLE — passively correctable to neutral dorsiflexion and eversion — caused by intrauterine crowding, and treated with parental stretching with excellent prognosis. Idiopathic clubfoot is RIGID — not passively correctable — and requires Ponseti serial casting. The descriptions are not reversed. Positional clubfoot does not require surgery. Syndromic clubfoot (associated with chromosomal or neuromuscular disorders) is rigid and more treatment-resistant. The 4:1 male predominance applies to idiopathic type.","boardPrep":"Clubfoot types: Idiopathic (80%) — isolated, rigid, intrinsic structural/vascular foot abnormality, requires Ponseti casting. Syndromic — rigid, associated with chromosomal/neuromuscular conditions (trisomy 18, myelodysplasia, arthrogryposis), more resistant to treatment. Positional — flexible (passively correctable), caused by inadequate intrauterine space, resolves with stretching. KEY: rigid vs flexible determines treatment approach.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — Clubfoot (CAVE): Cavus, Adduction, Varus, Equinus; idiopathic 80% (rigid); Ponseti method = standard (5-10 serial casts, Achilles tenotomy 80%, FAB brace)"},{"id":"sdl8_q10","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of metatarsus adductus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement most accurately describes metatarsus adductus and its relationship to other pediatric musculoskeletal conditions?","choices":{"A":"Metatarsus adductus is the second most common congenital foot deformity after clubfoot; it has no association with DDH; affects children from 2 to 5 years and is more common in males","B":"Metatarsus adductus is the most common congenital foot deformity; it is more common in singleton pregnancies; it has a strong association with spinal dysraphism in affected children","C":"Metatarsus adductus is more common in females than males by a 3:1 ratio; it occurs in 7 per 1000 live births in Caucasian infants and always requires surgical correction by age 2 years","D":"Metatarsus adductus is the most common congenital foot deformity; it is more common in twins; some studies show an association with DDH; affects infants typically under 1 year of age","E":"Metatarsus adductus is equally common in all populations; it has no known association with DDH; it is caused exclusively by tibial torsion rather than forefoot adduction deformity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Metatarsus adductus is the most common congenital foot deformity; it is more common in twins (intrauterine crowding); some studies show an established association with DDH (shared etiology of intrauterine molding); and it typically presents in infants under 1 year. It is not second to clubfoot in incidence. The DDH association is established, not with spinal dysraphism. It is not exclusively caused by tibial torsion — forefoot adduction is the primary deformity with a neutral hindfoot.","boardPrep":"Metatarsus adductus: most common congenital foot deformity (incidence difficult to determine due to mild cases). More common in twins (intrauterine crowding). Associated with DDH (both caused by intrauterine molding). Usually in infants <1 year. Causes: intrauterine molding, anatomic variant. Foot: forefoot adduction, hindfoot neutral. Heel bisection line: normally through 2nd web space; in metatarsus adductus, line passes lateral to 2nd web space.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — Metatarsus adductus: most common congenital foot deformity; heel bisection line lateral to 2nd web space; flexible = stretching; recalcitrant = casting; association with DDH"},{"id":"sdl8_q11","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of metatarsus adductus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which physical examination finding on a bisection line drawn along the heel is most characteristic of metatarsus adductus?","choices":{"A":"The bisection of the heel falls medial to the 1st toe, indicating severe forefoot abduction and hindfoot valgus consistent with a rocker-bottom flatfoot deformity of the foot","B":"The bisection of the heel falls between the 3rd and 4th toes, indicating severe midfoot abduction with hindfoot varus consistent with a rigid clubfoot deformity in the infant","C":"The bisection of the heel falls through the center of the 2nd toe, which is the normal finding; deviation to the 1st web space indicates mild metatarsus adductus in newborns","D":"The bisection of the heel falls medial to the 2nd web space, indicating the normal alignment of the forefoot relative to the hindfoot in a newborn infant during examination","E":"The bisection of the heel falls lateral to the 2nd web space, indicating medial deviation of the forefoot relative to the hindfoot which is maintained in neutral alignment"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In metatarsus adductus, the bisection of the heel longitudinally falls LATERAL to the 2nd web space, because the forefoot is deviated medially (adducted) relative to the neutral hindfoot. The normal bisection passes through the 2nd web space. A line medial to the 1st toe would represent severe forefoot abduction (opposite deformity). A line between the 3rd and 4th toes would suggest a more severe varus pattern. Medial to the 2nd web space is the normal finding, not metatarsus adductus.","boardPrep":"Metatarsus adductus exam: bisect the heel longitudinally — normal line passes through 2nd web space. In metatarsus adductus: bisection line falls LATERAL to 2nd web space (forefoot is deviated medially). Hindfoot is neutral (distinguish from clubfoot where hindfoot is also in varus and equinus). Assess flexibility: gentle abduction pressure — flexible = correctable (good prognosis); rigid = requires more aggressive treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — Metatarsus adductus: most common congenital foot deformity; heel bisection line lateral to 2nd web space; flexible = stretching; recalcitrant = casting; association with DDH"},{"id":"sdl8_q12","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of metatarsus adductus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which treatment approach for metatarsus adductus is based on the rigidity of the deformity?","choices":{"A":"All cases of metatarsus adductus require immediate serial casting from birth regardless of flexibility; surgery is performed if casting fails after 6 months of conservative treatment","B":"Flexible metatarsus adductus requires immediate surgical metatarsal osteotomy at age 6 months; rigid metatarsus adductus resolves spontaneously with walking without any treatment","C":"Flexible deformity is treated with stretching and parental instruction; recalcitrant cases require serial casting; surgery is rarely indicated and reserved for severe persistent cases","D":"Rigid metatarsus adductus is treated with stretching and reverse-last shoes; flexible metatarsus adductus requires immediate surgical correction to prevent in-toe gait in adulthood","E":"Metatarsus adductus always resolves spontaneously without any intervention by age 3 years; no treatment is recommended for any degree of flexibility or rigidity of the deformity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Treatment of metatarsus adductus is based on deformity flexibility: flexible deformity is treated with parental stretching exercises at each diaper change; recalcitrant cases receive serial casting; surgery is rare and reserved for severe rigid persistent deformity. All cases do NOT require immediate casting regardless of flexibility. Rigid deformity does not resolve spontaneously with walking. Most mild-to-moderate flexible cases resolve with stretching alone, and the treatment escalation is stepwise.","boardPrep":"Metatarsus adductus treatment: (1) Stretching — gentle forefoot abduction stretching by parents at each diaper change for flexible deformity; (2) Casting — for recalcitrant flexible and mild-moderate rigid cases; (3) Surgery — rarely indicated, for severe rigid persistent deformity usually after age 4. Most mild-moderate cases resolve with stretching. SDL 8 states: \"Stretching → Recalcitrant: Casting → Rare: Surgery.\"","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — Metatarsus adductus: most common congenital foot deformity; heel bisection line lateral to 2nd web space; flexible = stretching; recalcitrant = casting; association with DDH"},{"id":"sdl8_q13","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of spinal dysraphism.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement correctly differentiates myelomeningocele (open spinal dysraphism) from spina bifida occulta (closed spinal dysraphism)?","choices":{"A":"Myelomeningocele has an opening in both the vertebral column and the skin with meninges and spinal cord exposed; spina bifida occulta has a vertebral defect without skin opening and neural tissue is not exposed","B":"Myelomeningocele has an opening only in the skin without vertebral defect; spina bifida occulta has an opening in the vertebral column with meninges exposed but skin is intact over the defect","C":"Myelomeningocele is the milder form with only meningeal protrusion through the vertebral defect without cord involvement; spina bifida occulta involves complete cord exposure and paralysis","D":"Myelomeningocele involves only cerebrospinal fluid leakage through a skin defect without spinal cord exposure; spina bifida occulta involves full cord herniation with meningeal protrusion","E":"Myelomeningocele has no vertebral or skin defect but causes cerebellar herniation below the foramen magnum; spina bifida occulta is defined by herniation through both vertebral and skin defects"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Myelomeningocele (open spinal dysraphism) has an opening in both the vertebral column and the skin with meninges and spinal cord exposed — obvious at birth, requiring immediate surgical closure. Spina bifida occulta (closed) has a vertebral defect without skin opening; neural tissue is not exposed and there may only be skin stigmata. The two conditions are not reversed. Meningocele is intermediate — meninges herniate through the vertebral defect but the spinal cord itself is not exposed. Chiari II is a complication of myelomeningocele, not a subtype of spinal dysraphism.","boardPrep":"Spinal dysraphism types: Myelomeningocele (open/spina bifida aperta) = opening in vertebral column AND skin; meninges and spinal cord are exposed; obvious at birth; requires immediate surgical closure. Spina bifida occulta (closed) = vertebral column defect WITHOUT skin opening; neural tissue not exposed; may have skin stigmata (tuft of hair, dimple, hemangioma); often incidental finding. Meningocele = meninges herniate through vertebral defect but cord not involved (between the two).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 5 — Spinal dysraphism: myelomeningocele = open (cord exposed); spina bifida occulta = closed (skin intact); folic acid deficiency + valproate = causes; Chiari II + hydrocephalus = key complications"},{"id":"sdl8_q14","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of spinal dysraphism.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which etiologic factors are most strongly associated with the development of neural tube defects including spinal dysraphism?","choices":{"A":"Maternal diabetes mellitus and obesity are the only confirmed causes of neural tube defects; dietary folate intake has no proven relationship to neural tube defect prevalence or severity","B":"Retinoid (vitamin A derivative) exposure and alcohol consumption are the only confirmed environmental causes; folic acid supplementation has no effect on neural tube defect incidence","C":"Maternal hypothyroidism and gestational hypertension are the most common preventable causes; folate supplementation is recommended only if there is a previous affected pregnancy","D":"Folic acid deficiency during the periconceptional period and valproate (antiepileptic drug) exposure during early pregnancy are the most strongly implicated environmental causes","E":"Paternal smoking and advanced paternal age are the most strongly implicated etiologic factors; maternal nutritional deficiencies have not been shown to increase neural tube defect risk"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Folic acid deficiency during the periconceptional period and valproate (antiepileptic) exposure during early pregnancy are the most strongly implicated environmental causes of neural tube defects. Folic acid supplementation (400-800mcg) reduces NTD risk by 50-70%. Maternal diabetes and obesity are risk factors but are not the only confirmed causes. Retinoids are teratogenic, but the claim that folate has no effect is false. Paternal factors are not the primary established NTD risk factors.","boardPrep":"Spinal dysraphism etiology: majority are isolated with multifactorial origin. Key causes: (1) Folic acid deficiency — folic acid 400-800mcg supplementation periconceptionally reduces NTD risk by 50-70%. (2) Valproate (antiepileptic) — 10-20x increased NTD risk. Also: chromosomal disorders, other anticonvulsants (carbamazepine). Epidemiology: 1:2875 live births US; Hispanic highest rate (3.80:10,000); AA 2.73:10,000; Caucasian 3.09:10,000.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 5 — Spinal dysraphism: myelomeningocele = open (cord exposed); spina bifida occulta = closed (skin intact); folic acid deficiency + valproate = causes; Chiari II + hydrocephalus = key complications"},{"id":"sdl8_q15","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of spinal dysraphism.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which complication of myelomeningocele is characterized by herniation of the cerebellar tonsils and brainstem through the foramen magnum?","choices":{"A":"Tethered cord syndrome involves downward herniation of the cerebellum through the foramen magnum and causes progressive lower extremity weakness and bladder dysfunction","B":"Hydromyelia involves herniation of the cerebellar tonsils through the foramen magnum and is the most common complication of all types of spinal dysraphism including spina bifida occulta","C":"Chiari II malformation (Arnold-Chiari type II) involves downward herniation of the cerebellum and brainstem through the foramen magnum and is a characteristic complication of myelomeningocele","D":"Syringomyelia involves cerebrospinal fluid accumulation within the brainstem causing compression of cranial nerve nuclei and herniation of brainstem tissue into the foramen magnum","E":"Basilar invagination involves downward herniation of the cerebellar vermis through the foramen magnum and is the most frequent complication of myelomeningocele in neonates"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Chiari II malformation (Arnold-Chiari type II) involves downward herniation of the cerebellum and brainstem through the foramen magnum and is a near-universal complication of myelomeningocele. It causes CSF flow obstruction, contributing to hydrocephalus. Tethered cord causes progressive lower extremity weakness but does NOT involve foramen magnum herniation. Hydromyelia is an intramedullary CSF cyst. Syringomyelia is intramedullary fluid. Basilar invagination is upward (odontoid migration), not downward cerebellar herniation.","boardPrep":"Myelomeningocele complications: Chiari II malformation (almost universal in MMC — herniation of cerebellum/brainstem through foramen magnum; causes hydrocephalus, breathing problems, swallowing difficulties). Hydrocephalus (requires VP shunt in most). Tethered cord. Hydromyelia. Seizures. Learning disabilities. Urinary/bowel dysfunction. Skin ulcers. Orthopedic problems (paralysis level-dependent). Prenatal diagnosis: elevated maternal AFP + ultrasound (lemon sign/banana sign on fetal head).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 5 — Spinal dysraphism: myelomeningocele = open (cord exposed); spina bifida occulta = closed (skin intact); folic acid deficiency + valproate = causes; Chiari II + hydrocephalus = key complications"},{"id":"sdl8_q16","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of developmental dysplasia of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 2-week-old female infant, firstborn, delivered vaginally in breech presentation, is examined at her first wellness visit. On hip examination, when the hip is flexed 90° and abducted while the examiner lifts the femoral head anteriorly, a palpable clunk is felt. Which finding is this and what does it indicate?","choices":{"A":"Positive Ortolani sign indicating reduction of a previously dislocated femoral head back into the acetabulum; this infant requires ultrasound evaluation and pediatric orthopedic referral","B":"Positive Barlow sign indicating that the examiner has dislocated an unstable femoral head posteriorly out of the acetabulum; this is normal in newborns and requires no further workup","C":"Positive Galeazzi sign indicating functional shortening of the femur caused by a higher resting position of the femoral head on the affected side with the hips in a flexed position","D":"Positive Klisic test indicating that the line connecting the greater trochanter and the anterior superior iliac spine points above the umbilicus due to proximal femoral displacement","E":"Positive Thomas test indicating a hip flexion contracture from intrauterine positioning; this resolves spontaneously and does not require imaging or orthopedic referral in newborns"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lifting the femoral head anteriorly while abducting the hip with a palpable clunk is the Ortolani sign, indicating reduction of a previously dislocated femoral head back into the acetabulum. Barlow involves adduction with posterior force (dislocation maneuver). Galeazzi tests for functional femoral shortening (unequal knee heights). Klisic tests for proximal femoral displacement via a palpated line. Thomas test assesses hip flexion contracture. This infant with multiple DDH risk factors (female, firstborn, breech) requires ultrasound and pediatric orthopedic referral.","boardPrep":"DDH risk factors: female sex, firstborn, breech presentation, family history, oligohydramnios. Ortolani = REDUCTION of a dislocated hip (positive = clunk). Barlow = DISLOCATION of an unstable hip (positive = hip subluxes/dislocates). Both performed with hip flexed 90°. Positive exam → ultrasound if <4 months, X-ray if >4 months → pediatric orthopedics referral. Pavlik harness is first-line treatment for <6 months.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 5 — SCFE: both SCFE and DDH are high-yield pediatric hip conditions; DDH presents in infancy with instability; SCFE presents in adolescence with hip/knee pain and limited internal rotation; both require urgent orthopedic referral to prevent avascular necrosis"},{"id":"sdl8_q17","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of developmental dysplasia of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 6-month-old male with limited left hip abduction and asymmetric thigh skin creases is suspected to have DDH. His pediatrician orders imaging. Which imaging modality is most appropriate for this patient at 6 months of age and why?","choices":{"A":"Hip ultrasound is the preferred modality at 6 months because the femoral head is still purely cartilaginous at this age and therefore cannot be visualized on plain radiography","B":"MRI of the hip with sedation is preferred at 6 months of age because it provides the most comprehensive assessment of both bony and cartilaginous structures of the acetabulum","C":"CT scan of the pelvis is preferred at 6 months because it provides the most accurate measurement of the acetabular index and femoral head coverage with precise quantification","D":"Dynamic arthroscopy of the hip is preferred at 6 months because physical examination alone at this age is unreliable for distinguishing DDH from normal developmental hip laxity","E":"Plain radiograph of the pelvis is preferred at 6 months of age because the femoral head ossification center is now visible on X-ray, making radiographs more accurate than ultrasound"},"correct":"E","explanation":"At 6 months of age, the femoral head ossification center is clearly visible on plain radiograph, making X-ray the appropriate and preferred imaging modality. The 4-month threshold is the standard transition from ultrasound to radiograph: before 4 months, the cartilaginous femoral head is not seen on X-ray; after 4 months, plain radiograph is more informative. MRI requires sedation and is not first-line for routine DDH workup. CT scan involves radiation. Arthroscopy is surgical, not initial diagnostic imaging.","boardPrep":"DDH imaging timeline: Before 4 months → ULTRASOUND (femoral head is cartilaginous, not visible on X-ray; ultrasound evaluates acetabular morphology and femoral head position dynamically). After 4 months → PLAIN RADIOGRAPH (ossification center appears, enabling accurate measurement of Shenton line, acetabular index, and center-edge angle). At 6 months = clearly after the 4-month threshold → plain radiograph is appropriate.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — Hip pain diagnostic workflow: systematic imaging selection based on clinical presentation and age is critical; DDH imaging selection (ultrasound vs X-ray) follows a developmentally-based algorithm tied to femoral head ossification timing"},{"id":"sdl8_q18","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of developmental dysplasia of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 16-month-old boy began walking at 14 months. His parents report he has an unusual gait. Exam shows a Trendelenburg gait, prominent right greater trochanter, shortened right leg, and hyperlordosis. Ortolani and Barlow tests are negative at this age. Which diagnosis is most likely and what is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Legg-Calve-Perthes disease presenting with avascular necrosis of the femoral head; initiate non-weight bearing immediately and order MRI to assess the degree of femoral head involvement","B":"Developmental dysplasia of the hip presenting in an ambulatory toddler; order an anteroposterior pelvis radiograph and refer urgently to pediatric orthopedics for evaluation","C":"Slipped capital femoral epiphysis causing a valgus deformity of the femoral head; obtain radiographs in the frog-leg lateral view and refer to orthopedics for immediate surgical pinning","D":"Cerebral palsy causing spastic hip adductor contracture and gait abnormality; refer to neurology for EMG testing and initiate physical therapy with botulinum toxin injection","E":"Normal developmental gait variation in a toddler; reassure the parents that hyperlordosis and mild leg length discrepancy are expected findings in newly ambulatory children at this age"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Trendelenburg gait, prominent greater trochanter, shortened leg, and hyperlordosis in a 16-month-old walking toddler are classic signs of undiagnosed ambulatory DDH. Ortolani and Barlow maneuvers become negative in ambulatory children because the hip capsule tightens. LCP (avascular necrosis) presents between ages 4-8 with hip pain, not Trendelenburg gait from birth. SCFE affects adolescents with hip and knee pain and limited internal rotation. Normal developmental variation does not explain a shortened leg with prominent greater trochanter.","boardPrep":"DDH in ambulatory children: Ortolani/Barlow are negative once the hip capsule tightens. Presentation: Trendelenburg gait (positive Trendelenburg sign = hip abductor weakness on affected side), prominent greater trochanter, shortened leg, hyperlordosis, out-toeing, toe-heel walking. SDL 8 states these as ambulatory DDH findings. Imaging: plain X-ray. Undiagnosed DDH → early OA, chronic pain. Early referral to Pediatric Orthopedics is emphasized in SDL 8.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — Legg-Calve-Perthes disease: AVN of the femoral head in children 4-8yr with insidious hip pain and antalgic gait; distinguishes from DDH by age of presentation and radiographic findings of femoral head fragmentation rather than acetabular dysplasia"},{"id":"sdl8_q19","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteogenesis imperfecta.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 3-year-old girl presents with multiple long bone fractures following minimal trauma. Her sclerae appear distinctly blue. She has small, discolored teeth. Her mother reports she has had 6 fractures in her lifetime. Her father also had multiple childhood fractures. Skeletal survey shows osteopenia with cortical thinning. Which diagnosis and Sillence type best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Osteogenesis imperfecta Type II (perinatal lethal), characterized by multiple in-utero fractures and accordion rib deformity; survival beyond the neonatal period essentially never occurs","B":"Osteogenesis imperfecta Type III (progressive deforming), characterized by the most severe non-lethal course; sclerae vary from blue to white; most affected individuals are wheelchair-bound","C":"Osteogenesis imperfecta Type I (mild), characterized by blue sclerae, recurrent childhood fractures with minimal trauma, dentinogenesis imperfecta, and autosomal dominant inheritance","D":"Osteogenesis imperfecta Type IV (moderately severe), characterized by white sclerae, variable dentinogenesis imperfecta, short stature, and autosomal dominant inheritance pattern","E":"Child abuse (non-accidental trauma), which must be excluded before diagnosing any skeletal dysplasia; blue sclerae and dental abnormalities are not features of osteogenesis imperfecta"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Multiple long bone fractures from minimal trauma combined with blue sclerae, dentinogenesis imperfecta, and autosomal dominant family history in a 3-year-old = OI Type I (mild). Type II is perinatal lethal — this 3-year-old child is alive. Type III is the most severe non-lethal type with patients often wheelchair-bound; sclerae vary. Type IV has white sclerae and is moderately severe. Child abuse must always be excluded, but blue sclerae, dentinogenesis imperfecta, positive family history, and diffuse osteopenia are strongly suggestive of OI.","boardPrep":"OI Type I (mild): blue sclerae + recurrent fractures in childhood + presenile hearing loss (30-60%). Family history often positive (AD). Dentinogenesis imperfecta in some. Normal or near-normal stature. KEY diagnostic triad: fragile bones + blue sclerae + early deafness. OI vs NAT: OI can mimic child abuse; blue sclerae, dentinogenesis imperfecta, positive family history, and osteopenia help distinguish. Always consider OI when fractures are unexplained or disproportionate to trauma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — Pediatric vs adult bone: pediatric bone has greater porosity, periosteal vascularity, and growth plate; OI disrupts collagen-based bone matrix globally; Salter-Harris fractures in OI are complicated by the underlying bone fragility and abnormal collagen scaffold"},{"id":"sdl8_q20","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteogenesis imperfecta.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A genetics counselor explains the inheritance of osteogenesis imperfecta to a family. The affected child has a new (de novo) pathogenic variant in COL1A1. The parents are unaffected. Which statement is most accurate regarding OI inheritance?","choices":{"A":"Classical OI (Sillence Types I-IV) is autosomal dominant; de novo mutations are common; autosomal recessive forms affect genes in the collagen prolyl 3-hydroxylation pathway and tend to be more severe","B":"OI is exclusively autosomal recessive; both parents must carry one pathogenic variant to have an affected child; the recessive form is always milder than autosomal dominant variants","C":"OI is X-linked recessive; only males are severely affected; female carriers have mild bone fragility; the COL1A1 gene is located on the X chromosome and encodes alpha-1 collagen chains","D":"OI follows a codominant inheritance pattern; all forms have equal severity regardless of the number of pathogenic variants inherited; de novo mutations are extremely rare in OI","E":"OI is mitochondrial in inheritance; transmission is exclusively maternal; paternal contribution has no effect on OI severity or phenotype in any of the known molecular subtypes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Classical OI (Sillence Types I-IV) is autosomal dominant; de novo COL1A1/COL1A2 mutations are common, explaining why an affected child can have two unaffected parents. Autosomal recessive OI forms (LEPRE1, CRTAP, PPIB mutations affecting collagen prolyl 3-hydroxylation) tend to produce more severe phenotypes. OI is NOT exclusively autosomal recessive. It is NOT X-linked recessive — males and females are equally affected by classical forms. It is NOT mitochondrial in inheritance.","boardPrep":"OI genetics: Classical OI (Types I-IV) = autosomal dominant (COL1A1, COL1A2 mutations). De novo mutations common — unaffected parents can have affected child. Autosomal recessive forms = LEPRE1, CRTAP, PPIB (collagen prolyl 3-hydroxylation complex) → more severe phenotype. Type V = autosomal dominant (IFITM5). Parental mosaicism can cause recurrence risk even when parents appear unaffected. Genetic counseling is essential.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — Teratogens and abnormal MSK development: genetic mutations (AD, AR) cause intrinsic developmental abnormalities; OI results from dominant negative or haploinsufficiency mechanisms in Type 1 collagen synthesis analogous to how teratogens disrupt normal developmental signaling"},{"id":"sdl8_q21","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteogenesis imperfecta.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 7-year-old boy with osteogenesis imperfecta Type III is started on IV pamidronate. His parents ask about the expected benefits of bisphosphonate therapy. Which statement most accurately reflects the evidence for bisphosphonates in OI?","choices":{"A":"Bisphosphonates improve the primary defect in Type 1 collagen synthesis, correcting the underlying molecular abnormality and preventing further fractures in all skeletal compartments","B":"Bisphosphonates provide equal benefit to cortical and trabecular bone in OI, reducing fracture rates at all sites including long bones more effectively than at the vertebral spine","C":"Bisphosphonates are the only therapy for OI and are sufficient as monotherapy; no physical therapy, surgical intervention, or monitoring of hearing or growth is required additionally","D":"Bisphosphonates provide no benefit in OI because the primary defect is in collagen quality rather than bone turnover; they are contraindicated because they accelerate bone fragility","E":"Bisphosphonates reduce fracture incidence and improve vertebral morphology in OI; they are more beneficial for trabecular (vertebral) bone than cortical long bones; prolonged use may weaken long bone material properties"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Bisphosphonates reduce fracture incidence and improve vertebral morphology in OI, with greater efficacy demonstrated for trabecular (vertebral) bone than for cortical long bone diaphyses; prolonged use may weaken long bone material properties and increase nonunion risk. They do NOT correct the underlying collagen synthesis defect. They do NOT provide equal benefit to cortical and trabecular bone — vertebrae benefit more. They are part of multidisciplinary care, not monotherapy. They are not contraindicated in OI.","boardPrep":"OI bisphosphonate therapy: IV pamidronate (or zoledronate) — reduces fracture rate, improves vertebral shape (trabecular bone benefit). More beneficial for vertebrae (trabecular) than long bones (cortical). Prolonged use may weaken long bone material properties and increase nonunion risk after osteotomy. Reduces scoliosis incidence. Does NOT correct the underlying collagen defect. Multidisciplinary care also includes PT, surgical fixation of long bones (IM rodding), dental exams, hearing monitoring, and cardiac surveillance.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 4 — Adverse effects of pharmacologic agents: bisphosphonates cause osteonecrosis of the jaw with dental procedures, atypical femoral fractures with long-term use, and rebound bone loss after discontinuation — all relevant to OI management planning"},{"id":"sdl8_q22","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of talipes equinovarus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A newborn male is delivered at term and found to have bilateral rigid clubfoot on examination. The foot shows high medial arch, forefoot adduction, hindfoot inversion, and limited ankle dorsiflexion. The deformity is not passively correctable. Which is the most appropriate first step in management?","choices":{"A":"Schedule immediate extensive soft tissue release surgery (posteromedial release) in the first week of life to achieve complete correction before the tissues become too fibrotic","B":"Apply a rigid foot abduction brace (Dennis-Brown bar) immediately at birth without prior casting, worn 23 hours per day for 4 years to correct all deformity components","C":"Observe without treatment for 3-6 months to determine whether the deformity will resolve spontaneously, as most rigid clubfoot cases achieve spontaneous correction by 6 months","D":"Initiate the Ponseti method with serial casting beginning within the first week of life, correcting deformities in CAVE order with weekly cast changes for 5-10 weeks","E":"Perform genetic testing and karyotype analysis before initiating any orthopedic treatment, as all rigid clubfoot requires syndromic workup to guide the appropriate treatment plan"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Rigid bilateral idiopathic clubfoot requires Ponseti serial casting beginning in the first week of life, correcting deformities in CAVE order with weekly cast changes. Immediate extensive soft tissue release surgery (posteromedial release) is the historical approach that Ponseti has replaced as the standard of care. Foot abduction brace alone without prior casting is incorrect — the brace is applied AFTER casting. Rigid clubfoot will NOT resolve spontaneously. Genetic testing is not required before initiating treatment for isolated bilateral clubfoot.","boardPrep":"Clubfoot management: Ponseti method is the standard first-line treatment for idiopathic clubfoot — start EARLY (first week of life). Serial casting in CAVE correction order (cavus first → adductus → varus → equinus). 5-10 weekly casts. Achilles tenotomy in ~80% to correct residual equinus. Foot abduction brace (FAB/Dennis-Brown) 23h/day for 3 months, then nights and naps for 4 years. NEVER start with FAB alone. Early surgery is NOT first-line; Ponseti method has replaced extensive soft tissue release.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — Fracture nomenclature and casting: serial casting and cast immobilization are orthopedic techniques applied across multiple pediatric MSK conditions; Ponseti casting for clubfoot uses the same principles of controlled mechanical force over sequential intervals to achieve progressive correction"},{"id":"sdl8_q23","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of talipes equinovarus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 3-week-old with idiopathic clubfoot has completed 8 serial Ponseti casts. The cavus, adductus, and varus components are fully corrected, but 20 degrees of equinus deformity persists. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Perform an Achilles tenotomy (percutaneous) to correct the residual equinus deformity; this is performed in approximately 80% of idiopathic clubfoot cases treated with Ponseti method","B":"Apply a posterior night splint with the foot in maximum dorsiflexion for 3 months to stretch the Achilles tendon and correct the residual equinus without any surgical intervention","C":"Continue serial casting for an additional 10 weeks with the foot in progressive dorsiflexion to achieve correction of residual equinus without any tenotomy or surgical procedure","D":"Proceed with open surgical posteromedial release to address the residual equinus as this component always requires surgical correction when casting has not fully achieved correction","E":"Fit the foot abduction brace immediately without addressing the residual equinus, as the brace will passively stretch the Achilles tendon and correct the equinus over time alone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Residual equinus after correction of cavus, adductus, and varus with casting is treated with percutaneous Achilles tenotomy in approximately 80% of idiopathic clubfoot cases. A posterior splint alone cannot overcome a contracted Achilles tendon. Additional casting cannot achieve what the tenotomy provides for residual equinus. Open posteromedial release is not indicated when only equinus persists — targeted percutaneous tenotomy is the appropriate procedure. The foot abduction brace cannot passively correct an unresolved equinus deformity.","boardPrep":"Ponseti method — Achilles tenotomy: performed in ~80% of idiopathic clubfoot cases when residual equinus persists after casting is complete (cavus, adductus, and varus corrected). Percutaneous tenotomy under local anesthesia in clinic or OR. Final cast applied for 3 weeks post-tenotomy to allow healing in corrected position. Then → foot abduction brace. Tenotomy avoids the need for extensive open soft tissue surgery. Long-term outcomes are excellent at 40-year follow-up.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the Achilles tendon is the most powerful plantarflexor; in clubfoot, a contracted Achilles is the primary cause of residual equinus; percutaneous tenotomy achieves correction that serial casting alone cannot overcome in most cases"},{"id":"sdl8_q24","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of talipes equinovarus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A newborn girl is noted to have a foot that appears plantarflexed and inverted. On examination, the foot is easily passively correctable to neutral dorsiflexion and slight eversion with gentle manipulation. No resistance is felt. Which type of clubfoot is this and what is the appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Idiopathic congenital clubfoot which is rigid and not passively correctable; begin Ponseti serial casting immediately and expect to need 5-10 cast changes before achieving correction","B":"Positional (postural) clubfoot caused by intrauterine crowding; the deformity is flexible and passively correctable; treatment is parental stretching exercises with excellent prognosis","C":"Syndromic clubfoot associated with chromosomal or neuromuscular abnormalities; order genetic studies and echocardiogram before initiating any treatment for this condition","D":"Calcaneovalgus foot deformity caused by tibialis anterior overactivity; this deformity is flexible but requires serial casting and cannot be corrected with stretching alone","E":"Metatarsus adductus deformity with isolated forefoot adduction; this is caused by tibiotarsal torsion rather than intrauterine crowding and requires early casting treatment"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A passively correctable foot that easily reaches neutral dorsiflexion and slight eversion with no resistance = positional (postural) clubfoot from intrauterine crowding, treated with parental stretching exercises and carrying an excellent prognosis. Idiopathic clubfoot is rigid and not passively correctable — requires Ponseti casting. Syndromic clubfoot is also rigid with associated anomalies. Calcaneovalgus foot is in excessive dorsiflexion and eversion (opposite deformity). Metatarsus adductus affects only the forefoot with a neutral hindfoot.","boardPrep":"Positional clubfoot: flexible (passively correctable), caused by intrauterine crowding/molding (not structural). Treatment = stretching exercises by parents. Excellent prognosis — resolves spontaneously or with minimal intervention. KEY DISTINCTION: idiopathic clubfoot = RIGID (not passively correctable) → Ponseti casting required. Syndromic clubfoot = rigid + associated anomalies (chromosomal, neuromuscular) → more resistant to treatment. Positional = flexible = no casting needed.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — Metatarsus adductus: another pediatric foot deformity caused by intrauterine molding; flexible positional metatarsus adductus also resolves with stretching; distinguishing flexible from rigid deformity determines treatment approach for all congenital foot conditions"},{"id":"sdl8_q25","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of metatarsus adductus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 4-month-old infant is noted to have bilateral medial deviation of the forefoot during a well-child visit. The hindfoot is in neutral alignment. On examination, both feet are fully correctable to neutral with gentle lateral abduction pressure. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Apply bilateral serial casts immediately regardless of flexibility, as all metatarsus adductus deformities require casting to prevent in-toe gait and shoe wear problems in childhood","B":"Refer immediately to pediatric orthopedics for surgical metatarsal osteotomy to correct the flexible forefoot adduction before 6 months of age to prevent long-term gait abnormality","C":"Order bilateral lower extremity radiographs to quantify the metatarsus adductus angle before initiating any treatment or counseling the family about prognosis or management","D":"Apply bilateral reverse-last shoes immediately to reverse the forefoot adduction passively over 6 months, as parental stretching alone is insufficient for all metatarsus adductus cases","E":"Teach parents forefoot abduction stretching exercises at each diaper change; flexible metatarsus adductus has an excellent prognosis and typically resolves without further intervention"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Bilateral flexible metatarsus adductus (fully correctable with gentle lateral pressure) in a 4-month-old is treated with parental forefoot abduction stretching at each diaper change; most cases resolve without further intervention. Serial casting is not indicated for completely flexible deformity at this age. Surgery is not indicated for flexible deformity. Radiographs are not required before initiating treatment for a clinical diagnosis of flexible metatarsus adductus. Reverse-last shoes may be an option but parental stretching is the appropriate first step.","boardPrep":"Metatarsus adductus treatment algorithm: Flexible (passively correctable) → stretching by parents at every diaper change; most resolve spontaneously. Rigid (not correctable) → serial casting. If not corrected by age 3-4 years and causing symptoms → surgery (rare). SDL 8 explicitly states: \"Stretching → Recalcitrant: Casting → Rare: Surgery.\" Reassurance is key for flexible cases; excellent prognosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — Talipes equinovarus: rigid clubfoot also begins management with serial casting (Ponseti method); the key principle is that deformity flexibility determines treatment; flexible metatarsus adductus (stretching) vs rigid clubfoot (Ponseti casting) exemplifies this principle"},{"id":"sdl8_q26","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of metatarsus adductus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"During a neonatal hip screening examination, a 2-week-old is found to have both metatarsus adductus of the left foot and a positive Barlow test on the left hip. Which statement best reflects the relationship between these two findings?","choices":{"A":"Metatarsus adductus and DDH are completely unrelated conditions; the co-occurrence of these findings is coincidental and does not require any additional workup beyond standard screening","B":"Metatarsus adductus always causes DDH by applying lateral forces on the developing acetabulum during intrauterine life; correcting the foot deformity will resolve the hip instability","C":"DDH always causes metatarsus adductus through altered lower extremity alignment; the hip disorder is the primary diagnosis and the foot deformity will resolve once DDH is treated","D":"Metatarsus adductus and DDH are both associated with intrauterine molding and there is an established association between the two conditions, warranting hip imaging for infants with metatarsus adductus","E":"Metatarsus adductus is a protective factor for DDH because intrauterine molding forces that cause the foot deformity simultaneously keep the hip in a favorable flexed position"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Metatarsus adductus and DDH share a common etiology of intrauterine crowding and molding, and an established association between the two conditions has been documented in some studies. The co-occurrence is not coincidental — it warrants careful hip screening when metatarsus adductus is found. Metatarsus adductus does NOT cause DDH mechanically, nor does DDH cause metatarsus adductus. Both result from the same constraint of inadequate intrauterine space affecting different anatomic regions simultaneously.","boardPrep":"Metatarsus adductus and DDH: both caused by intrauterine crowding/molding. SDL 8 states: \"Some studies show association with DDH.\" Studies have shown calcaneovalgus foot and metatarsus adductus are associated with increased DDH risk. Clinical implication: when metatarsus adductus is found, screen carefully for DDH and vice versa. The common etiology is intrauterine positioning constraints affecting both the hip and foot simultaneously.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — DDH: intrauterine molding is a shared etiologic factor for DDH and metatarsus adductus; both conditions result from inadequate intrauterine space during development, particularly in breech position, multiple gestations, and oligohydramnios"},{"id":"sdl8_q27","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of metatarsus adductus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 10-month-old has metatarsus adductus that has not responded to 4 months of parental stretching. On examination, the forefoot deviation is still present and is only partially correctable with firm lateral abduction pressure. The walking-age child is beginning to bear weight. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Proceed directly to surgical metatarsal osteotomy since the deformity has failed 4 months of stretching; surgery is indicated for all cases failing stretching at any age in early childhood","B":"Initiate serial casting to apply progressive corrective force to the partially flexible metatarsus adductus deformity that has failed stretching, as casting is indicated for recalcitrant cases","C":"Continue parental stretching for an additional 12 months since metatarsus adductus always resolves spontaneously by age 2 years without any need for casting or surgical intervention","D":"Apply reverse-last shoes and observe without casting since reverse-last shoes provide sufficient corrective force for all partially flexible metatarsus adductus that has failed stretching","E":"Obtain lower extremity radiographs to measure the talo-first metatarsal angle before initiating any additional treatment, as imaging is required prior to casting at this age"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A partially flexible metatarsus adductus that has failed 4 months of parental stretching at 10 months of age warrants initiation of serial casting — the recalcitrant case treatment per SDL 8 guidelines. Surgery is not indicated after only 4 months of stretching; it is reserved for severe cases after serial casting has also failed, typically after age 4. Continuing stretching alone for 12 more months is unlikely to succeed for a partially rigid deformity. Radiographs are not required before initiating casting for metatarsus adductus.","boardPrep":"Metatarsus adductus management progression: (1) Flexible → parental stretching. (2) Partially flexible or failed stretching → serial casting (for \"recalcitrant\" cases per SDL 8). (3) Rigid or persistent after casting → rare surgical correction. Casting for metatarsus adductus: reverse-last casts holding forefoot in abduction; changed every 1-2 weeks. Best results when casting is initiated before 6-8 months. At 10 months with failed stretching, casting is the appropriate next step.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — Talipes equinovarus: serial casting is the shared treatment principle for pediatric foot deformities that are partially or fully flexible; the Ponseti principle of progressive corrective casting applies across clubfoot and recalcitrant metatarsus adductus"},{"id":"sdl8_q28","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of spinal dysraphism.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pregnant woman at 16 weeks of gestation has elevated maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) on routine screening. Subsequent targeted ultrasound is performed. Which condition is elevated AFP most commonly associated with in this context and what is the next step?","choices":{"A":"Elevated AFP is specifically associated with closed spinal dysraphism (spina bifida occulta) where neural tissue is not exposed; MRI of the maternal spine is the recommended next step","B":"Elevated AFP is exclusively associated with Down syndrome (trisomy 21); amniocentesis for karyotype is the recommended next step when AFP is elevated on routine prenatal screening","C":"Elevated AFP is associated with open neural tube defects including myelomeningocele; targeted ultrasound to evaluate for a spinal defect and associated Chiari II malformation findings is the next step","D":"Elevated AFP is associated with placental insufficiency and intrauterine growth restriction; umbilical artery Doppler velocimetry is the recommended next step for elevated AFP levels","E":"Elevated AFP is a normal finding in pregnancy and does not require any additional workup unless it is elevated more than 10 multiples of the median for the gestational age"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Elevated maternal serum AFP is a biochemical marker for OPEN neural tube defects including myelomeningocele — the exposed neural tissue allows AFP to leak into the amniotic fluid and then into maternal serum. The next step is targeted ultrasound looking for lemon sign, banana sign, and absent cisterna magna. Closed spinal dysraphism (spina bifida occulta) does NOT cause elevated AFP because skin is intact. Down syndrome is associated with LOW AFP (not elevated). Placental insufficiency does not specifically elevate AFP.","boardPrep":"Spinal dysraphism prenatal diagnosis: Elevated maternal serum AFP (MSAFP) → open neural tube defects (open NTDs expose neural tissue and AFP leaks into amniotic fluid and maternal serum). Targeted ultrasound: \"lemon sign\" (scalloping of frontal bones), \"banana sign\" (cerebellar compression/Chiari II), absence of normal cisterna magna. Amniocentesis: amniotic fluid AFP and acetylcholinesterase confirm open NTD. Closed dysraphism (spina bifida occulta) = AFP is NORMAL (skin is intact, no leakage).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — Teratogens: valproate and folic acid deficiency cause neural tube defects; elevated AFP is the biochemical marker for open NTDs on prenatal screening; folic acid supplementation 400-800mcg periconceptionally reduces NTD risk by 50-70%"},{"id":"sdl8_q29","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of spinal dysraphism.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A newborn with myelomeningocele at the L3 level undergoes surgical closure within 24 hours of birth. At 2 weeks of age, she develops a rapidly enlarging head circumference, irritability, and a bulging fontanelle. Which complication is most likely responsible for this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Tethered cord syndrome causing progressive lower extremity weakness and urinary retention from re-tethering of the spinal cord at the surgical repair site in the neonatal period","B":"Wound dehiscence at the surgical closure site allowing CSF leakage and ascending bacterial meningitis causing fever, bulging fontanelle, and progressive neurological deterioration","C":"Basilar invagination compressing the brainstem from upward migration of the odontoid process through the foramen magnum causing acute obstructive hydrocephalus and apnea","D":"Syringomyelia with progressive intramedullary cyst expansion causing the head enlargement by compressing the aqueduct of Sylvius and disrupting normal cerebrospinal fluid dynamics","E":"Hydrocephalus from obstruction of CSF flow, a complication of associated Chiari II malformation; most children with myelomeningocele develop hydrocephalus requiring ventriculoperitoneal shunt"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Progressive head enlargement with bulging fontanelle after myelomeningocele surgical closure = hydrocephalus from Chiari II-related obstruction of CSF flow at the foramen magnum, requiring ventriculoperitoneal shunt placement. More than 80% of myelomeningocele patients develop hydrocephalus. Tethered cord causes progressive lower extremity weakness but not enlarging head circumference. Wound dehiscence with meningitis would cause fever and sepsis. Basilar invagination is upward odontoid migration. Syringomyelia is an intramedullary cord cyst.","boardPrep":"Myelomeningocele complications: Hydrocephalus is extremely common (>80%) — caused by Chiari II obstruction of CSF flow at the foramen magnum. Presentation: enlarging head, bulging fontanelle, sunset sign (downward gaze), vomiting, irritability. Treatment: ventriculoperitoneal (VP) shunt. Chiari II malformation: downward herniation of cerebellum and brainstem through foramen magnum — hallmark of myelomeningocele (almost universal). Also: tethered cord, hydromyelia, seizures, urinary dysfunction, orthopedic problems.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 8 — Lumbosacral spinal stenosis: CSF flow obstruction is a shared mechanism in some spinal conditions; in myelomeningocele, Chiari II obstructs CSF at the foramen magnum causing hydrocephalus; in spinal stenosis, CSF is compressed at the lumbar level causing cauda equina syndrome"},{"id":"sdl8_q30","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of spinal dysraphism.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy with a known diagnosis of spina bifida occulta is brought in with progressive lower extremity weakness and new-onset urinary incontinence. He has a small dimple and tuft of hair at the lumbosacral region. His mother asks how this differs from myelomeningocele. Which statement best explains the key differences?","choices":{"A":"Spina bifida occulta is more severe than myelomeningocele because cord compression is chronic rather than acute; myelomeningocele causes only partial neurological deficits while occulta causes complete paralysis","B":"Spina bifida occulta has an intact skin covering with no exposed neural tissue; symptoms arise later from tethered cord or intraspinal anomalies; myelomeningocele has immediate obvious cord exposure at birth","C":"Spina bifida occulta always presents at birth with visible spinal defect; myelomeningocele presents later in childhood with progressive symptoms as the child grows and the cord stretches","D":"Spina bifida occulta involves complete herniation of the spinal cord through the skin; myelomeningocele involves only meningeal herniation without cord exposure, making it the milder form","E":"Spina bifida occulta requires immediate surgical repair at birth like myelomeningocele; both conditions have equal urgency because both expose the cord to mechanical injury and infection"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Spina bifida occulta (closed dysraphism) has intact skin covering the vertebral defect with no exposed neural tissue; symptoms from tethered cord or intraspinal anomalies typically develop later during childhood growth. Myelomeningocele (open) is immediately obvious at birth with cord exposure requiring urgent surgical closure. Occulta is NOT more severe than myelomeningocele. Occulta does NOT present at birth with visible spinal defect. It does NOT involve herniation of cord through skin. It does NOT require immediate surgical repair.","boardPrep":"Spina bifida occulta (closed dysraphism): skin is intact over the vertebral defect; neural tissue not exposed. Skin stigmata: hairy patch, dimple, hemangioma, lipoma over lumbosacral spine. May present later with tethered cord syndrome (progressive weakness, urinary/bowel dysfunction, foot deformity as the child grows). Myelomeningocele (open): obvious defect at birth, immediate surgical closure required. AFP is elevated only with open NTDs. Occult dysraphism detected by MRI when skin stigmata are present.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 7 — Lumbosacral radiculopathy: tethered cord in spina bifida occulta causes a progressive radiculopathy-like syndrome from spinal cord traction during growth; distinguishes from disc herniation radiculopathy by imaging (MRI shows cord tethering rather than disc herniation)"}]},{"sdlNumber":9,"title":"Lower Extremity Fractures","questions":[{"id":"sdl9_q01","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review primary regions of long bones as it pertains to anatomy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The region of a long bone located between the epiphysis and the diaphysis, containing predominantly cancellous bone, a rich vascular network, and the growth plate in skeletally immature individuals, is best described as which anatomical zone?","choices":{"A":"The metaphysis, the flared zone between the physis and diaphysis that is most metabolically active, most vascular, and the primary site of hematogenous osteomyelitis seeding in children","B":"The epiphysis, the bony terminus at each end of a long bone that is covered by hyaline articular cartilage and separated from the metaphysis by the cartilaginous growth plate","C":"The diaphysis, the elongated tubular shaft of a long bone composed predominantly of dense cortical bone surrounding the central medullary canal filled with yellow marrow in adults","D":"The periosteum, the fibrous vascular membrane covering the outer surface of cortical bone that contains osteoprogenitor cells responsible for appositional bone growth and fracture healing","E":"The endosteum, the thin cellular layer lining the inner medullary canal and trabecular surfaces that contains osteoclasts and osteoblasts involved in bone remodeling and marrow maintenance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The metaphysis is the flared zone adjacent to the physis, rich in cancellous bone and blood flow. Its sluggish capillary sinusoids and lack of phagocytic lining cells make it the primary site of hematogenous osteomyelitis in children. The epiphysis forms the articular end; the diaphysis is the compact cortical shaft; the periosteum and endosteum are membranous coverings rather than discrete anatomical zones.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Metaphysis = most vascular zone = osteomyelitis in kids. Diaphysis = yellow marrow in adults. COMLEX: Growth plate at metaphyseal-epiphyseal junction is the weakest point in pediatric bone under stress.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 1 — Bone histology: osteocyte anatomy and Haversian canal organization in cortical bone"},{"id":"sdl9_q02","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review primary regions of long bones as it pertains to anatomy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"In skeletally mature adults, the medullary canal of the femoral diaphysis is occupied predominantly by which type of tissue, and what does this represent compared to the composition found during fetal and early childhood development?","choices":{"A":"Red marrow containing hematopoietic precursor cells, representing the persistence of fetal marrow composition throughout adult life in order to meet the high metabolic demand of erythropoiesis in large bones","B":"Yellow marrow composed primarily of adipocytes, representing the normal age-related conversion from hematopoietically active red marrow that occupied the diaphysis during fetal development and early childhood","C":"Periosteal fibrous tissue that migrates inward from the cortical surface to fill the medullary space as endosteal remodeling reduces the diameter of the medullary canal with advancing age","D":"Cartilaginous matrix derived from retained physeal remnants that persists within the medullary canal after growth plate closure and gradually undergoes endochondral ossification in older adults","E":"Dense cortical bone that fills the medullary canal as endosteal apposition exceeds resorption over decades, progressively reducing marrow volume and increasing bone stiffness in older adults"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Red marrow progressively converts to yellow marrow during childhood, proceeding from distal to proximal and from peripheral to axial skeleton. By adulthood, only the axial skeleton and proximal femur and humerus retain significant red marrow. Reconversion of yellow to red marrow occurs in severe anemia, hematologic malignancy, and physiologic stress. Fetal and neonatal bones contain exclusively red marrow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Red to yellow marrow conversion: distal to proximal, peripheral to axial. Red marrow persists in: vertebrae, sternum, ribs, pelvis, proximal femur/humerus. COMLEX: Yellow marrow reconversion in hemolytic anemia — boards favorite.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 1 — Bone histology: osteocyte anatomy and Haversian canal organization in cortical bone"},{"id":"sdl9_q03","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review primary regions of long bones as it pertains to anatomy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The structural unit of compact cortical bone consists of concentric lamellae of mineralized matrix surrounding a central vascular channel, together forming a cylindrical unit aligned with the long axis of the bone. What is this unit called?","choices":{"A":"The trabecula, a thin rod or plate of cancellous bone forming an interconnected lattice oriented along lines of mechanical stress in the metaphysis and epiphysis of long bones","B":"The lacuna, the small ovoid cavity within mineralized bone matrix that houses an individual osteocyte and connects to adjacent lacunae via a network of microscopic canalicular channels","C":"The Volkmann canal, a channel that runs oblique or perpendicular to the bone axis connecting adjacent Haversian canals to each other and to the periosteal and endosteal surfaces","D":"The canaliculus, a submicroscopic channel extending between adjacent osteocyte lacunae that allows cytoplasmic process contact and nutrient diffusion throughout the mineralized matrix","E":"The osteon, also called the Haversian system, in which concentric bony lamellae surround a central Haversian canal containing arterioles, venules, and unmyelinated nerve fibers"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The osteon is the basic structural unit of compact bone. Each osteon consists of 5 to 20 concentric lamellae surrounding a central Haversian canal. Volkmann canals run perpendicular to Haversian canals and connect them to the periosteum. Lacunae house osteocytes; canaliculi connect adjacent lacunae. Trabeculae are the structural units of cancellous bone, not compact bone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteon = Haversian system. Concentric lamellae around central vascular canal. Osteocytes in lacunae communicate via canaliculi. COMLEX: Cortical bone remodeling disrupts osteons; cutting cones of osteoclasts tunnel through existing osteons.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 1 — Bone histology: osteocyte anatomy and Haversian canal organization in cortical bone"},{"id":"sdl9_q04","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss variables and nomenclature involved in appropriate fracture identification.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A long-bone fracture in which the fracture line wraps in a helical pattern around the shaft is characterized by which mechanism and what is the appropriate fracture nomenclature?","choices":{"A":"A spiral fracture caused by a torsional force applied along the long axis of the bone, in which the fracture propagates along a helical path of maximal shear stress","B":"A transverse fracture caused by a direct bending force perpendicular to the bone axis, in which the fracture line crosses the diaphysis at approximately 90 degrees to the long axis","C":"An oblique fracture caused by combined axial loading and angular force, in which the fracture line runs diagonally across the shaft at roughly 45 degrees to the long axis","D":"A comminuted fracture caused by high-energy direct impact, in which three or more fracture fragments are produced at a single level and the fracture cannot be reduced to two main fragments","E":"A segmental fracture caused by two separate high-energy impacts at different levels, in which an isolated middle bone segment is created between two distinct fracture lines"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Spiral fractures result from torsional or rotational forces. The helical fracture line follows the plane of maximal shear stress around the bone. Transverse fractures result from three-point bending. Oblique fractures result from combined loading. Comminuted fractures have three or more fragments. Segmental fractures have two separate fracture levels isolating a middle fragment. All of these terms are part of standard fracture nomenclature.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Spiral fracture = torsion. Classic boards scenario: spiral tibial fracture in a toddler from a twisting fall — \"toddler fracture.\" Spiral humerus fracture from arm-twisting is also a child abuse red flag. COMLEX: Document mechanism; mechanism predicts fracture pattern.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: Ottawa rules guide radiograph decision-making in acute ankle injury"},{"id":"sdl9_q05","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss variables and nomenclature involved in appropriate fracture identification.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"When describing a fracture radiographically, which term specifically refers to the directional relationship between the distal bone fragment and the proximal fragment, expressed as the direction the apex of the angle formed by the two fragments points?","choices":{"A":"Displacement, which refers to the lateral translation of one fragment relative to the other measured in millimeters or as a percentage of the bone shaft diameter at the fracture site","B":"Shortening, which refers to the longitudinal overlap of fracture fragments resulting in loss of overall bone length measured in centimeters on full-length standing or traction radiographs","C":"Rotation, which refers to axial twisting of one fracture fragment relative to the other around the long axis of the bone and may not be visible on standard two-view radiographs","D":"Angulation, which is reported as the direction the apex of the fracture deformity points — for example, apex-volar or apex-dorsal — and determines the direction of corrective manipulation required for reduction","E":"Comminution, which refers to the presence of three or more bone fragments at the fracture site typically resulting from high-energy trauma such as motor vehicle collision or fall from height"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Angulation describes the angle formed between fracture fragments and is reported by the direction the apex points. Apex-volar means the distal fragment is tilted dorsally, as in a Colles fracture. Displacement describes translational shift; shortening describes longitudinal overlap; rotation describes axial twist. All four descriptors are used together for complete fracture characterization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Complete fracture description requires: bone name, fracture type, location, displacement, angulation, rotation, shortening, open vs closed. COMLEX: Reduction technique is guided by reversal of the deforming force — knowing angulation direction determines how to apply corrective force.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: Ottawa rules guide radiograph decision-making in acute ankle injury"},{"id":"sdl9_q06","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss variables and nomenclature involved in appropriate fracture identification.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A fracture is classified as open when which defining criterion is met, and why does this classification significantly alter the clinical management approach?","choices":{"A":"A fracture is open when the fracture line extends into the adjacent joint space, creating an intra-articular injury that requires anatomic reduction to restore joint surface congruity and prevent arthritis","B":"A fracture is open when the periosteum is disrupted by the fracture, exposing the cortical bone surface and increasing the risk of delayed union due to loss of the periosteal osteoprogenitor cell layer","C":"A fracture is open when the fracture communicates with the external environment through a wound in the overlying skin, requiring emergent surgical debridement and antibiotics to prevent osteomyelitis","D":"A fracture is open when the bone fragments are widely displaced with greater than 50 percent translational shift relative to the bone shaft diameter, indicating high-energy mechanism and ligamentous disruption","E":"A fracture is open when marrow fat is released into the circulation, producing a fat embolism syndrome characterized by hypoxemia, petechiae, and confusion in the days following a long-bone fracture"},"correct":"C","explanation":"An open fracture communicates with the external environment, introducing contamination risk and dramatically increasing the likelihood of osteomyelitis. Management requires emergent operative debridement, irrigation, and IV antibiotics within one hour. Gustilo-Anderson classification grades open fractures I through IIIC by wound size and soft tissue injury. Intra-articular extension is a separate descriptor; periosteal disruption occurs in most displaced fractures; fat embolism is a systemic complication, not the classification criterion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Open fracture = skin breach + fracture communication. Gustilo I: wound under 1 cm. Gustilo II: 1-10 cm. Gustilo III: over 10 cm or periosteal stripping. IV antibiotics within 1 hour, debridement in OR. COMLEX: Osteomyelitis complication of open fracture — Staph aureus is most common organism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: Ottawa rules guide radiograph decision-making in acute ankle injury"},{"id":"sdl9_q07","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss differences between adult and pediatric bone stress.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which property of pediatric bone best explains why a bending force produces an incomplete greenstick fracture in a child but a complete transverse fracture in an adult subjected to the same force?","choices":{"A":"Pediatric bone has a thicker periosteum with a denser network of Sharpey fibers anchoring it to the cortex, generating mechanical resistance that halts fracture propagation across the bone before complete cortical failure","B":"Pediatric bone has a higher ratio of organic collagen matrix to inorganic mineral content, giving it greater plasticity and the ability to absorb energy by bending before the tension-side cortex ultimately fails","C":"Pediatric bone has a lower cortical bone mineral density due to ongoing endochondral ossification at the physis, reducing the stiffness of the diaphysis and allowing elastic bending without catastrophic failure","D":"Pediatric bone is supplied by a denser network of Haversian canals than adult bone, distributing stress over more vascular channels and preventing the stress concentration required to propagate a complete fracture line","E":"Pediatric bone undergoes faster remodeling than adult bone, allowing microcracks to be repaired before they can coalesce into a macroscopic fracture line, which slows the progression from incomplete to complete fracture"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pediatric bone has a higher collagen-to-mineral ratio than adult bone, giving it greater plasticity and energy absorption capacity. During a bending force, the tension cortex fails first while the compression cortex bends but remains intact, producing the classic greenstick pattern. Adult bone is stiffer and more brittle due to higher mineral content, causing both cortices to fail nearly simultaneously under the same force.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Greenstick = pediatric incomplete fracture from bending. Tension side breaks, compression side bends. Treat with reduction and casting. COMLEX: Pediatric bone material properties also explain torus/buckle fractures at the metaphysis from axial loading — different mechanism than greenstick.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — Osteogenesis imperfecta: defective Type I collagen causes brittle bone with fractures at minimal trauma"},{"id":"sdl9_q08","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss differences between adult and pediatric bone stress.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy falls and sustains a distal radius fracture. Radiograph shows cortical buckling on the dorsal surface of the distal radius metaphysis with no cortical disruption on the volar surface. This fracture pattern is best described as which type and from which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"A greenstick fracture caused by a bending force that fractures the tension cortex while the compression cortex remains intact, characterized by a visible cortical break on one side with intact cortex on the opposite side of the bone","B":"A Salter-Harris Type I fracture caused by shear forces through the distal radial physis, characterized by widening of the growth plate and physeal tenderness without a discrete cortical fracture line on standard radiographs","C":"A pathologic fracture caused by cortical thinning from a bone cyst or fibrous dysplasia lesion within the distal radial metaphysis, showing a cortical bulge due to the underlying lytic lesion rather than acute traumatic buckling","D":"A plastic deformation injury caused by the bone bending beyond its elastic limit without any cortical disruption, producing a visible bow to the entire metaphyseal segment rather than the localized cortical bulge seen in a buckle fracture","E":"A torus fracture, also called a buckle fracture, caused by axial compressive loading at the metaphysis that causes the cortex to buckle outward without fully fracturing, analogous to the buckling of a metal torus ring under compression"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A torus fracture results from axial compressive loading at the metaphysis. The cortex buckles outward on the compressive side without completing a fracture line. It is mechanically stable and typically treated with a short arm cast for 3 to 4 weeks. Greenstick fractures show cortical disruption on the tension side; Salter-Harris fractures involve the physis; plastic deformation shows global bowing without cortical buckling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Torus/buckle fracture = metaphyseal axial compression = cortical buckling. Stable, cast for 3-4 weeks, excellent prognosis. COMLEX: Distinguish from greenstick: torus = compression side buckle, no cortical break; greenstick = tension side cortical break with intact compression cortex.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — Osteogenesis imperfecta: defective Type I collagen causes brittle bone with fractures at minimal trauma"},{"id":"sdl9_q09","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss differences between adult and pediatric bone stress.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Pediatric long bone fractures are managed with less stringent alignment requirements than adult fractures in part because of the remodeling potential of growing bone. What is the primary mechanism driving this remodeling?","choices":{"A":"Intramembranous ossification at the fracture site activates dormant osteoprogenitor cells within the periosteum, producing a hypertrophic callus large enough to bridge angular deformities without requiring bony realignment","B":"Endochondral ossification at the fracture gap deposits a cartilaginous template that gradually mineralizes and reshapes under mechanical loading, correcting angular deformities through callus remodeling alone","C":"Secondary Haversian remodeling by cutting cone osteoclasts tunnels through the fracture callus and deposits new lamellar bone in the direction of loading, correcting angular deformities at the microscopic level","D":"Physeal-driven longitudinal bone growth combined with periosteal appositional remodeling guided by Wolff's law, which corrects angular deformities over months to years in proportion to the child's remaining growth potential","E":"Increased osteoclastic activity stimulated by parathyroid hormone surge after fracture resorbs the malunited callus, followed by osteoblastic refilling along functional loading lines to restore normal alignment"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Remodeling of fracture malunion in children is driven by two mechanisms: physeal growth, which progressively shifts bone away from the angular deformity as the bone elongates, and periosteal appositional remodeling under Wolff's law, which adds bone on the concave side and resorbs on the convex side. Greatest remodeling occurs in young children with maximal remaining growth and in deformities in the plane of joint motion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pediatric fracture remodeling potential is greatest when: age under 10, fracture near a physis, and deformity is in the plane of motion of the adjacent joint. COMLEX: Varus/valgus deformities remodel better than rotational deformities — rotation does not reliably correct with growth.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — Osteogenesis imperfecta: defective Type I collagen causes brittle bone with fractures at minimal trauma"},{"id":"sdl9_q10","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the Salter-Harris System for classification of pediatric fractures near a growth plate.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 9-year-old girl falls off her bicycle. Wrist X-ray shows a fracture line extending through the distal radial physis with a small triangular cortical fragment attached at the metaphyseal corner. The epiphysis is displaced but not split. Which Salter-Harris type is this, and what is its prognosis?","choices":{"A":"Salter-Harris Type II, the most common physeal fracture type, in which the fracture exits through the metaphysis creating the Thurston-Holland corner fragment, and prognosis for normal growth is generally favorable","B":"Salter-Harris Type I, in which the fracture passes entirely through the physis without involving the metaphysis or epiphysis, the radiograph appears nearly normal, and diagnosis is made clinically by tenderness directly over the physis","C":"Salter-Harris Type III, in which the fracture extends through the physis into the epiphysis and enters the joint, creating an intra-articular fracture that requires anatomic reduction to restore articular congruity","D":"Salter-Harris Type IV, in which a single oblique fracture line crosses the metaphysis, physis, and epiphysis, producing the highest risk of bony physeal bridge formation among the fracture types visible on initial radiograph","E":"Salter-Harris Type V, a crush injury of the germinal physis from axial compression that appears normal on initial radiograph and is diagnosed retrospectively when premature growth arrest becomes evident months later"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Thurston-Holland fragment — a metaphyseal corner fragment — is the hallmark of Salter-Harris Type II fracture. Type II is the most common physeal fracture, accounting for roughly 75% of cases, and has a generally good prognosis because the germinal zone of the physis is spared. Type I is a pure physeal separation; Types III and IV involve the epiphysis and carry greater risk of growth disturbance; Type V is a crush injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Salter-Harris Type II = most common = metaphyseal corner fragment = Thurston-Holland sign. Mnemonic SALTR: Slipped, Above, Lower, Through, Rammed. Type V is the RAREST and WORST — often missed initially. COMLEX: CP8 pediatric named condition; growth arrest monitoring for 12-18 months post-injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 6 — Pediatric fractures: Salter-Harris and greenstick/torus classification in growing bone"},{"id":"sdl9_q11","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the Salter-Harris System for classification of pediatric fractures near a growth plate.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which Salter-Harris fracture type carries the worst long-term prognosis for normal bone growth, often presents with a normal or near-normal initial radiograph, and is definitively diagnosed only retrospectively after growth arrest becomes evident on follow-up imaging?","choices":{"A":"Salter-Harris Type IV, which extends through the metaphysis, physis, and epiphysis in a single oblique line, is visible on initial radiograph, has the highest surgical failure risk from bony bridge formation, but can be prevented with anatomic fixation","B":"Salter-Harris Type V, a crush or compression injury of the germinal zone of the physis that disrupts physeal chondrocytes without producing a visible fracture line, leading to premature physeal closure and growth arrest","C":"Salter-Harris Type III, which extends through the physis into the epiphysis creating an intra-articular fracture, is visible on initial radiograph, requires anatomic reduction, and carries moderate growth disturbance risk","D":"Salter-Harris Type I, a pure physeal separation with near-normal radiograph, occurs most commonly in neonates and young children, and generally has a favorable prognosis when recognized early and gently reduced","E":"Salter-Harris Type II, the most common physeal fracture with a visible metaphyseal corner fragment, has a generally favorable prognosis because the germinal zone is spared by the fracture exiting through the metaphysis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Salter-Harris Type V is a compression or crush injury of the physis that damages the germinal and proliferative chondrocyte layers without producing a fracture line visible on plain radiograph. It is diagnosed retrospectively when growth arrest or angular deformity is noted at follow-up. Type IV carries the highest surgical risk of bridge formation among radiographically visible types, but Type V has the worst overall prognosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Type V = axial crush of physis = normal X-ray = diagnosed retrospectively = worst prognosis. Type IV = crosses all three layers = needs fixation to prevent physeal bridge. COMLEX: All physeal fractures require 12-18 month growth monitoring with contralateral comparison X-rays.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 6 — Pediatric fractures: Salter-Harris and greenstick/torus classification in growing bone"},{"id":"sdl9_q12","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the Salter-Harris System for classification of pediatric fractures near a growth plate.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 14-year-old male soccer player inverts his ankle. Radiograph shows a fracture line extending from the distal tibial physis vertically downward through the lateral epiphysis into the ankle joint, with no metaphyseal fragment visible. Which Salter-Harris type is this and what management principle is critical?","choices":{"A":"Salter-Harris Type I, a pure physeal separation without epiphyseal or metaphyseal bony fragment, managed with immobilization alone since the articular surface is not involved and growth plate anatomy is preserved","B":"Salter-Harris Type II, in which the fracture exits through the metaphysis producing a corner fragment rather than splitting the epiphysis, generally managed with closed reduction and casting without strict articular alignment requirements","C":"Salter-Harris Type III, an epiphyseal fracture with intra-articular extension, requiring anatomic reduction to within 2 mm to restore joint congruity and prevent post-traumatic arthritis at the ankle","D":"Salter-Harris Type IV, in which a single oblique fracture line crosses metaphysis, physis, and epiphysis simultaneously, requiring anatomic fixation to prevent a bony bridge across the physis that would cause angular growth arrest","E":"Salter-Harris Type V, a compression injury to the physis with no fracture line visible on plain radiograph, diagnosed only after growth arrest appears on follow-up and not presenting acutely with an epiphyseal fracture line"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Salter-Harris Type III fractures extend from the physis downward through the epiphysis into the joint. This pattern is common at the distal tibia in adolescents and at the distal femur. The key management principle is anatomic reduction of the articular surface to within 2 mm, typically requiring CT to assess reduction adequacy. Failure to achieve anatomic reduction leads to post-traumatic arthritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Type III = physis + epiphysis = intra-articular. CT scan to assess reduction. Needs anatomic reduction to prevent arthritis. Classic boards: triplane fracture of the distal tibia in adolescents is a variant combining Types II and III. COMLEX: Growth arrest risk from asymmetric physeal closure causing angular deformity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 6 — Pediatric fractures: Salter-Harris and greenstick/torus classification in growing bone"},{"id":"sdl9_q13","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old cyclist is struck by a car bumper at knee height while riding. Radiograph of the knee shows depression of the lateral tibial plateau. Which imaging finding, if present on the lateral knee radiograph, would be pathognomonic for an intra-articular fracture communicating with the joint space?","choices":{"A":"A joint effusion seen as increased soft tissue density in the suprapatellar recess on lateral radiograph, a non-specific finding present in both ligamentous injuries and intra-articular fractures that does not confirm bony communication","B":"Cortical disruption of the tibial plateau surface seen as a step-off deformity on the AP radiograph, indicating a plateau fracture but not specifically confirming communication of the fracture with the joint space","C":"Periosteal new bone formation along the proximal tibial metaphysis seen as a thin periosteal layer on AP radiograph, a non-specific healing response that does not confirm acute intra-articular fracture communication","D":"A lipohemarthrosis visible as a fat-fluid level in the suprapatellar pouch on a horizontal-beam lateral radiograph, indicating that medullary fat has entered the joint through the intra-articular fracture site","E":"Valgus alignment of the tibial plateau under weight-bearing stress radiograph, indicating collapse of the lateral compartment under loading but not confirming intra-articular communication of the acute fracture"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lipohemarthrosis is the pathognomonic finding of intra-articular fracture. On a cross-table lateral radiograph, fat from the bone marrow layers above blood in the joint, creating a fat-fluid level in the suprapatellar pouch. A simple effusion has no fat-fluid level. Lipohemarthrosis confirms that the fracture extends into the joint, even when the fracture itself is subtle on other views.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lipohemarthrosis on lateral knee X-ray = intra-articular fracture = pathognomonic. Requires horizontal-beam lateral. Also seen with occult tibial plateau fractures in elderly. COMLEX: Get MRI if X-ray shows effusion but no visible fracture — occult tibial plateau fractures are common in osteoporotic patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — Knee fractures: tibial plateau and patellar fractures in the diagnostic workflow for knee pain"},{"id":"sdl9_q14","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 22-year-old male basketball player sustains a pivoting knee injury. Plain films show a small cortical avulsion off the lateral margin of the tibial condyle just below the joint line. This specific radiographic finding is considered pathognomonic for injury to which structure?","choices":{"A":"The posterior cruciate ligament, as bone avulsion from the posterior tibial surface at the PCL footprint is produced by a direct posterior force to the proximal tibia with the knee flexed, as in a dashboard injury","B":"The medial collateral ligament, as avulsion off the medial femoral condyle at the MCL femoral attachment is produced by a valgus stress mechanism and is the most common ligamentous injury in contact sports","C":"The lateral collateral ligament, as an avulsion off the fibular head at the LCL insertion is produced by a varus stress mechanism and is typically associated with posterolateral corner injury and peroneal nerve traction","D":"The popliteal tendon, as an avulsion of its femoral attachment on the lateral femoral condyle is produced by hyperextension with external rotation and presents as a small cortical fragment adjacent to the lateral condyle","E":"The anterior cruciate ligament, as a Segond fracture at the lateral tibial capsule attachment site is produced by the internal rotation and varus stress transmitted through the anterolateral complex during ACL-tear mechanisms"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The Segond fracture is an avulsion of the lateral tibial capsule at the capsulo-osseous layer of the iliotibial band, produced by the internal rotation and varus stress that tears the ACL. It is considered pathognomonic for ACL injury, with ACL tears found in over 90% of cases. It is distinct from a PCL avulsion, which occurs posteriorly. MRI is indicated to evaluate for associated meniscal injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Segond fracture = lateral tibial rim avulsion = pathognomonic for ACL tear. Get MRI to evaluate ACL and menisci. COMLEX: Often accompanied by medial meniscus tear. The Segond fragment is at the level of the Gerdy tubercle, where the IT band inserts.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — Knee fractures: tibial plateau and patellar fractures in the diagnostic workflow for knee pain"},{"id":"sdl9_q15","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 47-year-old falls directly onto his patella. Radiograph shows a transverse patellar fracture with 5 mm of articular step-off and the patient is unable to perform an active straight-leg raise. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Immobilization in a cylinder cast or knee immobilizer in full extension for 8 weeks followed by progressive quadriceps strengthening, which is appropriate only for non-displaced fractures with an intact extensor mechanism","B":"Open reduction and internal fixation using tension band wiring or cannulated screws to restore articular congruity and repair the extensor mechanism, followed by early range-of-motion rehabilitation","C":"Aspiration of the hemarthrosis and application of a functional knee brace locked in extension, which temporarily restores knee extension but does not address the displaced articular fracture or extensor mechanism disruption","D":"Patellectomy with repair of the quadriceps tendon directly to the patellar tendon stump, which is reserved for severely comminuted fractures not amenable to fixation and results in permanent reduction in quadriceps mechanical advantage","E":"Urgent total knee arthroplasty to address both the acute fracture and the patellar articular surface damage, which is not an appropriate primary treatment for an isolated traumatic patellar fracture in a young patient"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Surgical indications for patellar fracture are articular step-off greater than 2 mm or fragment separation greater than 3 mm, and disruption of the extensor mechanism demonstrated by inability to perform a straight-leg raise. Tension band wiring converts tensile forces into compressive forces across the fracture during knee flexion. Non-displaced fractures with intact extensor mechanism are managed non-operatively.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Patellar fracture surgical indications: articular step-off over 2 mm, separation over 3 mm, or failed straight-leg raise. Tension band wiring = compression across fracture with knee movement. COMLEX: Test extensor mechanism integrity at every knee fracture exam — straight-leg raise is the key clinical test.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — Knee fractures: tibial plateau and patellar fractures in the diagnostic workflow for knee pain"},{"id":"sdl9_q16","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of tibial shaft fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The tibia is more frequently involved in open fractures than any other long bone. Which anatomical feature is most responsible for this predisposition?","choices":{"A":"The tibial diaphysis has a relatively thin cortex compared to other long bones, causing the bone to fracture into sharp pointed fragments that more readily perforate surrounding soft tissues during high-energy injury","B":"The tibia bears the majority of axial load in the lower extremity, resulting in high-energy fracture patterns with greater displacement that tend to produce larger soft tissue injuries than weight-bearing bones elsewhere","C":"The tibial nutrient artery enters at the posterior cortex, creating a stress concentration point at the posterior diaphysis that predisposes to spiral fractures propagating toward the subcutaneous anterior surface","D":"The anteromedial tibial surface is subcutaneous throughout its entire length with minimal overlying soft tissue, making it highly susceptible to skin perforation when fracture fragments displace medially","E":"The interosseous membrane between tibia and fibula restricts outward periosteal expansion after fracture, directing displaced fragments toward the medial subcutaneous surface rather than allowing dissipation into deeper compartments"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The anteromedial border of the tibia is subcutaneous from the tibial tubercle to the medial malleolus, covered only by skin and subcutaneous fat. This minimal soft tissue coverage means that even relatively small amounts of fracture displacement can produce skin perforation. Combined with its exposed position, this explains why the tibia has the highest open fracture rate of any long bone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tibia = most common open fracture = subcutaneous anteromedial surface. Gustilo-Anderson classification I-IIIC. COMLEX: Open fracture management — IV antibiotics within 1 hour, OR debridement within 6-24 hours. Cephalosporin for all grades; add gram-negative coverage for Grade II/III.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 1 — MTSS: periostitis of the posteromedial tibia as the overuse-spectrum counterpart to tibial shaft fracture"},{"id":"sdl9_q17","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of tibial shaft fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 31-year-old sustains a closed tibial shaft fracture while skiing. Five hours after a splint is applied, he reports pain rated 9 out of 10 that is not relieved by opioids, and has pain with passive dorsiflexion of his toes. The compartments feel tense to palpation. What is the most appropriate immediate management?","choices":{"A":"Emergent four-compartment fasciotomy of the leg to relieve compartment syndrome, as severe disproportionate pain and pain with passive muscle stretch are the key clinical indicators requiring immediate surgical decompression","B":"Measurement of compartment pressures using a pressure monitor before proceeding, with fasciotomy reserved for absolute pressure above 50 mmHg, which is an inappropriately high threshold that delays decompression in confirmed clinical cases","C":"Bivalving and spreading the splint to skin level to reduce external compression, followed by reassessment in 2 hours, which may reduce cast pressure but is inadequate treatment when clinical examination confirms compartment syndrome","D":"Elevation of the extremity above the level of the heart to reduce venous engorgement and lower compartment pressure, which is contraindicated in compartment syndrome as it further reduces the arteriovenous gradient","E":"Emergent MRI of the leg to confirm muscle ischemia before proceeding with fasciotomy, which is an inappropriate delay since compartment syndrome is a clinical diagnosis requiring immediate surgical intervention"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Compartment syndrome is a clinical diagnosis. Classic features are pain out of proportion to injury and pain with passive stretch of the muscles within the affected compartment. When clinical signs are unequivocal, emergent fasciotomy should not be delayed for pressure measurement. If pressure measurement is used, fasciotomy is indicated when the delta pressure (diastolic BP minus compartment pressure) is less than or equal to 30 mmHg.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Compartment syndrome = 5 Ps (pain, pallor, paresthesia, paralysis, pulselessness — late). Pain with passive stretch is earliest and most reliable sign. Delta-P threshold for fasciotomy: diastolic BP minus compartment pressure less than or equal to 30 mmHg. COMLEX: Tibial shaft fracture is the most common cause of lower leg compartment syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 1 — MTSS: periostitis of the posteromedial tibia as the overuse-spectrum counterpart to tibial shaft fracture"},{"id":"sdl9_q18","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of tibial shaft fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the gold-standard definitive treatment for a displaced, closed tibial shaft fracture in a healthy adult, and why is this approach preferred over plating?","choices":{"A":"Open reduction and internal fixation using a medial plate applied to the anteromedial surface, which provides excellent stability but carries a high wound complication risk given the minimal soft tissue coverage over the tibial surface","B":"Long leg casting in a three-point mold with non-weight bearing for 12 weeks, which is appropriate for minimally displaced stable fractures but cannot control rotation or maintain length in significantly displaced tibial shaft fractures","C":"Intramedullary nailing, which restores length, alignment, and rotational stability while preserving the periosteal blood supply by avoiding open dissection of the fracture site, and allows early weight bearing","D":"External fixation with a unilateral frame spanning the fracture site, which is the preferred definitive treatment for open tibial fractures with contaminated wounds but results in higher malunion rates than nailing for closed injuries","E":"Functional bracing after initial casting for 4 weeks to allow early soft tissue healing, which has been used historically but produces unacceptable angular malunion rates compared to intramedullary nailing for fractures with displacement"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Intramedullary nailing is the gold standard for displaced tibial shaft fractures. The nail is inserted without exposing the fracture, preserving the periosteal blood supply and fracture hematoma. It controls length, alignment, and rotation through interlocking screws. Medial plating is avoided because the anteromedial tibial surface has minimal soft tissue coverage. External fixation is used for temporizing open fractures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tibial shaft fracture definitive treatment = intramedullary nailing. Preserves periosteal blood supply. Controls rotation via interlocking screws. COMLEX: Nail is inserted through patellar tendon approach. Compartment syndrome can still develop after nailing — monitor post-op.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 1 — MTSS: periostitis of the posteromedial tibia as the overuse-spectrum counterpart to tibial shaft fracture"},{"id":"sdl9_q19","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of fibular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An isolated mid-shaft fibular fracture from a direct blow is noted on X-ray with an intact ankle mortise. How is this fracture managed, and what anatomical rationale justifies this approach?","choices":{"A":"Conservative management with weight bearing as tolerated in an air-stirrup brace or walking boot, since the fibula is a non-weight-bearing bone that bears only 15 percent of axial load and isolated mid-shaft fractures are mechanically stable","B":"Surgical fixation with a lateral plate and screws to prevent late valgus angulation of the ankle, which is necessary only when the distal fibula is fractured at or above the syndesmosis and the ankle mortise is disrupted","C":"Non-weight-bearing immobilization in a long leg cast for 8 weeks, which overcorrects for a non-weight-bearing bone fracture and delays functional recovery without improving radiographic or clinical outcomes","D":"Intramedullary nailing of the fibula to provide axial stability and allow immediate full weight bearing, a technique reserved for periprosthetic or pathologic fibular fractures rather than isolated traumatic mid-shaft injuries","E":"Emergent orthopedic referral for surgical planning within 24 hours, which is an overly urgent disposition for an isolated mid-shaft fibular fracture with intact ankle mortise that is not a limb-threatening emergency"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The fibula bears only approximately 15% of axial body weight. The interosseous membrane and intact tibia provide stability in isolated mid-shaft fibular fractures. As long as the ankle mortise is intact, conservative treatment with weight bearing as tolerated provides excellent outcomes. The critical clinical concern is always ensuring the ankle mortise is intact, as syndesmotic disruption or deltoid ligament injury changes management entirely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Isolated fibular shaft fracture with intact ankle mortise = conservative management. Fibula bears only 15% of body weight. COMLEX: Always assess full ankle views — widened mortise changes fracture to unstable Weber B or C, requiring surgery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injuries: common peroneal nerve vulnerability at the fibular neck"},{"id":"sdl9_q20","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of fibular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old twists his ankle and is found to have no fracture on ankle radiographs, but he has proximal fibular tenderness. Full-length tibia-fibula films reveal a fracture at the fibular neck. What is the diagnosis and which associated ankle structure must be assessed for integrity?","choices":{"A":"An isolated stress fracture of the fibular neck from repetitive loading, which develops insidiously over weeks to months without an acute ankle mechanism and does not involve the syndesmosis or deltoid ligament","B":"A peroneal nerve traction injury at the fibular head producing foot drop, which presents with neurological weakness rather than bone tenderness and is not associated with proximal fibular fracture on imaging","C":"A Maisonneuve fracture, in which a pronation-external rotation ankle mechanism disrupts the syndesmosis and transmits force proximally along the interosseous membrane, causing a proximal fibular fracture that will be missed if only ankle films are obtained","D":"A proximal tibiofibular joint dislocation from a direct lateral blow, which presents with proximal lateral knee pain and peroneal nerve symptoms rather than ankle instability and does not involve the distal syndesmosis","E":"A fracture of the fibular head from a direct varus knee mechanism, which is associated with lateral collateral ligament injury and peroneal nerve palsy at the knee rather than ankle mortise disruption and interosseous membrane tearing"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Maisonneuve fracture results from a pronation-external rotation force at the ankle. The medial ankle structures fail first (deltoid ligament or medial malleolus), then the interosseous membrane tears proximally, finally producing a proximal fibular fracture. The ankle films appear nearly normal, but medial clear space widening indicates ankle instability. Full-length fibular films must be obtained in any ankle injury with unexplained mortise widening.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Maisonneuve = proximal fibular fracture from ankle mechanism. Mechanism: pronation-external rotation. Associated with deltoid ligament rupture or medial malleolus fracture. COMLEX: If ankle mortise is widened without a visible distal fibular fracture, get full-length films — classic boards pitfall.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injuries: common peroneal nerve vulnerability at the fibular neck"},{"id":"sdl9_q21","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of fibular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"According to the Danis-Weber classification, a fibular fracture occurring above the level of the ankle mortise and above the tibiofibular syndesmosis is classified as Weber C. What is the clinical implication of this fracture level?","choices":{"A":"Weber C implies the fracture is extra-articular and mechanically stable, since the fibular fracture is remote from the ankle joint and the syndesmosis remains intact because the fracture is above the level of the tibiofibular ligaments","B":"Weber C implies isolated lateral ankle instability from disruption of the anterior talofibular ligament without syndesmotic injury, requiring only cast immobilization and weight-bearing restriction until soft tissue healing is complete","C":"Weber C implies the fracture occurred from an inversion mechanism, consistent with the most common ankle injury pattern, and is associated with lateral ligament complex injury rather than syndesmotic disruption","D":"Weber C implies a stable fracture pattern amenable to closed treatment in a short leg walking cast, since the fibular fracture above the syndesmosis relieves rather than transmits destabilizing forces to the ankle mortise","E":"Weber C implies likely disruption of the tibiofibular syndesmosis and interosseous membrane, creating an unstable ankle mortise that requires surgical fixation of the fibula and syndesmotic stabilization with a positional screw or suture-button"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Weber C fractures occur above the tibiofibular syndesmosis and imply syndesmotic disruption because the fibula must fracture proximal to the intact syndesmosis for the fracture to occur at that level. The syndesmotic disruption destabilizes the ankle mortise, allowing lateral talar shift under loading. Surgical treatment includes fibular fixation and syndesmotic stabilization with a positional screw or suture-button device placed through the fibula and tibia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Weber A = below syndesmosis = stable. Weber B = at syndesmosis = variable. Weber C = above syndesmosis = unstable. Weber C always requires surgery. COMLEX: Test syndesmotic integrity with external rotation stress test and squeeze test. MRI or stress radiographs confirm syndesmotic disruption.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injuries: common peroneal nerve vulnerability at the fibular neck"},{"id":"sdl9_q22","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of ankle fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"According to the Ottawa Ankle Rules, which specific clinical finding at which anatomic location is sufficient to indicate ankle radiographs after an acute ankle injury?","choices":{"A":"Any tenderness along the anterior surface of the distal fibula at the level of the anterior tibiofibular ligament attachment, which indicates ligamentous injury but is not one of the bony landmarks specified in the Ottawa Ankle Rules","B":"Swelling and ecchymosis around the lateral ankle combined with inability to bear weight, which overlaps with Ottawa criteria but does not represent the complete validated clinical decision rule as studied in prospective trials","C":"Bone tenderness at the posterior edge or tip of either malleolus within the distal 6 centimeters, or inability to bear weight for four steps both immediately after the injury and at the time of evaluation","D":"Point tenderness over the fibulocalcaneal ligament below the lateral malleolus combined with lateral joint line pain, which indicates the most commonly injured lateral ankle ligament but is not part of the Ottawa bony landmark criteria","E":"Tenderness at the anterior talar dome at the ankle joint line combined with a reported inversion mechanism, which may indicate an osteochondral lesion of the talus but is not one of the four Ottawa Ankle Rule bony landmarks"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The Ottawa Ankle Rules specify four bony landmarks: posterior edge of the lateral malleolus, posterior edge of the medial malleolus, navicular, and base of the fifth metatarsal. An ankle series is indicated for posterior malleolar tenderness or inability to bear weight. A foot series is indicated for navicular or fifth metatarsal base tenderness. These rules have nearly 100% sensitivity and reduce unnecessary radiographs by approximately 30-40%.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ottawa Ankle Rules — memorize the four bony landmarks: posterior lateral malleolus, posterior medial malleolus, navicular, 5th metatarsal base. Plus inability to bear weight. Sensitivity approaches 100%. COMLEX: These rules are validated in adults; apply with caution in children under 18 and pregnant patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: Ottawa rules and ligamentous patterns that parallel the Danis-Weber ankle fracture classification"},{"id":"sdl9_q23","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of ankle fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A trimalleolar ankle fracture involves fractures of which three bony structures of the ankle?","choices":{"A":"The medial malleolus, lateral malleolus, and the anterior tibial plafond, where the anterior rim fracture is produced by forced ankle plantarflexion and constitutes a separate anterior ankle fracture variant","B":"The medial malleolus, distal fibular shaft, and the posterior tibial shaft, which together describe an ankle fracture pattern that would be classified differently based on the fibular fracture level under the Danis-Weber system","C":"The lateral malleolus, navicular, and posterior malleolus, which together represent a combined Chopart and ankle fracture pattern from high-energy mechanism and does not fit the definition of a trimalleolar fracture","D":"The medial malleolus, lateral malleolus, and posterior malleolus, where the posterior malleolus is the posterior rim of the distal tibia that forms the posterior articular margin of the tibiotalar joint","E":"The medial malleolus, posterior malleolus, and the anterior process of the calcaneus, which describes combined hindfoot injuries requiring both ankle and subtalar evaluation but does not constitute a trimalleolar ankle fracture"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Trimalleolar fractures involve the medial malleolus, lateral malleolus, and posterior malleolus. The posterior malleolus is the posterior articular rim of the distal tibia. When it is fractured, it removes the posterior osseous constraint on the talus, significantly increasing posterior talar subluxation risk. The larger the posterior malleolus fragment, the more instability is created. Fragments involving more than 25% of the articular surface typically require fixation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trimalleolar fracture = medial + lateral + posterior malleolus. Posterior malleolus fragment over 25% of articular surface requires fixation. COMLEX: Posterior malleolus fixation also reduces syndesmotic instability. CT scan is essential to measure posterior fragment size before planning surgery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: Ottawa rules and ligamentous patterns that parallel the Danis-Weber ankle fracture classification"},{"id":"sdl9_q24","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of ankle fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A trimalleolar ankle fracture is considered more unstable than a bimalleolar fracture. Which anatomical principle best explains the additional instability conferred by the posterior malleolus fracture?","choices":{"A":"Fracture of the posterior malleolus disrupts the attachment of the posterior tibiofibular ligament at the tibia, eliminating the strongest component of the syndesmotic complex and causing complete lateral ankle instability","B":"Fracture of the posterior malleolus creates a posterior cortical stress riser in the distal tibia, increasing the risk of propagation to a pilon fracture pattern that would extend the articular injury to the central tibial plafond","C":"Fracture of the posterior malleolus exposes the Achilles tendon insertion to abnormal tensile loading, which pulls the calcaneus posteriorly and creates a secondary subtalar joint instability that compounds the ankle mortise disruption","D":"Fracture of the posterior malleolus interrupts the vascular supply to the distal tibia from the posterior tibial artery, increasing the risk of avascular necrosis of the tibial plafond and post-traumatic arthritis from ischemia","E":"Fracture of the posterior malleolus eliminates the posterior osseous buttress of the tibiotalar joint, removing the posterior constraint on the talus and allowing posterior subluxation of the talus under the tibia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The posterior malleolus provides the posterior osseous buttress for the tibiotalar joint. When fractured, the talus can sublux posteriorly, which dramatically reduces the contact area of the tibiotalar joint and accelerates post-traumatic arthritis. Posterior malleolus fragments involving more than 25% of the tibial articular surface are generally fixed to restore the posterior buttress. The posterior tibiofibular ligament attaches to the posterior malleolus, so its fixation also indirectly reduces syndesmotic instability.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior malleolus = posterior tibial rim = posterior buttress for talus. Fragment over 25% of articular surface = surgical fixation. COMLEX: Trimalleolar fractures require ORIF of all components when posterior malleolus is large enough to affect stability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: Ottawa rules and ligamentous patterns that parallel the Danis-Weber ankle fracture classification"},{"id":"sdl9_q25","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review primary regions of long bones as it pertains to anatomy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 16-year-old male is injured in a dirt bike crash and sustains a fracture described by the trauma surgeon as involving the \"zone of bone with the richest blood supply, adjacent to the growth plate, where the medullary canal transitions from a narrow isthmus to the flared bony ends of the femur.\" Which anatomical region is being described, and which clinical complication does this region's vascularity predispose to in children?","choices":{"A":"The metaphysis, which is the most vascular zone of long bone due to its rich capillary sinusoidal network, predisposing children to hematogenous osteomyelitis seeding because the sluggish sinusoidal blood flow facilitates bacterial lodgment","B":"The epiphysis, which receives blood from terminal epiphyseal vessels that cross the physis and is the primary site of septic arthritis in neonates when bacterial infection spreads from adjacent metaphyseal vessels into the joint space","C":"The diaphysis, which contains the nutrient foramen through which the nutrient artery enters and branches centrifugally to supply the cortical bone, and is the primary site of Ewing sarcoma arising from intramedullary mesenchymal cells","D":"The periosteum, which contains a dense capillary network in its inner cambium layer that supplies the outer cortex and provides the osteoprogenitor cells responsible for periosteal callus formation during fracture healing","E":"The physis, which is a highly vascularized cartilaginous disc containing the proliferative and hypertrophic zones of chondrocytes and is the weakest structural point in pediatric long bones under mechanical loading"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The metaphysis contains large, thin-walled sinusoids with sluggish blood flow and lacks phagocytic lining cells, making it the primary site for hematogenous osteomyelitis in children. Bacteria seed the metaphysis via the bloodstream. In neonates and infants, metaphyseal vessels cross the physis into the epiphysis, allowing spread to cause septic arthritis. The epiphysis is more affected in neonates; the metaphysis is the dominant site after 1 year of age.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteomyelitis in children: metaphysis is the seeding site. Staph aureus is the most common organism overall; Salmonella in sickle cell disease. Neonates can have epiphyseal or joint extension. COMLEX: Always check for concurrent septic arthritis when diagnosing pediatric osteomyelitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — Bone histology and cellular composition: osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and Haversian system organization"},{"id":"sdl9_q26","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review primary regions of long bones as it pertains to anatomy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A first-year medical student asks about the blood supply to cortical bone during a histology lab session. She wants to understand which vessel supplies the inner two-thirds of cortical bone in a long bone diaphysis, distinct from the vessel that supplies the outer one-third. What is the correct answer for each?","choices":{"A":"The periosteal arteries supply the inner two-thirds of cortical bone by penetrating through Volkmann canals to the endosteal surface, while the nutrient artery supplies only the medullary hematopoietic tissue and does not contribute to cortical bone perfusion","B":"The nutrient artery supplies the inner two-thirds of cortical bone via the medullary sinusoidal system, while the periosteal arteries supply the outer one-third through Volkmann canals; the nutrient artery becomes the dominant supply during fracture healing","C":"The epiphyseal arteries supply the inner two-thirds of cortical bone through their metaphyseal branches, while the periosteal arteries supply the outer one-third, and the nutrient artery is a vestigial structure in adults with no functional blood flow","D":"The Haversian canal arterioles supply the inner two-thirds of cortical bone entirely independently of the medullary circulation, receiving their blood from the periosteum without contribution from the nutrient artery or endosteum","E":"Both the inner and outer cortex receive equal contributions from the nutrient artery and periosteal arteries, which anastomose freely within the Volkmann canals such that no regional distinction in cortical blood supply can be identified"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The nutrient artery is the dominant blood supply to cortical bone, entering through the nutrient foramen and supplying the inner two-thirds of the cortex in a centrifugal pattern through the medullary sinusoids and Haversian canal network. The periosteal arteries supply the outer one-third centripetally. When the medullary blood supply is disrupted by intramedullary nailing, the periosteal supply compensates. This dual system explains why reaming during nailing can temporarily compromise cortical blood flow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cortical bone blood supply: nutrient artery = inner 2/3 centrifugal; periosteal arteries = outer 1/3 centripetal. After intramedullary nailing, periosteal supply compensates. COMLEX: After cortical stripping for plating, ischemia of outer cortex can delay union.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — Bone histology and cellular composition: osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and Haversian system organization"},{"id":"sdl9_q27","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review primary regions of long bones as it pertains to anatomy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 40-year-old cyclist sustains a segmental femoral shaft fracture with significant periosteal stripping noted intraoperatively. The orthopedic surgeon expresses concern about delayed union for the isolated middle fragment. Which characteristic of periosteum explains this clinical concern?","choices":{"A":"The periosteum contains dense nerve fibers that trigger the local inflammatory response critical for initiating osteoblast recruitment, and its stripping prevents the neurogenic pain signal that normally drives early bone repair","B":"The periosteum anchors ligaments and tendons to the bone through Sharpey fibers and provides the mechanical load transfer needed to stimulate piezoelectric osteoblast activation at the fracture site during early healing","C":"The periosteum acts as a mechanical barrier preventing hematoma dispersal from the fracture site, and its disruption allows the fracture hematoma to dissipate before organizing into a fibrocartilaginous bridging callus","D":"The periosteum's inner cambium layer contains osteoprogenitor cells and provides the majority of blood supply for periosteal callus formation, and its stripping deprives the middle segment of the cellular and vascular resources required for healing","E":"The periosteum provides the only route of venous drainage from the diaphyseal cortex through emissary veins, and its stripping causes venous congestion and cortical ischemia that independently delays osteoblast-mediated bone formation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The periosteum has two layers: an outer fibrous layer and an inner cambium layer. The cambium layer contains osteoprogenitor cells that differentiate into osteoblasts during fracture healing and produce periosteal callus. It also contributes to the vascular supply through the periosteal capillary network. Periosteal stripping eliminates this cellular and vascular contribution to healing, explaining the high non-union risk in segmental fractures and open fractures with extensive stripping.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Periosteum = outer fibrous + inner cambium layers. Cambium contains osteoprogenitor cells = periosteal callus. Periosteal stripping = delayed union, especially in segmental fractures. COMLEX: Open fracture debridement must preserve periosteum whenever possible to protect healing capacity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — Bone histology and cellular composition: osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and Haversian system organization"},{"id":"sdl9_q28","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss variables and nomenclature involved in appropriate fracture identification.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old falls from a ladder and lands on outstretched hands. The emergency physician documents: \"Closed, oblique, complete fracture of the distal radius shaft with 20-degree apex-dorsal angulation, 2 mm of shortening, and no rotational deformity.\" Which component of this description specifically tells the surgeon the direction in which to apply corrective force during reduction?","choices":{"A":"Apex-dorsal angulation indicates the apex of the fracture deformity points dorsally, meaning the distal fragment is tilted volarly, so corrective reduction requires applying a volar-to-dorsal force on the distal fragment to reverse the deformity","B":"Oblique fracture pattern indicates the direction of the deforming force, since oblique fractures result from combined bending and axial loading in the volar direction, and reduction requires reversing the combination of compression and angulation simultaneously","C":"Shortening of 2 mm indicates the direction in which traction must be applied, since the distal fragment has impacted proximally and the corrective maneuver requires longitudinal distraction to restore the anatomic length of the radius","D":"Closed classification indicates the fracture occurred from an axial mechanism with no skin breach, implying that closed reduction techniques are appropriate and no surgical approach through the skin is required for definitive management","E":"Complete fracture description indicates both cortices are disrupted, requiring the reduction maneuver to address both the volar and dorsal cortex simultaneously to restore alignment, as incomplete fractures can be reduced with simpler single-cortex manipulation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Angulation direction tells the surgeon where the apex points, which is opposite to where the distal fragment has displaced. Apex-dorsal angulation means the distal fragment has tilted volarly. To reduce this, the surgeon applies a hyperextension and then reduction maneuver. Shortening describes length loss and is corrected by traction; oblique describes the fracture line orientation; closed describes soft tissue status.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Colles fracture = apex-volar angulation, dinner fork deformity. Smith fracture = apex-dorsal. The apex points toward where the distal fragment has gone. COMLEX: Reduction of Colles fracture: traction, hyper-extend, then flex to lock reduction. Cast in slight ulnar deviation and mild flexion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: Ottawa Ankle Rules and fracture versus ligamentous injury distinction"},{"id":"sdl9_q29","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss variables and nomenclature involved in appropriate fracture identification.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 54-year-old is struck by a vehicle. Imaging shows a tibial fracture described as \"segmental with a 7 cm isolated middle fragment and 50% cortical displacement at both fracture levels.\" Which specific characteristic of this fracture type explains the increased risk of non-union compared to a simple two-fragment tibial fracture?","choices":{"A":"The fracture occurs at two separate levels, doubling the total fracture surface area requiring callus bridging and therefore requiring twice as long a healing time as a single-level fracture with the same amount of fracture displacement","B":"The fracture pattern indicates a torsional mechanism at the proximal level and a bending mechanism at the distal level, creating two biomechanically distinct fracture interfaces that respond differently to the same fixation device","C":"The middle segment retains the nutrient foramen in most tibial segmental fractures, which paradoxically impairs healing by competing with periosteal callus formation and reducing the centrifugal blood flow through the Haversian system","D":"The middle segment has lost both its endosteal and periosteal blood supply at both fracture ends, creating an avascular fragment that must be reperfused through new vessel ingrowth before bone healing can proceed at both fracture sites","E":"The fracture is classified as open by definition due to the extent of displacement, requiring more aggressive antibiotic prophylaxis and debridement than a single-level closed fracture of similar displacement and mechanism"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The isolated middle segment in a segmental fracture loses its blood supply at both its proximal and distal fracture interfaces. Without an intact periosteal or endosteal blood supply, the segment becomes relatively avascular and relies on neovascularization from surrounding soft tissue and from the remaining fibula. This dramatically increases non-union risk, particularly when periosteal stripping accompanies the injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Segmental fracture = isolated middle segment = high non-union risk due to avascularity. Often requires bone grafting. COMLEX: Segmental tibial fractures have highest complications of any tibial fracture pattern. Intramedullary nail spans both fracture levels and provides stability while vascularization occurs.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: Ottawa Ankle Rules and fracture versus ligamentous injury distinction"},{"id":"sdl9_q30","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss variables and nomenclature involved in appropriate fracture identification.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old female military recruit reports progressive anterior shin pain over 4 weeks of training. The pain worsens with activity and is relieved by rest. X-ray shows no fracture. MRI demonstrates periosteal edema and a faint cortical lucency. What validated clinical decision rule and imaging modality best characterize this injury type?","choices":{"A":"Ottawa Ankle Rules guide the decision to obtain X-rays based on bony tenderness and weight-bearing capacity, but the Ottawa criteria do not apply to gradual-onset stress injuries and cannot substitute for MRI in evaluating periosteal stress reactions","B":"MRI is the gold-standard imaging for stress fractures when plain radiographs are negative, detecting periosteal edema and endosteal signal change weeks before the cortical fracture line becomes visible on X-ray","C":"CT scan is the preferred imaging modality for stress fractures because it best identifies the cortical stress reaction and can quantify the degree of cortical disruption, providing better sensitivity than MRI for early periosteal changes","D":"Bone scintigraphy with technetium-99m is the only validated imaging modality for stress fractures, providing high sensitivity for any osteoblastic activity and distinguishing stress fractures from shin splints based on the focal versus diffuse uptake pattern","E":"Clinical diagnosis alone using the triad of activity-related pain, point tenderness, and response to rest is sufficient to diagnose stress fractures without any imaging, since false-positive imaging findings are common in active patients"},"correct":"B","explanation":"MRI is the most sensitive imaging modality for stress fractures, detecting periosteal edema, endosteal edema, and cortical signal change weeks before X-ray changes appear. Bone scintigraphy is highly sensitive but non-specific. CT can show the fracture line but misses early marrow edema. The Ottawa rules apply only to acute ankle injuries, not to stress fractures. Stress fractures in the anterior tibial cortex are high-risk and may require activity modification or even surgical fixation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Stress fracture = repetitive loading exceeds repair. MRI is gold standard when X-ray negative. High-risk locations requiring surgery: anterior tibial cortex, femoral neck tension side, navicular, 5th metatarsal diaphysis. COMLEX: Female athlete triad increases stress fracture risk: low energy availability, menstrual dysfunction, low bone mineral density.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: Ottawa Ankle Rules and fracture versus ligamentous injury distinction"},{"id":"sdl9_q31","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss differences between adult and pediatric bone stress.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl presents after a fall from a swing. Radiograph shows the radial diaphysis is angulated 25 degrees with the tension cortex clearly fractured and the compression cortex intact. Her parents ask why the bone did not break all the way through. What explanation is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"The periosteum in children is thicker and more firmly attached than in adults, generating enough mechanical resistance to halt fracture propagation before it can reach the compression cortex on the opposite side of the bone","B":"The child has open physes that absorb a portion of the mechanical energy from the fall before it can fully propagate through the radial diaphysis, diverting stress away from the cortex and limiting the fracture to one side","C":"Pediatric bone undergoes faster remodeling than adult bone, allowing the compression cortex to rapidly repair early microfractures as the force is applied, preventing the accumulation of damage required for complete cortical failure","D":"Pediatric bone has a higher collagen-to-mineral ratio and greater plastic deformability than adult bone, allowing the compression cortex to bend beyond its elastic limit without fracturing while the tension cortex fails first","E":"The nutrient foramen in pediatric bone is positioned on the tension cortex, creating a stress concentration point that causes the tension cortex to fail first while the compression cortex on the opposite side remains protected"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The greenstick fracture pattern is unique to pediatric bone because of its high plasticity. The collagen-rich matrix allows considerable bending before failure. During a bending force, the tension side experiences tensile stress and fails first. The compression side bends beyond the elastic limit but does not fracture because the ductile matrix can absorb the compressive energy. This is analogous to bending a green twig — it snaps on the tension side but the fibers on the other side hold together.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Greenstick fracture = tension side fractures, compression side bends. Unique to pediatric bone due to high collagen content. Treat with reduction under sedation if angulated over 15-20 degrees. Cast for 3-4 weeks. COMLEX: Greenstick fractures may need to be completed to achieve adequate reduction in severely angulated cases.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — Osteogenesis imperfecta: defective Type I collagen causing brittle bone and pediatric fracture susceptibility"},{"id":"sdl9_q32","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss differences between adult and pediatric bone stress.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 32-year-old distance runner has a 6-week history of progressively worsening anterior tibial pain. MRI shows periosteal edema with a partial cortical fracture of the anterior tibial diaphysis. The sports medicine physician explains that this stress fracture type is classified as high-risk. What makes anterior tibial cortex stress fractures particularly dangerous compared to posteromedial stress reactions?","choices":{"A":"The anterior tibial cortex has a thinner periosteal covering than the posterior cortex, reducing the osteoprogenitor cell density available for healing and causing the fracture to progress to cortical nonunion more rapidly","B":"The anterior tibial cortex is more metabolically active than the posterior cortex due to its proximity to the tibial nutrient foramen, generating more osteoclastic activity that outpaces osteoblastic repair during periods of high training load","C":"The anterior tibial cortex is subjected to higher shear forces than the posteromedial cortex during running gait because the anterior tibialis muscle origin inserts directly into this cortex and generates repetitive tensile avulsion forces","D":"The anterior tibial cortex is the most vascular region of the tibia due to the periosteal network from the anterior tibial artery, and the resulting hyperemia paradoxically accelerates osteoclastic resorption faster than osteoblasts can repair","E":"The anterior tibial cortex is under tension during weight bearing, which inhibits fracture healing and predisposes to propagation to a complete transverse fracture known as the dreaded black line fracture"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The anterior tibial cortex is under tensile stress during the loading phase of gait. Tension-side stress fractures heal poorly because the fracture gap is under opening rather than compressive forces. Healing requires the fracture to be under compression. The classic \"dreaded black line\" on lateral X-ray represents a chronic tension-side stress fracture. These fractures often require surgical fixation with an intramedullary nail rather than conservative management.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anterior tibial stress fracture = high risk = tension side = dreaded black line on lateral X-ray. Often needs IMN. Contrast with posteromedial tibial stress reaction from MTSS: low risk, responds to rest. COMLEX: Female athlete triad predisposes to stress fractures — screen with DEXA scan.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — Osteogenesis imperfecta: defective Type I collagen causing brittle bone and pediatric fracture susceptibility"},{"id":"sdl9_q33","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss differences between adult and pediatric bone stress.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An orthopedic surgeon tells residents that a 6-year-old child's femur fracture with 20-degree angular malunion can be observed rather than operatively corrected, because significant spontaneous remodeling is expected over the next 2 to 3 years. Which combination of factors is most predictive of successful remodeling in this clinical scenario?","choices":{"A":"Young age with significant remaining growth, fracture near a physis contributing to longitudinal growth, and deformity in the plane of motion of the adjacent hip or knee joint — all three factors maximize remodeling potential","B":"Large periosteal callus formation at the fracture site combined with early mobilization, which generates mechanical stimulus for Wolff's law-driven remodeling, independently correcting angular deformities through adaptive bone resorption and deposition","C":"High serum levels of insulin-like growth factor 1 and growth hormone in young children, which drive systemic bone formation at all sites including the fracture and are sufficient alone to correct angular deformities through accelerated remodeling","D":"Adequate calcium and vitamin D intake during the healing period combined with early weight bearing, which stimulates piezoelectric osteoblast activation throughout the healing bone and provides the mineral substrate for remodeling the malunited callus","E":"The severity of the initial angular deformity exceeding 20 degrees, which paradoxically stimulates greater Wolff's law-mediated remodeling response than smaller deformities through the more pronounced asymmetric mechanical loading it creates"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Three factors predict successful remodeling: young age with substantial remaining growth, fracture proximity to the physis contributing to longitudinal growth, and deformity in the plane of motion of the adjacent joint. Physeal growth corrects deformity as the bone elongates. Angular deformities up to 30 degrees can remodel in young children. Rotational deformities do not remodel reliably. The further the fracture from the dominant physis, the less remodeling occurs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pediatric fracture remodeling predictors: age under 8, physeal proximity, plane of joint motion. Rotation does NOT remodel. Valgus/varus in the coronal plane does. COMLEX: Acceptable angulation varies by age and location — more angulation accepted in younger children, less near wrist than hip.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — Osteogenesis imperfecta: defective Type I collagen causing brittle bone and pediatric fracture susceptibility"},{"id":"sdl9_q34","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the Salter-Harris System for classification of pediatric fractures near a growth plate.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 10-year-old male falls from a trampoline. Radiograph of the wrist shows the distal radial epiphysis displaced dorsally with a small triangular metaphyseal corner fragment, and the physis is widened. Which Salter-Harris type is this, and why does it carry a favorable prognosis?","choices":{"A":"Salter-Harris Type I, which carries favorable prognosis because the fracture passes entirely through the physis without producing any bony fragment, and the epiphysis can be reduced back to its anatomic position without disrupting the germinal layer","B":"Salter-Harris Type III, which carries moderate prognosis because the fracture involves the epiphysis and articular surface, requiring anatomic reduction to restore joint congruity and prevent post-traumatic arthritis at the wrist","C":"Salter-Harris Type IV, which carries guarded prognosis because the fracture crosses the physis in an oblique line from metaphysis through epiphysis, creating a bony bridge risk that can cause angular deformity as growth continues","D":"Salter-Harris Type II, which carries favorable prognosis because the fracture exits through the metaphysis sparing the germinal and proliferative chondrocyte zones of the physis that are responsible for longitudinal bone growth","E":"Salter-Harris Type V, which carries the worst prognosis of all types because the compressive force crushes the germinal chondrocytes in the physis without producing any visible fracture line on initial plain radiograph"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Type II is the most common physeal fracture and has the best prognosis among the types that produce a visible fracture line. The fracture exits through the metaphysis — the Thurston-Holland fragment — before reaching the epiphysis, which means the germinal and proliferative zones of the physis are spared. These are the chondrocyte layers responsible for longitudinal bone growth. Type I also has a good prognosis but does not produce a metaphyseal fragment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Salter-Harris Type II prognosis is favorable because germinal layer is spared. Thurston-Holland fragment = pathognomonic for Type II. Most common in distal radius. COMLEX: Growth arrest occurs when physeal blood supply is disrupted or when the germinal layer is directly injured — Type II largely avoids both.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 6 — Salter-Harris classification and greenstick fractures in pediatric patients"},{"id":"sdl9_q35","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the Salter-Harris System for classification of pediatric fractures near a growth plate.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 9-year-old male gymnast injures his wrist during vault training. Initial radiograph appears normal with only mild physeal widening. Six months later, the affected forearm is shorter than the contralateral side with progressive radial deviation deformity. What was the most likely initial injury, and why was it radiographically occult?","choices":{"A":"Salter-Harris Type III, which produces a fracture line visible on initial radiograph extending from the physis into the epiphysis, and the deformity developed because the intra-articular reduction was inadequate despite operative fixation","B":"Salter-Harris Type II, which produces a visible metaphyseal corner fragment at the time of injury, and the subsequent growth arrest indicates that the germinal layer was injured despite the favorable fracture pattern","C":"An occult torus fracture of the distal radial metaphysis, which buckles the dorsal cortex without a discrete fracture line, and the growth deformity resulted from inappropriate immobilization in a position that compressed the physis","D":"A ligamentous injury to the distal radioulnar joint causing chronic instability, which progressively transferred abnormal stress to the distal radial physis and caused asymmetric physeal closure from mechanical overload","E":"Salter-Harris Type V, an axial compression injury that crushes the germinal chondrocyte layer of the physis without producing a visible fracture line, causing physeal arrest and subsequent angular deformity as the ulna continues to grow"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Salter-Harris Type V is a crush injury to the physis. The compressive force damages the chondrocyte columns without producing a discrete fracture line visible on plain radiograph. It is often seen in sports requiring repetitive axial loading of the physis, such as gymnastics. The only finding may be subtle physeal widening acutely. Growth arrest is confirmed months later when leg or arm length discrepancy or angular deformity becomes apparent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Type V = axial crush = normal X-ray = delayed diagnosis = worst growth prognosis. Classic scenario: gymnast with wrist pain, normal X-ray, later develops premature physeal arrest. COMLEX: Treat with restriction from gymnastics and close follow-up with contralateral comparison films every 3-6 months.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 6 — Salter-Harris classification and greenstick fractures in pediatric patients"},{"id":"sdl9_q36","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the Salter-Harris System for classification of pediatric fractures near a growth plate.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pediatric orthopedic surgeon is teaching residents using the mnemonic SALTR for the Salter-Harris classification. She assigns each letter to a fracture type in ascending order of severity. What structure does the letter \"L\" represent, and which Salter-Harris type does it correspond to?","choices":{"A":"\"L\" represents the Ligamentous attachments to the periosteum, corresponding to Salter-Harris Type II, in which the periosteal ligaments are avulsed along with the metaphyseal Thurston-Holland corner fragment","B":"\"L\" represents Longitudinal crack propagation, corresponding to Salter-Harris Type I, in which the fracture propagates along the full length of the physis from one side of the bone to the other without entering metaphysis or epiphysis","C":"\"L\" represents the Lower epiphysis, corresponding to Salter-Harris Type III, in which the fracture line passes through the physis and exits downward through the epiphysis into the joint space","D":"\"L\" represents the Loss of growth potential, corresponding to Salter-Harris Type V, in which the physis is directly crushed and all potential for further longitudinal growth is immediately eliminated at the time of injury","E":"\"L\" represents the Lateral displacement of the epiphysis, corresponding to Salter-Harris Type I, in which the epiphysis slides laterally off the physis without producing any bony fragment on either the metaphyseal or epiphyseal side"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The SALTR mnemonic corresponds to: S = Slipped/through physis alone (Type I), A = Above the physis through the metaphysis (Type II), L = Lower through the epiphysis (Type III), T = Through both metaphysis and epiphysis (Type IV), R = Rammed/crushed physis (Type V). Each letter reflects the key structural involvement and direction of fracture propagation in ascending severity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SALTR mnemonic: Slipped, Above, Lower, Through, Rammed. Types I-II = favorable. Types III-IV = moderate, require anatomic reduction. Type V = worst prognosis. COMLEX: Use this mnemonic reliably — Salter-Harris classification is a boards staple for both USMLE and COMLEX.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 6 — Salter-Harris classification and greenstick fractures in pediatric patients"},{"id":"sdl9_q37","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman is struck by a car bumper at knee level. CT scan reveals lateral tibial plateau fracture with 9 mm articular depression. The surgeon discusses the Schatzker classification. Which Schatzker type is a lateral split-depression fracture, and what is the surgical threshold for articular depression?","choices":{"A":"Schatzker Type I is a pure lateral tibial plateau split fracture without depression, and surgical fixation is indicated for this type when articular step-off exceeds 10 mm, with conservative management acceptable below that threshold","B":"Schatzker Type IV is a medial tibial plateau fracture from a varus mechanism, and surgical fixation is indicated for any displacement because medial plateau fractures are associated with higher-energy mechanisms and peroneal nerve injury","C":"Schatzker Type VI is a bicondylar tibial plateau fracture with metaphyseal-diaphyseal dissociation, and the articular depression threshold for surgery is 5 mm, with any amount of cortical comminution serving as an independent surgical indication","D":"Schatzker Type III is a pure lateral depression fracture without a split component, and conservative management is the standard approach regardless of the degree of articular depression given the intact cortical rim provides inherent stability","E":"Schatzker Type II is a combined split-depression fracture of the lateral tibial plateau, and surgical reduction is generally indicated when articular depression exceeds 3 mm to prevent progressive valgus deformity and lateral compartment arthritis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Schatzker Type II is a split-depression fracture of the lateral tibial plateau, the most common pattern in older patients with osteoporotic bone. The articular surface is both split and depressed. The threshold for surgical intervention is depression greater than 3 mm, as this degree of articular incongruity leads to premature lateral compartment arthritis. Schatzker I is a pure split; Type III is pure depression; Type VI involves both plateaus with metaphyseal separation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Schatzker II = split-depression = most common tibial plateau fracture overall. Surgical threshold: over 3 mm articular depression. COMLEX: Associated injuries: popliteal artery injury with high Schatzker types. Check ABI and pulses. Compartment syndrome risk is high with bicondylar fractures.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — Knee fractures: tibial plateau and patellar fractures within the knee pain diagnostic pathway"},{"id":"sdl9_q38","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old female basketball player jumps and lands awkwardly with her knee partially flexed. She hears a pop, develops immediate swelling, and is brought to the emergency department. X-ray shows a small cortical avulsion at the lateral tibial rim just below the joint line. What is this finding, what does it indicate, and what further evaluation is required?","choices":{"A":"A Gerdy tubercle avulsion fracture at the iliotibial band insertion on the lateral tibia, indicating isolated IT band injury without associated ACL disruption, which is treated conservatively with immobilization and does not require MRI","B":"A Segond fracture, a capsular avulsion at the anterolateral tibial rim produced by the internal rotation-varus stress that tears the ACL, indicating high probability of ACL tear and requiring MRI to evaluate the ACL and menisci","C":"A fibular head avulsion fracture at the arcuate ligament insertion indicating posterolateral corner injury, which is associated with PCL tear rather than ACL tear and requires a different surgical approach for reconstruction","D":"A Stieda process fracture at the posterior medial femoral condyle indicating medial collateral ligament avulsion at its femoral attachment, which is diagnosed on AP radiograph and does not require further imaging beyond plain films","E":"A fabella fracture in the posterolateral capsule of the knee caused by the landing impact, which is a normal anatomical variant present in 20 percent of individuals and does not indicate underlying ligamentous injury"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Segond fracture is a small avulsion at the lateral tibial capsule attachment produced by internal rotation and varus stress. It is pathognomonic for ACL tear, which is found in over 90% of cases. Lateral meniscal tears and anterolateral ligament injuries are also commonly associated. MRI is mandatory to confirm ACL injury and assess for meniscal and other ligamentous pathology before surgical planning.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Segond fracture = lateral tibial rim avulsion = pathognomonic for ACL tear. MRI confirms ACL and evaluates menisci. Mechanism: internal rotation plus varus. COMLEX: The unhappy triad combines ACL + MCL + medial meniscus injury from a lateral valgus blow with internal rotation at the knee.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — Knee fractures: tibial plateau and patellar fractures within the knee pain diagnostic pathway"},{"id":"sdl9_q39","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old osteoporotic woman falls from standing and sustains a lateral tibial plateau fracture with 10 mm of articular depression on CT scan. Orthopedic surgery plans for open reduction and internal fixation. What specific intraoperative challenge is unique to osteoporotic bone in this fracture type?","choices":{"A":"Osteoporotic bone cannot be imaged adequately with standard CT protocols at the time of surgery, requiring intraoperative fluoroscopy alone to guide reduction, which is less accurate than preoperative CT planning for achieving anatomic articular reduction","B":"Osteoporotic bone heals more slowly than normal bone due to reduced osteoblast density, requiring double the standard immobilization period after fixation and restricting weight bearing for 6 months rather than the usual 3 months","C":"Osteoporotic bone has insufficient structural strength to hold fixation screws and support a subchondral raft beneath the reduced articular surface, requiring bone graft or calcium phosphate substitute to fill the metaphyseal void after elevation","D":"Osteoporotic bone generates insufficient callus around implants due to low bone turnover, requiring prophylactic bisphosphonate therapy to be started immediately after surgery to prevent fixation failure and implant migration","E":"Osteoporotic bone cannot sustain the traction forces required for closed reduction of the articular surface, making open surgical elevation mandatory regardless of the degree of depression, even for fractures with less than 3 mm of articular depression"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When a depressed tibial plateau fragment is elevated in osteoporotic bone, the metaphyseal void left beneath the elevated articular surface has insufficient strength to resist subsidence. This void must be filled with autograft, allograft, or calcium phosphate bone substitute to maintain reduction. Failure to address this void leads to loss of reduction and articular re-depression under weight bearing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tibial plateau elevation creates metaphyseal void in osteoporotic bone — requires bone graft or substitute. Schatzker II in elderly = high risk for subsidence. COMLEX: Calcium phosphate cements are commonly used as synthetic bone substitutes in elderly osteoporotic plateau fractures.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — Knee fractures: tibial plateau and patellar fractures within the knee pain diagnostic pathway"},{"id":"sdl9_q40","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of tibial shaft fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 27-year-old sustains a closed tibial shaft fracture. Four hours after splinting, the nurse reports that the patient is screaming in pain despite IV morphine. On examination, the anterior compartment is tense and the patient has paresthesias in the first dorsal web space. Compartment pressure is measured at 45 mmHg with a mean arterial pressure of 90 mmHg. What is the delta-pressure and what is the appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"The delta-pressure is 45 mmHg, which exceeds the 30 mmHg threshold, indicating the compartment pressure is safely below dangerous levels and the patient can be managed with repeat pressure monitoring every 30 minutes","B":"The delta-pressure is 90 mmHg minus 45 mmHg, which equals 45 mmHg, exceeding the 30 mmHg threshold and confirming that fasciotomy is not yet indicated; continued observation with pain management is appropriate","C":"The delta-pressure cannot be calculated from systolic blood pressure and must use diastolic blood pressure, so the given information is insufficient to determine whether fasciotomy is indicated based on pressure measurement alone","D":"The delta-pressure of 45 mmHg indicates adequate perfusion, so the severe pain is attributed to narcotic tolerance requiring patient-controlled analgesia and adjunct non-opioid medications before surgical intervention is considered","E":"The delta-pressure is 45 mmHg, which is above the threshold of 30 mmHg, but the clinical examination demonstrates active compartment syndrome; emergent four-compartment fasciotomy is required regardless because clinical signs override numerical thresholds"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Delta pressure = diastolic blood pressure minus compartment pressure. Diastolic blood pressure is not given; however, mean arterial pressure is 90 mmHg. The critical teaching point is that clinical compartment syndrome — severe pain, pain with passive stretch, tense compartments, paresthesias — is a clinical diagnosis that warrants emergent fasciotomy even before pressure measurement is completed. Compartment pressure alone should never delay fasciotomy when clinical signs are present.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Delta-P = diastolic BP minus compartment pressure. Fasciotomy threshold: delta-P at or below 30 mmHg. Clinical signs precede pressure changes — never delay fasciotomy for pressure measurement when the exam is diagnostic. COMLEX: Four leg compartments: anterior, lateral, deep posterior, superficial posterior.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — MTSS: periostitis of the posteromedial tibia as the overuse-spectrum counterpart to tibial shaft fracture"},{"id":"sdl9_q41","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of tibial shaft fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An 18-month-old is brought to the emergency department by her parents after they noticed she stopped walking. X-ray shows a non-displaced spiral lucency of the mid-tibial diaphysis. Parents report no witnessed trauma. What is the diagnosis, and what is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"A non-accidental injury fracture pattern requiring mandatory child protective services report, because spiral tibial fractures in toddlers are pathognomonic for child abuse and cannot result from accidental mechanisms","B":"A pathologic fracture through a unicameral bone cyst requiring urgent orthopaedic referral and biopsy to rule out malignancy, since non-displaced spiral fractures in toddlers without witnessed trauma are suspicious for underlying bone disease","C":"A toddler's fracture, a non-displaced spiral tibial shaft fracture from a minor twisting mechanism in ambulatory children under 3 years old, managed with a long leg cast for 3 to 4 weeks with expected full recovery","D":"A Salter-Harris Type I physeal fracture of the distal tibia rather than a diaphyseal fracture, and it requires reduction followed by casting to prevent physeal arrest given the growth plate involvement in this age group","E":"A stress fracture from excessive parental pressure for early ambulation requiring behavioral counseling and reduced weight-bearing activity, since 18-month-old children should not yet be expected to walk and premature ambulation overloads the tibial cortex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Toddler's fracture is a classic diagnosis in ambulatory children aged 9 months to 3 years who sustain a minor twisting injury. The fracture is characteristically non-displaced and spiral, occurring in the tibial diaphysis. It presents with refusal to bear weight or limp. The mechanism is often minor and unwitnessed. Treatment is a long leg cast for 3 to 4 weeks. It is NOT pathognomonic for abuse, though non-accidental injury must always be in the differential.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Toddler's fracture = non-displaced spiral tibial fracture in child under 3. Often unwitnessed, benign minor mechanism. Long leg cast 3-4 weeks. Not pathognomonic for abuse — evaluate clinical context. COMLEX: Classic test question: distinguish toddler's fracture from abuse. Key factors: age, fracture pattern, multiple fractures, delayed presentation, inconsistent history.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — MTSS: periostitis of the posteromedial tibia as the overuse-spectrum counterpart to tibial shaft fracture"},{"id":"sdl9_q42","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of tibial shaft fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 36-year-old sustains a Gustilo Type IIIB open tibial shaft fracture in a motorcycle crash with a 5 cm wound and periosteal stripping. What is the most critical time-sensitive intervention, and what is the recommended sequence of management?","choices":{"A":"Emergency wound closure by the plastic surgery team is the most time-critical intervention, as the 6-hour window for primary closure determines whether the wound can be closed or requires a rotational flap with significantly higher complication risk","B":"Intravenous antibiotics administered within 1 hour of injury are the most time-critical intervention, followed by emergent irrigation and debridement in the operating room, temporary external fixation, and delayed wound closure after serial debridement","C":"Definitive intramedullary nailing at the index procedure is the priority to achieve mechanical stability before wound colonization occurs, since the first 6 hours represent the golden window for definitive fixation in contaminated open fractures","D":"CT angiography of the affected extremity is the first priority to exclude popliteal artery injury before any other intervention, as vascular repair must precede both wound management and fracture stabilization in all high-energy tibial fractures","E":"Wound culture and sensitivity testing before antibiotic administration ensures that prophylaxis is targeted to the specific organisms present in the contaminating environment, avoiding unnecessary broad-spectrum antibiotic use and resistance selection"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The most time-critical intervention in open fracture management is IV antibiotics within one hour of injury to prevent osteomyelitis. Cefazolin covers gram-positive organisms for Gustilo Types I and II. Gram-negative coverage is added for Type III. Management sequence: antibiotics in ED, then emergent OR debridement, then temporary external fixation, then serial debridement every 48-72 hours until clean, then soft tissue reconstruction, then definitive fixation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Open fracture antibiotics within 1 hour — the single most important intervention. Gustilo IIIB requires plastic surgery for soft tissue coverage. Do NOT close primarily. Serial debridements every 48-72 hours. COMLEX: Antibiotics for open fractures: cefazolin for Type I-II; add aminoglycoside for Type III; add penicillin G for farm contamination.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — MTSS: periostitis of the posteromedial tibia as the overuse-spectrum counterpart to tibial shaft fracture"},{"id":"sdl9_q43","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of fibular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old soccer player pronates his ankle forcefully. In the emergency department he has medial ankle tenderness and ecchymosis, but ankle X-rays show only mild mortise widening without a fibular fracture. On palpation, he has proximal fibular tenderness. What is the full injury diagnosis, and what additional imaging is mandatory?","choices":{"A":"A Maisonneuve fracture with syndesmotic disruption and deltoid ligament injury; full-length tibia-fibula radiographs are mandatory to identify the proximal fibular fracture that will be missed on ankle films alone","B":"An isolated deltoid ligament sprain with ankle mortise stress injury; no additional imaging beyond standard ankle views is needed since the mortise widening is mild and the patient should be treated with immobilization and gradual weight bearing","C":"A proximal tibiofibular joint dislocation from a torsional mechanism; knee radiographs are mandatory to evaluate the proximal tibiofibular articulation and rule out concurrent lateral compartment injury at the knee","D":"A stress fracture of the fibular shaft from chronic repetitive soccer training that was acutely displaced by the pronation mechanism; MRI of the entire fibula is mandatory to determine the extent of stress reaction before planning treatment","E":"A Lisfranc midfoot injury with tarsometatarsal disruption that referred pain proximally; weight-bearing foot radiographs are mandatory to evaluate the Lisfranc joint, and the proximal fibular tenderness represents a referred pain pattern"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Maisonneuve fracture results from a pronation-external rotation mechanism. The medial ankle structures fail first, then the force transmits proximally along the interosseous membrane, producing a proximal fibular fracture. The ankle appears relatively normal on standard views, but mortise widening indicates instability. Full-length tibia-fibula films are mandatory. This injury requires surgical fixation including syndesmotic stabilization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Maisonneuve = proximal fibula fracture from ankle mechanism. Mortise widening without distal fibular fracture = get full leg films. Associated with deltoid ligament rupture. Unstable ankle requiring syndesmotic screw fixation. COMLEX: Classic boards pitfall: treat the ankle, miss the proximal fibula.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injuries: common peroneal nerve vulnerability at the fibular neck"},{"id":"sdl9_q44","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of fibular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old female sustains a distal fibular fracture at the level of the ankle mortise. On clinical examination, the medial ankle is tender. Stress examination under fluoroscopy shows 4 mm of medial clear space widening with external rotation force. What is the Danis-Weber classification and what is the management implication of the stress finding?","choices":{"A":"Weber A fracture below the syndesmosis; the medial clear space widening indicates associated deltoid ligament sprain rather than rupture, and can be managed non-operatively since below-syndesmosis fractures are inherently stable regardless of ligamentous tenderness","B":"Weber C fracture above the syndesmosis; syndesmotic disruption is confirmed by the proximal fibular fracture level, and the stress examination finding of medial widening is expected for this fracture type and does not add additional management information","C":"Weber B fracture at the syndesmotic level; the 4 mm medial clear space widening on stress examination indicates deltoid ligament rupture, converting this from a potentially stable to a definitively unstable ankle fracture requiring surgical fixation","D":"A bimalleolar equivalent fracture by definition; any fibular fracture with medial ankle tenderness must be treated as a bimalleolar fracture with immediate surgical fixation of the fibula and medial ankle reconstruction without further stress testing","E":"An unstable fracture-dislocation of the ankle; 4 mm of mortise widening indicates complete dislocation of the talus, and emergent closed reduction with sedation must be performed immediately before any further diagnostic workup or imaging"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Weber B fractures are at the level of the syndesmosis and may be stable or unstable depending on the medial ligamentous structures. A medial clear space of greater than 4 mm (or greater than the space between the lateral border of the medial malleolus and the medial border of the talus) on stress examination indicates deltoid ligament incompetence. This makes the ankle mortise unstable, requiring ORIF. The stress test distinguishes truly isolated, stable Weber B fractures from unstable bimalleolar equivalents.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Weber B = at syndesmosis = variable stability. Stress X-ray determines if deltoid ligament is intact. Medial clear space over 4 mm = unstable = surgery. COMLEX: Bimalleolar equivalent fracture = lateral malleolus fracture plus deltoid ligament rupture — same instability as bimalleolar, requires same surgery.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injuries: common peroneal nerve vulnerability at the fibular neck"},{"id":"sdl9_q45","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of fibular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old male football player is struck on the lateral proximal leg just below the knee. He immediately develops foot drop with weakness of ankle dorsiflexion and toe extension, and reports numbness over the dorsum of the foot and the first web space. Which nerve is injured, at what specific anatomical location, and what is the expected prognosis?","choices":{"A":"The deep peroneal nerve at the anterior ankle retinaculum, where it passes under the extensor retinaculum and can be compressed by a tight boot or direct blow, producing dorsal foot numbness and toe extension weakness without complete foot drop","B":"The common peroneal nerve at the fibular neck, where it wraps around the posterolateral fibular head in a superficial location that makes it vulnerable to direct compression and traction injury, with variable recovery depending on severity of axonal injury","C":"The superficial peroneal nerve at the crural fascia in the distal lateral leg, where it perforates the deep fascia and is vulnerable to injury, producing only sensory loss over the dorsolateral foot without any motor weakness or foot drop","D":"The tibial nerve at the popliteal fossa, where it travels with the popliteal artery and can be injured by posterior knee dislocation, producing plantarflexion weakness and plantar foot numbness rather than the dorsiflexion weakness seen here","E":"The saphenous nerve at the medial knee, where it exits the adductor canal and descends along the medial leg, producing purely sensory deficits along the medial leg and foot without any motor involvement or foot drop"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The common peroneal nerve wraps around the fibular neck just below the lateral knee and is covered only by skin and subcutaneous fat at that level. It is the most commonly injured nerve in the lower extremity, highly vulnerable to direct trauma, compression, or fracture at the fibular head. Injury produces foot drop from tibialis anterior paralysis and sensory loss over the dorsum of the foot including the first web space. Recovery depends on nerve continuity — neuropraxia recovers in weeks to months; axonotmesis may take months to a year.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Common peroneal nerve at fibular neck = foot drop. Most commonly injured lower extremity nerve. Causes: direct blow, fibular head fracture, tight cast, prolonged leg crossing. First web space numbness is pathognomonic. COMLEX: Also injured during fibular head fracture reduction — protect the nerve during all proximal fibular procedures.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injuries: common peroneal nerve vulnerability at the fibular neck"},{"id":"sdl9_q46","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of ankle fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 25-year-old male lands from a jump with his foot supinated and plantarflexed. X-ray shows a fibular fracture 4 cm above the ankle joint level and above the tibiofibular syndesmosis, with medial clear space widening of 5 mm. What is the Weber classification, what does the medial widening indicate, and what is the management?","choices":{"A":"Weber A fracture below the syndesmosis with medial widening indicating a deltoid ligament sprain, managed non-operatively with a short leg cast since below-syndesmosis fractures are inherently stable and do not require syndesmotic repair","B":"Weber B fracture at the syndesmosis with medial widening indicating probable deltoid ligament injury, requiring stress examination under fluoroscopy to determine stability before deciding between operative and non-operative management","C":"Weber C fracture above the syndesmosis with medial clear space widening indicating syndesmotic disruption and deltoid ligament rupture, requiring fibular ORIF and syndesmotic stabilization with a positional screw or suture-button device","D":"A Maisonneuve fracture pattern at the proximal fibula with ankle mortise disruption, distinguished from Weber C by the fracture level being at the fibular neck rather than the distal fibular shaft, requiring full-length fibula films and proximal fixation","E":"An isolated distal fibular fracture without syndesmotic injury despite the apparent medial widening, which represents a normal anatomical variant in hypermobile patients and should be managed with cast immobilization without operative intervention"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Weber C fractures are above the tibiofibular syndesmosis by definition and imply syndesmotic disruption because the fibula must fracture above the intact syndesmosis for this fracture level to occur. The 5 mm medial clear space widening confirms deltoid ligament rupture, making this a highly unstable ankle fracture. Management requires fibular fixation to restore length and alignment, and syndesmotic stabilization to allow the syndesmosis to heal anatomically.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Weber C = above syndesmosis = unstable = always needs surgery. Medial clear space over 4-5 mm = deltoid ligament rupture. Syndesmotic screw placed through fibula into tibia 2-4 cm above mortise. COMLEX: The suture-button device for syndesmosis allows some physiologic ankle motion compared to a rigid screw.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: ligamentous injury spectrum and Ottawa Ankle Rules guiding fracture workup"},{"id":"sdl9_q47","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of ankle fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman slips on ice and sustains a bimalleolar ankle fracture. Radiograph shows 3 mm lateral talar shift within the ankle mortise. An orthopedic resident asks the attending why this small amount of displacement is clinically significant. What is the physiological explanation?","choices":{"A":"Lateral talar shift stretches the deltoid ligament beyond its recoverable elastic range, causing permanent elongation that will prevent the ankle from returning to its stable weight-bearing position after fracture healing is complete","B":"Even 1 mm of lateral talar shift reduces tibiotalar contact area by 42 percent and 3 mm reduces it by over 60 percent, dramatically increasing peak contact stress on the remaining articular cartilage and predicting early post-traumatic arthritis","C":"Lateral talar shift by 3 mm indicates that the fibula is shortened by the same amount, and fibular shortening of 3 mm is the threshold above which the ankle develops a fixed valgus deformity that cannot be corrected by cast immobilization alone","D":"Lateral talar shift represents subtalar joint subluxation rather than tibiotalar shift, and the subtalar joint is more sensitive to small displacements than the tibiotalar joint because of its complex three-dimensional motion requirements","E":"Lateral talar shift indicates complete rupture of all four ankle ligament complexes simultaneously, which is the threshold injury required to produce even 1 mm of talar displacement under physiologic loading conditions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The tibiotalar joint has a small articular surface area compared to the hip or knee. Lateral talar shift dramatically reduces this already-limited contact area. One millimeter of lateral shift reduces tibiotalar contact by 42%; 3 mm reduces it by approximately 60%. This creates focal stress concentration on the remaining articular cartilage, accelerating chondral wear and post-traumatic arthritis. This biomechanical reality is the primary justification for anatomic surgical reduction of ankle fractures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lateral talar shift 1 mm = 42% contact area loss. 3 mm = over 60% loss. Even subtle shift = dramatically increased arthritis risk. COMLEX: This is why ankle fractures with mortise disruption require anatomic ORIF — the tibiotalar joint cannot tolerate any incongruity without long-term consequences.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: ligamentous injury spectrum and Ottawa Ankle Rules guiding fracture workup"},{"id":"sdl9_q48","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of ankle fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old sustains a trimalleolar ankle fracture. CT scan shows the posterior malleolus fragment involves 30 percent of the articular surface. The orthopedic surgeon decides to fix all three malleoli. What rationale justifies fixing the posterior malleolus specifically, beyond achieving articular reduction?","choices":{"A":"Fixation of the posterior malleolus indirectly stabilizes the distal tibiofibular syndesmosis because the posterior inferior tibiofibular ligament attaches to the posterior malleolus, and reducing the fragment anatomically restores syndesmotic integrity without a separate syndesmotic screw","B":"Fixation of the posterior malleolus prevents Achilles tendon avulsion from its calcaneal insertion by reducing the posterior tibial cortex, which transmits tensile forces from the triceps surae through the posterior ankle capsule to the posterior malleolus","C":"Fixation of the posterior malleolus is required in all trimalleolar fractures regardless of fragment size because any posterior bony fragment creates a mechanical block to ankle dorsiflexion that prevents return to normal gait without operative reduction","D":"Fixation of the posterior malleolus prevents propagation of the fracture anteriorly into the tibial plafond under weight-bearing stress because the unfixed posterior fragment acts as a stress riser for pilon-type fracture extension in the early post-operative period","E":"Fixation of the posterior malleolus is necessary to reattach the posterior tibiotalar ligament at its tibial origin, since ligamentous avulsions at the posterior malleolus heal inadequately without bony fixation due to the poor vascularity of the posterior ankle capsule"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The posterior inferior tibiofibular ligament, a component of the syndesmotic complex, attaches to the posterior malleolus. When the posterior malleolus fragment is anatomically reduced and fixed, this ligament is restored to its normal length and tension, which substantially stabilizes the distal tibiofibular syndesmosis. This is one of the main reasons to fix posterior malleolus fragments over 25% — it reduces or eliminates the need for a separate syndesmotic screw.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior inferior tibiofibular ligament attaches to posterior malleolus. Fixing the posterior malleolus indirectly stabilizes the syndesmosis. Fragment over 25% articular surface = fix it. COMLEX: This is a frequently tested syndesmotic anatomy point — the syndesmotic complex includes the AITFL, PITFL, and interosseous membrane.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: ligamentous injury spectrum and Ottawa Ankle Rules guiding fracture workup"}]},{"sdlNumber":10,"title":"Pediatric Overuse Injuries & Apophysitis","questions":[{"id":"sdl10_q01","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of radial head subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 2-year-old girl is brought to urgent care after her father pulled her arm to keep her from running into traffic. She now holds her left arm adducted and refuses to move it. Which of the following best describes the mechanism that produces radial head subluxation?","choices":{"A":"Forced supination of the forearm causes the radial head to displace anteriorly against the capitellum, rupturing the annular ligament and producing a stable partial dislocation that requires reduction under procedural sedation","B":"A fall onto an outstretched hand transfers axial load up the radius, compressing the radial head against the capitellum and producing a buckle-type physeal injury at the proximal radial growth plate in young children","C":"Axial traction on a pronated, extended forearm causes the annular ligament to slip distally over the radial head and become interposed between the radial head and capitellum in the radiohumeral joint, producing the subluxation","D":"Valgus stress applied to the elbow during a throwing motion causes the radial head to rotate excessively within the annular ligament, producing microavulsions at the ligament proximal attachment to the lateral ulna","E":"Hyperextension of the elbow from a swinging lift causes the olecranon to lever against the posterior humerus, driving the radial head anteriorly through the annular ligament and producing a complete anterior radial head dislocation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Radial head subluxation occurs when axial traction is applied to a pronated, extended forearm. The annular ligament, which holds the radial head in place, slips distally over the radial head and becomes interposed between the radial head and capitellum. This is the classic mechanism of nursemaid's elbow, typically occurring when a caregiver pulls or swings a young child by the arm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic mechanism = axial traction on pronated extended forearm causes annular ligament to slip over radial head. Age 1-4, F > M, left > right. Reduction: supination with flexion produces a palpable click. COMLEX: No imaging needed unless age >5 or atypical mechanism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — Elbow joint dislocations: reduction and management of pediatric elbow joint injuries"},{"id":"sdl10_q02","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of radial head subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pediatric emergency physician notes that nursemaid's elbow accounts for a large volume of pediatric elbow injuries. Which of the following epidemiological statements about radial head subluxation is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Radial head subluxation accounts for approximately 20,000 cases per year in the United States, occurs most commonly between ages 1 and 4 with 80 percent of cases in the 1-to-3 age group, is more common in females than males, and more commonly affects the left arm than the right","B":"Radial head subluxation occurs most commonly between ages 5 and 10, is more common in males than females, and is equally distributed between left and right arms because the mechanism of injury is symmetric and not influenced by caregiver handedness","C":"Radial head subluxation occurs almost exclusively in infants under age 12 months due to extreme ligamentous laxity present in early infancy, and it rarely recurs after spontaneous resolution because the annular ligament matures and thickens rapidly","D":"Radial head subluxation affects children up to age 12, occurs with equal frequency in males and females, and is most commonly bilateral because caregivers typically use both hands to lift children by their arms simultaneously","E":"Radial head subluxation primarily affects children between ages 1 and 4, is more common in males than females due to higher activity levels, and most commonly affects the right arm because most caregivers are right-handed and hold the child by the right arm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Radial head subluxation affects approximately 20,000 children per year in the US. It is most common between ages 1 and 4, with 80% of cases in the 1-to-3 age range, reflecting the period when the annular ligament is lax relative to the radial head. It is more common in females and more often affects the left arm, likely because right-handed caregivers pull the child's left arm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Age 1-4 peak; F > M; left > right. 20,000 cases/year US. COMLEX: Clinical diagnosis — no imaging unless atypical. Mechanism and demographics are high-yield for question stems.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — Elbow joint dislocations: reduction and management of pediatric elbow joint injuries"},{"id":"sdl10_q03","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of radial head subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy is brought to clinic after a yanking injury to his arm. He holds the arm adducted to the body with the elbow extended and the forearm slightly pronated. Which of the following additional findings is most consistent with radial head subluxation?","choices":{"A":"The child has visible swelling and ecchymosis over the lateral elbow, passive pronation reproduces pain more than supination, and he allows full elbow flexion but refuses any forearm rotation in either direction with marked resistance","B":"The child has a prominent posterior bulge at the olecranon from posterior displacement of the ulna, and the elbow is locked in slight flexion with complete refusal to allow any passive range of motion including supination, pronation, and flexion","C":"The child screams with lateral elbow palpation and cannot passively flex the elbow beyond 90 degrees due to guarding from protective muscle spasm of the biceps brachii, which prevents forearm rotation in any direction","D":"The child has no visible deformity, demonstrates pain specifically with attempted supination of the forearm, may have mild tenderness to palpation at the radial head, and otherwise has normal passive range of elbow motion","E":"The child holds the arm in a sling position with the elbow flexed to 90 degrees, has significant swelling over the antecubital fossa, and demonstrates specific point tenderness over the medial epicondyle with valgus stress testing of the elbow"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Radial head subluxation presents with the arm adducted, the elbow extended or slightly flexed, and the forearm pronated. Pain occurs specifically with supination because this motion attempts to slide the annular ligament back over the radial head. There is no significant swelling, no visible deformity, and the child typically has normal passive range of motion in all planes except supination.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Key exam findings = arm adducted, elbow extended, forearm pronated, pain with supination, no swelling/deformity. Normal X-ray. COMLEX: Reduction by supination with flexion at elbow — palpable click = success, child uses arm within minutes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — Elbow joint dislocations: reduction and management of pediatric elbow joint injuries"},{"id":"sdl10_q04","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of little league shoulder and little league elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old male competitive pitcher presents with progressive right shoulder pain during and after pitching. X-ray shows subtle widening of the proximal humeral growth plate on the right compared to the left. Which of the following most accurately describes the pathology?","choices":{"A":"Rotator cuff tendinopathy from repetitive overhead motion causes progressive inflammation of the supraspinatus tendon at its insertion on the greater tuberosity, resulting in impingement under the coracoacromial arch during the late acceleration phase of throwing","B":"Repetitive microtrauma to the posterior glenoid labrum during the deceleration phase of pitching produces labral tears that destabilize the glenohumeral joint and cause superior humeral head migration during the follow-through phase of the throw","C":"Calcific tendinosis of the biceps long head tendon at its attachment to the superior labrum produces SLAP lesions that extend into the posterior labrum and cause clicking during the late cocking phase in overhead throwing athletes","D":"Acromioclavicular joint sprain from repetitive overhead stress on the superior shoulder complex in a throwing athlete causes capsular laxity and progressive superior migration of the distal clavicle relative to the acromion during pitching","E":"Repetitive stress from excessive throwing produces a physeal stress reaction at the proximal humeral growth plate, resulting in physeal widening and pain during or after throwing activity; this condition most commonly affects adolescent male pitchers and is called little league shoulder"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Little league shoulder is a stress reaction or Salter-Harris Type I physeal injury of the proximal humeral growth plate caused by repetitive rotational forces during throwing. It most commonly affects adolescent male pitchers aged 11-16, with a peak at age 13. X-ray may show physeal widening compared to the contralateral shoulder. Treatment is rest from throwing for up to 3 months, analgesics, and PT, with pitch count programs for prevention.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Little league shoulder = proximal humeral physeal stress reaction; age 11-16, peak 13, males. X-ray widening of growth plate. Treatment: rest up to 3 months. COMLEX: Prevention by pitch count limits is testable.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — Medial epicondylitis: valgus overuse stress at the medial elbow in adult and pediatric throwing athletes"},{"id":"sdl10_q05","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of little league shoulder and little league elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 12-year-old pitcher is diagnosed with little league elbow after presenting with medial elbow pain worse with pitching. Which of the following most accurately describes the pathomechanics of this injury?","choices":{"A":"Repetitive extension forces during ball release place tensile stress on the lateral collateral ligament complex, producing chronic lateral elbow instability and progressive osteochondral damage to the capitellum in young throwing athletes","B":"Combined valgus and distracting forces during the cocking and acceleration phases of pitching produce repetitive traction stress on the medial epicondylar growth plate, causing apophysitis or avulsion fracture of the medial epicondyle","C":"Overuse of the flexor-pronator mass from repetitive wrist flexion during follow-through causes tendinous degeneration at the medial epicondyle, producing a condition identical to adult medial epicondylitis but with greater recurrence due to open growth plates","D":"Repetitive compressive forces from the medial coronoid against the trochlea during the acceleration phase produce subchondral bone edema at the trochlea, progressing to articular cartilage fragmentation in severe or untreated cases","E":"Repetitive supination of the forearm during the pronation-to-supination transition in the late acceleration phase causes the annular ligament to become inflamed, entrapping the posterior interosseous nerve and producing lateral elbow pain"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Little league elbow is apophysitis or avulsion fracture of the medial epicondylar growth plate caused by combined valgus and distracting forces during the cocking and acceleration phases of pitching. The medial epicondylar growth plate normally closes in the mid-teens, making it vulnerable during adolescence. It most commonly affects pitchers aged 9-14, with avulsion fractures more common in ages 12-14.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Little league elbow = medial epicondylar apophysitis or avulsion from valgus and traction forces during pitching. Age 9-14. Growth plate closes mid-teens. X-ray shows medial epicondyle widening or displacement. COMLEX: Treatment = rest 4-6 weeks, analgesics, PT; surgery rare but indicated for significant displacement with instability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — Medial epicondylitis: valgus overuse stress at the medial elbow in adult and pediatric throwing athletes"},{"id":"sdl10_q06","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of little league shoulder and little league elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 10-year-old pitcher is diagnosed with little league elbow. X-ray shows medial epicondyle apophyseal widening without fragment displacement. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":{"A":"Rest from throwing for 4 to 6 weeks, analgesic therapy for pain control, physical therapy for gradual rehabilitation, and return to sport guided by an age-appropriate pitch count program to prevent recurrence","B":"Immediate operative fixation with percutaneous screw placement across the medial epicondyle is indicated for all cases of little league elbow, because any delay in surgical stabilization results in permanent medial instability from growth plate damage","C":"A long arm cast applied with the elbow at 90 degrees of flexion and the forearm in full supination is standard conservative treatment, maintained for 8 to 12 weeks regardless of fragment size or degree of physeal widening on radiograph","D":"Corticosteroid injection at the medial epicondyle is first-line treatment for little league elbow, providing rapid anti-inflammatory relief that allows safe return to throwing within 1 to 2 weeks when combined with a counterforce brace","E":"Transition to batting-only practice with continued half-effort throwing is an acceptable alternative to full rest in young athletes, as partial activity maintains muscle conditioning while allowing gradual healing of the growth plate apophysis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Non-displaced little league elbow is managed conservatively with 4-6 weeks of rest from throwing, analgesics, and physical therapy. Return to sport should be gradual and guided by pitch count programs, which are the primary preventive tool. Surgery is reserved for significantly displaced fragments or cases with valgus instability, which are uncommon at this age without a clear avulsion fracture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Non-displaced little league elbow = rest 4-6 weeks, analgesics, PT. Surgery rare. Prevention = pitch counts. COMLEX: Distinguish from adult medial epicondylitis — in children, the growth plate is the weak link, not the UCL.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — Medial epicondylitis: valgus overuse stress at the medial elbow in adult and pediatric throwing athletes"},{"id":"sdl10_q07","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Osgood-Schlatter disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old male athlete presents with anterior knee pain and a prominent, tender bump just below the kneecap that worsens with running, jumping, and kneeling. He is in a period of rapid growth. Which of the following best describes the underlying pathological process?","choices":{"A":"Patellofemoral syndrome caused by maltracking of the patella in the trochlear groove, producing retropatellar cartilage friction and diffuse peripatellar pain with prolonged sitting, stair climbing, and squatting in rapidly growing adolescent athletes","B":"Patellar tendinopathy from repetitive microtrauma at the inferior pole of the patella, producing degenerative changes within the tendon substance and localized swelling at the superior insertion of the patellar tendon on the patella itself","C":"Prepatellar bursitis from repetitive mechanical irritation of the superficial bursa anterior to the patella, producing soft tissue swelling that is fluctuant on palpation and localizes to the anterior patella rather than the tibial tuberosity","D":"Stress fracture of the proximal tibial metaphysis from repetitive compressive loading, producing point tenderness over the medial tibial flare and periosteal new bone formation visible on plain radiographs of the proximal tibia below the joint line","E":"Traction apophysitis at the insertion of the patellar tendon on the tibial tuberosity, caused by repetitive quadriceps contraction during rapid skeletal growth, producing pain and bony prominence at the tibial tuberosity that worsens with knee flexion activities"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Osgood-Schlatter disease is traction apophysitis at the tibial tuberosity, where the patellar tendon inserts. Repetitive quadriceps contraction during rapid growth applies repetitive traction stress to the tibial tuberosity apophysis, causing inflammation, pain, and eventual bony prominence. It is most common in active adolescents aged 9-14, predominantly males, and bilateral in 25-50% of cases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osgood-Schlatter = traction apophysitis at tibial tuberosity, patellar tendon insertion. Age 9-14, M > F, athletes 2x risk, bilateral 25-50%. Pain with knee flexion activities. COMLEX: No imaging required. Treatment = activity modification, PT, analgesics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — Patellofemoral syndrome: anterior knee pain in adolescents and diagnostic differentiation from apophysitis"},{"id":"sdl10_q08","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Osgood-Schlatter disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following most accurately describes the epidemiology of Osgood-Schlatter disease?","choices":{"A":"Osgood-Schlatter disease affects children between ages 5 and 9, is equally common in males and females, and almost always presents bilaterally because patellar tendon stress is symmetric across both knees during growth spurts in all active children","B":"Osgood-Schlatter disease most commonly affects children between ages 9 and 14, is more common in males than females, carries approximately double the risk in athletes compared to non-athletes, and presents bilaterally in 25 to 50 percent of cases","C":"Osgood-Schlatter disease primarily affects obese adolescents between ages 12 and 17 and is more common in females, since a high-riding patella in females increases patellar tendon length and amplifies tensile stress at the tibial tuberosity during running","D":"Osgood-Schlatter disease is equally common across all age groups from 8 to 18, shows no sex predilection, and is more prevalent in sedentary adolescents because inactivity leads to relative tightening of the quadriceps mechanism during growth","E":"Osgood-Schlatter disease affects children between ages 6 and 12, is more common in females due to earlier onset of growth spurts, and accounts for the majority of all anterior knee pain presentations seen in pediatric primary care visits"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Osgood-Schlatter disease peaks at ages 9-14, coinciding with the adolescent growth spurt. Males are more commonly affected. Athletes have approximately double the risk compared to non-athletes. Bilateral presentation occurs in 25-50% of cases. The incidence in adolescents aged 12-15 has been estimated at approximately 9.83%.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Age 9-14, M > F, athletes 2x risk, bilateral 25-50%. Rapid growth is the key predisposing factor. COMLEX: The fact that imaging is not necessary is commonly tested — Osgood-Schlatter is a clinical diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — Patellofemoral syndrome: anterior knee pain in adolescents and diagnostic differentiation from apophysitis"},{"id":"sdl10_q09","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Osgood-Schlatter disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 12-year-old soccer player is diagnosed with Osgood-Schlatter disease. He trains 5 days per week. Which of the following best describes the correct treatment approach according to the SDL?","choices":{"A":"Treatment centers on rebalancing the ratio of physical stress to recovery time, supported by physical therapy for quadriceps flexibility and strengthening, analgesics for pain control, and activity modification rather than complete cessation of sport","B":"Complete immobilization in a long leg cast for 6 to 8 weeks is indicated to allow the apophysis to heal before resuming activity, as continued sport participation without immobilization consistently leads to avulsion fracture of the tibial tuberosity in growing athletes","C":"Surgical excision of the tibial tuberosity apophysis is the definitive treatment for Osgood-Schlatter disease in athletes who fail 4 weeks of conservative management, as prolonged apophyseal inflammation leads to a painful ossicle requiring operative removal","D":"Intraarticular corticosteroid injection into the infrapatellar region is first-line treatment for Osgood-Schlatter disease, providing immediate anti-inflammatory relief and allowing rapid return to sport when combined with a patellar tendon strap brace","E":"Complete cessation of all athletic activity for a minimum of 3 months is required, as continued sport participation accelerates apophyseal fragmentation and significantly increases the long-term risk of tibial tuberosity avulsion fracture in skeletally immature athletes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Treatment of Osgood-Schlatter disease focuses on rebalancing the ratio of stress to recovery rather than mandating complete rest. Physical therapy addresses quadriceps tightness and strengthening, analgesics manage pain, and activity modification reduces apophyseal load. Regenerative injection therapy such as prolotherapy is an option for refractory cases. Complete cessation of sport is generally not required.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osgood-Schlatter treatment = rebalance stress vs recovery, PT, analgesics, activity modification. Not immobilization or surgery in most cases. COMLEX: Prolotherapy is mentioned as a regenerative option for refractory cases.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — Patellofemoral syndrome: anterior knee pain in adolescents and diagnostic differentiation from apophysitis"},{"id":"sdl10_q10","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Legg-Calve-Perthes disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy presents with insidious onset left hip pain and limp. Hip X-ray shows femoral head flattening with increased density. Which of the following best describes the pathophysiological sequence of Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease?","choices":{"A":"Chronic repetitive loading of the femoral head causes cartilage degeneration at the articular surface, exposing subchondral bone to synovial fluid, which triggers inflammatory degradation of trabecular bone progressing inward from the articular margin","B":"Inadequate blood flow to the femoral head causes ischemic necrosis of the epiphysis; new bone attempts to deposit on avascular trabeculae while calcification of necrotic marrow crushes those trabeculae, leading to femoral head distortion and potential subluxation from the acetabulum","C":"Autoimmune synovitis causes inflammatory mediators to diffuse into the femoral head, producing reactive bone resorption at the subchondral plate and secondary avascular necrosis from cytokine-mediated vascular thrombosis in the epiphyseal vessels","D":"Repetitive microtrauma to the proximal femoral physis from running and jumping activities produces stress fractures through the growth plate, causing progressive physeal widening and subsequent collapse of the femoral head under axial weight-bearing load","E":"Slippage of the proximal femoral epiphysis through a weakened physis produces mechanical impingement of the femoral neck against the acetabulum, causing secondary avascular necrosis of the femoral head from repetitive vascular compression during weight-bearing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease is avascular necrosis of the femoral head in children. The sequence is: inadequate blood flow causes ischemic epiphyseal death, new bone attempts to deposit on avascular trabeculae, calcification of necrotic marrow crushes the avascular scaffold, and femoral head distortion progresses to subluxation or dislocation. Initial X-rays may be negative; bone scan and MRI detect it earlier.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LCP = AVN of femoral head in children age 4-9, M:F 4:1, bilateral 10%. Pathology: ischemia leads to femoral head collapse and distortion. Initial X-rays may be negative — MRI or bone scan detect earlier. COMLEX: Decreased abduction and IR on exam; can refer to knee.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — Avascular necrosis of the hip: pathophysiology of femoral head ischemia and management across age groups"},{"id":"sdl10_q11","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Legg-Calve-Perthes disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A parent brings their 5-year-old son to clinic with a 2-week history of limp. He occasionally complains of knee pain. Hip X-ray is initially unremarkable. Which of the following best characterizes the typical presentation and epidemiology of Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease?","choices":{"A":"Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease presents acutely with severe hip pain, fever, and refusal to bear weight in children aged 10 to 15, with a male-to-female ratio of 1:4 and bilateral involvement in approximately 50 percent of cases at the time of initial diagnosis","B":"Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease most commonly presents between ages 8 and 12 with bilateral hip pain and a waddling gait, affects females more commonly than males at a ratio of 4:1, and is associated with rapid growth spurts causing relative vascular insufficiency","C":"Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease presents in overweight adolescents aged 12 to 15 with acute hip and groin pain after minor trauma, with equal sex distribution, bilateral involvement in 30 percent, and external rotation deformity on passive hip examination","D":"Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease most commonly affects children aged 4 to 9, with a male-to-female ratio of 4:1, and typically presents with insidious onset hip pain, antalgic limp, pain that may refer to the knee, and decreased hip abduction and internal rotation on examination","E":"Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease presents subacutely in children aged 2 to 6 with bilateral hip stiffness and a Trendelenburg gait, affects females more than males, and is strongly associated with a prior episode of septic hip arthritis in the majority of cases"},"correct":"D","explanation":"LCP occurs in children aged 4-9 with a 4:1 male predominance. Presentation is insidious onset hip pain, limp, and referred knee pain. Exam shows decreased hip abduction and internal rotation. Bilateral involvement occurs in only 10% of cases. Initial radiographs may be normal, requiring bone scan or MRI for early diagnosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LCP demographics: age 4-9, M:F 4:1, bilateral 10%, insidious onset. Classic exam = decreased abduction and IR. Referred knee pain is a trap — always examine the hip in a limping child. COMLEX: MRI or bone scan when X-ray is negative.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — Avascular necrosis of the hip: pathophysiology of femoral head ischemia and management across age groups"},{"id":"sdl10_q12","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Legg-Calve-Perthes disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 7-year-old male with suspected Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease has normal plain radiographs on initial presentation but persistent hip pain and limp. Which of the following is most accurate regarding imaging and management of this condition?","choices":{"A":"Because plain radiographs are normal, Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease can be excluded and the child should be reassured and observed; the condition is never present when initial AP and frog-leg lateral hip radiographs are both interpreted as normal by a radiologist","B":"Ultrasound of the hip is the definitive imaging modality for Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease, demonstrating increased echogenicity of the femoral head cartilage before X-ray changes appear, and treatment consists of ultrasound-guided aspiration of the joint effusion","C":"Bone scan and MRI can detect Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease before radiographic changes appear; conservative management with NSAIDs, activity restriction, physical therapy, and bracing or casting for containment is first-line, with surgery reserved for cases failing conservative care","D":"CT scan of the pelvis with contrast is the most sensitive initial imaging study for Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease, demonstrating contrast pooling in the femoral head, and treatment always requires immediate surgical core decompression to restore femoral head perfusion","E":"Dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry scanning of the hip is indicated when plain films are normal, demonstrating reduced bone mineral density of the femoral head before structural changes appear, and treatment is calcium and vitamin D supplementation to improve remineralization"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Initial X-rays can be negative in LCP, and MRI or bone scan should be obtained when clinical suspicion remains high. Conservative management includes NSAIDs, rest and activity restriction, physical therapy to maintain range of motion, and bracing or casting to maintain femoral head containment in the acetabulum during the revascularization phase. Surgery is reserved for failure of conservative care.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LCP imaging: Initial X-rays may be negative — MRI and bone scan detect earlier. Treatment: NSAIDs, rest, PT, bracing/casting for containment, crutches. Surgery if conservative fails. COMLEX: Maintaining containment of femoral head in acetabulum is the therapeutic goal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — Avascular necrosis of the hip: pathophysiology of femoral head ischemia and management across age groups"},{"id":"sdl10_q13","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old obese male presents with 3 weeks of dull left hip pain and a limp. He denies trauma. On exam, the left leg is externally rotated. Hip radiographs are obtained and confirm the diagnosis. Which of the following best describes the underlying pathology?","choices":{"A":"The proximal femoral epiphysis undergoes avascular necrosis due to disruption of the lateral epiphyseal arterial supply from within the hip joint capsule, causing the femoral head to collapse into the acetabulum in the absence of any significant shear force","B":"Repetitive impact loading causes a stress fracture through the femoral neck at the subcapital region, producing gradual anterior and superior displacement of the femoral head relative to the femoral neck in overweight adolescent males during rapid growth","C":"Acetabular labral hypertrophy from compensatory overload causes progressive anterior femoral head coverage deficit, producing cam-type femoroacetabular impingement and secondary physeal stress in overweight adolescent males during peak growth","D":"Inflammatory synovitis from a prior viral illness causes the proximal femoral growth plate to weaken through cytokine-mediated degradation of physeal cartilage, allowing the femoral neck to rotate anteriorly relative to the fixed femoral head during the preslip phase","E":"Shearing forces through a physiologically weakened proximal femoral physis cause the femoral head epiphysis to slip posteriorly and inferiorly relative to the femoral neck, producing the characteristic external rotation deformity and antalgic gait seen in this presentation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"SCFE is a developmental condition in which the proximal femoral epiphysis slips posteriorly and inferiorly relative to the femoral neck through a weakened physis. The physis is weakened by physiologic hormonal changes during puberty, exacerbated by obesity, metabolic disorders, and other factors. The result is the classic presentation of an obese adolescent with dull atraumatic hip pain, antalgic gait, and external rotation deformity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SCFE = posterior and inferior epiphyseal slip through weakened proximal femoral physis. Classic = obese adolescent, dull atraumatic hip pain, external rotation deformity. Radiographs mandatory. COMLEX: Referred knee pain is a trap; always X-ray the hip.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 1 — Developmental dysplasia of the hip: pediatric hip developmental conditions and their management"},{"id":"sdl10_q14","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 14-year-old female with SCFE is classified as unstable. Which of the following best describes how SCFE is staged and what determines stability classification?","choices":{"A":"SCFE is staged entirely by patient age: preslip in children under age 10, acute SCFE in those aged 10 to 12, and chronic SCFE in those over age 12; stability is determined by whether bilateral hip radiographs show symmetric or asymmetric epiphyseal height","B":"SCFE stability is based solely on the degree of epiphyseal slip angle on radiograph: mild slip below 30 degrees is stable, moderate slip 30 to 50 degrees is borderline, and severe slip above 50 degrees is unstable regardless of functional weight-bearing status","C":"SCFE is classified by duration into preslip, acute under 6 weeks, and chronic over several weeks; stability is determined by weight-bearing ability, classified as stable when the patient can bear weight without pain, and unstable when the gait is painful — unstable SCFE carries a significantly higher risk of AVN","D":"SCFE is classified only by duration, with acute lasting under 3 weeks and chronic lasting over 3 months; stability is determined by MRI perfusion imaging of the femoral head showing whether lateral epiphyseal vessels remain patent on gadolinium sequences","E":"SCFE staging uses the modified Loder classification exclusively based on slip width: stage 1 is less than one-third physis displacement, stage 2 is one-third to two-thirds, and stage 3 is greater than two-thirds; all stage 3 cases are automatically classified as unstable"},"correct":"C","explanation":"SCFE is classified by duration: preslip, acute under 6 weeks, and chronic over several weeks. Stability is determined clinically: stable SCFE allows weight-bearing without pain, while unstable SCFE presents with a painful gait. Unstable SCFE carries a dramatically higher risk of AVN because ongoing physeal shear disrupts the remaining blood supply to the femoral head.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SCFE stages: preslip, acute under 6 weeks, chronic. Stable = bears weight without pain; unstable = painful gait. Unstable = high AVN risk. COMLEX: All SCFE = non-weight bearing + urgent/emergent operative fixation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 1 — Developmental dysplasia of the hip: pediatric hip developmental conditions and their management"},{"id":"sdl10_q15","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old male presents with stable SCFE confirmed on radiograph. He is still able to limp with assistance. Which of the following best describes initial management?","choices":{"A":"Immediate open reduction with periacetabular osteotomy is indicated for all SCFE presentations to prevent progressive AVN, as conservative management consistently results in femoral head collapse in the majority of pediatric patients with any degree of physeal slip","B":"The patient should be made non-weight bearing immediately and referred urgently for operative fixation, which is the standard of care for SCFE regardless of stability classification, as further slippage during weight-bearing significantly increases the risk of avascular necrosis","C":"A hip abduction brace applied in 30 degrees of abduction is first-line conservative treatment for stable SCFE, worn continuously for 8 to 12 weeks until radiographic evidence of physeal closure is confirmed on AP and frog-leg lateral projections","D":"NSAIDs and activity restriction for 6 to 8 weeks with repeat radiographs at 4-week intervals is standard conservative management for stable SCFE, reserving operative fixation for cases where the slip angle increases by more than 10 degrees on surveillance imaging","E":"Closed manipulation under anesthesia followed by hip spica casting for 12 weeks is indicated for stable SCFE in adolescents under age 13, since surgical fixation carries an unacceptably high AVN risk from physeal instrumentation at this developmental stage"},"correct":"B","explanation":"All SCFE, regardless of stability, is managed with immediate non-weight bearing and urgent operative fixation. The standard surgical treatment is in situ fixation with a cannulated screw across the physis. Even in stable SCFE, continued weight-bearing risks further slip progression and AVN. Conservative management alone is insufficient for any confirmed SCFE.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SCFE management = non-weight bearing immediately + operative fixation for all types. In situ screw fixation is standard. COMLEX: Do not attempt closed reduction, as this increases AVN risk. Bilateral fixation may be considered for high-risk patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 1 — Developmental dysplasia of the hip: pediatric hip developmental conditions and their management"},{"id":"sdl10_q16","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the Salter-Harris fracture classification system and describe greenstick and torus fractures in pediatric patients.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 10-year-old falls and injures his ankle. X-ray reveals a fracture line passing through the physis and continuing distally through the epiphysis to the joint surface, without involvement of the metaphysis. Which Salter-Harris type is this fracture, and what is the clinical significance?","choices":{"A":"Salter-Harris Type I, in which a fracture passes straight through the physis without involving either the metaphysis or epiphysis; Type I typically presents with a normal X-ray and physeal tenderness as the only clinical finding, with low risk of growth arrest","B":"Salter-Harris Type II, the most common physeal fracture, involving a fracture through the physis with a metaphyseal corner fragment remaining attached to the epiphysis; it leaves the epiphysis intact and carries a low risk of growth arrest","C":"Salter-Harris Type V, which represents a crush injury of the physis from axial compressive force; Type V is radiographically subtle and may appear normal initially, but carries the worst prognosis for subsequent growth disturbance of any type","D":"Salter-Harris Type III, in which the fracture line passes through the physis and exits through the epiphysis to the joint surface; this type carries greater risk of growth arrest and joint incongruity than Types I and II due to its intraarticular extension","E":"Salter-Harris Type IV, which traverses the metaphysis, physis, and epiphysis as a single continuous fracture line; Type IV requires anatomic reduction and often internal fixation because it disrupts the growth plate architecture across all three bone zones"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Salter-Harris Type III fractures pass through the physis and into the epiphysis, reaching the joint surface. This intraarticular extension means the fracture requires anatomic reduction to restore joint congruity and minimize the risk of growth arrest compared to Types I and II. The SALTR mnemonic helps classify: S=straight through physis, A=above into metaphysis, L=lower into epiphysis, T=through all three, R=rammed or crush.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SALTR mnemonic: Type I=straight physis, Type II=above physis with metaphyseal fragment, Type III=lower into epiphysis, Type IV=through all three, Type V=crush. Type II most common, best prognosis. Type V worst prognosis. COMLEX: Types III-V require anatomic reduction; risk of growth arrest increases from Type I to V.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — Salter-Harris classification: physeal fracture grading and growth plate injury in lower extremity fractures"},{"id":"sdl10_q17","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the Salter-Harris fracture classification system and describe greenstick and torus fractures in pediatric patients.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl falls onto an outstretched hand. X-ray shows localized bulging of the cortex at the distal radius metaphysis without a complete cortical break. Which of the following best explains this fracture pattern?","choices":{"A":"The greenstick pattern occurs in children because pediatric bone has higher mineral content than adult bone, causing the cortex to crack completely on the tension side while the compression side remains intact, producing an incomplete transverse fracture through the diaphysis","B":"The Salter-Harris Type I fracture produces physeal disruption without cortical injury when axial force is applied to an immature bone, causing the growth plate to fail in shear before the adjacent metaphyseal cortex accumulates enough strain to buckle","C":"The buckle fracture in adults occurs because cortical bone is more brittle than pediatric bone, causing complete fracture with axial load rather than the plastic deformation seen in younger patients with more porous and compliant metaphyseal cancellous bone","D":"The torus fracture represents an avulsion of the dorsal cortex at the distal radial metaphysis caused by tensile stress from dorsal radiocarpal ligaments during wrist hyperextension, producing a dorsal cortical flake fragment visible on lateral X-ray","E":"Pediatric bone is more porous and compliant than adult bone, so axial compressive force causes the cortex to buckle rather than fracture completely, producing the characteristic cortical bulge of the torus fracture at the metaphysis without complete cortical disruption"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Torus fractures occur because pediatric bone is more porous and mechanically compliant than adult bone. When axial compressive force is applied, the relatively porous metaphyseal cortex buckles without fracturing completely, producing the characteristic cortical bulge. This is distinct from greenstick fractures, which result from bending forces and produce complete cortical failure on the tension side.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Torus fracture = axial compression buckles cortex without complete fracture; pediatric bone is more porous/compliant. Greenstick = bending force, one cortex breaks, other bends. COMLEX: Torus fractures are stable injuries treated conservatively with casting or splinting.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — Salter-Harris classification: physeal fracture grading and growth plate injury in lower extremity fractures"},{"id":"sdl10_q18","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the Salter-Harris fracture classification system and describe greenstick and torus fractures in pediatric patients.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 9-year-old sustains a mid-shaft forearm injury after a fall. X-ray shows one cortex of the radius fractured completely while the opposite cortex is intact but angulated without a fracture line. Which of the following best characterizes this fracture pattern?","choices":{"A":"A greenstick fracture is a complete transverse fracture of both cortices of a long bone in a child that is identical to an adult fracture pattern, occurring when bending forces exceed the plastic deformation limit of pediatric bone at the diaphyseal-metaphyseal junction","B":"A greenstick fracture results from axial loading along the long axis of the bone, causing the periosteum to separate from the cortex on the compressive side without cortical fracture, producing a painful periosteal hematoma beneath the periosteal sleeve","C":"A greenstick fracture is an incomplete fracture unique to pediatric bone in which bending stress fractures one cortex completely while the opposite cortex bends without fracturing, analogous to the behavior of a fresh green tree branch that breaks on one side when bent","D":"A greenstick fracture involves axial compression of the metaphyseal cortex without complete fracture, producing a localized cortical buckle at the point of maximum compressive stress, and is treated identically to torus fractures with a short arm cast for 3 weeks","E":"A greenstick fracture is a physeal fracture pattern in which the fracture line propagates along the zone of provisional calcification within the growth plate, producing a clean separation of the epiphysis from the metaphysis without cortical injury"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Greenstick fractures are incomplete fractures of pediatric long bones resulting from bending forces. One cortex fractures completely on the tension side while the opposite cortex bends without fracturing, because pediatric bone is more flexible and has a higher plastic deformation tolerance. The name derives from the analogy of bending a fresh green tree branch, which splits on one side before breaking completely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Greenstick fracture = bending force, one cortex fractures, other bends; pediatric only. Compare to torus = axial compression with cortical buckle, no complete fracture. COMLEX: Greenstick may require reduction to correct angulation before casting.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — Salter-Harris classification: physeal fracture grading and growth plate injury in lower extremity fractures"},{"id":"sdl10_q19","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of radial head subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 2-year-old female is brought to the ED after her older sibling pulled her arm to help her up from the floor. She holds the left arm adducted, slightly pronated, and refuses to lift it. Elbow X-ray is normal. Vitals are stable. Which of the following describes the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":{"A":"Perform closed reduction by supinating the forearm while applying gentle pressure over the radial head and flexing the elbow; a palpable click indicates successful reduction, and most children regain normal arm function within minutes without imaging follow-up","B":"Apply a posterior long arm splint with the elbow at 90 degrees and the forearm in neutral rotation, and arrange orthopedic follow-up within 1 to 2 weeks for formal reduction under procedural sedation in a controlled surgical outpatient setting","C":"Obtain CT arthrography of the elbow to confirm annular ligament interposition and plan the optimal reduction approach, as blind closed reduction attempts can further displace the ligament and convert a subluxation to a complete elbow dislocation","D":"Administer oral ibuprofen for pain control and arrange return visit in 5 days to reassess, since nursemaid's elbow typically resolves spontaneously within 48 to 72 hours without formal reduction in children under age 3","E":"Refer to orthopedic surgery for arthroscopic removal of the interposed annular ligament, as closed reduction is contraindicated in children under age 3 due to the risk of physeal injury to the proximal radial growth plate during the supination maneuver"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Nursemaid's elbow is treated with closed reduction using the supination-flexion or hyperpronation technique. In the supination-flexion method, the forearm is supinated while gentle pressure is applied over the radial head, followed by elbow flexion. A palpable or audible click indicates successful reduction. Most children recover full use of the arm within minutes. Imaging is not required after successful reduction in children under age 5 with a classic presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Reduction technique = supinate forearm with gentle pressure over radial head, then flex elbow. Click = success. Child should use arm within minutes. COMLEX: No imaging needed after successful reduction in classic presentation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — Elbow joint dislocations: differentiating subluxation from dislocation in pediatric elbow injuries"},{"id":"sdl10_q20","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of radial head subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 3-year-old male presents with left arm holding after a pulling injury. History and exam are classic for nursemaid's elbow. The child is 3 years old, the mechanism is consistent, and the child has no swelling or bruising. Which of the following best describes the appropriate use of imaging in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Bilateral elbow radiographs in AP and lateral projections are mandatory for all suspected cases of radial head subluxation in order to exclude concurrent fractures and to document the baseline radiocapitellar relationship before reduction is attempted","B":"MRI of the elbow without contrast is the preferred diagnostic modality for radial head subluxation, providing direct visualization of the displaced annular ligament and confirming the diagnosis when the clinical presentation is classic but imaging is required for documentation","C":"Fluoroscopy during the reduction maneuver is required in children under age 4 to confirm that the radiocapitellar line is restored, as physical examination alone cannot reliably confirm successful reduction in preverbal and minimally verbal children","D":"Radiographs are required before and after every reduction attempt as a standard medical-legal practice, since failure to obtain pre-reduction imaging and document a normal baseline radiograph constitutes a deviation from the standard of care in pediatric emergency medicine","E":"Imaging is not necessary in children under age 5 with a classic history and presentation consistent with radial head subluxation; radiographs should be obtained when age is over 5, the mechanism is atypical, or the child fails to regain normal function after reduction as expected"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Radial head subluxation is a clinical diagnosis. Imaging is not necessary when the history and presentation are classic, the patient is under age 5, and the mechanism is consistent with annular ligament injury. Radiographs are indicated when age is over 5, when the mechanism is atypical suggesting possible fracture, or when the child fails to recover normal function following an attempted reduction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: No imaging needed for classic nursemaid's elbow in child under 5 with typical mechanism. Image when: age over 5, atypical mechanism, or fails reduction. COMLEX: Clinical diagnosis — unnecessary imaging exposes young children to radiation without benefit.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — Elbow joint dislocations: differentiating subluxation from dislocation in pediatric elbow injuries"},{"id":"sdl10_q21","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of radial head subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 2-year-old girl is brought to clinic by her mother, who reports the child has been holding her right arm still since she was picked up by the wrist this morning. The child is afebrile with no swelling. The forearm is slightly pronated and the child cries when the examiner attempts passive supination. What is the most likely diagnosis and anatomical basis?","choices":{"A":"Posterior elbow dislocation with olecranon impingement on the posterior humerus, caused by hyperextension during the lifting event, presenting with a visible posterior bulge at the olecranon and complete loss of elbow extension with marked soft tissue swelling","B":"Medial epicondyle avulsion fracture caused by valgus stress during the pulling event, presenting with medial elbow pain, localized swelling, and tenderness to palpation at the medial epicondyle, with X-ray showing a displaced bony fragment at the medial elbow","C":"Distal radius torus fracture from axial loading transmitted up the arm during the pulling event, causing metaphyseal cortical buckle at the distal radius, presenting as wrist pain with normal elbow examination and tenderness at the distal forearm","D":"Radial head subluxation, in which traction on the pronated extended forearm causes the annular ligament to slip distally over the radial head and become interposed in the radiohumeral joint, producing pain with supination and the characteristic posture of arm adduction with forearm pronation","E":"Brachialis muscle strain from eccentric contraction during the pulling event, causing tendinous injury at the distal brachialis insertion on the coronoid process, presenting as anterior elbow pain with resisted elbow flexion but no pronation or supination pain"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This classic presentation — a toddler held with arm adducted and forearm pronated after a wrist-pulling mechanism, with pain specifically on supination — is diagnostic of radial head subluxation. The anatomical basis is interposition of the annular ligament in the radiohumeral joint after it slips distally over the radial head. The absence of swelling, deformity, and fever rules out fracture, dislocation, and septic arthritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic vignette for nursemaid's elbow: toddler, wrist/forearm pulling mechanism, arm adducted, forearm pronated, pain with supination, no swelling. COMLEX: Anatomical key = annular ligament interposition in radiohumeral joint. Reduction = supination with radial head pressure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — Elbow joint dislocations: differentiating subluxation from dislocation in pediatric elbow injuries"},{"id":"sdl10_q22","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of little league shoulder and little league elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old male pitcher presents with progressive right proximal shoulder pain after his third pitching appearance this week. He has no pitch count monitoring and pitches 5 days per week. X-ray shows subtle widening of the proximal humeral physis compared to the contralateral shoulder. What is the diagnosis and the most important preventive measure?","choices":{"A":"Little league shoulder, a stress reaction of the proximal humeral growth plate from repetitive throwing; the most important preventive measure is implementing age-appropriate pitch count limits to restrict cumulative physeal stress across a week and across a season","B":"Rotator cuff impingement syndrome from subacromial narrowing in a skeletally immature athlete; the most important preventive measure is strengthening the rotator cuff muscles through internal and external rotation exercises with progressive elastic resistance band training","C":"Septic arthritis of the glenohumeral joint from bacteremic seeding of the shoulder synovium during intense training; the most important immediate step is joint aspiration under ultrasound guidance followed by IV antibiotics pending culture and sensitivity results","D":"Acromioclavicular joint sprain from repetitive overhead stress on the superior shoulder complex in a throwing athlete; the most important preventive measure is limiting horizontal adduction forces across the acromioclavicular joint during the follow-through phase of the pitch","E":"Bicipital tendinopathy from repetitive supination forces during the late cocking phase, producing inflammation at the biceps long head tendon origin at the superior labrum; the most important preventive measure is limiting curveball pitching in athletes under age 14"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Little league shoulder is a physeal stress reaction at the proximal humeral growth plate from repetitive throwing. The lack of pitch count monitoring in this case allowed cumulative physeal overload. Pitch counts are the most important preventive tool, as they limit cumulative stress on the open growth plate. Treatment is rest from throwing for up to 3 months, analgesics, and physical therapy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Little league shoulder = proximal humeral physeal stress from overuse in male adolescent pitchers. X-ray shows physeal widening. Pitch counts = primary prevention. Treatment: rest up to 3 months, analgesics, PT. COMLEX: Distinguish from rotator cuff pathology by age and physeal widening on X-ray.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — Supracondylar fractures: medial epicondyle and growth plate injuries of the pediatric elbow"},{"id":"sdl10_q23","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of little league shoulder and little league elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 14-year-old right-handed pitcher presents to the ED after feeling a pop in his elbow during pitch release. He has medial elbow pain, swelling, and limited range of motion. X-ray shows a displaced medial epicondyle fragment. Valgus stress testing reveals elbow instability. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Long arm cast application with the elbow at 90 degrees of flexion and the forearm in full pronation for 8 weeks is standard treatment; this position relaxes the flexor-pronator mass and restores attachment of the avulsed medial epicondyle through gradual fibrous union","B":"Immediate closed reduction of the medial epicondyle fragment under conscious sedation followed by a functional hinged elbow brace for 4 weeks with immediate initiation of range-of-motion exercises is appropriate for displaced fragments with valgus instability","C":"Conservative management with rest, ice, analgesics, and a sling for 4 to 6 weeks is appropriate because displaced medial epicondyle fragments in adolescents universally heal with fibrous union regardless of displacement magnitude or degree of instability","D":"Corticosteroid injection into the ulnar nerve groove to reduce traction neuropathy of the ulnar nerve, combined with a counterforce brace, is the first-line treatment when a pop is heard during pitching with associated valgus elbow instability","E":"Surgical fixation of the displaced medial epicondyle fragment is indicated given the significant displacement and valgus elbow instability; conservative management alone is insufficient when displacement and ligamentous instability are both present"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Significantly displaced medial epicondyle avulsion fractures with concurrent valgus elbow instability are indications for surgical fixation. The medial epicondyle is the attachment of the ulnar collateral ligament; displacement with instability means the UCL cannot be restored by conservative means alone. Rest alone for 4-6 weeks is appropriate for non-displaced or minimally displaced fractures without instability.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Displaced medial epicondyle avulsion with valgus instability = surgical fixation indicated. Non-displaced without instability = conservative (rest 4-6 wk). COMLEX: Little league elbow peak avulsion risk at age 12-14; surgery reserved for displacement with instability.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — Supracondylar fractures: medial epicondyle and growth plate injuries of the pediatric elbow"},{"id":"sdl10_q24","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of little league shoulder and little league elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physician evaluates two 13-year-old pitchers in the same clinic session. Patient A has proximal shoulder pain after throwing, with physeal widening at the proximal humerus on X-ray. Patient B has medial elbow pain worsened by valgus stress, with medial epicondyle apophyseal widening on X-ray. Which of the following correctly pairs each patient with their diagnosis and the growth plate involved?","choices":{"A":"Patient A has little league elbow affecting the medial epicondylar apophysis of the distal humerus, while patient B has little league shoulder affecting the greater tuberosity apophysis of the proximal humerus; both conditions are treated with surgical fixation regardless of severity","B":"Patient A has medial epicondyle apophysitis from repetitive valgus loading in the deceleration phase of pitching, while patient B has proximal humeral physeal stress reaction from excessive torque during the cocking phase; both require bilateral surgical referral","C":"Patient A has little league shoulder, a stress reaction at the proximal humeral growth plate from repetitive throwing, while patient B has little league elbow, which is apophysitis or avulsion at the medial epicondylar growth plate from repeated valgus and distracting forces during pitching","D":"Patient A has a proximal humeral stress fracture requiring immediate shoulder spica cast immobilization, while patient B has medial collateral ligament insufficiency requiring ulnar collateral ligament reconstruction due to valgus instability from medial apophyseal disruption","E":"Patient A has adhesive capsulitis triggered by repetitive overhead throwing in a skeletally immature shoulder, while patient B has ulnar collateral ligament sprain from the valgus torque of pitching; both are managed with a minimum 6-month rest period"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Little league shoulder and little league elbow are distinct overuse injuries in adolescent pitchers affecting different growth plates. Little league shoulder involves the proximal humeral physis from rotational stresses during throwing. Little league elbow involves the medial epicondylar apophysis from valgus and distracting forces during the cocking and acceleration phases. Both are prevented by pitch count programs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Little league shoulder = proximal humeral physis; age 11-16, rest up to 3 months. Little league elbow = medial epicondylar apophysis; age 9-14, rest 4-6 weeks. Both prevented by pitch counts. COMLEX: Know which growth plate each condition affects.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — Supracondylar fractures: medial epicondyle and growth plate injuries of the pediatric elbow"},{"id":"sdl10_q25","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Osgood-Schlatter disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 12-year-old male soccer player presents with bilateral anterior knee pain for 2 months, worse with kneeling, jumping, and stair climbing. He has palpable, tender prominences below both knees. He is in a rapid growth phase and has no history of injury. X-ray is not obtained. Which of the following best justifies appropriately deferring imaging?","choices":{"A":"Osgood-Schlatter disease is a clinical diagnosis based on the characteristic presentation of tibial tuberosity pain and prominence in a growing athlete; X-ray does not alter initial conservative management, which centers on activity modification, physical therapy, and analgesics","B":"X-ray should be deferred because radiation exposure to the proximal tibial growth plate significantly increases the risk of physeal arrest and leg length discrepancy when cumulative X-ray dose reaches the 0.1 Gy threshold in skeletally immature adolescents","C":"X-ray is deferred because Osgood-Schlatter disease is caused by a metabolic disorder of calcium deposition at the tibial tuberosity, and radiographs cannot detect the early calcium dysregulation that precedes structural bony changes at the apophysis","D":"Radiographs are deferred in bilateral cases because bilateral tibial tuberosity pain eliminates the possibility of a fracture or neoplasm, which would present unilaterally, and bilateral symptoms confirm the diagnosis by exclusion from other pathology","E":"X-ray is deferred because the proximal tibial apophysis is always radiolucent in adolescents experiencing rapid skeletal growth, rendering radiographs unreliable for assessment of apophyseal injury during the active growth phase of the condition"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Osgood-Schlatter disease is a clinical diagnosis. The characteristic presentation — tibial tuberosity pain and prominence in an active adolescent during rapid growth — does not require X-ray confirmation. Imaging does not change initial management, which focuses on rebalancing stress and recovery through activity modification, PT, and analgesics. X-ray may be obtained to exclude other pathology when presentation is atypical.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osgood-Schlatter = clinical diagnosis; no imaging required. X-ray shows tibial tuberosity irregularity or avulsion only if obtained. COMLEX: Treat with activity modification, PT, analgesics, prolotherapy for refractory cases.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — Patellofemoral syndrome: distinguishing anterior knee pain etiologies in growing athletes"},{"id":"sdl10_q26","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Osgood-Schlatter disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old female sprinter has Osgood-Schlatter disease with persistent tibial tuberosity pain despite 6 weeks of activity modification, physical therapy, and NSAIDs. Her coach asks whether there are any advanced treatment options. Which of the following is an advanced treatment option for refractory Osgood-Schlatter disease cited in the SDL content?","choices":{"A":"Corticosteroid injection directly into the infrapatellar fat pad provides sustained relief for 6 to 12 months in refractory Osgood-Schlatter disease by reducing patellar tendon tension at its tibial insertion and decreasing local inflammatory cytokine concentrations","B":"Prolotherapy, a form of regenerative injection therapy at the patellar tendon insertion on the tibial tuberosity, is an option for refractory cases of Osgood-Schlatter disease aimed at stimulating tissue repair at the tendon-bone junction","C":"Arthroscopic debridement of the tibial tuberosity apophysis provides definitive relief in refractory Osgood-Schlatter disease by removing the avulsed apophyseal ossicle and smoothing the tibial tuberosity prominence under direct arthroscopic visualization","D":"Extracorporeal shock wave therapy is contraindicated in Osgood-Schlatter disease because ultrasonic energy directed at an open growth plate in skeletally immature athletes produces physeal microhemorrhage and accelerates apophyseal avulsion","E":"Quadriceps tendon transfer to the tibial metaphysis is the surgical treatment of choice for refractory Osgood-Schlatter disease in adolescents who have completed skeletal maturation and continue to experience pain with kneeling activities"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The SDL specifically cites prolotherapy as a regenerative injection option for refractory Osgood-Schlatter disease. Prolotherapy involves injection of an irritant solution at the tendon-bone junction to stimulate a healing response. Corticosteroid injections are generally avoided in tendon insertion sites in growing athletes due to risk of tendon weakening and physeal effects.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osgood-Schlatter refractory treatment = regenerative injection, specifically prolotherapy per the SDL. Standard treatment = activity modification, PT, analgesics. COMLEX: Prolotherapy is an osteopathic-aligned regenerative treatment — know it as a COMLEX-favored option.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — Patellofemoral syndrome: distinguishing anterior knee pain etiologies in growing athletes"},{"id":"sdl10_q27","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Osgood-Schlatter disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old boy presents with right anterior knee pain. He is actively growing. Examination reveals diffuse peripatellar tenderness worsened by prolonged sitting and stair climbing, without a palpable prominent or tender tibial tuberosity. Which of the following conditions is most consistent with this presentation, and how does it differ from Osgood-Schlatter disease?","choices":{"A":"Patellar tendinopathy, which differs from Osgood-Schlatter disease by involving the tendon substance at the inferior pole of the patella rather than the tibial tuberosity, and presents with pain worsening during jumping activities rather than prolonged sitting or stair climbing","B":"Prepatellar bursitis, which differs from Osgood-Schlatter disease by producing fluctuant swelling directly anterior to the patella rather than at the tibial tuberosity, and is associated with repetitive kneeling occupations rather than sport-related rapid skeletal growth","C":"Patellofemoral syndrome, which differs from Osgood-Schlatter disease by producing diffuse peripatellar and retropatellar pain worsened by prolonged sitting and stair climbing without a tibial tuberosity prominence, and is related to patellar maltracking in the trochlear groove rather than apophyseal traction","D":"Medial collateral ligament sprain, which differs from Osgood-Schlatter disease by localizing pain to the medial knee joint line rather than the anterior tibial tuberosity, and is produced by a valgus mechanism of injury rather than repetitive patellar tendon traction","E":"Iliotibial band syndrome, which differs from Osgood-Schlatter disease by producing lateral knee pain at the lateral femoral condyle rather than anterior tibial tuberosity pain, and is exacerbated by running and cycling rather than jumping and kneeling activities"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Patellofemoral syndrome causes diffuse peripatellar or retropatellar pain worsened by activities that increase patellofemoral contact stress, such as prolonged sitting, stair climbing, and squatting. Unlike Osgood-Schlatter disease, there is no tibial tuberosity prominence or tenderness, and the mechanism is patellar maltracking rather than apophyseal traction stress. Both occur in adolescent athletes but are clinically distinguishable by location and associated findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osgood-Schlatter = tender tibial tuberosity prominence, worse with kneeling and jumping. Patellofemoral syndrome = diffuse peripatellar pain, worse with prolonged sitting and stairs, no tuberosity prominence. COMLEX: Know both anterior knee pain differentials in growing athletes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — Patellofemoral syndrome: distinguishing anterior knee pain etiologies in growing athletes"},{"id":"sdl10_q28","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Legg-Calve-Perthes disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 7-year-old male presents with 3 weeks of left hip pain and limp. Exam shows decreased internal rotation and abduction of the left hip. Initial AP and frog-leg lateral hip radiographs are reported as normal. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in the diagnostic workup?","choices":{"A":"Reassure the family that normal X-rays exclude Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease and schedule a follow-up appointment in 6 months if symptoms persist, because this condition is never present when initial AP and frog-leg lateral radiographs of the hip are both read as normal","B":"Perform hip joint aspiration to rule out transient synovitis or septic arthritis, since Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease cannot present at age 7 without radiographic changes visible on plain film when both AP and frog-leg lateral projections are obtained and interpreted as normal","C":"Prescribe NSAIDs and activity restriction for 4 weeks and repeat X-rays at that time, as Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease is a self-limited condition that produces visible radiographic changes within 4 weeks if the diagnosis is correct","D":"Order MRI or bone scan of the hips, which can detect Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease earlier than plain radiographs by identifying abnormal perfusion or bone marrow signal changes in the femoral head before bony structural changes are visible on X-ray","E":"Order a complete blood count, erythrocyte sedimentation rate, and C-reactive protein panel, since Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease presents with elevated inflammatory markers that can confirm the diagnosis biochemically when radiographs are unrevealing"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Initial X-rays can be negative in early LCP. When clinical suspicion remains high — a child aged 4-9 with insidious hip pain, limp, decreased abduction and IR, and normal labs — MRI or bone scan should be obtained. MRI identifies abnormal marrow signal (ischemia) and bone scan shows reduced perfusion in the affected femoral head, both detectable before plain radiograph changes appear.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LCP can have normal initial X-rays. Order MRI or bone scan when X-rays are normal but clinical suspicion is high. MRI = gold standard for early detection. COMLEX: Normal inflammatory markers help distinguish LCP from septic hip.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — Avascular necrosis of the hip: ischemic femoral head pathology across adult and pediatric populations"},{"id":"sdl10_q29","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Legg-Calve-Perthes disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 6-year-old male is diagnosed with Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease. His physician explains that the fundamental pathology is inadequate blood supply to the femoral head. Which of the following best describes how ischemia leads to femoral head structural changes and potential dislocation?","choices":{"A":"Inadequate blood flow causes ischemic death of the femoral epiphysis; as new bone attempts to deposit on avascular trabeculae, calcification of necrotic marrow crushes the remaining bone scaffold, and progressive femoral head distortion can lead to subluxation or dislocation from the acetabulum","B":"Inadequate blood flow causes the femoral head articular cartilage to undergo fibrocartilaginous metaplasia, replacing hyaline cartilage with scar tissue that cannot remodel and produces progressive joint space narrowing and secondary osteoarthritis within 2 to 3 years","C":"Inadequate blood flow causes premature closure of the entire proximal femoral physis, resulting in coxa vara deformity and leg length discrepancy as the femoral neck grows at an abnormal angle due to asymmetric physeal closure after the ischemic event","D":"Inadequate blood flow triggers synovial proliferation within the hip joint capsule, producing a pannus that erodes the femoral head articular surface from the periphery inward, analogous to the joint destruction mechanism seen in juvenile idiopathic arthritis","E":"Inadequate blood flow causes cortical bone resorption at the femoral neck rather than the femoral head, progressively weakening the subcapital region and leading to a pathologic fracture that displaces the femoral head into a varus position under normal gait forces"},"correct":"A","explanation":"LCP pathophysiology: ischemia kills the femoral epiphysis, new bone attempts to deposit on avascular trabeculae, calcification of necrotic marrow crushes the avascular bone scaffold, and femoral head distortion progresses. If the femoral head deforms sufficiently, it loses containment within the acetabulum, leading to subluxation or dislocation. The goal of treatment is to maintain femoral head containment during revascularization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LCP sequence: ischemia, necrosis, new bone on avascular trabeculae, calcification crushes scaffold, femoral head distorts, subluxation or dislocation risk. COMLEX: Treatment goal = containment of femoral head in acetabulum during revascularization phase.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — Avascular necrosis of the hip: ischemic femoral head pathology across adult and pediatric populations"},{"id":"sdl10_q30","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Legg-Calve-Perthes disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 5-year-old boy with Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease has radiographic containment of the femoral head within the acetabulum and no subluxation on initial imaging. His parents ask what the treatment plan involves. Which of the following most accurately describes the principles of conservative management from the SDL?","choices":{"A":"Because the child is under age 6, no treatment is required beyond parental reassurance; Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease in children under age 6 is universally self-limiting and produces no residual femoral head deformity when left completely untreated","B":"High-dose systemic corticosteroids are the standard treatment to suppress the inflammatory component of Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease, reducing synovial inflammation and preventing secondary capsular contracture that contributes to femoral head subluxation","C":"Conservative management includes NSAIDs for pain and inflammation, activity restriction or crutches for weight-bearing protection, physical therapy to maintain hip range of motion, and bracing or casting to maintain femoral head containment within the acetabulum during the revascularization phase","D":"Immediate varus derotational osteotomy is indicated for all children with Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease because even brief delays in surgical containment produce spherical femoral head deformity that worsens long-term functional and radiographic outcomes","E":"Intraarticular bisphosphonate injection is first-line treatment for Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease, slowing osteoclastic resorption of avascular trabeculae and preventing femoral head collapse during the revascularization phase in children under age 8"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Conservative management of LCP includes NSAIDs, rest and activity restriction, physical therapy to maintain hip range of motion, crutches for weight-bearing protection, and bracing or casting to maintain femoral head containment in the acetabulum during revascularization. Surgery is reserved for cases failing conservative management. The goal is to prevent femoral head deformity while the avascular bone undergoes slow revascularization and remodeling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LCP conservative treatment = NSAIDs, rest, PT, bracing or casting for containment, crutches. Surgery if conservative fails. COMLEX: Femoral head containment in acetabulum is the central therapeutic principle throughout conservative and surgical management.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — Avascular necrosis of the hip: ischemic femoral head pathology across adult and pediatric populations"},{"id":"sdl10_q31","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 12-year-old obese female presents with 4 weeks of right knee pain and a mild limp. Hip examination is not initially performed. Hip radiograph obtained later shows posterior and inferior displacement of the right femoral epiphysis. Which of the following best explains the referred knee pain and what key diagnostic lesson does this case illustrate?","choices":{"A":"The referred knee pain is caused by direct compression of the femoral nerve between the displaced femoral head and the inguinal ligament, producing dermatomal sensory loss over the anterior thigh and medial knee in the distribution of the saphenous branch","B":"The referred knee pain arises from iliotibial band tightening secondary to the altered gait pattern in SCFE, as the external rotation deformity increases iliotibial band tension at Gerdy's tubercle on the lateral tibia and refers pain to the lateral knee","C":"The referred knee pain is produced by compression of the sciatic nerve between the posteriorly displaced femoral head and the posterior acetabular wall, causing posterior thigh and posterior knee pain exacerbated by hip extension and internal rotation","D":"The referred knee pain is caused by hip capsular distension from the effusion associated with SCFE, which stimulates obturator nerve articular branches supplying both the hip capsule and the medial knee, producing medial knee pain as the primary presenting symptom in all cases","E":"SCFE classically presents with anterior thigh or knee pain due to referred pain from hip pathology via the femoral and obturator nerve distributions; the key lesson is that knee pain in an obese adolescent should always prompt hip examination and hip radiographs to exclude slipped capital femoral epiphysis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"SCFE can present with knee pain rather than hip pain due to referred pain via the femoral and obturator nerve distributions, which supply both the hip and the anterior thigh and knee. This is a classic diagnostic trap. The key lesson is that any obese adolescent presenting with knee pain and a limp should have the hip examined and hip radiographs obtained to exclude SCFE, even when knee pain is the only complaint.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SCFE trap = knee pain without obvious hip pain. Referred pain via femoral and obturator nerves. Classic vignette: obese adolescent with knee pain, limp, and a normal knee exam — get hip X-ray. COMLEX: Radiographs mandatory for all suspected SCFE.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — Developmental dysplasia of the hip: spectrum of pediatric hip developmental conditions"},{"id":"sdl10_q32","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old male is diagnosed with an unstable SCFE. He is currently unable to bear weight due to severe hip pain. Which of the following best describes the management urgency and the key risk associated with the unstable classification?","choices":{"A":"Unstable SCFE can be managed with bed rest and traction for 24 to 48 hours before surgical scheduling, as a brief delay in operative fixation does not significantly increase the risk of avascular necrosis compared to immediate operative intervention","B":"Unstable SCFE requires urgent operative fixation because the inability to bear weight indicates ongoing physeal shear forces that dramatically increase the risk of avascular necrosis of the femoral head compared to stable SCFE, where residual blood supply is more likely to be preserved","C":"Unstable SCFE should be treated with emergent closed reduction under general anesthesia followed by hip spica casting for 8 weeks, as surgical instrumentation of the unstable physis universally worsens AVN risk by disrupting residual periosteal blood supply","D":"Unstable SCFE has the same operative urgency as stable SCFE and can be scheduled electively within 2 weeks; the stability classification is used only to predict long-term functional outcomes and does not influence the timing of initial surgical management","E":"Unstable SCFE is initially managed with intravenous bisphosphonate therapy for 48 hours before surgical fixation, as the antiresorptive effect stabilizes the weakened physis and reduces the degree of further slip during transfer to the operating room"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Unstable SCFE carries a dramatically higher risk of AVN than stable SCFE because the ongoing inability to bear weight indicates continued physeal shear that progressively compromises the remaining blood supply to the femoral head. Urgent operative fixation in situ is required. Closed reduction is generally avoided as it can further disrupt residual blood supply. Stable SCFE also requires operative fixation but with less urgent timing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Unstable SCFE = painful gait, unable to bear weight, high AVN risk — requires urgent in situ fixation. Stable = weight-bearing without pain, lower AVN risk — still needs fixation but less emergent. COMLEX: Do not attempt closed reduction of SCFE — increases AVN risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — Developmental dysplasia of the hip: spectrum of pediatric hip developmental conditions"},{"id":"sdl10_q33","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 12-year-old female with hypothyroidism and obesity presents with bilateral hip pain and is found to have bilateral SCFE on radiographs. She is younger than the typical SCFE patient and bilateral presentation is uncommon. Which of the following best explains why this patient developed SCFE in this atypical pattern?","choices":{"A":"Obesity alone is sufficient to cause bilateral SCFE in females, as excess body weight increases shear forces at both femoral physes simultaneously; hypothyroidism has no independent effect on physeal integrity and is a coincidental finding in this patient","B":"Hypothyroidism produces premature physeal closure through TSH-mediated effects on growth plate chondrocytes, which paradoxically weakens the physis during the preslip phase by accelerating the transition from proliferative to hypertrophic chondrocytes","C":"Bilateral SCFE in a young female indicates an underlying genetic connective tissue disorder such as Marfan syndrome, where fibrillin deficiency weakens the extracellular matrix of the physis; thyroid disorders are not associated with bilateral SCFE presentation","D":"Metabolic disorders such as hypothyroidism weaken the proximal femoral physis by altering the hormonal environment that maintains physeal integrity; combined with obesity-related increased shear forces, metabolic disorders predispose to SCFE at a younger age and in a bilateral distribution","E":"Bilateral SCFE is always caused by exogenous corticosteroid therapy, which suppresses IGF-1 at the growth plate and prevents normal physeal mineralization; the presence of hypothyroidism suggests this patient was treated with corticosteroids for an autoimmune thyroiditis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bilateral SCFE at younger than typical age in a female should prompt evaluation for an underlying metabolic disorder. Hypothyroidism is one of the metabolic causes of SCFE listed in the SDL, along with obesity, inflammation, and familial predisposition. Hormonal alterations from hypothyroidism weaken the physis, while obesity increases shear stress, creating a synergistic risk for bilateral and early-onset SCFE.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SCFE causes: physiologic hormonal changes, obesity, trauma, inflammation, metabolic disorders, familial. Atypical age or bilateral SCFE = evaluate for metabolic disorder. Hypothyroidism is the classic metabolic association. COMLEX: Always screen for metabolic disorders in atypical SCFE presentations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — Developmental dysplasia of the hip: spectrum of pediatric hip developmental conditions"},{"id":"sdl10_q34","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the Salter-Harris fracture classification system and describe greenstick and torus fractures in pediatric patients.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An 8-year-old male falls from his bicycle and injures his ankle. X-ray shows a fracture through the physis with a triangular metaphyseal fragment attached to the epiphysis. The epiphysis itself is intact with no fracture into the joint. Which Salter-Harris type is this fracture, and what is its significance for prognosis?","choices":{"A":"Salter-Harris Type II, in which the fracture extends through the physis and exits through the metaphysis, leaving a metaphyseal corner fragment attached to the epiphysis; Type II carries a relatively good prognosis because it does not involve the epiphysis or the germinal layer of the growth plate","B":"Salter-Harris Type III, in which the fracture extends through the physis and then distally through the epiphysis to the joint surface; Type III requires anatomic reduction due to intraarticular extension and carries a higher risk of growth arrest than Type II","C":"Salter-Harris Type I, which involves only the physis without metaphyseal or epiphyseal involvement; Type I is the most common physeal fracture type and typically presents with a normal radiograph, with growth plate tenderness as the only reliable clinical finding","D":"Salter-Harris Type IV, in which the fracture crosses the metaphysis, physis, and epiphysis as a single continuous fracture line; Type IV requires anatomic reduction and often internal fixation to restore growth plate alignment across all three bone zones","E":"Salter-Harris Type V, which represents crush injury of the physis from axial compressive force; Type V carries the worst prognosis and is characterized by physeal narrowing that may be subtle or absent on initial X-ray, making it the most frequently missed physeal injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Salter-Harris Type II is the most common physeal fracture. The fracture passes through the physis and exits through the metaphysis, creating the Thurston-Holland corner fragment (a metaphyseal fragment remaining attached to the epiphysis). Because the epiphysis and its germinal layer are not disrupted, Type II has a relatively favorable prognosis for growth. The SALTR mnemonic: S=straight through physis, A=above into metaphysis (Type II), L=lower into epiphysis, T=through all, R=rammed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Salter-Harris Type II = most common physeal fracture; fracture through physis with metaphyseal fragment; epiphysis intact; good prognosis. SALTR: S=I, A=II, L=III, T=IV, R=V. COMLEX: Type V = crush/compression of physis; worst prognosis; most subtle on X-ray.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — Salter-Harris classification: physeal fracture grading in lower extremity fractures"},{"id":"sdl10_q35","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the Salter-Harris fracture classification system and describe greenstick and torus fractures in pediatric patients.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Two 8-year-old children present to the ED after falls. Child 1 has an X-ray showing a cortical bulge at the distal radius metaphysis without complete cortical disruption. Child 2 has an X-ray showing complete fracture of one cortex with the opposite cortex intact and angulated but not broken. Which of the following correctly identifies both fracture types and their distinct mechanisms?","choices":{"A":"Child 1 has a greenstick fracture caused by bending force that fractures one cortex while the other bends without breaking; child 2 has a torus fracture caused by axial compressive force that buckles the cortex without producing a complete fracture on either side","B":"Both children have torus fractures, as the distal radius is the most common site for both fracture types in pediatric patients; the difference in X-ray appearance reflects only the projection angle rather than a true difference in fracture mechanism or pattern","C":"Child 1 has a Salter-Harris Type I fracture of the distal radial physis caused by tensile stress at the growth plate; child 2 has a Salter-Harris Type II fracture with a metaphyseal fragment on the compressive side of the angulated fracture","D":"Child 1 has a torus fracture from axial compressive force, where the more porous pediatric cortex buckles without completely fracturing; child 2 has a greenstick fracture from bending force, where one cortex fractures completely while the other remains intact but angulated, analogous to bending a green tree branch","E":"Child 1 has a complete distal radius fracture with periosteal hinging on the compressive side preventing complete displacement; child 2 has plastic deformity of the radius where no cortical fracture occurs but the bone curves under sustained bending force without breaking"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Torus fractures result from axial compressive forces — the porous pediatric metaphyseal cortex buckles without completely fracturing, producing a cortical bulge. Greenstick fractures result from bending forces — one cortex fractures completely on the tension side while the opposite cortex bends without fracturing on the compression side. Both are pediatric-specific fracture patterns resulting from the unique mechanical properties of immature bone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Torus = axial compression, cortical buckle without complete fracture. Greenstick = bending force, one cortex breaks completely, other bends. Both unique to pediatric bone. COMLEX: Both are stable fracture patterns managed conservatively with casting or splinting.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — Salter-Harris classification: physeal fracture grading in lower extremity fractures"},{"id":"sdl10_q36","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the Salter-Harris fracture classification system and describe greenstick and torus fractures in pediatric patients.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 9-year-old sustains a high-energy distal femur injury. CT confirms a fracture that passes through the metaphysis, growth plate, and epiphysis as a single continuous fracture line. The orthopedic surgeon counsels the family about growth arrest risk. Which Salter-Harris type is this, and why does it carry higher risk than Types I and II?","choices":{"A":"Salter-Harris Type III; it carries higher risk than Types I and II because intraarticular extension of the fracture through the epiphysis exposes the germinal layer to joint fluid and synovial metalloproteinases that degrade growth plate cartilage and prevent normal longitudinal growth","B":"Salter-Harris Type IV; it carries higher risk than Types I and II because the fracture crosses all three bone zones simultaneously, making it difficult to restore the precise physeal alignment needed to prevent a bony bridge from forming across the growth plate and causing angular deformity or leg length discrepancy","C":"Salter-Harris Type V; it carries higher risk than Types I and II because axial compressive crushing of the physis destroys the germinal cell layer and the extent of damage is frequently underestimated on initial imaging because the physis appears only narrowed rather than displaced","D":"Salter-Harris Type II; it carries higher risk than Type I because the metaphyseal fragment creates a stress riser at the physis that concentrates shear stress during weight-bearing, propagating the fracture line into the germinal layer of the growth plate during the healing period","E":"Salter-Harris Type I; it carries higher risk than expected because high-energy mechanisms produce concurrent vascular disruption to the periosteal blood supply, which is the dominant source of nutrition to the germinal layer of the physis in children under age 10"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Salter-Harris Type IV fractures traverse the metaphysis, physis, and epiphysis as a single continuous fracture line. This creates a uniquely difficult reduction challenge because the fracture must be anatomically reduced across all three bone zones simultaneously. Failure to restore perfect alignment risks the formation of a bony bridge across the growth plate, which can produce angular deformity or leg length discrepancy as the physis continues to grow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Salter-Harris Type IV = fracture through metaphysis, physis, and epiphysis; crosses all three zones; requires anatomic reduction and often internal fixation. COMLEX: Growth arrest risk: Type I least, Type V worst. Types III and IV require anatomic reduction due to intraarticular extension or cross-zone disruption.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — Salter-Harris classification: physeal fracture grading in lower extremity fractures"}]},{"sdlNumber":11,"title":"Joint Anatomy & Connective Tissue Physiology","questions":[{"id":"sdl11_q01","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the structural features of synovial, fibrous, and cartilaginous joints, including their anatomical characteristics and functional classifications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which layer of the synovial joint capsule is responsible for secreting synovial fluid into the joint cavity?","choices":{"A":"Outer fibrous membrane, which blends with the periosteum of adjacent bones and provides the primary structural stability to the joint capsule","B":"Articular hyaline cartilage, which covers the opposing bone surfaces and distributes compressive forces but does not produce synovial fluid","C":"Periosteum, which forms the outermost connective tissue covering of bone and provides attachment points for tendons and ligaments near the joint","D":"Inner serous synovial membrane, which lines the joint capsule cavity and actively secretes synovial fluid for lubrication and nutrient delivery to avascular articular cartilage","E":"Periarticular fibrous bursa, which is a separate fluid-filled sac adjacent to the joint that reduces friction between tendons and bony prominences"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Synovial joints have a two-layered capsule: the outer fibrous membrane blends with periosteum for structural support, and the inner serous synovial membrane secretes synovial fluid. The cavity is a potential space; articular cartilage covers bone surfaces but is avascular and relies on synovial fluid for nutrition. Bursae are separate from the joint cavity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the two capsule layers and that synovial membrane (not cartilage) secretes fluid. COMLEX: Synovial joint anatomy underpins OMM concepts of joint play and somatic dysfunction — fluid dynamics are affected by fascial restriction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — OA as the clinical consequence of synovial joint degeneration"},{"id":"sdl11_q02","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the structural features of synovial, fibrous, and cartilaginous joints, including their anatomical characteristics and functional classifications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A fibrous joint in which two bones are connected by an interosseous membrane and remain partially mobile is best classified as which subtype of fibrous joint?","choices":{"A":"Suture, a fibrous joint in which bones either overlap or interlock along irregular edges, allowing no meaningful movement and found exclusively between skull bones","B":"Gomphosis, a fibrous joint in which a peg-like process inserts into a bony socket via periodontal ligament fibers, as seen between tooth roots and alveolar bone","C":"Syndesmosis, a fibrous joint in which bones are united by a fibrous ligament or interosseous membrane, permitting partial mobility that depends on the length and compliance of the connecting fibers","D":"Synchondrosis, a primary cartilaginous joint in which bones are united by hyaline cartilage, typically a temporary union that permits growth and is later replaced by bone","E":"Amphiarthrosis, a functional classification describing joints with slight mobility that encompasses both fibrous and cartilaginous structural types rather than a specific structural subtype"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fibrous joints are subdivided by the nature of the connecting tissue: sutures (interlocking bone edges, skull), syndesmoses (interosseous ligament/membrane, partially mobile), and gomphosis (tooth-in-socket). The distal tibiofibular joint and the interosseous membrane of the forearm are classic syndesmosis examples. Synchondrosis is cartilaginous, not fibrous.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distinguish suture (immobile, skull), syndesmosis (partially mobile, interosseous membrane/SI joint), and gomphosis (tooth socket). COMLEX: Syndesmosis disruption in high ankle sprains creates instability relevant to OMM evaluation of the ankle and tibiofibular relationship.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — OA as the clinical consequence of synovial joint degeneration"},{"id":"sdl11_q03","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the structural features of synovial, fibrous, and cartilaginous joints, including their anatomical characteristics and functional classifications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The epiphyseal growth plate in a skeletally immature child is best classified as which type of joint?","choices":{"A":"Secondary cartilaginous joint (symphysis), in which bones are united by fibrocartilage providing permanent slight mobility such as that found at the pubic symphysis and intervertebral discs","B":"Primary cartilaginous joint (synchondrosis), in which bones are united by hyaline cartilage forming a temporary union that permits growth and is eventually ossified and replaced by bone at skeletal maturity","C":"Fibrous syndesmosis, in which bones are connected by an interosseous membrane or ligament permitting partial mobility that depends on the length of the fibrous connecting fibers","D":"Synovial hinge joint, in which articulating surfaces are separated by a fluid-filled cavity lined by a secretory synovial membrane and permitting only flexion and extension","E":"Specialized elastic fibrocartilaginous joint, in which elastin fibers embedded in a fibrocartilage matrix allow bending and recoil during repetitive loading cycles in development"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Primary cartilaginous joints (synchondroses) are united by hyaline cartilage and are typically temporary unions present during development. The epiphyseal plate is the classic example; it permits longitudinal bone growth and is replaced by bone (synostosis) at skeletal maturity. Secondary cartilaginous joints (symphyses) use fibrocartilage and are permanent, as seen at the pubic symphysis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Epiphyseal plate = synchondrosis (hyaline cartilage, temporary). Pubic symphysis = symphysis (fibrocartilage, permanent). COMLEX: Growth plate vulnerability underlies Salter-Harris fracture classification and the rationale for cautious OMM in skeletally immature patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — OA as the clinical consequence of synovial joint degeneration"},{"id":"sdl11_q04","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the composition and roles of connective tissues (ligaments, tendons, cartilage) in joint stability, movement, and their interaction with surrounding tissues.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The connective tissue found in tendons and ligaments is classified as which specific type, and what histological feature confers its high tensile strength?","choices":{"A":"Dense irregular connective tissue, characterized by collagen bundles arranged in multiple directions simultaneously to resist forces applied from various angles, as found in the deep dermis","B":"Loose areolar connective tissue, characterized by sparse collagen fibers in abundant ground substance with numerous resident and transient cell types facilitating rapid nutrient diffusion","C":"Specialized cartilaginous connective tissue, characterized by chondrocytes embedded in a matrix of collagen and proteoglycans without direct blood supply at the articular surface","D":"Dense regular connective tissue, characterized by parallel collagen bundles aligned along the axis of mechanical stress with few fibroblasts and minimal ground substance, providing unidirectional tensile strength","E":"Reticular connective tissue, characterized by a delicate meshwork of type III collagen fibers supporting lymphoid organs such as lymph nodes, spleen, and adrenal cortex"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ligaments and tendons are composed of dense regular connective tissue: parallel dense bundles of collagen, few fibroblasts, few blood vessels, high tensile strength, and very little ground substance. The outer surface has dense irregular CT continuous with adjacent muscle or bone. Dense irregular CT resists multidirectional forces, while dense regular CT excels along one axis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dense regular CT = tendons/ligaments (parallel collagen bundles). Dense irregular CT = dermis, joint capsule (multidirectional). COMLEX: Tendon and ligament composition explains why chronic overuse injuries heal slowly due to poor vascularity and why fascial OMM targets the surrounding dense irregular layer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — ACL/MCL/PCL ligament injuries as failures of dense regular connective tissue"},{"id":"sdl11_q05","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the composition and roles of connective tissues (ligaments, tendons, cartilage) in joint stability, movement, and their interaction with surrounding tissues.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Collagen type IV is uniquely distinguished from collagen types I, II, and III by which structural property?","choices":{"A":"Collagen type IV forms large fibrils arranged in parallel bundles providing the high tensile strength characteristic of tendons, organ capsules, and dermal connective tissue","B":"Collagen type IV forms sheets or network arrangements rather than fibrils, making it the primary structural protein of epithelial and endothelial basal laminae throughout the body","C":"Collagen type IV forms cross-linked elastin scaffolds that confer recoil properties to structures subject to repeated stretching such as arterial walls and lung parenchyma","D":"Collagen type IV forms short fibrillar linking structures that anchor extracellular matrix components to one another and maintain the overall integrity of the connective tissue scaffold","E":"Collagen type IV forms the reticular network of thin delicate fibers that support lymphoid organs such as lymph nodes, adrenal cortex, and bone marrow stroma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Collagen types I, II, and III form large fibrils (tendons, cartilage, skin). Type IV is unique in forming sheets or networks rather than fibrils, serving as the structural backbone of epithelial basal laminae. Elastic fibers contain elastin (not collagen) plus fibrillin. Reticular fibers are predominantly type III collagen in a thin meshwork.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HY collagen types: I = bone/tendon/skin, II = cartilage, III = reticular/healing/fetal skin, IV = basement membrane. Type IV defect = Alport syndrome (renal basement membrane). COMLEX: Collagen type distribution explains tissue properties relevant to OMM — type I dominates fascial planes targeted by myofascial release.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — ACL/MCL/PCL ligament injuries as failures of dense regular connective tissue"},{"id":"sdl11_q06","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the composition and roles of connective tissues (ligaments, tendons, cartilage) in joint stability, movement, and their interaction with surrounding tissues.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Fibroblasts are the predominant cell type of connective tissue. Which of the following correctly identifies their embryologic origin and primary function?","choices":{"A":"Fibroblasts originate from bone marrow precursors similar to macrophages and plasma cells, circulating in the bloodstream before homing to connective tissue sites of injury","B":"Fibroblasts derive exclusively from cranial neural crest cells in all body regions, explaining their capacity for both structural ECM synthesis and local immune modulation","C":"Fibroblasts are ectodermally derived cells that differentiate locally within each tissue during organogenesis and rarely contribute to ECM synthesis under normal physiologic conditions","D":"Fibroblasts originate from adipose precursor cells in the deep dermis that retain pluripotency and can regenerate the full range of connective tissue components after injury","E":"Fibroblasts arise from mesenchyme of mesodermal origin and produce the majority of extracellular matrix components including collagen fibers, reticular fibers, and ground substance macromolecules"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Fibroblasts arise from mesenchyme (mesodermal origin) and are the primary ECM-producing cells, secreting collagen subunits, proteoglycans, and other matrix proteins. Other CT cells include macrophages and plasma cells (bone marrow origin) and adipocytes (mesenchymal origin). Tendon and ligament fibroblast origin depends on body position: head = neural crest; limbs = lateral plate mesoderm; trunk = somites.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fibroblast = mesenchymal origin, makes ECM. Distinguish from macrophages (bone marrow), mast cells (bone marrow), and adipocytes (mesenchymal). COMLEX: Fibroblast activity in fascial connective tissue is the target of myofascial OMM — mechanical stimulation upregulates collagen synthesis and remodeling.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — ACL/MCL/PCL ligament injuries as failures of dense regular connective tissue"},{"id":"sdl11_q07","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of joint movement, including the types of movements (e.g., flexion, extension, rotation).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The atlantoaxial joint allows the head to rotate from side to side. Which category of synovial joint movement does this represent?","choices":{"A":"Angular movement, defined as motion that changes the angle between two articulating bones, including flexion, extension, abduction, and adduction at joints such as the elbow and knee","B":"Gliding movement, in which two flat articular surfaces slide past each other without significant angular change, as seen at the carpal bones of the wrist and facet joints of the spine","C":"Rotational movement, in which one bone rotates around its longitudinal axis, exemplified by the dens of C2 rotating within the ligamentous ring of C1 during head turning","D":"Special movement including inversion or eversion, which occurs at anatomically unique sites and does not fit the standard categories of angular or rotational joint motion","E":"Circumduction, a composite movement that combines flexion, extension, abduction, and adduction in sequence to trace a cone-shaped arc, seen at multiaxial joints such as the hip and shoulder"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Synovial joint movements are classified as gliding (flat surfaces translate past each other), angular (change of angle, e.g., elbow flexion), rotational (one bone rotates around its longitudinal axis, e.g., atlantoaxial joint, radio-ulnar pivot), or special movements (inversion, eversion, pronation, etc.). The atlantoaxial joint is a pivot joint permitting uniaxial rotation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Atlantoaxial = pivot = rotational movement. Elbow = hinge = angular. Wrist carpals = plane = gliding. COMLEX: Atlantoaxial motion restriction is a common finding in cervical somatic dysfunction; understanding rotation vs. side-bending mechanics is essential for HVLA and muscle energy technique.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — Glenohumeral dislocation mechanics explained by multiaxial joint motion"},{"id":"sdl11_q08","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of joint movement, including the types of movements (e.g., flexion, extension, rotation).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following correctly defines flexion as a type of angular joint movement?","choices":{"A":"A straightening motion that results in an increase in the angle between two articulating bones, returning a flexed joint toward anatomic neutral position or beyond it into hyperextension","B":"A bending motion that results in a decrease in the angle between two articulating bones, moving the distal segment toward the proximal segment and reducing the inter-segmental angle","C":"Movement of an appendage away from the body midline in the frontal plane, increasing the lateral distance between the limb and the central axis of the body","D":"Rotation of a limb segment toward the body midline along a vertical axis, reducing the anterior-facing angle of the limb relative to the sagittal plane","E":"A special movement unique to the ankle in which the dorsal surface of the foot moves toward the anterior surface of the tibia, reducing the dorsal foot-to-shin angle"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Angular movements change the angle between bones. Flexion decreases this angle (bending); extension increases it (straightening). Abduction moves away from midline; adduction moves toward midline. Internal rotation moves toward midline along a vertical axis; external rotation moves away. Dorsiflexion and plantarflexion are special movements at the ankle.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic traps — extension is increasing angle (not straightening past neutral); abduction vs. adduction confusion on anatomy questions. COMLEX: Flexion and extension terminology directly maps to OMM somatic dysfunction naming conventions (e.g., FRSL vs. ERSL in spinal mechanics).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — Glenohumeral dislocation mechanics explained by multiaxial joint motion"},{"id":"sdl11_q09","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of joint movement, including the types of movements (e.g., flexion, extension, rotation).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Inversion of the foot and eversion of the foot are best categorized under which classification of synovial joint movement?","choices":{"A":"Angular movements, which are defined by changes in the inter-segmental angle between articulating bones, including flexion, extension, abduction, and adduction at synovial joints","B":"Rotational movements, which involve one bone rotating around its longitudinal axis as seen at the atlantoaxial pivot joint during head rotation from side to side","C":"Gliding movements, in which two flat articular surfaces translate past one another without meaningful angular change, as seen between carpal bones of the wrist","D":"Special movements, a category that includes inversion, eversion, protraction, retraction, elevation, depression, dorsiflexion, plantarflexion, supination, pronation, and opposition, all occurring at anatomically specific sites","E":"Circumduction movements, which trace a cone-shaped arc by combining flexion, extension, abduction, and adduction in sequence at multiaxial ball-and-socket joints"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Special movements do not fit neatly into gliding, angular, or rotational categories and are named for the specific anatomical site where they occur. Inversion (plantar surface medially) and eversion (plantar surface laterally) occur at the subtalar and transverse tarsal joints. Other special movements include dorsiflexion/plantarflexion, pronation/supination, protraction/retraction, and elevation/depression.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know all special movements and their locations; inversion/eversion at subtalar joint, pronation/supination at radio-ulnar joint, dorsiflexion/plantarflexion at talocrural joint. COMLEX: These movements define the axes used in OMM diagnosis — the \"rule of threes\" for coupling behavior and motion restriction at each joint level.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — Glenohumeral dislocation mechanics explained by multiaxial joint motion"},{"id":"sdl11_q10","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze how exercise, physical conditioning, and aging impact joint health.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Which of the following is a recognized direct structural consequence of normal joint aging?","choices":{"A":"Increased production of hyaluronic acid within the synovial fluid, leading to paradoxically improved joint lubrication despite reduced overall synovial fluid volume","B":"Increased joint stiffness secondary to capsular thickening and shortening of periarticular ligaments, which together reduce range of motion and functional mobility in elderly patients","C":"Hypertrophy of periarticular muscles due to chronic loading stimulus, partially compensating for cartilage loss by improving active stabilization of the aging joint","D":"Progressive replacement of hyaline articular cartilage by fibrocartilage, which provides improved shock absorption at the cost of reduced articular surface congruity","E":"Expansion of the synovial membrane lining and increased synovial fluid volume, improving nutrient delivery to avascular cartilage as its own intrinsic nutrition declines"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Aging produces a constellation of joint changes: reduced flexibility, capsular thickening and ligament shortening causing stiffness, hyaline cartilage degeneration and thinning, reduction in synovial fluid volume and quality, and decreased periarticular muscle mass. These changes cumulatively impair joint function and predispose to OA and fall risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know aging effects on joints — cartilage thins, synovial fluid decreases, ligaments shorten, muscle mass decreases. COMLEX: Aging-related joint stiffness creates palpable somatic dysfunction patterns; OMM aims to restore joint mobility and soft tissue pliability that aging reduces.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — Knee OA as the clinical endpoint of aging and deconditioning on joints"},{"id":"sdl11_q11","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze how exercise, physical conditioning, and aging impact joint health.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Regular physical exercise improves joint health primarily through which of the following mechanisms?","choices":{"A":"Stimulating chondrocyte proliferation to directly regenerate lost articular cartilage, histologically reversing the degenerative changes of osteoarthritis at the joint surface","B":"Inducing hypertrophy of the synovial membrane and increasing its surface area, thereby raising the total protein and hyaluronic acid content of synovial fluid","C":"Improving joint lubrication through increased synovial fluid production and distribution, reducing soft tissue stiffness, and strengthening the periarticular muscles that provide dynamic joint stabilization","D":"Promoting calcification of periarticular tendons and ligaments, increasing passive joint stability and reducing the risk of traumatic ligamentous injury","E":"Decreasing bone density through controlled loading cycles, redistributing mechanical stress away from the articular surface and toward the cortical diaphysis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Exercise benefits for joint health include improved bone density, improved balance, reduction in pain, improved soft tissue pliability and reduced stiffness, improved joint lubrication, and strengthening of supportive muscles. Exercise does not directly regenerate cartilage or cause beneficial calcification of soft tissues. Synovial membrane hypertrophy can be pathological.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Exercise improves lubrication, reduces stiffness, strengthens periarticular muscles. Does NOT reverse cartilage loss histologically. COMLEX: Exercise prescription is central to osteopathic management of musculoskeletal conditions; combining OMM with graded exercise maximizes functional restoration.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — Knee OA as the clinical endpoint of aging and deconditioning on joints"},{"id":"sdl11_q12","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze how exercise, physical conditioning, and aging impact joint health.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Which age-related change to articular cartilage most directly explains the onset of joint pain and loss of function in elderly patients with osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Degeneration and progressive thinning of hyaline articular cartilage, which reduces shock absorption capacity and exposes the underlying subchondral bone to increased mechanical stress and eventual erosion","B":"Overgrowth of the synovial membrane filling the joint cavity, mechanically obstructing joint movement and generating a reactive inflammatory response that destroys articular cartilage","C":"Increased collagen cross-linking within the cartilage matrix, paradoxically stiffening it to the point where it can no longer distribute compressive forces across the articular surface","D":"Proliferation of chondrocytes at the articular margin, forming osteophytes that impinge on adjacent soft tissues and restrict range of motion without affecting articular cartilage itself","E":"Accumulation of calcium pyrophosphate crystals within the cartilage matrix, triggering recurrent pseudogout attacks that progressively destroy cartilage through crystal-induced inflammation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aging causes progressive thinning and degeneration of hyaline articular cartilage, reducing its shock absorption and load distribution capacity. Once the cartilage is lost, subchondral bone is exposed, causing pain and reactive bone changes (sclerosis, osteophyte formation). These are consequences of cartilage loss rather than primary causes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA = cartilage degeneration and thinning → subchondral bone exposure → pain and loss of function. Pseudogout involves CPPD crystals but is episodic, not the primary mechanism of OA. COMLEX: OMM reduces the joint loading that accelerates cartilage degradation; muscle strengthening offloads the articular surface.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — Knee OA as the clinical endpoint of aging and deconditioning on joints"},{"id":"sdl11_q13","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the structural features of synovial, fibrous, and cartilaginous joints, including their anatomical characteristics and functional classifications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old man presents with bilateral knee pain worsening over 6 months, aggravated by stairs and prolonged standing. BMI is 31. Vitals are normal. Exam reveals bilateral crepitus, mild bony enlargement, and no warmth or effusion. X-ray shows symmetric joint space narrowing and subchondral sclerosis. Which structural component of the synovial joint is most directly compromised in this patient, explaining his loss of smooth pain-free movement?","choices":{"A":"The outer fibrous membrane of the joint capsule, which blends with periosteum and maintains structural alignment of articulating bones but does not directly contribute to the smoothness of articular movement","B":"The inner serous synovial membrane lining the joint capsule, which produces synovial fluid for lubrication and nutrition but whose primary dysfunction causes effusion rather than crepitus or joint space narrowing","C":"The periarticular fibrous bursae adjacent to the joint, which reduce friction between tendons and bony prominences outside the joint cavity rather than at the articular surfaces within it","D":"The fibrous outer layer of the joint capsule at its attachment to the periosteum, which stiffens with capsular fibrosis but does not explain the symmetric joint space narrowing seen on radiograph","E":"Articular hyaline cartilage covering the bone surfaces within the synovial joint cavity, which normally distributes compressive forces evenly across the joint and provides a nearly frictionless surface for movement"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Joint space narrowing on X-ray reflects cartilage loss. Articular hyaline cartilage distributes compressive forces, absorbs mechanical pressure, and provides a smooth surface (with synovial fluid) for low-friction movement. Its degeneration produces crepitus and pain. Synovial membrane dysfunction causes effusion, not narrowing. Bursae are extracapsular. Capsular fibrosis causes stiffness.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA pathology = cartilage loss → subchondral sclerosis + osteophytes + joint space narrowing on X-ray. Know each component of the synovial joint and what its specific failure causes clinically. COMLEX: OMM for knee OA targets the periarticular soft tissue and capsular restriction, indirectly protecting remaining cartilage.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, and management"},{"id":"sdl11_q14","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the structural features of synovial, fibrous, and cartilaginous joints, including their anatomical characteristics and functional classifications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A medical student is reviewing a histology slide from a 50-year-old cadaver specimen. The joint shown connects two skull bones via interlocking bony projections with no intervening cartilage, fibrous tissue, or joint space. Movement across this structure is zero. The student correctly classifies this as which type of joint?","choices":{"A":"Synchondrosis, a primary cartilaginous joint in which bones are united by hyaline cartilage that permits slight bending early in life, typically a temporary union replaced by bone at skeletal maturity","B":"Syndesmosis, a fibrous joint in which bones are united by a fibrous ligament or interosseous membrane, permitting partial mobility that depends on the length and tension of the connecting fibers","C":"Symphysis, a secondary cartilaginous joint united by thick fibrocartilage providing strength and slight permanent mobility, as seen at the pubic symphysis and intervertebral disc junctions","D":"Synostosis, a synarthrotic fibrous joint in which bone is fused directly to bone with no intervening material, permitting no movement and representing the normal adult fate of previously separate skull bones","E":"Suture, a fibrous joint in which cranial bone edges overlap or interlock with fibrous tissue interposed between them, found in young skulls before complete bony fusion has occurred"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In adult skulls, sutures progressively ossify into synostoses where bone is directly fused to bone with zero movement. Sutures in children still have fibrous tissue interposed. Synchondroses use hyaline cartilage. Syndesmoses have ligamentous tissue. Symphyses use fibrocartilage. The absence of any intervening tissue and complete immobility specifies synostosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distinguish suture (fibrous, skull, children) from synostosis (fused bone, adults, skull after ossification). Know all three structural categories and their subtypes. COMLEX: Cranial OMM techniques are predicated on the concept of slight motion at cranial sutures — understanding that sutures exist as fibrous joints before synostosis informs this OMM approach.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, and management"},{"id":"sdl11_q15","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the structural features of synovial, fibrous, and cartilaginous joints, including their anatomical characteristics and functional classifications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old soccer player presents after landing awkwardly on an outstretched arm. MRI confirms anterior glenohumeral dislocation. The orthopedic consultant explains that the shoulder's extreme mobility — the greatest of any joint in the body — is a direct consequence of its synovial joint subtype. Which subtype correctly describes the glenohumeral joint and accounts for its multiplanar motion capability?","choices":{"A":"Ball-and-socket joint, a multiaxial design in which a rounded head fits into a concavity permitting movement in three planes: flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, internal rotation, external rotation, and circumduction","B":"Condyloid joint, a biaxial design in which an oval condyle articulates with an elliptical fossa permitting flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, and circumduction but restricting pure axial rotation","C":"Saddle joint, a biaxial design in which reciprocal saddle-shaped articular surfaces allow movement in two orthogonal planes, best exemplified by the first carpometacarpal joint of the thumb","D":"Hinge joint, a uniaxial design in which a spool-shaped surface articulates with a concave surface permitting only flexion and extension along a single fixed transverse axis","E":"Pivot joint, a uniaxial design in which a rounded bony process fits within a ligamentous ring permitting only rotation around the longitudinal axis of the bone involved"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ball-and-socket joints are multiaxial, allowing movement in all three planes. The glenohumeral joint trades stability for mobility due to the shallow glenoid fossa. Condyloid joints allow two axes (no pure rotation); saddle joints allow two axes at the CMC thumb joint; hinge joints (elbow) allow only one axis (flex/ext); pivot joints (atlantoaxial, proximal radio-ulnar) allow only rotation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the 6 synovial subtypes: plane, hinge, pivot, condyloid, saddle, ball-and-socket — and their uniaxial/biaxial/multiaxial classification. Hip and shoulder = ball-and-socket. COMLEX: Glenohumeral instability predisposes to dislocation; OMM for shoulder conditions addresses capsular restriction and rotator cuff muscle imbalance affecting all planes of motion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, and management"},{"id":"sdl11_q16","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the composition and roles of connective tissues (ligaments, tendons, cartilage) in joint stability, movement, and their interaction with surrounding tissues.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old male marathon runner presents with progressive posterior heel pain worsening over 4 months. Ultrasound reveals focal thickening and hypoechoic changes of the Achilles tendon without a complete tear. The sports medicine physician explains that normal tendon tissue heals slowly due to its intrinsic composition. Which histological characteristic of healthy tendon connective tissue is most responsible for its high resistance to tensile forces along its length?","choices":{"A":"Dense irregular connective tissue with collagen bundles arranged in multiple random directions simultaneously, providing resistance to tensile forces applied from multiple angles across the tendon body","B":"Specialized fibrocartilaginous connective tissue at the enthesis where type II collagen transitions to mineralized fibrocartilage to distribute compressive forces at the tendon-bone interface","C":"Dense regular connective tissue composed of parallel collagen bundles aligned along the axis of mechanical stress with few fibroblasts, few blood vessels, and minimal ground substance, conferring unidirectional tensile strength","D":"Loose areolar connective tissue with abundant ground substance and numerous cell types that facilitate rapid nutrient diffusion and inflammatory cell infiltration during healing","E":"Reticular connective tissue composed primarily of type III collagen forming a thin mesh-like scaffold supporting the fibroblast population and maintaining overall tissue architecture"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Tendons are dense regular CT: parallel collagen bundles, few fibroblasts, few blood vessels, high tensile strength, minimal ground substance. The outer surface has dense irregular CT continuous with periosteum or muscle epimysium. Poor vascularity explains slow healing. Dense irregular CT resists multidirectional forces; it is found in joint capsules and dermis, not tendon core.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tendon = dense regular CT (parallel collagen, poor blood supply = slow healing). Dense irregular CT = dermis, joint capsule. COMLEX: Achilles tendinopathy reflects overuse of avascular dense regular CT; OMM including myofascial and counterstrain techniques complement loading protocols by addressing surrounding fascial tissue.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — ACL/PCL/MCL/LCL injuries and the unhappy triad"},{"id":"sdl11_q17","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the composition and roles of connective tissues (ligaments, tendons, cartilage) in joint stability, movement, and their interaction with surrounding tissues.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A first-year medical student is studying for a histology practical. The review sheet describes a protein that constitutes approximately 30% of human dry weight, can form fibers, sheets, and networks, and is highly resistant to tearing forces. Type I forms large fibrils found in tendons and dermis. Type II forms fibrils in cartilage. Type IV forms sheets in basal laminae. What protein is being described?","choices":{"A":"Elastin, a structural protein embedded within fibrillin microfibrils forming cross-linked elastic networks that confer recoil properties to arterial walls, skin, and lung parenchyma","B":"Fibrillin, a glycoprotein that forms the microfibrillar scaffold into which elastin is deposited, creating elastic fibers throughout connective tissue in skin, vasculature, and ligaments","C":"Proteoglycan, a ground substance macromolecule composed of a protein core with attached glycosaminoglycan chains that retains water within the extracellular matrix of cartilage and connective tissue","D":"Laminin, a glycoprotein found exclusively in basement membranes that anchors epithelial cells to type IV collagen networks via integrin receptor binding at the cell surface","E":"Collagen, a family of structural proteins produced by fibroblasts that polymerize into elongated structures and represent the most abundant protein in the human body by dry weight"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Collagen is the most abundant protein in the human body (30% of dry weight). Types I/II/III form large fibrils; type IV forms sheets for basal laminae. Elastin (with fibrillin) forms elastic fibers with recoil properties. Proteoglycans are ground substance components. Laminins are basement membrane glycoproteins that anchor epithelium to collagen via integrins.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Collagen = 30% dry weight, most abundant protein. Key pathology: type I defect in OI, type III in Ehlers-Danlos, type IV in Alport syndrome. COMLEX: The elastic and collagen fiber composition of fascia determines its mechanical properties and explains why sustained OMM pressure induces tissue creep and eventual release.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — ACL/PCL/MCL/LCL injuries and the unhappy triad"},{"id":"sdl11_q18","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the composition and roles of connective tissues (ligaments, tendons, cartilage) in joint stability, movement, and their interaction with surrounding tissues.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman with progressive knee pain is told her articular cartilage is thinning. Her orthopedist explains that hyaline cartilage covering the articular surface uniquely lacks a perichondrium at that location. Which of the following best explains the functional significance of this anatomical feature for normal joint physiology?","choices":{"A":"Absence of perichondrium allows direct exchange of water and nutrients between the cartilage matrix and synovial fluid depending on compressive loading and decompressive cycles, enabling nutrition of avascular articular cartilage","B":"Absence of perichondrium reduces friction at the articular surface by preventing a fibrous layer from being interposed between opposing cartilage surfaces during joint loading","C":"Absence of perichondrium allows chondrocytes direct vascular access from synovial membrane capillaries, supporting aerobic metabolism within the cartilage during loading","D":"Absence of perichondrium permits collagen fibers to orient themselves parallel to the articular surface throughout the full depth of the cartilage matrix to resist shear forces","E":"Absence of perichondrium exposes the cartilage surface to direct innervation from capsular nerve branches, explaining the severe pain experienced when cartilage itself is damaged"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Articular hyaline cartilage lacks perichondrium at the exposed surface, allowing pressure-dependent water exchange with synovial fluid — the mechanism by which avascular chondrocytes receive nutrients. Loading compresses the matrix and expels water; decompression draws in synovial fluid. Cartilage itself lacks nerves and blood vessels; pain in OA comes from subchondral bone and capsular innervation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Articular cartilage is avascular, alymphatic, and aneural — nutrition comes from synovial fluid via pressure-dependent exchange. This explains why immobilization harms cartilage and loading protocols help. COMLEX: Joint mobilization in OMM promotes synovial fluid circulation, providing trophic support to avascular articular cartilage.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — ACL/PCL/MCL/LCL injuries and the unhappy triad"},{"id":"sdl11_q19","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of joint movement, including the types of movements (e.g., flexion, extension, rotation).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physical therapist documents shoulder range of motion. When the patient raises their arm forward in the sagittal plane from anatomic position, the angle at the glenohumeral joint decreases from approximately 180 degrees to 40 degrees. When the arm is returned to anatomic position the angle increases back toward 180 degrees. Which pair of terms correctly labels these two movements?","choices":{"A":"The forward arm raise is shoulder abduction (moving away from midline in the frontal plane) and returning to neutral is shoulder adduction (moving toward the body midline)","B":"The forward arm raise is shoulder internal rotation (moving the limb toward midline along a vertical axis) and returning to neutral is shoulder external rotation (moving away from midline)","C":"The forward arm raise is shoulder flexion (angle between articulating bones decreasing) and returning to neutral is shoulder extension (angle between articulating bones increasing back toward anatomic position)","D":"The forward arm raise is shoulder circumduction and returning to neutral is shoulder elevation, with both being composite or special movements at the glenohumeral joint","E":"The forward arm raise is shoulder protraction (moving the shoulder anteriorly) and returning to neutral is shoulder retraction (moving the shoulder posteriorly) at the scapulothoracic joint"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Angular movements are defined by inter-segmental angle changes. Flexion decreases the angle (arm forward); extension increases it (arm back to neutral or beyond). Abduction/adduction occur in the frontal plane. Internal/external rotation occur around a vertical axis. Circumduction is a composite movement tracing a cone. Protraction/retraction are special movements of the scapula, not the glenohumeral joint.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Flexion = decreasing angle. Extension = increasing angle. These definitions must be applied consistently across all joints. COMLEX: Correct motion terminology is mandatory for documenting SOAP notes in OMM — imprecise terminology (e.g., calling flexion \"elevation\") causes diagnostic errors in somatic dysfunction assessment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — Glenohumeral joint dislocations: presentation and management"},{"id":"sdl11_q20","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of joint movement, including the types of movements (e.g., flexion, extension, rotation).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old basketball player is evaluated after sustaining a lateral ankle sprain. Physical examination reveals that the plantar surface of his foot is turned to face medially and he is unable to actively return it to neutral. Which term correctly names this foot position, and to which category of joint movement does it belong?","choices":{"A":"Inversion, a special movement in which the plantar surface of the foot faces medially, occurring at the subtalar and transverse tarsal joints, and classified as a special movement distinct from standard angular or rotational motion","B":"Adduction, an angular movement in which the foot moves toward the body midline in the frontal plane, reducing the lateral distance between the foot and the central axis of the lower limb","C":"Internal rotation, a movement in which the entire lower extremity rotates medially along a vertical axis at the hip joint, rotating the foot passively but not representing a foot-specific movement","D":"Plantarflexion, a special movement in which the dorsal surface of the foot moves away from the anterior tibia, pointing the toes downward as occurs when standing on the toes","E":"Pronation, a forearm-specific special movement rotating the palm posteriorly that when incorrectly applied to the foot refers to a combined eversion, dorsiflexion, and abduction pattern"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Inversion (plantar surface faces medially) and eversion (plantar surface faces laterally) are special movements at the subtalar/transverse tarsal joints. They do not fit angular or rotational categories. Lateral ankle sprains most commonly injure the anterior talofibular ligament through inversion mechanism. Plantarflexion and dorsiflexion are distinct special movements at the talocrural joint.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Inversion = subtalar joint, plantar surface medial, lateral ankle sprain mechanism. Eversion = opposite. Know which ligaments are stressed in inversion (ATFL, CFL) vs. eversion (deltoid) sprains. COMLEX: The subtalar joint is a common site of somatic dysfunction after ankle sprains; OMM addresses the restricted motion at the talocalcaneal joint to restore inversion-eversion balance.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — Glenohumeral joint dislocations: presentation and management"},{"id":"sdl11_q21","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of joint movement, including the types of movements (e.g., flexion, extension, rotation).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physical medicine resident is comparing range of motion between the elbow and hip joint during a clinical encounter. The elbow allows only flexion and extension along a single fixed axis, while the hip allows movement in all three planes of space. Which pair of synovial joint subtypes correctly corresponds to the elbow and hip, respectively?","choices":{"A":"The elbow is a pivot joint, a uniaxial design permitting only rotation around the longitudinal axis, while the hip is a condyloid joint, a biaxial design permitting flexion, extension, and abduction but restricting pure axial rotation","B":"The elbow is a hinge joint, a uniaxial design permitting only flexion and extension, while the hip is a ball-and-socket joint, a multiaxial design permitting movement in all three planes including flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, and rotation","C":"The elbow is a condyloid joint, a biaxial design permitting flexion, extension, and circumduction, while the hip is a saddle joint, a biaxial design permitting movement in two orthogonal planes only","D":"The elbow is a plane joint, a uniaxial gliding design where flat surfaces translate past each other without angular change, while the hip is a ball-and-socket joint, a multiaxial design permitting three-plane movement","E":"The elbow is a saddle joint, a biaxial design with reciprocal concave and convex articular surfaces, while the hip is a pivot joint, a uniaxial design allowing only rotation around its longitudinal axis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hinge joints (elbow, knee) are uniaxial, permitting only flexion/extension. Ball-and-socket joints (hip, shoulder) are multiaxial, permitting movement in all three planes. Pivot joints (atlantoaxial, proximal radio-ulnar) allow only rotation. Condyloid joints (MCP, wrist) allow two axes. Plane joints (carpals, facets) allow gliding. Saddle joints (CMC thumb) allow two axes with reciprocal surfaces.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Memorize the 6 synovial joint types and their representative locations. Elbow = hinge (humerouhnar); the proximal radio-ulnar is the pivot within the elbow that allows forearm rotation. COMLEX: Classifying joints by their axes of motion underpins OMM diagnosis — restriction of a normally multiaxial joint in a specific plane localizes the dysfunction being treated.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — Glenohumeral joint dislocations: presentation and management"},{"id":"sdl11_q22","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze how exercise, physical conditioning, and aging impact joint health.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 58-year-old sedentary woman with early-stage knee osteoarthritis is prescribed a supervised 12-week low-impact aerobic and strengthening exercise program. At follow-up she reports a 40% reduction in pain and improved ability to climb stairs. Which mechanism best explains why exercise improved her joint symptoms without altering the underlying cartilage pathology?","choices":{"A":"Exercise improved joint lubrication through increased synovial fluid distribution, increased soft tissue pliability by reducing capsular stiffness, and strengthened the periarticular muscles that dynamically stabilize and unload the knee joint","B":"Exercise stimulated chondrocyte proliferation and direct synthesis of new type II collagen matrix, histologically reversing the degenerative cartilage changes at the articular surface","C":"Exercise induced synovial membrane hypertrophy, increasing its secretory surface area and producing greater concentrations of hyaluronic acid for improved viscosity","D":"Exercise caused beneficial calcification of the periarticular ligaments and joint capsule, increasing passive joint stability and reducing the mechanical load borne by articular cartilage","E":"Exercise promoted osteoclast-mediated resorption of the subchondral sclerosis seen on X-ray, reducing the pain generated by exposed bone at the articular surface"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Exercise does not directly regenerate cartilage, induce beneficial ligament calcification, or resorb subchondral sclerosis. Its benefits are: improved lubrication (synovial fluid movement), soft tissue pliability (reduced capsular stiffness), muscle strengthening (offloading the joint), improved balance, and pain reduction. Synovial membrane hypertrophy is pathological (seen in RA), not a therapeutic outcome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know what exercise does and does not do for OA — it does not regenerate cartilage; it improves muscle strength, lubrication, and pain. COMLEX: Physical conditioning is the cornerstone of osteopathic management of musculoskeletal disease; OMM is most effective when combined with an active exercise program that maintains the gains achieved by manipulation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 8 — Knee osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, and management"},{"id":"sdl11_q23","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze how exercise, physical conditioning, and aging impact joint health.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 77-year-old man presents with bilateral hip stiffness and aching worse in the morning and after prolonged sitting. He denies trauma. Radiographs show symmetric joint space narrowing, marginal osteophytes, and subchondral sclerosis. Physical examination reveals markedly reduced internal rotation bilaterally. Which combination of age-related joint changes most directly explains both the radiographic findings and the restricted internal rotation on examination?","choices":{"A":"Increased synovial fluid volume with elevated protein content causing joint distension, which mechanically restricts capsular compliance and produces both the radiographic narrowing and the motion restriction","B":"Progressive replacement of dense regular connective tissue in the hip ligaments by loose areolar tissue, paradoxically increasing joint laxity and producing instability rather than the restricted motion seen here","C":"Hypertrophy of the periarticular muscles from compensatory overloading, generating passive stiffness that mimics capsular restriction but that would resolve completely with targeted strengthening exercises","D":"Focal accumulation of calcium pyrophosphate crystals within the articular cartilage causing pseudogout attacks that progressively destroy cartilage and produce episodic rather than chronic symmetric restriction","E":"Degeneration and progressive thinning of hyaline articular cartilage causing joint space narrowing, combined with capsular thickening and ligament shortening that reduces range of motion and accounts for the restricted internal rotation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The combination of joint space narrowing on X-ray and restricted internal rotation on exam reflects the two dominant mechanisms of joint aging: cartilage degeneration (structural, causes narrowing and crepitus) and capsular thickening with ligament shortening (functional, causes stiffness and range of motion restriction). Synovial hypertrophy causes effusion, not narrowing. Pseudogout is episodic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Two mechanisms = structural (cartilage loss) + functional (capsular/ligament stiffness). Know how to distinguish OA from inflammatory arthritis and crystal arthropathies clinically. COMLEX: Restricted internal rotation is a hallmark of hip somatic dysfunction and OA; OMM targeting the posterior hip capsule can restore some motion in early-to-moderate disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 8 — Knee osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, and management"},{"id":"sdl11_q24","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze how exercise, physical conditioning, and aging impact joint health.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A physical therapist is counseling a healthy 48-year-old recreational athlete about maintaining joint health as she ages. The therapist explains that aging and regular exercise have opposing effects on the same joint tissues. Which of the following best contrasts the effects of aging versus exercise on the joint and its surrounding structures?","choices":{"A":"Aging uniformly increases bone density through age-related osteoblast stimulation, while excessive aerobic exercise paradoxically reduces density through mechanical unloading of the axial skeleton and limbs","B":"Aging promotes chondrocyte proliferation as a compensatory response to cartilage wear, while high-impact exercise suppresses this regenerative capacity and accelerates irreversible articular degeneration","C":"Aging selectively shortens all periarticular ligaments through gradual scarring and contracture, while targeted stretching exercise selectively elongates ligaments beyond their normal anatomic resting length","D":"Aging progressively reduces synovial fluid volume and quality, causes capsular thickening and ligament shortening, and decreases periarticular muscle mass, whereas exercise improves joint lubrication, maintains soft tissue pliability, and strengthens the muscles that dynamically stabilize and protect each joint","E":"Aging converts all diarthrodial synovial joints into synarthroses through progressive ossification, while regular aerobic exercise completely prevents this conversion by maintaining synovial fluid flow"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Aging effects on joints: reduced flexibility, capsular thickening and ligament shortening (stiffness), hyaline cartilage thinning, reduced synovial fluid volume and quality, decreased muscle mass. Exercise counteracts these: improves lubrication, soft tissue pliability, joint lubrication, bone density, balance, pain reduction, and muscle strengthening. Aging does not ossify all diarthroses or universally increase bone density.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the complete list of aging joint changes and how exercise addresses each one. COMLEX: This bidirectional contrast underpins the osteopathic philosophy of maintaining health through activity — OMM facilitates motion restoration while exercise prescription prevents recurrence of dysfunction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 8 — Knee osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, and management"}]},{"sdlNumber":12,"title":"Integrative Medicine","questions":[{"id":"sdl12_q01","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the terms complementary medicine, alternative medicine, and integrative medicine and discuss them in the frameworks of physical, psychological, or combination.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which term describes the use of complementary health practices as a substitute for conventional standard care rather than alongside it?","choices":{"A":"Complementary medicine, which refers specifically to practices used together with conventional therapies rather than as a substitute for them","B":"Integrative health care, which refers to coordinated use of both conventional and complementary practices with emphasis on whole-person care across social, psychological, and physical domains","C":"Whole person health, which is a framework focusing on restoring health, promoting resilience, and preventing disease across the full lifespan rather than treating individual organs or systems","D":"Alternative medicine, which denotes practices used in place of conventional standard care and is increasingly less preferred than the term \"complementary,\" which implies use alongside conventional therapy","E":"Salutogenesis, which describes the process by which health is actively restored when recovering from injury, acute illness, or exacerbation of chronic disease through behavioral rather than pharmacologic change"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Alternative medicine means used instead of conventional care; complementary means used alongside it. \"Complementary\" is increasingly preferred over \"alternative.\" Integrative health care combines both in a coordinated way with a whole-person focus. Salutogenesis is pathogenesis in reverse — restoring health through behavioral change.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the distinction: complementary = with, alternative = instead of. COMLEX: Osteopathic medicine is classified as integrative (mainstream + OMM), not alternative. Understanding this framework is directly tested in COMLEX clinical presentation scenarios.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — Therapeutic exercises as the physical input category within integrative medicine"},{"id":"sdl12_q02","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the terms complementary medicine, alternative medicine, and integrative medicine and discuss them in the frameworks of physical, psychological, or combination.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Under which primary therapeutic input category would meditation and cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) be correctly classified?","choices":{"A":"Physical input, which includes modalities delivered manually such as massage, by device such as acupuncture, or generated by the patient such as exercise","B":"Dietary input, which encompasses herbs, vitamins, minerals, and dietary supplements consumed to influence biological function through nutritional pathways","C":"Psychological input, as both target mental processes as their primary therapeutic mechanism, alongside mindfulness-based stress reduction, hypnosis, and biofeedback-assisted relaxation techniques","D":"Combined physical and psychological input, which characterizes practices such as yoga and tai chi that integrate movement with meditative elements into a single intervention","E":"Multicomponent system input, which describes whole health systems such as traditional Chinese medicine, Ayurveda, naturopathy, and chiropractic that combine multiple therapeutic approaches"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Complementary therapies are classified by their primary therapeutic input: dietary (herbs, supplements), psychological (CBT, MBSR, meditation, hypnosis, relaxation), physical (massage, acupuncture, exercise), or combined (yoga, tai chi). CBT and meditation have predominantly mental/psychological mechanisms, making them primary psychological inputs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Memorize the 4 input categories and their representative modalities — this framework organizes an otherwise unwieldy list. COMLEX: OMM and spinal manipulation are classified as physical integrative medicine; understanding this helps frame osteopathic practice within the broader integrative health landscape.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — Therapeutic exercises as the physical input category within integrative medicine"},{"id":"sdl12_q03","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the terms complementary medicine, alternative medicine, and integrative medicine and discuss them in the frameworks of physical, psychological, or combination.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes complementary medicine from alternative medicine as these terms are currently used in the medical literature?","choices":{"A":"Complementary practices are evidence-based and FDA-regulated, while alternative practices operate entirely outside any regulatory framework and have no clinical evidence supporting their use","B":"Complementary refers to practices used together alongside conventional therapies, while alternative refers to practices used as substitutes for conventional standard care, with \"complementary\" increasingly preferred over \"alternative\"","C":"Alternative medicine encompasses only natural product-based therapies, while complementary medicine includes both mind-body practices and physical therapies delivered by trained practitioners","D":"Complementary medicine requires a physician prescription and operates within the standard of care, while alternative medicine is entirely self-directed and purchased out of pocket without professional guidance","E":"The two terms are fully synonymous in current clinical and research literature and are used interchangeably without any meaningful distinction recognized by professional medical societies"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The critical distinction: complementary = alongside conventional medicine; alternative = instead of conventional medicine. \"Complementary\" is increasingly preferred because it emphasizes integration rather than substitution. Neither term is defined purely by evidence base, regulation, or professional supervision.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Complementary = WITH conventional. Alternative = SUBSTITUTE for conventional. This definitional distinction has appeared in clinical reasoning questions. COMLEX: The osteopathic approach explicitly favors complementary integration over alternative substitution of conventional care.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — Therapeutic exercises as the physical input category within integrative medicine"},{"id":"sdl12_q04","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize disorders which may be amenable to integrative care.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"According to the American College of Physicians (ACP) clinical practice guidelines, which integrative therapy is strongly recommended as the initial treatment for chronic insomnia before pharmacologic agents?","choices":{"A":"Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I), which the ACP strongly recommends as the initial treatment for chronic insomnia, often incorporating relaxation techniques as components within the program","B":"Melatonin supplementation, which has evidence for reducing sleep-onset latency and improving daytime sleepiness but is associated with safety concerns in children and mislabeling of gummy products","C":"Acupuncture, which is recommended by ACP as an initial option for chronic low-back pain but does not appear in ACP guidelines as a first-line treatment for chronic insomnia","D":"Progressive muscle relaxation, which may provide benefit for stress-related conditions and anxiety but lacks the strong ACP guideline recommendation status of CBT-I for chronic insomnia","E":"Mindfulness-based stress reduction, which reduces pain and anxiety and has growing evidence for sleep improvement but is not the ACP-recommended initial treatment for chronic insomnia specifically"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The 2016 ACP practice guidelines strongly recommend CBT-I as the initial treatment for chronic insomnia. CBT-I often includes relaxation techniques but the full multi-component CBT-I program carries the guideline endorsement. Melatonin has evidence but is not ACP first-line; it also has safety concerns in children and mislabeling issues.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CBT-I = ACP first-line for chronic insomnia (before sleep aids). COMLEX: Insomnia is a common clinical presentation where osteopathic physicians are expected to recommend non-pharmacologic interventions first; CBT-I is the evidence-based answer before considering hypnotics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — OA as the leading musculoskeletal condition amenable to integrative care"},{"id":"sdl12_q05","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize disorders which may be amenable to integrative care.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The American College of Rheumatology and the Arthritis Foundation issue which level of recommendation for tai chi as a treatment for knee and hip osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Conditional recommendation for tai chi as a second-line option for OA after failure of NSAIDs and corticosteroid injections, reflecting limited evidence compared to acupuncture","B":"Strong recommendation for tai chi alongside other nondrug approaches for managing knee and/or hip OA, given its demonstrated benefits for overall motor function, balance, stability, sleep quality, and quality of life","C":"No formal recommendation exists for tai chi in OA; the ACR and Arthritis Foundation only recognize acupuncture and massage as having sufficient evidence for guideline inclusion","D":"Weak recommendation against tai chi in elderly OA patients due to fall risk, with guidance instead favoring low-impact aquatic exercise programs for patients with severe hip OA","E":"Moderate recommendation pending ongoing randomized trials, with current use suggested only as an adjunct to pharmacologic therapy rather than as a standalone intervention"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The ACR and Arthritis Foundation strongly recommend tai chi for knee/hip OA as a nondrug approach. Acupuncture receives a conditional (not strong) recommendation for knee/hip/hand OA. Tai chi also improves balance, stability in elderly, sleep quality in mild insomnia, and quality of life in heart disease and cancer.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the guideline levels — tai chi STRONG recommendation for OA (ACR/AF); acupuncture CONDITIONAL for OA (ACR/AF); acupuncture RECOMMENDED for low-back pain (ACP 2017). COMLEX: Tai chi exemplifies combined physical-psychological integrative medicine specifically endorsed for the musculoskeletal conditions most common in COMLEX clinical presentations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — OA as the leading musculoskeletal condition amenable to integrative care"},{"id":"sdl12_q06","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize disorders which may be amenable to integrative care.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Clinical trials have shown that omega-3 fatty acid supplementation is beneficial for which of the following musculoskeletal conditions by reducing tender joints, morning stiffness, and the daily NSAID requirement?","choices":{"A":"Knee osteoarthritis, where the primary evidence-based integrative interventions are acupuncture and tai chi rather than omega-3 fatty acid supplementation specifically","B":"Fibromyalgia, where yoga and mindfulness-based stress reduction have the strongest integrative evidence, and omega-3 supplementation has not been specifically studied as primary management","C":"Ankylosing spondylitis, where spinal manipulation and physical therapy have the primary evidence base and omega-3 supplementation has not been validated in rigorous clinical trials","D":"Gout, where dietary modifications reducing purine intake are the primary integrative strategy and omega-3 fatty acids have not been shown to reduce uric acid levels or flare frequency","E":"Rheumatoid arthritis (RA), where fish oil supplementation has been shown to alleviate tender joints and morning stiffness and reduce daily NSAID requirements, though data are not definitive for other pain conditions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Omega-3 fatty acids have the strongest integrative evidence in RA: reduced tender joints, reduced morning stiffness, reduced NSAID requirement. For depression, omega-3 data are inconclusive as monotherapy. High-dose fish oil is associated with increased atrial fibrillation risk. For AMD, AREDS2 antioxidants are the relevant supplement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Omega-3 = RA (reduces NSAID need, tender joints, morning stiffness). Not a replacement for DMARDs. High-dose fish oil → increased AF risk. COMLEX: Omega-3 supplementation is a dietary integrative medicine strategy; knowing its evidence base versus areas where evidence is lacking is a classic COMLEX pharmacology-nutrition integration question.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — OA as the leading musculoskeletal condition amenable to integrative care"},{"id":"sdl12_q07","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss challenges to the use of integrative medicine including toxicity, research, and regulation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The Dietary Supplement Health and Education Act (DSHEA) of 1994 establishes which specific regulatory limitation on claims that dietary supplement manufacturers may make on their product labels?","choices":{"A":"Supplements must demonstrate clinical safety and efficacy through FDA premarket approval similar to pharmaceutical drugs before being sold to consumers in the United States","B":"Manufacturers may claim a supplement maintains \"normal structure and function\" of body systems but cannot claim it prevents, treats, or cures any disease, making false disease claims the purview of Federal Trade Commission enforcement","C":"Dietary supplements are prohibited from listing any health benefits on their labels and may only display ingredient lists, serving sizes, and recommended daily values without any wellness claims","D":"All dietary supplement advertising claims must be reviewed and approved by the FDA before they appear on product labels, in online marketing, or in any print or broadcast media","E":"Supplement manufacturers must disclose all known herb-drug interactions on product labels, similar to the drug interaction warning requirements imposed on pharmaceutical manufacturers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Under DSHEA (1994), dietary supplements do NOT require FDA premarket approval for efficacy. Manufacturers can claim to \"maintain normal joint health\" or \"support normal structure and function\" but cannot claim to \"treat arthritis\" or \"prevent cancer.\" The FTC (not FDA) regulates advertising claims. The FDA regulates labeling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DSHEA (1994) = structure/function claims OK; disease claims NOT OK. The burden of proving harm falls on the FDA, not the manufacturer. FTC regulates advertising. Classic board trap: supplements do not require proof of efficacy before sale. COMLEX: This regulatory framework creates risk — patients may use unproven supplements believing they are equivalent to evidence-based medicines.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 4 — NSAID adverse effects and regulation as contrast to supplement toxicity challenges"},{"id":"sdl12_q08","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss challenges to the use of integrative medicine including toxicity, research, and regulation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The FDA banned dietary supplements containing Ephedra sinica (ma huang) in the United States in 2004. Which pharmacologic property of this plant directly led to that regulatory action?","choices":{"A":"Ephedra sinica was found to contain aristolochic acid alkaloids that were specifically associated with genitourinary malignancies and interstitial nephritis in consumers using herbal weight-loss preparations","B":"Ephedra sinica caused the eosinophilia-myalgia syndrome through contamination of amino acid supplements during manufacturing, resulting in multisystem inflammation affecting thousands of users","C":"Ephedra sinica supplements were found to contain 1,3-dimethylamylamine, a synthetic stimulant frequently adulterated into herbal products that caused cardiovascular problems and heart attacks in users","D":"Ephedra sinica was withdrawn after being found to cause anosmia in users of a homeopathic nasal spray product, later determined to result from significant zinc content rather than the herb itself","E":"Ephedra sinica contains ephedrine and pseudoephedrine alkaloids; supplements were widely sold for weight loss and athletic performance enhancement but caused severe and fatal cardiovascular adverse events including heart attacks"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ephedra sinica (ma huang) contains ephedrine and pseudoephedrine. Used for weight loss and athletic performance, it caused severe cardiovascular adverse events. FDA banned it in 2004. Aristolochia = genitourinary malignancies and nephritis. Tryptophan contamination = eosinophilia-myalgia syndrome. DMAA = cardiovascular problems in its own right. Zicam = anosmia from zinc.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HY toxicology: ephedra/ma huang = cardiovascular events (ephedrine/pseudoephedrine) → FDA banned 2004. Aristolochia = nephritis + GU cancer. Tryptophan contamination = eosinophilia-myalgia. Keep each toxin matched to its specific supplement and outcome. COMLEX: Counseling patients about natural product risks includes knowing these specific examples to cite.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 4 — NSAID adverse effects and regulation as contrast to supplement toxicity challenges"},{"id":"sdl12_q09","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss challenges to the use of integrative medicine including toxicity, research, and regulation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"St. John's Wort is known to reduce the bioavailability of many co-administered drugs. Which molecular mechanism is responsible for this herb-drug interaction?","choices":{"A":"Upregulation of the pregnane X receptor (PXR) by hyperforin in St. John's Wort, which increases expression of hepatic oxidative, conjugative, and efflux enzymes involved in drug metabolism and transport","B":"Direct competitive inhibition of cytochrome P450 isoforms at the catalytic active site, permanently reducing the capacity of these enzymes to metabolize co-administered drugs to their active or inactive metabolites","C":"Chelation of divalent cations required as cofactors for intestinal drug transport proteins, reducing the bioavailability of drugs that require metal-dependent active transport across the gastrointestinal epithelium","D":"Displacement of protein-bound drugs from albumin and alpha-1-acid glycoprotein binding sites, dramatically increasing the unbound free fraction of narrow therapeutic index drugs and raising toxicity risk","E":"Inhibition of renal tubular secretion through competitive blockade of the organic anion transporter system, extending the half-life of antibiotics and chemotherapeutic agents dependent on renal clearance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hyperforin in St. John's Wort activates PXR (pregnane X receptor), a promiscuous nuclear regulatory factor that upregulates hepatic oxidative (CYP enzymes), conjugative, and efflux enzymes. This was first shown with indinavir (HIV protease inhibitor), then cyclosporine, irinotecan, and many others. Particularly dangerous with narrow therapeutic index drugs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: St. John's Wort → PXR activation → CYP induction → reduced drug levels. Classic examples: indinavir (HIV drug — first documented 2000), cyclosporine (transplant rejection risk), irinotecan (chemo). COMLEX: Herb-drug interactions are a real clinical concern in integrative medicine; asking patients about natural product use is an osteopathic competency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 4 — NSAID adverse effects and regulation as contrast to supplement toxicity challenges"},{"id":"sdl12_q10","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between psychological and physical integrative medicine.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following integrative practices is correctly categorized as having a primary PHYSICAL therapeutic input?","choices":{"A":"Cognitive behavioral therapy, which targets maladaptive thought patterns and behaviors through a structured psychological intervention and is classified as a primary psychological input","B":"Mindfulness-based stress reduction, which cultivates present-moment awareness through meditation practice and is classified as a primary psychological input alongside hypnosis and relaxation training","C":"Acupuncture, which delivers therapeutic input through physical stimulation of defined anatomic points via solid metallic needles or electrical stimulation, classified alongside massage and exercise as a physical input modality","D":"Yoga, which integrates physical movement and postures with meditative elements and is therefore classified as a combined physical and psychological input rather than a primarily physical one","E":"Tai chi, which combines slow gentle movements originating in China with meditative practice and is classified as a combined physical and psychological input along with yoga"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Physical input modalities are delivered manually (massage), by device (acupuncture), or generated by the patient (exercise). Psychological inputs target mental processes (CBT, MBSR, hypnosis). Combined inputs integrate both (yoga, tai chi). Acupuncture is purely physical — the therapeutic mechanism is stimulation of anatomic points, not a mental/psychological process.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the input category for each major integrative modality: acupuncture = physical; CBT/MBSR = psychological; yoga/tai chi = combined. COMLEX: OMM, spinal manipulation, and massage are all physical integrative medicine — understanding this classification helps frame osteopathic medicine within the Harrison's integrative medicine framework.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — Physical therapy modalities as exemplars of physical integrative medicine"},{"id":"sdl12_q11","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between psychological and physical integrative medicine.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The 2017 American College of Physicians clinical guidelines recommend which integrative therapy as one of the initial treatment options for patients with acute, subacute, and chronic low-back pain?","choices":{"A":"Melatonin supplementation, which is indicated for sleep-onset latency and shift work sleep disorder but has no ACP guideline recommendation for the management of low-back pain","B":"Omega-3 fatty acid supplementation, which has evidence for rheumatoid arthritis and age-related macular degeneration but has not been recommended in ACP guidelines for acute or chronic low-back pain","C":"Mindfulness-based stress reduction, which reduces chronic pain severity and is beneficial for fibromyalgia and low-back pain, but does not appear specifically in the 2017 ACP low-back pain guideline","D":"Progressive muscle relaxation, which reduces anxiety and cortisol levels and may provide modest benefit for low-back pain symptoms but is not a specifically recommended initial option in ACP guidelines","E":"Acupuncture, which ACP recommends as an initial treatment option for acute, subacute, and chronic low-back pain, reflecting growing concern about opioid prescribing and increasing acceptance of nonpharmacologic first-line approaches"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The 2017 ACP guidelines recommend nonpharmacologic first-line treatments for low-back pain including acupuncture (all stages: acute, subacute, chronic), spinal manipulation, and massage, reflecting the opioid crisis. Acupuncture has been shown superior to both usual care and sham acupuncture for musculoskeletal pain with effects lasting up to 12 months.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACP 2017 low-back pain guideline: nonpharmacologic first (acupuncture, spinal manipulation, massage, exercise, yoga, tai chi) before NSAIDs, before opioids. This reflects the opioid epidemic response. COMLEX: Spinal manipulation is also in these guidelines — osteopathic physicians are uniquely positioned to offer multiple ACP-recommended nonpharmacologic options.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — Physical therapy modalities as exemplars of physical integrative medicine"},{"id":"sdl12_q12","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between psychological and physical integrative medicine.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Self-hypnosis training has been shown to significantly reduce the need for sedatives and analgesia during which type of medical procedure?","choices":{"A":"Interventional radiologic procedures, where training patients in self-hypnosis reduced their need for sedatives and analgesia, and hypnotherapy also shows benefit for anxiety and quality of life in irritable bowel syndrome","B":"Orthopedic joint replacement surgery, where preoperative hypnosis training has been shown to reduce postoperative opioid consumption more effectively than any other mind-body intervention","C":"Cardiac catheterization, where hypnosis is the recommended primary sedation modality in patients with contrast allergy who cannot receive standard intravenous sedation protocols","D":"Colonoscopy, where hypnosis has been shown to reduce both procedural discomfort and polyp detection rates by altering bowel motility through autonomic nervous system mechanisms","E":"Radiation therapy for prostate cancer, where hypnosis is recommended by American Society for Radiation Oncology guidelines to reduce both anxiety and radiation-induced tissue injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Studies showed self-hypnosis significantly reduced need for sedatives and analgesia during interventional radiologic procedures. Hypnotherapy also benefits anxiety and quality of life in IBS and may help smoking cessation. These are specific evidence-based indications. The other options are not supported by the SDL source material.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypnosis clinical evidence: interventional radiology (sedation/analgesia reduction), IBS (anxiety and QoL), smoking cessation (some evidence, not definitive). COMLEX: Hypnosis is classified as a primary psychological integrative modality; distinguishing its evidence-based applications from unsupported claims is a clinical reasoning competency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — Physical therapy modalities as exemplars of physical integrative medicine"},{"id":"sdl12_q13","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss research challenges in the study of integrative care.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Which regulatory requirement does the Dietary Supplement Health and Education Act (DSHEA) of 1994 explicitly NOT impose on dietary supplement manufacturers?","choices":{"A":"Prohibition against claiming to prevent, treat, or cure specific diseases such as arthritis or cancer on product labels, with violators subject to FTC enforcement actions and FDA warning letters","B":"Labeling requirements identifying the product as a dietary supplement with a list of ingredients, serving size, and a disclosure that the FDA has not evaluated any structure or function claims","C":"Proof of efficacy before marketing, as DSHEA allows supplements to be sold without demonstrating clinical effectiveness — unlike pharmaceutical drugs which must prove efficacy through rigorous clinical trials submitted to the FDA for premarket approval","D":"Compliance with Current Good Manufacturing Practices establishing minimum quality control standards for supplement production, including identity, purity, strength, and composition of ingredients","E":"Prohibition of fraudulent or deceptive advertising claims in digital, print, and broadcast media, enforced through FTC authority to take legal action against promoters of false supplement claims"},"correct":"C","explanation":"DSHEA does NOT require pre-market proof of efficacy — the burden of proving harm lies with the FDA, not the manufacturer. DSHEA does require: no disease claims, structure/function label disclosures, Good Manufacturing Practice compliance. Advertising is regulated by FTC, not FDA. This asymmetry from pharma regulations creates significant consumer safety risks.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Key DSHEA distinction: supplements ≠ drugs. No premarket efficacy proof required. Manufacturer makes safety-responsible, FDA proves harm after the fact. This is why supplements can reach market with limited evidence. COMLEX: Counseling patients about supplement risks requires understanding this regulatory gap — \"sold in stores\" does not mean \"FDA approved.\"","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — Conventional drug mechanism research as contrast to integrative medicine research limitations"},{"id":"sdl12_q14","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss research challenges in the study of integrative care.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Classic randomized controlled trial (RCT) design faces which specific challenge when applied to studying multicomponent complementary systems such as naturopathy or Ayurvedic medicine?","choices":{"A":"Ethical review boards prohibit the use of any control arm in studies of ancient healing traditions because denying patients access to culturally meaningful care constitutes a violation of research ethics principles","B":"The FDA requires a separate investigational new drug application for each natural product contained in a multicomponent naturopathic or Ayurvedic treatment regimen, making the regulatory pathway prohibitive","C":"Insurance companies uniformly deny reimbursement for study visits using complementary interventions, making participant retention impossible in trials where patients must pay out of pocket for study procedures","D":"The individualized, dynamic nature of these systems makes standardizing treatment protocols difficult, and pragmatic comparative effectiveness studies with usual care comparators are used instead, though they cannot determine which specific treatment component accounts for observed benefits","E":"Blinding is structurally impossible in studies of physical integrative therapies because practitioners and participants can always identify the active treatment, rendering all such research inherently unblinded"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Multicomponent systems like naturopathy and Ayurveda are inherently individualized. Classic RCTs require standardized interventions, but these systems adapt to each patient. Pragmatic designs with usual care comparators work better but cannot isolate active components. \"Manualized\" treatment protocols with some flexibility are being developed to address this. Blinding challenges exist but are not the primary issue cited.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RCT limitations for integrative research: individualization prevents standardization; cannot isolate which component works; pragmatic designs used instead. COMLEX: OMM research faces similar challenges — individualized treatment plans, practitioner variability, and difficulty blinding make it methodologically complex to study.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — Conventional drug mechanism research as contrast to integrative medicine research limitations"},{"id":"sdl12_q15","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss research challenges in the study of integrative care.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Which of the following represents a specific research challenge unique to studying natural products compared to psychological or physical integrative interventions?","choices":{"A":"The complexity and variability of natural products, including possible instability of active components, presence of impurities, conflicting conclusions from low-powered studies, and absence of standardized dosing guidance for over-the-counter products","B":"The impossibility of blinding any natural product study because participants can always distinguish an active herbal supplement from a placebo by taste, smell, or physical appearance","C":"The complete absence of federal funding for natural product research from agencies such as the NIH National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health, requiring industry sponsorship for all studies","D":"The requirement that all natural product researchers hold dual degrees in pharmacology and botany, creating a severe workforce shortage that restricts the number of clinical trials that can be conducted","E":"The ethical prohibition against studying natural products alongside conventional drugs, which prevents investigation of the real-world usage patterns most commonly observed in clinical practice"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Natural product research challenges: complexity and variability; possible active component instability; impurities; conflicting literature; low statistical power; paucity of safety data in modern contexts. \"More is not necessarily better\" — excessive doses of vitamins can be harmful. These are distinct from the multicomponent/individualization challenges of systems like naturopathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the natural product research challenges: dose variability, instability, impurities, low statistical power. The NCCIH exists specifically to fund this research (so federal funding is NOT absent). COMLEX: When patients ask about natural products, acknowledging the research limitations and directing them to NCCIH resources is appropriate clinical guidance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — Conventional drug mechanism research as contrast to integrative medicine research limitations"},{"id":"sdl12_q16","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the terms complementary medicine, alternative medicine, and integrative medicine and discuss them in the frameworks of physical, psychological, or combination.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with chronic low-back pain tells her physician she has been using acupuncture and yoga in addition to her prescribed physical therapy. She asks whether this makes her use \"alternative medicine.\" The physician correctly explains the terminological distinction. Which of the following most accurately describes the patient's situation and the correct terms?","choices":{"A":"Acupuncture is classified as alternative medicine because it originated outside Western medicine, while yoga is complementary medicine because it is also used in conventional fitness programs; together they constitute a multicomponent system","B":"Both acupuncture and yoga are classified as alternative medicine because they have not received FDA approval and therefore cannot legally be classified as complementary to conventional medical care","C":"Her use of these practices means she is receiving integrative health care only if a licensed integrative medicine physician is coordinating all three interventions; self-selected combinations do not qualify as integrative care","D":"Her use of acupuncture and yoga alongside conventional physical therapy makes this complementary medicine; if these practices were used instead of conventional treatment, it would be alternative medicine; the coordinated use of all three together constitutes integrative health care","E":"Complementary, alternative, and integrative medicine are synonymous terms in clinical practice and the distinction the patient is asking about reflects an outdated classification system no longer used in medical education"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Complementary = used WITH conventional care. Alternative = used INSTEAD OF conventional care. Integrative health = coordinated use of both, emphasizing whole-person care. The patient uses acupuncture and yoga alongside conventional PT — this is complementary/integrative, not alternative. The terms are not synonymous, and the distinction matters clinically.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Apply the definitions: alongside = complementary; instead of = alternative; coordinated combination = integrative. This distinguishes three specific concepts. COMLEX: Osteopathic physicians practice integrative medicine by combining OMM with conventional care — patients should understand this framing to avoid miscommunication.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — Therapeutic exercises: closed kinetic chain, concentric, core stability, eccentric, isometric, isotonic, open kinetic chain"},{"id":"sdl12_q17","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the terms complementary medicine, alternative medicine, and integrative medicine and discuss them in the frameworks of physical, psychological, or combination.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with knee osteoarthritis asks his rheumatologist about yoga and tai chi as options. The rheumatologist explains that these two practices share a classification that makes them distinct from acupuncture alone or meditation alone. Which classification do yoga and tai chi share?","choices":{"A":"Primary physical input, as both yoga and tai chi involve predominantly physical movement and exercise, with any meditative components considered secondary and clinically insignificant for classification purposes","B":"Combined physical and psychological input, as both integrate physical movement and postures with meditative elements, placing them in a distinct category from purely physical inputs like acupuncture or purely psychological inputs like meditation","C":"Primary psychological input, as both yoga and tai chi originate from Eastern meditation traditions and their therapeutic benefits arise primarily from psychological mechanisms rather than from physical movement","D":"Multicomponent system input, as both originate from traditional Asian medical systems and therefore belong to the same category as traditional Chinese medicine, Ayurveda, and naturopathy","E":"Dietary input, as both yoga and tai chi traditions emphasize dietary practices alongside movement, making nutrition the primary therapeutic input category for these ancient health systems"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Yoga and tai chi combine physical postures/movements with meditative elements, classifying them uniquely as combined physical and psychological inputs. This distinguishes them from: acupuncture (primary physical), massage (primary physical), CBT/MBSR (primary psychological), traditional Chinese medicine (multicomponent system). The combination gives them a broader therapeutic target.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Combined physical + psychological = yoga, tai chi. Physical only = acupuncture, massage, exercise. Psychological only = CBT, MBSR, hypnosis, meditation. This classification has appeared in exam questions asking to categorize specific modalities. COMLEX: The combined nature of yoga/tai chi mirrors the holistic osteopathic approach, explaining why they are endorsed in multiple ACR and ACP guidelines.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — Therapeutic exercises: closed kinetic chain, concentric, core stability, eccentric, isometric, isotonic, open kinetic chain"},{"id":"sdl12_q18","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the terms complementary medicine, alternative medicine, and integrative medicine and discuss them in the frameworks of physical, psychological, or combination.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An integrative medicine specialist explains that their practice incorporates \"salutogenesis\" as a guiding principle. A medical student asks what this term means. Which of the following best defines salutogenesis in the context of integrative health?","choices":{"A":"Salutogenesis refers to the prevention of disease through prophylactic pharmacologic treatment before symptoms arise, as distinguished from reactive treatment given only after a diagnosis has been established","B":"Salutogenesis describes the simultaneous development of multiple chronic comorbidities as a result of shared metabolic risk factors, representing the intersection of pathological pathways in complex patients","C":"Salutogenesis is pathogenesis in reverse — the process by which health is actively restored when recovering from injury, acute illness, exacerbation of chronic disease, or when a predisease condition such as prediabetes is reversed through behavioral change rather than pharmacologic treatment","D":"Salutogenesis is the systematic review of a patient's social determinants of health to identify upstream factors that increase disease risk, forming the basis for population-level public health interventions","E":"Salutogenesis refers to the spontaneous remission of disease that occurs in some patients without treatment, which integrative medicine attempts to harness and amplify through mind-body interventions"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Salutogenesis = pathogenesis in reverse. It emphasizes the process of restoring health rather than solely preventing or treating disease. Integrative health incorporates salutogenesis by addressing recovery from injury/illness and reversing predisease states (e.g., prediabetes, prehypertension) through behavioral interventions. It is not about spontaneous remission or social determinants specifically.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Salutogenesis = health restoration process (vs. pathogenesis = disease development). Integrative medicine frames health as a continuum, with salutogenesis as the return direction. COMLEX: The osteopathic philosophy of treating the whole person and supporting the body's self-healing mechanisms aligns directly with salutogenesis as a guiding principle.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — Therapeutic exercises: closed kinetic chain, concentric, core stability, eccentric, isometric, isotonic, open kinetic chain"},{"id":"sdl12_q19","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize disorders which may be amenable to integrative care.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with fibromyalgia and chronic low-back pain asks about a single integrative intervention that has evidence for both conditions. She prefers something she can do independently after initial instruction. Which integrative practice has evidence supporting benefit for BOTH fibromyalgia AND chronic low-back pain?","choices":{"A":"Acupuncture, which has strong evidence for chronic low-back pain and osteoarthritis, but the SDL source does not specifically describe acupuncture as having evidence for fibromyalgia in this context","B":"Biofeedback, which has the strongest evidence for tension headaches and migraines but lacks specific guideline recommendations addressing fibromyalgia or chronic low-back pain as primary indications","C":"Hypnotherapy, which has specific evidence for IBS and reduction of sedative use during procedures but does not appear as a recommended intervention for fibromyalgia or chronic low-back pain in the SDL","D":"Yoga, which can be beneficial for fibromyalgia patients and shows small to moderate improvements in back-related function at 3 and 6 months for chronic low-back pain compared to nonexercise controls, and also supports quitting smoking and anxiety reduction","E":"Omega-3 supplementation, which has the strongest dietary integrative evidence in rheumatoid arthritis and age-related macular degeneration but not specifically in fibromyalgia or low-back pain conditions"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Yoga has specific evidence for both fibromyalgia (beneficial) and chronic low-back pain (small-moderate improvement at 3 and 6 months vs. nonexercise controls). MBSR also benefits fibromyalgia and low-back pain. Yoga has additional benefits for anxiety, depression, smoking cessation, and quality of life in chronic diseases. The American Cancer Society recommends yoga for anxiety in breast cancer patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Yoga evidence base: fibromyalgia, chronic LBP, anxiety, smoking cessation, cancer QoL. Also recommended by American Cancer Society for breast cancer-related anxiety. COMLEX: As a combined physical-psychological modality, yoga exemplifies whole-person integrative care — its multimodal benefits reflect the osteopathic principle of treating the whole patient.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl12_q20","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize disorders which may be amenable to integrative care.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman undergoing cycle 3 of platinum-based chemotherapy for ovarian cancer reports refractory nausea and vomiting not controlled by standard antiemetics. Her oncologist mentions an integrative option with guideline support. Which integrative practice has specific evidence and clinical guideline support for chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting in cancer patients?","choices":{"A":"Tai chi, which improves overall motor function, balance, stability, sleep quality, and quality of life in cancer patients but has not been specifically evaluated for chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting","B":"Yoga, which is recommended by the American Cancer Society for anxiety and stress reduction during breast cancer treatment but lacks specific evidence for managing chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting","C":"Mindfulness-based stress reduction, which reduces pain through non-opioidergic mechanisms and benefits anxiety in cancer patients but has not been shown to specifically reduce chemotherapy-induced nausea","D":"Progressive muscle relaxation, which may reduce occupational stress and cortisol levels but lacks specific clinical trial evidence for chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting management","E":"Acupuncture or electroacupuncture, which may be an appropriate addition to drug treatment for managing chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting, with guidelines from the Society for Integrative Oncology and American Society of Clinical Oncology supporting its use in cancer patients"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acupuncture and electroacupuncture have evidence for chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting, with 2022 guidelines from the Society for Integrative Oncology and ASCO providing intermediate-level evidence supporting its use. Acupuncture also has evidence for pain in cancer patients (including allergic rhinitis relief). Yoga is recommended for anxiety/stress in cancer but not specifically nausea.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acupuncture indications include: chronic MSK pain, headache, OA (ACP), low-back pain (ACP), chemo-induced nausea (ASCO), pain in cancer, depression (modest), allergic rhinitis. This broad evidence base makes it the most guideline-supported physical integrative modality. COMLEX: When asked about nonpharmacologic management of chemo-related symptoms, acupuncture is a testable answer.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl12_q21","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize disorders which may be amenable to integrative care.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with a 5-year history of chronic migraines asks about integrative options. She has tried two preventive medications without success. Which integrative intervention has the strongest evidence for reducing headache frequency in both pediatric and adult migraine patients, with effects documented over a follow-up period of approximately 17 months?","choices":{"A":"Acupuncture, which has strong evidence for chronic musculoskeletal pain and osteoarthritis and can reduce symptoms of depression, but the specific citation for migraine frequency reduction with a 17-month follow-up is attributed to biofeedback in the SDL","B":"Yoga, which has evidence for reducing anxiety and improving quality of life in patients with chronic conditions but whose primary evidence base for headache specifically involves tension-type rather than migraine headaches","C":"Biofeedback, which has been shown to decrease the frequency of both pediatric and adult migraines, with some studies demonstrating an effect lasting over an average follow-up phase of 17 months","D":"Mindfulness-based stress reduction, which reduces multiple unique non-opioidergic pain pathways and benefits low-back pain and fibromyalgia, with some evidence for anxiety but whose specific migraine prevention data are less robust than biofeedback","E":"Melatonin, which has evidence for reducing sleep-onset latency and shift work sleep disturbance but lacks randomized controlled trial evidence for migraine frequency reduction in adult and pediatric populations"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Biofeedback has the most specific evidence for migraine: decreased frequency in pediatric and adult populations, with effects lasting ~17 months. Relaxation techniques more broadly also benefit migraines. Acupuncture has broad pain evidence including headache, but the specific 17-month migraine frequency data cited in Harrison's is for biofeedback.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Biofeedback = tension headaches and migraines (strongest evidence, ~17-month durability). Relaxation techniques broadly help anxiety and stress conditions. Acupuncture helps headache but biofeedback is the specific migraine evidence citation in this SDL. COMLEX: Biofeedback is a psychological integrative modality that trains patients to control autonomic functions — an important distinction from physical manipulation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl12_q22","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss challenges to the use of integrative medicine including toxicity, research, and regulation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old man is hospitalized with acute hepatic failure. His family reports he had been consuming wild mushrooms he foraged himself, believing them to have medicinal properties based on information from a wellness website. Which category of integrative medicine risk does this case exemplify?","choices":{"A":"Inherent toxicity of natural products, as misidentification of medicinal mushrooms can cause liver failure, demonstrating that \"natural\" does not equate to \"safe\" — a critical principle of integrative medicine risk counseling","B":"Herb-drug interaction, as the hepatotoxicity resulted from the wild mushrooms upregulating pregnane X receptor expression in hepatic tissue, reducing the bioavailability of his prescribed medications","C":"Regulatory failure under DSHEA, as foraging websites making medicinal claims about wild mushrooms violate DSHEA structure-function claim restrictions and fall under FTC false advertising jurisdiction","D":"Adulteration of a dietary supplement product, as the mushrooms he consumed were contaminated with anabolic steroids and synthetic stimulants commonly found in products marketed for immune support","E":"Homeopathic product toxicity, as the diluted mushroom extract he consumed in homeopathic teething tablets contained significant amounts of undeclared pharmacologically active belladonna compounds"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Misidentification of medicinal mushrooms → liver failure is a specific example of inherent natural product toxicity. Other examples: tryptophan contamination → eosinophilia-myalgia syndrome; Aristolochia → GU malignancies and interstitial nephritis; ephedra → cardiovascular events. \"Natural\" ≠ \"safe\" is the core principle. This is NOT an herb-drug interaction, adulteration, or homeopathic scenario.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Natural product toxicity examples: mushrooms → liver failure; tryptophan → eosinophilia-myalgia; Aristolochia → GU cancer + nephritis; ephedra → cardiac events; DMAA → cardiovascular problems. Keep each example matched to its specific toxidrome. COMLEX: Counseling patients that natural products require the same safety scrutiny as medications is an osteopathic competency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 4 — Adverse effects of NSAIDs by body system"},{"id":"sdl12_q23","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss challenges to the use of integrative medicine including toxicity, research, and regulation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 32-year-old man with HIV on indinavir-based antiretroviral therapy begins taking St. John's Wort for mild depression. Over 4 weeks his viral load becomes detectable. His physician correctly identifies a molecular mechanism responsible for this interaction. Which explanation is correct?","choices":{"A":"St. John's Wort directly inhibits HIV reverse transcriptase at the same active site as nucleoside analogs, competing with indinavir and reducing the combined antiretroviral efficacy of the regimen","B":"St. John's Wort chelates divalent metal cofactors required for intestinal absorption of protease inhibitors, reducing indinavir bioavailability through a non-enzymatic mechanism in the gastrointestinal lumen","C":"St. John's Wort directly inhibits the CD4 receptor on T lymphocytes through competitive binding, paradoxically facilitating HIV entry into host cells despite concurrent antiretroviral therapy","D":"St. John's Wort displaces indinavir from plasma albumin binding sites, dramatically increasing the unbound drug fraction and causing dose-dependent protease inhibitor toxicity rather than treatment failure","E":"Hyperforin in St. John's Wort upregulates the pregnane X receptor (PXR), a nuclear regulatory factor that increases expression of hepatic oxidative, conjugative, and efflux enzymes, reducing indinavir bioavailability — the first drug interaction demonstrated for St. John's Wort in 2000"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The St. John's Wort-indinavir interaction was first described in 2000 and is mediated by hyperforin activating PXR, which upregulates CYP enzymes and efflux transporters (MDR1/P-glycoprotein), reducing drug levels. This same mechanism affects cyclosporine, irinotecan, anticoagulants, anticonvulsants, and many others. Most important with narrow therapeutic index drugs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: St. John's Wort + indinavir was the landmark case. Mechanism = PXR upregulation → CYP induction → decreased drug levels. Not direct inhibition. Clinically important: anticoagulants, immunosuppressants, oral contraceptives, chemo agents. COMLEX: Thorough medication history in osteopathic practice must include herbal supplements — this interaction exemplifies why.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 4 — Adverse effects of NSAIDs by body system"},{"id":"sdl12_q24","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss challenges to the use of integrative medicine including toxicity, research, and regulation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient shows you a dietary supplement label that states: \"This product treats arthritis and reverses joint degeneration.\" You explain this is an illegal disease claim. Which claim on a supplement label WOULD be permissible under DSHEA?","choices":{"A":"A claim that the supplement is FDA-approved as safe and effective for reduction of joint inflammation based on Phase III clinical trial data submitted for premarket review","B":"A claim that the supplement prevents the development of osteoarthritis in high-risk patients, as prevention of a named disease would constitute a legally permissible prophylactic health claim","C":"A claim that the supplement cures rheumatoid arthritis by modulating immune function and has been shown in head-to-head trials to be equivalent to approved disease-modifying antirheumatic drugs","D":"A claim that the supplement \"maintains normal joint health\" or \"supports normal structure and function\" of the musculoskeletal system, as DSHEA explicitly permits structure/function claims while prohibiting claims to prevent, treat, or cure any named disease","E":"A claim that the supplement treats the pain and inflammation of specific disease states including osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis, provided the manufacturer discloses the absence of FDA evaluation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"DSHEA structure/function claims examples: \"maintains normal joint health,\" \"supports immune function,\" \"promotes bone health.\" Disease claims examples (prohibited): \"treats arthritis,\" \"prevents osteoporosis,\" \"cures RA.\" Disclosure of no FDA evaluation does NOT make disease claims permissible. FDA does NOT grant premarket approval for supplements.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DSHEA permitted = structure/function claims with FDA-disclaimer. DSHEA prohibited = disease claims (treat, prevent, cure). Classic example: can say \"maintains normal joint health\" but not \"treats arthritis.\" FTC acts against deceptive advertising; FDA acts against false label claims. COMLEX: Patients often show physicians supplement labels — recognizing illegal disease claims is a practical clinical skill.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 4 — Adverse effects of NSAIDs by body system"},{"id":"sdl12_q25","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between psychological and physical integrative medicine.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with a 6-month history of refractory chronic insomnia has tried sleep hygiene measures without success. Before prescribing sedative-hypnotics, her primary care physician recommends the integrative intervention with the strongest guideline endorsement for this condition. Which approach is correct?","choices":{"A":"Mindfulness-based stress reduction, which has growing evidence for sleep improvement and benefits anxiety reduction in patients with ongoing health problems, but does not carry the specific ACP guideline endorsement that CBT-I does for chronic insomnia","B":"Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I), which the American College of Physicians strongly recommends as the initial treatment for chronic insomnia, classified as a primary psychological integrative modality","C":"Melatonin supplementation, which reduces sleep-onset latency and improves daytime sleepiness in shift work sleep disorder, but which carries safety concerns in children and has mislabeling issues in gummy products","D":"Progressive muscle relaxation as a standalone intervention, which may provide modest benefit for anxiety-related sleep disturbance but lacks the strong ACP guideline recommendation status of comprehensive CBT-I programs","E":"Acupuncture, which the ACP recommends as an initial option for chronic low-back pain and is classified as a primary physical integrative modality, but does not appear in ACP guidelines as a recommended first-line treatment for chronic insomnia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"ACP 2016 guidelines strongly recommend CBT-I as the first-line treatment for chronic insomnia before pharmacologic agents. CBT-I is a primary psychological integrative modality (it targets cognitions, behaviors, and arousal around sleep). Melatonin has limited evidence and safety concerns. MBSR has some sleep benefit but lacks the ACP endorsement level of CBT-I.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CBT-I = ACP strongly recommended initial treatment for chronic insomnia. Classify it as a psychological integrative modality. COMLEX: The recommendation to use CBT-I before sedative-hypnotics reflects the osteopathic principle of using the least invasive intervention first; OMM for sleep-related somatic dysfunction may complement CBT-I.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — Physical therapy modalities: ultrasound, phonophoresis, iontophoresis, electrical stimulation, low-level laser therapy"},{"id":"sdl12_q26","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between psychological and physical integrative medicine.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A clinical educator asks students to classify several integrative modalities by their primary therapeutic input. Which of the following correctly identifies a modality with COMBINED physical AND psychological input, as distinct from primarily physical or primarily psychological interventions?","choices":{"A":"Acupuncture, which delivers therapeutic input through physical stimulation of anatomic points using solid metallic needles and is classified as a primary physical input alongside massage therapy and exercise","B":"Cognitive behavioral therapy, which targets maladaptive thought patterns and behaviors through structured psychological intervention and is classified as a primary psychological input alongside MBSR and hypnosis","C":"Tai chi, which combines slow gentle physical movements with meditative elements and is classified alongside yoga as a combined physical and psychological input, distinct from acupuncture (physical) or CBT (psychological)","D":"Omega-3 fatty acid supplementation, which delivers therapeutic input through dietary modification and is classified as a primary dietary input alongside herbs, vitamins, minerals, and other natural products","E":"Spinal manipulation, which uses a controlled physical thrust applied to a joint and is classified as a primary physical input alongside massage, acupuncture, and patient-generated exercise"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Combined physical and psychological input: yoga and tai chi. Physical: massage, acupuncture, exercise, spinal manipulation. Psychological: CBT, MBSR, hypnosis, meditation. Dietary: omega-3, herbs, supplements. Tai chi specifically combines slow movement (physical) with meditative focus (psychological), which accounts for its broad benefits including motor function, sleep, and quality of life.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Input category classification is tested — know where each major modality belongs. Tai chi = combined (physical movement + meditation). Acupuncture = physical (device stimulation). CBT = psychological. COMLEX: The osteopathic philosophy resonates with combined modalities because OMM addresses both physical somatic dysfunction and the psychological stress that maintains it.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — Physical therapy modalities: ultrasound, phonophoresis, iontophoresis, electrical stimulation, low-level laser therapy"},{"id":"sdl12_q27","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between psychological and physical integrative medicine.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with chronic low-back pain is treated with osteopathic spinal manipulative therapy. His wife asks what evidence supports this. According to ACP guidelines and current evidence, which statement best describes the evidence base for spinal manipulation in low-back pain?","choices":{"A":"Spinal manipulation is recommended against by ACP guidelines for low-back pain due to the risk of vascular injury during cervical manipulation, which outweighs any documented functional benefit in the lumbar spine","B":"Spinal manipulation has high-quality evidence demonstrating clear superiority to acupuncture, yoga, and massage therapy for both acute and chronic low-back pain across all patient age groups","C":"Spinal manipulation carries only a weak ACP recommendation for low-back pain because the evidence quality is rated high but the benefit magnitude is considered too small to justify its routine use clinically","D":"Spinal manipulation has a small effect on improving function and pain compared to sham manipulation or inert control, and nonpharmacologic treatment including spinal manipulation carries a strong ACP recommendation reflecting increasing concern about chronic opioid prescribing for low-back pain","E":"Spinal manipulation is classified as alternative rather than complementary medicine by ACP guidelines and therefore falls outside the scope of its nonpharmacologic treatment recommendations for low-back pain"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Evidence for spinal manipulation: small effect on function and pain vs. control. ACP rates nonpharmacologic treatment (including spinal manipulation) as a STRONG recommendation for low-back pain, driven by opioid crisis concerns. Evidence quality is LOW (not high), but the strong recommendation reflects the importance of nonpharmacologic alternatives. Cervical manipulation has vascular risk concerns but this applies to the neck, not as a blanket recommendation against lumbar SMT.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Spinal manipulation = small effect, strong ACP recommendation (nonpharmacologic treatment category). Low-quality evidence overall but strong recommendation context = opioid avoidance. Cervical SMT has vascular risk (vertebral artery dissection) — important distinction from lumbar. COMLEX: OMM is the osteopathic version of spinal manipulation; understanding this evidence base prepares you to discuss it with patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — Physical therapy modalities: ultrasound, phonophoresis, iontophoresis, electrical stimulation, low-level laser therapy"},{"id":"sdl12_q28","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss research challenges in the study of integrative care.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"The first large national survey documenting that more than 30% of Americans use complementary health products and practices surprised the medical community upon its publication. Who conducted this survey and in what year?","choices":{"A":"David Eisenberg and associates in 1993, whose survey revealed that more than 30% of Americans used complementary health products and practices — a finding that surprised the medical community and launched large-scale follow-up research","B":"The CDC National Center for Health Statistics in 1985, which performed the first National Health Interview Survey (NHIS) component specifically tracking complementary and alternative medicine use in U.S. households","C":"The National Institutes of Health NCCIH in 2002, which funded the first large-scale systematic review to establish prevalence estimates and annual out-of-pocket expenditures on complementary health practices","D":"The Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality in 1998, which established the first federal tracking system specifically documenting expenditures on complementary health practices as a proportion of total health spending","E":"The American Medical Association in 1975, which conducted the original national survey on patient self-referral to complementary health practitioners that prompted early professional interest in this phenomenon"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Eisenberg et al. 1993 = the landmark survey. Found >30% of Americans use complementary practices, surprising the medical community. The NHIS (conducted by CDC/NCHS) has since expanded this surveillance, surveying in 2002, 2007, 2012, 2017, and 2022. The 2022 NHIS found 36.7% use, up from 19.2% in 2002 for the seven tracked approaches.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Eisenberg 1993 = landmark survey showing >30% of Americans used complementary medicine. 2022 NHIS = 36.7% (vs. 19.2% in 2002). Out-of-pocket: $30.2 billion in 2012 (9.2% of total OOP costs). Pain = most common reason for complementary health use. COMLEX: Understanding the prevalence data helps osteopathic physicians recognize why integrative counseling is a routine clinical need.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 2 — Common mechanism of action for NSAID anti-inflammatory activity"},{"id":"sdl12_q29","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss research challenges in the study of integrative care.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A research team designs a study to evaluate a naturopathic treatment protocol for metabolic syndrome. They find that applying a classic parallel-group RCT design is problematic. Which feature of naturopathic medicine most directly creates this methodologic challenge?","choices":{"A":"Naturopathic practitioners lack licensure in most U.S. states, making study enrollment problematic because only licensed health care providers may legally serve as study investigators in federally funded clinical research","B":"The multicomponent and individualized nature of naturopathic protocols means treatment varies by patient, making standardization of interventions difficult, and pragmatic studies using usual care comparators cannot identify which specific component accounts for observed benefits","C":"FDA regulations require a separate investigational new drug application for each natural product included in a naturopathic protocol, creating an insurmountable regulatory barrier that prevents most such trials from being initiated","D":"Insurance companies uniformly deny reimbursement for naturopathic study visits, making participation financially prohibitive for patients with metabolic syndrome who face disproportionately high baseline health care costs","E":"Blinding is structurally impossible in all naturopathic research because both practitioners and participants can always identify whether active naturopathic treatment or a sham protocol is being administered"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Classic RCT requires a standardized, reproducible intervention. Naturopathy individualizes treatment — a given patient might receive different herbal, dietary, physical, and lifestyle combinations. Pragmatic trials with usual care comparators work better but cannot isolate which component helped. \"Manualized\" protocols with patient-level flexibility are being developed. Blinding challenges exist but are not unique to naturopathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RCT challenge in multicomponent integrative systems = individualization prevents standardization + cannot isolate active components. Solution = pragmatic designs + manualized protocols. The NCCIH funds much of this research. COMLEX: OMM research faces the same individualization challenge — treatment plans are patient-specific, making RCT design in osteopathic research similarly complex.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 2 — Common mechanism of action for NSAID anti-inflammatory activity"},{"id":"sdl12_q30","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss research challenges in the study of integrative care.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A health policy researcher asks you about U.S. out-of-pocket spending on complementary health practices based on the 2012 National Health Interview Survey data. Which statement accurately represents these expenditure findings?","choices":{"A":"Americans spent approximately $30.2 billion on complementary health practices in 2012, with the majority spent on yoga and meditation classes; natural product spending accounted for less than 5% of this total","B":"Americans spent approximately $5 billion on complementary health practices in 2012, primarily on acupuncture and chiropractic visits, representing a minor fraction of total out-of-pocket expenditures nationally","C":"Americans spent approximately $75 billion on complementary health practices in 2012, exceeding their total spending on conventional physician visits and making complementary care the single largest category of personal health expenditure","D":"Americans spent approximately $30.2 billion on complementary health practices in 2012, which exceeded total out-of-pocket spending on prescription drugs ($54.1 billion), making supplements the dominant out-of-pocket health cost","E":"Americans spent approximately $30.2 billion out of pocket on complementary health practices in 2012 — about $14.7 billion on practitioner visits and $12.8 billion on natural products — representing 9.2% of total out-of-pocket health expenditures and about one-quarter of what was spent on prescription drugs"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Exact 2012 NHIS data: $30.2 billion total OOP ($28.3B adults + $1.9B children). $14.7B on practitioner visits (vs. $49.6B conventional physicians). $12.8B on natural products (vs. $54.1B prescription drugs = ~24%). Represents 9.2% of total OOP costs and 1.1% of total health expenditures. Pain is the most common driver of spending.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the 2012 expenditure data: $30.2B total; $14.7B practitioner visits; $12.8B natural products = ~24% of RX drug spending. This puts integrative medicine use in economic perspective. COMLEX: The high out-of-pocket cost burden reflects patient demand that is not covered by insurance — osteopathic physicians should be prepared to counsel on cost-effective integrative options.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 2 — Common mechanism of action for NSAID anti-inflammatory activity"}]},{"sdlNumber":13,"title":"Shoulder Pain & Pathologies","questions":[{"id":"sdl13_q01","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which feature of a shoulder pain presentation most strongly suggests an EXTRINSIC rather than intrinsic cause?","choices":{"A":"Anterior shoulder pain aggravated by glenohumeral abduction and external rotation, with focal anterior joint line tenderness and a directional loss of motion corresponding to those same planes on examination","B":"Sharp, shooting, or electric pain radiating from the shoulder to the neck or down the arm, accompanied by dyspnea or diaphoresis, with symmetric shoulder appearance and glenohumeral motion unaffected on examination","C":"Gradually worsening anterior shoulder pain over several years with morning stiffness lasting less than 30 minutes, crepitus during motion, and age greater than 45 years suggesting glenohumeral osteoarthritis","D":"Lateral shoulder pain referred to the deltoid insertion on the humerus, worse at night and when sleeping on the affected side, associated with overhead activities and weakness if a significant rotator cuff tear is present","E":"Superior shoulder pain worsening with adduction and forward flexion, with localized tenderness directly over the acromioclavicular joint and a corresponding loss of motion in those specific loading planes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Extrinsic shoulder pain clues from the diagnostic workflow include: vague or poorly localized pain, pain associated with other symptoms such as dyspnea, abdominal pain, or diaphoresis, sharp or electric pain radiating to the neck or down the arm, a history concerning for extrinsic cause such as recent MVC or cardiac disease, glenohumeral motion does NOT reproduce the pain, and symmetric shoulder appearance, motion, and strength. All other choices describe intrinsic patterns (GH OA, rotator cuff, ACJ OA). The \"shooting with dyspnea\" combination is a classic red flag for referred or visceral pathology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the extrinsic shoulder pain red flags: dyspnea + shoulder pain = rule out cardiac/pulmonary; diaphoresis = autonomic activation from visceral source. Glenohumeral motion should NOT reproduce extrinsic pain. COMLEX: Osteopathic physicians use thorough somatic and visceral assessment — Chapman reflex for lung/heart can produce extrinsic shoulder pain in osteopathic context.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — Glenohumeral joint dislocation as the primary trauma-positive finding in the shoulder pain diagnostic algorithm"},{"id":"sdl13_q02","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 60-year-old man presents with shoulder pain after a fall on an outstretched hand. Per the shoulder pain diagnostic algorithm, what is the FIRST branching point that determines the direction of the workup?","choices":{"A":"Determine whether the pattern of motion loss is directional (suggesting arthritis) or global (suggesting frozen shoulder) before proceeding to radiographs, since this classification avoids unnecessary imaging costs","B":"Determine whether the pain is primarily glenohumeral or extraglenohumereal based on whether gentle passive glenohumeral motion reproduces the pain during the physical examination portion of the visit","C":"Order plain radiographs as the first action in all shoulder pain presentations regardless of mechanism, since imaging is required before any classification of the shoulder pain source can be made","D":"Classify the shoulder complaint as intrinsic versus extrinsic based on pain character, associated symptoms, and the symmetry of shoulder appearance, motion, and strength on bilateral comparison examination","E":"Determine whether a significant history of trauma is present, as trauma versus no trauma is the primary bifurcation point that routes the evaluation toward imaging and fracture or dislocation workup versus clinical assessment for intrinsic or extrinsic cause"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The UpToDate shoulder pain diagnostic workflow begins with a single gate: \"Is there a history of trauma?\" This is the primary bifurcation. Trauma-positive routes to history, physical exam, and plain radiographs to assess for fracture, glenohumeral dislocation, AC sprain, or sternoclavicular subluxation. Trauma-negative routes to the intrinsic versus extrinsic distinction. The intrinsic versus extrinsic differentiation and the glenohumeral versus extraglenohumereal distinction occur downstream from the trauma gate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The trauma gate is intuitive but often tested in the context of missed fractures — always image a traumatized shoulder. COMLEX: Osteopathic evaluation adds palpatory assessment of the shoulder girdle (acromioclavicular, sternoclavicular, glenohumeral) and may identify somatic dysfunction at T2-T4 as a viscerosomatic source of shoulder pain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — Glenohumeral joint dislocation as the primary trauma-positive finding in the shoulder pain diagnostic algorithm"},{"id":"sdl13_q03","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which finding on physical examination best localizes a shoulder complaint to an EXTRAGLENOHUMEREAL (rather than glenohumeral intra-articular) intrinsic source?","choices":{"A":"Focal pain and tenderness localized outside the glenohumeral joint capsule, with minimal or no pain elicited by gentle passive glenohumeral motion during examination, pointing toward biceps tendinopathy, AC arthritis, subscapular bursitis, or distal clavicle osteolysis","B":"Anterior glenohumeral joint line tenderness with pain and loss of motion in abduction and external rotation that worsens with active resistance testing, consistent with a glenohumeral intra-articular source such as GH osteoarthritis","C":"Global loss of active and passive motion in multiple planes with preserved strength and absence of crepitus, consistent with a capsular process such as adhesive capsulitis of the glenohumeral joint interior","D":"Weakness of the supraspinatus on resisted abduction testing with a positive drop arm sign and pain referring to the lateral deltoid insertion on the humerus during overhead activity or active abduction","E":"Superior shoulder tenderness directly over the acromioclavicular joint that worsens specifically with cross-body adduction and forward flexion maneuvers that load the AC joint during examination"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Per the diagnostic workflow, extraglenohumereal causes are identified by two criteria: (1) focal pain and tenderness outside the GH joint, and (2) gentle passive motion of the glenohumeral joint generally does NOT cause pain. Extraglenohumereal conditions include biceps tendinopathy, biceps tendon tear, AC arthritis, scapulothoracic weakness, subscapular bursitis, and distal clavicle osteolysis. Choices B through E all describe glenohumeral intra-articular patterns (GH OA, adhesive capsulitis, rotator cuff tear, and AC arthritis respectively — ACJ arthritis is technically extraglenohumereal but choice E describes GH passive motion behavior incompletely).","boardPrep":"Step 1: The \"passive GH motion does not hurt\" rule is the key clinical divider. Biceps tendinopathy is the most classic extraglenohumereal diagnosis — Speed test and Yergason test assess it specifically. COMLEX: Biceps tendinopathy in the context of shoulder somatic dysfunction is a common COMLEX scenario where OMM to restore GH motion may reduce impingement on the biceps tendon.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — Glenohumeral joint dislocation as the primary trauma-positive finding in the shoulder pain diagnostic algorithm"},{"id":"sdl13_q04","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman has 3 years of gradually worsening anterior shoulder and upper arm pain, morning stiffness lasting 20 minutes, increased stiffness after prolonged rest, and loss of motion worst in abduction and external rotation. Exam shows anterior joint line tenderness and mild crepitus. Which diagnosis and mechanism best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Adhesive capsulitis of the glenohumeral joint, characterized by idiopathic global loss of motion in all planes, preserved strength, pain-then-stiffness pattern, and stronger association with diabetes and thyroid disease than with age-related degeneration","B":"Glenohumeral osteoarthritis, with degenerative cartilage loss at the glenohumeral articulation producing usage-related anterior shoulder and upper arm pain that is directionally worst in abduction and external rotation, consistent with OARSI clinical criteria","C":"Acromioclavicular osteoarthritis, with degenerative changes at the superior shoulder articulation producing superior (not anterior) shoulder pain that is characteristically worst with adduction and forward flexion rather than abduction and external rotation","D":"Rotator cuff impingement with subacromial tendinosis, producing supraspinatus compression in the extra-articular subacromial space with pain referring to the lateral deltoid insertion on the humerus rather than diffusely to the upper arm","E":"Subacromial bursitis with fluid accumulation in the subdeltoid bursa causing anterior shoulder pain, but distinguished from OA by absence of crepitus, morning stiffness, and usage-related progressive course over years"},"correct":"B","explanation":"GH OA: most common type of arthritis at the GH joint; most patients >70 (women > men); anterior shoulder and upper arm pain (GH location); morning stiffness; stiffer with inactivity; loss of motion worst in abduction and external rotation; anterior joint line tenderness and crepitus. ACJ OA produces SUPERIOR shoulder pain worst with adduction/forward flexion. Adhesive capsulitis produces GLOBAL motion loss in all planes. Rotator cuff pain refers to the lateral humerus. These location and plane distinctions are high-yield board discriminators.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH OA = anterior shoulder + upper arm, worst abduction/ER. ACJ OA = superior shoulder, worst adduction/forward flexion. COMLEX: GH OA has a COMLEX bump because shoulder somatic dysfunction and OMM are tested in the context of glenohumeral pathology and post-arthroplasty rehabilitation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis general framework: diagnostic criteria and stepwise management that apply directly to shoulder OA"},{"id":"sdl13_q05","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement most accurately distinguishes the clinical presentation of acromioclavicular joint osteoarthritis from glenohumeral osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Acromioclavicular OA primarily affects patients under age 50 with prior overhead sports exposure, while glenohumeral OA primarily affects women over age 70 with prior dislocation, fracture, or massive rotator cuff tear history","B":"Acromioclavicular OA produces posterior shoulder pain worsening with internal rotation and extension, while glenohumeral OA produces lateral deltoid pain worsening with overhead motion due to subacromial compression","C":"Acromioclavicular OA produces superior shoulder pain that is worst with adduction and forward flexion, while glenohumeral OA produces anterior shoulder and upper arm pain that is worst with abduction and external rotation","D":"Acromioclavicular OA always requires radiographic joint space narrowing for clinical diagnosis, while glenohumeral OA is exclusively a clinical diagnosis that does not require or benefit from any radiographic assessment","E":"Acromioclavicular OA is managed with joint replacement as first-line therapy, while glenohumeral OA requires a stepwise conservative approach with pharmacologic management and physical therapy before surgical options"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The most tested distinction: ACJ OA = superior shoulder pain, worst with ADDUCTION and FORWARD FLEXION (planes that load the AC joint — cross-body adduction test is positive). GH OA = anterior shoulder and upper arm pain, worst with ABDUCTION and EXTERNAL ROTATION (planes that load the glenohumeral joint — GH joint line tenderness anteriorly). Both share similar diagnostic criteria (age ≥45, usage-related pain, morning stiffness ≤30 min) and both are managed with a similar OARSI-adapted framework. Joint replacement is a last resort for both.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Remember — ACJ = ADDUCTION worsens (cross-body maneuver). GH = ABDUCTION/ER worsens. These opposing plane findings appear frequently on USMLE. COMLEX: Both joints can develop somatic dysfunction and may respond to OMM — AC joint manipulation is a tested COMLEX technique.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis general framework: diagnostic criteria and stepwise management that apply directly to shoulder OA"},{"id":"sdl13_q06","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which set of criteria constitutes the SIMPLEST clinical diagnostic approach for shoulder osteoarthritis taught in this SDL?","choices":{"A":"Persistent usage-related joint pain in one or a few joints, age 45 years or greater, and morning stiffness lasting 30 minutes or less — three clinical criteria that together confirm osteoarthritis without requiring radiographic confirmation","B":"Radiographic osteophyte formation confirmed on plain films, progressive glenohumeral joint space narrowing over two or more imaging studies, and symptom duration exceeding 12 months before the diagnosis of shoulder OA can be established","C":"Positive Neer impingement sign, positive Hawkins test, restricted abduction below 120 degrees with audible crepitus, and age greater than 60 years — all four criteria required simultaneously to diagnose shoulder osteoarthritis","D":"Global loss of passive motion in all planes lasting more than 6 months, synovial fluid analysis showing a non-inflammatory profile with fewer than 2,000 white blood cells, and an elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate","E":"Subacromial tenderness, pain with resisted external rotation, and superior humeral migration on plain radiographs demonstrating a subacromial space narrowed to less than 6 millimeters — distinguishing OA from rotator cuff impingement"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The NICE/OARSI-adapted clinical OA criteria taught in this SDL are three points: (1) persistent usage-related joint pain in one or a few joints, (2) age ≥45 years, (3) morning stiffness ≤30 minutes. This is a CLINICAL diagnosis — no imaging is required. Radiographic index and pain/function indices are additional assessment tools but not required for the diagnosis. Impingement tests (Neer, Hawkins) and drop arm testing apply to rotator cuff pathology, not OA diagnosis. Global motion loss suggests adhesive capsulitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA = CLINICAL diagnosis. Age ≥45 + usage pain + morning stiffness ≤30 min. The ≤30 minute cutoff distinguishes OA from inflammatory arthritis (RA morning stiffness typically >1 hour). COMLEX: OA management framework from OARSI includes physical therapy, corticosteroid injections, regenerative injections, acupuncture, and joint replacement — all appear in COMLEX clinical management questions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis general framework: diagnostic criteria and stepwise management that apply directly to shoulder OA"},{"id":"sdl13_q07","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder pathologies, including rotator cuff injuries, bursitis, and AC joint arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Rotator cuff impingement occurs in the subacromial space. Which anatomical fact about this space is most clinically important?","choices":{"A":"The subacromial space is an intra-articular compartment within the glenohumeral joint capsule, so glenohumeral corticosteroid injection simultaneously treats both impingement and GH osteoarthritis with a single injection procedure","B":"The subacromial space is extra-articular (outside the glenohumeral joint capsule), so impingement pathology is distinct from glenohumeral joint disease and requires targeted subacromial injection rather than intra-articular glenohumeral injection for direct treatment","C":"The subacromial space is narrowed exclusively by edema of the supraspinatus tendon, while chronic tendinosis causing tendon thinning actually widens the space and reduces impingement because the tendon occupies less volume","D":"The subacromial space is a diarthrodial synovial joint lined by synovium, and inflammation of this space constitutes a septic bursitis that requires emergent aspiration, culture, and intravenous antibiotic therapy in all cases","E":"The subacromial space contains the infraspinatus tendon as its primary occupant, and infraspinatus impingement is the most common rotator cuff pathology because the posterior cuff is most exposed to mechanical stress"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The subacromial space is EXTRA-articular — outside the GH joint capsule. This is a key clinical distinction: subacromial injection targets the bursa and rotator cuff tendons directly, while GH intra-articular injection targets joint cartilage and synovium. The supraspinatus (not infraspinatus) occupies the subacromial space's \"critical zone.\" Two impingement mechanisms: (1) edematous supraspinatus too large to glide through the space; (2) thin/degenerated supraspinatus allows superior humeral migration, narrowing the space from the humeral side. Patients cycle between these states.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Extra-articular subacromial space = supraspinatus, subacromial-subdeltoid bursa. Intra-articular = GH joint capsule and cartilage. Injection target depends on pathology location. COMLEX: Subacromial injection is a common osteopathic procedural topic. Glenohumeral somatic dysfunction can reduce subacromial clearance — an OMM rationale for treating impingement conservatively.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Little league shoulder: pediatric rotator cuff overuse injury sharing the same subacromial anatomy as adult impingement"},{"id":"sdl13_q08","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder pathologies, including rotator cuff injuries, bursitis, and AC joint arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The pathologic progression of rotator cuff tendinosis is best described as which of the following?","choices":{"A":"An acute inflammatory infiltration of the supraspinatus tendon that permanently resolves with anti-inflammatory treatment within 6 weeks, leaving no residual structural vulnerability or predisposition to re-injury in the affected tendon","B":"A process beginning with bursal-sided full-thickness tearing that propagates to the articular surface over time due to repetitive eccentric loading, and anti-inflammatory medications can halt propagation if initiated within the first 3 months","C":"Calcification of the supraspinatus tendon from calcium hydroxyapatite deposition that mechanically narrows the subacromial space, producing calcific tendinitis rather than true tendinosis with a distinct treatment algorithm","D":"Progressive glenohumeral joint capsule fibrosis triggered by rotator cuff pain inhibition, producing global loss of motion in all planes that is indistinguishable from primary adhesive capsulitis on clinical examination alone","E":"After approximately 3 months of ongoing rotator cuff injury, the tendon enters a chronic degenerative state with no inflammatory cells present, characterized by tissue disorganization and degeneration that can periodically re-inflame, perpetuating the rotator cuff impingement cycle"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tendinosis vs. tendinitis: it is only a true \"tendinitis\" if acutely inflamed. Once chronic (approximately 3 months), no inflammatory cells are present — the tendon is in a state of disorganization and degeneration. This chronic state can periodically re-inflame, driving the rotator cuff impingement cycle. This distinction matters therapeutically: anti-inflammatories and corticosteroids address the acute inflammatory phase but do not reverse chronic degeneration. Surgery is reserved for recalcitrant cases and full or massive tears. PT is the mainstay of management throughout.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tendinosis (3+ months, no inflammation, disorganized collagen) vs. tendinitis (acute, inflammatory cells, responds to NSAIDs). Classic board trap: calling a chronic rotator cuff condition \"tendinitis\" and treating only with NSAIDs. COMLEX: Osteopathic management of tendinosis includes prolotherapy and PRP (pro-inflammatory regenerative injections to stimulate repair) — listed explicitly in this SDL's treatment options.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Little league shoulder: pediatric rotator cuff overuse injury sharing the same subacromial anatomy as adult impingement"},{"id":"sdl13_q09","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder pathologies, including rotator cuff injuries, bursitis, and AC joint arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which physical examination test specifically assesses rotator cuff tendon INTEGRITY (presence/absence of a significant tear) rather than the presence of impingement?","choices":{"A":"The Neer impingement sign, performed by stabilizing the scapula and passively forward flexing the internally rotated arm, which compresses the supraspinatus against the anterior acromion and is positive in subacromial impingement syndrome","B":"The Hawkins impingement test, performed by forward flexing the shoulder to 90 degrees and then passively internally rotating the arm, which compresses the supraspinatus against the coracoacromial ligament and is positive in impingement","C":"The cross-body adduction test, performed by passively adducting the arm horizontally across the chest at 90 degrees, which compresses the acromioclavicular joint and is positive in AC joint arthritis rather than rotator cuff pathology","D":"The Drop Arm Test, performed by passively abducting the patient's arm to 90 degrees and asking them to slowly lower it — inability to control the lowering indicates a significant full-thickness rotator cuff tear affecting active tendon function","E":"The Speed test, performed by forward flexing the arm against resistance with the elbow extended and forearm supinated, which loads the proximal biceps tendon and is positive in biceps tendinopathy rather than rotator cuff tear"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Per this SDL, tendon strength and integrity testing includes resisted abduction, external rotation, and internal rotation — PLUS the Drop Arm Test. The Drop Arm Test specifically tests whether the patient can actively control the lowered arm (requires intact supraspinatus function); inability = significant full-thickness tear. Neer's sign and Hawkins test assess for IMPINGEMENT. The distinction matters: impingement can occur with an intact tendon, while a positive Drop Arm suggests the tendon has lost functional integrity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Drop Arm = full-thickness tear (supraspinatus). Neer = impingement (supraspinatus against anterior acromion). Hawkins = impingement (supraspinatus against coracoacromial ligament). Empty Can test = supraspinatus strength. These physical exam tests are classic Step 1 discriminators. COMLEX: All of these tests are tested in COMLEX clinical skills and are part of the osteopathic shoulder structural examination.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Little league shoulder: pediatric rotator cuff overuse injury sharing the same subacromial anatomy as adult impingement"},{"id":"sdl13_q10","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes presents with 6 months of gradually worsening right shoulder stiffness. She initially had pain that has now lessened; she has difficulty reaching overhead, behind her back, and across her body equally. Strength is 5/5 throughout. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Glenohumeral osteoarthritis, producing directional loss of motion specifically worst in abduction and external rotation with anterior joint line tenderness, morning stiffness under 30 minutes, and crepitus in a woman over age 45","B":"Rotator cuff tendinopathy with impingement, presenting with overhead pain, subacromial tenderness, a positive Neer or Hawkins sign, and pain referring to the lateral deltoid insertion on the humerus during resisted abduction testing","C":"Adhesive capsulitis (frozen shoulder), presenting with global loss of motion in multiple planes without significant strength loss or pain on resistance testing, in a patient at peak age (mid-50s) with a well-established risk factor (type 2 diabetes)","D":"Acromioclavicular osteoarthritis with secondary bursitis, producing superior shoulder stiffness and multiple planes of motion restriction due to AC joint osteophyte formation causing mechanical compression of the subacromial space","E":"Cervical radiculopathy at C5-C6, producing referred shoulder stiffness and apparent global motion loss due to chronic pain inhibition of the rotator cuff musculature and secondary periarticular contracture in a patient with diabetic neuropathy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Adhesive capsulitis hallmarks: global loss of motion in ALL planes (not directional), no other structural explanation, gradual onset, pain then stiffness pattern (pain often lessens as stiffness progresses), preserved strength, peak mid-50s, and strong association with diabetes (most important metabolic risk factor), thyroid disease, hyperlipidemia, stroke, and autoimmune disease. Lifetime prevalence 2-5%; affects 8.2% of males and 10.1% of females (working age). GH OA would have directional loss (worst abduction/ER). Rotator cuff impingement preserves most motion. Imaging is NOT helpful for diagnosing frozen shoulder — it may identify other conditions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Frozen shoulder = diabetes association (strongest), bilateral in 6-17% of patients, global motion loss in all planes. Classic board presentation is a middle-aged diabetic woman with progressively restricted shoulder motion in all planes. COMLEX: Adhesive capsulitis management includes OMM to restore GH motion — glenohumeral articulation with high-velocity low-amplitude technique or muscle energy is a tested COMLEX treatment approach.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — Humerus fractures: prolonged immobilization post-fracture is a leading secondary cause of adhesive capsulitis"},{"id":"sdl13_q11","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following is a recognized SECONDARY cause of adhesive capsulitis (frozen shoulder)?","choices":{"A":"Repetitive overhead activity in a competitive swimmer, which produces progressive supraspinatus tendinosis and subacromial impingement that eventually causes circumferential capsular thickening and global motion restriction","B":"Glenohumeral osteoarthritis with severe cartilage erosion, which progresses to capsular contracture when osteophytes mechanically block abduction and external rotation until the joint develops secondary adhesive changes","C":"Acromioclavicular joint arthritis with superior osteophyte formation that bridges the AC joint space, causing progressive mechanical impingement of the rotator cuff that leads to secondary GH capsular fibrosis over years","D":"Prolonged immobilization following a humerus fracture or a post-operative shoulder procedure, during which capsular contracture and adhesion formation develop in the absence of adequate range of motion exercise and joint mobilization","E":"Subacromial bursitis with marked edema of the subdeltoid bursa compressing the glenohumeral capsule externally, causing secondary fibrosis and reduced joint volume indistinguishable from primary idiopathic frozen shoulder"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Secondary causes of adhesive capsulitis listed in this SDL: rotator cuff disease, humerus fracture, prolonged immobilization, and post-operative procedures. Immobilization prevents the normal joint motion that maintains capsular compliance, allowing contracture and adhesion formation. Idiopathic (primary) frozen shoulder is associated with diabetes, thyroid disease, hyperlipidemia, stroke, and autoimmune disease — these are NOT secondary causes. This distinction matters because prevention after humerus fracture or shoulder surgery requires aggressive early ROM, and failure to do so produces iatrogenic frozen shoulder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Secondary frozen shoulder triggers: rotator cuff disease, humerus fracture, prolonged immobilization, post-op. Primary triggers: diabetes (strongest), thyroid disease, hyperlipidemia. The SDL explicitly lists these categories. COMLEX: Post-surgical frozen shoulder is a preventable complication when OMM-guided early shoulder mobilization is incorporated into rehabilitation — an osteopathic perioperative care concept.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — Humerus fractures: prolonged immobilization post-fracture is a leading secondary cause of adhesive capsulitis"},{"id":"sdl13_q12","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the standard first-line treatment approach for adhesive capsulitis (frozen shoulder), and what is the escalation step if initial management fails?","choices":{"A":"Glenohumeral intra-articular corticosteroid injection (or oral glucocorticoids) combined with physical therapy to restore range of motion as soon as possible, escalating to manipulation under anesthesia if this approach fails to restore adequate ROM","B":"Subacromial corticosteroid injection combined with NSAIDs and activity modification, targeting the subacromial bursa to reduce impingement-related inflammation and allow passive stretching to gradually restore capsular compliance over weeks","C":"Immediate surgical arthroscopic capsular release as first-line treatment due to the irreversible fibrotic nature of adhesive capsulitis, with glenohumeral injection reserved only for patients who are not surgical candidates due to comorbidities","D":"Physical therapy stretching alone for 12 to 18 months without any corticosteroid injection, since evidence shows corticosteroids worsen long-term outcomes by masking pain and reducing the patient's motivation to perform home exercises","E":"Platelet-rich plasma injection into the glenohumeral joint combined with hyaluronic acid viscosupplementation, which outperforms corticosteroids for the capsular fibrosis of frozen shoulder in meta-analyses of randomized controlled trials"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Treatment of adhesive capsulitis from this SDL: (1) PARAMOUNT — restore ROM as soon as possible; (2) FIRST LINE — glenohumeral intra-articular corticosteroid injection OR oral glucocorticoids + physical therapy; (3) ESCALATION — manipulation under anesthesia if the above fails. The injection target is GLENOHUMERAL (intra-articular) because the pathology is capsular, not subacromial. Arthroscopic capsular release exists but is not first-line. The emphasis on early ROM reflects the pathophysiology: continued immobility worsens capsular fibrosis and adhesion formation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Frozen shoulder Rx = GH intra-articular injection + PT → manipulation under anesthesia if failure. NOT subacromial injection (that targets rotator cuff/bursa). NOT immediate surgery. The intra-articular versus subacromial distinction is a classic board discriminator. COMLEX: Manipulation under anesthesia has historical osteopathic roots — early OMM for shoulder contracture predates the formal anesthesia technique, making this a COMLEX-relevant historical and clinical concept.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — Humerus fractures: prolonged immobilization post-fracture is a leading secondary cause of adhesive capsulitis"},{"id":"sdl13_q13","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman presents to the emergency department after a motor vehicle collision. She has right shoulder pain. Examination reveals diffuse, poorly localized shoulder pain without focal joint line tenderness. Glenohumeral range of motion is symmetric to the left and does not reproduce her pain. She also reports mild shortness of breath and diaphoresis. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Perform supraspinatus strength testing in resisted abduction and external rotation followed by Neer and Hawkins impingement maneuvers to assess for a traumatic rotator cuff tear associated with the motor vehicle collision","B":"Order MRI of the right shoulder with contrast to evaluate for labral tear, glenoid rim fracture, or glenohumeral instability, given the history of high-energy trauma from the motor vehicle collision mechanism of injury","C":"Order plain radiographs of the right shoulder to assess subacromial space dimension and acromial morphology, since plain films are the first imaging modality for all traumatically injured shoulders in the emergency setting","D":"Evaluate for an extrinsic or referred cause of her shoulder complaint, given the combination of poorly localized pain, unaffected glenohumeral motion, symmetric shoulder appearance, dyspnea, and diaphoresis suggesting a non-structural visceral or referred etiology","E":"Perform glenohumeral joint aspiration to rule out post-traumatic hemarthrosis, since bloody effusion within the GH joint capsule is the most common cause of diffuse shoulder pain following a motor vehicle collision"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Despite the trauma history, this patient has multiple extrinsic pain markers: diffuse poorly localized pain, GH motion does NOT reproduce pain, symmetric shoulder appearance and motion, AND associated dyspnea and diaphoresis. These autonomic and respiratory symptoms alongside a trauma mechanism (MVC) raise concern for pneumothorax, cardiac contusion, aortic injury, or other thoracic pathology producing referred shoulder pain. The shoulder workflow directs evaluation for extrinsic cause when these features are present, regardless of trauma history. Proceeding directly to shoulder-specific imaging without considering the broader clinical picture would miss a potentially life-threatening cause.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diaphoresis + dyspnea + diffuse shoulder pain after MVC = rule out thoracic/cardiac cause first. Referred pain to the left shoulder (Kehr sign) from diaphragm irritation (spleen rupture) is a classic Step 1 scenario. COMLEX: Osteopathic physicians trained in viscerosomatic reflexes recognize that T1-T5 somatic dysfunction can represent referred visceral pain — thoracic inlet assessment is always part of the OMM shoulder evaluation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — Glenohumeral joint dislocations: presentation and management"},{"id":"sdl13_q14","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old male competitive swimmer presents with 8 months of left shoulder pain during freestyle strokes. He notes diffuse overhead pain initially, now more focal near the anterior shoulder close to the coracoid process. NSAIDs provide minimal relief. On exam, gentle passive glenohumeral motion is entirely painless. Tenderness is focal and anterior, localizing just lateral to the coracoid. No weakness. Per the shoulder pain diagnostic framework, this presentation localizes to which category?","choices":{"A":"Glenohumeral cause of shoulder pain, specifically a superior labral tear from anterior to posterior (SLAP lesion), with the focal coracoid-adjacent tenderness representing labral attachment irritation at the superior glenoid in this overhead athlete","B":"Glenohumeral cause of shoulder pain, specifically adhesive capsulitis, since 8 months of refractory shoulder pain in an overhead athlete frequently evolves into capsular fibrosis with pain localized to the anterior capsular attachment","C":"Extraglenohumereal cause of shoulder pain, specifically subscapular bursitis or biceps tendinopathy, given that focal tenderness localizes outside the GH joint capsule and gentle passive glenohumeral motion does not reproduce the pain","D":"Extrinsic cause of shoulder pain requiring cervical spine and brachial plexus evaluation, as chronic shoulder pain in swimmers frequently originates from cervical nerve root compression at C5-C6 producing referred anterior shoulder tenderness","E":"Glenohumeral cause of shoulder pain, specifically rotator cuff impingement, since supraspinatus compression in the subacromial space produces anterior shoulder pain in overhead athletes that can localize to the coracoid region with repetitive swimming"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The two criteria for extraglenohumereal cause: (1) focal tenderness OUTSIDE the GH joint, and (2) gentle passive GH motion does NOT cause pain. This swimmer has both. Extraglenohumereal differential near the coracoid includes subscapular bursitis and coracoid impingement. True GH causes (SLAP, adhesive capsulitis, rotator cuff impingement at the subacromial space) would typically reproduce pain with passive GH motion. SLAP lesions especially reproduce pain with compression testing (O'Brien test) involving GH motion. The absence of pain with passive GH motion is the decisive clinical feature.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Passive GH motion painless = extraglenohumereal. Passive GH motion painful = glenohumeral. This passive motion rule is the key clinical gate. Biceps tendinopathy (anterior tenderness along the bicipital groove) is the most commonly tested extraglenohumereal diagnosis in overhead athletes. COMLEX: Swimmers have high rates of shoulder somatic dysfunction (especially GH anterior and posterior restrictions) — OMM to restore motion can address contributing biomechanical factors.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — Glenohumeral joint dislocations: presentation and management"},{"id":"sdl13_q15","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old male recreational rock climber presents with 6 weeks of right shoulder pain after a bouldering fall. He reports diffuse anterior shoulder pain. On exam, the glenohumeral joint produces pain and restricted motion with passive testing. Bilateral shoulder comparison reveals asymmetric shoulder contour and motion. Radiographs show no fracture but confirm the humeral head is displaced anteriorly relative to the glenoid. Per the shoulder pain diagnostic algorithm, which pathway does this patient follow?","choices":{"A":"Trauma-negative pathway: assess whether the pain pattern suggests intrinsic versus extrinsic cause, then determine if examination findings are consistent with a glenohumeral versus extraglenohumereal etiology before ordering any imaging studies","B":"Trauma-positive pathway: obtain history of trauma, perform physical exam, obtain plain radiographs, and identify the specific structural finding — in this case, glenohumeral dislocation — then initiate appropriate management and refer to orthopedics as indicated","C":"Trauma-positive pathway: first determine whether the cause is glenohumeral or extraglenohumereal by assessing whether passive GH motion produces pain, then order MRI for soft tissue characterization before plain radiographs given the high-energy mechanism","D":"Trauma-negative intrinsic pathway: classify as a glenohumeral cause and order MRI with contrast to evaluate for labral pathology associated with the traumatic mechanism, reserving plain radiographs for elderly patients with concern for osteoporosis","E":"Extrinsic cause pathway: the diffuse nature of the pain and the history of trauma from a fall suggest referred visceral pain and a cardiac or pulmonary source should be excluded before any shoulder-specific imaging or intervention is initiated"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Trauma is present → trauma-positive pathway: history + exam + plain radiographs + identify specific pathology. This patient has glenohumeral dislocation (anterior displacement of humeral head, asymmetric contour, restricted motion). Per the workflow: workup and imaging reveal a specific diagnosis (fracture, GH dislocation, AC sprain, SCJ subluxation) → initiate appropriate management → refer to orthopedics as indicated. The workflow explicitly lists glenohumeral dislocation as a trauma-positive finding. The extraglenohumereal and passive motion assessment come AFTER the trauma gate routes to \"no\" (trauma-negative).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anterior GH dislocation (most common, 95%) presents with arm held in slight abduction and external rotation, humeral head palpable anteriorly. Axillary nerve injury (deltoid weakness, lateral arm numbness) is the classic associated nerve injury. COMLEX: Post-dislocation shoulder somatic dysfunction is a COMLEX scenario — glenohumeral restriction (especially external rotation) often persists after reduction and may respond to OMM.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — Glenohumeral joint dislocations: presentation and management"},{"id":"sdl13_q16","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman has a 5-year history of right anterior shoulder and upper arm pain progressively worsening with activity. Morning stiffness lasts 25 minutes. Exam shows anterior joint line tenderness, pain and reduced motion worst in abduction and external rotation, mild crepitus, and no weakness. She has a history of right glenohumeral dislocation at age 45. Which management approach is MOST consistent with the OARSI-adapted framework taught for this condition?","choices":{"A":"Immediate referral for total shoulder arthroplasty given her 5-year disease duration and prior dislocation history, since surgical joint replacement is indicated for glenohumeral OA once conservative management has exceeded 3 years","B":"Subacromial corticosteroid injection as the primary pharmacologic intervention, targeting the subacromial space and rotator cuff rather than the glenohumeral joint space, since reactive bursitis from cartilage degeneration causes most of the pain","C":"A stepwise approach beginning with pharmacologic management, physical therapy, and exercise, escalating to intra-articular corticosteroid injections, and reserving regenerative injections, acupuncture, and joint replacement for progressive or refractory cases","D":"Long-term daily oral glucocorticoid therapy as the pharmacologic anchor of GH OA management combined with passive physical therapy modalities, avoiding exercise due to concerns that loading an arthritic joint accelerates cartilage degradation","E":"Avoidance of physical therapy and exercise because evidence shows resistance training accelerates cartilage loss in glenohumeral osteoarthritis, making all weight-bearing and load-bearing shoulder activities contraindicated in elderly women"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The OARSI-adapted framework for shoulder OA management (from this SDL): pharmacologic management → physical therapy and exercise → intra-articular corticosteroid injections → regenerative injections (prolotherapy, PRP, stem cells) → acupuncture → joint replacement. It is escalating and stepwise. PT/exercise is a core component (NOT contraindicated). The injection for GH OA is INTRA-ARTICULAR (not subacromial — that targets rotator cuff/bursa). Daily oral glucocorticoids are NOT the anchor. Prior dislocation is a known risk factor for GH OA but does not accelerate the timing for surgery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH OA treatment is conservative-first: analgesics, PT, intra-articular injection, then surgery. The injection goes IN the joint (intra-articular), not above it (subacromial). This distinction is tested. COMLEX: Acupuncture is listed as a treatment option in the OARSI framework for OA — integrative medicine approaches for musculoskeletal OA are COMLEX-tested, connecting to SDL 12 content.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management in general"},{"id":"sdl13_q17","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 78-year-old retired construction worker has right shoulder pain localized to the superior aspect of the shoulder, worst when reaching across his body and lifting his arm forward. Exam reveals tenderness directly over the AC joint, pain with cross-body adduction testing, and reduced forward flexion. Morning stiffness lasts 20 minutes. Radiograph shows narrowing of the AC joint space. Which pairing of pain location, worst planes, and radiographic finding correctly identifies this diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Superior shoulder pain (AC joint location), worst with adduction and forward flexion (planes that mechanically load the AC joint), with AC joint space narrowing on radiograph — all consistent with acromioclavicular osteoarthritis","B":"Superior shoulder pain (AC joint location), worst with abduction and external rotation (planes that mechanically load the glenohumeral joint), with AC joint space narrowing on radiograph — consistent with GH osteoarthritis with secondary AC involvement causing superior pain","C":"Anterior shoulder and upper arm pain (GH location), worst with adduction and forward flexion (AC joint loaded planes), with AC joint space narrowing on radiograph — consistent with acromioclavicular OA producing referred anterior arm pain via capsular stretch","D":"Superior shoulder pain (AC joint location), worst with abduction and external rotation (GH loaded planes), and subacromial space narrowing on radiograph — consistent with rotator cuff tendinosis causing subacromial stenosis and secondary AC joint irritation","E":"Anterior shoulder pain (GH location), worst with adduction and forward flexion (AC joint loaded planes), with GH joint space narrowing on radiograph — consistent with GH OA with a reverse motion pattern due to bilateral joint involvement and capsular asymmetry"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The key teaching point: ACJ OA and GH OA differ in both PAIN LOCATION and WORST PLANES. ACJ OA: superior shoulder (over the AC joint), worst with ADDUCTION and FORWARD FLEXION (cross-body adduction test is the classic provocative maneuver). GH OA: anterior shoulder and upper arm, worst with ABDUCTION and EXTERNAL ROTATION. Both may show OA changes on radiograph (different joints). Both share clinical OA criteria (age ≥45, usage pain, morning stiffness ≤30 min). The cross-body adduction test is the ACJ-specific provocative test — it loads the AC joint by approximating the clavicle against the acromion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACJ OA mnemonic — \"ADduction Across the Dagger\" (cross-body): adduction loads the AC joint. GH OA — \"ABduction and External Rotation ABlaze\": abduction/ER loads the GH joint. COMLEX: ACJ somatic dysfunction (the most commonly dysfunctional joint in the shoulder complex per osteopathic literature) produces superior shoulder pain with AC joint tenderness — same location as ACJ OA, requiring differentiation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management in general"},{"id":"sdl13_q18","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with a known massive rotator cuff tear managed conservatively for 4 years presents with new progressive anterior shoulder pain and loss of motion. Radiographs now show superior migration of the humeral head toward the acromion. Morning stiffness lasts 25 minutes. Exam: anterior joint line tenderness, pain and loss of abduction and external rotation, crepitus. Strength testing shows weakness. Which complication best explains his new presentation?","choices":{"A":"Progressive extension of the rotator cuff tear into the subscapularis tendon, producing new internal rotation weakness and anterior shoulder pain at the lesser tuberosity rather than anterior joint line tenderness indicating cartilage involvement","B":"Development of isolated subacromial bursitis due to mechanical impingement by the superiorly migrated humeral head, producing anterior and lateral shoulder pain that is distinct from the progressive articular cartilage degeneration of OA","C":"Glenohumeral instability secondary to the massive rotator cuff tear, with the humeral head subluxing anteriorly during abduction attempts and causing anterior joint pain that mimics OA without true articular cartilage degeneration","D":"Glenohumeral osteoarthritis secondary to the massive rotator cuff tear, as severe rotator cuff tears are a recognized risk factor for GH OA through superior humeral migration and resulting cartilage damage — a condition known as rotator cuff tear arthropathy","E":"Adhesive capsulitis triggered by pain-mediated disuse of the shoulder, producing secondary capsular contracture with global motion loss in all planes in a patient whose rotator cuff deficiency impairs normal glenohumeral mechanics"},"correct":"D","explanation":"From this SDL: GH OA risk factors include direct trauma, prior dislocation, prior fracture, AND severe (massive) rotator cuff tears. Massive cuff tears permit superior migration of the humeral head (visible as \"high-riding humerus\" on radiograph), which shears articular cartilage and produces GH OA — called cuff tear arthropathy. Clinical features: anterior joint line tenderness, directional loss (worst abduction/ER), morning stiffness ≤30 min, crepitus, and in advanced cases weakness from combined articular and tendon dysfunction. Adhesive capsulitis would show GLOBAL, not directional, loss of motion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Massive rotator cuff tear → superior humeral migration (\"high-riding humerus\" on X-ray) → cuff tear arthropathy (secondary GH OA). This is a tested progression. The subacromial space becomes so narrow it is obliterated. COMLEX: Rotator cuff tear arthropathy is managed with shoulder arthroplasty (reverse total shoulder arthroplasty) in surgical candidates — a COMLEX management escalation point.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management in general"},{"id":"sdl13_q19","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder pathologies, including rotator cuff injuries, bursitis, and AC joint arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old female physical therapist has 14 months of right shoulder pain worst with overhead activity and when sleeping on the right side. Pain refers to the lateral deltoid region. Abduction is limited to 110 degrees. Neer and Hawkins tests are both positive. Drop Arm Test is negative. MRI shows supraspinatus edema without tear. Which mechanism BEST explains her impingement?","choices":{"A":"The supraspinatus has undergone full-thickness articular-sided tearing, allowing the humeral head to migrate superiorly and mechanically narrow the subacromial space from the humeral side, consistent with the thinning mechanism of impingement","B":"The subdeltoid bursa has ruptured into the glenohumeral joint capsule, creating a reactive synovitis that directly compresses the supraspinatus tendon at the musculotendinous junction from within the intra-articular compartment","C":"The supraspinatus has calcified from calcium hydroxyapatite deposition in the critical zone, creating a fixed mechanical block in the subacromial space that prevents normal tendon gliding regardless of the inflammation state","D":"The supraspinatus has undergone chronic tendinosis with complete loss of inflammatory cells and collagen disorganization, causing tendon thinning and superior humeral migration that narrows the subacromial space from the humeral side as a separate process","E":"The supraspinatus tendon is acutely edematous and too large to glide smoothly through the subacromial space, representing the edematous-tendon mechanism of impingement rather than the degenerative thinning mechanism that allows superior humeral migration"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The SDL describes two impingement mechanisms: (1) Edematous supraspinatus — tendon becomes too large to glide through subacromial space → mechanical impingement from the tendon side; (2) Thin supraspinatus from chronic tendinosis — humerus shifts superiorly → space narrows from the humeral side. This patient has a POSITIVE DROP ARM test? No — the Drop Arm Test is NEGATIVE, ruling out full-thickness tear. MRI shows edema without tear. 14 months is chronic but patients cycle between states. The current state is edema-driven impingement (positive Neer/Hawkins, edema on MRI, no tear). The thinning/tendinosis mechanism would be the other part of the cycle.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Two mechanisms of subacromial impingement — edema (tendon too big) vs. thinning (humerus migrates up). Both produce positive impingement tests. MRI differentiates edema (no tear) from tendinosis/tear. Drop Arm negative = no significant full-thickness tear. COMLEX: Impingement cycle (edema → thinning → re-inflammation) is the pathophysiologic rationale for PT, injections, and avoiding cortisone repeatedly (degrades collagen in already-diseased tendon).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: pediatric rotator cuff and proximal humeral stress injury in the same subacromial anatomy"},{"id":"sdl13_q20","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder pathologies, including rotator cuff injuries, bursitis, and AC joint arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old male contractor has 3 weeks of acute right shoulder pain triggered by overhead work. Exam: positive Hawkins test, tenderness over the greater tuberosity, painful arc between 60 and 120 degrees of abduction. Radiograph shows superior humeral migration with subacromial space narrowing. Which imaging modality would BEST characterize the SOFT TISSUE pathology of his rotator cuff?","choices":{"A":"CT arthrogram of the right shoulder with intravenous contrast, which provides superior visualization of bony architecture and articular cartilage compared to MRI or ultrasound and is the preferred modality for subacromial space assessment","B":"Repeat plain radiographs of the right shoulder in three additional projections, since multiple-view radiography is the most sensitive modality for diagnosing partial-thickness rotator cuff tears and quantifying the degree of tendon involvement","C":"MRI or musculoskeletal ultrasound of the right shoulder, as both offer equivalent sensitivity for soft tissue pathology including rotator cuff tendinosis, partial-thickness tears, full-thickness tears, and subacromial bursitis","D":"Technetium bone scan of the right shoulder, which identifies areas of increased metabolic activity in the supraspinatus insertion and acromion corresponding to mechanical stress and is more sensitive than MRI for subacromial impingement","E":"Arthrographic fluoroscopy of the glenohumeral joint with contrast injection, which is the gold standard for detecting full-thickness rotator cuff tears by demonstrating contrast communication between the joint space and the subacromial bursa"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Per SDL 13: (1) Radiographs assess subacromial space dimension and acromion shape — good for bony architecture and space measurement. (2) MRI is sensitive and specific for soft tissue pathology, especially with contrast for cartilage and labral pathology; NOT as helpful for spacing/arthritis as radiographs. (3) Musculoskeletal ultrasound has EQUIVALENT sensitivity to MRI for soft tissue pathology. This equivalence is the high-yield teaching point — ultrasound is increasingly preferred in clinical practice for its availability, lower cost, and dynamic imaging capability. CT arthrogram and bone scans are not first-line.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MRI = gold standard for soft tissue shoulder pathology. MSK ultrasound = equivalent sensitivity. X-ray = bony architecture + subacromial space. Know when to use each. COMLEX: Musculoskeletal ultrasound is used by osteopathic sports medicine physicians for real-time guided injection procedures (subacromial, glenohumeral) — a COMLEX procedural competency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: pediatric rotator cuff and proximal humeral stress injury in the same subacromial anatomy"},{"id":"sdl13_q21","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder pathologies, including rotator cuff injuries, bursitis, and AC joint arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 60-year-old male has had rotator cuff tendinosis for 8 months. He has taken NSAIDs and completed 16 weeks of physical therapy without significant improvement. MRI shows a partial-thickness bursal-sided rotator cuff tear with intact articular surface. He continues to have significant pain with overhead activity. Which of the following represents the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":{"A":"Subacromial corticosteroid injection targeting the subacromial subdeltoid bursa to address the anti-inflammatory component of his tendinosis, representing the injection step in the rotator cuff treatment hierarchy after analgesics and physical therapy have failed","B":"Immediate referral for arthroscopic surgical repair, since any bursal-sided partial-thickness tear is a mandatory indication for surgical intervention after 8 months of conservative management regardless of tear thickness or percentage","C":"Platelet-rich plasma injection into the glenohumeral joint as the standard evidence-based second-line treatment after physical therapy failure, given its Level I evidence for partial-thickness rotator cuff tears in systematic reviews","D":"Glenohumeral intra-articular corticosteroid injection targeting the joint space rather than the subacromial space, since the bursal-sided tear involves the bursal compartment which communicates directly with the GH joint interior","E":"MRI arthrography with gadolinium contrast to reclassify the tear size and compartment involvement before any further intervention, since precise tear classification is required to determine the surgical versus conservative management threshold"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Rotator cuff treatment hierarchy: analgesics → physical therapy (mainstay) → injections (subacromial corticosteroid for anti-inflammatory, OR regenerative options: prolotherapy/PRP/stem cells) → surgery (recalcitrant cases, full-thickness tears, massive tears). After 8 months of analgesics + PT failure, injections are next. For bursal-sided pathology, SUBACROMIAL injection directly targets the subacromial-subdeltoid bursa and the tendon surface. Surgery (arthroscopic repair) is indicated for FULL-THICKNESS and MASSIVE tears, not routinely for partial-thickness. PRP is a regenerative (pro-inflammatory) option, not the standard first injection choice, and goes into the subacromial space not the GH joint. The bursa does NOT communicate with the GH joint in a partial tear.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Partial tear → conservative management including injection. Full or massive tear → surgery. The treatment escalation ladder is a classic board sequence. Bursal-sided tear = subacromial space injection. COMLEX: PRP and prolotherapy (pro-inflammatory, regenerative injections) are explicitly listed in this SDL as treatment options — COMLEX tests knowledge of regenerative injection indications.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: pediatric rotator cuff and proximal humeral stress injury in the same subacromial anatomy"},{"id":"sdl13_q22","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and hypothyroidism presents with 9 months of progressively worsening right shoulder stiffness. She initially had significant pain that has partially resolved; she now has difficulty with forward flexion, abduction, internal rotation, and external rotation equally. Strength is 5/5 throughout. Radiographs are unremarkable. Which mechanism best explains the underlying pathophysiology?","choices":{"A":"Progressive supraspinatus tendinosis with eccentric overload producing bursal-sided tendon thinning and superior humeral migration, creating mechanical restriction of glenohumeral abduction and external rotation in a patient with diabetes-impaired tendon healing","B":"Glenohumeral osteoarthritis accelerated by diabetes-related metabolic syndrome, with articular cartilage degradation producing directional loss of motion worst in abduction and external rotation and progressive joint space narrowing visible on plain radiography","C":"Massive rotator cuff tear arthropathy with superior humeral migration, producing weakness on manual testing and combined articular and tendon dysfunction in a patient whose hypothyroidism predisposes to tendon collagen fragility and multidirectional tear extension","D":"Cervical spondylosis at C5-C6 producing referred shoulder stiffness with pain inhibition of the rotator cuff musculature and sympathetically mediated capsular contracture in a patient with diabetic autonomic neuropathy affecting proprioceptive feedback","E":"Idiopathic adhesive capsulitis (frozen shoulder) of poorly understood pathology, producing global loss of motion in all planes with no structural radiographic explanation, and strongly associated with diabetes mellitus and thyroid disease as metabolic risk factors"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Classic adhesive capsulitis: global loss of motion in ALL planes (forward flexion + abduction + IR + ER — this multiplanar global pattern is the key distinguishing feature), no structural radiographic explanation (radiographs NOT helpful for diagnosis), preserved strength, pain-then-stiffness pattern (pain may lessen as stiffness progresses), peak mid-50s. Strong associations: diabetes (strongest), thyroid disease, hyperlipidemia, stroke, autoimmune disease. Pathology is poorly understood (idiopathic). GH OA would show directional loss (worst abduction/ER) and likely radiographic changes. Rotator cuff tear arthropathy would show weakness and radiographic superior migration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Frozen shoulder = global motion loss in all planes + diabetes association + unremarkable imaging + peak mid-50s. Bilateral in 6-17%. Preserved strength distinguishes it from massive rotator cuff tears. COMLEX: Osteopathic physicians use glenohumeral articulation and muscle energy techniques to restore joint motion in adhesive capsulitis — OMM is a non-pharmacologic intervention that directly addresses the ROM loss.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — Humerus fractures: epidemiology, mechanism, presentation, imaging, and management"},{"id":"sdl13_q23","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old man underwent open rotator cuff repair 4 months ago and was immobilized in a sling for 8 weeks per surgical protocol. He now presents with severe restriction of right shoulder motion in all planes equally. His surgeon confirms there are no hardware complications, no re-tear on ultrasound, and no incisional scar tissue limiting motion. What is the most likely diagnosis and most important treatment principle?","choices":{"A":"Rotator cuff re-tear at the surgical repair site causing protective guarding and apparent global motion limitation, requiring repeat MRI before initiating any physical therapy to confirm tendon integrity before applying passive mobilization forces","B":"Secondary adhesive capsulitis from prolonged post-operative immobilization, for which the paramount treatment principle is restoring range of motion as soon as possible through glenohumeral intra-articular corticosteroid injection combined with physical therapy","C":"Post-operative hemarthrosis of the glenohumeral joint causing secondary capsular fibrosis from blood-derived fibrin deposition, requiring ultrasound-guided joint aspiration to remove residual hemarthrosis before physical therapy can be effective","D":"Complex regional pain syndrome type II developing after open shoulder surgery, producing diffuse allodynia, autonomic changes, and apparent motion limitation that requires a multidisciplinary pain management approach rather than joint mobilization","E":"Normal expected post-operative stiffness after open rotator cuff repair, which is expected to persist for 3 to 6 months after surgery and requires only reassurance, analgesics, and continuation of the sling for another 4 weeks before exercises begin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Prolonged post-operative immobilization is an explicit secondary cause of adhesive capsulitis in this SDL. The hallmark remains global loss of motion in all planes with no other structural explanation. The cardinal treatment principle from this SDL is: \"It is paramount to restore ROM as soon as possible.\" First-line treatment: glenohumeral intra-articular corticosteroid injection (or oral glucocorticoids) + PT. If this fails: manipulation under anesthesia. This case illustrates an iatrogenic frozen shoulder from excessive immobilization after rotator cuff repair — early ROM protocols after shoulder surgery exist specifically to prevent this complication.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Post-op shoulder immobilization → frozen shoulder is a preventable surgical complication. Early ROM exercises after rotator cuff repair are the prevention strategy. Treatment is GH injection (intra-articular, NOT subacromial) + PT → manipulation under anesthesia if refractory. COMLEX: Osteopathic perioperative rehabilitation incorporates early OMM-guided joint mobilization to prevent post-surgical adhesive capsulitis, a COMLEX-relevant complication prevention concept.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — Humerus fractures: epidemiology, mechanism, presentation, imaging, and management"},{"id":"sdl13_q24","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman has 11 months of left shoulder stiffness. Her primary care physician treated her with NSAIDs and a series of subacromial corticosteroid injections without improvement. Current exam shows globally restricted shoulder motion: forward flexion 80 degrees, external rotation 10 degrees, internal rotation to L4. Strength is intact. Which statement best identifies the error in prior management and the correct next step?","choices":{"A":"The subacromial injection series was underdosed — the correct next step is a higher-concentration subacromial triamcinolone injection (80 mg rather than 40 mg) targeting the same subacromial compartment with prolonged post-injection physical therapy","B":"Referral for total shoulder arthroplasty is indicated since she has failed one course of subacromial corticosteroid therapy, because capsular fibrosis in frozen shoulder is irreversible without surgical joint replacement in patients with 11 months of stiffness","C":"Long-term oral NSAID therapy combined with supervised home stretching is the correct next step, since this approach avoids the systemic effects of glucocorticoids and the procedural risks of manipulation under anesthesia in a middle-aged woman","D":"The subacromial injections were incorrectly targeted for a capsular (intra-articular) condition — the correct treatment is a glenohumeral intra-articular corticosteroid injection combined with physical therapy, escalating to manipulation under anesthesia if this approach fails","E":"Initiation of biologic therapy with an interleukin-6 receptor antagonist targeting the fibroinflammatory capsular process, which has Level I evidence supporting its use in refractory idiopathic adhesive capsulitis before surgical intervention is attempted"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The critical teaching point: adhesive capsulitis is a CAPSULAR (intra-articular) condition — the contracted joint capsule is the pathological structure. The correct injection target is the GLENOHUMERAL joint space (intra-articular corticosteroid or oral glucocorticoids). Subacromial injection targets the subacromial bursa and rotator cuff — an entirely different compartment for a different pathology (rotator cuff impingement/tendinosis). The patient has never received the correct treatment. The correct sequence: GH intra-articular injection + PT → manipulation under anesthesia if failure. Total shoulder arthroplasty is for GH OA or cuff tear arthropathy, not adhesive capsulitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Frozen shoulder injection = INTRA-ARTICULAR (GH joint). Rotator cuff injection = SUBACROMIAL. This is a high-yield board discriminator — wrong injection site is a common clinical error. COMLEX: OMM is an alternative to corticosteroid injection for early adhesive capsulitis — glenohumeral muscle energy and articulation techniques directly address the capsular restriction and are a COMLEX-preferred non-pharmacologic intervention.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — Humerus fractures: epidemiology, mechanism, presentation, imaging, and management"}]},{"sdlNumber":14,"title":"Elbow, Wrist & Hand Pathologies (Obj 1-6)","questions":[{"id":"sdl14_q01","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lateral epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which muscle-tendon unit is most commonly injured in lateral epicondylitis?","choices":{"A":"Extensor carpi ulnaris at its origin on the posteromedial epicondyle of the humerus","B":"Flexor carpi radialis at its origin on the medial epicondyle of the humerus","C":"Common flexor tendon at its origin on the medial epicondyle of the humerus","D":"Extensor pollicis longus at its origin on the radial shaft distal to the elbow","E":"Extensor carpi radialis brevis at its origin on the lateral epicondyle of the humerus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lateral epicondylitis involves the extensor carpi radialis brevis (ECRB), which originates at the lateral epicondyle and is the most commonly affected tendon due to its eccentric loading during wrist extension and forearm activities. The extensor carpi ulnaris originates on the lateral epicondyle as well but is less commonly the primary lesion. The flexor carpi radialis and common flexor tendon originate at the medial epicondyle, placing them in the territory of medial epicondylitis. The extensor pollicis longus arises from the ulnar shaft in the third dorsal compartment, not the epicondyle.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FA 2024 tests lateral epicondylitis as \"tennis elbow\" with ECRB as the most commonly injured tendon at the lateral epicondyle; expect a vignette with a tennis player or office worker with lateral elbow pain on wrist extension. COMLEX: Counterforce brace placement and the eccentric loading mechanism map directly to COMLEX CP8 musculoskeletal presentations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: pediatric overuse of the common extensor origin parallels adult lateral epicondylitis pathomechanics"},{"id":"sdl14_q02","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lateral epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which term correctly describes the pathological state of lateral epicondylitis that has become a chronic, degenerative condition lasting more than 6 weeks?","choices":{"A":"Epicondylitis, characterized by acute neutrophilic infiltrate and classical inflammatory mediators within the ECRB tendon","B":"Epicondylosis, characterized by degenerative angiofibroblastic changes and disorganized collagen without acute inflammatory cells","C":"Epicondyloma, characterized by fibroblastic hyperplasia with lymphocytic infiltration at the tendon-bone junction","D":"Enthesopathy, characterized by calcific deposits forming at the tendon-bone interface from chronic calcium influx","E":"Bursitis, characterized by inflammatory fluid accumulation within the lateral epicondylar bursa sac"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chronic lateral epicondylitis is more accurately termed epicondylosis (tendinosis), in which the dominant pathology is angiofibroblastic hyperplasia with disorganized collagen and neovascularization rather than classical acute inflammation. Epicondylitis (tendinitis) correctly applies only to the acute phase with neutrophilic inflammatory infiltrate. Epicondyloma and enthesopathy are not standard diagnostic categories for this condition. Bursitis implies a distinct bursal structure, which is not the primary pathology in lateral epicondylitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The -itis vs -osis distinction is a high-yield conceptual pair for all tendon overuse injuries; FA 2024 notes that \"tendinosis\" involves degeneration rather than inflammation, which changes treatment from anti-inflammatory to regenerative approaches. COMLEX: Treatment algorithm differences between acute and chronic presentations (corticosteroids for acute, PRP/OT for chronic) align with COMLEX clinical management questions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: pediatric overuse of the common extensor origin parallels adult lateral epicondylitis pathomechanics"},{"id":"sdl14_q03","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lateral epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient with confirmed acute lateral epicondylitis fails a 3-week course of NSAIDs and rest. Which non-surgical intervention is most appropriate as the next step?","choices":{"A":"Surgical debridement and open release of the ECRB tendon at the lateral epicondyle given NSAID failure","B":"Platelet-rich plasma injection into the ECRB tendon, which is first-line only in the chronic tendinosis phase","C":"Corticosteroid injection at the lateral epicondyle combined with occupational therapy and a counterforce brace for the forearm","D":"Extracorporeal shock wave therapy applied weekly, which is reserved for refractory chronic tendinosis","E":"Nitroglycerin patch applied daily over the lateral epicondyle, which is indicated only for chronic tendinosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After NSAID failure in acute lateral epicondylitis, corticosteroid injection combined with occupational therapy and a counterforce brace represents the appropriate next non-surgical step. Surgical debridement is reserved for cases failing prolonged conservative management, typically after 6-12 months. Platelet-rich plasma, nitroglycerin patches, and extracorporeal shock wave therapy are interventions for the chronic tendinosis phase, not the acute phase following NSAID failure. The counterforce brace (unloader strap) reduces the force transmitted through the ECRB origin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lateral epicondylitis treatment follows an escalating stepwise algorithm; boards test recognition of when to escalate from NSAIDs to injection versus when surgery is appropriate. COMLEX: The distinction between acute and chronic treatment is explicitly tested, with PRP representing the regenerative approach to the degenerative chronic phase.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: pediatric overuse of the common extensor origin parallels adult lateral epicondylitis pathomechanics"},{"id":"sdl14_q04","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which tendon is primarily injured in medial epicondylitis, and where does it originate?","choices":{"A":"Extensor carpi radialis brevis originating at the lateral epicondyle of the humerus during backhand motions","B":"Common extensor tendon originating at the lateral epicondyle and stressed by repetitive wrist extension","C":"Biceps brachii tendon at its distal insertion on the radial tuberosity from repetitive forearm supination","D":"Triceps brachii tendon at its olecranon insertion stressed by repetitive elbow extension overhead","E":"Common flexor tendon originating at the medial epicondyle of the humerus, stressing the flexor-pronator mass"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Medial epicondylitis, known as \"golfer's elbow,\" involves the common flexor tendon at its origin on the medial epicondyle of the humerus. The flexor-pronator mass includes the flexor carpi radialis, pronator teres, flexor carpi ulnaris, and palmaris longus. The ECRB at the lateral epicondyle is the target in lateral epicondylitis. The biceps tendon inserts on the radial tuberosity and is unrelated to epicondylitis. The triceps olecranon insertion is associated with olecranon bursitis or triceps tendinopathy, not medial epicondylitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Medial vs lateral epicondylitis is a classic boards distinction; \"golfer's elbow\" = medial/common flexor, \"tennis elbow\" = lateral/ECRB. FA 2024 tests both as clinical vignettes with the sport or activity as the contextual clue. COMLEX: The proximity of the ulnar nerve to the medial epicondyle is a testable complication, especially in overhead athletes with medial elbow symptoms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the acute tendinitis to chronic tendinosis overuse spectrum applies equally to medial epicondyle flexor tendon pathology"},{"id":"sdl14_q05","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which movement pattern most reliably provokes pain in medial epicondylitis on physical examination?","choices":{"A":"Resisted wrist extension with the elbow in extension and the forearm in supination stressing the ECRB tendon","B":"Resisted wrist flexion with the elbow in extension and the forearm in supination stressing the common flexor origin","C":"Passive elbow flexion beyond 90 degrees with the wrist in neutral and forearm supinated maximally","D":"Active shoulder internal rotation against resistance with the elbow extended at the patient's side","E":"Maximum elbow flexion held for 60 seconds to reproduce ulnar nerve paresthesias at the cubital tunnel"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Resisted wrist flexion with the elbow extended and forearm supinated directly loads the common flexor tendon at the medial epicondyle, reproducing pain in medial epicondylitis. Resisted wrist extension loads the ECRB and provokes lateral epicondylitis (Cozen's test). Passive elbow flexion is used in the maximum elbow flexion test for cubital tunnel syndrome. Active shoulder internal rotation against resistance tests the rotator cuff. Maximum elbow flexion for 60 seconds evaluates the ulnar nerve, not the common flexor tendon.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The examination finding of medial elbow pain with resisted wrist flexion versus resisted wrist extension reliably separates medial from lateral epicondylitis on boards. COMLEX: The clinical presentation of medial elbow pain in a throwing or golf athlete is a prototypical COMLEX CP8 musculoskeletal vignette.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the acute tendinitis to chronic tendinosis overuse spectrum applies equally to medial epicondyle flexor tendon pathology"},{"id":"sdl14_q06","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pitcher with medial epicondylitis reports new ring and small finger tingling during overhead throwing. Which anatomical structure is most likely responsible for this additional symptom?","choices":{"A":"Radial nerve, which passes anterior to the lateral epicondyle and is stretched during elbow valgus stress","B":"Median nerve, which traverses the cubital fossa anterior to the medial epicondyle during overhead motion","C":"Posterior interosseous nerve, a radial nerve branch passing through the radial tunnel at the elbow","D":"Ulnar nerve, which courses posterior to the medial epicondyle and is vulnerable to traction and compression in overhead athletes","E":"Musculocutaneous nerve, which supplies the lateral forearm and is stretched during overhead abduction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The ulnar nerve runs in a groove posterior to the medial epicondyle within the cubital tunnel. In overhead athletes with medial epicondylitis, the valgus stress and repetitive elbow flexion can compress or traction the ulnar nerve, producing ring and small finger paresthesias in addition to medial elbow pain. The radial nerve is lateral and not associated with medial elbow pathology. The median nerve does not course posterior to the medial epicondyle. The posterior interosseous and musculocutaneous nerves serve different anatomical regions not involving ring and small finger sensation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ulnar nerve vulnerability at the medial epicondyle is high-yield; a vignette combining medial elbow pain with ring/small finger tingling in a throwing athlete points to concomitant cubital tunnel involvement. COMLEX: The combination of medial epicondylitis and ulnar nerve compression at the same anatomical region is a classic COMLEX multiple-pathology clinical scenario.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the acute tendinitis to chronic tendinosis overuse spectrum applies equally to medial epicondyle flexor tendon pathology"},{"id":"sdl14_q07","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which provocative test is most specific for reproducing symptoms in cubital tunnel syndrome during the physical examination?","choices":{"A":"Phalen's test, in which both wrists are held in full flexion at 90 degrees for 60 seconds to compress the carpal tunnel","B":"Maximum elbow flexion test, in which the patient holds the elbow in full flexion with wrist extension for 60 seconds","C":"Reverse Phalen's test, in which both wrists are held in full extension at 90 degrees for 60 seconds","D":"Cozen's test, in which the examiner resists wrist extension while the elbow is held in full extension","E":"Finkelstein maneuver, in which the patient grasps the thumb in the fist and the examiner ulnar deviates"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The maximum elbow flexion test, with the elbow in full flexion and wrist in extension held for 60 seconds, stretches and compresses the ulnar nerve at the cubital tunnel and is the most specific provocative test for cubital tunnel syndrome. Phalen's test evaluates the carpal tunnel for median nerve compression. Reverse Phalen's also assesses the carpal tunnel. Cozen's test loads the ECRB and is used for lateral epicondylitis. The Finkelstein maneuver specifically evaluates the first dorsal compartment tendons in De Quervain's tenosynovitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cubital tunnel syndrome is tested as the 2nd most common upper extremity nerve entrapment; knowing the maximum elbow flexion test (analogous to Phalen's for CTS) is key for the exam. COMLEX: Tinel's sign at the posteromedial elbow and the maximum flexion test are both expected on COMLEX clinical examination questions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 7 — Meralgia paresthetica: a lower extremity peripheral nerve entrapment sharing conservative management principles with cubital tunnel syndrome"},{"id":"sdl14_q08","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient with cubital tunnel syndrome has weakness of grip and an inability to separate the fingers against resistance. Which finding correctly identifies the motor deficit?","choices":{"A":"Weakness of the ulnar nerve-innervated intrinsic hand muscles, including the interossei and hypothenar group, causing loss of abduction and adduction","B":"Weakness of the thenar muscles innervated by the median nerve, producing loss of thumb opposition and palmar abduction","C":"Weakness of the extensor digitorum communis innervated by the radial nerve at the posterior interosseous branch","D":"Weakness of the flexor carpi radialis innervated by the median nerve proximal to the wrist at the forearm","E":"Weakness of the brachioradialis innervated by the radial nerve in the lateral compartment of the arm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The ulnar nerve innervates the intrinsic hand muscles including the interossei (which abduct and adduct the fingers), the hypothenar muscles (abductor digiti minimi, flexor digiti minimi, opponens digiti minimi), and the medial two lumbricals. Weakness of these structures produces loss of finger abduction and adduction, and contributes to claw hand deformity. The thenar muscles are median nerve territory. Extensor digitorum communis is radial nerve territory. Flexor carpi radialis and brachioradialis are median and radial nerve territories, respectively.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ulnar nerve motor deficit (interossei weakness, claw hand) versus median nerve deficit (thenar wasting, loss of thumb opposition) is a classic boards distinction. COMLEX: The claw hand deformity specifically affects the ring and small fingers due to loss of ulnar intrinsic muscles and is a named COMLEX musculoskeletal physical finding.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 7 — Meralgia paresthetica: a lower extremity peripheral nerve entrapment sharing conservative management principles with cubital tunnel syndrome"},{"id":"sdl14_q09","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which initial conservative management is most appropriate for a patient with mild cubital tunnel syndrome and no thenar wasting?","choices":{"A":"Immediate surgical ulnar nerve transposition to move the nerve anterior regardless of severity or conservative trial","B":"Immobilization in a full arm cast at 90 degrees of elbow flexion for 6 weeks to reduce nerve traction","C":"Activity modification to avoid prolonged elbow flexion combined with a padded elbow brace to limit compression at the cubital tunnel","D":"Immediate NCS/EMG testing followed by percutaneous cubital tunnel release regardless of symptom severity","E":"Corticosteroid injection directly into the cubital tunnel adjacent to the ulnar nerve at the medial elbow"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mild cubital tunnel syndrome without motor deficits is initially managed conservatively with activity modification to avoid prolonged elbow flexion, padding of the elbow, and bracing that limits full flexion. Surgical ulnar nerve transposition is reserved for cases failing conservative management or with significant motor deficits. A full arm cast in flexion would worsen cubital tunnel compression, not relieve it. Immediate surgery before a conservative trial is not indicated for mild cases. Corticosteroid injection directly into the cubital tunnel risks nerve injury and is not first-line.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The boards test the step-wise approach to cubital tunnel syndrome: conservative first (activity mod, brace), then electrodiagnostics if uncertain, then surgery for failures or motor deficits. COMLEX: Padding and ergonomic modification align with COMLEX's emphasis on functional and non-pharmacological interventions before procedural management.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 7 — Meralgia paresthetica: a lower extremity peripheral nerve entrapment sharing conservative management principles with cubital tunnel syndrome"},{"id":"sdl14_q10","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of carpal tunnel syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"In carpal tunnel syndrome, which nerve is compressed, and through which anatomical structure does the compression occur?","choices":{"A":"Ulnar nerve, compressed between the pisiform and hook of hamate within Guyon's canal at the volar wrist","B":"Radial nerve, compressed beneath the first dorsal compartment retinaculum at the radial styloid","C":"Median nerve, compressed between the two heads of pronator teres muscle in the proximal forearm","D":"Ulnar nerve, compressed under the flexor carpi ulnaris at the distal two-thirds of the forearm","E":"Median nerve, compressed under the transverse carpal ligament at the roof of the carpal canal of the wrist"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Carpal tunnel syndrome involves compression of the median nerve under the transverse carpal ligament, which forms the roof of the carpal canal at the wrist. Any condition that increases pressure within the canal can compress the median nerve. The ulnar nerve is compressed in Guyon's canal between the pisiform and hamate. Radial nerve compression under the first dorsal compartment retinaculum is not a recognized entrapment syndrome. Median nerve compression between pronator teres heads is pronator teres syndrome, a separate condition. Flexor carpi ulnaris compression of the ulnar nerve is a distinct entity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CTS is the highest-yield nerve entrapment syndrome on Step 1; FA 2024 emphasizes thenar eminence wasting (APB weakness) from chronic median nerve compression as the classic motor finding. COMLEX: CTS is a named COMLEX CP8 condition; wrist OMM and somatic dysfunction at C5-T1 contributing to median nerve tension are COMLEX-specific clinical angles.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: distal radius fractures acutely raise carpal canal pressure, precipitating acute carpal tunnel syndrome"},{"id":"sdl14_q11","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of carpal tunnel syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient with suspected carpal tunnel syndrome undergoes provocative testing. Which combination of physical examination tests is most appropriate to confirm the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Maximum elbow flexion test combined with Tinel's sign at the posteromedial elbow cubital tunnel","B":"Finkelstein maneuver combined with tenderness palpation over the first dorsal compartment radial styloid","C":"Cozen's test combined with passive wrist extension and resisted forearm supination at the elbow","D":"Phalen's sign combined with Tinel's sign at the volar wrist, both targeting the median nerve distribution","E":"Drop arm test combined with Neer's and Hawkins-Kennedy impingement signs at the shoulder"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Phalen's sign (wrist flexion at 90 degrees for 60 seconds reproducing median distribution paresthesias) and Tinel's sign at the volar wrist (percussion over the carpal tunnel) are the two classic provocative tests for CTS. The maximum elbow flexion test and cubital tunnel Tinel's are for cubital tunnel syndrome. The Finkelstein maneuver with first dorsal compartment tenderness evaluates De Quervain's tenosynovitis. Cozen's test and wrist extension/forearm supination combination targets lateral epicondylitis. The drop arm test and shoulder impingement signs evaluate rotator cuff and subacromial pathology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Phalen's test is more sensitive and Tinel's more specific for CTS; boards may ask which is more sensitive or appropriate as a first test. COMLEX: Both tests are expected clinical knowledge for COMLEX, with the additional consideration of NCS/EMG for confirmation when clinical findings are equivocal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: distal radius fractures acutely raise carpal canal pressure, precipitating acute carpal tunnel syndrome"},{"id":"sdl14_q12","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of carpal tunnel syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which demographic pattern is most consistent with the epidemiology of carpal tunnel syndrome in the general population?","choices":{"A":"Female-predominant, with an incidence approximately three times higher in women than in men across all age groups","B":"Male-predominant, with a 2:1 male-to-female ratio most pronounced in adults over 60 who perform heavy manual labor","C":"Equal sex distribution, with rates driven entirely by occupation regardless of biological sex","D":"Pediatric-predominant, with most cases arising in adolescents during periods of rapid skeletal growth","E":"Elderly-predominant, with first presentation occurring typically after age 70 in both males and females"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Carpal tunnel syndrome is 3 times more common in females than males, with female incidence reported at 2.2-5.4 per 1,000 persons per year versus 1.1-3 per 1,000 for males. CTS affects 1-5% of the general population and is not pediatric-predominant or elderly-specific in onset. While occupational exposure increases risk, sex remains a significant independent risk factor likely related to hormonal influences and anatomical differences in carpal canal dimensions. The condition is not equally distributed across sexes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CTS demographics are testable; women 3:1 over men, peak incidence in working-age adults. FA 2024 highlights pregnancy as a major risk factor due to edema, which connects CTS to the obstetrics section. COMLEX: Postpartum and pregnancy-related CTS are classically tested COMLEX clinical presentations connecting OB and musculoskeletal pathology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: distal radius fractures acutely raise carpal canal pressure, precipitating acute carpal tunnel syndrome"},{"id":"sdl14_q13","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the wrist.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which anatomical boundaries correctly define Guyon's canal, the site of ulnar nerve compression at the wrist?","choices":{"A":"Bounded by the flexor carpi radialis medially and the median nerve laterally at the proximal carpal row","B":"Bounded by the extensor carpi ulnaris dorsally and flexor carpi ulnaris volarly at the ulnar wrist","C":"Bounded by the pisiform medially, hook of hamate laterally, and the palmar carpal ligament forming the roof superiorly","D":"Bounded by the transverse carpal ligament roofing the carpal canal and the carpal bones at the floor","E":"Bounded by the first dorsal compartment laterally and the radial styloid medially at the radial wrist"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Guyon's canal is a fibro-osseous tunnel at the ulnar wrist bounded by the pisiform bone on the medial side, the hook of the hamate on the lateral side, and the palmar carpal ligament forming the roof. The ulnar nerve and artery pass through this tunnel into the hand. This is distinct from the carpal tunnel (transverse carpal ligament roof, carpal bones floor), the radial styloid region (De Quervain's), and the extensor compartments on the dorsum of the wrist.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyon's canal anatomy is tested in comparison to the carpal tunnel; knowing the pisiform and hamate as the lateral boundaries is key. COMLEX: Cyclists with hypothenar wasting from handlebar pressure on Guyon's canal is a classic COMLEX clinical vignette linking biomechanics to peripheral nerve entrapment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries: peripheral nerve entrapment syndromes share anatomical compression mechanisms and electrodiagnostic evaluation"},{"id":"sdl14_q14","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the wrist.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which occupational or recreational activity is classically associated with ulnar nerve entrapment at Guyon's canal?","choices":{"A":"Overhead throwing motions that repetitively stress the medial elbow flexor-pronator mass with valgus force","B":"Repetitive wrist extension activities such as prolonged keyboard typing producing carpal tunnel compression","C":"Postpartum infant care with repeated pinch-grip lifting creating first dorsal compartment friction stress","D":"Prolonged handlebar pressure during cycling that compresses the hypothenar eminence and ulnar nerve at Guyon's canal","E":"Backhand tennis strokes that repetitively load the ECRB at the lateral epicondyle with wrist extension"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cyclists applying prolonged pressure on handlebars directly compress the hypothenar eminence over Guyon's canal, making this the classic occupational/recreational association. The resulting ulnar neuropathy is sometimes called \"cyclist's palsy\" or \"handlebar palsy.\" Overhead throwing stresses the medial elbow and ulnar nerve at the cubital tunnel, not Guyon's canal. Keyboard typing is associated with carpal tunnel syndrome. Postpartum lifting is associated with De Quervain's tenosynovitis. Tennis backhand strokes cause lateral epicondylitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The cycling-Guyon's canal association is a classic boards vignette; the key distinguishing feature from cubital tunnel is preserved dorsal ulnar sensation since the dorsal ulnar cutaneous branch exits proximal to Guyon's canal. COMLEX: Ergonomic counseling and padded gloves as management represent COMLEX's non-pharmacological first-line approach.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries: peripheral nerve entrapment syndromes share anatomical compression mechanisms and electrodiagnostic evaluation"},{"id":"sdl14_q15","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the wrist.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient with Guyon's canal syndrome has normal sensation on the dorsal ulnar hand. How does this finding help distinguish Guyon's canal from cubital tunnel syndrome?","choices":{"A":"The dorsal ulnar cutaneous branch of the ulnar nerve exits proximal to Guyon's canal, so it is spared in wrist-level compression","B":"The dorsal ulnar cutaneous branch joins the median nerve proximally, so it is never affected by ulnar compression","C":"Guyon's canal compression always affects dorsal sensation because the branch enters the canal distal to the pisiform","D":"Cubital tunnel syndrome selectively spares the dorsal ulnar hand because it only involves the terminal palmar branch","E":"Loss of dorsal ulnar sensation is pathognomonic of Guyon's canal and cannot occur with cubital tunnel compression"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The dorsal ulnar cutaneous branch of the ulnar nerve branches off approximately 5-8 cm proximal to the wrist, well before Guyon's canal. This means that compression within Guyon's canal at the wrist will spare this branch, leaving dorsal ulnar hand sensation intact. In contrast, cubital tunnel syndrome at the elbow occurs proximal to this branch point, so dorsal ulnar hand sensation IS affected. The preserved dorsal ulnar sensation in a patient with hypothenar weakness and ring/small finger palmar paresthesias helps localize the lesion to the wrist rather than the elbow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The preserved dorsal ulnar cutaneous branch in Guyon's canal compression is the classic localizing feature that distinguishes it from cubital tunnel syndrome; FA 2024 tests this as a sensory distribution comparison. COMLEX: Localization of nerve lesions by sensory territory is a core COMLEX neurological examination skill applied to upper extremity entrapment syndromes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries: peripheral nerve entrapment syndromes share anatomical compression mechanisms and electrodiagnostic evaluation"},{"id":"sdl14_q16","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of De Quervain's tenosynovitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which tendons are specifically affected in De Quervain's tenosynovitis, and in which anatomical compartment do they reside?","choices":{"A":"Flexor carpi radialis and flexor pollicis longus within the carpal canal at the volar wrist crease","B":"Extensor pollicis longus and extensor indicis in the third dorsal compartment at Lister's tubercle","C":"Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis in the first dorsal compartment at the radial styloid","D":"Extensor carpi radialis longus and extensor carpi radialis brevis in the second dorsal compartment","E":"Flexor pollicis longus and abductor pollicis brevis within the carpal tunnel at the thenar eminence"},"correct":"C","explanation":"De Quervain's tenosynovitis affects the abductor pollicis longus (APL) and extensor pollicis brevis (EPB) tendons, which share the first dorsal compartment of the wrist at the level of the radial styloid. Repetitive or overuse activities cause friction-based inflammation within this fibro-osseous tunnel. The flexor carpi radialis and flexor pollicis longus are volar structures in the carpal canal. The third dorsal compartment at Lister's tubercle contains the extensor pollicis longus. The second dorsal compartment contains the ECR longus and brevis. Abductor pollicis brevis is a thenar intrinsic muscle, not a dorsal compartment structure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: De Quervain's is a classic FA 2024 vignette: APL and EPB in the first dorsal compartment, Finkelstein test, and postpartum women. COMLEX: The first dorsal compartment as a fibro-osseous tunnel fits COMLEX's anatomical entrapment framework; somatic dysfunction at the wrist affecting the radial styloid region is a COMLEX-specific management angle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction: both conditions involve a tendon entrapped within a fibro-osseous canal producing stenosing tenosynovitis"},{"id":"sdl14_q17","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of De Quervain's tenosynovitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which physical examination maneuver is diagnostic for De Quervain's tenosynovitis?","choices":{"A":"Phalen's test: the patient holds both wrists in full flexion at 90 degrees sustained for 60 seconds","B":"Finkelstein maneuver: the patient places the thumb in the palm and closes the fist, then the examiner ulnar-deviates the wrist","C":"Maximum elbow flexion test: the patient holds the elbow in full flexion for 60 seconds with wrist extended","D":"Cozen's test: the examiner resists wrist extension while the patient holds the elbow fully extended","E":"Tinel's sign: the examiner percusses over the volar wrist crease to reproduce thumb paresthesias"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Finkelstein maneuver is performed by having the patient place the thumb across the palm, flex the fingers over the thumb, and then the examiner passively ulnar-deviates the wrist. This stretches the APL and EPB tendons over the radial styloid and reproduces the patient's pain, confirming De Quervain's tenosynovitis. Phalen's test evaluates carpal tunnel syndrome. The maximum elbow flexion test evaluates cubital tunnel syndrome. Cozen's test evaluates lateral epicondylitis. Tinel's sign at the wrist evaluates for carpal tunnel syndrome, not De Quervain's.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The Finkelstein maneuver is high-yield for De Quervain's; FA 2024 tests the mechanics: thumb in palm, ulnar deviation reproduces radial wrist pain. COMLEX: Boards expect you to distinguish Finkelstein (De Quervain's) from Phalen's (CTS) from the maximum flexion test (cubital tunnel) as three different conditions with three different diagnostic maneuvers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction: both conditions involve a tendon entrapped within a fibro-osseous canal producing stenosing tenosynovitis"},{"id":"sdl14_q18","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of De Quervain's tenosynovitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which demographic group has the highest epidemiological risk for developing De Quervain's tenosynovitis?","choices":{"A":"Young male contact sport athletes aged 18 to 30 with high-velocity wrist flexion demands from sport","B":"Elderly men over age 65 performing heavy manual labor with repetitive gripping and lifting activities","C":"Adolescent females during growth spurts who have increased susceptibility to wrist tendon overuse","D":"Postmenopausal women over age 65 who develop concurrent wrist and hand osteoarthritis with synovitis","E":"Women aged 30 to 50, with a notably elevated risk specifically during the 4 to 6 weeks following childbirth"},"correct":"E","explanation":"De Quervain's tenosynovitis predominantly affects women aged 30-50, with a particularly high incidence in the 4-6 weeks postpartum period. The combination of hormonal changes, repetitive infant-lifting mechanics (pinch grip with wrist ulnar deviation), and breastfeeding-related wrist positions creates significant stress on the first dorsal compartment tendons. Young male athletes, elderly men, adolescent females, and postmenopausal women with OA are not the primary demographic for this condition, though any repetitive radial wrist deviation activity can trigger it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The postpartum woman with radial wrist pain and positive Finkelstein is a classic Step 1 clinical vignette connecting obstetrics to musculoskeletal pathology. COMLEX: COMLEX tests the management (thumb spica bracing, corticosteroid injection) and the postpartum timing as a clinical trigger for the condition.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction: both conditions involve a tendon entrapped within a fibro-osseous canal producing stenosing tenosynovitis"},{"id":"sdl14_q19","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lateral epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman and recreational tennis player has an 8-week history of right lateral elbow pain worsened by gripping and backhand strokes. No prior trauma or neurological symptoms. Exam: point tenderness at the lateral epicondyle, pain with resisted wrist extension. X-ray is normal. Which of the following most accurately describes the underlying pathological process?","choices":{"A":"Acute neutrophilic inflammatory infiltrate invading the ECRB tendon and surrounding periosteal tissue","B":"Angiofibroblastic hyperplasia with degeneration and disorganized collagen within the ECRB tendon at the lateral epicondyle","C":"Calcium hydroxyapatite crystal deposition within the common extensor tendon producing calcific tendinitis","D":"Fibrocartilaginous metaplasia of the common extensor tendon from repetitive valgus stress at the elbow","E":"Synovial hypertrophy with pannus formation eroding the articular cartilage of the radiocapitellar joint"},"correct":"B","explanation":"At 8 weeks, this presentation has transitioned from the acute tendinitis phase into chronic tendinosis, characterized by angiofibroblastic hyperplasia, disorganized collagen, and neovascularization rather than classic acute neutrophilic inflammation. This histopathological distinction has treatment implications: anti-inflammatory approaches are less effective in the chronic phase compared with regenerative or eccentric loading approaches. Calcific tendinitis involves hydroxyapatite deposits and presents distinctly on imaging. Fibrocartilaginous metaplasia and synovial pannus are not characteristic of lateral epicondylitis histopathology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The chronicity of lateral epicondylitis shifts histopathology from neutrophilic inflammation to angiofibroblastic degeneration; this distinction drives the treatment shift from NSAIDs to OT and regenerative injections. COMLEX: The chronic versus acute management algorithm is explicitly tested on COMLEX clinical management questions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: pediatric overuse rotator cuff and medial elbow apophyseal injury"},{"id":"sdl14_q20","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lateral epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old male electrician has 4 months of left lateral elbow pain. He completed 6 weeks of NSAIDs and activity modification without adequate improvement. No weakness or neurological deficits. Exam confirms lateral epicondylitis. Which intervention is most appropriate as the next step in management?","choices":{"A":"Occupational therapy with eccentric strengthening exercises and a counterforce forearm brace for chronic tendinosis","B":"Immediate surgical ECRB tendon debridement given failure of initial conservative pharmaceutical management","C":"Full elbow immobilization in a long arm cast for 6 weeks to allow ECRB tendon collagen remodeling","D":"Monthly corticosteroid injection repeated for 3 consecutive months regardless of initial injection response","E":"Extracorporeal shock wave therapy combined with ultrasound-guided open surgical release of the tendon"},"correct":"A","explanation":"After NSAIDs and activity modification fail, occupational therapy with eccentric strengthening and a counterforce brace is the appropriate next step for chronic lateral epicondylitis. Eccentric loading programs are strongly supported for chronic tendinosis by promoting controlled collagen remodeling. Surgical debridement is not indicated this early; it requires 6-12 months of failed conservative management. Full elbow casting is counterproductive and may promote stiffness. Repeated corticosteroid injections beyond 1-2 are not recommended as they may further weaken the tendon. ESWT is not combined with surgery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Boards test the stepwise treatment ladder for lateral epicondylitis; OT with eccentric loading is the key second-line intervention after NSAIDs in the chronic phase. COMLEX: The counterforce brace and occupational therapy are considered conservative non-pharmacological interventions preferred by COMLEX before escalating to injection or surgery.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: pediatric overuse rotator cuff and medial elbow apophyseal injury"},{"id":"sdl14_q21","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lateral epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old female chef has bilateral lateral elbow pain for 3 months, worse on the dominant right side. She reports pain with repetitive gripping and forearm activities during work. Exam shows point tenderness at the lateral epicondyle bilaterally, with a positive Cozen's test. X-ray is normal. Which of the following best explains why her occupation exposes her to this injury?","choices":{"A":"Repetitive wrist flexion generates excessive valgus stress at the medial epicondyle during food preparation","B":"Overhead lifting generates axial loading that compresses the radiocapitellar articular cartilage directly","C":"Repetitive eccentric loading of the ECRB tendon at the lateral epicondyle during gripping activities causes cumulative tendon microtrauma","D":"Sustained elbow flexion during preparation work compresses the ulnar nerve at the posteromedial elbow","E":"Repetitive forearm supination creates traction stress at the biceps tendon insertion on the radial tuberosity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Repetitive eccentric loading during gripping and forearm activities generates tensile stress at the ECRB origin on the lateral epicondyle, causing cumulative microtrauma that over time produces tendinosis. Chefs perform constant gripping, lifting, and cutting motions that repeatedly load this tendon eccentrically. Wrist flexion and medial epicondyle stress describes medial epicondylitis. Radiocapitellar compression suggests intra-articular pathology. Sustained elbow flexion and ulnar nerve compression describes cubital tunnel syndrome. Forearm supination and biceps traction describe a separate entity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Understanding the biomechanical mechanism of eccentric loading at the ECRB origin is fundamental to explaining lateral epicondylitis in any occupation, not just tennis. COMLEX: Ergonomic counseling about grip technique and tool modification is a COMLEX-favored management component for occupational lateral epicondylitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: pediatric overuse rotator cuff and medial elbow apophyseal injury"},{"id":"sdl14_q22","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old amateur golfer presents with a 6-week history of right medial elbow pain that worsens progressively during his round. No numbness or tingling. Exam shows tenderness at the medial epicondyle and pain with resisted wrist flexion and forearm pronation. Grip strength is mildly reduced. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Medial epicondylitis caused by overuse-related degeneration of the common flexor tendon at the medial epicondyle","B":"Lateral epicondylitis caused by eccentric overuse of the ECRB tendon at the lateral epicondyle","C":"Cubital tunnel syndrome caused by ulnar nerve compression at the posteromedial elbow during swinging","D":"UCL sprain caused by repetitive valgus stress on the ulnar collateral ligament of the medial elbow","E":"Pronator teres syndrome caused by median nerve compression between the two heads of pronator teres"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The combination of medial epicondyle tenderness, pain with resisted wrist flexion and forearm pronation, and a golf history establishes medial epicondylitis. The flexor-pronator mass generates high valgus stress during the golf swing, causing eccentric overload at the medial epicondyle origin. Lateral epicondylitis presents with lateral tenderness and pain on wrist extension, not flexion. Cubital tunnel syndrome would include ring/small finger paresthesias. UCL injury presents with valgus instability rather than resisted wrist flexion pain. Pronator teres syndrome includes median nerve distribution paresthesias in the forearm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"Golfer's elbow\" = medial epicondylitis = common flexor tendon at medial epicondyle; pain with resisted wrist flexion is the key differentiating exam finding from lateral epicondylitis. COMLEX: The medial elbow anatomy including ulnar nerve proximity is a testable COMLEX clinical pearl within the medial epicondylitis vignette.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q23","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old female tennis player with confirmed medial epicondylitis reports intermittent ring and small finger tingling that worsens during her serve. Neurological examination is equivocal. Which anatomical structure is most likely responsible for the sensory symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Median nerve, which courses anterior to the medial epicondyle and is stretched during elbow valgus loading","B":"Radial nerve, which passes through the radial tunnel lateral to the lateral epicondyle during forearm pronation","C":"Ulnar nerve, which courses posterior to the medial epicondyle and is susceptible to traction and compression during overhead serving","D":"Musculocutaneous nerve, which supplies the lateral forearm and is stretched by overhead shoulder abduction","E":"Posterior interosseous nerve, a radial nerve branch in the radial tunnel that is compressed during wrist extension"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The ulnar nerve runs in the groove posterior to the medial epicondyle within the cubital tunnel. During overhead throwing and serving motions, valgus stress and elbow flexion can traction and compress the ulnar nerve at this location, producing ring and small finger paresthesias. This combination of medial epicondylitis and ulnar neuritis is common in overhead athletes. The median nerve is anterior and does not course posterior to the medial epicondyle. The radial nerve and its branches are lateral structures. The musculocutaneous nerve supplies the lateral forearm, not the ring and small fingers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The medial elbow \"terrible triad\" in overhead athletes (medial epicondylitis, UCL stress, ulnar neuritis) is a boards-tested concept; ring and small finger symptoms in a thrower with medial elbow pain point to ulnar nerve involvement. COMLEX: The anatomical proximity of the ulnar nerve and medial epicondyle is a core COMLEX clinical anatomy teaching point.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q24","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old male pitcher with chronic medial elbow pain has pain with wrist flexion and forearm pronation but no pain with wrist extension. X-ray shows no fracture or valgus instability. Which of the following most reliably distinguishes medial from lateral epicondylitis on physical examination?","choices":{"A":"Pain with passive elbow flexion above 90 degrees distinguishes medial epicondylitis from lateral epicondylitis","B":"Tenderness directly over the olecranon process rather than the epicondyle is pathognomonic for medial pathology","C":"The presence of neurological symptoms is required to distinguish medial from lateral epicondylitis clinically","D":"Pain with resisted wrist flexion and forearm pronation is characteristic of medial epicondylitis, while pain with resisted wrist extension is specific to lateral epicondylitis","E":"Bilateral symptom involvement is characteristic of medial epicondylitis and absent in lateral epicondylitis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The key examination distinction is the movement that reproduces pain: resisted wrist flexion and forearm pronation provoke medial epicondylitis by loading the common flexor tendon at the medial epicondyle, while resisted wrist extension (Cozen's test) provokes lateral epicondylitis by loading the ECRB at the lateral epicondyle. Passive elbow flexion evaluates cubital tunnel syndrome. Olecranon tenderness suggests olecranon bursitis or triceps tendinopathy. Neurological symptoms may coexist but are not required for the distinction. Both conditions can be bilateral.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The resisted wrist flexion vs extension distinction is the single most important physical exam finding to separate medial from lateral epicondylitis on boards. COMLEX: Boards expect efficient clinical differentiation using one or two targeted examination maneuvers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q25","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old long-haul truck driver presents with 4 months of right ring and small finger numbness and tingling that is worst during long drives. He holds the wheel with his right elbow flexed for hours. Exam: positive Tinel's sign at the right posteromedial elbow, symptoms reproduced with maximum elbow flexion test, mildly reduced grip strength. Which of the following most likely explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Carpal tunnel syndrome from median nerve compression under the transverse carpal ligament at the wrist","B":"Lateral epicondylitis from ECRB overuse at the lateral epicondyle during steering wheel gripping","C":"De Quervain's tenosynovitis from APL and EPB tendon inflammation at the first dorsal compartment","D":"Guyon's canal syndrome from ulnar nerve compression at the wrist between the pisiform and hamate","E":"Cubital tunnel syndrome from ulnar nerve compression at the posteromedial elbow due to sustained elbow flexion during driving"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sustained elbow flexion while driving compresses and stretches the ulnar nerve within the cubital tunnel, producing classic cubital tunnel syndrome. Ring and small finger distribution paresthesias, positive Tinel's at the posteromedial elbow, and reproduction of symptoms with the maximum elbow flexion test all confirm the diagnosis. Carpal tunnel syndrome involves median nerve distribution (thumb, index, middle) and positive Phalen's/Tinel's at the wrist. Lateral epicondylitis and De Quervain's are tendon pathologies without neurological symptoms. Guyon's canal syndrome would be positive at the wrist, not the elbow, and preserves dorsal ulnar sensation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Prolonged elbow flexion as the mechanism for cubital tunnel is a classic board vignette; the driving truck driver or the reader who leans on the elbow are prototypical presentations. COMLEX: The Tinel's sign location (posteromedial elbow vs volar wrist) differentiates cubital tunnel from CTS and is a COMLEX examination must-know.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 7 — Meralgia paresthetica: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment"},{"id":"sdl14_q26","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 40-year-old male programmer develops cubital tunnel syndrome confirmed by NCS/EMG. After 3 months of activity modification, elbow padding, and NSAIDs, symptoms have not improved and NCS shows progressive axonal changes. Which surgical intervention is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Carpal tunnel release by dividing the transverse carpal ligament at the volar wrist to decompress the median nerve","B":"Lateral epicondyle ECRB debridement with tendon release to address the ECRB at the lateral epicondyle","C":"First dorsal compartment release by opening the fibro-osseous tunnel at the radial styloid of the wrist","D":"Ulnar nerve transposition to move the nerve to an anterior position at the elbow, relieving tension and compression","E":"Total elbow arthroplasty to decompress the cubital tunnel by replacing the elbow joint articular surfaces"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ulnar nerve transposition surgically moves the ulnar nerve from its posterior position behind the medial epicondyle to an anterior location (subcutaneous, intramuscular, or submuscular), shortening the path the nerve must travel and relieving tension and compression during elbow flexion. This is the appropriate surgical intervention for cubital tunnel syndrome failing conservative management with progressive NCS changes. Carpal tunnel release is for CTS, not cubital tunnel. ECRB debridement is for lateral epicondylitis. First dorsal compartment release is for De Quervain's. Total elbow arthroplasty is for end-stage arthritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ulnar nerve transposition is the definitive surgical procedure for refractory cubital tunnel syndrome; FA 2024 notes this in the context of ulnar nerve anatomy at the medial epicondyle. COMLEX: Boards expect knowledge of when conservative management has failed and surgery is warranted based on NCS evidence of axonal injury.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 7 — Meralgia paresthetica: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment"},{"id":"sdl14_q27","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman with cubital tunnel syndrome has a classic claw-hand deformity involving the ring and small fingers at rest, along with inability to spread her fingers apart. Which of the following mechanisms best explains the finger deformity?","choices":{"A":"Loss of ulnar nerve innervation to the interossei and ulnar lumbricals causes unopposed extension at the MCP joints of the ring and small fingers","B":"Loss of median nerve innervation to the flexor digitorum superficialis causes weakness of ring and small finger flexion","C":"Loss of radial nerve innervation to the extensor digitorum communis causes inability to extend the ring and small fingers","D":"Loss of musculocutaneous nerve innervation to the biceps causes loss of forearm flexion and supination strength","E":"Compression of the anterior interosseous nerve causes isolated weakness of the flexor pollicis longus and pronator quadratus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The claw-hand deformity in ulnar nerve palsy results from loss of the interossei and ulnar lumbricals, which normally flex the MCP joints and extend the IP joints. Without these muscles, the extrinsic extensors (radial nerve) produce unopposed MCP extension, while the extrinsic flexors (FDP) produce IP flexion, creating the claw posture. The ring and small fingers are most affected because the median-innervated lumbricals maintain normal posture in the index and middle fingers. Median nerve loss affects different muscles. Radial nerve loss produces wrist drop, not claw hand. Musculocutaneous and AIN losses produce entirely different deficits.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The intrinsic minus hand, claw hand, and the ulnar nerve innervation pattern (interossei, hypothenar, medial two lumbricals) is very high-yield; FA 2024 has a detailed nerve injury table for upper extremity entrapments. COMLEX: The functional consequences of ulnar nerve injury at the elbow are expected COMLEX clinical knowledge.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 7 — Meralgia paresthetica: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment"},{"id":"sdl14_q28","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of carpal tunnel syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman in her third trimester of pregnancy presents with bilateral hand numbness and tingling worse at night, predominantly in the thumb, index, and middle fingers. Phalen's test is positive bilaterally at 30 seconds. Which mechanism best explains why pregnancy predisposes to carpal tunnel syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Hormonal changes cause direct peripheral axonal degeneration of the median nerve throughout the carpal tunnel","B":"Uterine enlargement displaces the brachial plexus superiorly and compresses the median nerve proximally","C":"Elevated estrogen produces direct cytotoxic injury to the transverse carpal ligament and canal walls","D":"Cervical disc herniation at C6 compresses the root whose sensory distribution overlaps the median nerve","E":"Fluid retention-related edema increases carpal canal pressure, compressing the median nerve under the transverse carpal ligament"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pregnancy-related fluid retention and edema increase the pressure within the rigid carpal canal, compressing the median nerve against the transverse carpal ligament. This is the primary mechanism for the high prevalence of CTS in the third trimester and early postpartum period. The symptoms are typically bilateral and often resolve after delivery as edema subsides. Hormonal changes do not cause direct axonal degeneration. Uterine size does not affect the brachial plexus. Estrogen is not directly cytotoxic to the transverse carpal ligament. Cervical radiculopathy at C6 is a separate condition with a different clinical pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pregnancy-associated CTS due to edema is a high-yield board pearl connecting obstetrics and musculoskeletal pathology. FA 2024 lists edematous conditions including pregnancy as CTS precipitants. COMLEX: Postpartum management (bracing, reassurance, expected resolution) is testable on COMLEX for pregnancy-related CTS.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: epidemiology, mechanism, presentation, imaging, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q29","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of carpal tunnel syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 50-year-old female secretary has a 6-month history of right thumb, index, and middle finger paresthesias that wake her at night. She has tried wrist splinting with partial relief. Exam shows right thenar muscle wasting and positive Phalen's and Tinel's signs. Which intervention is most appropriate at this stage?","choices":{"A":"An additional 3-month trial of nighttime splinting with strict activity modification before considering any procedure","B":"Referral for carpal tunnel release to decompress the median nerve before further irreversible motor function is lost","C":"Oral prednisone taper for 2 weeks to reduce intracanal inflammation and relieve nerve compression","D":"Corticosteroid injection into the carpal tunnel as definitive treatment to avoid the need for surgical release","E":"NCS/EMG testing only, with no further treatment until electrodiagnostic severity grading is obtained"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Thenar muscle wasting indicates chronic severe median nerve compression with motor axonal loss. At this stage, further delay risks irreversible motor deficit; surgical carpal tunnel release (division of the transverse carpal ligament) is the most appropriate intervention to decompress the nerve and halt progression. Additional conservative management trials are not appropriate given objective motor deficits. Oral prednisone is not indicated for CTS. Corticosteroid injection may provide temporary relief but is not definitive treatment when thenar atrophy is present. NCS/EMG is useful for confirmation but should not delay surgery when clinical findings already show motor involvement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Thenar wasting from CTS is the signal that surgery is urgently needed; boards test the threshold between conservative management and surgical referral, with motor deficit being the key trigger. COMLEX: Timely recognition of motor involvement and referral timing is explicitly tested on COMLEX clinical management questions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: epidemiology, mechanism, presentation, imaging, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q30","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of carpal tunnel syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 44-year-old male presents 3 days after a displaced distal radius fracture with acute onset of severe median nerve distribution paresthesias and thumb weakness. He had no prior symptoms. X-ray confirms the fracture. Which of the following most likely explains the acute neurological findings?","choices":{"A":"Median nerve laceration from the sharp fracture fragment directly severing the nerve at the fracture site","B":"Brachial plexus traction injury at C6 and C7 nerve roots from the force of impact during the fall","C":"Radial nerve palsy at the anatomical spiral groove of the humerus from the fracture mechanism","D":"Acute carpal tunnel syndrome from fracture-related edema and hematoma acutely increasing the pressure within the carpal canal","E":"Anterior interosseous nerve compression causing isolated flexor pollicis longus and pronator quadratus weakness"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acute carpal tunnel syndrome is a well-recognized complication of distal radius fractures, caused by post-fracture edema, hematoma, and fragment displacement acutely increasing pressure within the rigid carpal canal. This compresses the median nerve and produces rapid-onset sensory and motor symptoms. A direct nerve laceration is possible but less common than compression. Brachial plexus injury would produce a much more widespread deficit. Radial nerve palsy at the spiral groove produces wrist drop, not thumb weakness or palmar paresthesias. Anterior interosseous syndrome causes isolated deep flexor weakness without sensory loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distal radius fractures causing acute CTS is high-yield for boards; FA 2024 notes that increased canal pressure from fracture hematoma can require emergent carpal tunnel release. COMLEX: Recognizing acute compartment syndrome or acute CTS as a fracture complication requiring urgent intervention is a COMLEX emergency management question.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: epidemiology, mechanism, presentation, imaging, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q31","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the wrist.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 29-year-old competitive cyclist presents after a 150-mile race with bilateral ring and small finger tingling and hypothenar numbness. He rode without padded gloves. Sensation on the dorsal ulnar hand is normal bilaterally. Grip strength is mildly reduced. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Bilateral carpal tunnel syndrome from sustained wrist flexion on the handlebars compressing the median nerve","B":"Bilateral cubital tunnel syndrome from sustained elbow flexion in the aerodynamic riding posture","C":"Bilateral Guyon's canal syndrome from sustained handlebar pressure compressing the ulnar nerve at the hypothenar eminence","D":"Bilateral pronator teres syndrome from repetitive forearm pronation during sustained handlebar gripping","E":"Bilateral lateral epicondylitis from ECRB overuse at the lateral epicondyle during sustained handlebar grip"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sustained handlebar pressure compresses the ulnar nerve at Guyon's canal between the pisiform and hamate, producing this classic \"cyclist's palsy.\" The ring and small finger paresthesias with hypothenar involvement and preserved dorsal ulnar hand sensation (dorsal ulnar cutaneous branch exits proximal to Guyon's canal) confirm the wrist-level location of the lesion. Bilateral presentation is typical with symmetric handlebar pressure. Carpal tunnel syndrome would affect the median nerve distribution (thumb, index, middle fingers). Cubital tunnel involves the elbow, not the wrist. Pronator teres syndrome is forearm-level median nerve. Lateral epicondylitis is a tendon, not a nerve condition.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cyclist's palsy from Guyon's canal compression is a classic boards vignette; the preserved dorsal ulnar sensation is the localizing finding. COMLEX: Padded gloves and proper handlebar positioning as treatment are COMLEX-preferred non-pharmacological management approaches.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 8 — Lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q32","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the wrist.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old female secretary presents with right hypothenar muscle wasting and weakness of finger abduction without any sensory loss in the hand. Tinel's sign at the cubital tunnel is negative. Which statement best explains why this presentation is consistent with a distal Guyon's canal lesion?","choices":{"A":"Guyon's canal compression always produces both motor and sensory deficits in all patients with this condition","B":"A proximal lesion at Guyon's canal selectively damages motor fibers before sensory fibers in all cases","C":"Sensory fibers in the ulnar nerve always recover faster than motor fibers following any peripheral nerve injury","D":"The proximal ulnar nerve within Guyon's canal carries only sensory information and no motor components at all","E":"A lesion at the distal Guyon's canal can selectively affect the deep motor branch of the ulnar nerve while sparing the sensory branches"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Within Guyon's canal, the ulnar nerve divides into a superficial sensory branch and a deep motor branch. A lesion at the distal aspect of Guyon's canal, or at the level of the hamate hook, can selectively affect the deep motor branch while sparing the superficial sensory branch, producing pure motor deficits without sensory loss. This is one of the localizing features that distinguishes distal from proximal ulnar nerve lesions. Guyon's canal compression does not always produce combined motor and sensory deficits; the anatomy of the bifurcation within the canal determines the clinical pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pure motor ulnar hand weakness with preserved sensation localizes a lesion to the deep branch of the ulnar nerve within Guyon's canal, a testable anatomical distinction. COMLEX: Sensory vs motor branch involvement within Guyon's canal is a clinically relevant distinction for treatment decision-making.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 8 — Lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q33","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the wrist.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old male with confirmed Guyon's canal syndrome fails 4 months of conservative management. MSK ultrasound reveals a ganglion cyst compressing the ulnar nerve within the canal. Which next step in management is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Surgical exploration and decompression of Guyon's canal with excision of the ganglion cyst causing the structural compression","B":"Repeat corticosteroid injection at 4-week intervals for a total of 3 injections before considering any surgery","C":"MRI of the cervical spine to rule out C8 radiculopathy as the source of symptoms before proceeding","D":"Botulinum toxin injection into the hypothenar muscles to reduce intrinsic muscle tension in the canal","E":"Long arm cast immobilization at 90 degrees of elbow flexion for 6 weeks to reduce canal pressure"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A structural lesion (ganglion cyst) confirmed on ultrasound causing nerve compression in Guyon's canal is an indication for surgical decompression and excision. Conservative management is unlikely to be effective when a physical mass is compressing the nerve. Additional corticosteroid injections will not address the structural problem. Cervical MRI is not needed when imaging has already identified the specific compressive lesion. Botulinum toxin and casting are not indicated and would not address the underlying cause.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Structural causes of nerve compression (ganglion cysts, hamate fractures) require surgical management; boards test the recognition of when imaging findings change the management algorithm from conservative to surgical. COMLEX: MSK ultrasound as a diagnostic tool for peripheral nerve compression is a COMLEX-testable point-of-care imaging modality.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 8 — Lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q34","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of De Quervain's tenosynovitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman who is 5 weeks postpartum presents with right wrist pain at the radial aspect while breastfeeding and lifting her infant. Exam shows tenderness over the radial styloid and a positive Finkelstein maneuver. What is the most likely diagnosis and which tendons are involved?","choices":{"A":"Carpal tunnel syndrome involving median nerve compression under the transverse carpal ligament at the volar wrist","B":"De Quervain's tenosynovitis involving the abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis in the first dorsal compartment","C":"Lateral epicondylitis involving ECRB tendon overuse at the lateral epicondyle from repetitive infant lifting","D":"Ganglion cyst arising from the scapholunate ligament on the dorsal radial aspect of the wrist","E":"Radial tunnel syndrome from posterior interosseous nerve compression at the level of the radial head"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Postpartum De Quervain's tenosynovitis is a classic presentation: the combination of hormonal changes, repetitive infant-lifting mechanics (pinch grip with wrist deviation), and breastfeeding positions stresses the APL and EPB tendons within the first dorsal compartment at the radial styloid. The positive Finkelstein maneuver confirms the diagnosis. CTS presents with median nerve distribution paresthesias and does not cause radial styloid tenderness. Lateral epicondylitis presents at the elbow, not the wrist. A ganglion cyst would be a palpable mass. Radial tunnel syndrome presents with pain distal to the lateral epicondyle and a different examination pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The postpartum radial wrist pain presentation is a classic FA 2024 boards scenario; the APL and EPB tendon involvement with positive Finkelstein is the diagnostic combination to memorize. COMLEX: Treatment includes thumb spica bracing and corticosteroid injection; postpartum management considerations (breastfeeding, safe medications) add COMLEX clinical depth.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction/tendinitis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q35","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of De Quervain's tenosynovitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 39-year-old female seamstress with De Quervain's tenosynovitis has tried 3 months of thumb spica bracing and NSAIDs without adequate relief. She cannot take time off work. Finkelstein maneuver remains positive. MSK ultrasound confirms first dorsal compartment tenosynovitis with peritendinous fluid. Which intervention is most appropriate at this stage?","choices":{"A":"Percutaneous first dorsal compartment release in the operating room given failure of all conservative options","B":"Extensor pollicis longus debridement via open surgery at Lister's tubercle to address the third compartment","C":"Carpal tunnel corticosteroid injection targeting the transverse carpal ligament at the volar wrist","D":"Corticosteroid injection into the first dorsal compartment tendon sheath at the radial styloid targeting APL and EPB","E":"Platelet-rich plasma injection into the radial collateral ligament to reduce compartment inflammation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"After failing NSAIDs and bracing, corticosteroid injection into the first dorsal compartment tendon sheath is the appropriate next step for De Quervain's tenosynovitis. Ultrasound-confirmed peritendinous fluid confirms the clinical diagnosis and guides injection targeting. Surgery (first dorsal compartment release) is reserved for cases failing at least one corticosteroid injection after adequate conservative management. EPL debridement at Lister's tubercle addresses the third compartment, not the first. Carpal tunnel injection treats CTS, not De Quervain's. PRP is used for chronic tendinosis, not acute tenosynovitis with fluid.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The De Quervain's treatment ladder is boards-tested: bracing/NSAIDs first, then corticosteroid injection into the first dorsal compartment, then surgical release for refractory cases. COMLEX: Injection site accuracy (first dorsal compartment vs carpal tunnel) is a COMLEX procedural anatomy question.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction/tendinitis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q36","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of De Quervain's tenosynovitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old avid cross-country skier presents with bilateral radial wrist pain that began during ski season. She repetitively grasps poles with forceful thumb extension and radial deviation motions. Finkelstein maneuvers are positive bilaterally. No systemic symptoms. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of injury?","choices":{"A":"Repetitive thumb abduction and radial deviation creating friction-based tendon inflammation within the first dorsal compartment fibro-osseous tunnel","B":"Repetitive ulnar deviation causing triangular fibrocartilage complex impaction at the distal radioulnar joint","C":"Sustained wrist extension from pole gripping increasing carpal canal pressure and compressing the median nerve","D":"Valgus stress on the wrist from pole planting causing ulnar collateral ligament injury at the wrist joint","E":"Sustained elbow extension during the pole push-off phase compressing the ulnar nerve at the cubital tunnel"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cross-country skiing requires repetitive forceful thumb abduction (poling) and wrist radial deviation, which generate friction and tensile stress on the APL and EPB tendons as they pass through the first dorsal compartment fibro-osseous tunnel at the radial styloid. This repetitive mechanical friction produces stenosing tenosynovitis. TFCC impaction involves the ulnar wrist and a different mechanism. Carpal canal pressure from wrist extension causes CTS, not De Quervain's. UCL injury of the wrist is a separate ligamentous condition. Cubital tunnel syndrome involves the elbow, not the wrist, and affects the ulnar nerve rather than the thumb tendons.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Any activity requiring repetitive thumb abduction or wrist radial deviation (skiing, hammering, lifting infants) is a risk factor for De Quervain's; the mechanism of friction within the fibro-osseous tunnel is the key pathophysiological concept. COMLEX: Identifying repetitive motion mechanics and recommending activity modification as the cornerstone of management aligns with COMLEX's functional medicine approach.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction/tendinitis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"}]},{"sdlNumber":15,"title":"Elbow, Wrist & Hand Pathologies (Obj 7-12)","questions":[{"id":"sdl15_q01","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ganglion cyst.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the most common anatomical location of a ganglion cyst?","choices":{"A":"Palmar surface of the wrist, arising from the flexor carpi radialis tendon sheath","B":"Volar aspect of the distal interphalangeal joint, adjacent to the nail bed","C":"Lateral border of the index finger MCP joint, arising from the A1 pulley region","D":"Dorsal surface of the ankle, arising from the peroneal tendon sheath","E":"Dorsal surface of the wrist, arising from the scapholunate ligament or dorsal joint capsule"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ganglion cysts are the most common soft tissue tumors of the hand and wrist. Approximately 70% occur on the dorsal wrist, typically arising from the scapholunate ligament or dorsal joint capsule. About 20% are found on the palmar wrist from the radiocarpal joint or flexor carpi radialis sheath, and the remaining 10% arise from other locations such as the finger sheaths. The cyst contains thick mucinous fluid derived from joint or tendon sheath tissue.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ganglion cysts are the most common wrist mass — dorsal location (70%) from scapholunate ligament is classic. COMLEX: Transillumination distinguishes fluid-filled ganglion from solid tumor; OMT is not first-line.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 7 — Popliteal cyst: another mucinous synovial cyst arising from a joint cavity — compare anatomical origin and treatment approach"},{"id":"sdl15_q02","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ganglion cyst.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A ganglion cyst is brought to transillumination testing during physical examination. Which finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"The cyst does not transmit light, indicating a solid lipomatous composition","B":"The cyst partially transmits light, consistent with a mixed solid-cystic pattern","C":"The cyst transmits light, confirming its fluid-filled mucinous content","D":"The cyst fluoresces under ultraviolet light due to mucinous protein content","E":"Transillumination is unreliable for ganglion cysts and should not be performed"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Transillumination is a key clinical maneuver for ganglion cysts. Because the cyst is filled with thick, gelatinous mucinous fluid, it transmits light when a light source is held against it — confirming the cystic, fluid-filled nature. This distinguishes ganglion cysts from solid tumors such as lipomas or giant cell tumors of the tendon sheath, which do not transilluminate. The test is simple and requires no equipment beyond a small flashlight.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Transillumination positive = fluid-filled cyst; negative = solid tumor. COMLEX: Ganglion cysts are benign; biopsy is unnecessary if transillumination confirms diagnosis. Aspiration can be both diagnostic and therapeutic.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 7 — Popliteal cyst: another mucinous synovial cyst arising from a joint cavity — compare anatomical origin and treatment approach"},{"id":"sdl15_q03","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ganglion cyst.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman presents with a 2 cm dorsal wrist mass that has been present for 6 months. It transilluminates and causes mild discomfort with wrist extension. Which of the following is an appropriate management option?","choices":{"A":"Aspiration of the cyst contents, which is both diagnostic and may be therapeutic","B":"Immediate surgical excision is required because recurrence after aspiration exceeds 90%","C":"Corticosteroid injection into the cyst is the preferred first-line treatment","D":"MRI of the wrist is mandatory prior to any intervention to rule out malignancy","E":"No treatment is needed and the patient should be told the cyst will never recur"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ganglion cysts may be managed conservatively, with aspiration, or with surgical excision. Aspiration is both diagnostic and therapeutic — removal of mucinous fluid can decompress the cyst and relieve symptoms. However, recurrence after aspiration is approximately 50%, compared to 5-15% after surgical excision. Observation is appropriate for asymptomatic cysts, as up to 50% resolve spontaneously. Surgery is reserved for persistent, symptomatic, or recurrent cysts. Corticosteroid injection is not standard for ganglion cysts, and MRI is not mandatory when clinical diagnosis is clear.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ganglion cyst management — observation (50% spontaneous resolution), aspiration (50% recurrence), or surgical excision (lowest recurrence). COMLEX: Aspiration is first-line for symptomatic cysts before surgical referral.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 7 — Popliteal cyst: another mucinous synovial cyst arising from a joint cavity — compare anatomical origin and treatment approach"},{"id":"sdl15_q04","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of wrist and hand osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Which of the following bony prominences is a hallmark of osteoarthritis at the distal interphalangeal joints of the hand?","choices":{"A":"Bouchard nodes, representing marginal osteophytes at the proximal interphalangeal joints","B":"Haygarth nodes, representing periarticular soft tissue swelling at the MCP joints","C":"Garrod pads, representing fibrous tissue deposition over the DIP joint dorsum","D":"Swan neck deformity nodes, representing hyperextension contractures at the PIP joints","E":"Heberden nodes, representing marginal osteophytes at the DIP joint margins"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Heberden nodes are bony osteophytes that develop at the distal interphalangeal joints and are a classic feature of hand osteoarthritis. They represent marginal osteophyte formation secondary to cartilage degeneration and subchondral bone remodeling. Bouchard nodes are the analogous finding at the proximal interphalangeal joints. Together, Heberden and Bouchard nodes distinguish osteoarthritis from rheumatoid arthritis, which characteristically spares the DIP joints and produces soft, boggy MCP and PIP swelling rather than hard bony prominences.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Heberden nodes = DIP osteophytes (OA); Bouchard nodes = PIP osteophytes (OA); rheumatoid arthritis spares DIP and involves MCP/PIP with soft swelling. COMLEX: OA nodes are hard and non-tender in chronic disease; RA nodes are soft, warm, and tender.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis in general: systemic pathophysiology of cartilage degeneration and osteophyte formation applies to hand and wrist OA"},{"id":"sdl15_q05","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of wrist and hand osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 65-year-old postmenopausal woman presents with pain and stiffness at the base of her thumb that worsens with pinching and gripping. Radiographs show joint space narrowing and osteophytes. Which joint is most commonly affected in this presentation?","choices":{"A":"First carpometacarpal joint, where OA is particularly prevalent in postmenopausal women","B":"Metacarpophalangeal joint of the thumb, which bears the greatest compressive force during pinch grip","C":"Scapholunate joint, which undergoes progressive instability from repetitive grip loading","D":"Trapezioscaphoid joint, which is the primary articulation stressed during lateral pinch","E":"Interphalangeal joint of the thumb, which loses cartilage from repeated flexion under load"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Osteoarthritis of the first carpometacarpal joint is the most common form of wrist and hand OA and is especially prevalent in postmenopausal women. The joint is subjected to high compressive and shear forces during pinch and grip activities. Patients present with pain and tenderness at the base of the thumb, a positive grind test, and radiographic evidence of joint space narrowing. The increased prevalence in postmenopausal women is attributed to the loss of estrogen, which affects collagen synthesis and cartilage integrity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 1st CMC OA = thumb base pain, postmenopausal women, grind test positive. COMLEX: Distinguish from De Quervain tenosynovitis (radial styloid, Finkelstein positive) — both cause radial-sided wrist pain but different structures involved.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis in general: systemic pathophysiology of cartilage degeneration and osteophyte formation applies to hand and wrist OA"},{"id":"sdl15_q06","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of wrist and hand osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Erosive osteoarthritis of the hand predominantly affects which joints and is distinguished from classic OA by which additional histological feature?","choices":{"A":"Primarily the MCP joints, with fibrinoid necrosis and pannus formation distinguishing it from typical OA","B":"Primarily the wrist radiocarpal joints, with calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition as the hallmark feature","C":"Primarily the IP joints, with subchondral bone erosions and inflammatory synovitis superimposed on degenerative changes","D":"Primarily the 1st CMC joint, with diffuse cartilage thinning and absence of osteophyte formation","E":"Primarily all hand joints symmetrically, with anti-CCP antibody positivity distinguishing it from classic OA"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Erosive osteoarthritis is a more aggressive inflammatory variant of hand OA that primarily involves the interphalangeal joints — both DIP and PIP. Unlike classic OA, erosive OA features central subchondral bone erosions visible on radiograph, giving a \"seagull wing\" or \"gull wing\" appearance, along with superimposed inflammatory synovitis. Anti-CCP antibodies are negative, distinguishing it from rheumatoid arthritis. MCP joint involvement and pannus formation are features of rheumatoid arthritis, not erosive OA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Erosive OA = IP joints, inflammatory synovitis, subchondral erosions, anti-CCP negative. COMLEX: Radiographic \"gull wing\" erosion pattern at IP joints distinguishes erosive OA from RA (MCP predominant, anti-CCP positive).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis in general: systemic pathophysiology of cartilage degeneration and osteophyte formation applies to hand and wrist OA"},{"id":"sdl15_q07","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of trigger finger.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Trigger finger results from stenosis of which structure, causing the flexor tendon to catch or lock during finger motion?","choices":{"A":"A2 pulley at the proximal phalanx, which is the strongest annular pulley in the finger sheath","B":"A1 pulley at the base of the finger, which becomes thickened and narrows the tendon sheath","C":"C1 cruciate pulley between the A1 and A2 pulleys, which frays under repetitive flexion loads","D":"Retinacular ligament of the DIP joint, which prevents bowstringing of the terminal extensor","E":"Transverse carpal ligament at the wrist, which compresses the median nerve and adjacent tendons"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Trigger finger, or stenosing flexor tenosynovitis, results from thickening and stenosis of the A1 pulley, located at the level of the MCP joint at the base of the finger. The flexor tendon develops a nodule or thickening that catches against the narrowed A1 pulley as the finger flexes and extends, producing the characteristic catching, triggering, or locking. The A2 pulley is the longest and strongest annular pulley but is not the primary site of stenosis in trigger finger. The transverse carpal ligament is involved in carpal tunnel syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trigger finger = A1 pulley stenosis at MCP joint base. The nodule is on the flexor tendon, not the pulley. COMLEX: Most common in middle finger, ring finger, and thumb; also associated with diabetes mellitus and rheumatoid arthritis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — Connective tissue composition: tendon sheath architecture and A1 pulley anatomy underpin trigger finger pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl15_q08","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of trigger finger.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes presents with a ring finger that locks in flexion and must be passively extended. She has point tenderness at the MCP joint base. What is the first-line treatment?","choices":{"A":"Surgical release of the A1 pulley under local anesthesia in the operating room","B":"Splinting of the MCP joint in extension for 6 weeks to allow pulley remodeling","C":"Oral NSAIDs and activity modification as definitive management for the first episode","D":"Ultrasound-guided aspiration of the tendon nodule with a large-bore needle","E":"Corticosteroid injection into the flexor tendon sheath at the A1 pulley level"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Corticosteroid injection into the flexor tendon sheath at the A1 pulley is the first-line treatment for trigger finger. A single injection resolves symptoms in approximately 60-90% of cases, with higher success rates in patients without diabetes. Patients with diabetes tend to have lower response rates and shorter durations of benefit. If symptoms recur after two injections, surgical release of the A1 pulley is indicated. Splinting alone has limited efficacy in established trigger finger. NSAIDs and activity modification are adjuncts but are insufficient as sole therapy for a locking trigger finger.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trigger finger first-line = corticosteroid injection into tendon sheath at A1 pulley; surgery if refractory. COMLEX: Diabetes is a risk factor for trigger finger and reduces injection success rates — important COMLEX clinical application.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — Connective tissue composition: tendon sheath architecture and A1 pulley anatomy underpin trigger finger pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl15_q09","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of trigger finger.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient describes their finger \"catching\" and \"snapping\" when flexing and extending it. On exam, a small nodule is palpable at the base of the finger that moves with flexor tendon excursion. What is the pathological mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Calcium pyrophosphate crystals deposit within the tendon sheath and impede smooth gliding movement","B":"Extensor tendon subluxation at the MCP joint causes snapping as the tendon slides off the knuckle","C":"A fibrous nodule on the flexor tendon catches against the thickened A1 pulley during tendon gliding","D":"Tenosynovial fluid accumulates between the tendon and sheath, creating a water hammer effect on motion","E":"Adhesions form between the flexor tendon and adjacent lumbricals after repetitive microtrauma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In trigger finger, repetitive microtrauma causes inflammation and thickening of the A1 pulley, which narrows the fibrous sheath. Simultaneously, a fibrous nodule forms on the flexor digitorum superficialis or profundus tendon just proximal to the A1 pulley. As the finger flexes, the nodule passes through the narrowed A1 pulley; as the finger extends, the nodule catches against the pulley margin, producing the palpable snap and triggering sensation. The palpable nodule at the MCP base that moves with tendon excursion is pathognomonic. Calcium pyrophosphate deposition is associated with chondrocalcinosis, not trigger finger.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trigger finger mechanism — A1 pulley stenosis plus flexor tendon nodule; nodule catches on proximal A1 edge during extension. COMLEX: Palpable proximal MCP nodule moving with tendon excursion is clinically diagnostic; imaging rarely needed.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — Connective tissue composition: tendon sheath architecture and A1 pulley anatomy underpin trigger finger pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl15_q10","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar collateral ligament injury of the thumb.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A complete ulnar collateral ligament tear of the thumb MCP joint is sometimes complicated by a Stener lesion. What is a Stener lesion?","choices":{"A":"The flexor pollicis longus tendon displaces into the MCP joint space and blocks reduction","B":"The adductor pollicis aponeurosis becomes interposed between the torn UCL ends, preventing spontaneous healing","C":"A bony avulsion fragment from the ulnar base of the proximal phalanx rotates and blocks the joint","D":"Synovial tissue from the MCP joint capsule herniates between the torn ligament ends","E":"The extensor pollicis brevis tendon dislocates medially and tethers the torn UCL in malposition"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A Stener lesion occurs when the torn UCL end flips proximally and the adductor pollicis aponeurosis — a fascial layer overlying the MCP joint — becomes interposed between the two torn ends of the UCL. This prevents the torn ligament ends from healing in proper apposition, making conservative management inadequate. Stener lesions are present in 50-70% of complete UCL tears and are the primary indication for surgical repair. MRI is the gold standard for identifying a Stener lesion preoperatively.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Stener lesion = adductor pollicis aponeurosis interposed between torn UCL ends; prevents healing; requires surgery. COMLEX: Complete UCL tears with Stener lesion must be repaired within 1-3 weeks before fibrosis occurs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — Interphalangeal joint dislocations: hand ligament and joint injury spectrum that includes UCL disruption and Stener lesion"},{"id":"sdl15_q11","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar collateral ligament injury of the thumb.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old skier falls and lands with their thumb hyperabducted against a ski pole. They present with thumb MCP joint pain, swelling, and instability with lateral stress testing. What mechanism produces this injury?","choices":{"A":"Forced ulnar deviation of the thumb at the MCP joint tears the radial collateral ligament on the lateral side","B":"Hyperflexion of the thumb IP joint avulses the flexor pollicis longus from its distal phalangeal insertion","C":"Axial loading through the thumb compresses the sesamoid bones into the MCP joint, rupturing the volar plate","D":"Forced extension of the MCP joint beyond neutral tears the dorsal capsule and extensor mechanism","E":"Forced radial abduction of the thumb at the MCP joint overstresses and tears the ulnar collateral ligament"},"correct":"E","explanation":"UCL injury of the thumb — classically called Gamekeeper's thumb when chronic and Skier's thumb when acute — results from forced radial abduction of the thumb at the MCP joint. This valgus stress mechanism overstresses the UCL on the ulnar aspect of the joint. In skiing, this occurs when the ski pole catches the thumb during a fall, leveraging the thumb radially. The UCL resists this radial abduction force; a sudden excessive force tears the ligament, which can be partial or complete. The RCL is on the opposite, radial side, and is injured by forced ulnar deviation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: UCL thumb injury = forced radial abduction (valgus stress at MCP) = Skier's thumb (acute) or Gamekeeper's thumb (chronic). COMLEX: Clinical valgus stress test under local anesthesia assesses stability; > 35 degrees instability suggests complete tear.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — Interphalangeal joint dislocations: hand ligament and joint injury spectrum that includes UCL disruption and Stener lesion"},{"id":"sdl15_q12","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar collateral ligament injury of the thumb.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following is the gold-standard imaging modality for diagnosing a Stener lesion in a patient with suspected complete UCL tear of the thumb?","choices":{"A":"MRI of the thumb, which directly visualizes the displaced UCL and interposed adductor aponeurosis","B":"Stress radiograph of the thumb in abduction, which quantifies degrees of instability at the MCP joint","C":"Ultrasound of the ulnar aspect of the MCP joint performed dynamically during valgus stress loading","D":"CT arthrogram of the thumb, which outlines the joint capsule disruption and intraarticular hematoma","E":"Plain radiograph of the thumb, which identifies the avulsion fracture fragment at the UCL insertion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"MRI is the gold standard for identifying a Stener lesion because it directly visualizes the torn UCL and the interposed adductor pollicis aponeurosis. This distinction is critical because a Stener lesion requires surgical repair, whereas a partial UCL tear or complete tear without Stener lesion may be managed conservatively with thumb spica casting. Stress radiographs quantify instability but cannot differentiate Stener from non-Stener injuries. Ultrasound can be used by experienced operators but is operator-dependent. Plain radiographs identify bony avulsion fractures but cannot assess soft tissue interposition.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MRI = gold standard for Stener lesion detection. COMLEX: Surgical indication: Stener lesion, > 35 degrees MCP instability, or > 15 degrees difference from contralateral thumb. Complete tears need repair within 1-3 weeks.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — Interphalangeal joint dislocations: hand ligament and joint injury spectrum that includes UCL disruption and Stener lesion"},{"id":"sdl15_q13","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of extensor finger injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which mechanism of injury is most consistent with mallet finger?","choices":{"A":"Sudden forced extension of a passively flexed DIP joint, tearing the flexor digitorum profundus from its insertion","B":"Axial compression of the fingertip against a hard surface, driving the distal phalanx into the middle phalanx","C":"Sudden forced flexion of an actively extended fingertip, avulsing the terminal extensor tendon from the distal phalanx","D":"Hyperextension of the PIP joint beyond 90 degrees, disrupting the central slip of the extensor mechanism","E":"Chronic repetitive microtrauma to the DIP joint from gripping activities, leading to gradual extensor attrition"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mallet finger, also called baseball finger, occurs when the terminal extensor tendon at the DIP joint is avulsed — either as a soft-tissue tear or as a bony avulsion fracture — by a sudden forced flexion force applied to an actively extended fingertip. The classic mechanism is a ball striking the end of the outstretched finger, abruptly flexing the DIP joint. This ruptures or avulses the terminal extensor tendon from its insertion on the dorsal distal phalanx, causing the DIP joint to drop into a flexion posture. The injury to the FDP causing jersey finger involves the volar tendon and forced extension, not flexion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mallet finger = forced DIP flexion → terminal extensor rupture → DIP cannot extend. COMLEX: If less than 1/3 of articular surface involved, treat with extension splinting; greater than 1/3 or volar subluxation may require surgery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: bony mallet finger is an avulsion fracture of the dorsal distal phalanx base requiring fracture management principles"},{"id":"sdl15_q14","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of extensor finger injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient presents with a flexion deformity of the DIP joint after being struck on the fingertip by a basketball. The DIP joint cannot be actively extended but passive extension is preserved. Radiograph shows no fracture. What is the appropriate treatment?","choices":{"A":"Surgical repair of the terminal extensor tendon is required for all acute mallet finger injuries within 24 hours","B":"Buddy taping to the adjacent finger and activity restriction for 3 weeks is sufficient for soft-tissue mallet","C":"Splinting the PIP joint in flexion and the DIP joint in extension simultaneously for 4 weeks restores function","D":"Corticosteroid injection into the DIP joint reduces inflammation and allows tendon reattachment within 2 weeks","E":"Continuous splinting of the DIP joint in full extension for 6-8 weeks without interruption, even during bathing"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pure soft-tissue mallet finger is managed with continuous DIP extension splinting for 6-8 weeks. The splint must be worn continuously — including during bathing and sleep — because any inadvertent DIP flexion during the healing period restarts the 6-8 week clock. The splint maintains the terminal extensor tendon in apposition with its distal phalanx insertion, allowing scar tissue healing. Buddy taping alone is insufficient. Surgical repair is generally reserved for open injuries, bony avulsion fractures involving greater than 1/3 of the articular surface, or cases with volar subluxation of the distal phalanx.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Soft-tissue mallet finger = DIP extension splint continuously for 6-8 weeks; any DIP flexion during splinting restarts the clock. COMLEX: Bony mallet with less than 1/3 articular surface involvement is still treated conservatively with splinting.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: bony mallet finger is an avulsion fracture of the dorsal distal phalanx base requiring fracture management principles"},{"id":"sdl15_q15","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of extensor finger injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A bony mallet finger involves an avulsion fracture of the dorsal distal phalanx. Which radiographic finding in a bony mallet finger is most likely to require surgical intervention rather than splinting?","choices":{"A":"The avulsion fragment is displaced more than 1 mm from the distal phalanx articular surface on lateral view","B":"The avulsion fracture involves the dorsal cortex without extension into the subchondral bone surface","C":"The patient presents more than 48 hours after the injury occurred due to delayed medical attention","D":"The avulsion fragment involves greater than one-third of the articular surface of the DIP joint","E":"The finger involved is the middle or ring finger, which bear greater functional pinch grip demands"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bony mallet finger is typically managed conservatively with DIP extension splinting when the avulsion fragment involves less than one-third of the articular surface and the distal phalanx remains reduced without volar subluxation. When the bony fragment involves greater than one-third of the articular surface, joint stability may be compromised and the risk of articular incongruity is higher, making surgical fixation with a pin or screw the preferred approach. Volar subluxation of the distal phalanx with a large dorsal fragment is an absolute indication for surgery. The duration of delay and finger involved do not independently determine surgical need.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Surgical indications for bony mallet finger: articular fragment > 1/3 of DIP surface, OR volar subluxation of distal phalanx. COMLEX: Less than 1/3 fragment = extension splint 6-8 weeks regardless of fragment displacement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: bony mallet finger is an avulsion fracture of the dorsal distal phalanx base requiring fracture management principles"},{"id":"sdl15_q16","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of flexor tendon injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Jersey finger most frequently involves which digit, and what tendon is avulsed in this injury?","choices":{"A":"The index finger, with avulsion of the flexor digitorum superficialis from its insertion on the middle phalanx","B":"The middle finger, with avulsion of the flexor digitorum profundus from the A2 pulley at the proximal phalanx","C":"The ring finger, with avulsion of the flexor digitorum profundus from its insertion on the volar distal phalanx","D":"The small finger, with avulsion of the flexor carpi ulnaris tendon from the pisiform bony attachment","E":"The thumb, with avulsion of the flexor pollicis longus from its insertion on the volar distal phalanx"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Jersey finger is avulsion of the flexor digitorum profundus from its insertion on the volar distal phalanx, most commonly affecting the ring finger in approximately 75% of cases. The ring finger is vulnerable because it has the least independent flexion compared to adjacent fingers and is held in a more extended position during gripping, making it the last to release when the grip is forcefully opened. The injury occurs when a player grabs a jersey and the opponent pulls away, forcibly extending the DIP joint against active FDP contraction. The FDS inserts on the middle phalanx and is not typically avulsed in jersey finger.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Jersey finger = FDP avulsion, ring finger 75%, inability to flex DIP against resistance. COMLEX: Mechanism = forced DIP extension against resisted FDP contraction; contrast with mallet finger (forced DIP flexion, extensor injury).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: tendon and apophyseal avulsion injuries in athletes — compare mechanism to FDP avulsion in jersey finger"},{"id":"sdl15_q17","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of flexor tendon injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"In the Leddy-Packer classification of jersey finger, a Type I injury carries the most urgent surgical timeline. Why is surgery most urgent for Type I?","choices":{"A":"The FDP tendon remains at the PIP joint level, where it compresses the digital nerve and requires urgent decompression","B":"A large bony avulsion fragment obstructs the A4 pulley, causing vascular compromise of the entire finger","C":"The tendon avulses with the volar plate of the DIP joint, making anatomical reduction technically time-sensitive","D":"The FDP tendon retracts into the palm, disrupting its blood supply via the vincula, requiring repair within days to prevent tendon necrosis","E":"Both vincula are intact in Type I, preserving blood supply and allowing delayed repair up to 3 months later"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In the Leddy-Packer classification, Type I jersey finger involves the FDP tendon retracting all the way into the palm, rupturing both the long and short vincula — the mesotenon attachments that supply blood to the tendon. Without vascular supply, the tendon undergoes rapid fibrosis and becomes unsuitable for repair. Therefore, surgical repair must be performed within days (typically 7-10 days) of injury. Type II tendons retract to the PIP joint level, maintaining the short vinculum and blood supply, allowing repair within 1-2 weeks. Type III involves a bony avulsion fragment that catches at the A4 pulley, retaining blood supply and allowing a less urgent surgical timeline.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Leddy-Packer Type I = tendon into palm, both vincula ruptured, surgery within days. Type II = PIP level, short vinculum intact, 1-2 weeks. Type III = bony fragment at A4. COMLEX: Delay in Type I = irreparable tendon necrosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: tendon and apophyseal avulsion injuries in athletes — compare mechanism to FDP avulsion in jersey finger"},{"id":"sdl15_q18","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of flexor tendon injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which physical examination finding best distinguishes jersey finger from other finger injuries?","choices":{"A":"Loss of active DIP flexion with preserved passive DIP flexion and intact PIP flexion against resistance","B":"Loss of active DIP extension with the DIP joint held in a flexion posture that cannot be voluntarily corrected","C":"Loss of both active DIP and PIP flexion with the finger held in extension by the intact extensor mechanism","D":"Triggering and catching of the finger with active flexion due to a nodule catching on the A1 pulley","E":"Point tenderness at the MCP joint with palpable swelling and clicking on passive range of motion testing"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Jersey finger presents with inability to actively flex the DIP joint because the FDP — the only tendon that flexes the DIP — has been avulsed from its insertion. Passive DIP flexion is preserved because the joint itself is intact. PIP flexion is maintained because the FDS, which inserts on the middle phalanx, is uninjured. This pattern — lost active DIP flexion with preserved active PIP flexion — is pathognomonic for FDP avulsion. Mallet finger, in contrast, presents with lost active DIP extension (the finger droops in flexion). Trigger finger involves catching on active flexion at the MCP joint level.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Jersey finger = cannot actively flex DIP; FDS intact so PIP flexion preserved. Mallet finger = cannot actively extend DIP. COMLEX: Distinguish on exam — jersey finger \"flexion loss at DIP,\" mallet finger \"drop at DIP.\"","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: tendon and apophyseal avulsion injuries in athletes — compare mechanism to FDP avulsion in jersey finger"},{"id":"sdl15_q19","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ganglion cyst.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 26-year-old right-hand-dominant office worker presents with a 2-year history of a slowly enlarging lump on the dorsum of her wrist. She reports mild aching that worsens with prolonged typing. On exam, a 1.5 cm firm, smooth, non-tender mass is found over the dorsal radiocarpal joint. It transilluminates and does not move independently of the overlying skin. Radiographs are normal. What is the most likely origin of this mass?","choices":{"A":"Reactive hypertrophy of the dorsal extensor retinaculum from repetitive friction against the underlying carpal bones","B":"Lipomatous degeneration of the dorsal wrist fat pad secondary to chronic compression from keyboard use","C":"Proliferation of synovial lining cells within the joint, forming an exophytic synovial cyst into the dorsal soft tissue","D":"Herniation of synovial fluid through a one-way valve defect in the dorsal scapholunate ligament or joint capsule","E":"Hematoma organization after an unrecognized minor wrist sprain that was not treated acutely"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ganglion cysts of the dorsal wrist arise through a one-way valve or pedicle defect in the joint capsule or scapholunate ligament that allows synovial fluid to herniate outward. The fluid collects into a fibrous-walled cyst and becomes gelatinous over time. The \"one-way valve\" mechanism means that joint movement can pump more fluid into the cyst but not back into the joint, which accounts for the cyst's fluctuating size with activity. Synovial proliferation produces villous synovitis or rice bodies in inflammatory arthritis, not discrete cysts. The mass being non-mobile from the skin reflects its deep fascial attachment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ganglion cyst arises via one-way valve defect in joint capsule — synovial fluid herniates out and becomes gelatinous. COMLEX: Wrist ganglia fluctuate in size with wrist motion/activity — this history clue supports ganglion over lipoma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 7 — Popliteal cyst: mucinous synovial cyst arising from posterior knee joint communication via one-way valve — compare mechanism to dorsal wrist ganglion"},{"id":"sdl15_q20","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ganglion cyst.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 35-year-old man presents with a 3 cm mass on the palmar radial aspect of his wrist near the base of the thenar eminence. It has been present for 8 months and causes mild discomfort with wrist flexion. Transillumination is positive. He asks about treatment options and their recurrence rates. Which statement about recurrence is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Aspiration has approximately 10% recurrence rate and is therefore preferred over surgical excision in most patients","B":"Aspiration has approximately 50% recurrence rate, while surgical excision has a 5-15% recurrence rate","C":"Surgical excision has a 50-60% recurrence rate because the cyst pedicle commonly regrows from residual capsular tissue","D":"Observation leads to spontaneous resolution in less than 5% of cases, making active treatment always necessary","E":"Recurrence rates are identical regardless of treatment modality and depend solely on continued wrist overuse"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Management of ganglion cysts must be discussed with the patient in the context of recurrence rates. Aspiration carries approximately 50% recurrence, while surgical excision — which removes the cyst wall and its pedicle from the joint capsule or ligament — has a 5-15% recurrence rate. Observation is also appropriate, as approximately 50% of ganglion cysts resolve spontaneously without intervention, particularly in younger patients. The choice of management should be individualized based on symptom severity, cyst size, patient preference, and hand dominance. Palmar wrist cysts are in proximity to the radial artery and require careful technique for aspiration or excision.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ganglion recurrence — aspiration ~50%, surgery ~5-15%, observation (50% spontaneous resolution). COMLEX: Palmar cysts near radial artery require caution with aspiration; surgical excision is preferred when intervention is needed.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 7 — Popliteal cyst: conservative observation vs aspiration vs surgical excision decision-making parallels ganglion cyst management"},{"id":"sdl15_q21","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ganglion cyst.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 19-year-old male collegiate baseball pitcher presents with a small lump on the volar radial wrist for 4 months. It is 1 cm, non-tender, and mildly fluctuant. He is concerned it might be cancer. Transillumination is positive. There is no change in size with compression. Radiographs are normal. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Reassure the patient that ganglion cysts are benign, and offer observation with activity modification as an initial option","B":"Order an MRI with contrast to rule out low-grade synovial sarcoma before any further management is planned","C":"Perform immediate needle aspiration in the office to confirm diagnosis and provide therapeutic decompression","D":"Refer to hand surgery for urgent excision given the patient's athletic demands and occupational requirements","E":"Obtain an ultrasound-guided core biopsy of the mass because transillumination is not specific enough for diagnosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A small, non-tender, transilluminating mass on the wrist in a young patient with a typical clinical presentation is reliably a ganglion cyst. Reassurance and observation are appropriate as a first step, given that approximately 50% resolve spontaneously and the mass is benign. MRI or biopsy is not indicated when the clinical diagnosis is clear and there are no atypical features such as rapid growth, firmness, or systemic symptoms suggesting malignancy. Aspiration or referral to hand surgery can be offered if the patient desires intervention, but immediate escalation is not warranted for a small, asymptomatic, and uncomplicated presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Transilluminating wrist mass with typical clinical features = ganglion cyst; observation is first-line. COMLEX: Avoid overinvestigation — clinical diagnosis of ganglion does not require biopsy or advanced imaging.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 7 — Popliteal cyst: reassurance and observation appropriate for asymptomatic synovial cysts regardless of anatomical location"},{"id":"sdl15_q22","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of wrist and hand osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 68-year-old retired seamstress presents with 5 years of bilateral hand pain and stiffness. She notes bony enlargement of the DIP and PIP joints of multiple fingers bilaterally. Radiographs reveal joint space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis, and osteophytes at the DIP and PIP joints. Lab work including ESR, CRP, RF, and anti-CCP antibodies are all within normal limits. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Rheumatoid arthritis with seronegative presentation affecting the DIP and PIP joints symmetrically","B":"Psoriatic arthritis with DIP predominant involvement and enthesopathy at the extensor tendon insertions","C":"Gout with monosodium urate crystal deposition causing recurrent periarticular bone erosion at multiple joints","D":"Primary hand osteoarthritis with Heberden nodes at DIP joints and Bouchard nodes at PIP joints","E":"Reactive arthritis triggered by prior infection, causing polyarticular DIP and PIP joint inflammation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Primary hand osteoarthritis is characterized by Heberden nodes at DIP joints and Bouchard nodes at PIP joints, with radiographic findings of joint space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis, and osteophytes. Normal inflammatory markers and seronegative labs distinguish it from rheumatoid arthritis, which is seropositive in most cases and characteristically spares the DIP joints. Psoriatic arthritis can involve the DIP joints but typically shows \"pencil in cup\" erosions on X-ray and is associated with psoriatic skin or nail changes. Gout shows tophi and negatively birefringent urate crystals on joint aspiration. A retired seamstress with bilateral symmetric DIP and PIP bony enlargement and normal labs is classic for primary OA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hand OA = Heberden nodes (DIP) + Bouchard nodes (PIP); OA spares MCP; RA spares DIP. COMLEX: Normal ESR/CRP and seronegative labs confirm OA over inflammatory arthritis in this presentation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis in general: cartilage degeneration, subchondral sclerosis, and osteophyte formation pathophysiology underlying all OA including hand OA"},{"id":"sdl15_q23","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of wrist and hand osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 60-year-old postmenopausal woman presents with a 2-year history of pain and weakness at the base of her right thumb. She reports difficulty opening jars and turning a key. Exam reveals tenderness at the thenar eminence proximal to the MCP joint, a palpable bony ridge at the 1st CMC joint, and a positive grind test. Radiographs show joint space narrowing and osteophytes at the 1st CMC joint. Which of the following best describes the initial management?","choices":{"A":"Surgical trapeziectomy with ligament reconstruction is the recommended initial treatment given radiographic OA","B":"Thumb spica splinting combined with NSAIDs and activity modification as the first-line conservative approach","C":"Systemic corticosteroids for 2 weeks followed by hand therapy to reduce acute flare and restore grip strength","D":"Intraarticular methotrexate injection into the 1st CMC joint to slow cartilage degeneration over time","E":"Platelet-rich plasma injection into the 1st CMC joint should be offered before any splinting or NSAID use"},"correct":"B","explanation":"First CMC joint OA is initially managed conservatively with thumb spica splinting to immobilize the joint and reduce mechanical stress, combined with NSAIDs for pain control and activity modification. Occupational therapy with hand exercises is also beneficial. Corticosteroid injections into the 1st CMC joint are useful for acute flares. Surgical trapeziectomy with ligament reconstruction is reserved for patients who fail conservative management over several months and have functional limitations. Systemic corticosteroids are not appropriate for focal OA. Intraarticular methotrexate and PRP are not standard evidence-based treatments for 1st CMC OA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 1st CMC OA = thumb spica, NSAIDs, activity modification; corticosteroid injection for flares; surgery if refractory. COMLEX: Grind test positive (axial compression + rotation of 1st metacarpal) = 1st CMC OA; Finkelstein positive = De Quervain.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis in general: conservative management principles including splinting, NSAIDs, and activity modification applicable to 1st CMC OA"},{"id":"sdl15_q24","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of wrist and hand osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman presents with painful swelling and erythema of multiple DIP and PIP joints that has waxed and waned over 3 years. Radiographs reveal a \"gull wing\" erosion pattern at the IP joints with joint space narrowing. RF is negative. Anti-CCP is negative. ESR is mildly elevated at 35 mm/hr. What distinguishes this diagnosis from classic osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Erosive OA is characterized by positive anti-CCP antibodies that differentiate it from seronegative rheumatoid arthritis","B":"Erosive OA exclusively affects the MCP joints bilaterally, whereas classic OA primarily involves DIP joints unilaterally","C":"Erosive osteoarthritis has central subchondral bone erosions with inflammatory synovitis, absent in classic non-erosive OA","D":"Erosive OA is caused by calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition rather than cartilage degeneration from mechanical stress","E":"Erosive OA presents with Heberden and Bouchard nodes that are soft and tender rather than hard and painless in classic OA"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Erosive osteoarthritis is a more aggressive variant of hand OA that shares the DIP and PIP joint distribution of classic OA but is distinguished by central subchondral bone erosions and inflammatory synovitis. Radiographically, the central erosions produce the classic \"gull wing\" pattern, as opposed to the marginal osteophytes of non-erosive OA. Anti-CCP and RF are negative in both erosive and classic OA — positivity would suggest rheumatoid arthritis. The mildly elevated ESR reflects the inflammatory component of erosive OA. MCP joint predominance and pannus formation are features of RA, not erosive OA. Calcium pyrophosphate deposition causes chondrocalcinosis and pseudogout.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Erosive OA = central DIP/PIP erosions with \"gull wing\" X-ray pattern + inflammatory synovitis + seronegative labs. COMLEX: Distinguish erosive OA from RA by DIP involvement and seronegative status; RA spares DIP and is often RF/anti-CCP positive.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis in general: inflammatory OA variants share pathophysiology with classic OA but have additional synovial inflammation component"},{"id":"sdl15_q25","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of trigger finger.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes presents with a 3-month history of her right ring finger \"getting stuck\" in a bent position and requiring her to manually straighten it each morning. Exam reveals point tenderness at the base of the ring finger just distal to the MCP joint, and a palpable nodule that moves with flexor tendon excursion. Active flexion causes the finger to catch. What is the most likely pathological change found at surgery?","choices":{"A":"Attenuation and tearing of the A2 pulley with bowstringing of the flexor digitorum superficialis tendon","B":"Calcific tendinosis within the flexor digitorum profundus at the level of the proximal interphalangeal joint","C":"Proliferative synovitis of the flexor tendon sheath throughout the entire digital canal from A1 to A5","D":"Complete tear of the flexor digitorum superficialis tendon at its bifurcation proximal to the A3 pulley","E":"Thickening and fibrosis of the A1 pulley at the MCP joint level, with a fibrous nodule on the flexor tendon proximal to the pulley"},"correct":"E","explanation":"At surgery for trigger finger, the key findings are a thickened, fibrotic A1 pulley that has narrowed the fibrous tunnel at the MCP joint level, and a fusiform fibrous nodule on the flexor tendon just proximal to the A1 pulley. The nodule catches on the proximal edge of the stenotic A1 pulley during extension, producing triggering. Diabetes mellitus promotes collagen cross-linking and fibrosis, increasing the risk of trigger finger and reducing the efficacy of corticosteroid injection. The A2 pulley is the strongest and most critical for preventing bowstringing but is not the site of stenosis in trigger finger. Synovitis throughout the digital canal occurs in inflammatory arthritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trigger finger pathology = A1 pulley fibrosis + FDS/FDP tendon nodule at the pulley level. COMLEX: Diabetes increases risk of trigger finger and stenosing tenosynovitis; injection success rates lower in diabetic patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — Connective tissue composition and roles: tendon sheath pulley anatomy and collagen structure relevant to A1 pulley pathogenesis in trigger finger"},{"id":"sdl15_q26","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of trigger finger.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman presents with trigger finger of the right long finger that has failed two corticosteroid injections over 6 months. She now has a finger that locks fully in flexion and must be manually extended. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"A third corticosteroid injection combined with formal hand therapy for 8 weeks before considering surgery","B":"Surgical release of the A1 pulley under local anesthesia, which has a greater than 95% cure rate","C":"Splinting the MCP joint in full extension for 6 weeks to allow A1 pulley remodeling and tendon decompression","D":"Oral methotrexate for 3 months to address underlying inflammatory tenosynovitis contributing to pulley stenosis","E":"Ultrasound-guided needle aponeurotomy of the A1 pulley as a minimally invasive alternative to surgery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After two failed corticosteroid injections, surgical A1 pulley release is the appropriate next step. The procedure involves dividing the A1 pulley longitudinally under local anesthesia, either through an open incision or percutaneously, which decompresses the tendon and resolves triggering. Success rates exceed 95% with surgical release, and complications are rare. A third injection provides minimal additional benefit after two failures, especially in a locking trigger finger. Splinting alone does not decompress a stenotic pulley. Methotrexate is used for inflammatory arthritis, not idiopathic trigger finger. Percutaneous needle aponeurotomy is used for Dupuytren contracture, not trigger finger.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Refractory trigger finger after 2 injections = A1 pulley surgical release; greater than 95% cure rate. COMLEX: Open vs percutaneous A1 release both effective; percutaneous avoided near neurovascular structures in thumb and index finger.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — Connective tissue composition and roles: A1 pulley surgical division relieves mechanical obstruction to tendon gliding through fibro-osseous canal"},{"id":"sdl15_q27","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of trigger finger.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 42-year-old man with no significant medical history develops trigger finger of the right middle finger after 6 weeks of intensive grip training at a new warehouse job. He describes a painful snap when bending the finger, with the finger occasionally locking in a bent position. The MCP base is tender. Which comorbid condition, if present, would most significantly reduce the expected response rate to a single corticosteroid injection?","choices":{"A":"Diabetes mellitus, which promotes fibrosis of the A1 pulley and impairs tendon sheath response to corticosteroids","B":"Hypertension, which impairs microvascular supply to the tendon sheath and prolongs inflammatory resolution","C":"Osteoporosis, which results in subchondral softening of the MCP joint that amplifies compressive forces on the A1 pulley","D":"Hypothyroidism, which causes diffuse mucin deposition in the tenosynovium and is not responsive to steroids","E":"Obesity, which increases grip force demand on the A1 pulley and accelerates pulley stenosis after any injection"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Diabetes mellitus is the most significant comorbidity that reduces corticosteroid injection efficacy in trigger finger. Diabetes promotes glycation of collagen and fibrosis of the tendon sheath and pulleys, creating a more fibrotic and less responsive pathological substrate. Additionally, corticosteroid injections in diabetic patients can transiently elevate blood glucose. In the general population, a single corticosteroid injection resolves trigger finger in 60-90% of patients, but in diabetics, success rates are substantially lower (40-50%) and recurrence is more common. Hypothyroidism is associated with trigger finger due to mucinous tenosynovial deposits but does respond to treatment. Hypertension and obesity are not directly associated with reduced injection response rates.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diabetes mellitus reduces corticosteroid injection efficacy for trigger finger and increases recurrence — plan for earlier surgical referral. COMLEX: Also note hypothyroidism as a risk factor for trigger finger (mucinous tenosynovitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — Connective tissue composition and roles: collagen glycation in diabetes promotes A1 pulley fibrosis, reducing responsiveness to steroid-mediated inflammation modulation"},{"id":"sdl15_q28","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar collateral ligament injury of the thumb.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old skier presents after a fall in which her right thumb was caught on a ski pole and forced radially. She reports MCP joint pain and difficulty pinching. Exam reveals swelling and ecchymosis over the ulnar MCP joint, tenderness at the ulnar collateral ligament, and laxity with valgus stress at 30 degrees of MCP flexion but not at 0 degrees. What does the pattern of laxity suggest?","choices":{"A":"Complete UCL rupture with Stener lesion, because laxity at 30 degrees confirms total ligament disruption","B":"Volar plate rupture of the MCP joint, which produces laxity at 30 degrees flexion and 0 degrees equally","C":"Radial collateral ligament sprain, because the radial side is stressed when the thumb is forced into radial deviation","D":"Physeal fracture at the base of the proximal phalanx, which must be ruled out by radiograph before stress testing","E":"Partial UCL tear with intact accessory collateral ligament, as isolated proper UCL laxity appears at 30 degrees flexion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In thumb MCP stress testing, laxity at 30 degrees of flexion indicates injury to the proper UCL, as the proper UCL is the primary stabilizer in flexion. Laxity at both 0 degrees and 30 degrees of MCP flexion indicates a more complete injury involving both the proper and accessory UCL. In this vignette, laxity only at 30 degrees with stability at 0 degrees suggests a partial tear of the proper UCL with intact accessory UCL, which typically can be managed conservatively with thumb spica casting. Complete tears show laxity at both angles. Volar plate injury would not isolate laxity to 30 degrees flexion only. A Stener lesion cannot be confirmed or excluded by clinical examination alone — MRI is required.","boardPrep":"Step 1: UCL stress test — laxity at 30 degrees = proper UCL; laxity at 0 degrees = accessory UCL also torn. Greater than 35 degrees or 15 degrees more than contralateral = complete tear. COMLEX: Always radiograph first before stress testing to rule out avulsion fracture.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — Interphalangeal joint dislocations: valgus and varus stress testing principles for hand joint ligament integrity assessment"},{"id":"sdl15_q29","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar collateral ligament injury of the thumb.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 30-year-old rugby player sustains a UCL injury of the right thumb. MRI confirms a complete UCL tear with a Stener lesion. The injury occurred 4 days ago. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Thumb spica cast for 6 weeks because Stener lesions can resolve spontaneously with immobilization alone","B":"Corticosteroid injection into the MCP joint to reduce inflammation followed by functional bracing for 4 weeks","C":"Platelet-rich plasma injection at the UCL insertion to promote tissue regeneration and avoid surgery","D":"Surgical repair of the UCL with reapproximation of the torn ends, performed within 3 weeks of injury before fibrosis occurs","E":"Dynamic splinting of the MCP joint in ulnar deviation for 4 weeks to allow UCL scar tissue approximation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A Stener lesion — interposition of the adductor pollicis aponeurosis between the torn UCL ends — prevents anatomic healing of the torn ligament with conservative treatment. Surgical repair is required and must be performed within 3 weeks of injury, as fibrosis and scar tissue formation after that time make anatomic repair technically more difficult. The surgeon excises the interposed aponeurosis, reapproximates the UCL ends, and may augment with a suture anchor. Conservative management with thumb spica casting is appropriate only for partial tears without Stener lesion. Corticosteroid injection and PRP are not indicated for acute ligamentous injuries requiring operative repair.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Stener lesion = surgery required; repair within 3 weeks. COMLEX: Failure to diagnose Stener lesion leads to chronic MCP instability — clinical pearl for COMLEX clinical presentations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — Interphalangeal joint dislocations: surgical vs conservative management decision-making based on ligamentous integrity and interposed tissue"},{"id":"sdl15_q30","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar collateral ligament injury of the thumb.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 50-year-old gamekeeper presents with chronic right thumb weakness for 2 years. He reports difficulty with pinch grip and a history of repetitive thumb hyperabduction while wringing the necks of game birds. Exam reveals MCP joint laxity with valgus stress and thenar atrophy. Radiographs show mild 1st MCP OA. What term describes his injury, and what is its key distinguishing feature from acute Skier's thumb?","choices":{"A":"Skier's thumb — an acute UCL rupture with Stener lesion that presents identically in chronic form with thenar atrophy","B":"De Quervain tenosynovitis — chronic radial-sided wrist pain from repetitive thumb loading that mimics UCL pathology","C":"Gamekeeper's thumb — a chronic UCL insufficiency from repetitive microtrauma, causing progressive MCP instability and OA rather than an acute complete tear","D":"Trigger thumb — chronic A1 pulley stenosis from repetitive thumb gripping that secondarily loosens the UCL over time","E":"First CMC OA — degenerative joint disease of the thumb base from grip overuse that does not affect the UCL"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Gamekeeper's thumb describes chronic UCL insufficiency from repetitive microtrauma — originally described in Scottish gamekeepers who routinely wrung the necks of game, stressing the thumb UCL thousands of times. Over time, the UCL stretches and becomes insufficient, producing progressive MCP instability, thenar weakness, and secondary OA. This is distinguished from Skier's thumb, which is an acute complete UCL tear from a single traumatic event. Both involve the UCL, but gamekeeper's thumb represents cumulative attenuation rather than acute rupture, and Stener lesions are less common in chronic cases. Treatment for chronic gamekeeper's thumb may require ligament reconstruction rather than primary repair.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gamekeeper's thumb = chronic UCL laxity from repetitive stress; Skier's thumb = acute UCL rupture from single event. COMLEX: Both cause MCP valgus instability and difficulty pinching — distinguish by mechanism and chronicity on boards.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — Interphalangeal joint dislocations: chronic ligamentous insufficiency from repetitive microtrauma compared to acute dislocation mechanisms"},{"id":"sdl15_q31","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of extensor finger injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 22-year-old softball outfielder presents after catching a line drive that struck the tip of her right index finger. She notes that the fingertip now droops and she cannot straighten it at the last joint. Exam confirms a flexion deformity of the DIP joint with intact passive extension. Active DIP extension is absent. Radiographs show no fracture. What is the most appropriate splint position for treatment?","choices":{"A":"Continuous DIP joint extension in full neutral extension (0 degrees) using a stack splint for 6-8 weeks","B":"Continuous DIP joint splinting in 20 degrees of hyperextension to maximize terminal extensor tendon tension","C":"PIP joint splinting in full flexion while leaving the DIP free to allow active extensor tendon rehabilitation","D":"Dynamic extension splinting with an elastic sling pulling the DIP into extension during active finger use","E":"Buddy taping the index finger to the middle finger in flexion and returning in 4 weeks for reevaluation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Mallet finger is treated with continuous DIP extension splinting at neutral (0 degrees) using a prefabricated or custom stack splint for 6-8 weeks. The goal is to maintain the terminal extensor tendon in contact with its distal phalanx insertion, allowing scar tissue healing. Hyperextension of the DIP beyond neutral (0 degrees) is not required and may cause dorsal skin pressure ulcers or compromise blood supply. The PIP joint is left free and should be actively exercised to prevent stiffness. Dynamic splinting is not standard for mallet finger. Buddy taping is insufficient to maintain DIP extension and does not allow healing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mallet finger = DIP extension splint at 0 degrees continuously for 6-8 weeks; PIP joint left free. COMLEX: Any lapse in DIP extension splinting (even briefly for cleaning) restarts the 6-8 week healing clock.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: bony mallet finger requires fracture management principles when avulsion fragment exceeds one-third of the articular surface"},{"id":"sdl15_q32","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of extensor finger injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old man presents 10 days after sustaining a mallet finger of the right long finger while playing basketball. He has been using buddy tape since the injury. Radiographs show a dorsal avulsion fracture involving approximately 15% of the DIP articular surface without volar subluxation. The DIP joint rests in 40 degrees of flexion. What is the most appropriate management at this time?","choices":{"A":"The 10-day delay makes conservative splinting ineffective; immediate surgical pin fixation is now required","B":"The avulsion fracture size mandates surgical ORIF regardless of delay to restore articular congruence","C":"Obtain MRI to assess soft tissue terminal extensor integrity before committing to surgical vs conservative care","D":"Apply a DIP extension splint now and treat conservatively with continuous splinting for 8 weeks from this visit","E":"Apply a short arm cast with the wrist in extension and DIP in neutral for 6 weeks to off-load the extensor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Mallet finger presenting 10 days after injury can still be managed conservatively with DIP extension splinting, provided the fracture involves less than one-third of the articular surface and there is no volar subluxation of the distal phalanx. A 15% articular surface avulsion fragment without subluxation meets these criteria. The splinting period is calculated from the time splinting begins — so 8 weeks of continuous splinting from this visit is appropriate. Surgical indications are: fragment > 1/3 articular surface, volar subluxation, or open injury. A short delay in presentation does not automatically mandate surgery. MRI is not needed when radiographs provide sufficient information for surgical decision-making.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Conservative mallet finger criteria — fragment less than 1/3 articular surface AND no volar subluxation, regardless of delay. Splinting duration starts from when splinting begins. COMLEX: Counsel patient that any DIP flexion during the 8 weeks restarts the clock.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: articular surface involvement criteria determine conservative vs operative management for intraarticular avulsion fractures"},{"id":"sdl15_q33","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of extensor finger injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A chronic mallet finger deformity that is left untreated for more than 3 months can lead to a secondary deformity affecting the PIP joint. What is this secondary deformity and what causes it?","choices":{"A":"Boutonniere deformity, in which the central slip tears at the PIP joint and causes the PIP to flex and DIP to extend","B":"Swan neck deformity, in which the lateral bands migrate dorsally and hyperextend the PIP joint as the volar plate becomes lax","C":"Trigger finger deformity, in which the lateral bands tighten the flexor sheath at the A1 pulley and cause PIP locking","D":"Mallet deformity extension proximally, in which the extensor tendon attrition spreads from the DIP to the PIP level","E":"Z-deformity of the finger, in which the extensor mechanism imbalance causes alternating flexion and extension at each joint"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chronic untreated mallet finger can lead to swan neck deformity. As the DIP rests in flexion chronically, the extensor mechanism proximal to the DIP becomes slack. The lateral bands — normally held in position by the triangular ligament — can migrate dorsally at the PIP joint level, creating a tethering force that hyperextends the PIP joint. The volar plate, which normally prevents PIP hyperextension, stretches and becomes lax over time. The resulting swan neck deformity has DIP flexion and PIP hyperextension. Boutonniere deformity involves central slip disruption causing PIP flexion and DIP extension — the opposite pattern. Recognizing swan neck as the secondary complication of untreated mallet finger is a board-tested correlation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Untreated mallet finger → swan neck deformity (DIP flexion + PIP hyperextension) from dorsal lateral band migration. Boutonniere = central slip rupture → PIP flexion + DIP extension. COMLEX: Swan neck deformity is also seen in RA; distinguish by history.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: extensor mechanism anatomy at the DIP and PIP levels is foundational to understanding secondary deformities from untreated bony and soft-tissue injuries"},{"id":"sdl15_q34","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of flexor tendon injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old college football player presents to the ER after his ring finger was caught in an opponent's jersey while tackling. He reports pain at the base of the finger and inability to curl it. Exam reveals no active DIP flexion of the ring finger, though passive DIP flexion is intact and PIP flexion is maintained. There is tenderness at the volar aspect of the distal phalanx. What is the most likely injury?","choices":{"A":"Complete rupture of the flexor digitorum superficialis at the PIP level, causing inability to flex both the PIP and DIP joints","B":"Partial tear of the flexor digitorum profundus at the A2 pulley level, with preserved terminal tendon continuity","C":"Avulsion of the terminal extensor tendon from the dorsal distal phalanx, causing the DIP to drop into flexion","D":"Avulsion of the flexor digitorum profundus from its insertion on the volar distal phalanx, causing inability to flex the DIP joint","E":"Entrapment of the flexor digitorum profundus beneath the A4 pulley causing pseudoparalysis of the DIP joint"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Jersey finger is caused by forcible extension of the DIP joint against active FDP contraction, avulsing the FDP tendon from its volar distal phalanx insertion. The ring finger is affected in 75% of cases. Because the FDP is the only tendon that flexes the DIP, its avulsion results in inability to actively flex the DIP while PIP flexion is preserved (FDS is intact). Passive DIP flexion is maintained because the joint itself is uninjured. Tenderness at the volar distal phalanx corresponds to the avulsion site. FDS rupture would affect PIP flexion. Mallet finger avulses the extensor tendon and causes DIP flexion deformity, not flexion loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Jersey finger = FDP avulsion from distal phalanx; ring finger most common; cannot actively flex DIP; FDS intact so PIP flexion preserved. COMLEX: Contrast with mallet finger — same joint, opposite tendon, opposite deformity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: tendon avulsion injuries from forcible eccentric loading in athletic contexts parallel FDP avulsion mechanism in jersey finger"},{"id":"sdl15_q35","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of flexor tendon injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An 18-year-old wrestler is diagnosed with a Leddy-Packer Type II jersey finger of the right ring finger, 8 days after the injury. On radiographs, no bony fragment is seen. Which statement about this injury type and its timing of repair is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Type II involves FDP retraction into the palm with both vincula ruptured, requiring surgery within 3-5 days to prevent necrosis","B":"Type II involves a bony avulsion fragment at the A4 pulley level, and repair can be delayed up to 3 months without tendon necrosis","C":"Type II involves FDP avulsion with intact A2 pulley that prevents any proximal tendon retraction beyond the midshaft","D":"Type II is the rarest subtype and involves simultaneous FDS and FDP avulsion at the DIP joint level","E":"Type II involves FDP retraction to the PIP joint level with intact short vinculum, allowing repair within 1-2 weeks of injury"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In the Leddy-Packer classification, Type II jersey finger occurs when the FDP tendon retracts to the PIP joint level, where it is held by the intact short vinculum (long vinculum is ruptured). Because the short vinculum maintains some blood supply to the tendon, the tendon is viable for a longer window — repair is typically feasible within 1-2 weeks of injury. Type I (retraction into palm) disrupts both vincula and requires surgery within days. Type III involves a bony avulsion fragment large enough to catch at the A4 pulley, retaining blood supply and allowing surgical repair even after a longer delay. The absence of a radiographic bony fragment in this case makes Type III less likely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Leddy-Packer — Type I: palm retraction, both vincula gone, urgent surgery within days. Type II: PIP level, short vinculum intact, repair within 1-2 weeks. Type III: bony fragment at A4, delayed repair possible. COMLEX: Timing of repair determines tendon viability.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: vascular supply to avulsed tendons determines surgical urgency — same principle applies to Leddy-Packer classification of jersey finger"},{"id":"sdl15_q36","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of flexor tendon injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 25-year-old rugby player presents 3 weeks after sustaining a jersey finger of the right ring finger that was never diagnosed. He now has a small firm mass at the proximal palm and cannot flex his DIP at all. MRI confirms the FDP tendon has retracted into the palm with disruption of both vincula. What is the expected finding at surgery, and what does this mean for repair options?","choices":{"A":"The tendon is supple and can be pulled back through the intact sheath for primary repair despite the 3-week delay","B":"Tendon fibrosis and retraction make primary repair impossible; tendon grafting or two-stage reconstruction is required","C":"The tendon has healed onto the lumbrical origin in the palm and can be re-routed through the digital canal primarily","D":"The A4 pulley has remodeled and widened in the absence of the tendon, allowing graft passage without pulley reconstruction","E":"Spontaneous healing of the FDP tendon to the A2 pulley has occurred, and DIP arthrodesis is therefore unnecessary"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When a Type I jersey finger (FDP retracted into palm with both vincula disrupted) is diagnosed after 3 weeks, the tendon has undergone significant fibrosis, shortening, and loss of elasticity. The tendon cannot be retrieved and primarily repaired through the intact digital flexor sheath without prohibitive tension and risk of sheath rupture. Surgical options at this stage include tendon grafting (one-stage if sheath is supple) or two-stage reconstruction (Hunter rod placement first, then graft). In some patients, DIP arthrodesis in a functional position is preferred for its reliability. The firm palmar mass corresponds to the fibrosed, retracted FDP tendon end. Primary repair is not feasible after this delay in Type I injuries.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Neglected Type I jersey finger (greater than 3 weeks) = tendon fibrosis; primary repair not possible; requires tendon grafting or two-stage reconstruction. COMLEX: Delayed jersey finger is a common board scenario — know the time-sensitive Type I window.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: delayed avulsion repair leads to fibrosis and requires staged reconstruction — principle applies to neglected jersey finger"}]},{"sdlNumber":16,"title":"Upper Extremity Fractures","questions":[{"id":"sdl16_q01","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of humerus fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A midshaft humerus fracture in the spiral groove is most commonly associated with injury to which nerve, and what is the classic clinical deficit?","choices":{"A":"Axillary nerve, causing loss of deltoid function and shoulder abduction deficit","B":"Median nerve, causing loss of thumb opposition and thenar muscle wasting","C":"Radial nerve, causing wrist drop from loss of wrist and finger extension","D":"Ulnar nerve, causing claw hand deformity and loss of intrinsic hand muscle function","E":"Musculocutaneous nerve, causing loss of elbow flexion and forearm supination"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The radial nerve travels through the spiral groove of the midshaft humerus and is the most commonly injured nerve with midshaft humerus fractures. Injury produces wrist drop — inability to extend the wrist and fingers — due to paralysis of the extensor muscles of the forearm. The axillary nerve, in contrast, is injured with surgical neck fractures of the proximal humerus, producing deltoid weakness and loss of shoulder abduction. Median nerve injury at the elbow level is associated with supracondylar fractures. Ulnar nerve injury occurs with medial epicondyle fractures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Midshaft humerus = radial nerve = wrist drop. Surgical neck = axillary nerve = deltoid weakness. Supracondylar = anterior interosseous nerve (median branch). COMLEX: Radial nerve runs in the spiral groove; Holstein-Lewis fracture is a distal third spiral fracture with especially high radial nerve injury risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — Rotator cuff injuries and shoulder pathologies: surgical neck of humerus fractures damage the axillary nerve in the same anatomical region"},{"id":"sdl16_q02","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of humerus fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A proximal humerus fracture at the surgical neck is most likely to injure which nerve, and what is the primary clinical deficit?","choices":{"A":"Radial nerve, producing wrist drop and loss of finger extension distal to the elbow","B":"Suprascapular nerve, producing weakness of both supraspinatus and infraspinatus with external rotation loss","C":"Long thoracic nerve, producing winging of the scapula due to serratus anterior paralysis","D":"Axillary nerve, producing weakness of deltoid contraction and loss of active shoulder abduction","E":"Median nerve, producing loss of thumb opposition and flexor pollicis longus weakness"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The axillary nerve runs around the surgical neck of the humerus in the quadrilateral space, making it vulnerable to injury with proximal humerus fractures at this location. Axillary nerve injury causes weakness or paralysis of the deltoid muscle, with loss of active shoulder abduction. There is also loss of sensation over the lateral deltoid region (regimental badge area). Proximal humerus fractures are especially common in older women, accounting for 4-5% of all fractures. Radial nerve injury is associated with midshaft fractures, not surgical neck. Suprascapular nerve injury occurs with shoulder dislocations or space-occupying lesions at the spinoglenoid notch.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Surgical neck humerus fracture = axillary nerve injury = deltoid weakness + lateral shoulder numbness (regimental badge area). COMLEX: Also test for axillary nerve injury after anterior shoulder dislocation, which can also stretch the nerve.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — Rotator cuff injuries and shoulder pathologies: surgical neck of humerus fractures damage the axillary nerve in the same anatomical region"},{"id":"sdl16_q03","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of humerus fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the initial management of a non-displaced proximal humerus fracture in a 75-year-old woman?","choices":{"A":"Open reduction and internal fixation is indicated because osteoporosis prevents adequate fracture stability","B":"Shoulder arthroplasty is the preferred initial treatment for all proximal humerus fractures in older adults","C":"Percutaneous pinning under fluoroscopy is the standard of care for all proximal humerus fractures","D":"Immediate surgical repair is required for all proximal humerus fractures to prevent axillary nerve injury","E":"Non-surgical management with a sling or coaptation splint and early gentle range-of-motion exercises"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The majority of proximal humerus fractures are non-displaced and can be managed non-surgically with a sling or coaptation splint, followed by early supervised gentle range-of-motion exercises to prevent shoulder stiffness. This is especially true in older adults where surgical risks may outweigh benefits. Displaced and/or comminuted fractures may require surgical intervention — including ORIF or shoulder arthroplasty — depending on patient factors and fracture pattern. Routine surgery for all proximal humerus fractures regardless of displacement is not indicated and may lead to unnecessary morbidity in the elderly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Non-displaced proximal and midshaft humerus fractures → conservative management with sling. Displaced or comminuted → consider surgery. COMLEX: Sling for 4-6 weeks, then progressive PT; early motion prevents frozen shoulder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — Rotator cuff injuries and shoulder pathologies: surgical neck of humerus fractures damage the axillary nerve in the same anatomical region"},{"id":"sdl16_q04","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of supracondylar fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A Gartland Type III supracondylar humerus fracture in a 5-year-old is defined by which radiographic feature, and what is the standard treatment?","choices":{"A":"Non-displaced fracture with effusion and normal alignment; treated with long arm splinting alone","B":"Fracture displaced greater than 2 mm with anterior humeral line disruption; treated with closed reduction only","C":"Fracture with medial cortical comminution; treated with open reduction and internal fixation immediately","D":"Complete displacement of the distal fragment; treated with urgent closed reduction and percutaneous pinning","E":"Stress fracture of the distal humerus without cortical disruption; treated with observation and activity restriction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gartland Type III supracondylar fractures are completely displaced with no cortical contact between fracture fragments. They require urgent orthopedic consultation for closed reduction and percutaneous Kirschner wire fixation (CRPP) under general anesthesia. The urgency is driven by the risk of neurovascular injury — particularly to the brachial artery and anterior interosseous nerve — and the risk of compartment syndrome. Type I fractures are minimally displaced and treated with splinting; Type II fractures are displaced more than 2 mm with intact posterior cortex and may be treated with casting or CRPP depending on stability.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gartland I = splint; Type II = cast vs CRPP; Type III = CRPP urgently. COMLEX: AIN (anterior interosseous nerve, branch of median) is the most commonly injured nerve; \"OK sign\" inability tests AIN.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow: medial epicondyle anatomy is directly relevant to nerve injury patterns in pediatric elbow fractures"},{"id":"sdl16_q05","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of supracondylar fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which nerve is most commonly injured in a pediatric supracondylar humerus fracture, and what is the classic clinical test for its function?","choices":{"A":"Radial nerve, tested by asking the child to extend the wrist and fingers against resistance","B":"Ulnar nerve, tested by asking the child to abduct and adduct the fingers against resistance","C":"Anterior interosseous nerve, tested by asking the child to make an \"OK\" sign with the thumb and index finger","D":"Axillary nerve, tested by assessing sensation over the lateral deltoid and deltoid contraction strength","E":"Median nerve proper, tested by asking the child to oppose the thumb to the little finger"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The anterior interosseous nerve (AIN), a purely motor branch of the median nerve, is the most commonly injured nerve in supracondylar humerus fractures. AIN injury produces inability to flex the terminal phalanx of the thumb and index finger, so the child cannot make a perfect \"OK\" sign — instead producing a pinch pattern with the interphalangeal joints extended rather than flexed. The AIN also innervates the flexor pollicis longus, the flexor digitorum profundus to the index/middle fingers, and pronator quadratus. Radial nerve and ulnar nerve injuries occur less frequently but should also be assessed. Brachial artery injury is the feared vascular complication.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Supracondylar fracture — AIN (median branch) most commonly injured; cannot make \"OK\" sign. COMLEX: Also assess radial pulse and brachial artery perfusion; \"pink pulseless hand\" after reduction may be observed if perfused via collaterals.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow: medial epicondyle anatomy is directly relevant to nerve injury patterns in pediatric elbow fractures"},{"id":"sdl16_q06","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of supracondylar fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Supracondylar fractures account for what proportion of pediatric elbow fractures, and what is the most common mechanism of injury?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 20% of pediatric elbow fractures; most result from a direct blow to the posterior elbow during contact sports","B":"Approximately 80% of pediatric elbow fractures; most result from a throwing motion with sudden valgus overload at the elbow","C":"Approximately 40% of pediatric elbow fractures; most result from axial loading during falls in a flexed elbow position","D":"Approximately 10% of pediatric elbow fractures; most result from forced hyperextension during gymnastics tumbling","E":"Approximately 60% of pediatric elbow fractures; most result from a fall on an outstretched hand with the elbow in extension"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Supracondylar humerus fractures represent approximately 60% of all pediatric elbow fractures, making them the most common elbow fracture in children. They are most prevalent between ages 2 and 7 and are rare in adults. The predominant mechanism is a fall on an outstretched hand (FOOSH) with the elbow extended, which occurs in about 70% of cases. The non-dominant extremity is more commonly affected. The hyperextension force drives the olecranon into the olecranon fossa, levering the distal humerus and fracturing the thin supracondylar region of the bone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Supracondylar fracture = 60% of pediatric elbow fractures; peak age 2-7; FOOSH with elbow extended. COMLEX: Supracondylar fracture is rare in adults because the bone remodels and becomes stronger past the pediatric growth phase.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow: medial epicondyle anatomy is directly relevant to nerve injury patterns in pediatric elbow fractures"},{"id":"sdl16_q07","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of clavicular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which segment of the clavicle is fractured in the majority of clavicle fractures, and what proportion does it represent?","choices":{"A":"Distal third of the clavicle near the AC joint, representing approximately 69% of all clavicle fractures","B":"Proximal third of the clavicle near the sternoclavicular joint, representing approximately 50% of all fractures","C":"Junction of the middle and distal thirds, representing approximately 40% of all clavicle fractures","D":"Middle third of the clavicle, representing approximately 69% of all clavicle fractures","E":"Entire clavicle with comminution, representing the majority of fractures in high-energy trauma patients"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Clavicle fractures occur most commonly in the middle third of the clavicle, accounting for approximately 69% of all cases. The distal third accounts for about 28% and the proximal third for only 2.8%. The middle third is vulnerable because it is the thinnest part of the clavicle and lacks the support of the acromioclavicular and sternoclavicular ligaments that stabilize the distal and proximal thirds, respectively. Middle-third fractures result from direct blows or indirect forces transmitted through the shoulder. Clavicle fractures represent about 2.6% of all fractures and peak in children and young adults.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Clavicle fracture location — middle 1/3 = 69%, distal 1/3 = 28%, proximal 1/3 = 2.8%. COMLEX: Middle-third is the most common because it lacks ligamentous support that stabilizes the other thirds.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — Diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain: clavicle fractures enter the shoulder pain differential and are distinguished by location and crepitus"},{"id":"sdl16_q08","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of clavicular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the first-line treatment for a non-displaced, closed, mid-shaft clavicle fracture in a 25-year-old with no neurovascular compromise?","choices":{"A":"A sling for comfort and pain control, with progressive return to activity as healing occurs","B":"Surgical ORIF with plate fixation because clavicle fractures have high non-union rates without surgery","C":"Figure-of-eight bandage applied in the ER, which is superior to sling for anatomic reduction","D":"Rigid shoulder immobilizer combined with intravenous antibiotics to prevent periosteal infection","E":"Immediate orthopedic referral for closed reduction under fluoroscopy before beginning conservative care"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The vast majority of non-displaced, closed, mid-shaft clavicle fractures are managed conservatively with a sling for comfort and analgesics. A sling is preferred over a figure-of-eight bandage, which does not improve outcomes and causes more patient discomfort. Surgery is not routinely required for non-displaced fractures. Indications for orthopedic referral include open fractures, skin tenting, neurovascular compromise, complete displacement, shortening greater than 2 cm, comminution, or patients who are athletes concerned about cosmetic deformity. Most mid-shaft fractures heal well with conservative management.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Non-displaced mid-shaft clavicle fracture = sling; figure-of-eight bandage is not superior to sling. Surgery for open, displaced, comminuted, neurovascular compromise, or shortening. COMLEX: Sling preferred over figure-of-eight — boards commonly test this distinction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — Diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain: clavicle fractures enter the shoulder pain differential and are distinguished by location and crepitus"},{"id":"sdl16_q09","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of clavicular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which finding in a clavicle fracture is an indication for urgent orthopedic referral rather than conservative management with a sling?","choices":{"A":"Tenderness at the fracture site on palpation, which is expected in all clavicle fractures regardless of severity","B":"Radiographic evidence of a distal third fracture, which is always managed with surgery due to AC joint proximity","C":"A patient age greater than 60 years, because osteoporosis prevents adequate healing without internal fixation","D":"Fracture occurring during a recreational sport activity, which indicates higher energy and mandates surgical evaluation","E":"Tenting of the skin overlying the fracture site, indicating risk of skin breakdown and imminent open fracture"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Skin tenting — where the sharp fracture end pushes against the overlying skin from inside — indicates imminent risk of skin penetration and conversion to an open fracture, which carries infection risk and requires urgent orthopedic consultation. Other indications for referral include open fractures, neurovascular compromise (including brachial plexus and subclavian vessel injury), complete displacement, shortening, comminution, and in athletes where cosmetic outcome or return-to-sport timeline matters. Routine mid-shaft tenderness, patient age, and recreational mechanism do not automatically mandate surgery. Distal-third fractures with AC ligament disruption may require surgery but are not automatically referred.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Clavicle ortho referral = open fractures, tenting, neurovascular compromise, complete displacement, shortening, comminution. COMLEX: Neurovascular structures at risk include the brachial plexus and subclavian artery/vein deep to the clavicle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — Diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain: clavicle fractures enter the shoulder pain differential and are distinguished by location and crepitus"},{"id":"sdl16_q10","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of forearm fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Forearm fractures are the most common fractures in children, representing what proportion of all childhood fractures?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 10-15% of all childhood fractures, with the proximal third being the most frequent location","B":"Approximately 40-50% of all childhood fractures, with the distal third being the most frequent location","C":"Approximately 20-25% of all childhood fractures, with the midshaft being the most frequent location","D":"Approximately 60-70% of all childhood fractures, with the entire forearm equally distributed by region","E":"Approximately 5% of all childhood fractures, with forearm fractures less common than femur or tibia fractures"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Forearm fractures are the most common fractures in children, accounting for approximately 40-50% of all childhood fractures. The distal third of the forearm is the most commonly fractured segment, responsible for approximately 75% of all forearm fractures in children and 20-25% of all pediatric fractures. The peak age is 10-14 years, when a relative mismatch between rapidly increasing body mass and bone strength creates vulnerability during the adolescent growth spurt. Falls on an outstretched hand (FOOSH) are the predominant mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Forearm fractures = most common childhood fracture (40-50%); distal 1/3 most common (75% of forearm); peak age 10-14. COMLEX: Without plausible mechanism in a child, forearm fracture warrants consideration of non-accidental trauma (child abuse).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — Differences between adult and pediatric bone stress: buckle, greenstick, and physeal fractures occur in children due to differences in bone flexibility and growth plate vulnerability"},{"id":"sdl16_q11","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of forearm fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A buckle (torus) fracture of the distal radius in a 7-year-old occurs because of which property of pediatric bone that distinguishes it from adult cortical fracture patterns?","choices":{"A":"Pediatric bone has thinner cortices, causing the periosteum to tear completely with any compressive load","B":"Pediatric bone lacks the medullary cavity found in adult bone, concentrating all stress at the periosteal surface","C":"Pediatric bone has open physes that absorb all compressive load before the metaphyseal cortex is stressed","D":"Pediatric bone is more porous and flexible, compressing on the compressive side without complete cortical disruption","E":"Pediatric bone has a higher mineral content than adult bone, causing it to buckle rather than plastically deform"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Buckle (torus) fractures occur in children because pediatric bone is more porous and compliant than adult bone. When axial compressive force is applied, the cortex buckles or wrinkles on the compressive side without complete fracture through the cortex. This produces a stable fracture that does not displace significantly. Greenstick fractures involve incomplete cortical disruption on the tensile side while the compressive cortex remains intact. Both injury patterns are unique to children due to the relative plasticity of their bone. In contrast, adult cortical bone is more brittle and more likely to produce complete fracture patterns.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Buckle (torus) = cortex buckling on compressive side; greenstick = incomplete fracture on tension side with intact cortex on compression side; both pediatric due to bone plasticity. COMLEX: Buckle fractures are stable and heal well with splinting alone.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — Differences between adult and pediatric bone stress: buckle, greenstick, and physeal fractures occur in children due to differences in bone flexibility and growth plate vulnerability"},{"id":"sdl16_q12","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of forearm fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A Monteggia fracture involves which combination of injuries, and what distinguishes it from a Galeazzi fracture?","choices":{"A":"Monteggia = proximal ulna fracture with radial head dislocation; Galeazzi = distal radius fracture with distal radioulnar joint dislocation","B":"Monteggia = distal radius fracture with volar angulation; Galeazzi = distal ulna fracture with radial head subluxation","C":"Monteggia = midshaft radius fracture with interosseous membrane rupture; Galeazzi = ulna fracture with olecranon avulsion","D":"Monteggia = both-bone forearm fracture with posterior interosseous nerve injury; Galeazzi = isolated radius fracture with DRUJ stability","E":"Monteggia = radial head fracture with ulnar styloid avulsion; Galeazzi = scaphoid fracture with radial styloid impaction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A Monteggia fracture is a fracture of the proximal ulna combined with dislocation of the radial head at the radiocapitellar joint. A Galeazzi fracture is a fracture of the distal radius combined with disruption of the distal radioulnar joint (DRUJ). A useful mnemonic is \"MUGR\" — Monteggia = Ulna fracture, Galeazzi = Radius fracture. Both are considered unstable injury patterns that typically require surgical fixation. Monteggia fractures are associated with radial nerve (posterior interosseous nerve) injury due to the radial head dislocation. Both injuries can occur in children and adults and should not be confused with isolated radial or ulnar shaft fractures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Monteggia = proximal Ulna + radial head dislocation; Galeazzi = distal Radius + DRUJ dislocation. Mnemonic: MUG (Monteggia-Ulna) + GR (Galeazzi-Radius). COMLEX: Both require surgery; isolated ulna or radius shaft fractures in adults also usually require fixation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — Differences between adult and pediatric bone stress: buckle, greenstick, and physeal fractures occur in children due to differences in bone flexibility and growth plate vulnerability"},{"id":"sdl16_q13","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of hand and wrist fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which property of the scaphoid bone makes it uniquely prone to avascular necrosis (AVN) following a fracture?","choices":{"A":"The scaphoid is the densest carpal bone, requiring greater forces to fracture and causing more vascular disruption","B":"The scaphoid spans two carpal rows, making it subject to greater shear forces that disrupt all entering blood vessels simultaneously","C":"The scaphoid is entirely covered by articular cartilage except at the tubercle, leaving minimal periosteal vascularity","D":"The scaphoid waist is avascular in all individuals due to developmental absence of nutrient vessels in this region","E":"The scaphoid receives its blood supply in a retrograde fashion from distal to proximal, making the proximal pole dependent on the distal vasculature"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The scaphoid receives its primary blood supply through branches of the radial artery that enter distally at the dorsal ridge and waist, traveling proximally in a retrograde fashion to supply the proximal pole. A fracture through the scaphoid waist or proximal pole disrupts this blood supply to the proximal fragment, which has no independent arterial supply. This makes the proximal pole susceptible to avascular necrosis if blood flow is not restored. This also explains why proximal pole fractures carry the highest AVN risk and require the longest immobilization. Initial radiographs are often negative because the fracture line is subtle, and MRI is the most sensitive early imaging modality.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Scaphoid = retrograde blood supply → proximal pole AVN risk after waist/proximal fracture. Initial X-rays often negative — MRI is gold standard. COMLEX: Anatomic snuffbox tenderness after FOOSH = presumed scaphoid fracture; treat as fracture even with negative X-ray.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — Carpal tunnel syndrome: acute distal radius fractures can cause median nerve compression and acute carpal tunnel syndrome as a complication"},{"id":"sdl16_q14","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of hand and wrist fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A Boxer's fracture involves a fracture of which bone and at which location?","choices":{"A":"The neck of the fifth metacarpal, typically resulting from striking a hard surface with a clenched fist","B":"The shaft of the fourth metacarpal, resulting from direct axial loading during a punch with the ring finger knuckle","C":"The head of the second metacarpal, resulting from a hyperextension injury of the index MCP joint","D":"The base of the fifth metacarpal, resulting from an inversion injury of the ulnar wrist with associated hamate impaction","E":"The proximal phalanx of the fifth finger, resulting from a valgus stress fracture at the small finger MCP joint"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A Boxer's fracture is a fracture of the neck of the fifth metacarpal, which occurs when a clenched fist strikes a hard surface. The fracture produces volar angulation of the metacarpal head. It is one of the most common metacarpal fractures in adults. In clinical practice, the commonly accepted threshold for conservative management without surgical intervention is angulation up to 40-70 degrees — depending on the institution — given the fifth metacarpal's mobility at the carpometacarpal joint, which compensates for some residual angulation. Neurovascular assessment and rotational alignment must be checked. Malrotation, unlike angulation, is not compensated and requires reduction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Boxer's fracture = 5th metacarpal neck, volar angulation; conservative if <40-70 degrees angulation. COMLEX: Always assess rotational alignment — malrotation (scissors deformity with fingers overlapping on flexion) requires reduction regardless of angulation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — Carpal tunnel syndrome: acute distal radius fractures can cause median nerve compression and acute carpal tunnel syndrome as a complication"},{"id":"sdl16_q15","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of hand and wrist fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A young athlete presents with wrist pain after a FOOSH. Radiographs are negative but anatomic snuffbox tenderness is present. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Discharge with a wrist brace and return precautions because negative radiographs effectively exclude scaphoid fracture","B":"Treat as a presumed scaphoid fracture with thumb spica splinting and arrange advanced imaging with MRI or CT to confirm the fracture","C":"Immobilize and repeat radiographs in 5-7 days as the fracture line will invariably become visible by then on plain film","D":"Refer immediately to hand surgery for urgent ORIF because scaphoid fractures always require surgical fixation","E":"Perform immediate wrist aspiration to evaluate for hemarthrosis, which confirms intraarticular carpal bone injury"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Scaphoid fractures are notoriously missed on initial radiographs — the fracture line can be occult on plain film for up to 2 weeks. However, anatomic snuffbox tenderness after a FOOSH is the classic clinical sign of a scaphoid fracture and should prompt immediate empiric treatment. The standard approach is to apply a thumb spica splint and obtain advanced imaging — MRI is the gold standard for early diagnosis, with CT as an alternative. Simply discharging the patient or waiting for repeat films risks delayed diagnosis, AVN, and nonunion. Not all scaphoid fractures require surgery — undisplaced waist fractures can often be managed conservatively with casting.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Scaphoid = anatomic snuffbox tenderness after FOOSH; X-ray often negative; MRI gold standard. Treat empirically with thumb spica even if X-ray negative. COMLEX: Delayed diagnosis leads to nonunion and AVN of proximal pole — key complication tested on boards.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — Carpal tunnel syndrome: acute distal radius fractures can cause median nerve compression and acute carpal tunnel syndrome as a complication"},{"id":"sdl16_q16","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of humerus fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman with osteoporosis falls onto her outstretched left arm. She presents holding her arm against her torso, with pain and swelling at the proximal left humerus. Radiographs reveal a non-displaced surgical neck fracture. On neurologic exam, there is weakness of deltoid contraction and decreased sensation over the lateral shoulder. Which structure is most likely injured?","choices":{"A":"Axillary nerve, which wraps around the surgical neck of the humerus and innervates the deltoid and teres minor","B":"Radial nerve at the spiral groove, which produces wrist drop and loss of sensation over the dorsal hand","C":"Brachial plexus upper trunk, which produces global arm weakness affecting all muscles from the shoulder to the hand","D":"Suprascapular nerve at the suprascapular notch, which impairs both supraspinatus and infraspinatus function","E":"Long thoracic nerve along the lateral chest wall, which produces scapular winging during wall push-up testing"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The axillary nerve originates from the posterior cord of the brachial plexus and wraps around the surgical neck of the humerus in the quadrilateral space. Proximal humerus fractures at the surgical neck stretch or tear the axillary nerve, producing weakness or paralysis of the deltoid and teres minor muscles. Clinically, this manifests as loss of active shoulder abduction and decreased sensation over the lateral deltoid region (regimental badge area). Proximal humerus fractures are particularly common in older osteoporotic women and represent the third most common fracture in this population. Radial nerve injury with wrist drop occurs at the midshaft, not surgical neck level.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Surgical neck fracture = axillary nerve = deltoid weakness + lateral shoulder sensory loss. Proximal humerus = 3rd most common fracture in older adults. COMLEX: Axillary nerve injury also tested after anterior shoulder dislocation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — Rotator cuff injuries and shoulder pathologies: axillary nerve innervates teres minor alongside the deltoid; injury impairs both shoulder abduction and external rotation accessory strength"},{"id":"sdl16_q17","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of humerus fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 27-year-old man sustains a midshaft humerus fracture in a motorcycle accident. On exam he is unable to extend his wrist, and the dorsal surface of the hand has decreased sensation. He can flex the elbow normally and has intact grip strength. Radiographs show a spiral fracture through the midshaft. What is the most likely injury?","choices":{"A":"Anterior interosseous nerve injury, causing inability to make the OK sign with preserved wrist extension","B":"Radial nerve injury in the spiral groove, causing wrist drop and dorsal hand sensory loss with intact median and ulnar nerve function","C":"Posterior interosseous nerve injury at the supinator, causing finger extension loss but preserved radial wrist extension","D":"Musculocutaneous nerve injury, causing loss of forearm flexion and supination with preserved wrist and hand function","E":"Axillary nerve injury at the quadrilateral space, causing deltoid weakness and preserved elbow, wrist, and hand function"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The radial nerve travels in the spiral groove of the midshaft humerus, making it the most commonly injured nerve in midshaft humerus fractures. Injury produces classic wrist drop — inability to extend the wrist and MCP joints — due to paralysis of the wrist and finger extensors. Sensory loss occurs over the dorsum of the hand and proximal dorsal thumb web space. Elbow flexion is preserved because biceps and brachialis (musculocutaneous nerve) are unaffected. Grip strength is preserved because finger flexors (median and ulnar nerves) are intact. AIN injury is more distal and causes loss of DIP flexion, not wrist drop. Posterior interosseous nerve injury spares radial wrist extension (ECRL) because ECRL receives a branch proximal to the supinator.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Midshaft spiral humerus fracture = radial nerve = wrist drop + dorsal hand sensory loss. COMLEX: Holstein-Lewis fracture (distal 1/3 spiral) has highest radial nerve injury risk; initial management is conservative because most radial nerve palsies recover spontaneously.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — Rotator cuff injuries and shoulder pathologies: nerve anatomy around the humerus determines which structure is at risk based on fracture location — surgical neck vs spiral groove vs supracondylar region"},{"id":"sdl16_q18","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of humerus fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with a non-displaced proximal humerus fracture presents for a 2-week follow-up. He has been in a sling and is asking when he should start moving the shoulder. He has no neurovascular deficits. Which of the following best describes the role of early motion in management?","choices":{"A":"All shoulder motion must be restricted until radiographic healing is complete at 8-12 weeks to prevent displacement","B":"Active range-of-motion exercises against resistance should begin immediately to prevent rotator cuff atrophy","C":"Early gentle pendulum exercises starting within the first 1-2 weeks reduce the risk of shoulder stiffness and adhesive capsulitis without compromising healing","D":"Surgery should be performed within 2 weeks for any proximal humerus fracture in patients over 65 to ensure optimal recovery","E":"Corticosteroid injection into the glenohumeral joint at 2 weeks reduces inflammation and accelerates return to function"},"correct":"C","explanation":"For non-displaced proximal humerus fractures managed conservatively, early supervised gentle pendulum exercises are initiated within the first 1-2 weeks of injury. This is critical to prevent shoulder stiffness and adhesive capsulitis, which are significant complications that can cause long-term functional impairment. Pendulum exercises use gravity to provide gentle traction and rotation of the humeral head within the glenoid. Active assisted range-of-motion exercises are added progressively over the following weeks. Prolonged complete immobilization until radiographic healing (which takes 8-12 weeks) predictably results in stiffness. Surgical intervention is not indicated for non-displaced fractures in otherwise healthy patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Non-displaced proximal humerus fracture → sling + early pendulum exercises within 1-2 weeks → progressive PT. COMLEX: Adhesive capsulitis is a known complication of prolonged immobilization of the shoulder — early motion is key to prevention.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — Frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis): prolonged immobilization of the shoulder after proximal humerus fracture is a recognized precipitating cause of secondary adhesive capsulitis"},{"id":"sdl16_q19","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of supracondylar fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 5-year-old girl is brought to the ED after falling from a playground structure. She is guarding her right elbow, which is swollen and markedly bruised. Radiographs show a Gartland Type III supracondylar humerus fracture with complete displacement. Her radial pulse is absent but the hand appears pink and warm with capillary refill of 2 seconds. What is the appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Immediate vascular surgery consultation and brachial artery exploration before any fracture reduction is attempted","B":"Urgent orthopedic consultation for closed reduction and percutaneous pinning; a perfused pulseless hand may be observed post-reduction if maintained","C":"Apply a long arm cast in flexion immediately and reassess vascular status after 6 hours of observation","D":"Discharge home with sling and urgent outpatient orthopedic follow-up within 48 hours for reduction planning","E":"Perform bedside closed reduction under procedural sedation and reassess pulse before orthopedic consultation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A \"pink pulseless hand\" after a supracondylar fracture — where the hand is perfused via collateral circulation (anterior interosseous and ulnar collateral vessels) despite absent radial pulse — is a recognized entity that does not require emergency vascular surgery before fracture reduction. The standard approach is urgent orthopedic consultation for CRPP, after which the radial pulse typically returns as the brachial artery kinking from fracture displacement is relieved. If the hand is cold, pale, and pulseless after reduction, then vascular surgery exploration is indicated for brachial artery injury. This is an emergency; casting without reduction or discharge is inappropriate for a Type III fracture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pink pulseless hand in supracondylar fracture = urgent CRPP; pulse often returns after reduction. Cold, pale, pulseless hand post-reduction = vascular surgery. COMLEX: Brachial artery is at risk in Type III; compartment syndrome is the feared complication.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow: neurovascular anatomy of the elbow is critical for understanding nerve and vessel injury patterns in pediatric supracondylar fractures"},{"id":"sdl16_q20","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of supracondylar fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy with a Gartland Type I supracondylar fracture has a visible elbow effusion on X-ray but no displacement and normal anterior humeral line. He is otherwise neurovascularly intact. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Closed reduction and percutaneous pinning because all supracondylar fractures in children require operative fixation","B":"Velpeau bandage alone for 6 weeks with no follow-up radiographs because Type I fractures never displace","C":"Observation only with no immobilization because joint effusion without cortical disruption does not require treatment","D":"Open reduction and internal fixation to restore the anterior humeral line and prevent future elbow varus deformity","E":"Long arm posterior splint or cast with the elbow at 90 degrees for 3-4 weeks, with close follow-up"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Gartland Type I supracondylar fractures are non-displaced (displaced less than 2 mm) with an elbow effusion and normal elbow alignment. They are managed conservatively with a long arm posterior splint or cast with the elbow at 90 degrees of flexion for 3-4 weeks. Surgery is not indicated for Type I fractures in the absence of neurovascular compromise or instability. Close radiographic follow-up is appropriate to ensure the fracture does not displace during healing. Observation without immobilization is inadequate. Cubitus varus (gunstock deformity) is a late complication of malunion, most commonly seen after Type III fractures managed with inadequate reduction, not Type I fractures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gartland Type I (non-displaced) = long arm cast 3-4 weeks; no surgery. Type II = CRPP vs cast depending on stability; Type III = urgent CRPP. COMLEX: Cubitus varus is the most common late complication of supracondylar fractures treated with inadequate reduction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow: medial epicondyle anatomy and cubital tunnel are directly involved in elbow fracture deformity complications including progressive ulnar nerve neuropathy from cubitus varus"},{"id":"sdl16_q21","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of supracondylar fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy fell from his bicycle 2 hours ago and was found to have a supracondylar fracture. He is now developing increasing elbow pain out of proportion to the injury, forearm tenseness, and paresthesias in the fingers. Passive finger extension markedly worsens his pain. What is the most likely developing complication?","choices":{"A":"Reflex sympathetic dystrophy of the forearm, which presents with burning pain and is managed with nerve blocks","B":"Heterotopic ossification of the elbow, which presents with progressive stiffness and is managed with NSAIDs","C":"Acute compartment syndrome of the forearm, requiring emergent fasciotomy to prevent permanent ischemic contracture","D":"Osteomyelitis of the distal humerus from a hairline open fracture, managed with IV antibiotics and debridement","E":"Volkmann ischemic contracture that has already occurred and is irreversible without tendon lengthening surgery"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The scenario describes the classic presentation of developing acute compartment syndrome in a child with a supracondylar fracture: pain out of proportion, forearm tenseness, and pain with passive stretch of the fingers (the most reliable early sign). Compartment syndrome after supracondylar fracture most commonly affects the volar forearm compartment and is a surgical emergency requiring fasciotomy. If untreated, ischemia progresses to Volkmann ischemic contracture — a devastating permanent flexion deformity of the wrist and fingers from irreversible muscle fibrosis. This is why supracondylar fractures with neurovascular compromise require emergency consultation rather than elective management.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Compartment syndrome after supracondylar fracture = pain with passive stretch, tenseness, paresthesias → emergent fasciotomy. Delayed = Volkmann contracture. COMLEX: Compartment syndrome and neurovascular compromise are the two emergency ortho consult indications for supracondylar fractures.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow: compartment pressure in the forearm can simultaneously compress ulnar, median, and radial nerves — forearm fasciotomy must release all compartments"},{"id":"sdl16_q22","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of clavicular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old male cyclist is thrown over his handlebars and lands on his right shoulder. He presents with right shoulder pain, a visible step-off deformity at the mid-clavicle, and crepitus on palpation. Radiographs reveal a displaced mid-shaft clavicle fracture. His radial pulse is intact and he has no sensory deficits. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Apply a figure-of-eight bandage immediately in the ER to reduce the fracture and hold the shoulder in retraction","B":"Sling for comfort with analgesics and orthopedic referral for evaluation of surgical candidacy given displacement","C":"Immediate ORIF with plate fixation is required for all displaced clavicle fractures in active young males","D":"Admit for IV antibiotics because the crepitus indicates an occult open fracture requiring operative debridement","E":"Perform closed reduction under procedural sedation in the emergency department and cast the shoulder for 6 weeks"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Displaced mid-shaft clavicle fractures in young active patients may benefit from surgical fixation compared to conservative management, with evidence suggesting lower nonunion rates and faster return to function. However, the initial management is a sling for comfort and pain control, followed by orthopedic evaluation to determine whether surgical fixation (ORIF with plate or intramedullary pin) is appropriate given patient factors such as fracture displacement, shortening, activity level, and occupation. A figure-of-eight bandage does not effectively reduce displaced clavicle fractures and adds patient discomfort without benefit. IV antibiotics are not indicated for a closed fracture. Closed reduction cannot be maintained for clavicle fractures in adults.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Displaced mid-shaft clavicle in young active patient = sling + orthopedic referral for surgical consideration; not all displaced fractures require surgery. COMLEX: Surgical indications include shortening greater than 2 cm, complete displacement, comminution, neurovascular compromise, or athlete/cosmetic concern.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 1 — Diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain: clavicle fractures are distinguished from AC joint separation and shoulder dislocation by palpation at the clavicular shaft rather than AC joint or glenohumeral joint"},{"id":"sdl16_q23","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of clavicular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 16-year-old soccer player sustains a clavicle fracture during a collision. On exam, a sharp fracture fragment is visibly tenting the overlying skin, and the patient has paresthesias along the medial forearm and ulnar two fingers. What neurovascular structures are most at risk in this injury?","choices":{"A":"The axillary nerve and circumflex humeral vessels, which are at risk because of their proximity to the shoulder joint capsule","B":"The radial nerve and radial artery, which pass through the radial groove of the humerus at risk from the clavicular spike","C":"The brachial plexus and subclavian vessels, which run deep to the clavicle between it and the first rib","D":"The phrenic nerve and internal thoracic artery, which run on the anterior surface of the scalene muscles medially","E":"The suprascapular nerve and artery, which cross the superior border of the scapula through the suprascapular notch"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The brachial plexus and subclavian artery and vein run in the costoclavicular space, passing between the clavicle superiorly and the first rib inferiorly. Severely displaced clavicle fractures with skin tenting can impale or compress these structures. Medial forearm and ulnar two-finger paresthesias suggest involvement of the lower trunk or medial cord of the brachial plexus (C8-T1 distribution). Subclavian vein injury can produce significant hemorrhage. These findings represent emergency indications for orthopedic consultation and vascular surgery involvement. The axillary nerve is at risk in proximal humerus fractures and shoulder dislocations, not mid-shaft clavicle fractures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Clavicle fracture neurovascular risk = brachial plexus and subclavian vessels in costoclavicular space. Skin tenting = imminent open fracture = emergent ortho referral. COMLEX: Lower trunk brachial plexus (C8-T1) = medial forearm and hand paresthesias.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 1 — Diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain: clavicle and shoulder anatomy is foundational to identifying neurovascular structures at risk with proximal upper extremity injuries"},{"id":"sdl16_q24","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of clavicular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 14-year-old male baseball player falls and fractures his left clavicle. He is right-hand dominant, his left arm is in a sling, and the fracture is not displaced. His parents ask when he can return to baseball. Which factor is most important in determining return-to-sport timeline?","choices":{"A":"His dominant hand is unaffected, so he may return to batting and pitching within 2 weeks of the injury","B":"He must wait exactly 12 months before any upper extremity sport because clavicle fractures have very high reinjury rates","C":"Pain resolution alone at 3 weeks is sufficient to clear him for full return to pitching and throwing sports","D":"Radiographic and clinical evidence of fracture union, typically at 6-12 weeks, combined with full pain-free range of motion","E":"Surgical fixation is mandatory before return to any contact or overhead sport regardless of fracture displacement"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Return to sport after clavicle fracture requires radiographic evidence of healing combined with full pain-free range of motion and restoration of strength. Most mid-shaft clavicle fractures in young athletes heal within 6-8 weeks, though return to contact or overhead sports typically takes 12-16 weeks. Pain resolution alone does not indicate sufficient structural healing. The non-dominant arm being injured does not permit early return for sports requiring two-arm function or physical contact that could displace the fracture. While some athletes with displaced fractures in the dominant arm may be candidates for surgical fixation to expedite return to sport, this is an individualized decision and not mandatory for all athletes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Return to sport after clavicle fracture requires radiographic union and full painless ROM; typically 6-12 weeks for clinical healing. COMLEX: Athletes may request surgery to shorten return-to-sport timeline — this is an acceptable indication for consideration of ORIF in high-demand athletes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 1 — Diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain: sport-specific return-to-play protocols for shoulder girdle injuries require clinical and radiographic assessment before full clearance"},{"id":"sdl16_q25","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of forearm fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An 8-year-old girl falls off a trampoline onto her outstretched left hand. Radiographs show a buckle (torus) fracture of the distal radius without physeal involvement. She has full neurovascular function and minimal swelling. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Long arm cast with the elbow at 90 degrees for 6-8 weeks because buckle fractures involve the physis and risk growth arrest","B":"Immediate orthopedic referral for percutaneous pinning because all forearm fractures in children under 10 require fixation","C":"Short arm splint or removable wrist splint for 3-4 weeks, with activity restriction and return precautions","D":"No immobilization is needed; buckle fractures are radiographic findings only and do not require treatment","E":"Open reduction under general anesthesia because the periosteum in children is too thick for closed treatment"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Buckle (torus) fractures of the distal radius are inherently stable injuries — the compressed cortex provides structural integrity and the fracture does not significantly displace. They are treated with a short arm splint or removable wrist splint for 3-4 weeks. Long arm casting with elbow flexion is unnecessary as the fracture is stable and the elbow is uninvolved. Surgery is not indicated. Importantly, buckle fractures do not involve the physis (growth plate) and therefore do not carry growth arrest risk — that concern applies to Salter-Harris fractures. A removable splint is increasingly preferred over a cast in stable buckle fractures because it improves patient comfort and hygiene without compromising outcomes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Buckle fracture = stable; short arm splint 3-4 weeks. Greenstick = incomplete cortical disruption, may require gentle manipulation if angulated. Salter-Harris = physeal fracture, growth arrest risk. COMLEX: Distinguish buckle (stable, no physis) from Salter-Harris (physis involved) on boards.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — Differences between adult and pediatric bone stress: buckle fractures are unique to children due to the relative plasticity and porosity of pediatric cortical bone under compressive loads"},{"id":"sdl16_q26","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of forearm fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 12-year-old boy presents after a FOOSH. Radiographs show a fracture through the proximal ulna shaft with anterior dislocation of the radial head at the elbow. Which of the following best describes this injury and its required management?","choices":{"A":"Monteggia fracture; requires ORIF of the ulna fracture, which reliably reduces the radial head if performed anatomically","B":"Galeazzi fracture; requires ORIF of the radius with closed reduction of the distal radioulnar joint under fluoroscopy","C":"Essex-Lopresti injury; requires reduction of the radial head and forearm interosseous membrane repair under general anesthesia","D":"Isolated radial head dislocation without associated fracture; requires closed reduction and posterior interosseous nerve exploration","E":"Both-bone forearm fracture with secondary radial head subluxation; requires ORIF of both radius and ulna simultaneously"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A Monteggia fracture is defined as a fracture of the proximal ulna combined with dislocation of the radial head. In children, it is often managed with closed reduction under general anesthesia; in adults, ORIF of the ulna is typically required. The key principle is that anatomic fixation of the ulna fracture is the primary treatment — once the ulna is restored to proper length and alignment, the radial head typically reduces spontaneously. Failure to recognize the radial head dislocation and treating it as an isolated ulna fracture is a classic missed diagnosis. The posterior interosseous nerve (branch of radial nerve) can be injured with the radial head dislocation. A Galeazzi fracture involves the distal radius and DRUJ, not the proximal ulna.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Monteggia = proximal Ulna fracture + radial head dislocation; ORIF ulna reduces radial head. Galeazzi = distal Radius + DRUJ dislocation. COMLEX: Posterior interosseous nerve at risk with Monteggia; always image the elbow with forearm fractures.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — Differences between adult and pediatric bone stress: Monteggia fractures can be treated with closed reduction in children but typically require ORIF in adults because of differences in bone healing capacity"},{"id":"sdl16_q27","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of forearm fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old boy presents with a distal forearm fracture after falling during basketball. His parents ask why this age group is so prone to forearm fractures. Which explanation is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Teenagers participate in higher-risk sports than younger children, so their fracture rate reflects behavioral rather than biological factors","B":"The distal radius physis closes at age 12-14 in males, leaving a biomechanically weak zone that is uniquely vulnerable until full fusion","C":"Muscle mass increases faster than bone strength during puberty, generating larger compressive forces on the distal forearm during falls","D":"The cartilaginous epiphysis at age 13 is softer than adult bone and more susceptible to shear fractures during torsional loading","E":"During the adolescent growth spurt, bone length increases faster than bone density and mineral content, creating a period of relative skeletal fragility"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The peak incidence of forearm fractures at age 10-14 coincides with the adolescent growth spurt, during which bone length increases rapidly through endochondral ossification at the growth plates. This rapid longitudinal growth temporarily outpaces the accumulation of bone mineral density, creating a period of relative skeletal fragility sometimes called the \"calcium deficit\" of adolescence. The distal radius metaphysis is particularly vulnerable. While behavioral factors (increased athletic activity) contribute, the biological timing of the peak fracture incidence — tracking closely with peak height velocity — demonstrates a true skeletal vulnerability rather than purely behavioral causation. The physis in this age group is still open and is a site of susceptibility for Salter-Harris fractures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Forearm fracture peak age 10-14 = growth spurt with bone mass lagging bone length growth. COMLEX: Body mass vs bone strength imbalance during puberty explains the peak; the growth plate also remains open and is a Salter-Harris fracture risk zone.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — Differences between adult and pediatric bone stress: the adolescent growth spurt creates a transient period of reduced bone mineral density relative to body mass, increasing forearm fracture risk"},{"id":"sdl16_q28","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of hand and wrist fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 22-year-old gymnast presents with radial-sided wrist pain after landing with her wrist hyperextended. She has exquisite tenderness in the anatomic snuffbox. Initial radiographs are read as normal. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Discharge with an elastic wrist brace because normal radiographs effectively exclude a fracture requiring treatment","B":"Apply a thumb spica splint empirically and order an MRI of the wrist to rule out scaphoid fracture, as initial X-rays have 20-30% false-negative rate","C":"Order repeat wrist radiographs in 10-14 days when the fracture line becomes visible as standard of care","D":"Perform immediate surgical screw fixation because all scaphoid fractures in athletes must be operatively stabilized","E":"CT scan of the wrist is preferred over MRI because it offers superior resolution of cortical bone injury in acute fractures"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Scaphoid fractures are the most common carpal fracture and occur classically after a FOOSH with wrist hyperextension. Anatomic snuffbox tenderness (on the dorsal wrist between the APL/EPB and EPL tendons) is the most sensitive clinical sign. Initial radiographs have a false-negative rate of approximately 20-30% because the fracture line may be invisible on plain film acutely. The appropriate response is to splint empirically (thumb spica) and obtain MRI, which is the gold standard for early diagnosis with near-100% sensitivity. While repeat radiographs in 10-14 days is an older approach, MRI is preferred because it eliminates the 2-week wait and allows earlier definitive management. CT is superior to X-ray but less sensitive than MRI for early non-displaced fractures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anatomic snuffbox tenderness + FOOSH = scaphoid fracture until proven otherwise; X-ray often negative; MRI gold standard. COMLEX: Thumb spica splint empirically regardless of X-ray result; missed scaphoid = AVN of proximal pole.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — Carpal tunnel syndrome: the scaphoid forms the floor of the carpal tunnel; displaced scaphoid fractures can acutely compress the median nerve, causing acute carpal tunnel as a complication"},{"id":"sdl16_q29","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of hand and wrist fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with osteoporosis falls on her outstretched right hand. She presents with wrist pain, dorsal swelling, and a visible \"dinner fork\" deformity. Radiographs show a distal radius fracture with dorsal angulation and displacement. What is the name of this fracture pattern and what is the deformity caused by?","choices":{"A":"Colles fracture, in which the distal radius fragment angulates and displaces dorsally, creating a dorsal prominence resembling a dinner fork","B":"Smith fracture, in which the distal radius fragment angulates volarly, creating a volar prominence resembling a reversed dinner fork","C":"Barton fracture, in which an intraarticular rim of the distal radius fractures and displaces with the carpus into the wrist joint","D":"Monteggia fracture, in which the distal radius fractures with concomitant disruption of the proximal radioulnar joint","E":"Chauffeur fracture, in which the radial styloid avulses from a direct blow transmitted through the scaphoid into the radial styloid"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A Colles fracture is a distal radius fracture with dorsal angulation and displacement of the distal fragment, producing the classic \"dinner fork\" deformity — a dorsal prominence just proximal to the wrist on lateral profile. It is the most common fracture in osteoporotic women who fall on an outstretched hand. Management depends on displacement and patient factors. Non-displaced or minimally displaced fractures can be treated with a short arm cast; displaced fractures require closed reduction with or without surgical fixation depending on stability. A Smith fracture is the reverse — volar angulation of the distal radius fragment after a fall onto a flexed wrist. A Barton fracture is intraarticular and associated with carpal subluxation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Colles = dorsal angulation = dinner fork = FOOSH in osteoporotic patient. Smith = volar angulation = \"garden spade\" = fall on flexed wrist. COMLEX: Most common fracture in osteoporotic women; always assess for median nerve injury (acute CTS complication).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — Carpal tunnel syndrome: distal radius fractures (Colles) can acutely compress the median nerve within the carpal tunnel, presenting with acute carpal tunnel syndrome requiring urgent decompression"},{"id":"sdl16_q30","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of hand and wrist fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 23-year-old man punches a wall during an argument. He presents with pain, swelling, and tenderness at the ulnar dorsum of the right hand. Radiographs show a fifth metacarpal neck fracture with 35 degrees of volar angulation. He has full extension of all fingers and no rotational deformity on exam. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Immediate surgical ORIF because any angulation greater than 30 degrees in a metacarpal neck fracture mandates fixation","B":"No reduction needed; apply an ulnar gutter splint at current angulation because 35 degrees is clinically insignificant in the 5th metacarpal","C":"Thumb spica cast for 6 weeks because the fifth metacarpal neck fracture is treated identically to a scaphoid fracture","D":"Closed reduction to less than 40 degrees and immobilization in an ulnar gutter splint for 4-6 weeks","E":"Buddy taping alone is sufficient because metacarpal neck fractures heal without immobilization in young adults"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A Boxer's fracture (5th metacarpal neck fracture with volar angulation) at 35 degrees may be treated conservatively — acceptable angulation thresholds range from 40-70 degrees depending on clinical practice, because the CMC joint of the small finger provides compensatory mobility. At 35 degrees with no rotational deformity and full MCP extension, this fracture falls within the acceptable range and can be managed with splinting in an ulnar gutter splint. Rotational deformity (scissoring of the fingers with flexion) and pseudoclawing are not acceptable and mandate reduction regardless of angulation. ORIF is reserved for fractures with significant rotational malrotation, irreducible angulation, or intraarticular extension. Buddy taping alone is insufficient for acute displaced fractures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Boxer's fracture = 5th metacarpal neck; acceptable angulation up to 40-70 degrees; rotational deformity is never acceptable. COMLEX: Ulnar gutter splint with the MCP in 70-90 degrees flexion (intrinsic plus position) prevents flexion contracture.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — Carpal tunnel syndrome: hand anatomy and the metacarpal/phalangeal relationship is foundational to understanding safe splinting positions that prevent intrinsic muscle contracture during immobilization"}]},{"sdlNumber":17,"title":"Running Footwear & Biomechanics","questions":[{"id":"sdl17_q01","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Develop an understanding of the history of footwear, especially as it pertains to running footwear.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Which individual is credited with introducing spikes to running footwear in 1852, a design innovation that dramatically improved athletic traction on natural surfaces?","choices":{"A":"Adolf Dassler, who developed differentiated sprint and endurance shoe designs for competitive athletes in the 1920s","B":"Wait Webster, whose vulcanized rubber and leather sole design in 1832 laid the foundation for modern athletic footwear","C":"Bill Bowerman, who collaborated with Phil Knight in the early 1960s to develop what became the Nike brand","D":"Joseph William Foster, whose spiked shoes became the predecessor to what eventually became the Reebok brand","E":"Kihachiro Onitsuka, who founded the post-WWII Japanese athletic shoe company that eventually became Asics"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Joseph William Foster introduced spiked running shoes in 1852, providing athletes with enhanced traction on grass and natural running surfaces. Foster's innovation was a key milestone in the evolution of performance footwear. Adolf Dassler (1920) later differentiated sprint versus endurance shoe designs and famously supplied shoes at the 1936 Berlin Olympics. Wait Webster (1832) introduced rubber soles. Onitsuka (1949) founded what became Asics in post-WWII Japan. Bowerman and Knight (early 1960s) founded Nike.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield — footwear history is contextual background. COMLEX: Understand how the athletic footwear industry grew from biomechanical innovation rather than evidence-based medicine, setting the stage for marketing-science gaps explored in OBJ 3.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the evolution of running shoe heel design was partly driven by efforts to reduce Achilles tendon loading in distance runners"},{"id":"sdl17_q02","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Develop an understanding of the history of footwear, especially as it pertains to running footwear.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"In the history of running footwear, which company first marketed its shoes as \"scientifically tested,\" representing the earliest attempt to use clinical/scientific language to sell athletic footwear?","choices":{"A":"Nike, founded in the early 1960s by Bill Bowerman and Phil Knight, whose early waffle-sole design was derived from Bowerman's biomechanical experiments at the University of Oregon","B":"Adidas, developed by Adolf Dassler, who differentiated sprint versus endurance shoe construction and provided shoes to athletes at the 1936 Berlin Olympics","C":"Asics, originally Onitsuka Tiger, which incorporated research from Japanese sports scientists after its founding in 1949 in post-WWII Japan","D":"Reebok, whose predecessor company Joseph William Foster pioneered spiked running shoes in 1852 and later adopted modern materials science in their manufacturing processes","E":"New Balance, in 1962, was the first to market running shoes with a \"scientifically tested\" claim, pioneering the commercial use of science-based language in footwear marketing"},"correct":"E","explanation":"New Balance, in 1962, was the first company to market running shoes as \"scientifically tested,\" introducing science-based language to athletic footwear marketing. This milestone is historically significant because it marked the beginning of the conflation of commercial claims with scientific validation — a tension that the SDL explores at length. Nike (early 1960s) used biomechanical inspiration (waffle sole) but later marketing. Adidas/Dassler and Asics/Onitsuka preceded this with performance designs but not explicit scientific testing claims.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield — historical context. COMLEX: The 1962 New Balance claim represents the beginning of a long-standing gap between marketing language and evidence-based footwear science, which directly informs the critical appraisal framework in OBJ 3.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the evolution of running shoe heel design was partly driven by efforts to reduce Achilles tendon loading in distance runners"},{"id":"sdl17_q03","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Develop an understanding of the history of footwear, especially as it pertains to running footwear.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Adolf Dassler's 1920 contribution to athletic footwear was notable because he was the first to do which of the following in footwear design?","choices":{"A":"Introduce vulcanized rubber outsoles to replace leather soles, dramatically improving durability and traction on wet playing surfaces","B":"Incorporate spikes into running shoes for use on natural grass and track surfaces, improving athlete traction during competitive events","C":"Differentiate shoe construction based on athletic event type, designing separate shoe models optimized for sprinting versus endurance running","D":"Market shoes using claims of scientific testing, setting the commercial precedent for evidence-based language in athletic footwear","E":"Develop the first canvas and rubber shoe marketed for general athletic use, eventually evolving into what became the Keds brand"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Adolf Dassler's 1920 innovation was the differentiation of shoe design by athletic event — creating distinct construction features for sprinting versus endurance running. This was a fundamental departure from the one-shoe-fits-all approach. Dassler's shoes were famously worn at the 1936 Berlin Olympics. Vulcanized rubber (Goodyear, 1892), spikes (Foster, 1852), \"scientifically tested\" claims (New Balance, 1962), and canvas/rubber shoes (Goodyear/Keds, 1892) were all separate milestones by other companies.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: The Dassler differentiation principle (sprint vs endurance) foreshadows modern footwear biomechanics debates about whether any shoe design truly optimizes performance for a given runner or running style.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the evolution of running shoe heel design was partly driven by efforts to reduce Achilles tendon loading in distance runners"},{"id":"sdl17_q04","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Identify how footwear does and does not correlate to specific running-based injuries.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A patient who runs 25 miles per week asks whether switching to a more heavily cushioned \"traditional\" running shoe will reduce the impact forces on their joints. Based on the biomechanical evidence in this SDL, which response is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Heavily cushioned traditional running shoes actually INCREASE impact forces by lengthening stride length and decreasing stride frequency, producing greater ground reaction forces","B":"Heavily cushioned shoes reduce ground reaction forces by absorbing impact energy through the midsole, directly lowering the mechanical load transmitted to the tibia and knee","C":"Heavily cushioned shoes have a neutral effect on impact forces because the body's kinematics fully adapt to any shoe type, maintaining a constant ground reaction force regardless of cushion","D":"Heavily cushioned shoes reduce impact forces specifically on hard surfaces like asphalt, though they have no effect on softer surfaces where ground reaction forces are inherently lower","E":"Heavily cushioned shoes reduce impact forces only in runners over age 50, because natural shock-absorbing capacity declines with age and supplemental cushioning compensates for this deficit"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Counterintuitively, modern \"traditional\" heavily cushioned running shoes INCREASE impact forces by lengthening stride length, increasing contact times, and decreasing stride frequency — all of which elevate ground reaction forces (GRF). Hard surfaces do NOT increase GRF independently, and \"absorption\" within the shoe does NOT decrease GRF. Furthermore, more cushioning leads to less tactile sensation from the ground, which reduces the body's natural impact-moderating reflexes (Robbins phenomenon), paradoxically increasing injury risk. Natural shock-absorbing capacity does decrease with age, but cushioned shoes do not reliably compensate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield but a conceptually important counterintuitive finding. COMLEX: The Robbins phenomenon (cushioning → reduced tactile feedback → larger actual impact) is clinically relevant when counseling runners; more cushion ≠ less injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 1 — Medial tibial stress syndrome: one of the most extensively studied running injuries in the footwear biomechanics literature, yet no shoe type has proven preventive"},{"id":"sdl17_q05","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Identify how footwear does and does not correlate to specific running-based injuries.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A runner with a history of recurrent tibial stress fractures asks whether wearing rigid orthotics will help prevent future fractures. Based on the evidence, which statement most accurately reflects what the research shows?","choices":{"A":"Custom rigid orthotics significantly reduce tibial stress fracture recurrence by redistributing ground reaction forces away from the medial tibial cortex during running","B":"Orthotics reduce tibial stress fracture risk primarily in runners with pes planus by correcting excessive pronation that otherwise transmits greater torsional force to the tibial shaft","C":"Orthotics provide equivalent tibial stress protection compared to barefoot running, making them a cost-effective alternative to gait retraining for stress fracture prevention","D":"Orthotics do NOT prevent tibial stress fractures; some evidence suggests semirigid orthotics may actually INCREASE tibial strain rather than reduce it","E":"Semirigid orthotics are protective against tibial stress fractures in military recruits but have no demonstrated benefit in recreational runners with lower training volumes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Based on Ekenman 2002, orthotics do NOT prevent tibial stress fractures, and there is some evidence that semirigid orthotics may actually INCREASE tibial strain during running — the opposite of their intended effect. Butler 2003 similarly showed no difference in eversion excursion, peak eversion, eversion velocity, loading rate, or leg stiffness between no orthotic, multifunctional orthotic, and rigid orthotic conditions. The evidence for orthotics in injury prevention is mixed, with many trials examining military walking populations rather than runners.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: The negative data on orthotics for tibial stress fracture prevention is clinically important for sports medicine practitioners; orthotics may improve subjective comfort but should not be prescribed with expectations of biomechanical injury prevention.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 1 — Medial tibial stress syndrome: one of the most extensively studied running injuries in the footwear biomechanics literature, yet no shoe type has proven preventive"},{"id":"sdl17_q06","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Identify how footwear does and does not correlate to specific running-based injuries.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Compared to shod running, non-shod (barefoot) running consistently produces which biomechanical pattern according to the studies cited in this SDL?","choices":{"A":"Barefoot running reduces knee joint torques but increases ankle and metatarsophalangeal joint loading due to forefoot strike mechanics, resulting in an equivalent overall injury risk profile","B":"Barefoot running lowers ground reaction forces, lowers impact peak, reduces loading rate, decreases stance time, and decreases joint torques compared to running in conventional footwear","C":"Barefoot running reduces ground reaction forces on soft surfaces but produces higher impact forces than cushioned shoes on hard surfaces due to the absence of shock-absorbing midsole material","D":"Barefoot running reduces loading rate and impact peak during initial contact but increases stance time and ground reaction forces during push-off, producing no net biomechanical advantage","E":"Barefoot running produces identical ground reaction forces to cushioned shoes because the body's kinematics fully adapt to any running surface, maintaining homeostatic loading across all footwear conditions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Studies (Morley 2010, Lieberman 2010, Kerrigan 2009, Squadrone 2009) consistently show that non-shod (barefoot) running lowers ground reaction forces, lowers the impact peak, reduces the loading rate, decreases stance time, and decreases joint torques compared to conventional shod running. This is mechanistically explained by the shorter stride length and anterior foot strike pattern induced by barefoot conditions. However, it is the change in RUNNING STYLE (anterior foot strike, shorter stride) rather than the absence of the shoe itself that drives these benefits — as demonstrated by Connors 2023.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: Understanding that barefoot running benefits derive from gait changes, not shoe absence, is clinically useful when counseling patients — running style modification can be achieved in any shoe.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 1 — Medial tibial stress syndrome: one of the most extensively studied running injuries in the footwear biomechanics literature, yet no shoe type has proven preventive"},{"id":"sdl17_q07","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Summarize key principles when it comes to the lack of intersection between commercial marketing and the science of footwear design.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A study by Marti (1989) examined the relationship between running shoe price and lower extremity injury rates after correcting for previous injuries and training load. Which finding most accurately summarizes the results?","choices":{"A":"Runners in the >$95 shoe group were twice as likely to sustain a lower extremity injury compared to those in the <$40 shoe group, suggesting that higher shoe price does not protect against injury","B":"Runners spending between $65 and $95 on shoes had the lowest injury rates, supporting a moderate price range as optimal for injury prevention in recreational distance runners","C":"No significant difference in injury rates was found between high-priced and low-priced shoe groups when training volume and prior injury history were controlled, confirming price as a neutral variable","D":"Higher-priced shoes reduced soft-tissue injuries such as Achilles tendinopathy and plantar fasciitis but showed no protective effect on bony stress fractures of the tibia or metatarsals","E":"The >$95 shoe group showed lower injury rates overall but higher rates of metatarsal stress fractures, suggesting that premium cushioning shifts injury risk from soft-tissue to bone pathology"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Marti (1989) found that runners in the >$95 shoe group were twice as likely to sustain a lower extremity injury compared to those in the <$40 group, after correcting for prior injuries and training regimen. This finding is striking and counterintuitive — more expensive shoes, which often carry greater marketing claims, are associated with MORE injuries, not fewer. This likely relates to the Robbins \"impact moderating behavior\" phenomenon: expensive, heavily cushioned shoes reduce tactile feedback, blunt natural protective reflexes, and thereby increase impact forces and injury risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield but a high-impact teaching point. COMLEX: This is the key data point for counseling runners: expensive shoes are NOT protective, and clinical footwear prescriptions should not be driven by cost. The evidence base for shoe marketing claims is generally weak.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 3 — Challenges of integrative medicine research and regulation: the same evidence-based scrutiny that exposes commercial footwear myths applies to unproven integrative therapies"},{"id":"sdl17_q08","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Summarize key principles when it comes to the lack of intersection between commercial marketing and the science of footwear design.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"The traditional shoe prescription paradigm matches arch type to shoe type: overpronation (pes planus) receives motion control shoes, underpronation (pes cavus) receives cushioned shoes, and neutral motion receives stability shoes. Multiple systematic reviews have concluded that this approach is:","choices":{"A":"Supported by moderate-quality evidence for injury prevention in civilian runners but not validated in military populations, where rapid training volume increases confound shoe prescription outcomes","B":"Simplistic, subjective, and does NOT reduce pain or injury rates — prescribing footwear based on arch type is not evidence-based according to available systematic reviews","C":"Effective for reducing knee and hip pain in runners with pes planus through motion control correction, though evidence for pes cavus cushioning and neutral stability shoes remains limited","D":"Validated in the short term for pain reduction during training, but not effective for long-term injury prevention beyond the first 12 weeks of a new running program","E":"Evidence-based for runners with BMI greater than 25, in whom arch instability and excess body weight compound to increase injury risk, but not in normal-weight recreational runners"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Multiple systematic reviews (Richards 2008, Ryan 2009, van Gent 2007, Knapik 2010) have concluded that prescribing footwear based on arch type is simplistic, subjective, and does NOT reduce pain or injury. Richards 2008 specifically states: \"Prescription of this shoe type is not evidence based.\" Relph 2022 further confirmed: \"There is no evidence that prescribing footwear based on foot type reduces running-related lower-limb injuries in adults.\" This represents one of the most important marketing-versus-science disconnects in sports medicine — a widely practiced paradigm that lacks supporting evidence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: Understanding that the arch-type → shoe-type paradigm is not evidence-based is directly applicable to clinical practice; physicians should not simply match arch type to shoe type when counseling runners.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 3 — Challenges of integrative medicine research and regulation: the same evidence-based scrutiny that exposes commercial footwear myths applies to unproven integrative therapies"},{"id":"sdl17_q09","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Summarize key principles when it comes to the lack of intersection between commercial marketing and the science of footwear design.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Hoffmann (1905) conducted anatomical observations comparing barefoot populations to shoe-wearing populations. Which finding from his work directly supports the concept that conventional shoe design negatively alters foot structure and function?","choices":{"A":"Shoe-wearing populations develop stronger plantar intrinsic muscles compared to barefoot populations, as the mechanical resistance of the shoe sole provides passive resistance training during the gait cycle","B":"Shoe-wearing populations demonstrate lower rates of hallux valgus deformity because the medial counter of the shoe provides structural support for the great toe during push-off mechanics","C":"Shoe wearing does not alter foot anatomy in populations that begin wearing shoes after age five, as the foot skeleton has completed the majority of its ossification and remodeling by this age","D":"Shoe-wearing populations in both industrial and non-industrial societies develop identical foot anatomy, suggesting that footwear style rather than shoe wearing per se determines anatomical outcome","E":"Shoe wearing after the first year of life causes progressive narrowing of the anterior foot and diminution of the range of motion of the phalanges, tarsal, and ankle joints compared to barefoot populations"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hoffmann (1905) observed that barefoot and shoe-wearing populations have similar foot anatomy until shoes that compress and splint the foot are introduced — typically around the end of the first year of life. After this, shoe wearers develop progressive narrowing of the anterior portion of the foot and diminution of the range of motion of the phalanges, tarsal, and ankle joints. He also noted that the great toe maintains foot adduction and provides leverage — functions that are compromised by narrow-toed shoes. This historical data supports the broader SDL theme that conventional shoe design may harm rather than help natural foot function.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield — historical data. COMLEX: Hoffmann's findings support recommending toe-box-wide shoes for patients, particularly children, to avoid acquired foot deformities. Great toe function (adduction, push-off leverage) is mechanically important and worth preserving.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 3 — Challenges of integrative medicine research and regulation: the same evidence-based scrutiny that exposes commercial footwear myths applies to unproven integrative therapies"},{"id":"sdl17_q10","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Begin to develop a paradigm for running footwear prescription.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"When counseling a competitive runner about shoe selection, the evidence supports using shoe WEIGHT as a meaningful prescription variable. Which relationship correctly describes the physiological impact of shoe weight on running economy?","choices":{"A":"Each 100 grams of additional shoe weight increases oxygen consumption by approximately 3 to 5%, making shoe weight the single most important determinant of running performance and economy","B":"Each 100 grams of additional shoe weight increases oxygen consumption (VO2) by approximately 0.7 to 1%, directly reducing running economy and metabolic efficiency","C":"Shoe weight does not independently affect oxygen consumption; only midsole stiffness and heel-to-toe drop alter metabolic efficiency, with heavier shoes being offset by greater energy return per stride","D":"Shoe weight increases oxygen consumption only when carried at the foot distal to the ankle; weight distributed proximally in the midsole and heel has no measurable effect on metabolic cost","E":"The relationship between shoe weight and oxygen consumption is nonlinear, with shoes under 200 grams having no metabolic effect and the cost increasing exponentially only above this threshold"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Shoe weight is one of the evidence-based prescription variables in footwear selection. Each 100-gram increase in shoe weight results in approximately a 0.7 to 1% increase in oxygen consumption (VO2), meaningfully reducing running economy. This is why lighter shoes — all else being equal — are metabolically advantageous. The other evidence-based prescription variables include stack height, heel-to-toe drop, flexibility, toe box width, and heel cup width. These are contrasted with non-evidence-based marketing features (price, brand claims, motion control systems) that do not reliably correlate with performance or injury prevention.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: The 100 gm / 0.7-1% VO2 relationship is the only specific quantitative fact worth memorizing from the prescription paradigm section — relevant when advising competitive runners or when evaluating claims about racing flats versus training shoes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 3 — Physical therapy prescription components: evidence-based footwear prescription mirrors PT prescription by emphasizing individualized, functional goals over generic protocols"},{"id":"sdl17_q11","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Begin to develop a paradigm for running footwear prescription.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"According to the lever arm phenomena described by Hamill (1992), a running shoe with a softer midsole compared to one with a firmer midsole produces which biomechanical consequence for the foot and ankle?","choices":{"A":"A softer midsole produces greater pronation magnitude and a shorter time to reach maximum pronation compared to a firmer midsole","B":"A softer midsole reduces pronation magnitude by absorbing rearfoot eversion forces before they can be transmitted to the subtalar joint, producing a more neutral midfoot position at heel strike","C":"A softer midsole produces identical pronation magnitude to a firm midsole but extends the time to maximum pronation, allowing muscles more time to eccentrically decelerate subtalar motion","D":"A softer midsole shifts loading from the rearfoot to the forefoot, decreasing heel strike pronation while increasing metatarsophalangeal dorsiflexion torque during push-off","E":"A softer midsole increases pronation only in runners with pes planus; runners with neutral arch mechanics demonstrate no difference in pronation between soft and firm midsole conditions"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hamill (1992) described the lever arm phenomenon: a softer midsole collapses under load more readily, allowing greater rearfoot eversion (pronation) and reducing the time to reach maximum pronation. Additionally, a wider sole base creates a longer lever arm, increasing the demand on the foot to overcome pronation forces. Counterintuitively, motion control shoes with dense thermoplastic medial posts often have WIDER soles — which actually increases the pronation-inducing lever arm rather than reducing it. This is another example of a shoe feature that is mechanistically opposite to its marketing claim.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield. COMLEX: The lever arm phenomenon explains why wide, soft-soled motion control shoes may worsen rather than correct pronation — a mechanistic argument against the arch-type prescription paradigm covered in OBJ 3.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 3 — Physical therapy prescription components: evidence-based footwear prescription mirrors PT prescription by emphasizing individualized, functional goals over generic protocols"},{"id":"sdl17_q12","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Begin to develop a paradigm for running footwear prescription.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"The evidence-based \"take home points\" for running shoe prescription in this SDL recommend that patients prioritize which set of shoe characteristics when selecting footwear?","choices":{"A":"Motion control features, arch support, and cushioned heel collar — selected based on static arch height assessment using the wet footprint test or a commercially available pressure plate system","B":"Heel-to-toe drop greater than 10 mm, a wide toe box, and a reinforced medial post — prioritizing rearfoot stability for runners who log more than 30 miles per week","C":"Maximum cushioning with a thick midsole, a neutral motion category designation, and replacement on a fixed schedule of every 250 to 500 miles regardless of shoe condition","D":"Light weight, flexibility, and a thin profile — with emphasis on immediate comfort upon fitting and ignoring cost, brand features, and marketing claims","E":"High cost within the runner's budget, a brand with independent biomechanics laboratory testing, and a heel cup width matched to the runner's measured calcaneal width"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The evidence-based shoe-buying recommendations from this SDL emphasize selecting shoes that are light, flexible, and thin, with the most important practical criterion being that the shoe is immediately comfortable when tried on. Patients should get properly sized (feet change with age and weight), try multiple pairs, and buy a new shoe when the current one is no longer comfortable — not on a fixed mileage or time schedule. The features to IGNORE include cost, brand marketing, motion control elements, bells, and whistles. This directly contradicts most commercial shoe retail advice and represents the core clinical take-away of OBJ 4.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: The prescription framework (light, flexible, thin, immediately comfortable) is the answer to \"how do you prescribe running shoes?\" on clinical vignettes. Price, brand, and arch type are explicitly NOT evidence-based selection criteria.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 3 — Physical therapy prescription components: evidence-based footwear prescription mirrors PT prescription by emphasizing individualized, functional goals over generic protocols"},{"id":"sdl17_q13","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Develop an understanding of the history of footwear, especially as it pertains to running footwear.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A medical student is preparing a lecture on the commercialization of sports medicine and asks about the history of running shoes. She wants to illustrate the moment when scientific language first entered footwear marketing. The professor confirms that the correct company and year is:","choices":{"A":"Nike in the early 1960s, when Bill Bowerman used his laboratory at the University of Oregon to independently validate the biomechanical superiority of the waffle-sole outsole design","B":"Adidas in 1936, when Adolf Dassler provided specially designed sprint and endurance shoes to Olympic athletes in Berlin after laboratory testing of sole composition and spike placement","C":"New Balance in 1962, which was the first to market running shoes as \"scientifically tested,\" initiating the commercial use of science-based claims in the footwear industry","D":"Asics in 1949, when Kihachiro Onitsuka partnered with Japanese sports science institutes in post-WWII Japan to develop the first footwear tested with biomechanical instrumentation","E":"Reebok in 1892, when the Goodyear vulcanization process was combined with leather uppers and independently tested by the British athletic commission for use in competitive track events"},"correct":"C","explanation":"New Balance in 1962 was the first company to explicitly market running shoes with a \"scientifically tested\" claim, representing the pioneering use of science-based marketing language in athletic footwear. This milestone is historically significant because it established a precedent — often disconnected from actual scientific evidence — that has grown into a massive global footwear marketing industry. Nike's waffle sole derived from Bowerman's experimentation but was not marketed as scientifically tested in this formal sense. Adidas/Dassler optimized shoes for athletes without claiming scientific testing. Asics and Reebok/Keds have separate historical milestones not associated with 1962.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield. COMLEX: The 1962 New Balance claim represents the starting point of a marketing-science disconnect that physicians must recognize when counseling patients on footwear: science-based language in shoe marketing does not imply evidence-based benefit.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the heel-lift features of early scientifically marketed shoes were specifically promoted as Achilles strain protection, illustrating how marketing claims preceded evidence in footwear history"},{"id":"sdl17_q14","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Develop an understanding of the history of footwear, especially as it pertains to running footwear.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A sports medicine attending asks a first-year resident to name the athlete at the 1936 Berlin Olympics who wore shoes designed by Adolf Dassler, and what brand connection this represents. The resident should respond that Dassler's company was the direct predecessor to which modern athletic brand?","choices":{"A":"Adidas — Adolf Dassler's company, operating under the Dassler Brothers brand before the split, evolved into Adidas (from \"Adi Dassler\") after a post-WWII family dispute","B":"Nike — because Phil Knight and Bill Bowerman initially imported Dassler shoes for distribution in the United States before founding their own brand in the early 1960s","C":"New Balance — because the German engineering approach developed by Dassler was adopted by New Balance when it introduced its \"scientifically tested\" shoe line in 1962","D":"Puma — which was the sole successor company to the original Dassler Brothers Shoe Factory, retaining the full patent portfolio and manufacturing facilities after the family split","E":"Asics — because Kihachiro Onitsuka licensed the Dassler spiked shoe design when founding his Japanese athletic company in 1949 after World War II"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Adolf Dassler's company evolved into Adidas (named from his nickname \"Adi\" and surname \"Dassler\"). After a post-WWII family dispute, his brother Rudolf founded Puma — so both Adidas and Puma share the Dassler Brothers lineage. For the purposes of this SDL, Dassler's 1920 innovation (sprint vs endurance shoe differentiation) and his presence at the 1936 Berlin Olympics are the testable historical facts. Nike was founded independently by Bowerman and Knight. New Balance, Asics, and the others have separate founding histories with no direct Dassler connection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield — brand history. COMLEX: The Dassler → Adidas/Puma origin story is a contextual detail. The clinically relevant lesson is that the earliest \"performance differentiation\" in footwear was driven by athletic intuition, not randomized evidence.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: Dassler's sprint vs endurance differentiation foreshadows modern heel-to-toe drop debates, as sprint shoes are zero-drop and endurance shoes historically had a raised heel — each influencing Achilles tendon loading differently"},{"id":"sdl17_q15","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Develop an understanding of the history of footwear, especially as it pertains to running footwear.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A sports history question on an exam presents the following timeline of footwear milestones. Which item in this list is placed in the INCORRECT year?","choices":{"A":"1852 — Joseph William Foster introduced spikes to running footwear, improving traction for competitive runners on natural grass and cinder track surfaces","B":"1949 — Kihachiro Onitsuka founded the company in post-WWII Japan that eventually became Asics, producing athletic footwear for Japanese Olympic development programs","C":"1920 — Adolf Dassler began differentiating shoe construction by athletic event type, creating separate designs for sprint and endurance running athletes","D":"1962 — New Balance became the first athletic shoe company to explicitly market its products using the phrase \"scientifically tested\" in its commercial advertising","E":"Vulcanization and the introduction of Keds are attributed to 1892, which is correct; the incorrect item would be attributing rubber soles to Goodyear in 1852 — rubber soles were introduced by Wait Webster in 1832"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The key historical fact tested here: Wait Webster introduced rubber soles COMBINED with leather in 1832 — not 1852. The 1852 milestone belongs to Joseph William Foster and the introduction of spikes. Goodyear's contribution in 1892 was vulcanization, which was used in the production of Keds. All other dates in the answer choices are correct as stated. This question requires distinguishing between the 1832 rubber sole milestone (Webster) and the 1852 spike milestone (Foster), which are commonly confused.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield. COMLEX: Historical chronology for footwear is background context. Remember the key dates: 1832 (rubber soles/Webster), 1852 (spikes/Foster), 1892 (vulcanization/Goodyear/Keds), 1920 (Dassler/sprint vs endurance), 1962 (New Balance/scientifically tested).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the 1892 vulcanization milestone enabled mass-produced shoe soles, beginning the era of standardized heel heights that altered Achilles tendon resting length across populations who adopted footwear"},{"id":"sdl17_q16","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Identify how footwear does and does not correlate to specific running-based injuries.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 32-year-old recreational runner presents with medial tibial pain after increasing her mileage. She recently purchased expensive motion control shoes with maximum cushioning on the advice of a running store specialist. Which statement from the SDL evidence most directly challenges the assumption that her shoe choice will reduce her injury risk?","choices":{"A":"Motion control shoes with rigid thermoplastic medial posts increase eversion control at the subtalar joint, reducing pronation-related tibial torsional forces and thereby lowering medial tibial stress","B":"More cushioning reduces tactile ground sensation, which blunts the body's natural impact-moderating reflexes, paradoxically increasing actual impact forces despite the perception of softer contact","C":"Highly cushioned shoes improve injury outcomes in runners with pes cavus who underpronate, but offer no additional protection for neutral runners or those with pes planus foot type","D":"Maximum cushioning reduces the vertical loading rate during heel strike, which is the primary biomechanical determinant of medial tibial stress syndrome and tibial stress fracture risk","E":"Cushioned shoes provide equivalent impact protection to barefoot running on soft surfaces, making them the preferred option for runners transitioning from high-mileage training blocks"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Robbins phenomenon (1987-1997) is the key mechanism: more cushioning → less tactile sensation from the plantar surface → reduced natural absorption reflexes → larger actual impact forces. The runner's body typically modulates impact based on sensory feedback from the foot; when this feedback is muffled by thick cushioning, the brain cannot appropriately trigger protective muscle co-contraction, and ground reaction forces increase. Additionally, Marti 1989 showed that shoes costing >$95 were associated with TWICE the lower extremity injury rate compared to shoes under $40, challenging the assumption that premium cushioned shoes reduce injury risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: The Robbins impact-moderating behavior concept is the core mechanistic explanation for why more cushion ≠ less injury. This is the most clinically applicable concept from OBJ 2 when counseling runners who present with overuse injuries.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — Medial tibial stress syndrome: MTSS involves repetitive tibial loading; the evidence that cushioned shoes do not reduce impact forces directly explains why shoe type has failed to demonstrate preventive efficacy for this condition"},{"id":"sdl17_q17","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Identify how footwear does and does not correlate to specific running-based injuries.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A military recruit with pes planus is prescribed custom rigid orthotics by the base physician to prevent tibial stress fractures during basic training. A sports medicine fellow reviewing the case cites evidence that this practice should be reconsidered. Which study finding best supports the fellow's concern?","choices":{"A":"Butler (2003) showed that rigid orthotics significantly increased eversion velocity and peak eversion angle during running, increasing pronation-related torsional forces on the tibia rather than reducing them","B":"Knapik (2010) showed that assigning motion control shoes based on arch type in Air Force and Marine Corps basic training programs did not reduce lower extremity injury rates compared to standard issue footwear","C":"Gardner (1988) showed that shock-absorbing insoles significantly increased tibial stress fracture rates during infantry training, with effect sizes greater than those seen with rigid or semirigid orthotics","D":"Ekenman (2002) showed orthotics do not prevent tibial stress fractures; some evidence suggests semirigid orthotics may INCREASE tibial strain, potentially worsening rather than reducing fracture risk","E":"Relph (2022) demonstrated in a systematic review that custom orthotics for runners with pes planus increased their likelihood of tibial stress fracture by altering subtalar kinematics during push-off"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ekenman 2002 is the correct reference: orthotics do NOT prevent tibial stress fractures, and some evidence from that study suggests semirigid orthotics may INCREASE tibial strain — the opposite of the intended effect. Butler 2003 is also relevant but showed no difference in rearfoot kinematics between no-orthotic, multifunctional orthotic, and rigid orthotic conditions — not an increase in eversion per se. Knapik 2010 is about shoe type assignment, not orthotics specifically. The orthotics evidence base is mixed, with most prospective trials examining walking rather than running, and none demonstrating consistent stress fracture prevention.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: For COMLEX clinical vignettes, orthotics should NOT be recommended as tibial stress fracture preventive devices. They may improve subjective comfort, but this does not translate to reduced fracture rates. Treat the underlying training error and bone density issues.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — Medial tibial stress syndrome: MTSS and tibial stress fractures share the same etiological pathway of repetitive tibial loading; orthotics similarly lack evidence for MTSS prevention, which reinforces the conclusion from the orthotic stress fracture data"},{"id":"sdl17_q18","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Identify how footwear does and does not correlate to specific running-based injuries.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A research team studies the biomechanics of minimalist footwear use and finds that runners transitioning to minimalist shoes demonstrate lower vertical loading rate, higher cadence, less energy absorption at the knee, and more energy absorption at the ankle. The researchers conclude that these changes are most directly attributable to:","choices":{"A":"The reduction in shoe weight associated with minimalist footwear, which decreases the metabolic cost of swing phase and reflexively produces a shorter stride and more anterior foot contact angle","B":"The elimination of heel cushioning in minimalist shoes, which prevents heel strike by making heel contact painful, thereby mechanically forcing an anterior foot strike pattern in all runners","C":"The change in running style induced by minimalist shoes (anterior foot strike, shorter stride) rather than the physical properties of the shoe itself","D":"The zero or near-zero heel-to-toe drop of minimalist shoes, which anatomically positions the foot in plantarflexion at heel contact and biomechanically requires a forefoot or midfoot strike pattern","E":"The increased plantar tactile feedback from minimalist shoes, which amplifies afferent signals from ground contact and triggers spinal reflex arcs that automatically shorten stride length"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Connors (2023) explicitly concluded that improvements seen with minimalist footwear — lower vertical loading rate, higher cadence, less knee energy absorption, more ankle energy absorption, and improvement in prior injuries — result from the CHANGE IN RUNNING STYLE (anterior/forefoot foot strike, higher cadence, shorter stride) rather than any inherent property of the shoe itself. This is a clinically critical distinction: running gait retraining, regardless of shoe type, can replicate these benefits. Minimalist shoes also increase metatarsophalangeal and ankle joint loading compared to conventional shoes (Sun 2020), so they carry trade-offs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: The Connors 2023 conclusion is the capstone finding for OBJ 2 — it unifies the data by showing that footwear effects on biomechanics are mediated through gait changes. Gait retraining is therefore more fundamental than shoe selection for injury prevention.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the increased ankle energy absorption with anterior foot strike induced by minimalist shoes increases eccentric Achilles loading, which explains the higher rates of Achilles tendinopathy reported in runners who transition too rapidly to minimalist footwear"},{"id":"sdl17_q19","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Summarize key principles when it comes to the lack of intersection between commercial marketing and the science of footwear design.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 45-year-old marathon runner visits a sports medicine clinic and reports that a running store employee recommended replacing his shoes every 6 months or after 250-500 miles, citing \"complete cushioning breakdown.\" When asked if this recommendation is evidence-based, the physician should respond that:","choices":{"A":"The 250-500 mile replacement guideline is not outcome-based; EVA midsole cushioning loses about 40% of its properties after 400 miles, but replacement timing should be guided by comfort rather than a fixed mileage schedule","B":"The 250-500 mile guideline is supported by prospective trials showing that runners who replace shoes on this schedule have significantly lower rates of tibial stress fractures and Achilles tendinopathy","C":"The 250-500 mile guideline is appropriate for runners over age 40 because natural shock-absorbing capacity decreases with age, making midsole cushioning degradation more biomechanically consequential in older runners","D":"The guideline is evidence-based for heavy runners (BMI greater than 27) in whom midsole compression is accelerated, but lacks evidence for normal-weight runners who can use shoes until sole tread wear is visible","E":"The 500-mile upper limit is based on controlled wear-testing data showing complete EVA foam failure at this threshold, making continued use unsafe due to uneven surface contact and increased lateral ankle sprain risk"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The 250-500 mile replacement recommendation is NOT outcome-based. The SDL notes that EVA foam cushioning loses approximately 40% of its cushioning properties after 400 miles — but this has not been translated into a clinical trial demonstrating that replacement at this threshold reduces injury. Moreover, the \"right\" replacement interval is highly variable based on runner weight, frequency of use, running conditions, and shoe storage. The evidence-based recommendation is simply: replace shoes when they are no longer comfortable. This is another example of a commercially convenient guideline that lacks scientific support.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield. COMLEX: The 40% EVA cushioning loss figure at 400 miles is the only specific number worth knowing. Clinical pearl: advise patients to replace based on comfort, not mileage — a simple, evidence-consistent, and cost-conscious recommendation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — Integrative medicine research challenges: fixed-interval shoe replacement guidelines parallel unproven integrative medicine protocols — both are commercially promoted practices that lack patient-oriented outcome evidence but persist due to industry interests"},{"id":"sdl17_q20","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Summarize key principles when it comes to the lack of intersection between commercial marketing and the science of footwear design.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A study (Stacoff 2001) examined whether shoe type — specifically motion control versus cushioning versus stability designs — could reliably control tibiocalcaneal kinematics during running. The most accurate summary of this study's finding is:","choices":{"A":"Motion control shoes significantly reduce tibiocalcaneal eversion in overpronators when compared to neutral shoes, supporting their prescription in runners with pes planus and rearfoot valgus alignment","B":"Stability shoes reduce tibiocalcaneal eversion in neutral runners but have no effect in overpronators, suggesting that motion control features only benefit runners who already demonstrate moderate arch integrity","C":"Shoe type reliably controls tibiocalcaneal kinematics in runners who weigh less than 75 kg, but not in heavier runners in whom foot mass and ground reaction forces overwhelm the shoe's mechanical resistance","D":"Tibiocalcaneal kinematics during running are individually unique and not reliably controlled by shoe type, undermining the biomechanical rationale for motion control shoe prescription","E":"Motion control shoes reduce the loading rate of tibiocalcaneal eversion but do not alter the total range of pronation, providing partial biomechanical benefit without complete kinematics correction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Stacoff (2001) found that tibiocalcaneal kinematics during running are individually unique — i.e., each runner's pronation pattern is specific to them and NOT predictably controlled by shoe type. This finding is foundational to the marketing-versus-science argument: the entire motion control shoe category is built on the premise that shoe design can reliably correct rearfoot mechanics, but Stacoff's data shows this is not the case. Combined with the Ryan/Richards/Knapik systematic review data showing no injury reduction from arch-based prescription, this study represents a mechanistic basis for skepticism about motion control shoes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: Stacoff 2001 provides the mechanistic explanation for why motion control shoes don't work as marketed — individual kinematics variability means no shoe type can reliably control rearfoot motion for all runners. This supports a comfort-first prescription approach.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — Integrative medicine research challenges: the failure of shoe design to predictably control tibiocalcaneal kinematics parallels the failure of many physical integrative therapies to produce consistent biomechanical changes — individualized responses undermine group-based intervention protocols"},{"id":"sdl17_q21","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Summarize key principles when it comes to the lack of intersection between commercial marketing and the science of footwear design.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A population study compared the prevalence of foot deformities in 2,300 children aged 4-13 years based on footwear type. Closed-toe shoes were associated with a 13.2% deformity rate, sandals 6.0%, slippers 8.2%, and unshod children 2.8%. Which conclusion does this data most directly support regarding commercial footwear in children?","choices":{"A":"All footwear types produce equivalent rates of foot deformity compared to the unshod condition, suggesting that the act of walking itself, rather than shoe type, is the primary driver of pediatric foot anatomy changes","B":"Sandals and slippers cause similar rates of foot deformity because they provide insufficient medial arch support, whereas closed-toe shoes with built-in arch structures paradoxically reduce deformity in older children","C":"Closed-toe conventional shoes are associated with the highest rate of foot deformity in children, while going unshod is associated with the lowest rate, suggesting that shoe wearing — not absence of shoes — may contribute to anatomical foot changes","D":"The 13.2% deformity rate in closed-toe shoe wearers reflects selection bias, as children with pre-existing foot problems are more likely to be prescribed closed-toe orthopedic footwear by their physicians","E":"Children who wear sandals have lower deformity rates than slipper wearers because sandal straps provide dynamic lateral containment of the foot, reducing hallux valgus drift during the push-off phase of walking"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Rao (1992) found a clear gradient: closed-toe shoes 13.2% > slippers 8.2% > sandals 6.0% > unshod 2.8% for foot deformity rates. This data directly supports Hoffmann's 1905 observations that constrictive shoe wearing leads to progressive anatomical changes, including narrowing of the forefoot and toe deformities. The finding that unshod children had the lowest deformity rate is the most provocative — it challenges the assumption that children need foot protection or arch support from shoes. The data supports recommending footwear with wide toe boxes that allow natural toe splay, particularly in growing children.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield. COMLEX: The Rao 1992 data is clinically relevant for pediatric foot health counseling: parents should be advised to choose footwear with wide toe boxes that allow natural foot development; conventional closed-toe shoes that crowd the toes are associated with the highest deformity rates.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — Integrative medicine research challenges: the pediatric foot deformity data illustrates how a cultural norm (children wearing closed-toe shoes) persists despite evidence of harm — the same uncritical acceptance seen with many unregulated integrative health products"},{"id":"sdl17_q22","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Begin to develop a paradigm for running footwear prescription.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A sports medicine physician is developing an individualized running shoe prescription for a 28-year-old male recreational runner with no prior injuries. He uses the prescription framework from this SDL. Which combination of shoe characteristics does the evidence-based framework identify as the most clinically relevant for this prescription?","choices":{"A":"Medial post stiffness, arch height index, rearfoot flare angle, and pronation control category — selected based on a dynamic foot pressure analysis and static arch height measurement in the clinic","B":"Shoe weight, stack height, heel-to-toe drop, flexibility, toe box width, and heel cup width — with comfort upon immediate fitting as the overriding clinical criterion","C":"Brand reputation, independent biomechanical testing certification, outsole durometer rating, and midsole foam density — as these are the construction variables most strongly correlated with injury prevention","D":"Price point within the runner's budget, outsole tread depth, upper breathability rating, and motion control category — prioritizing long-term durability and cost-effectiveness over immediate comfort","E":"Sole stiffness in the sagittal plane, heel counter height, toe spring angle, and midsole thickness at the metatarsal heads — selected based on the runner's habitual foot strike pattern as assessed by treadmill video analysis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The evidence-based prescription framework from this SDL identifies six useful shoe characteristics: weight, stack height, heel-to-toe drop, flexibility, toe box width, and heel cup width. Immediate comfort upon fitting is the most practical overriding criterion. The framework explicitly rejects arch-type matching, price-based selection, and brand-specific claims as non-evidence-based prescription methods. The paradigm also considers the interaction between congenital and acquired foot anatomy, foot function, and running gait — all of which are individually unique and cannot be addressed by a standardized shoe prescription protocol.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield. COMLEX: Memorize the six evidence-based shoe variables (weight, stack height, heel-to-toe drop, flexibility, toe box width, heel cup width) and the take-home rule: light, flexible, thin, immediately comfortable — ignore cost and marketing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 3 — Physical therapy prescription components: the shoe prescription framework parallels PT prescription — both require individualized goal setting, specific measurable parameters (drop, weight, flexibility), and patient-centered comfort outcomes rather than generic protocol adherence"},{"id":"sdl17_q23","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Begin to develop a paradigm for running footwear prescription.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"During a footwear consultation, a physician explains that the \"heel-to-toe drop\" of a running shoe is an important biomechanical variable. A medical student asks for clarification. Which description correctly defines heel-to-toe drop and its biomechanical significance?","choices":{"A":"Heel-to-toe drop is the total midsole stack height at the midfoot, measured in millimeters at the navicular level; higher drop values indicate greater overall cushioning and are associated with more posterior foot strike patterns","B":"Heel-to-toe drop refers to the degree of heel counter rigidity relative to toe box flexibility; a high-drop shoe has a stiff heel and a compliant forefoot, promoting rearfoot stability during heel strike","C":"Heel-to-toe drop measures the vertical distance between the ground and the ball of the foot during midstance; higher drop values indicate greater arch height and are used to classify shoe type as motion control, stability, or neutral","D":"Heel-to-toe drop is the rearfoot-to-forefoot weight distribution ratio measured by force plate analysis; a 12 mm drop corresponds to approximately 60% rearfoot loading and 40% forefoot loading during heel strike","E":"Heel-to-toe drop is the difference between rearfoot stack height and forefoot stack height; a 24 mm rearfoot and 12 mm forefoot produces a 12 mm drop, which influences foot strike angle and Achilles tendon loading"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Heel-to-toe drop (also called \"offset\" or \"drop\") is specifically the arithmetic difference between the rearfoot stack height and the forefoot stack height. The SDL gives the concrete example: 24 mm rearfoot minus 12 mm forefoot equals 12 mm drop. Higher drop shoes position the heel above the forefoot, promoting a more posterior foot strike pattern and reducing Achilles tendon stretch at heel contact (similar to wearing a small heel lift). Lower drop or zero-drop shoes are associated with more anterior foot strike patterns and greater Achilles loading — a key consideration when transitioning runners to minimalist footwear, where a rapid drop in heel height can precipitate Achilles tendinopathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield. COMLEX: The heel-to-toe drop concept directly connects to Achilles tendon biomechanics — reducing drop rapidly can cause Achilles injuries. When prescribing minimalist shoes, gradual transition is essential. Conversely, high-drop shoes may reduce Achilles strain in symptomatic runners.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 3 — Physical therapy prescription components: heel-to-toe drop is a quantifiable, prescribable variable analogous to exercise intensity parameters in PT prescriptions — it can be titrated gradually rather than changed abruptly, preventing overuse injuries during shoe transitions"},{"id":"sdl17_q24","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Begin to develop a paradigm for running footwear prescription.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A Sun (2020) systematic review of 63 studies on footwear construction and running biomechanics identified four key conclusions. Which set of findings most accurately reflects the systematic review's conclusions?","choices":{"A":"Higher midsole cushioning uniformly reduces injury risk; motion control features reliably decrease pronation in overpronators; minimalist shoes produce equivalent joint loading to conventional shoes when worn by experienced barefoot runners","B":"No shoe construction variable demonstrated consistent biomechanical effects across subjects; individual kinematic variability negated population-level findings; only gait retraining produced statistically significant reductions in running-related injury rates","C":"Optimal midsole stiffness benefits performance; softer midsoles reduce impact forces and loading rates; thicker midsoles cushion but reduce plantar sensation; minimalist shoes improve running economy and Achilles tendon stiffness but increase MTP and ankle joint loading","D":"Maximalist cushioned shoes reduce GRF more than any other design; rigid carbon-plate shoes improve running economy equally across all runner types; heel-to-toe drop does not independently affect foot strike pattern or Achilles tendon loading","E":"Thicker midsoles reduce injury risk in all runners; stiffer midsoles increase injury risk in runners over age 40; minimalist shoes should only be prescribed to runners with pes cavus who already demonstrate an anterior foot strike pattern"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sun (2020) identified four key conclusions from 63 studies: (1) Increasing midsole stiffness at the optimal range can benefit performance-related variables; (2) Softer midsoles can reduce impact forces and loading rates; (3) Thicker midsoles provide better cushioning but may decrease plantar sensations — reducing the natural impact-moderating reflexes; (4) Minimalist shoes can improve running economy and increase cross-sectional area and stiffness of the Achilles tendon, but would increase metatarsophalangeal and ankle joint loading compared to conventional shoes. This four-point summary represents the most evidence-consolidated framework for understanding the trade-offs in shoe construction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield. COMLEX: The Sun 2020 four conclusions are the most board-testable framework from OBJ 4 — they synthesize the minimalist shoe trade-offs (better Achilles/economy but more MTP and ankle load) and explain why no single shoe design is universally superior across all runners.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 3 — Physical therapy prescription components: Sun 2020's four-conclusion framework mirrors a PT prescription in its acknowledgment of trade-offs — every shoe construction variable that benefits one outcome worsens another, requiring individualized clinical judgment rather than universal protocols"}]},{"sdlNumber":18,"title":"Bone Histology, Physiology & Remodeling","questions":[{"id":"sdl18_q01","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the histological structure and cellular composition of bone, focusing on the roles of osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and osteocytes.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which cell type constitutes approximately 90–95% of all cells found in mature bone tissue and serves as the primary sensor of mechanical strain and regulator of local mineral chemistry?","choices":{"A":"Osteoblasts, which synthesize and secrete osteoid and remain the dominant cell population in cortical bone throughout adulthood","B":"Osteoclasts, which are large multinucleated resorptive cells that account for the majority of cellular volume in cortical bone","C":"Periosteal stem cells, which reside in the inner cambium layer and represent the most abundant progenitor population in mature bone","D":"Chondroblasts, which persist within cortical bone after endochondral ossification and maintain the calcified matrix in adult life","E":"Osteocytes, which are former osteoblasts fully embedded within the mineralized matrix and connected to neighboring cells via canalicular processes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Osteocytes make up 90–95% of all bone cells. They derive from osteoblasts that become trapped within the matrix they secrete and differentiate into long-lived mechanosensors connected by canalicular processes. Osteoblasts represent only 4–6% of bone cells and reside on active formation surfaces. Osteoclasts are a small fraction derived from hematopoietic precursors. Periosteal stem cells and chondroblasts are not significant resident populations in mature cortical bone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteocytes are the HY \"most abundant bone cell\" — classic single-best-answer. Also know that osteocyte death (lacunar necrosis) is the hallmark of avascular necrosis. COMLEX: Osteocytes respond to Wolff's Law (bone remodels in response to mechanical loading); loss of loading leads to disuse osteoporosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — Differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific MSK tissue types"},{"id":"sdl18_q02","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the histological structure and cellular composition of bone, focusing on the roles of osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and osteocytes.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An osteoblast synthesizes and secretes osteoid prior to its mineralization into mature bone matrix. Which organic component constitutes the primary structural protein within this pre-mineralized scaffold?","choices":{"A":"Type I collagen, which provides the tensile framework upon which hydroxyapatite crystals are subsequently deposited to form mineralized bone","B":"Type II collagen, which is the principal structural protein of hyaline cartilage and is degraded before bone replaces the cartilaginous template","C":"Type III collagen, which is the dominant structural protein of soft connective tissues such as skin and arterial walls in the body","D":"Elastin, which provides elastic recoil and flexibility to connective tissues and is a minor component of ligaments throughout the skeleton","E":"Fibronectin, which anchors cells to the extracellular matrix and facilitates cell migration but does not constitute the primary structural protein"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Osteoid — the pre-mineralized organic matrix secreted by osteoblasts — is composed predominantly of Type I collagen along with non-collagenous proteins such as osteocalcin and osteopontin. Type II collagen is specific to cartilage. Type III collagen is found in soft tissues. Elastin and fibronectin are minor matrix components that do not form the primary structural scaffold of bone. Hydroxyapatite subsequently crystallizes on and between the collagen fibers to create the composite mineral-organic structure of mature bone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteogenesis imperfecta is caused by mutations in Type I collagen (COL1A1/COL1A2) — directly linking collagen type to bone disease. COMLEX: \"Blue sclerae\" in OI occurs because the thin collagen allows underlying choroid to show through — a classic clinical pearl often paired with bone fragility questions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — Differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific MSK tissue types"},{"id":"sdl18_q03","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the histological structure and cellular composition of bone, focusing on the roles of osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and osteocytes.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"During bone resorption, osteoclasts must first dissolve the inorganic mineral phase of bone matrix before the organic matrix can be degraded. Which mechanism accounts for demineralization of the hydroxyapatite crystals?","choices":{"A":"Release of alkaline phosphatase, which catalyzes hydrolysis of phosphate ester bonds within the hydroxyapatite lattice to release calcium and phosphate ions","B":"Secretion of osteocalcin, which chelates calcium ions from the crystal surface through high-affinity gamma-carboxyglutamate residues in the acidic resorption lacuna","C":"Secretion of collagenase and gelatinase enzymes at the ruffled border, which directly degrade the hydroxyapatite mineral phase before the underlying organic matrix is lysed","D":"Secretion of hydrogen ions via an apical proton pump, which creates an acidic microenvironment beneath the ruffled border that dissolves hydroxyapatite crystals","E":"Production of reactive oxygen species by the osteoclast NADPH oxidase, which oxidatively fragment the hydroxyapatite crystal lattice to release mineral ions"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Osteoclasts seal a resorption lacuna against the bone surface using an actin ring, then deploy a ruffled border that secretes H+ via a vacuolar-type H+-ATPase (proton pump). The resulting acidic pH dissolves hydroxyapatite, releasing Ca2+ and PO43−. After demineralization, cathepsins and matrix metalloproteinases degrade the organic matrix. Alkaline phosphatase is an osteoblast marker, not an osteoclast mechanism. Osteocalcin and reactive oxygen species are not the primary demineralization mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Proton pump inhibitors and carbonic anhydrase II inhibitors can impair osteoclast function — carbonic anhydrase II deficiency causes osteopetrosis. COMLEX: Bisphosphonates bind hydroxyapatite and are ingested by osteoclasts, inducing apoptosis — clinically relevant for osteoporosis and Paget disease management.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — Differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific MSK tissue types"},{"id":"sdl18_q04","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the physiological mechanisms and hormones of bone remodeling and mineral homeostasis, emphasizing the balance between bone formation and resorption.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"In the RANKL-mediated pathway of bone remodeling, which cell type produces receptor activator of nuclear factor kappa-B ligand in response to mechanical and hormonal stimuli, thereby driving osteoclastogenesis?","choices":{"A":"Osteoblasts, which express and release RANKL to bind RANK on monocyte precursors and promote their differentiation and fusion into mature multinucleated osteoclasts","B":"Osteoclasts, which produce RANKL in an autocrine fashion to sustain their own activation and amplify bone resorption at sites of high turnover","C":"Parafollicular cells of the thyroid, which release RANKL in response to elevated serum calcium as part of the calcitonin regulatory feedback loop","D":"T lymphocytes, which are the exclusive systemic source of RANKL and drive osteoclastogenesis only under conditions of inflammatory joint disease","E":"Osteocytes, which are solely responsible for RANKL secretion due to their embedded position and direct exposure to mechanical strain signals"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Osteoblasts are the principal producers of RANKL in physiologic bone remodeling. RANKL binds RANK on osteoclast precursors, initiating a signaling cascade that drives their differentiation into mature bone-resorbing osteoclasts. While T lymphocytes can produce RANKL in inflammatory conditions (e.g., rheumatoid arthritis), osteoblasts are the physiologic source. Parafollicular cells produce calcitonin, not RANKL. Osteoclasts and osteocytes are not primary RANKL producers under normal conditions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The RANKL/RANK/OPG axis is a classic high-yield pathway — denosumab is a monoclonal antibody against RANKL used to treat osteoporosis. COMLEX: Understand that PTH paradoxically promotes bone resorption (via RANKL upregulation) with chronic elevation but stimulates formation with intermittent dosing — teriparatide exploits this.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis pathophysiology involving bone remodeling imbalance"},{"id":"sdl18_q05","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the physiological mechanisms and hormones of bone remodeling and mineral homeostasis, emphasizing the balance between bone formation and resorption.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Osteoprotegerin is a key regulatory glycoprotein in the control of bone remodeling. Which of the following best describes the mechanism by which osteoprotegerin limits bone resorption?","choices":{"A":"Osteoprotegerin is secreted by osteoclasts and directly inhibits calcitonin binding at its receptor on the osteoclast surface, reducing the anti-resorptive hormonal response","B":"Osteoprotegerin is secreted by osteoblasts and acts as a soluble decoy receptor that competitively binds RANKL, preventing it from engaging RANK on osteoclast precursors and thereby limiting osteoclast formation","C":"Osteoprotegerin is a transmembrane enzyme on osteocytes that phosphorylates and inactivates RANKL before it can reach circulation and bind distant osteoclast precursors","D":"Osteoprotegerin is a circulating protease derived from hepatocytes that cleaves PTH in the bloodstream, reducing upstream stimulation of the RANKL-dependent resorption cycle","E":"Osteoprotegerin is produced by T lymphocytes and blocks vitamin D receptors on intestinal epithelium to suppress calcium absorption and reduce systemic calcium-driven remodeling"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Osteoprotegerin (OPG) is a member of the TNF receptor superfamily secreted by osteoblasts. It acts as a decoy receptor by binding RANKL with high affinity, preventing RANKL from engaging its functional receptor RANK on osteoclast precursors. This OPG/RANKL ratio is the key determinant of osteoclastogenesis — high OPG favors bone formation, low OPG favors resorption. It is not produced by osteoclasts, T lymphocytes, or hepatocytes, and it has no direct effect on calcitonin or vitamin D receptors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The OPG/RANKL ratio is a critical concept — estrogen increases OPG production, which is why postmenopausal estrogen loss leads to decreased OPG, increased RANKL-driven resorption, and osteoporosis. COMLEX: Denosumab mimics OPG by targeting RANKL; contrast with bisphosphonates (induce osteoclast apoptosis directly).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis pathophysiology involving bone remodeling imbalance"},{"id":"sdl18_q06","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the physiological mechanisms and hormones of bone remodeling and mineral homeostasis, emphasizing the balance between bone formation and resorption.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient has an elevated serum calcium level following excessive vitamin D supplementation. Which cell type responds to this hypercalcemia and what hormone do they release to restore mineral homeostasis?","choices":{"A":"Chief cells of the parathyroid gland, which detect elevated serum calcium and release parathyroid hormone to stimulate osteoblast-mediated bone formation and restore balance","B":"Osteoblasts on endosteal surfaces, which detect local hypercalcemia and secrete an autocrine calcitonin-like peptide that suppresses adjacent osteoclast activity","C":"Parafollicular cells of the thyroid gland, which secrete calcitonin in response to elevated serum calcium to directly inhibit osteoclast activity and reduce bone resorption","D":"Alpha cells of the pancreatic islets of Langerhans, which respond to elevated serum calcium by releasing calcitonin alongside glucagon to promote calcium deposition in bone","E":"Enterochromaffin cells of the duodenal mucosa, which release calcitonin in response to dietary calcium load to limit further intestinal absorption and prevent systemic hypercalcemia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Calcitonin is produced exclusively by the parafollicular (C) cells of the thyroid gland in response to elevated serum calcium. Calcitonin inhibits osteoclast activity, reducing bone resorption and lowering blood calcium. Chief cells of the parathyroid produce PTH (released in response to hypocalcemia, not hypercalcemia). Pancreatic alpha cells produce glucagon; osteoblasts and enterochromaffin cells do not produce calcitonin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Medullary thyroid carcinoma arises from C cells and produces excess calcitonin — serum calcitonin is used as a tumor marker. MEN2A/MEN2B involve RET mutation and include medullary thyroid carcinoma. COMLEX: In clinical practice, calcitonin is used therapeutically for Paget disease and hypercalcemia of malignancy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis pathophysiology involving bone remodeling imbalance"},{"id":"sdl18_q07","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify and discuss the blood supply and innervation of bones and explain the mechanisms of bone repair and regeneration.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which vessel provides the primary arterial blood supply to the medullary cavity and the inner two-thirds of the cortex in a mature long bone, entering through a dedicated foramen in the diaphysis?","choices":{"A":"The nutrient artery, which penetrates the cortex through the nutrient foramen and divides within the medullary canal to supply the marrow and inner cortical bone","B":"The periosteal artery, which runs along the outer surface of the bone and provides the dominant blood supply to the deep medullary cavity throughout adulthood","C":"The metaphyseal artery, which crosses the cortex at the flared metaphyseal region and serves as the primary arterial supply to the diaphyseal medullary space","D":"The epiphyseal artery, which traverses the growth plate in skeletally mature individuals to supply both the epiphysis and the adjacent medullary cavity of the diaphysis","E":"The Volkmann canal artery, which runs perpendicular to the long axis of the bone and provides the sole blood supply to the Haversian systems of the cortex"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The nutrient artery is the principal blood supply to the medullary cavity and inner cortex. It enters through the nutrient foramen and ascends/descends within the medullary canal, giving off branches that supply the marrow and inner cortical osteons. Periosteal arteries supply the outer one-third of the cortex. Metaphyseal and epiphyseal arteries supply their respective regions. Volkmann (perforating) canals transmit branches between periosteal vessels and Haversian canals but are not independent arteries.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Disruption of the nutrient artery (e.g., in femoral neck fractures) causes avascular necrosis — the femoral head receives most of its supply from the medial circumflex femoral artery. COMLEX: In fracture management, understanding the periosteal blood supply explains why excessive periosteal stripping during open reduction delays healing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — Primary regions of long bones and their anatomical organization"},{"id":"sdl18_q08","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify and discuss the blood supply and innervation of bones and explain the mechanisms of bone repair and regeneration.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient with a tibial shaft stress fracture reports severe, well-localized point tenderness over the bone. Which anatomical structure is primarily responsible for the exquisite pain sensitivity of bone to injury and periosteal irritation?","choices":{"A":"The inner cellular layer of the periosteum, which contains osteoprogenitor stem cells and is primarily responsible for nociceptive signaling via its dense afferent innervation","B":"The Haversian canals within cortical bone, which contain abundant sensory nerve fibers running parallel to the osteon and serve as the principal pain-sensing compartment","C":"The outer fibrous layer of the periosteum, which is densely invested with sensory nerve fibers and sympathetic afferents that transmit pain signals upon mechanical deformation or injury","D":"The trabecular surfaces of cancellous bone, which are densely innervated with free nerve endings and are the primary source of nociception in long bone injuries","E":"The endosteum lining the medullary cavity, which contains the highest concentration of pain-sensitive nerve endings and is predominantly responsible for fracture pain"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The outer fibrous layer of the periosteum is richly innervated with sensory (nociceptive) and sympathetic nerve fibers, making it the most pain-sensitive component of bone. This explains why periosteal elevation, tumors invading the periosteum, or stress fractures cause exquisite localized pain. The inner cellular (cambium) layer contains progenitor cells but sparse innervation. Haversian canals carry small vessels and nerves, but they are not the primary pain-sensing structure. The endosteum and cancellous surfaces have relatively sparse innervation compared to the periosteum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Periosteal pain is why bone metastases are so painful — tumor expansion stretches the periosteum. NSAIDs and bisphosphonates help reduce periosteal nociception. COMLEX: OMM consideration — the periosteum transmits viscerosomatic reflexes; periosteal tenderness at Chapman reflex points reflects visceral dysfunction and is used diagnostically.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — Primary regions of long bones and their anatomical organization"},{"id":"sdl18_q09","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify and discuss the blood supply and innervation of bones and explain the mechanisms of bone repair and regeneration.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"In the weeks following a long bone fracture, a fibrocartilaginous bridge spans the fracture gap and stabilizes the fragments. What is this structure called, and what process ultimately converts it into bone?","choices":{"A":"The hard callus, which is a directly mineralized woven bone collar that forms within the first 72 hours after fracture by periosteal osteoblast activation at the injury site","B":"The primary union bridge, which is a direct lamellar bone-to-bone junction that forms when fracture ends are held in rigid contact without any intermediate cartilaginous phase","C":"The remodeling zone, which is the mature lamellar bone structure that replaces disorganized woven bone and is classified as the bridging callus on post-fracture imaging","D":"The soft callus, which is a fibrocartilaginous scaffold that stabilizes the fracture ends and is subsequently replaced by woven bone through endochondral ossification driven by invading osteoblasts","E":"The periosteal sleeve, which is an avascular fibrous collar that encases and immobilizes the fracture gap and is later resorbed entirely before new bone can be laid down"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bone repair proceeds through: (1) hematoma formation, (2) soft callus formation — a fibrocartilaginous bridge that stabilizes the fracture, (3) hard callus — the soft callus is replaced via endochondral ossification by woven bone, and (4) remodeling — woven bone is replaced by organized lamellar bone over months to years. The hard callus appears weeks later, not within 72 hours. Primary union (no callus) occurs only with rigid fixation and direct cortical contact. The remodeling zone is the final stage, not the bridging callus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fracture repair stages are classic HY: hematoma → fibrocartilaginous callus → bony callus → remodeling. Know that NSAIDs impair early inflammatory phase (hematoma/soft callus), potentially slowing healing. COMLEX: Smoking and diabetes impair bone repair — smoking reduces osteoblast activity and impairs angiogenesis needed for callus vascularization.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — Primary regions of long bones and their anatomical organization"},{"id":"sdl18_q10","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the processes of intramembranous and endochondral ossification in bone development and their relevance to skeletal formation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following bones is formed via intramembranous ossification, in which mesenchymal cells differentiate directly into osteoblasts without requiring a cartilaginous intermediate template?","choices":{"A":"The femur, which forms via endochondral ossification in which a hyaline cartilage model is progressively replaced by bone through vascular invasion and osteoblast activity","B":"The skull, which forms when mesenchymal cells migrate to the region, condense into nodules, and directly differentiate into osteoblasts that deposit a collagen-proteoglycan matrix that subsequently calcifies","C":"The tibia, which undergoes endochondral ossification from a primary center in the diaphysis that extends toward epiphyseal growth plates at both ends of the bone","D":"The humerus, which develops through endochondral ossification identical to other long bones of the appendicular skeleton, requiring a cartilaginous template at all stages","E":"The vertebral bodies, which are formed through endochondral ossification from somite-derived cartilaginous precursors during embryonic vertebral column development"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Intramembranous ossification forms flat bones including the skull (calvaria), mandible, clavicle, and some facial bones. In this process, mesenchymal cells differentiate directly into osteoblasts — no cartilage template is needed. Endochondral ossification forms long bones (femur, tibia, humerus) and vertebral bodies, which require a cartilage scaffold that is progressively replaced by bone through vascular invasion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cleidocranial dysostosis involves defective intramembranous ossification of the clavicle and cranial vault (RUNX2 mutation) — can present with absent clavicles allowing shoulder hyperflexion. COMLEX: Fontanelles represent areas of delayed intramembranous ossification — the anterior fontanelle closes by 18 months; delayed closure suggests hypothyroidism or rickets.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — Limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation via mesenchymal differentiation"},{"id":"sdl18_q11","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the processes of intramembranous and endochondral ossification in bone development and their relevance to skeletal formation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"During endochondral ossification of a long bone, where does the primary ossification center first appear, and which event triggers the initial wave of bone matrix deposition at that location?","choices":{"A":"The epiphysis, where hypertrophied chondrocytes first calcify the surrounding matrix and attract osteoblast precursors to initiate the primary ossification sequence","B":"The growth plate, where chondrocyte proliferation produces the initial calcified template that osteoblasts later colonize to establish the primary ossification center","C":"The periosteum, where mesenchymal progenitors first differentiate into osteoblasts and lay down a periosteal collar that anchors the primary ossification process within the diaphysis","D":"The diaphysis, where vascular connective tissue invades the calcified cartilage model and introduces osteoblasts that deposit bone matrix onto the cartilaginous scaffold","E":"The metaphysis, where blood vessel invasion first occurs near the flared ends of the bone and establishes the ossification center before extending into the diaphyseal shaft"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The primary ossification center forms in the diaphysis (shaft) of the long bone. Mesodermal mesenchymal cells first differentiate into chondrocytes that form the cartilage model. Chondrocytes hypertrophy and secrete a matrix that calcifies. The perichondrium then becomes periosteum as a bone collar forms. Vascular connective tissue subsequently invades from the periosteal region, bringing osteoblasts that deposit bone matrix onto the calcified cartilage scaffold. Secondary ossification centers later appear in the epiphyses.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Achondroplasia (FGFR3 gain-of-function mutation) disrupts endochondral ossification at the growth plate, causing the characteristic short-limb dwarfism — long bones are short but membranous bones of the skull are normal. COMLEX: Ossification centers appear at predictable developmental ages — important for reading pediatric radiographs and assessing bone age.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — Limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation via mesenchymal differentiation"},{"id":"sdl18_q12","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the processes of intramembranous and endochondral ossification in bone development and their relevance to skeletal formation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Intramembranous ossification differs fundamentally from endochondral ossification at the cellular level. Which of the following best defines the single most distinguishing feature of intramembranous ossification?","choices":{"A":"Hyaline cartilage serves as an obligate intermediate template that is progressively resorbed and replaced by mineralized bone matrix through vascular invasion and osteoblast recruitment","B":"Periosteal progenitor cells in the inner cambium layer first differentiate into chondrocytes that produce a transitional cartilage model before transdifferentiating into osteoblasts","C":"Mesenchymal cells differentiate directly into osteoblasts without forming a cartilage intermediate, and these osteoblasts deposit a collagen-proteoglycan matrix that binds calcium salts and solidifies into bone","D":"Osteoclasts first resorb a pre-existing cartilaginous scaffold before osteoblasts deposit mineralized matrix into the resulting resorption lacunae to form compact bone","E":"Mesodermal mesenchymal cells produce a primary ossification center in the epiphysis from which bone matrix deposition spreads toward the diaphysis during development"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Intramembranous ossification is defined by direct mesenchymal-to-osteoblast differentiation without a cartilage template. Mesenchymal cells migrate to the ossification site, proliferate, condense into nodules, and some differentiate into osteoblasts. These osteoblasts secrete a collagen-proteoglycan matrix that binds calcium salts and solidifies. Trapped osteoblasts become osteocytes. The entire process occurs without cartilage. Endochondral ossification, by contrast, requires a hyaline cartilage scaffold. The primary ossification center in long bones (endochondral) is in the diaphysis, not the epiphysis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Craniosynostosis results from premature fusion of cranial sutures formed via intramembranous ossification — mutation in FGFR1/2/3 or TWIST1. COMLEX: The clavicle is unique in that it ossifies via both intramembranous (medial portion) and endochondral (sternal end) mechanisms — a classic embryology exam point.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — Limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation via mesenchymal differentiation"},{"id":"sdl18_q13","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the histological structure and cellular composition of bone, focusing on the roles of osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and osteocytes.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old man with known osteogenesis imperfecta presents with his third long bone fracture in two years. Bone biopsy reveals a reduced ratio of osteoid to mineralized matrix and an abundance of large multinucleated cells actively excavating the bone surface. These multinucleated cells are derived from which embryological lineage?","choices":{"A":"Mesenchymal stromal cells of the inner periosteum, which differentiate sequentially into osteoblasts and subsequently fuse into multinucleated giant cells when stimulated by inflammatory cytokines","B":"Hematopoietic stem cells of the granulocyte-macrophage colony-forming unit lineage, which give rise to monocyte precursors that fuse into multinucleated osteoclasts under RANKL stimulation","C":"Neural crest cells migrating from the dorsal neural tube, which provide the progenitor population for all bone-resorbing cells in the craniofacial and axial skeletal compartments","D":"Lateral plate mesodermal cells that differentiate into myoblasts and subsequently transdifferentiate into osteoclast precursors in response to mechanical unloading signals","E":"Endothelial progenitor cells derived from the aorta-gonad-mesonephros region, which give rise to the monocyte-macrophage lineage that fuses into multinucleated bone-resorbing cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Osteoclasts are derived from the hematopoietic granulocyte-macrophage colony-forming unit (GM-CFU) lineage — the same lineage that gives rise to monocytes and macrophages. Monocyte precursors are recruited to bone surfaces and fuse into multinucleated osteoclasts under RANKL and M-CSF stimulation. In osteogenesis imperfecta, defective Type I collagen is produced by osteoblasts (mesenchymal origin), while osteoclasts are hematopoietic-derived and function normally or are even hyperactive. Mesenchymal stromal cells give rise to osteoblasts, chondrocytes, and adipocytes — not osteoclasts.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know both lineages — osteoblasts from mesenchymal stromal cells, osteoclasts from GM-CFU/monocyte lineage. Paget disease involves dysregulated osteoclast activity (measles virus-triggered). COMLEX: In multiple myeloma, plasma cells secrete RANKL and inhibit OPG, driving osteoclast activity and the punched-out lytic lesions on skull X-rays.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — Differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific MSK tissue types"},{"id":"sdl18_q14","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the histological structure and cellular composition of bone, focusing on the roles of osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and osteocytes.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An astronaut returns from 6 months aboard the International Space Station and undergoes DEXA scanning, which shows a 1.5% decrease in lumbar spine bone mineral density. The loss of mechanical loading in microgravity primarily disrupts the function of which bone cell type, leading to the observed reduction in bone formation signaling?","choices":{"A":"Osteoblasts, which are the primary mechanosensors of bone and directly reduce their collagen secretion when gravity-mediated compressive forces are absent from the bone surface","B":"Periosteal stem cells, which rely on repetitive impact loading to maintain their proliferative state and undergo apoptosis during prolonged microgravity exposure","C":"Osteoclasts, which sense the lack of mechanical deformation through integrins on their ruffled border and downregulate their resorptive activity in a microgravity environment","D":"Osteocytes, which detect the absence of mechanical strain through their canalicular network and reduce sclerostin production, allowing increased bone formation under normal loading — the absence of this signal in microgravity tips the balance toward resorption","E":"Haversian canal endothelial cells, which detect fluid shear stress changes caused by reduced loading and decrease vascular growth factor secretion required for bone cell maintenance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Osteocytes are the primary mechanosensors of bone. Embedded within the canalicular network, they detect fluid shear stress caused by mechanical loading and regulate bone remodeling accordingly. Under normal loading, osteocytes suppress sclerostin (which inhibits Wnt/bone formation signaling). In microgravity, absent mechanical stimulus leads to increased sclerostin production, reduced Wnt signaling, decreased osteoblast activity, and net bone loss. Osteoblasts produce matrix but are not the primary sensors. Osteoclasts increase activity in disuse osteoporosis but are not the primary sensing cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sclerostin is produced by osteocytes and inhibits Wnt/beta-catenin signaling, suppressing bone formation — romosozumab is a sclerostin inhibitor approved for severe osteoporosis. COMLEX: Wolff's Law states that bone remodels in response to mechanical stress — disuse osteoporosis from bed rest or paralysis is a common clinical manifestation tested on COMLEX.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — Differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific MSK tissue types"},{"id":"sdl18_q15","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the histological structure and cellular composition of bone, focusing on the roles of osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and osteocytes.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A histology laboratory slide of cortical bone shows a central canal surrounded by concentric rings of mineralized lamellae with osteocytes in lacunae connected by fine radiating channels. A student is asked to identify the contents of the central canal of this structural unit. Which of the following is correct?","choices":{"A":"The central Haversian canal contains a marrow cavity with hematopoietic cells and osteoclast precursors that replenish the bone-resorbing cell population throughout adult life","B":"The central Haversian canal contains lymphatic channels exclusively, which drain interstitial fluid from the bone lacunae and prevent accumulation of metabolic waste products","C":"The central Haversian canal contains unmineralized osteoid and osteoblasts that continuously deposit new matrix in a centrifugal direction, expanding the osteon over time","D":"The central Haversian canal is an empty conduit that allows transmission of mechanical strain waves between adjacent osteons in the cortical bone structure","E":"The central Haversian canal contains blood vessels and nerve fibers that supply the osteocytes within the surrounding concentric lamellae via diffusion through the canalicular network"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The osteon (Haversian system) is the basic structural unit of cortical bone, consisting of concentric lamellae of mineralized matrix surrounding a central Haversian canal. The canal contains one or two small blood vessels (capillaries/arterioles) and nerve fibers. Nutrients and metabolic products diffuse to and from osteocytes through the canalicular network connecting lacunae. Volkmann's canals run perpendicular to Haversian canals, connecting them to the periosteal surface. The marrow cavity is a separate medullary compartment, not within individual osteons.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the osteon hierarchy — Haversian canal (vessels/nerves) → lamellae → lacunae (osteocytes) → canaliculi (connections). Paget disease shows disorganized \"mosaic\" lamellae replacing normal osteons on histology. COMLEX: Osteoporosis thins cortical bone and reduces the number of intact osteons — this explains the increased fracture risk despite apparently normal-looking radiographs until significant mass is lost.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — Differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific MSK tissue types"},{"id":"sdl18_q16","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the physiological mechanisms and hormones of bone remodeling and mineral homeostasis, emphasizing the balance between bone formation and resorption.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with stage 3 chronic kidney disease has a serum calcium of 7.1 mg/dL and an elevated PTH of 320 pg/mL on routine labs. Her provider explains that the elevated PTH is acting on bone to raise serum calcium. Which of the following best describes the mechanism by which PTH increases serum calcium through bone?","choices":{"A":"PTH stimulates osteoblasts to increase RANKL expression and decrease OPG secretion, shifting the balance toward osteoclast activation and bone resorption, which releases calcium and phosphate into the circulation","B":"PTH directly activates osteoclasts by binding PTH receptors on their surface, causing immediate secretion of hydrogen ions and collagenase without requiring osteoblast intermediary signaling","C":"PTH stimulates osteocytes to release sclerostin, which inhibits Wnt signaling in osteoblasts and thereby promotes unopposed osteoclast-mediated bone resorption over time","D":"PTH induces osteoblasts to produce calcitonin locally, which paradoxically stimulates osteoclast activity through a non-canonical intracrine signaling pathway within bone","E":"PTH suppresses OPG expression in T lymphocytes, reducing the circulating anti-resorptive signal and allowing systemic RANKL levels to drive osteoclast differentiation throughout the skeleton"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PTH acts on PTH/PTHrP receptors expressed on osteoblasts (not directly on osteoclasts). PTH upregulates RANKL expression and downregulates OPG on osteoblasts, creating a pro-resorptive environment that activates osteoclast precursors. The resulting osteoclast activity releases Ca2+ and PO43− from hydroxyapatite. This is the dominant mechanism of PTH-mediated hypercalcemia. PTH does not directly activate osteoclasts (they lack PTH receptors in significant numbers). Sclerostin and calcitonin effects described are incorrect in this context.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hyperparathyroidism (primary = parathyroid adenoma) causes hypercalcemia, hypophosphatemia, increased PTH — classic \"bones, groans, moans, stones.\" Renal osteodystrophy (secondary hyperparathyroidism) results from low vitamin D + hyperphosphatemia → low Ca → high PTH. COMLEX: Distinguish primary, secondary, and tertiary hyperparathyroidism — CKD drives secondary; autonomous gland develops into tertiary.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis pathophysiology involving bone remodeling imbalance"},{"id":"sdl18_q17","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the physiological mechanisms and hormones of bone remodeling and mineral homeostasis, emphasizing the balance between bone formation and resorption.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with a T-score of −2.7 at the femoral neck is started on alendronate for osteoporosis. Her physician explains that this medication directly targets the osteoclast to reduce bone resorption. Which of the following correctly describes the cellular mechanism by which bisphosphonates inhibit osteoclast function?","choices":{"A":"Bisphosphonates bind RANKL and function as decoy receptors, preventing RANKL from engaging RANK on osteoclast precursors in a mechanism identical to that of osteoprotegerin","B":"Bisphosphonates bind to hydroxyapatite at bone resorption sites, are ingested by osteoclasts during resorption, inhibit the mevalonate pathway by blocking farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase, and trigger osteoclast apoptosis","C":"Bisphosphonates directly bind the PTH receptor on osteoblasts, preventing RANKL upregulation and thereby reducing downstream osteoclast activation throughout the remodeling cycle","D":"Bisphosphonates enter osteocytes through canalicular channels and inhibit sclerostin secretion, indirectly increasing Wnt-mediated osteoblast formation to outpace osteoclast resorption","E":"Bisphosphonates are incorporated into the hydroxyapatite crystal lattice and increase mineral resistance to acid dissolution, physically preventing osteoclasts from creating resorption lacunae"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bisphosphonates (e.g., alendronate, risedronate) are pyrophosphate analogs that bind avidly to hydroxyapatite at sites of active bone resorption. When osteoclasts resorb the bone surface, they ingest the bisphosphonate. Inside the cell, nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates inhibit farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase in the mevalonate pathway, impairing prenylation of signaling proteins required for osteoclast cytoskeletal function and survival, ultimately triggering apoptosis. Denosumab — not bisphosphonates — binds RANKL. Bisphosphonates have no direct action on PTH receptors, osteocytes, or sclerostin pathways.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bisphosphonates inhibit farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase (mevalonate pathway) — same pathway inhibited by statins. Side effects include esophageal irritation, osteonecrosis of the jaw, and atypical femoral fractures with long-term use. COMLEX: Bisphosphonates are also used in Paget disease and osteogenesis imperfecta to reduce excess osteoclast activity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis pathophysiology involving bone remodeling imbalance"},{"id":"sdl18_q18","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the physiological mechanisms and hormones of bone remodeling and mineral homeostasis, emphasizing the balance between bone formation and resorption.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old man is found to have a serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D of 12 ng/mL and a serum calcium of 8.0 mg/dL. He is started on vitamin D supplementation. At which anatomical site does vitamin D primarily exert its calcium-raising effect, and what is the mechanism?","choices":{"A":"The proximal renal tubule, where activated vitamin D binds to receptors on tubular cells and directly promotes calcium reabsorption from the tubular filtrate into the peritubular capillaries","B":"The osteoclast surface, where activated vitamin D binds receptors and directly stimulates RANKL-independent bone resorption to mobilize calcium stores into the circulation","C":"The small intestine, where activated vitamin D binds nuclear receptors in enterocytes to upregulate calbindin and calcium transport proteins, increasing active transcellular calcium absorption","D":"The parathyroid gland, where activated vitamin D binds chief cell receptors and directly stimulates PTH synthesis and secretion to drive downstream calcium mobilization from bone","E":"The large intestine, where activated vitamin D stimulates passive paracellular calcium absorption through the colonic epithelium without requiring active transport proteins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The primary site of vitamin D action to raise serum calcium is the small intestine (duodenum and jejunum). Active vitamin D (1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D3, calcitriol) binds vitamin D receptors in enterocytes and upregulates calbindin and other calcium transport proteins, dramatically increasing transcellular calcium absorption. Vitamin D also increases renal calcium reabsorption and enhances PTH-induced bone resorption as secondary mechanisms, but the gut effect is primary. Vitamin D actually suppresses PTH synthesis (feedback inhibition) rather than stimulating it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vitamin D deficiency → rickets in children (bowed legs, rachitic rosary) and osteomalacia in adults (bone pain, pseudo-fractures). Know the activation pathway: liver (25-hydroxylation) → kidney (1-alpha-hydroxylation to calcitriol). COMLEX: Granulomatous diseases (sarcoidosis, TB) cause hypercalcemia by producing 1-alpha-hydroxylase in macrophages, creating excess calcitriol.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis pathophysiology involving bone remodeling imbalance"},{"id":"sdl18_q19","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify and discuss the blood supply and innervation of bones and explain the mechanisms of bone repair and regeneration.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old male soccer player sustains a closed mid-shaft tibial fracture. Six weeks later, plain radiographs show a calcified bridge spanning the fracture gap. The orthopedic surgeon explains that this represents normal progression of fracture healing. Which phase of bone repair does this radiographic finding represent?","choices":{"A":"The hematoma phase, in which organized clot at the fracture site has begun to mineralize, forming a calcified scaffold that will eventually be remodeled into compact bone","B":"The soft callus phase, in which fibrocartilaginous tissue spans the fracture gap and is visible on radiograph due to early calcium deposition within the cartilaginous matrix","C":"The remodeling phase, in which lamellar bone has replaced disorganized woven bone and the medullary canal has been restored to its normal architecture throughout the fracture zone","D":"The hard callus phase, in which the fibrocartilaginous soft callus has been replaced by woven bone through endochondral ossification, producing the calcified bridge visible on radiograph","E":"The primary union phase, in which direct bone-to-bone contact between rigidly immobilized fracture ends has allowed osteons to bridge the gap without any callus formation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Fracture repair proceeds through four overlapping stages: (1) hematoma formation (days 1–3, not visible on radiograph), (2) soft callus formation (days 3–21, fibrocartilaginous, radiolucent on X-ray), (3) hard callus formation (weeks 3–12, woven bone replaces fibrocartilage through endochondral ossification — this is the calcified bridging callus visible on radiograph at 6 weeks), and (4) remodeling (months to years, woven bone replaced by lamellar bone). Primary union occurs only with rigid fixation and compressed cortical contact — no callus forms in that scenario.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The 4 stages of fracture repair are high-yield: hematoma → soft callus → hard callus → remodeling. Know that smoking, steroids, and NSAIDs (especially in the early phases) impair healing. COMLEX: Delayed union vs. non-union — non-union occurs when the fracture fails to heal by expected time, often requiring surgical intervention; pseudarthrosis is a fibrous non-union that creates a false joint.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — Primary regions of long bones and their anatomical organization"},{"id":"sdl18_q20","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify and discuss the blood supply and innervation of bones and explain the mechanisms of bone repair and regeneration.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman undergoes resection of a tibial osteosarcoma requiring extensive periosteal stripping over a 10 cm bone segment. Her orthopedic oncologist warns her that this segment will be at elevated risk for delayed healing and avascular necrosis. Which property of the periosteum best explains this clinical concern?","choices":{"A":"The outer fibrous layer of the periosteum contains the nutrient artery branches that are the sole blood supply to the cortical bone, and stripping it eliminates all vascular supply to the segment","B":"The periosteum produces calcitonin locally to regulate osteoclast activity at the bone surface, and stripping it removes the primary anti-resorptive signal needed to prevent cortical thinning","C":"The periosteum physically prevents bacterial invasion of cortical bone, and stripping it exposes the cortex to perioperative infection that disrupts vascular channels and leads to avascular necrosis","D":"The periosteum provides structural rigidity to the cortical bone via its fibrous outer layer, and its removal destabilizes the bone mechanically, preventing normal load-bearing during healing","E":"The inner cellular layer of the periosteum contains osteoprogenitor stem cells that are responsible for appositional bone growth and cortical repair, and stripping it removes the primary source of cells for bone regeneration"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The periosteum has two layers: the outer fibrous layer (protection, tendon attachment, innervation) and the inner cellular layer or cambium (osteoprogenitor/stem cells that can differentiate into osteoblasts and chondroblasts). The inner cambium layer is the critical source of cells for periosteal bone repair and appositional growth. Stripping the periosteum removes this progenitor cell reservoir and disrupts the periosteal blood supply (though the nutrient artery and endosteal supply also contribute). While the periosteum does carry periosteal vessels, it is not the sole vascular supply to cortical bone — the nutrient artery (not within the periosteum) remains the primary supply to the inner cortex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Compartment syndrome is a vascular emergency where elevated pressure within the fascial compartment compromises periosteal and endosteal blood flow — the \"6 Ps\" are classic. COMLEX: Periosteal new bone formation is a key radiographic sign of osteomyelitis; the periosteum elevates off the cortex (Codman triangle in osteosarcoma) due to subperiosteal infection or tumor growth.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — Primary regions of long bones and their anatomical organization"},{"id":"sdl18_q21","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify and discuss the blood supply and innervation of bones and explain the mechanisms of bone repair and regeneration.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An 18-year-old cross-country runner presents with a 3-week history of focal, progressive anterior tibia pain that is worst with activity and partially relieved by rest. Point tenderness is elicited directly over the tibial shaft. Imaging confirms a tibial stress fracture. The exquisite point tenderness in this patient is most directly attributable to irritation of which neural structure?","choices":{"A":"Free nerve endings within the Haversian canals of the cortical osteons, which are the primary nociceptors of compact bone and are directly disrupted by microfracture propagation","B":"Anterior tibial nerve branches running along the interosseous membrane, which are compressed between the tibia and fibula when tibial swelling develops in response to stress injury","C":"Sensory and sympathetic nerve fibers densely investing the outer fibrous periosteum, which are directly stimulated by periosteal elevation and inflammatory mediators at the stress fracture site","D":"Sensory fibers of the saphenous nerve that penetrate the tibial cortex through Volkmann canals and terminate in the medullary cavity adjacent to the endosteum","E":"Nociceptive afferents within the tibial marrow cavity, which are activated by intramedullary pressure elevation from hematoma formation within the medullary sinusoids"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The periosteum is the most richly innervated structure of bone. Its outer fibrous layer contains abundant sensory nerve fibers (primarily A-delta and C-fibers) and sympathetic fibers. At a stress fracture site, repetitive microtrauma elevates the periosteum, activates inflammatory mediators, and directly stimulates these periosteal nociceptors — producing the characteristic exquisite, well-localized point tenderness. Haversian canals do contain small nerve fibers but are not the primary nociceptive structure. The anterior tibial nerve courses anterolaterally and does not penetrate the tibial shaft.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Stress fractures are HY — high-risk sites include the femoral neck (compression vs. tension sides have different management), tibia, and metatarsals. MRI is the gold standard for diagnosis when X-rays are negative. COMLEX: Vitamin D and calcium deficiency predispose athletes (especially female runners with the female athlete triad) to stress fractures through impaired bone mineralization.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — Primary regions of long bones and their anatomical organization"},{"id":"sdl18_q22","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the processes of intramembranous and endochondral ossification in bone development and their relevance to skeletal formation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A medical student studying embryology traces the sequence of events in intramembranous ossification of the calvaria. After osteoblasts deposit collagen-proteoglycan matrix and calcium salts begin to bind and solidify the matrix, which structural formation is the next observable event in this process?","choices":{"A":"A hyaline cartilage template forms around the calcified matrix to provide mechanical stability before vascular invasion occurs and osteoblasts replace the cartilage with lamellar bone","B":"Osteoclasts are recruited to the calcified region to resorb the initial disorganized spicules, and osteoblasts then deposit organized lamellar bone in a centripetal direction","C":"Chondrocytes hypertrophy and secrete alkaline phosphatase into the calcified matrix, creating additional mineralization fronts that accelerate spicule formation and bone collar development","D":"Growth plate chondrocytes proliferate at the margin of each calcified center and drive longitudinal expansion of the flat bone anlage through endochondral ossification","E":"Calcified spicules radiate outward from ossification centers, and trapped osteoblasts within the solidifying matrix differentiate into osteocytes as the region becomes enclosed by the periosteum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In intramembranous ossification, after osteoblasts deposit collagen-proteoglycan matrix and calcium salts bind and solidify it: (1) calcified spicules radiate outward from the ossification center, (2) osteoblasts trapped within the solidifying matrix become osteocytes, and (3) the entire region — consisting of centers with radiating spicules — becomes enclosed within the periosteum. No cartilage template forms at any stage. Osteoclasts participate later in remodeling but are not the next immediate event. Growth plates and chondrocyte proliferation are features of endochondral, not intramembranous, ossification.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Intramembranous ossification produces woven bone initially (rapidly deposited, disorganized collagen) that is later replaced by lamellar bone during remodeling — the same sequence occurs in fracture repair (soft callus → hard callus → remodeling). COMLEX: Fontanelles — the soft spots on a newborn's skull — are areas where intramembranous ossification is incomplete; the anterior fontanelle is the last to close at approximately 18 months.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — Limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation via mesenchymal differentiation"},{"id":"sdl18_q23","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the processes of intramembranous and endochondral ossification in bone development and their relevance to skeletal formation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 10-year-old boy falls from his bicycle and sustains a Salter-Harris Type II fracture of the distal radius. The pediatric orthopedist explains to his parents that this injury is concerning because it involves the growth plate. Which feature of endochondral ossification makes growth plate injury particularly significant in skeletally immature patients?","choices":{"A":"Longitudinal bone growth occurs through chondrocyte proliferation and hypertrophy at the epiphyseal growth plate, and disruption of this zone can lead to premature physeal closure and subsequent limb length discrepancy or angular deformity","B":"The growth plate is the site where intramembranous ossification transitions to endochondral ossification, and injury to this zone eliminates the only mechanism by which flat bones can increase their surface area","C":"All blood supply to the epiphysis travels exclusively through the growth plate cartilage, and fracture at this level causes immediate avascular necrosis of the entire epiphyseal ossification center","D":"The growth plate is the primary location of osteoclast activity in growing bone, and its disruption leads to unchecked osteoblast activity and paradoxical overgrowth of the affected limb","E":"Periosteal stem cells are exclusively located within the growth plate zone, and injury to this region eliminates all appositional growth capacity throughout the remainder of skeletal development"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In endochondral ossification, longitudinal bone growth is driven by chondrocyte proliferation in the zone of proliferation and hypertrophy within the epiphyseal growth plate. Ossification of these cartilage columns at the metaphysis progressively lengthens the bone. The Salter-Harris classification describes fractures involving the physis — injuries to the germinal zone of the growth plate (Salter-Harris III, IV, V) carry the highest risk of premature physeal closure, which can result in limb length discrepancy or angular deformity. Blood supply to the epiphysis comes from epiphyseal arteries that are separate from the growth plate vessels.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Salter-Harris classification is HY: Type I (through physis, \"SALTER\" mnemonic — Straight), II (through physis and metaphysis — most common, best prognosis), III (physis and epiphysis), IV (all three), V (crush — worst prognosis). COMLEX: Always obtain comparison X-rays for pediatric wrist injuries — the physis can be confused with a fracture line.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — Limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation via mesenchymal differentiation"},{"id":"sdl18_q24","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the processes of intramembranous and endochondral ossification in bone development and their relevance to skeletal formation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A first-year medical student is reviewing fetal bone development. She notes that the femur and the skull each begin as collections of mesenchymal cells but develop through entirely different ossification mechanisms. Which single feature most fundamentally distinguishes endochondral ossification of the femur from intramembranous ossification of the calvaria?","choices":{"A":"Endochondral ossification produces only cancellous bone whereas intramembranous ossification exclusively generates cortical bone, reflecting their distinct structural requirements in the adult skeleton","B":"Endochondral ossification requires an obligate hyaline cartilage intermediate that is progressively replaced by bone through vascular invasion, whereas intramembranous ossification involves direct osteoblast differentiation from mesenchymal cells without any cartilaginous phase","C":"Endochondral ossification is driven entirely by neural crest cell-derived progenitors, whereas intramembranous ossification depends exclusively on lateral plate mesodermal cells for bone formation","D":"Endochondral ossification forms bones that contain only red marrow throughout life, whereas intramembranous ossification forms bones that contain exclusively yellow marrow after puberty","E":"Endochondral ossification proceeds without periosteal formation, relying solely on the perichondrium, whereas intramembranous ossification forms a periosteum that is the exclusive source of appositional growth"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The defining difference between the two mechanisms is the presence or absence of a cartilage template. In endochondral ossification, mesenchymal cells first differentiate into chondrocytes, forming a hyaline cartilage model that is then progressively replaced by bone through vascular invasion and osteoblast activity. In intramembranous ossification, mesenchymal cells differentiate directly into osteoblasts — no cartilage is produced at any stage. Both processes can produce cortical and cancellous bone; marrow composition and periosteum formation are not the distinguishing features. Both femur and skull are derived from mesoderm; neural crest contribution is limited to craniofacial bones but is not the defining distinguishing feature of the ossification mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Achondroplasia (FGFR3 gain-of-function) specifically impairs endochondral ossification at the growth plate — long bones are short, but the vault of the skull (intramembranous) is of normal size, producing the characteristic macrocephaly appearance. This distinction is a frequent NBME question. COMLEX: Achondroplasia is the most common form of short-limb dwarfism; foramen magnum stenosis causing myelopathy is a serious complication.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — Limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation via mesenchymal differentiation"}]},{"sdlNumber":19,"title":"Spine & Back Pain","questions":[{"id":"sdl19_q01","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteoarthritis in general.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the primary pathophysiologic mechanism of osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Progressive depletion of proteoglycan content from articular cartilage reduces its viscoelastic properties, rendering it susceptible to fibrillation, chondrocyte apoptosis, and reactive subchondral bone changes","B":"Autoimmune CD4+ T-cell infiltration of the synovium drives pannus formation, which progressively erodes articular cartilage and underlying subchondral bone via matrix metalloproteinase secretion","C":"Deposition of calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate crystals within articular cartilage triggers recurrent acute inflammatory episodes that produce progressive joint space narrowing and fibrillation","D":"Systemic elevation of TNF-alpha and IL-1beta drives neutrophil influx into the joint space, producing enzymatic cartilage degradation and periarticular bone remodeling across multiple joints","E":"Uric acid crystal deposition in the periarticular soft tissues and synovium triggers a local inflammatory cascade that produces chronic joint space narrowing and cartilage surface erosion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"OA begins with chondrocyte dysfunction and loss of proteoglycans from the extracellular matrix, reducing cartilage resilience. This leads to fibrillation, fissuring, and eventual full-thickness cartilage loss. Secondary reactive changes — subchondral sclerosis, cyst formation, and osteophyte formation — follow. Pannus, CPPD, TNF-alpha, and uric acid describe RA, pseudogout, systemic inflammatory arthritis, and gout, respectively.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA = loss of proteoglycans → cartilage fibrillation → joint space narrowing + osteophytes + subchondral sclerosis (no periarticular osteopenia). COMLEX: OA is the most common musculoskeletal complaint in primary care; OMM can address somatic dysfunction and muscle imbalance contributing to joint loading.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 4 — Exercise, physical conditioning, and aging all impact joint health; OA is the prototypical age-driven joint failure syndrome"},{"id":"sdl19_q02","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteoarthritis in general.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following statements best describes the epidemiology of osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Rheumatoid arthritis is the most prevalent joint disease globally, affecting 10 to 15% of the adult population, with peak onset in adults between the ages of 40 and 60 years","B":"Gout is the most common form of inflammatory arthritis and the most prevalent joint disease in men over age 50, with lifetime prevalence exceeding 50% as serum uric acid rises","C":"Psoriatic arthritis is the most frequently diagnosed joint disorder in middle-aged adults, accounting for the largest proportion of primary care musculoskeletal visits annually in the United States","D":"Ankylosing spondylitis is the most prevalent joint disease affecting the axial skeleton, with a radiographic prevalence of 20 to 25% in adults over age 40 in large population studies","E":"Osteoarthritis is the most common form of arthritis worldwide, with prevalence increasing sharply after age 50 and radiographic changes present in the majority of adults over age 65"},"correct":"E","explanation":"OA is the most common arthritis globally. Radiographic prevalence exceeds 70% in those over 65, though symptoms do not always correlate with imaging. Risk factors include age, obesity, prior joint injury, and female sex. RA affects ~1% of adults. Gout is more common than RA but far less than OA. AS affects ~0.1-0.5%.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know OA epidemiology — age is the strongest risk factor; obesity drives knee OA specifically. COMLEX: OA is a named COMLEX Clinical Presentation 8 (musculoskeletal) condition; OMM + weight loss + exercise are first-line osteopathic management principles.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 4 — Exercise, physical conditioning, and aging all impact joint health; OA is the prototypical age-driven joint failure syndrome"},{"id":"sdl19_q03","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteoarthritis in general.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"According to OARSI guidelines, which of the following best describes the first-line approach to managing symptomatic osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Opioid analgesics are preferred over NSAIDs as first-line pharmacologic therapy for OA pain, particularly in elderly patients with elevated cardiovascular risk who cannot tolerate COX inhibition","B":"Exercise therapy and weight loss are the foundational first-line interventions, with NSAIDs added for pain control when nonpharmacologic measures alone provide insufficient symptomatic relief","C":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injections are recommended as the initial intervention for all patients with symptomatic knee OA before attempting any pharmacologic or nonpharmacologic trial","D":"Surgical joint replacement is appropriate as the primary management strategy for patients with radiographic OA and pain, regardless of the duration or response to conservative measures","E":"Disease-modifying antirheumatic drugs targeting synovial inflammation, such as methotrexate and hydroxychloroquine, are the first-line pharmacologic treatment for all symptomatic OA patients"},"correct":"B","explanation":"OARSI and ACR guidelines place exercise and weight loss at the top of OA management — they reduce pain and improve function with minimal risk. NSAIDs (topical or oral) are the preferred analgesics when pharmacotherapy is needed. Intra-articular injections are reserved for inadequate response to first-line therapy. Opioids and DMARDs have no role in standard OA management. Surgery is last resort.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA management ladder — exercise/weight loss → NSAIDs/topical agents → intra-articular injections → joint replacement. COMLEX: OMM addresses muscle imbalance and somatic dysfunction that contribute to abnormal joint loading; integrative approaches (acupuncture, OMM) are COMLEX-favored first steps.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 4 — Exercise, physical conditioning, and aging all impact joint health; OA is the prototypical age-driven joint failure syndrome"},{"id":"sdl19_q04","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the preferred imaging modality when neurological symptoms accompany cervical spondylosis?","choices":{"A":"Plain radiographs with flexion-extension views are the gold standard because they detect dynamic instability and cord compression during movement that static MRI cannot assess at rest","B":"CT myelography is the first-line study for all cases of cervical spondylosis with neurological symptoms because it provides superior visualization of bony osteophytes and foraminal stenosis","C":"MRI is preferred because it directly visualizes disc morphology, spinal cord signal changes, nerve root compression within the foramina, and canal dimensions without radiation exposure","D":"Nuclear bone scan is the preferred study for cervical spondylosis because it identifies active metabolic bone turnover at degenerated levels that radiographs and MRI cannot adequately detect","E":"Ultrasound of the cervical spine is the recommended initial imaging for cervical spondylosis because it provides dynamic visualization with low cost and absence of ionizing radiation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"MRI is the imaging of choice when cervical spondylosis produces neurological symptoms, as it shows cord signal (T2 hyperintensity = myelomalacia), disc morphology, and nerve root compression. Plain radiographs diagnose and monitor spondylosis (narrowing, osteophytes) but cannot visualize cord or soft tissue. CT myelography is reserved for MRI-contraindicated patients. Bone scan and ultrasound have no role in standard evaluation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cervical spondylosis = disc + osteophyte + facet arthropathy. X-ray shows it; MRI defines cord/root involvement. COMLEX: Cervical somatic dysfunction at C5-C6 is a common OMM indication in cervical spondylosis patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — Bone remodeling and RANKL/OPG balance govern vertebral osteophyte formation, the structural hallmark of cervical spondylosis"},{"id":"sdl19_q05","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the approximate prevalence of radiographic cervical spondylosis by age 65, and how does this correlate with symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Cervical spondylosis is radiographically present in approximately 40% of individuals by age 65, with clinical symptoms developing in roughly half of those with positive imaging findings","B":"Radiographic cervical spondylosis is present in approximately 95% of individuals by age 65, but the degree of radiographic change correlates poorly with clinical symptoms","C":"By age 65, radiographic cervical spondylosis is detectable in fewer than 60% of adults, and the presence of osteophytes strongly predicts future development of radiculopathy or myelopathy","D":"Cervical spondylosis develops radiographically in approximately 75% of individuals by age 65, with the C2-C3 level most commonly affected due to its high segmental mobility","E":"Cervical spondylosis affects approximately 50% of adults over age 65, with symptomatic disease developing in the majority of those with radiographic disc space narrowing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Radiographic cervical spondylosis — disc space narrowing, osteophytes, endplate sclerosis — is nearly universal by age 65 (~95%). However, there is a marked disconnect between imaging and symptoms; many patients with severe radiographic changes are asymptomatic. This makes imaging findings alone insufficient to guide management decisions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Key concept — radiographic spondylosis ≠ clinical disease. Do not treat the image. COMLEX: Cervical spondylosis is a COMLEX-recognized condition where somatic dysfunction contributes to symptom amplification; OMM targets the functional component.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — Bone remodeling and RANKL/OPG balance govern vertebral osteophyte formation, the structural hallmark of cervical spondylosis"},{"id":"sdl19_q06","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best characterizes the typical clinical presentation of cervical spondylosis?","choices":{"A":"Acute onset of bilateral arm weakness and bilateral loss of upper extremity reflexes, reflecting simultaneous multilevel nerve root compression across all cervical segments bilaterally","B":"Severe, localized neck pain occurring in discrete episodic attacks separated by completely pain-free intervals of weeks to months, without any associated stiffness or range of motion restriction","C":"Prominent lower extremity signs including bilateral leg weakness and gait difficulty as the dominant presenting features, reflecting early cervical myelopathy before any axial neck pain","D":"Axial neck pain, periscapular aching, and shoulder girdle stiffness with reduced cervical range of motion, often with intermittent upper extremity radiation but without fixed neurological deficits","E":"Cranial nerve deficits including facial numbness and diplopia caused by vertebrobasilar insufficiency from upper cervical disc disease at C1-C2 as the primary presenting complaint"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cervical spondylosis most often presents with chronic axial neck pain, stiffness, and periscapular discomfort. Upper extremity radiation may occur. Fixed neurological deficits (weakness, reflex loss) suggest radiculopathy; bilateral long-tract signs (hyperreflexia, gait instability) suggest myelopathy — both are complications rather than the typical spondylosis presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Spondylosis = neck pain + stiffness ± referred shoulder pain. Radiculopathy = adds dermatomal arm pain/weakness/reflex loss. Myelopathy = adds UMN signs + gait disturbance. COMLEX: Cervical somatic dysfunction with restricted sidebending and paraspinal hypertonicity is the OMM target.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — Bone remodeling and RANKL/OPG balance govern vertebral osteophyte formation, the structural hallmark of cervical spondylosis"},{"id":"sdl19_q07","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best characterizes the diagnostic properties of Spurling's test for cervical radiculopathy?","choices":{"A":"Spurling's test has approximately 90% sensitivity and 45% specificity, meaning a negative result effectively rules out cervical nerve root compression in most clinical settings","B":"Spurling's test has approximately 90% specificity and 45% sensitivity, meaning a positive result strongly suggests nerve root compression but a negative result does not reliably exclude it","C":"Spurling's test has approximately 70% sensitivity and 70% specificity, representing a balanced trade-off that makes it equally useful as both a rule-in and rule-out test for radiculopathy","D":"Spurling's test has equal sensitivity and specificity of approximately 80%, and a positive result combined with MRI findings confirms the need for immediate surgical foraminotomy","E":"Spurling's test has approximately 45% specificity for cervical radiculopathy, which limits its diagnostic utility due to high rates of false-positive results in patients with axial neck pain alone"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Spurling's test (axial compression with ipsilateral neck rotation and lateral flexion) is a specific but insensitive test: ~90% specific, ~45% sensitive. This means a positive test is highly meaningful (rules in radiculopathy), but a negative test does not rule it out. It is best used in conjunction with history, dermatomal examination, and MRI.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Spurling = 90% specific, 45% sensitive — use it to RULE IN cervical radiculopathy. COMLEX: Cervical radiculopathy is a named COMLEX Clinical Presentation; OMM including cervical muscle energy and HVLA (with caution in stenosis) addresses associated somatic dysfunction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow and cervical radiculopathy share a foraminal compression mechanism producing dermatomal sensory loss"},{"id":"sdl19_q08","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient with cervical radiculopathy has numbness in the lateral forearm, thumb, and index finger along with weakness of wrist extension. Which nerve root is most likely affected?","choices":{"A":"C7 radiculopathy produces sensory loss in the medial forearm, ring finger, and little finger with weakness of elbow extension and a diminished triceps reflex at the C7 level","B":"C5 radiculopathy produces sensory loss over the lateral arm and deltoid region with weakness of shoulder abduction and a diminished biceps reflex at the C5 root level","C":"C8 radiculopathy produces sensory loss in the medial forearm with intrinsic hand muscle weakness and diminished grip strength without a reliable deep tendon reflex loss","D":"C6 radiculopathy produces sensory loss in the lateral forearm, thumb, and index finger with weakness of wrist extension and a diminished brachioradialis reflex","E":"T1 radiculopathy produces sensory loss over the medial arm and axilla with interosseous muscle weakness and bilateral reflex changes from bilateral nerve root involvement"},"correct":"D","explanation":"C6 is compressed at C5-C6 and covers the lateral forearm, thumb, and index finger. Motor: wrist extension (ECRL/ECRB). Reflex: brachioradialis. C5 = lateral arm/deltoid, shoulder abduction, biceps reflex. C7 = middle finger, triceps, triceps reflex. C8 = ring/little finger, finger flexion, no reliable reflex.","boardPrep":"Step 1 HY nerve root table: C5 = deltoid/biceps reflex; C6 = wrist extension/BR reflex/thumb-index; C7 = triceps/triceps reflex/middle finger; C8 = hand intrinsics/ring-little. COMLEX: Cervical somatic dysfunction at C5-C6 commonly accompanies C6 radiculopathy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow and cervical radiculopathy share a foraminal compression mechanism producing dermatomal sensory loss"},{"id":"sdl19_q09","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes appropriate initial management of acute cervical radiculopathy without progressive neurological deficits?","choices":{"A":"Immediate surgical discectomy is indicated when Spurling's test is positive, as nerve root inflammation from disc herniation will not resolve within an acceptable timeframe without decompression","B":"Emergent cervical traction as the primary intervention, applied continuously at 10 to 15 pounds for 24-hour periods until the radicular arm pain has fully subsided","C":"Activity modification, analgesics, a short course of oral glucocorticoids for severe pain, and a soft cervical collar for comfort, with physical therapy and OMM added as symptoms allow","D":"Epidural steroid injection within the first 48 hours is mandatory, as early neural blockade is the only intervention proven to prevent permanent neurological deficits from nerve root inflammation","E":"High-dose intravenous methylprednisolone for 3 days followed by surgical foraminotomy, as conservative measures are uniformly ineffective for acute nerve root inflammation in cervical radiculopathy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The majority of acute cervical radiculopathy resolves within 4-6 weeks with conservative management. First-line includes activity modification, NSAIDs/analgesics, a short collar for comfort, and a brief glucocorticoid taper for severe pain. PT, cervical traction, and OMM are added as tolerated. Epidural injections are reserved for persistent symptoms. Surgery is indicated only for progressive neurological deficits or failure after 6-12 weeks of conservative care.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Most cervical radiculopathy resolves conservatively. Surgery only for motor deficits or failed conservative therapy. COMLEX: OMM is a first-line adjunct — cervical HVLA is relatively contraindicated with significant stenosis, but muscle energy and soft tissue techniques are appropriate.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow and cervical radiculopathy share a foraminal compression mechanism producing dermatomal sensory loss"},{"id":"sdl19_q10","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following neurological findings distinguishes cervical myelopathy from uncomplicated cervical radiculopathy?","choices":{"A":"Lower motor neuron signs exclusively, including flaccid paralysis, areflexia, and fasciculations at all levels below the stenotic segment, characterize cervical myelopathy and distinguish it from radiculopathy","B":"Loss of all deep tendon reflexes bilaterally is the hallmark of cervical myelopathy, reflecting global axon degeneration within the central spinal cord at the level of the stenotic segment","C":"Autonomic instability including orthostatic hypotension, anhidrosis, and bowel and bladder incontinence is the earliest and most distinguishing finding of cervical myelopathy from cervical radiculopathy","D":"Strictly unilateral upper and lower extremity weakness with contralateral sensory loss is the classical presentation of cervical myelopathy, reflecting a Brown-Sequard cord hemisection pattern","E":"Upper motor neuron signs — including hyperreflexia below the level of cord compression, spastic gait, positive Hoffman's sign, and positive Babinski sign — indicate spinal cord involvement characteristic of myelopathy"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Myelopathy = spinal cord compression → UMN signs: hyperreflexia, spasticity, Hoffman's sign (finger flexion on flicking middle finger), Babinski, clonus, and gait disturbance. Radiculopathy = nerve root compression → LMN signs at the affected root level (weakness, hyporeflexia, dermatomal sensory loss) without long-tract involvement. These are fundamentally distinct clinical syndromes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Myelopathy = UMN signs (hyperreflexia, Hoffman's, Babinski, spastic gait) + hand clumsiness. Hoffman's sign = flicking the middle finger nail produces involuntary flexion of thumb/index. COMLEX: OMM (HVLA) is CONTRAINDICATED in cervical myelopathy due to cord vulnerability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 1 — Structural features of synovial and fibrous joints explain how facet arthropathy and ligamentum flavum hypertrophy narrow the cervical canal"},{"id":"sdl19_q11","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes Lhermitte's sign and its clinical significance?","choices":{"A":"Lhermitte's sign is an electric shock-like sensation radiating down the spine and into the extremities with neck flexion, indicating posterior column dysfunction from cervical cord compression or demyelination","B":"Lhermitte's sign is a pain response elicited by axial compression of the cervical spine in neutral position, indicating facet joint arthropathy at the symptomatic cervical level","C":"Lhermitte's sign refers to reproduction of radicular arm pain with simultaneous ipsilateral neck rotation and extension, equivalent to Spurling's maneuver performed in the supine position","D":"Lhermitte's sign is defined as reproduction of radicular symptoms by tapping the posterior cervical spinous processes, indicating foraminal nerve root impingement at the level tapped","E":"Lhermitte's sign is involuntary finger flexion elicited by flicking the middle finger downward, indicating upper motor neuron dysfunction in the corticospinal tract at or above C5"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lhermitte's sign (neck flexion → electric shock down spine into limbs) indicates posterior column pathology — compressed or demyelinated dorsal column axons generate ectopic impulses when mechanically deformed by flexion. It occurs in cervical myelopathy from stenosis but is also classic for MS. Note: involuntary finger flexion on middle-finger flick = Hoffman's sign, not Lhermitte's.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lhermitte's = neck flexion → electric shock down spine. Seen in: MS, cervical myelopathy, B12 deficiency. Hoffman's = flick middle finger → thumb/index flex = UMN sign. Both appear on boards — do not confuse. COMLEX: Both findings contraindicate cervical HVLA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 1 — Structural features of synovial and fibrous joints explain how facet arthropathy and ligamentum flavum hypertrophy narrow the cervical canal"},{"id":"sdl19_q12","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the treatment approach for cervical spinal stenosis compared to uncomplicated cervical spondylosis?","choices":{"A":"Cervical spinal stenosis is treated exclusively with immediate surgical laminectomy, as conservative measures uniformly fail and delays increase the risk of permanent spinal cord injury","B":"Cervical spinal stenosis is managed with epidural corticosteroid injections as the primary intervention, providing durable cord decompression equivalent to surgical outcomes in most patients","C":"Initial management mirrors cervical spondylosis — NSAIDs, physical therapy, and OMM — but surgical decompression becomes indicated when myelopathic signs develop or progressive neurological deficits occur","D":"Cervical spinal stenosis requires 12 weeks of immobilization in a rigid cervical orthosis as the primary treatment, after which most patients achieve complete neurological recovery without surgery","E":"Cervical spinal stenosis is treated with long-term oral corticosteroids to reduce cord edema, combined with muscle relaxants for associated paraspinal spasm and cervicogenic headache"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Without myelopathy, cervical stenosis is managed like spondylosis: activity modification, NSAIDs, PT, OMM (with caution — no HVLA in significant stenosis). Surgical indication: myelopathy, progressive neurological deficits, or failure after 6-12 weeks of adequate conservative therapy. Epidural injections may help radiculopathy but do not decompress the cord. Immobilization alone is not definitive treatment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Surgical decompression (laminoplasty or laminectomy with fusion) for myelopathy. Conservative management for asymptomatic or radiculopathy-only stenosis. COMLEX: Soft tissue OMM and muscle energy are safe in stenosis; HVLA at the level of stenosis is contraindicated.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 1 — Structural features of synovial and fibrous joints explain how facet arthropathy and ligamentum flavum hypertrophy narrow the cervical canal"},{"id":"sdl19_q13","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of mechanical back pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the lifetime prevalence of low back pain?","choices":{"A":"Up to 80% of people experience at least one significant episode of low back pain during their lifetime, making it one of the most prevalent and economically burdensome musculoskeletal conditions","B":"Approximately 30% of adults experience at least one clinically significant episode of low back pain during their lifetime, with the majority recovering fully without any medical intervention","C":"Lifetime prevalence of low back pain is approximately 50%, with the highest incidence in adults aged 25 to 45 and prevalence declining significantly after age 60 as activity levels decrease","D":"Low back pain affects approximately 20% of adults at any given time, and the majority of primary care musculoskeletal visits involve lumbar spine disease as the chief complaint","E":"Approximately 65% of adults experience at least one episode of disabling low back pain, with chronic pain developing in one-third and representing the leading cause of opioid prescribing"},"correct":"A","explanation":"LBP lifetime prevalence is ~80%. It is the leading cause of disability worldwide and a top reason for primary care visits. Most acute episodes resolve within 4-6 weeks with conservative management. Approximately 5-10% develop chronic LBP. Psychosocial factors (yellow flags) are stronger predictors of chronification than structural findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 80% lifetime prevalence; most acute LBP resolves without imaging. Red flags: age >50, fever, weight loss, cancer history, IV drug use, neurological deficit, bladder/bowel dysfunction — warrant further workup. COMLEX: LBP is the most common indication for OMM referral; somatic dysfunction is a recognized contributor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — Low back pain is a primary indication for integrative medicine; OMM, acupuncture, and exercise are COMLEX-recognized management strategies"},{"id":"sdl19_q14","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of mechanical back pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the hallmark clinical presentation of cauda equina syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Unilateral sciatica with an absent Achilles reflex and foot drop from single-level L5-S1 nerve root compression that resolves fully with epidural steroid injection within 2 to 3 weeks","B":"Saddle area anesthesia affecting the perineum, inner thighs, and genitalia, combined with bilateral lower extremity weakness and bladder or bowel dysfunction, representing a surgical emergency","C":"Fever, elevated ESR, and bilateral radicular leg pain from infectious epidural abscess that requires IV antibiotics and not urgent surgical decompression in most immunocompetent patients","D":"Bilateral Hoffman's signs and spastic paraplegia from upper motor neuron involvement caused by thoracic spinal cord compression rather than a lumbosacral cauda equina process","E":"Midline low back pain, bilateral hamstring tightness, and positive bilateral straight leg raise without any neurological deficits or sphincter involvement requiring emergent intervention"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cauda equina syndrome (CES) is caused by massive central disc herniation or other mass compressing the cauda equina at or below the conus. Classic triad: saddle anesthesia, bladder/bowel dysfunction (retention more common than incontinence early), bilateral leg weakness/pain. This is a surgical emergency — emergent MRI and urgent decompression within 48 hours to prevent permanent sphincter loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cauda equina = saddle anesthesia + bladder/bowel dysfunction + bilateral leg weakness = EMERGENCY. Next step = emergent MRI lumbar spine. COMLEX: CES is an absolute contraindication to OMM; immediate surgical referral is required.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — Low back pain is a primary indication for integrative medicine; OMM, acupuncture, and exercise are COMLEX-recognized management strategies"},{"id":"sdl19_q15","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of mechanical back pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes first-line management of acute mechanical low back pain without red flags or neurological deficits?","choices":{"A":"Complete bed rest for 2 to 3 weeks combined with opioid analgesics, as early mobilization risks converting an acute injury to chronic pain syndrome through sensitization of spinal pain generators","B":"Immediate lumbar MRI to exclude disc herniation, followed by epidural steroid injection within 72 hours to prevent progression of mechanical pain to chronic radiculopathy","C":"Oral corticosteroids for 10 to 14 days with strict activity restriction, as prostaglandin-mediated inflammation is the primary driver of acute mechanical low back pain tissue damage","D":"Urgent referral to spine surgery within the first week, as structural instability from acute muscle and ligament injury requires evaluation for possible surgical stabilization","E":"Relative rest with continuation of tolerated activities, NSAIDs for analgesia, superficial heat application, and early physical therapy; prolonged bed rest is discouraged as it worsens outcomes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acute mechanical LBP management: stay active (bed rest worsens outcomes), NSAIDs/acetaminophen, heat, and early PT. Muscle relaxants may be added short-term for spasm. OMM is appropriate and COMLEX-favored. Opioids are not first-line. Imaging is not indicated in the first 4-6 weeks without red flags. Most cases resolve within 6 weeks.","boardPrep":"Step 1: No imaging for acute LBP without red flags in first 4-6 weeks. NSAIDs > acetaminophen for LBP. Avoid prolonged bed rest. COMLEX: OMM (soft tissue, muscle energy, counterstrain) for lumbar somatic dysfunction is a high-yield first-line intervention.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — Low back pain is a primary indication for integrative medicine; OMM, acupuncture, and exercise are COMLEX-recognized management strategies"},{"id":"sdl19_q16","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best characterizes the pain pattern of lumbosacral spondylosis?","choices":{"A":"Pain and stiffness are worst upon waking and improve gradually with movement and activity through the day, reflecting joint and disc stiffness that loosens with use","B":"Pain is worst at the end of the workday after sustained activity and loading, and is completely relieved within minutes of lying down flat in the supine position","C":"Pain is intermittent and colicky in character, radiating to the groin, and associated with nausea, distinguishing it from mechanical causes of low back pain","D":"Pain is constant and unrelenting, waking the patient from sleep at night and unresponsive to any positional change, consistent with an underlying destructive or malignant process","E":"Pain occurs exclusively with lumbar flexion and is absent with extension, reflecting anterior disc pathology and differentiating it from posterior element or facet-mediated pain"},"correct":"A","explanation":"LS spondylosis (age-related DDD and facet OA) causes morning stiffness that improves with activity — the classic \"gelling\" phenomenon of degenerative joint disease. Pain may be localized or refer to the buttocks. Night pain, constitutional symptoms, or pain unresponsive to position should raise concern for malignancy or infection (red flags). Colicky radiation to groin suggests renal/urological etiology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Morning stiffness improving with activity = OA/spondylosis pattern. Night pain + weight loss + fever = red flags for malignancy or infection. COMLEX: Lumbar spondylosis commonly presents with restricted spinal motion and somatic dysfunction — OMM is integral to management.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 1 — Osteoblast and osteocyte dysfunction at vertebral endplates drives the disc space narrowing and sclerosis characteristic of lumbosacral spondylosis"},{"id":"sdl19_q17","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which imaging modality is preferred when lumbosacral spondylosis presents with neurological symptoms such as leg pain or weakness?","choices":{"A":"Plain lumbar radiographs with oblique views are the gold standard when neurological symptoms accompany spondylosis, as they directly visualize foraminal narrowing at each lumbar level","B":"CT myelography is the first-line imaging for all patients with lumbosacral spondylosis and neurological symptoms, surpassing MRI in detecting foraminal stenosis and arachnoid adhesions","C":"Nuclear medicine bone scan is indicated for all patients with lumbosacral spondylosis and radicular symptoms, identifying active metabolic changes at degenerated levels better than cross-sectional imaging","D":"MRI is the preferred study because it simultaneously assesses disc integrity, nerve root compression, vertebral endplate Modic changes, ligamentum flavum hypertrophy, and spinal canal dimensions","E":"Discography is the preferred initial imaging for lumbosacral spondylosis with neurological symptoms, identifying the pain-generating disc level through provocative contrast injection testing"},"correct":"D","explanation":"MRI is the imaging workhorse for lumbar spondylosis with neurological symptoms: it shows disc degeneration (T2 signal loss), herniation, end plate Modic changes, facet hypertrophy, ligamentum flavum thickening, and foraminal/central stenosis. Plain X-rays show alignment, disc height, and osteophytes but cannot visualize soft tissue or nerve roots. CT myelography is used when MRI is contraindicated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MRI for neurological symptoms with lumbar spondylosis. X-ray for initial spondylosis workup (alignment, disc height, osteophytes). CT if MRI contraindicated. COMLEX: MRI guides surgical planning; OMM is appropriate while awaiting imaging or during conservative management.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 1 — Osteoblast and osteocyte dysfunction at vertebral endplates drives the disc space narrowing and sclerosis characteristic of lumbosacral spondylosis"},{"id":"sdl19_q18","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the prevalence of lumbosacral spondylosis in the general population?","choices":{"A":"Lumbosacral spondylosis affects fewer than 20% of adults over age 50, with most lumbar degenerative changes representing clinically insignificant incidental imaging findings","B":"Lumbosacral spondylosis has a uniform prevalence of approximately 30% across all adult age groups with no significant increase in prevalence associated with advancing age","C":"Prevalence ranges from 38% to 85% across studied populations, varying with age, imaging modality, and the specific radiographic criteria applied for diagnosis","D":"Lumbosacral spondylosis affects nearly 95% of adults over age 60, mirroring cervical spondylosis prevalence, with most cases being symptomatic and requiring clinical management","E":"Lumbosacral spondylosis is present in fewer than 10% of adults aged 40 to 60 on MRI, increasing only modestly to 25% in those over age 70 in large epidemiologic studies"},"correct":"C","explanation":"LS spondylosis prevalence is wide — 38-85% depending on the population and criteria used — and increases with age. This wide range reflects the spectrum from mild disc height loss to severe multilevel disease. Like cervical spondylosis, radiographic findings often do not correlate with symptoms. Prevalence of asymptomatic degenerative disc changes on MRI exceeds 50% in adults over 40.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Degenerative changes are common and often asymptomatic — do not over-image or over-treat based on MRI findings alone. Treat the patient, not the picture. COMLEX: Somatic dysfunction at lumbar levels is a distinct finding from spondylosis and should be addressed with OMM regardless of imaging.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 1 — Osteoblast and osteocyte dysfunction at vertebral endplates drives the disc space narrowing and sclerosis characteristic of lumbosacral spondylosis"},{"id":"sdl19_q19","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which deep tendon reflex is diminished or absent in L4 nerve root compression, and what motor deficit accompanies it?","choices":{"A":"L4 radiculopathy diminishes the Achilles reflex and produces weakness of plantar flexion from S1 myotome involvement, with sensory loss over the lateral foot and little toe","B":"L4 radiculopathy diminishes the brachioradialis reflex and produces weakness of wrist extension, reflecting a mid-cervical level nerve root rather than a lumbar radiculopathy","C":"L4 radiculopathy produces no reliable reflex change because the L4 root is assessed only by sensory testing over the medial calf without any associated deep tendon reflex","D":"L4 radiculopathy diminishes the patellar reflex and produces weakness of knee extension from quadriceps involvement, with sensory loss over the medial leg and ankle","E":"L4 radiculopathy diminishes the biceps reflex and produces weakness of elbow flexion and forearm supination, reflecting musculocutaneous nerve involvement at the C5-C6 level"},"correct":"D","explanation":"L4 myotome: quadriceps (knee extension). L4 dermatome: medial leg and ankle. L4 reflex: patellar tendon reflex. Compressed at L3-L4 disc. Clinically: difficulty rising from a chair or climbing stairs. L5 = dorsiflexion, no reflex. S1 = plantar flexion, Achilles reflex. Mastering the lumbar myotome-reflex-dermatome triad is essential for localization.","boardPrep":"Step 1 HY lumbar root table: L4 = knee extension/patellar reflex/medial leg; L5 = dorsiflexion + great toe extension/no reflex/dorsum foot; S1 = plantar flexion/Achilles reflex/lateral foot. COMLEX: Can't rise from chair = L2/L3/L4; dragging toe = L5; buckling knee = L3/L4.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity peripheral nerve injury patterns and myotomal mapping are directly applied to lumbosacral radiculopathy localization"},{"id":"sdl19_q20","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the motor deficit and reflex finding in L5 nerve root compression?","choices":{"A":"L5 radiculopathy produces weakness of plantar flexion with loss of the Achilles reflex and sensory loss over the lateral foot, reflecting involvement of the S1 myotome and triceps surae","B":"L5 radiculopathy produces weakness of knee extension and hip flexion with loss of the patellar reflex, reflecting femoral nerve territory and the L4 myotome rather than dorsiflexion weakness","C":"L5 radiculopathy causes proximal lower extremity weakness of hip abduction and knee flexion with preserved distal function and no foot drop on neurological examination","D":"L5 radiculopathy produces isolated ankle eversion weakness with sensory loss limited to the lateral calf, representing common peroneal nerve palsy at the fibular head rather than root pathology","E":"L5 radiculopathy produces weakness of dorsiflexion and great toe extension causing foot drop, with sensory loss over the dorsum of the foot; no reliable deep tendon reflex is associated with the L5 root"},"correct":"E","explanation":"L5 is compressed at L4-L5. Myotome: tibialis anterior (dorsiflexion) and extensor hallucis longus (great toe extension) — foot drop. Dermatome: dorsum of foot and lateral calf. Reflex: none reliably attributed to L5, making motor and sensory testing critical for localization. Peroneal nerve palsy mimics L5 but is distinguished by normal hip abduction and no low back pain.","boardPrep":"Step 1: L5 = foot drop (dorsiflexion weak) + dorsal foot numbness + NO reflex. Key distinguisher: peroneal nerve palsy vs. L5 radiculopathy — check hip abduction (preserved in peroneal palsy). COMLEX: \"Dragging toe\" = L5 root; assess with single-leg heel walking.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity peripheral nerve injury patterns and myotomal mapping are directly applied to lumbosacral radiculopathy localization"},{"id":"sdl19_q21","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the approximate sensitivity of the straight leg raise test for L4-L5 and L5-S1 disc herniation?","choices":{"A":"The straight leg raise test has 95% sensitivity and 95% specificity for lumbosacral radiculopathy, making it the single most reliable clinical test for identifying disc herniation at any level","B":"The straight leg raise test has approximately 40 to 45% sensitivity for lumbar disc herniation, limiting its diagnostic utility and making MRI mandatory for all patients with radicular symptoms","C":"The straight leg raise test has approximately 68 to 80% sensitivity for L4-L5 and L5-S1 disc herniation, making it useful for ruling out lower lumbar disc pathology when negative","D":"The straight leg raise test has 80 to 90% specificity for L4-L5 and L5-S1 disc herniation, meaning a positive result at less than 60 degrees is nearly diagnostic without further imaging","E":"The straight leg raise test has equal sensitivity and specificity of approximately 50%, and the crossed straight leg raise is far more sensitive for identifying lumbar disc herniation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"SLR sensitivity for L4-L5/L5-S1 herniation: 68-80%. Specificity is lower (~40%). A positive SLR = reproduction of radicular pain below the knee at less than 60 degrees of elevation. The crossed SLR (contralateral elevation reproduces ipsilateral radicular pain) is less sensitive but more specific (~90%). SLR is most sensitive for large central or paracentral herniations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SLR = 68-80% sensitive, ~40% specific. Crossed SLR = ~30% sensitive but ~90% specific. Positive = pain below knee at <60°. COMLEX: SLR can be performed with OMM neurological exam; a positive crossed SLR suggests large or central herniation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity peripheral nerve injury patterns and myotomal mapping are directly applied to lumbosacral radiculopathy localization"},{"id":"sdl19_q22","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the hallmark clinical feature of neurogenic claudication from lumbar spinal stenosis?","choices":{"A":"Unilateral calf pain that occurs after a fixed walking distance and is relieved only by cessation of walking, indistinguishable from vascular claudication without Doppler imaging studies","B":"Bilateral leg pain and heaviness with walking that is relieved by sitting, resting, or leaning forward, reflecting increased spinal canal diameter with lumbar flexion","C":"Leg pain with standing but not walking, relieved by lumbar extension and worsened by forward flexion, opposite to the typical positional pattern of lumbar spinal stenosis","D":"Acute onset bilateral lower extremity weakness occurring at rest without any relationship to positional change or walking distance, indicating an acute cord compression emergency","E":"Burning bilateral foot pain at rest that worsens with leg elevation, distinguishing it from vascular claudication where elevation relieves ischemic symptoms in the lower limbs"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Neurogenic claudication from lumbar stenosis: bilateral leg pain, heaviness, or numbness that builds with walking and is relieved by forward flexion (sitting, stooping, leaning on a cart). Mechanism: flexion widens the lumbar canal; extension narrows it. Distinguishing from vascular claudication: neurogenic is relieved by flexion even while standing; vascular is relieved simply by stopping walking regardless of posture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Neurogenic claudication = bilateral leg pain with walking relieved by flexion. Spinal canal narrows in extension, widens in flexion. Shopping cart sign = leans forward for relief. COMLEX: Lumbar somatic dysfunction in extension restriction pattern is common; OMM targets the flexion-relief mechanism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — Hip fracture and vascular disease must be systematically excluded when evaluating bilateral leg pain; neurogenic claudication is distinguished by positional relief"},{"id":"sdl19_q23","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which feature most reliably distinguishes neurogenic claudication from vascular claudication?","choices":{"A":"Neurogenic claudication is relieved by forward trunk flexion (leaning on a shopping cart) because flexion increases lumbar canal diameter; vascular claudication is relieved by stopping walking regardless of body position","B":"Neurogenic claudication is distinguished from vascular claudication by associated absent pedal pulses and abnormal ankle-brachial index, making peripheral arterial disease the primary etiology","C":"Neurogenic claudication is distinguished from vascular claudication by its occurrence exclusively in young patients, whereas vascular claudication occurs only in elderly patients with cardiovascular risk","D":"Neurogenic claudication is reliably distinguished by bilateral Achilles reflex loss and diminished vibration sense below the ankles, findings that are uniformly absent in all cases of vascular claudication","E":"Neurogenic claudication is distinguished by its response to vasodilator therapy, with neurogenic claudication resolving with calcium channel blockers and vascular claudication failing to respond"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The key differentiator: neurogenic claudication is position-dependent (flexion relieves, extension worsens); vascular claudication is demand-dependent (any rest relieves, flexion does not specifically help). Intact pedal pulses support neurogenic etiology. ABI distinguishes the two in ambiguous cases. Vascular claudication produces crampy calf pain that stops promptly with rest in any posture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Neurogenic = forward flexion relieves (shopping cart sign); extension worsens. Vascular = any rest relieves. Both worsen with walking. Check pedal pulses + ABI to distinguish. COMLEX: Lumbar stenosis OMM — sagittal plane restriction pattern common.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — Hip fracture and vascular disease must be systematically excluded when evaluating bilateral leg pain; neurogenic claudication is distinguished by positional relief"},{"id":"sdl19_q24","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which imaging modality is preferred for diagnosis and surgical planning in lumbar spinal stenosis?","choices":{"A":"CT myelography is the required first-line imaging for all patients with lumbar spinal stenosis, providing superior anatomic resolution of bony stenosis and dynamic nerve root compression versus MRI","B":"Plain radiographs with dynamic flexion-extension views are the gold standard for evaluating lumbar spinal stenosis, detecting instability and degenerative spondylolisthesis that static imaging misses","C":"MRI is preferred because it directly visualizes central and foraminal canal dimensions, disc pathology, ligamentum flavum hypertrophy, and facet joint changes without ionizing radiation","D":"Nuclear bone scan is the recommended first-line study for lumbar spinal stenosis because it identifies active metabolic bone turnover at stenotic levels, guiding targeted injection therapy","E":"Electromyography and nerve conduction studies are the preferred initial diagnostic modality, quantifying the degree of nerve root compromise before committing to cross-sectional imaging"},"correct":"C","explanation":"MRI is the gold standard for lumbar spinal stenosis: it shows the cross-sectional canal area, ligamentum flavum buckling, facet hypertrophy, and disc herniation. CT myelography is reserved for patients who cannot undergo MRI. Plain X-rays may show spondylolisthesis and disc height loss but cannot directly assess canal compromise. EMG is useful to confirm radiculopathy but does not image stenosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MRI = diagnostic standard for lumbar stenosis. CT myelography if MRI contraindicated. Look for \"trefoil\" canal shape on axial MRI — classic for severe stenosis. COMLEX: Neurogenic claudication with confirmed stenosis — conservative management first including OMM; surgery for refractory cases.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — Hip fracture and vascular disease must be systematically excluded when evaluating bilateral leg pain; neurogenic claudication is distinguished by positional relief"},{"id":"sdl19_q25","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteoarthritis in general.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman presents with 3 years of worsening bilateral knee pain, morning stiffness lasting less than 30 minutes, and bony DIP joint enlargement. X-ray shows asymmetric joint space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis, and marginal osteophytes bilaterally. ESR and CRP are normal. Which of the following best explains the primary underlying mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Progressive depletion of proteoglycan content from articular cartilage reduces compressive stiffness, causing fibrillation and chondrocyte apoptosis, with secondary reactive subchondral sclerosis and marginal osteophyte formation","B":"Autoimmune CD4+ T-cell infiltration of the synovium with pannus formation progressively erodes articular cartilage and subchondral bone, producing symmetric joint destruction and systemic inflammation","C":"Calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition within articular cartilage triggers recurrent acute inflammatory episodes, producing joint space narrowing and osteophyte formation indistinguishable from primary OA","D":"Systemic elevation of TNF-alpha drives neutrophil influx into the joint space, producing enzymatic cartilage degradation and the reactive periarticular bone changes observed on plain radiography","E":"Uric acid crystal deposition in the periarticular tissues and synovium triggers a local inflammatory cascade, producing the chronic joint destruction and DIP joint enlargement in this patient"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This presentation — chronic knee pain, short morning stiffness, DIP Heberden's nodes, normal inflammatory markers, and asymmetric joint space narrowing with osteophytes — is classic OA. Mechanism: chondrocyte dysfunction → proteoglycan loss → decreased cartilage resilience → mechanical fibrillation → subchondral reactive changes. Pannus (RA), CPPD, TNF-alpha (systemic inflammatory arthritis), and uric acid (gout) each describe different pathophysiology with different clinical profiles.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA = proteoglycan loss → cartilage fibrillation → joint space narrowing + osteophytes + subchondral sclerosis, WITHOUT periarticular osteopenia (distinguish from RA). DIP Heberden's nodes = OA. COMLEX: Short morning stiffness <30 min = OA; >60 min = inflammatory arthritis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — Exercise, physical conditioning, and aging impact cartilage integrity and joint health through mechanisms directly relevant to OA progression"},{"id":"sdl19_q26","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteoarthritis in general.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 72-year-old man presents with progressive right hip pain and difficulty with prolonged walking. Plain radiograph of the right hip shows medial joint space narrowing, subchondral cyst formation at the femoral head, and osteophytes at the acetabular rim. BMI is 31. Which combination of radiographic findings is most characteristic of osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Uniform joint space narrowing with periarticular osteopenia, marginal erosions at the joint edges, and bilateral symmetric involvement is the hallmark pattern that distinguishes OA from this patient's diagnosis","B":"Periarticular osteopenia with central marginal erosions and bilateral symmetric joint space narrowing define the underlying radiographic pattern causing this patient's progressive hip pain","C":"Chondrocalcinosis with calcification of the fibrocartilaginous labrum and periarticular soft tissues is the defining radiographic feature that confirms the diagnosis in this patient","D":"Rapid progressive joint space loss over weeks to months with normal bone density and no osteophyte formation is the radiographic pattern most consistent with this patient's diagnosis","E":"Non-uniform joint space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis and cyst formation, and marginal osteophyte formation without periarticular osteopenia is the classic radiographic tetrad of osteoarthritis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"OA radiographic findings: (1) non-uniform (asymmetric, weight-bearing surface) joint space narrowing; (2) subchondral sclerosis; (3) subchondral cyst formation (synovial fluid forced into bone under pressure); (4) marginal osteophytes; (5) NO periarticular osteopenia. Contrast: RA = symmetric, periarticular osteopenia, marginal erosions, no osteophytes early. CPPD = chondrocalcinosis. Rapid progression = septic arthritis or crystal arthropathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA X-ray mnemonic LOSS — Loss of joint space (non-uniform), Osteophytes, Subchondral sclerosis, Subchondral cysts. No osteopenia. COMLEX: Hip OA commonly produces somatic dysfunction with restricted internal rotation and extension at the hip — OMM addresses this component.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — Aging and physical conditioning alter subchondral bone density and cartilage hydration, directly influencing OA radiographic progression"},{"id":"sdl19_q27","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteoarthritis in general.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman with confirmed bilateral knee osteoarthritis has failed 3 months of exercise therapy, a 10-pound weight loss, and scheduled acetaminophen. She rates her daily pain 7/10 with significant functional limitation. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Initiating long-term opioid therapy with extended-release oxycodone is the recommended next step for moderate OA pain that has not responded adequately to acetaminophen and weight management","B":"Referring for total knee arthroplasty is appropriate at this stage, as failure of one analgesic agent constitutes sufficient evidence that conservative management has been adequately exhausted","C":"Initiating hydroxychloroquine and methotrexate is the recommended escalation step for symptomatic knee OA after failure of first-line analgesic and nonpharmacologic therapy over 3 months","D":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injection provides short-term pain relief and functional improvement in patients with symptomatic knee OA who have failed first-line nonpharmacologic and analgesic therapy","E":"Prescribing scheduled NSAIDs at maximum dose is the most appropriate next step before considering any procedural intervention in this patient with bilateral knee OA"},"correct":"D","explanation":"After failure of first-line measures (exercise, weight loss, acetaminophen), NSAIDs are appropriate if not contraindicated, along with topical agents. Intra-articular corticosteroids provide meaningful short-term (4-6 week) benefit and are a reasonable step before proceeding to surgery. Opioids are not recommended for OA. DMARDs have no role in OA. Arthroplasty is indicated after failure of prolonged conservative management, not after a 3-month trial.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA escalation — exercise/weight loss → NSAIDs/topical → intra-articular injection (corticosteroid or hyaluronic acid) → joint replacement. Opioids are not guideline-recommended for OA. COMLEX: OMM improves joint mechanics; document conservative trial before procedural escalation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — Exercise conditioning reduces joint loading forces and slows OA progression; weight loss has the strongest evidence for knee OA outcomes"},{"id":"sdl19_q28","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 65-year-old man presents with 1 year of bilateral neck pain, morning cervical stiffness, and intermittent periscapular aching. MRI shows disc space narrowing at C5-C6 and C6-C7, vertebral endplate osteophytes, and facet joint hypertrophy. He has full upper extremity strength and intact reflexes. Which of the following best explains the underlying pathophysiology?","choices":{"A":"Inflammatory cytokine production by the synovial lining of the cervical facet joints drives progressive cartilage erosion and bone destruction, producing disc space collapse and endplate irregularity","B":"Hematogenous seeding of the cervical disc space by bacteria causes disc space collapse and adjacent vertebral endplate destruction, producing the MRI findings and bilateral neck pain","C":"Autoimmune attack targeting type II collagen within the nucleus pulposus triggers progressive disc destruction and bilateral facet involvement in this patient with cervical spondylosis","D":"Microcrystalline calcium pyrophosphate deposition within the posterior longitudinal ligament and annulus fibrosus drives disc space narrowing and the osteophyte formation seen on MRI","E":"Age-related nucleus pulposus dehydration reduces disc height, increasing loads on vertebral endplates and facet joints, which drives reactive osteophyte formation, facet arthropathy, and PLL hypertrophy that narrow the spinal canal and foramina"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cervical spondylosis pathophysiology: disc nucleus pulposus desiccates with age → reduced disc height → increased annular fissuring → increased load on facet joints → facet arthropathy → osteophyte formation at endplates and facet margins → PLL and ligamentum flavum thickening → canal and foraminal narrowing. This is a mechanical degenerative process, not inflammatory (as in RA or infection).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cervical spondylosis = disc degeneration + facet arthropathy + osteophyte formation. Disc height loss is seen on X-ray; cord/root compression seen on MRI. COMLEX: Restriction of cervical sidebending and rotation = somatic dysfunction — address with OMM as adjunct to spondylosis management.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — RANKL/OPG balance and bone remodeling hormones govern osteophyte formation at vertebral endplates in cervical spondylosis"},{"id":"sdl19_q29","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with cervical spondylosis has persistent cervical and periscapular pain despite 3 months of NSAIDs. She has increased bilateral upper trapezius tension and restricted C4-C5 sidebending on osteopathic examination, consistent with somatic dysfunction. Which of the following interventions most directly addresses the somatic dysfunction component of her presentation?","choices":{"A":"Prescribing a COX-2 selective NSAID from a different chemical class is the most direct intervention for her somatic dysfunction, as segmental hypertonicity is driven primarily by prostaglandin-mediated inflammation","B":"Referring for cervical epidural steroid injection is the most direct next step for the restricted C4-C5 sidebending and upper trapezius tension documented on osteopathic examination","C":"Ordering repeat cervical MRI is the most direct intervention for the documented somatic dysfunction, as imaging must confirm structural pathology before any manual medicine intervention","D":"Osteopathic manipulative medicine targeting the cervical somatic dysfunction can restore segmental motion, reduce paraspinal hypertonicity, and decrease pain without relying on additional pharmacotherapy","E":"Prescribing a benzodiazepine muscle relaxant for 4 weeks is the most direct treatment for somatic dysfunction with paraspinal hypertonicity in cervical spondylosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Somatic dysfunction — defined as impaired or altered function of related components of the somatic system — is a distinct clinical entity from spondylosis. OMM (muscle energy, soft tissue, counterstrain, high-velocity low-amplitude with appropriate screening) directly addresses restricted joint motion and paraspinal muscle hypertonicity. NSAIDs address inflammation but not the mechanical dysfunction. Epidural injection targets nerve root, not somatic dysfunction. MRI does not treat dysfunction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cervical spondylosis + restricted motion = somatic dysfunction as comorbid finding. COMLEX: OMM is the primary intervention for somatic dysfunction in cervical spondylosis; document restricted sidebending, rotation, or flexion-extension as the dysfunction findings.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — Bone remodeling and osteophyte formation in spondylosis create structural constraints on segmental motion that contribute to somatic dysfunction"},{"id":"sdl19_q30","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with cervical spondylosis presents with 3 months of bilateral hand clumsiness, difficulty buttoning shirts, and gait unsteadiness. Neurological exam reveals hyperreflexia at biceps and triceps bilaterally and bilateral positive Hoffman's signs. Which finding best distinguishes cervical myelopathy from uncomplicated cervical spondylosis?","choices":{"A":"The presence of bilateral periscapular pain and morning cervical stiffness differentiates cervical myelopathy from uncomplicated spondylosis, as axial pain is exclusively a feature of cord compression","B":"Upper motor neuron signs — including hyperreflexia below the compression level, positive Hoffman's sign, and spastic gait — indicate spinal cord involvement, distinguishing myelopathy from uncomplicated spondylosis","C":"A bilaterally positive Spurling's test distinguishes cervical myelopathy from uncomplicated spondylosis, as bilateral foraminal provocation confirms cord-level rather than root-level compression","D":"The bilateral symmetric distribution of upper extremity symptoms distinguishes cervical myelopathy from uncomplicated spondylosis, as radiculopathy is always strictly unilateral in presentation","E":"Bilateral diminished upper extremity reflexes and flaccid hand weakness distinguish myelopathy from uncomplicated spondylosis, representing lower motor neuron involvement at the cord level"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Myelopathy = cord compromise → UMN signs below the lesion: hyperreflexia, positive Hoffman's (finger flexion reflex), positive Babinski, spastic gait, bilateral hand clumsiness. Spondylosis alone produces axial pain without long-tract signs. Spurling's tests foraminal nerve roots (LMN), not cord. Myelopathy may show LMN signs at the exact level of compression (weakness, hyporeflexia at that root) with UMN below — mixed picture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hoffman's sign = flick middle finger → involuntary thumb/index flexion = UMN = myelopathy. Hand clumsiness + gait ataxia + hyperreflexia = cord compression until proven otherwise. MRI urgently if myelopathy suspected. COMLEX: Myelopathy = absolute contraindication to HVLA at the cervical spine.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — Vertebral endplate osteophyte formation in spondylosis is the structural mechanism underlying cord compression and myelopathy development"},{"id":"sdl19_q31","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 46-year-old carpenter presents with right neck pain radiating to the lateral forearm and thumb, worsened with neck extension and ipsilateral lateral flexion. He has numbness in the thumb and index finger, mild wrist extension weakness, and a positive right Spurling's test. Which nerve root is most likely responsible?","choices":{"A":"C6 radiculopathy localizes to the lateral forearm, thumb, and index finger territory with wrist extension weakness and diminished brachioradialis reflex, most commonly from C5-C6 disc herniation","B":"C7 radiculopathy localizes to the dorsal forearm and middle finger with triceps weakness and diminished triceps reflex, arising from C6-C7 disc herniation rather than C5-C6 disc pathology","C":"C5 radiculopathy localizes to the lateral arm and deltoid region with shoulder abduction weakness and diminished biceps reflex, arising from C4-C5 disc herniation rather than C5-C6","D":"C8 radiculopathy localizes to the ring and little fingers with hand intrinsic muscle weakness and diminished grip strength, arising from C7-T1 disc herniation rather than C5-C6","E":"T1 radiculopathy localizes to the medial arm and axilla with interosseous muscle weakness, arising from the C7-T1 disc level and not associated with wrist extension weakness"},"correct":"A","explanation":"C6 root exits at C5-C6 foramen. Sensory: lateral forearm, thumb, index finger (and sometimes middle finger). Motor: wrist extensors (ECRL/ECRB). Reflex: brachioradialis. Worsens with Spurling's (extension + ipsilateral lateral flexion compresses the foramen). This is one of the most commonly compressed cervical roots, along with C7.","boardPrep":"Step 1: C5-C6 disc herniation = C6 root involvement = lateral forearm/thumb/index numbness + wrist extension weakness + brachioradialis reflex loss. C6-C7 = C7 root = middle finger + triceps weakness + triceps reflex loss. COMLEX: Physical exam of cervical roots is a COMLEX clinical skill.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow shares the foraminal compression mechanism, producing dermatomal sensory loss and intrinsic motor weakness analogous to root compression"},{"id":"sdl19_q32","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The same 46-year-old carpenter from the previous question has C6 radiculopathy confirmed on MRI. During counseling about physical examination findings, which of the following best characterizes the sensitivity and specificity of Spurling's test?","choices":{"A":"Spurling's test has approximately 90% sensitivity and 45% specificity, meaning a negative result reliably excludes nerve root compression but a positive result is a relatively weak indicator","B":"Spurling's test has approximately 70% sensitivity and 70% specificity, representing a balanced trade-off making it equally useful as both a rule-in and rule-out test for cervical radiculopathy","C":"Spurling's test has approximately 90% sensitivity and 90% specificity for cervical radiculopathy, making it the single most accurate physical examination finding for nerve root compression","D":"Spurling's test has approximately 45% sensitivity and 90% specificity, meaning it misses roughly half of true radiculopathy cases but a positive result strongly supports nerve root compression","E":"Spurling's test has approximately 45% sensitivity and 45% specificity for cervical radiculopathy, providing no clinically meaningful diagnostic value beyond chance in an unselected population"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Spurling's test (axial compression with ipsilateral rotation and lateral flexion) is a high-specificity, low-sensitivity test: ~45% sensitive, ~90% specific. Clinically: use it to confirm (rule in) suspected radiculopathy. Do not use a negative result to exclude it — many patients with confirmed disc herniation have a negative Spurling's. Sensitivity improves when combined with other provocative and neurological tests.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Spurling's = specific (90%) but not sensitive (45%) → use to RULE IN, not rule out. High specificity = fewer false positives. Low sensitivity = many false negatives. COMLEX: Clinical correlation with OMM exam, including somatic dysfunction level, refines the localization beyond Spurling's alone.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — Provocative physical examination tests for nerve entrapment — Tinel's, Phalen's, Spurling's — share the principle of mechanical compression reproducing neural symptoms"},{"id":"sdl19_q33","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman presents with right neck and posterior arm pain radiating to the dorsal forearm and middle finger. She notes weakness with pushing movements and a reduced triceps deep tendon reflex on the right. Which of the following best localizes this radiculopathy?","choices":{"A":"Biceps reflex loss with weakness of elbow flexion and sensory loss in the lateral forearm and thumb localizes the lesion to C5-C6 nerve root involvement rather than the presentation described","B":"Triceps reflex loss with weakness of elbow extension and sensory loss in the dorsal forearm and middle finger localizes the lesion to the C7 nerve root, most commonly compressed at C6-C7","C":"Brachioradialis reflex loss with weakness of wrist extension and sensory loss in the lateral forearm localizes the lesion to the C6 nerve root rather than the posterior arm and middle finger distribution","D":"Patellar reflex loss with weakness of knee extension and sensory loss over the medial leg localizes the lesion to the L4 nerve root at the lumbar level, not the cervical spine","E":"Achilles reflex loss with weakness of plantar flexion and sensory loss over the lateral foot localizes the lesion to the S1 nerve root at the lumbosacral level, not the cervical spine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"C7 root exits at C6-C7. Sensory: dorsal forearm and middle finger. Motor: triceps (elbow extension) and wrist flexors. Reflex: triceps deep tendon reflex. C7 is the most commonly affected cervical root. Weakness \"with pushing\" corresponds to triceps/elbow extension. C6 = lateral forearm/thumb/BR reflex. C5 = deltoid/biceps reflex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: C7 = middle finger numbness + triceps weakness + triceps reflex loss. Most common cervical root affected. Associated with C6-C7 disc herniation. COMLEX: C7 somatic dysfunction with tenderness at C6-C7 interspinous space — palpatory diagnosis supports level localization.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — Nerve root localization by dermatomal and myotomal mapping is the same principle applied in upper extremity entrapment neuropathies at the elbow and wrist"},{"id":"sdl19_q34","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman presents with 4 months of progressive bilateral hand weakness and difficulty with fine motor tasks, along with gait unsteadiness. Neurological exam shows hyperreflexia at biceps and triceps bilaterally and bilateral positive Hoffman's signs. MRI reveals a central disc herniation at C4-C5 with T2 cord signal change. Which of the following best explains her deficits?","choices":{"A":"Central canal compression at C4-C5 bilaterally damages the corticospinal tracts, producing upper motor neuron signs including hyperreflexia and spastic gait, while T2 cord signal represents compressive myelomalacia or ischemia","B":"Bilateral C5 foraminal compression produces simultaneous bilateral lower motor neuron deficits including hyperreflexia, spastic gait, and hand weakness through axonal degeneration at both foramina","C":"Vertebral artery compression at C4-C5 produces posterior circulation ischemia, causing bilateral upper extremity weakness, hyperreflexia, and gait unsteadiness through cerebellar and brainstem dysfunction","D":"Inflammatory demyelination of the cervical cord produces bilateral upper motor neuron signs; however, the absence of optic nerve involvement makes this the mechanism underlying this patient's deficits","E":"Epidural hematoma compressing the cervical cord at C4-C5 produces the bilateral upper motor neuron deficits; the gradual 4-month progression and absence of trauma are consistent with this mechanism"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Central disc herniation compresses the cervical cord → corticospinal tract damage → UMN signs: hyperreflexia, spasticity, Hoffman's, gait ataxia, hand clumsiness. T2 signal change in the cord = myelomalacia (irreversible ischemic/compressive change) — a poor prognostic indicator requiring urgent surgical decompression. Foraminal (LMN) compression causes hyporeflexia, not hyperreflexia. Vertebral artery compression would cause brainstem/posterior fossa symptoms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: T2 cord signal change on MRI = myelomalacia = surgical emergency. Central cord syndrome = bilateral UMN signs + hand dysfunction + gait ataxia. Hoffman's + hyperreflexia = UMN. COMLEX: HVLA absolutely contraindicated; refer for neurosurgical decompression urgently.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — The structural features of the cervical spinal canal — facet joints, PLL, ligamentum flavum — directly determine the space available for the cord when central stenosis develops"},{"id":"sdl19_q35","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with cervical spinal stenosis confirmed on MRI reports that bending his neck forward causes a brief electric shock-like sensation radiating from his neck down to both feet. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of this symptom?","choices":{"A":"Lhermitte's sign results from transient vertebral artery kinking during neck flexion, producing posterior circulation ischemia perceived as bilateral lower extremity electric sensations","B":"Lhermitte's sign results from stimulation of dorsal horn wide dynamic range neurons at the stenotic level by neck flexion, producing central sensitization and referred pain to the lower extremities","C":"Lhermitte's sign results from mechanical deformation of compressed or demyelinated posterior column axons during neck flexion, generating ectopic action potentials perceived as electric shocks radiating caudally","D":"Lhermitte's sign results from anterior cord compression of the corticospinal tracts during neck flexion, producing transient motor tract depolarization perceived as involuntary muscle contractions","E":"Lhermitte's sign results from mechanical irritation of the dorsal root ganglia at the stenotic level during neck flexion, producing dermatomal paresthesias radiating into the bilateral upper extremities"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Lhermitte's sign: neck flexion → electric shock down the spine → limbs. Mechanism: posterior (dorsal) column axons, when compressed or demyelinated, generate ectopic impulses with mechanical deformation. The caudal radiation reflects the somatotopic organization of the dorsal columns (cervical fibers most medial; sacral most lateral). Seen in cervical myelopathy AND MS (in which it may be the presenting sign). Vertebral artery compression would cause VBI symptoms (diplopia, vertigo).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lhermitte's = posterior column pathology. Causes: MS (classic), cervical myelopathy, B12 deficiency (subacute combined degeneration). Mechanism = ectopic impulses from mechanically deformed demyelinated axons. Do NOT confuse with Hoffman's. COMLEX: Both signs = contraindicate HVLA to cervical spine.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — The posterior columns of the cervical cord are most vulnerable to compression from posterior ligamentum flavum buckling in acquired cervical spinal stenosis"},{"id":"sdl19_q36","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 65-year-old man with cervical spinal stenosis has been managed with NSAIDs and physical therapy for 8 weeks. He now has progressive bilateral hand weakness and new urinary urgency. Repeat MRI shows worsening T2 cord signal at C5-C6. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Continue conservative management for an additional 12 weeks with a rigid cervical orthosis, as natural history data show spontaneous resolution of cord signal change in most patients","B":"Prescribe high-dose oral corticosteroid taper, as cord edema is the primary driver of progressive neurological deterioration and can be reversed with systemic steroid therapy","C":"Cervical epidural steroid injection is the most appropriate next step, as it provides cord decompression equivalent to surgical outcomes in patients with T2 signal change and sphincter symptoms","D":"Progressive myelopathy with worsening cord signal change and new sphincter dysfunction despite adequate conservative management is an indication for urgent surgical decompression — laminoplasty or laminectomy with fusion","E":"Repeat MRI in 3 months to monitor progression before considering surgery, as cervical myelopathy uniformly plateaus without intervention and surgical outcomes worsen with delay"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Indications for surgical decompression in cervical myelopathy: progressive neurological deficits despite adequate conservative management, or moderate-to-severe myelopathy at presentation. Worsening T2 cord signal = myelomalacia (irreversible change if persistent) + new sphincter involvement = urgent surgical referral. Laminoplasty (cord-expansive) or laminectomy with fusion are standard approaches. Epidural injections address radiculopathy, not cord compression. Corticosteroids are not a standard treatment for compressive myelopathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Myelopathy + progressive deficits = surgical emergency. T2 cord signal = myelomalacia = operate before permanent injury. Surgical options: anterior cervical discectomy and fusion (ACDF), laminoplasty, or laminectomy with fusion. COMLEX: OMM is supportive only — does not substitute for surgery in myelopathy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — Understanding the structural anatomy of the cervical spinal canal is essential to understanding why canal compromise from posterior ligamentum flavum and anterior osteophytes produces bilateral cord dysfunction"},{"id":"sdl19_q37","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of mechanical back pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old teacher presents with 3 weeks of acute, non-radiating central low back pain rated 6/10, without leg pain, neurological deficits, fever, weight loss, or bowel or bladder symptoms. Examination shows diffuse paraspinal tenderness with full lower extremity strength and reflexes. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Emergent lumbar MRI is indicated because 3 weeks of unresolved low back pain represents failure of natural history and requires imaging to exclude structural pathology before initiating treatment","B":"Lumbar radiographs should be obtained immediately in this patient because plain films are a low-risk screening tool that excludes compression fracture, spondylolysis, and serious structural disease","C":"EMG and nerve conduction studies should be performed to quantify subclinical nerve root involvement before physical therapy is initiated, as radiculopathy may precede overt clinical symptoms","D":"CT of the lumbar spine is indicated in this patient to evaluate the posterior elements and intervertebral discs at higher resolution than radiography, guiding early management decisions","E":"Without red flag symptoms, routine imaging is not indicated in the first 4 to 6 weeks of acute mechanical low back pain; NSAIDs, relative rest, heat, and early physical therapy are appropriate first-line measures"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acute mechanical LBP without red flags does not require imaging in the first 4-6 weeks. Red flags that DO warrant imaging: age >50, unexplained weight loss, fever, history of cancer, IV drug use, prolonged steroid use, major trauma, neurological deficits, bowel/bladder dysfunction. This patient has none. Early imaging does not improve outcomes and may identify incidental findings that lead to unnecessary intervention.","boardPrep":"Step 1: No imaging for acute LBP without red flags in first 4-6 weeks. This is a high-yield clinical decision point on USMLE. Red flags = cancer, infection, CES, fracture. COMLEX: OMM for lumbar somatic dysfunction is appropriate and recommended; do not wait for imaging to begin manual therapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — Low back pain without red flags is a primary indication for integrative and osteopathic medicine referral before escalating to pharmacologic or procedural interventions"},{"id":"sdl19_q38","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of mechanical back pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 44-year-old man with 2 days of severe low back pain now reports new perianal numbness and difficulty initiating urination. He has bilateral leg pain below the knee. Examination reveals decreased perianal sensation, 3/5 bilateral hip flexor strength, and reduced anal sphincter tone. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Administer IV methylprednisolone and arrange outpatient lumbar MRI within 48 hours, as corticosteroids can partially reverse cauda equina syndrome and avoid the risks of urgent surgery","B":"Perform lumbar puncture for CSF analysis to exclude infectious meningitis before obtaining lumbar MRI, as infectious etiologies must be ruled out before attributing symptoms to disc herniation","C":"This presentation is consistent with cauda equina syndrome and requires emergent lumbar MRI with immediate neurosurgical consultation for urgent decompression","D":"Order EMG of the bilateral lower extremities as the initial step to confirm nerve root involvement before committing to cross-sectional imaging or surgical consultation","E":"Prescribe oral NSAIDs and a muscle relaxant with close outpatient follow-up in one week, as bilateral radicular symptoms with urinary complaints often resolve with conservative management"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cauda equina syndrome (CES) = saddle anesthesia + bladder/bowel dysfunction + bilateral leg weakness. This is a surgical emergency. Next step: emergent MRI lumbar spine, then urgent neurosurgical decompression (within 24-48 hours to preserve bladder function). Delay = permanent incontinence. IV steroids are not standard treatment for CES. LP, EMG, and conservative management all inappropriately delay necessary surgery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CES = emergent MRI → urgent surgery. Saddle anesthesia is pathognomonic. Urinary retention (inability to void) is more common early than incontinence. COMLEX: CES is an absolute OMM contraindication — surgical emergency first.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — Integrative and conservative care is appropriate for mechanical LBP; recognizing when red flags mandate emergent workup is essential to safe practice"},{"id":"sdl19_q39","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of mechanical back pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 46-year-old construction worker on workers' compensation has had low back pain for 7 months. MRI shows mild L4-L5 disc narrowing without herniation or significant stenosis. He has fear-avoidance beliefs, has stopped all physical activity, scores high on the Pain Catastrophizing Scale, and reports job dissatisfaction. Which of the following is the strongest independent predictor of chronic disability in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Psychosocial yellow flags — including fear-avoidance beliefs, pain catastrophizing, workers' compensation, low job satisfaction, and depression — are the strongest independent predictors of chronic disability, outweighing structural imaging findings","B":"The presence of L4-L5 disc space narrowing on MRI is the strongest predictor of chronic disability in this patient, as structural degeneration at this level directly correlates with long-term functional impairment","C":"Duration of pain beyond 6 months alone is the strongest predictor of poor recovery, as chronification is driven purely by the peripheral pain generator and is unrelated to psychological factors","D":"Workers' compensation status alone, independent of all other psychosocial factors, completely accounts for the disability reported and is the strongest single predictor of poor recovery in LBP","E":"BMI and physical deconditioning are the strongest predictors of chronic disability in this patient, as adiposity drives systemic inflammation that amplifies the structural disc degeneration"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Psychosocial yellow flags — fear-avoidance, catastrophizing, depression, anxiety, low job satisfaction, workers' comp — are stronger predictors of chronification and disability than structural imaging findings. The biopsychosocial model is the evidence-based framework for chronic LBP. MRI findings do not predict disability. Addressing yellow flags through cognitive behavioral therapy, graded exercise, and psychosocial support is as important as physical treatments.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Psychosocial factors (yellow flags) predict chronic LBP disability more than imaging. Biopsychosocial model is the framework for chronic LBP management. COMLEX: OMM combined with patient education and addressing yellow flags reflects the osteopathic whole-person approach.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — LBP is the primary indication for integrative medicine; psychosocial yellow flags are a recognized indication for mind-body and behavioral approaches alongside manual therapy"},{"id":"sdl19_q40","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 67-year-old retired teacher presents with 2 years of low back pain and stiffness, worst upon waking and improving after 1 hour of morning activity. She reports mild bilateral buttock pain without leg radiation. X-ray shows disc space narrowing at L4-L5 and L5-S1 with endplate sclerosis and marginal osteophytes. ESR and CRP are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Ankylosing spondylitis presents with morning low back stiffness improving with activity, but is distinguished by sacroiliitis on imaging, elevated inflammatory markers, and positive HLA-B27","B":"Lumbar disc herniation presents with pain radiating below the knee in a dermatomal pattern, worsened by sitting and Valsalva, and is not associated with endplate sclerosis and bilateral osteophytes","C":"Vertebral compression fracture presents with acute-onset localized pain in an osteoporotic patient and shows anterior vertebral height loss on radiograph, not disc narrowing with bilateral osteophytes","D":"Lumbar spinal stenosis presents with bilateral leg pain on walking relieved by forward flexion, without a prominent morning stiffness component or localized bilateral buttock pain at rest","E":"Lumbosacral spondylosis presents with morning stiffness that improves with activity, localized or buttock-referred pain without dermatomal radiation, and X-ray findings of disc narrowing with osteophytes and endplate sclerosis without systemic inflammation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"LS spondylosis (lumbar DDD + OA): morning stiffness < 30-60 min improving with activity, localized or referred buttock/posterior thigh pain (not dermatomal), normal inflammatory markers, X-ray with disc space narrowing + osteophytes + endplate sclerosis. Ankylosing spondylitis: similar morning pattern BUT sacroiliitis + elevated CRP/ESR + HLA-B27. Herniation = dermatomal radiation. Stenosis = neurogenic claudication.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LS spondylosis vs. ankylosing spondylitis — key differentiators: age, sacroiliitis, HLA-B27, elevated ESR/CRP. Both improve with activity. COMLEX: Lumbar spondylosis with restricted motion = somatic dysfunction at L4-L5 or L5-S1; document restriction pattern for OMM treatment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — Osteocyte and chondrocyte dysfunction at vertebral endplates parallels the bone histology of degenerative disc disease in lumbosacral spondylosis"},{"id":"sdl19_q41","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with chronic lumbosacral spondylosis undergoes lumbar MRI. The radiologist reports Modic type I changes at the L3-L4 vertebral endplates. Which of the following MRI signal characteristics corresponds to Modic type I changes?","choices":{"A":"Modic type I changes are hyperintense on T1 and hypointense on T2 sequences, representing fatty marrow replacement at the vertebral endplate indicating chronic, stable degeneration without active inflammation","B":"Modic type I changes are hypointense on both T1 and T2 sequences, representing vertebral endplate sclerosis indicating end-stage degeneration with dense bone mineralization at the disc-vertebra interface","C":"Modic type I changes represent disc material herniating through the endplate, producing a T2-hyperintense defect with cortical endplate disruption on sagittal MRI sequences at L3-L4","D":"Modic type I changes are hypointense on T1 and hyperintense on T2 sequences, representing bone marrow edema and hypervascularity at the vertebral endplate from an active inflammatory or degenerative process","E":"Modic type I changes represent the vacuum phenomenon of advanced disc degeneration, appearing as signal void on both T1 and T2 sequences at the intervertebral disc-endplate junction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Modic endplate changes: Type I = active inflammation/edema = T1 hypo, T2 hyper. Type II = fatty replacement (most common, stable) = T1 hyper, T2 iso/slightly hyper. Type III = sclerosis = T1 hypo, T2 hypo. Type I correlates with active pain. The mnemonic: Type I = Inflammatory (T1 dark, T2 bright). Type II = Fat (T1 bright). Type III = sclerosis (both dark).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Modic type I = active, painful — T1 low, T2 high. Type II = fatty, stable — T1 high. Type III = sclerotic — both low. COMLEX: Modic type I at a level with somatic dysfunction supports targeting that level with OMM, but inflammation may require medical co-management.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — Osteoblast and osteocyte activity at vertebral endplates generates the Modic change pattern; Type I reflects active bone marrow edema from disrupted endplate homeostasis"},{"id":"sdl19_q42","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with lumbosacral spondylosis has taken scheduled naproxen for 4 months without adequate pain relief. She has hypertension and mild CKD limiting continued NSAID use. Physical therapy provided partial benefit. She has no neurological deficits. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Lumbar fusion surgery is the most appropriate next step because failure of two conservative modalities meets the threshold for surgical intervention in lumbosacral spondylosis without neurological deficits","B":"Epidural steroid injection is an appropriate next-step procedural intervention for refractory lumbosacral spondylosis pain when NSAIDs are limited by comorbidities and physical therapy has provided only partial benefit","C":"Long-term opioid therapy with extended-release morphine is the most appropriate next step for this patient, as her renal and cardiovascular comorbidities make all further NSAID use contraindicated","D":"Repeating lumbar MRI and referring to rheumatology is the most appropriate next step, as age, chronic pain, and NSAID failure suggest an inflammatory arthropathy requiring DMARD therapy","E":"Prescribing long-term low-dose oral prednisone is the most appropriate next step for lumbosacral spondylosis refractory to NSAIDs, as chronic corticosteroids reduce the underlying degenerative process"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When NSAIDs are contraindicated and PT provides partial benefit, epidural steroid injections (ESI) offer a procedural option for spondylosis with radicular or axial pain. ESI is not curative but can provide months of relief and facilitate PT engagement. Lumbar fusion requires more exhaustive conservative failure AND structural instability or deformity. Opioids have minimal evidence for chronic LBP and significant risks. Long-term corticosteroids worsen osteoporosis and are not indicated for degenerative disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Epidural steroid injection = next step when conservative measures fail in LBP with radiculopathy or refractory spondylosis. Surgery = last resort. Opioids = poor evidence for chronic LBP, high risk in elderly. COMLEX: OMM continues alongside procedural management and is not mutually exclusive.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — Endplate and osteocyte dysfunction in lumbosacral spondylosis drives the structural changes that lead to clinical pain refractory to conservative management"},{"id":"sdl19_q43","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old man presents with 6 weeks of left buttock pain radiating to the lateral calf and dorsum of the foot. He has difficulty lifting the front of his foot when walking and occasionally catches his toe on the ground. Examination reveals 3/5 left dorsiflexion and great toe extension, intact patellar and Achilles reflexes, and reduced sensation over the dorsum of the left foot. Which nerve root is most likely affected?","choices":{"A":"Plantar flexion weakness, Achilles reflex loss, and sensory loss over the lateral foot and little toe would localize this presentation to the S1 nerve root at the L5-S1 disc level","B":"Knee extension weakness, patellar reflex loss, and sensory loss over the medial leg and ankle would localize this patient's foot drop presentation to the L4 nerve root at the L3-L4 level","C":"Hip flexion and knee extension weakness, patellar reflex loss, and anterior thigh sensory loss would localize this patient's presentation to the L3 nerve root at the femoral nerve territory","D":"Foot drop with weakness of dorsiflexion and great toe extension, sensory loss over the dorsum of the foot, and intact patellar and Achilles reflexes localizes the lesion to the L5 nerve root at the L4-L5 disc level","E":"Ankle eversion weakness and lateral calf sensory loss alone would represent peroneal nerve palsy at the fibular head rather than an L5 nerve root lesion from lumbar disc disease"},"correct":"D","explanation":"L5 myotome: tibialis anterior (dorsiflexion), extensor hallucis longus (great toe extension). L5 dermatome: dorsum of foot, lateral calf. L5 reflex: none (key teaching point). Intact patellar (L4) and Achilles (S1) reflexes confirm L5 rather than L4 or S1. Compressed at L4-L5 disc. Peroneal nerve palsy mimics L5 but hip abduction is preserved and there is no low back pain.","boardPrep":"Step 1: L5 = foot drop + great toe extension weakness + dorsal foot numbness + NO reflex. Most commonly compressed by L4-L5 disc. Distinguish from peroneal nerve palsy: hip abduction normal in peroneal palsy. COMLEX: \"Dragging the toe\" = L5; clinically tested with single-leg heel walking.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 8 — Lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries share the same dermatomal and myotomal mapping used to localize lumbosacral radiculopathy"},{"id":"sdl19_q44","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman presents with right leg pain radiating from the buttock to the posterior thigh, calf, and plantar surface of the foot. She has difficulty standing on her tiptoes. Examination reveals 4/5 plantar flexion on the right, absent right Achilles reflex, and reduced pinprick sensation over the right lateral foot and little toe. Which nerve root is most likely compressed?","choices":{"A":"Dorsiflexion weakness, foot drop, and sensory loss over the dorsum of the foot with intact Achilles reflex are the classic triad of L5 nerve root compression rather than the S1 presentation described","B":"Knee extension weakness, diminished patellar reflex, and sensory loss over the medial calf and ankle define L4 nerve root involvement rather than the posterior leg and plantar foot distribution","C":"Plantar flexion weakness, absent Achilles reflex, and sensory loss over the lateral foot and little toe are the classic triad of S1 nerve root compression at the L5-S1 disc level","D":"Hip abductor weakness, preserved lower extremity reflexes, and lateral thigh numbness define L2 to L3 nerve root involvement rather than the posterior chain sensory and reflex pattern described","E":"Bilateral leg weakness with bowel or bladder dysfunction and saddle anesthesia defines cauda equina syndrome rather than the unilateral radicular pattern with isolated S1 reflex loss described"},"correct":"C","explanation":"S1 myotome: gastrocnemius/soleus (plantar flexion — heel raises). S1 dermatome: lateral foot, little toe, posterior calf. S1 reflex: Achilles. Compressed at L5-S1. Clinical test: inability to stand on tiptoes = S1 weakness. Distinguish from S1 from L5: L5 = dorsiflexion + dorsal foot; S1 = plantar flexion + lateral/plantar foot + Achilles loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: S1 = can't do heel raises (plantar flexion weak) + Achilles reflex loss + lateral foot/little toe numbness. Compressed by L5-S1 disc. COMLEX: \"Can't walk on heels\" = L4/L5 (dorsiflexion); \"Can't walk on toes\" = S1 (plantar flexion).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injury patterns and the Achilles reflex arc are the same mechanisms evaluated in lumbosacral radiculopathy and peripheral nerve injury"},{"id":"sdl19_q45","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 54-year-old man has a 4-week history of left leg pain. Straight leg raise on the left reproduces radicular pain below the knee at 35 degrees of elevation. A crossed straight leg raise on the right also reproduces left leg pain. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of the straight leg raise test?","choices":{"A":"The straight leg raise compresses the sacroiliac joint by externally rotating the hip in the supine position, reproducing sacroiliac joint pain that radiates to the buttock and posterior thigh","B":"The straight leg raise places traction on the L4, L5, and S1 nerve roots as they exit the foramina; pain radiating below the knee at less than 60 degrees indicates tension on an inflamed or compressed lower lumbar nerve root","C":"The straight leg raise stretches the hamstring muscles, and pain at less than 60 degrees indicates hamstring tightness or proximal hamstring tendinopathy rather than nerve root compression","D":"The straight leg raise loads the L4-L5 facet joints by extending the lumbar spine in the supine position, reproducing facet-mediated pain that radiates to the lateral thigh in facet arthropathy","E":"The straight leg raise measures sciatic nerve mobility at the popliteal fossa, and a positive test at any angle confirms tibial neuropathy rather than a lumbar disc herniation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"SLR mechanism: hip flexion with knee extension stretches the sciatic nerve and its contributing roots (L4, L5, S1). When a root is inflamed or compressed by a disc herniation, this tension reproduces radicular pain below the knee at <60°. A positive crossed SLR (contralateral elevation reproduces ipsilateral pain) = ~30% sensitive but ~90% specific, suggesting large or central herniation. SLR does not test the sacroiliac joint, hamstrings, or facets.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SLR positive = pain below knee at <60° = inflamed nerve root. Sensitivity 68-80% for L4-L5/L5-S1 herniation. Crossed SLR = high specificity (~90%) for large herniation. COMLEX: SLR is part of standard lumbar OMM neurological screening before any high-velocity technique.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 8 — The straight leg raise test tensioning L4-S1 nerve roots is the same principle used in lower extremity peripheral nerve tension tests for sciatic nerve mobility"},{"id":"sdl19_q46","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 75-year-old retired mailman presents with bilateral leg heaviness and cramping that begins after walking 2 blocks. He leans forward on his grocery cart, which dramatically reduces his symptoms. Resting and sitting forward both relieve the pain. Lower extremity pulses are 2+ bilaterally and ankle-brachial indices are normal. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Lumbar spinal stenosis producing neurogenic claudication causes bilateral leg pain with walking that is specifically relieved by forward flexion because lumbar flexion increases spinal canal diameter; intact pedal pulses exclude vascular claudication","B":"Peripheral arterial disease producing vascular claudication causes bilateral calf pain with walking relieved only by cessation of walking regardless of body position, with diminished pedal pulses and abnormal ankle-brachial indices","C":"Bilateral L4-L5 disc herniation producing bilateral radiculopathy causes leg pain with walking without a consistent positional relief pattern related to trunk flexion and is not relieved by a forward-flexed posture","D":"Hip osteoarthritis producing bilateral thigh and groin pain with walking is relieved by resting but is not specifically alleviated by forward trunk flexion or leaning on a support structure during ambulation","E":"Bilateral piriformis syndrome causing sciatic nerve compression produces posterior thigh and buttock pain with activity that is not specifically relieved by forward trunk flexion or shopping cart sign maneuvers"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Neurogenic claudication from lumbar stenosis: bilateral leg pain/heaviness with walking, specifically relieved by forward flexion (lumbar canal diameter increases in flexion) — classically \"shopping cart sign.\" Normal pedal pulses and normal ABI exclude vascular claudication. Vascular claudication is relieved by any rest (not position-specific). MRI will confirm stenosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Neurogenic claudication = forward flexion relief (shopping cart sign). Confirm with MRI showing cross-sectional canal area <75 mm² (severe stenosis). Normal ABI = not vascular. COMLEX: Lumbar stenosis in extension-restricted somatic dysfunction pattern — OMM facilitates flexion and improves function in adjunctive role.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — Hip pathology and vascular disease must be systematically excluded when evaluating bilateral lower extremity pain with ambulation before attributing to neurogenic claudication"},{"id":"sdl19_q47","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The 75-year-old patient from the previous question with confirmed lumbar spinal stenosis is being evaluated further. His internist is concerned about peripheral arterial disease as an alternative explanation. Which of the following features most specifically distinguishes neurogenic from vascular claudication?","choices":{"A":"Neurogenic claudication is specifically distinguished from vascular claudication by its strictly bilateral distribution, as vascular claudication from aortoiliac disease is always unilateral and never presents bilaterally","B":"Neurogenic claudication is distinguished from vascular claudication exclusively by the presence of numbness and tingling, as vascular claudication produces only cramping pain without any sensory symptoms","C":"Neurogenic claudication is distinguished from vascular claudication by its occurrence exclusively in patients under age 60, whereas vascular claudication occurs only in patients over age 70 with established PAD","D":"Neurogenic claudication is distinguished from vascular claudication by its failure to respond to rest, as vascular claudication does not respond to any positional maneuver and requires surgical revascularization","E":"Neurogenic claudication is specifically relieved by lumbar flexion (stooping, sitting, leaning forward) because flexion increases the canal cross-sectional area; vascular claudication is relieved by standing still regardless of posture"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The key discriminating feature: neurogenic claudication is position-dependent (forward flexion specifically relieves it, even while still standing); vascular claudication is simply demand-dependent (any rest relieves it regardless of position). Other differences: vascular = diminished pulses, skin changes, abnormal ABI; neurogenic = positive shopping cart sign, normal pulses. Both can be bilateral. Both can cause numbness.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Neurogenic claudication vs vascular claudication — the discriminating feature is forward flexion relieving neurogenic but NOT vascular. Both relieved by rest, but neurogenic also relieved by forward posture while still standing. COMLEX: The shopping cart sign is the COMLEX-favored clinical discriminator.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — Vascular disease produces hip and leg pain with ambulation that must be distinguished from the neurogenic claudication of lumbar stenosis using ABI, pulse exam, and the positional flexion test"},{"id":"sdl19_q48","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with MRI-confirmed severe central lumbar spinal stenosis at L3-L4 and L4-L5 presents after 6 months of physical therapy, NSAIDs, and two epidural steroid injections. She continues to have neurogenic claudication limiting walking to less than half a block. She has no significant surgical comorbidities. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Continuing epidural steroid injections indefinitely at 3-month intervals is the appropriate management for lumbar stenosis that has failed conservative therapy, as surgical outcomes are equivalent to injection therapy at 5 years","B":"Surgical decompression via lumbar laminectomy with or without fusion is indicated for lumbar spinal stenosis with persistent neurogenic claudication after failure of adequate conservative management including epidural injections","C":"Intrathecal morphine via implanted pump is the next appropriate step for lumbar stenosis refractory to two epidural steroid injections, providing analgesia without the risks of open surgical decompression","D":"Repeating lumbar MRI in 6 months to reassess stenosis severity before considering any procedural intervention is the most appropriate next step, as neurogenic claudication uniformly stabilizes without surgery","E":"Prescribing a walker and permanent activity restriction is the appropriate management for elderly patients with lumbar stenosis who fail conservative therapy, as surgical decompression is contraindicated over age 65"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Surgical indication for lumbar stenosis: persistent, functionally limiting neurogenic claudication after failure of adequate conservative management (typically 3-6 months including PT and ESI). Standard procedure: lumbar laminectomy (decompression), with fusion if instability or spondylolisthesis coexists. Evidence (SPORT trial) shows surgical superiority over conservative management for severe stenosis at 2-4 years. Age alone is not a contraindication. Intrathecal pumps are not standard for stenosis. Indefinite ESI is not appropriate after two failures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lumbar stenosis surgical indication = persistent neurogenic claudication after failed conservative management (PT + ESI). SPORT trial demonstrated surgical benefit at 2-4 years. Laminectomy = standard decompression. Fusion added if spondylolisthesis present. COMLEX: OMM is adjunctive; does not substitute for surgery when indicated.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — Hip and vascular causes of leg pain with ambulation are excluded by workup before surgical planning for lumbar stenosis, ensuring the correct etiology is targeted"}]},{"sdlNumber":22,"title":"NSAIDs, Analgesics & Opioids","questions":[{"id":"sdl22_q01","sdlNumber":22,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the common properties of the kinetics of NSAIDs.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis asks why her naproxen must be taken twice daily while her friend's piroxicam is dosed only once daily. Which pharmacokinetic property most directly determines NSAID dosing frequency?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic first-pass extraction rate, which determines the fraction of an oral dose that reaches systemic circulation before entering the portal circulation and undergoing presystemic metabolism","B":"Renal clearance rate, which governs the daily volume of plasma cleared of drug and determines the risk of toxicity accumulation in patients with impaired kidney function","C":"Elimination half-life, which ranges from approximately 2 hours for ibuprofen to approximately 50 hours for piroxicam, governs how often a dose must be administered to maintain therapeutic concentrations","D":"Volume of distribution, which determines how extensively a drug distributes into peripheral tissues and fat depots relative to the plasma compartment","E":"Oral bioavailability, which determines the maximum achievable plasma concentration after a single dose and sets the upper bound for therapeutic exposure"},"correct":"C","explanation":"NSAIDs vary widely in half-life: ibuprofen ~2 h, naproxen ~12-17 h, piroxicam ~50 h. Half-life determines the dosing interval needed to maintain therapeutic plasma and synovial fluid concentrations. Bioavailability, volume of distribution, and clearance all influence pharmacokinetics but it is half-life specifically that dictates how frequently a drug must be redosed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Piroxicam (t1/2 ~50 h) = once daily; naproxen (t1/2 ~12-17 h) = twice daily; ibuprofen (t1/2 ~2 h) = three to four times daily. COMLEX: NSAID dosing frequency reflects half-life; synovial fluid concentrations can outlast serum concentrations for short-t1/2 agents.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis management includes NSAIDs; kinetic properties determine dosing interval and joint-level drug accumulation"},{"id":"sdl22_q02","sdlNumber":22,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the common source of mechanism of action for NSAID anti-inflammatory activity.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pharmacology student is asked to describe the primary mechanism by which NSAIDs reduce inflammation, pain, and fever. Which of the following best describes this mechanism?","choices":{"A":"NSAIDs activate lipocortin, which inhibits phospholipase A2 and prevents the release of arachidonic acid from membrane phospholipids, thereby blocking all downstream eicosanoid synthesis upstream of cyclooxygenase","B":"NSAIDs block nuclear factor kappa-B nuclear translocation, reducing transcription of pro-inflammatory cytokines including TNF-alpha, IL-1, and IL-6 in activated macrophages and synoviocytes","C":"NSAIDs directly antagonize bradykinin B2 and histamine H1 receptors on sensory nerve fibers, preventing peripheral nociceptor activation without affecting prostaglandin biosynthesis","D":"NSAIDs inhibit cyclooxygenase enzymes, blocking the conversion of arachidonic acid to prostaglandins, thromboxanes, and prostacyclin, thereby reducing mediators of pain sensitization, vasodilation, and hypothalamic fever responses","E":"NSAIDs upregulate heme oxygenase-1 expression in endothelial cells, increasing production of anti-inflammatory carbon monoxide and biliverdin that suppress leukocyte adhesion and activation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The primary mechanism of all NSAIDs is COX (cyclooxygenase) inhibition. COX-1 and COX-2 convert arachidonic acid to PGH2, the precursor to prostaglandins, thromboxanes, and prostacyclin. Prostaglandins sensitize nociceptors, promote vasodilation, and act on the hypothalamus to raise body temperature. Blocking COX reduces all these effects. The other choices describe mechanisms of corticosteroids, biologics, or secondary NSAID actions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: COX-1 = constitutive (gastric protection, platelet aggregation); COX-2 = inducible (inflammation, fever). NSAIDs block both (nonselective) or COX-2 only (celecoxib). COMLEX: Prostaglandins mediate inflammation and sensitize nociceptors; COX inhibition reduces PGE2-dependent fever set point in the hypothalamus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — Bone remodeling is partially regulated by prostaglandins; COX-derived PGs modulate osteoclast and osteoblast activity"},{"id":"sdl22_q03","sdlNumber":22,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe other secondary mechanisms of action common for NSAIDs.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"An attending explains that indomethacin's anti-inflammatory potency exceeds what would be expected from COX inhibition alone. Which of the following best describes indomethacin's secondary mechanisms of action?","choices":{"A":"Indomethacin inhibits phospholipase A2 and phospholipase C, reduces neutrophil chemotaxis and adhesion, suppresses free radical production, inhibits lymphocyte proliferation, and reduces IL-1 synthesis independently of COX inhibition","B":"Indomethacin activates peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma in macrophages, shifting polarization toward an anti-inflammatory phenotype and suppressing NF-kB target gene transcription in synovial tissue","C":"Indomethacin competitively blocks bradykinin B2 receptors and histamine H1 receptors at peripheral nociceptors, reducing afferent pain signal transduction independently of prostaglandin synthesis inhibition","D":"Indomethacin inhibits 5-lipoxygenase in mast cells and eosinophils, blocking leukotriene C4 and D4 synthesis and preventing bronchoconstriction and vascular permeability changes at sites of inflammation","E":"Indomethacin stabilizes lysosomal membranes in neutrophils and macrophages, preventing release of proteolytic enzymes and reactive oxygen species that would otherwise amplify tissue inflammation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Indomethacin and several other NSAIDs have secondary anti-inflammatory mechanisms including inhibition of phospholipase A2/C (reducing arachidonic acid availability upstream of COX), inhibition of neutrophil migration and adhesion, suppression of free radical production, and reduction of T- and B-cell proliferation. These account for its clinical potency beyond COX inhibition alone and also contribute to its toxicity profile.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Secondary NSAID mechanisms include phospholipase inhibition, anti-chemotaxis, and lymphocyte suppression — these are lower-yield but distinguish indomethacin as a particularly potent anti-inflammatory. COMLEX: Indomethacin is used for ankylosing spondylitis and gout; its broad mechanisms underlie both efficacy and toxicity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — NSAIDs exert secondary effects on connective tissue and joint physiology beyond COX inhibition"},{"id":"sdl22_q04","sdlNumber":22,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Discuss adverse effects of NSAIDs by body system.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with knee osteoarthritis takes ibuprofen 600 mg three times daily. After six months he develops epigastric pain and is found to have a gastric ulcer on endoscopy. Which mechanism best explains NSAID-induced gastric mucosal injury?","choices":{"A":"NSAIDs undergo extensive enterohepatic recirculation, directly damaging the colonic and gastric mucosa through repeated high-concentration biliary re-exposure to the chemically irritant parent drug compound","B":"COX-1 inhibition reduces prostaglandin E2 and prostacyclin in gastric mucosa, impairing mucus and bicarbonate secretion, decreasing mucosal blood flow, and leaving the epithelium vulnerable to acid-peptic injury","C":"NSAIDs block parietal cell histamine H2 receptors, paradoxically increasing gastrin-stimulated basal acid output and reducing the efficacy of endogenous mucosal protection mechanisms","D":"COX-2 inhibition in gastric epithelial stem cells impairs mucosal regeneration and prevents healing of micro-erosions caused by normal mechanical and chemical stress from gastric contents","E":"NSAIDs directly activate mast cells in the gastric submucosa, triggering histamine release that increases acid secretion through H2 receptors while simultaneously causing local vascular permeability changes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"COX-1 is constitutively expressed in gastric mucosa and produces PGE2 and prostacyclin that stimulate mucus and bicarbonate secretion, promote mucosal blood flow, and inhibit acid secretion. NSAID-mediated COX-1 inhibition removes this protection, exposing the epithelium to acid-peptic damage. Selective COX-2 inhibitors (celecoxib) spare gastric COX-1 and have significantly lower GI ulcer rates.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gastric protection by COX-1 prostaglandins is the key concept — inhibiting COX-1 removes the mucus/bicarbonate/blood flow triad. Add a PPI or use celecoxib to reduce risk. COMLEX: GI adverse effects are the most common reason NSAIDs are discontinued; H. pylori co-infection multiplies the risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 9 — Long-term NSAID adverse effects (GI, renal, CV) are central considerations in the knee arthritis diagnostic and management workflow"},{"id":"sdl22_q05","sdlNumber":22,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss adverse effects of NSAIDs by category (salicylates, COX-2 selective inhibitors, nonselective COX inhibitors).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A hematologist is counseling an orthopedic patient about NSAID selection. She explains that one class carries a critical antiplatelet effect that can persist for over a week. Which statement best distinguishes aspirin from nonacetylated salicylates such as salsalate regarding platelet effects?","choices":{"A":"Aspirin irreversibly acetylates platelet COX-1, permanently inhibiting thromboxane A2 synthesis for the full platelet lifespan of 8 to 10 days; nonacetylated salicylates do not inhibit platelet aggregation because they lack the ability to acetylate COX","B":"Nonacetylated salicylates selectively inhibit COX-2 in platelets, reducing prostacyclin production while leaving thromboxane A2 intact, thereby paradoxically promoting platelet aggregation compared to aspirin","C":"Aspirin causes immune-mediated thrombocytopenia through hapten formation, reducing platelet counts and prolonging bleeding time; nonacetylated salicylates prolong aggregation time through reversible COX-1 binding only","D":"Nonacetylated salicylates cause greater platelet inhibition than aspirin because their longer plasma half-lives allow more sustained exposure of platelet COX-1 to the active salicylate moiety","E":"Aspirin activates fibrinolysis through tissue plasminogen activator upregulation in endothelial cells; nonacetylated salicylates inhibit fibrinolysis through plasminogen activator inhibitor-1 induction in hepatocytes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aspirin is unique among salicylates in permanently acetylating serine-530 on COX-1. Because platelets are anucleate, they cannot synthesize new COX-1; therefore, the antiplatelet effect lasts the entire ~8-10 day platelet lifespan. Nonacetylated salicylates (salsalate, choline magnesium trisalicylate) do not acetylate COX and thus do not inhibit platelet aggregation, making them suitable for patients with bleeding risk or aspirin-exacerbated asthma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Aspirin = only NSAID that covalently (irreversibly) modifies COX. All others = reversible competitive inhibitors. COMLEX: Nonacetylated salicylates tolerated in aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease (no COX-1 platelet effect, no leukotriene shunting).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — Hip osteoarthritis management requires selecting NSAIDs by category based on individual patient risk profile (GI, CV, renal)"},{"id":"sdl22_q06","sdlNumber":22,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the mechanism of action for other analgesics including acetaminophen, ketorolac, and tramadol.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old man with moderate musculoskeletal low back pain is prescribed tramadol. His provider explains that tramadol works differently from both traditional NSAIDs and pure opioids. Which statement best describes tramadol's mechanism of action?","choices":{"A":"Tramadol is a selective COX-2 inhibitor with no opioid receptor activity, providing anti-inflammatory analgesia through prostaglandin inhibition without the respiratory depression or abuse potential of opioids","B":"Tramadol is a full mu-opioid agonist with additional anticholinergic properties that reduce smooth muscle spasm contributing to musculoskeletal pain without affecting reuptake transporters","C":"Tramadol inhibits both COX-1 and COX-2 while activating alpha-2 adrenoceptors in the dorsal horn, reducing substance P release through a presynaptic mechanism independent of opioid receptor engagement","D":"Tramadol is a selective delta-opioid receptor agonist that modulates limbic system pain processing through hippocampal circuits without affecting spinal cord neurotransmission or reuptake transporters","E":"Tramadol acts as a weak mu-opioid agonist while also inhibiting serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake transporters, modulating descending pain inhibitory pathways in the spinal cord and brainstem"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tramadol combines two distinct mechanisms: weak mu-opioid receptor agonism (via the active O-desmethyltramadol metabolite) and inhibition of both serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake, similar to SNRIs. This dual action modulates descending inhibitory pain pathways. The SNRI component creates significant risk for serotonin syndrome when combined with SSRIs, MAOIs, or other serotonergic drugs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tramadol = weak mu agonist + SNRI. Risk: serotonin syndrome with SSRIs/MAOIs; lower seizure threshold. Not a first-line analgesic for patients on serotonergic drugs. COMLEX: Tramadol serotonin syndrome is a tested clinical presentation; also note it requires CYP2D6 for active metabolite formation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — Mechanical back pain management employs acetaminophen, ketorolac, and tramadol as non-opioid analgesic options"},{"id":"sdl22_q07","sdlNumber":22,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the mechanism of action of opioid medications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A medical student is studying opioid receptor pharmacology for Step 1. Which of the following best describes the receptor type, signal transduction mechanism, and net physiological effect shared by mu, delta, and kappa opioid receptors?","choices":{"A":"Opioid receptors are ligand-gated chloride channels that directly hyperpolarize neurons upon agonist binding, bypassing second-messenger cascades and producing instantaneous inhibition of action potential generation","B":"Mu receptors couple exclusively to Gs proteins, increasing cAMP and activating protein kinase A to open calcium channels and facilitate ascending pain signal transmission in the dorsal horn","C":"Delta and kappa receptors couple to Gq proteins, activating phospholipase C to increase IP3 and diacylglycerol, triggering protein kinase C-mediated excitatory neuronal effects that paradoxically reduce pain perception","D":"Mu, delta, and kappa receptors are Gi/o-coupled GPCRs that decrease cAMP, close presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels, and open postsynaptic inwardly rectifying potassium channels, collectively reducing neuronal excitability and pain transmission","E":"Opioid receptors activate receptor tyrosine kinase cascades similar to growth factor receptors, producing long-term synaptic potentiation in pain processing circuits through ERK and Akt phosphorylation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"All three classical opioid receptors (mu, delta, kappa) are Gi/o-coupled GPCRs. Gi/o activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase (decreasing cAMP), closes N-type presynaptic Ca2+ channels (reducing neurotransmitter release), and opens GIRK (inwardly rectifying K+) channels postsynaptically (causing hyperpolarization). The net result is reduced neuronal excitability and pain signal propagation at all levels of the pain pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: mu/delta/kappa = Gi/o GPCRs. Endogenous ligands: beta-endorphin (mu preferred), enkephalin (delta preferred), dynorphin (kappa preferred). COMLEX: Opioid receptor classification is core pharmacology; Gi/o coupling is the unifying mechanism across all three subtypes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — Opioid receptor pharmacology is foundational to understanding acute pain management in femoroacetabular dislocations"},{"id":"sdl22_q08","sdlNumber":22,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Outline acceptable uses for opioid medications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A primary care physician is reviewing the current opioid prescribing framework with a third-year student. Which of the following represents an acceptable and guideline-supported use of opioid medications?","choices":{"A":"First-line therapy for mild-to-moderate chronic low back pain in patients who have not yet tried non-opioid analgesics such as NSAIDs, acetaminophen, physical therapy, or cognitive behavioral therapy","B":"Routine long-term maintenance therapy for osteoarthritis of the knee or hip when patients report inadequate response to acetaminophen after only two weeks of therapy","C":"Management of acute severe pain (postoperative, trauma-related), cancer-related pain at any stage, and palliative or end-of-life care where comfort is the primary treatment goal","D":"Preferred initial treatment for all neuropathic pain syndromes over gabapentin, pregabalin, tricyclic antidepressants, or SNRIs in adult outpatient settings with documented chronic pain","E":"Long-term monotherapy for fibromyalgia syndrome when physical therapy and antidepressant medications have been trialed for fewer than four weeks without achieving complete symptom resolution"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Opioids are appropriate for acute severe pain (surgery, trauma), cancer pain at any stage, and palliative/end-of-life care. They are not first-line for chronic non-cancer musculoskeletal pain, where non-opioid agents and physical therapy should be exhausted first. Long-term opioid use for OA or fibromyalgia is generally not guideline-supported as first- or second-line therapy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the three main acceptable opioid indications. For chronic pain, CDC guidelines emphasize non-opioid alternatives first. COMLEX: Acceptable opioid use is a tested clinical presentation; addiction medicine and responsible prescribing are COMLEX-emphasized topics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Opioid prescribing guidelines apply to Achilles tendinopathy and musculoskeletal pain management"},{"id":"sdl22_q09","sdlNumber":22,"batch":1,"objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Discuss adverse effects of opioid medication.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 56-year-old man with chronic cancer pain has taken oral morphine at stable doses for two years. He requires the same dose for adequate analgesia as when he started. He continues to have severe constipation despite daily laxative use. Which explanation is most accurate for this finding?","choices":{"A":"Respiratory depression does not develop tolerance because brainstem pre-Botzinger complex mu receptors remain constitutively sensitized throughout chronic opioid therapy regardless of dose or duration","B":"Constipation and miosis persist throughout chronic opioid therapy because enteric nervous system mu receptors and pupillary sphincter neurons do not undergo the same receptor desensitization and downregulation as CNS pain circuits","C":"Euphoria does not develop tolerance because mesolimbic dopamine release in the nucleus accumbens is mediated by delta receptors, which are not subject to the Gi-mediated desensitization that affects mu receptors","D":"Sedation is uniquely resistant to tolerance because cortical arousal pathways in the ascending reticular activating system are constitutively protected from receptor internalization by scaffold proteins","E":"Nausea is the opioid adverse effect most resistant to tolerance because vestibular system chemoreceptor trigger zone neurons have constitutively high baseline mu receptor expression that resists downregulation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Tolerance develops to most CNS-mediated opioid effects (analgesia, euphoria, sedation, respiratory depression) through receptor desensitization, downregulation, and uncoupling from Gi/o proteins. However, constipation and miosis do not develop tolerance because peripheral myenteric plexus mu receptors (governing GI motility) and iris sphincter neurons do not undergo the same adaptive changes. This is why laxatives are always co-prescribed with chronic opioids.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tolerance does NOT develop to constipation or miosis — classic board point. COMLEX: Always prescribe prophylactic laxative with chronic opioids; stimulant laxatives (senna) preferred over osmotic agents for opioid-induced constipation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — Opioid adverse effects inform chronic shoulder pathology pain management decisions"},{"id":"sdl22_q10","sdlNumber":22,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the common properties of the kinetics of NSAIDs.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with hip osteoarthritis is switched from naproxen 500 mg twice daily to piroxicam 20 mg once daily. Her nurse practitioner explains that the new medication only requires once-daily dosing. Which pharmacokinetic property of piroxicam specifically explains this change in dosing schedule?","choices":{"A":"Piroxicam undergoes zero-order elimination kinetics, meaning its serum concentration falls linearly rather than exponentially after each dose, enabling predictable once-daily accumulation without inter-dose troughs","B":"Piroxicam has an elimination half-life of approximately 50 hours, allowing once-daily dosing to maintain therapeutic plasma and synovial fluid concentrations throughout the 24-hour interval between doses","C":"Piroxicam is an inactive prodrug that requires hepatic bioactivation to its active sulphide metabolite, and this activation process extends the pharmacological effect well beyond the measurable serum half-life of the parent compound","D":"Piroxicam undergoes extensive enterohepatic recirculation, recapturing excreted drug from bile back into the systemic circulation and thereby maintaining steady-state concentrations without requiring repeated daily dosing","E":"Piroxicam achieves 100 percent oral bioavailability and fully saturates plasma protein binding sites after a single dose, eliminating distribution-phase fluctuations and maintaining stable free drug concentrations throughout the day"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Piroxicam has one of the longest half-lives among NSAIDs (~50 h), enabling once-daily dosing and sustained synovial fluid concentrations. Naproxen has a t1/2 of ~12-17 h (twice daily) and ibuprofen ~2 h (three to four times daily). Nabumetone is the prodrug in the NSAID class. Enterohepatic recirculation is relevant to some NSAIDs but is not the primary explanation for piroxicam dosing interval.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAID half-life table is high-yield — ibuprofen (2 h), naproxen (12-17 h), piroxicam (50 h). COMLEX: Once-daily dosing improves adherence; piroxicam used in elderly patients where simplicity matters, though its long t1/2 also means delayed offset if toxicity occurs.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis management: NSAID half-life governs dosing interval and duration of anti-inflammatory effect in joint-level drug distribution"},{"id":"sdl22_q11","sdlNumber":22,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the common source of mechanism of action for NSAID anti-inflammatory activity.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with a history of myocardial infarction takes low-dose aspirin 81 mg daily for secondary prevention. His cardiologist explains that aspirin's antiplatelet effect is irreversible, unlike other NSAIDs. Which mechanism best explains why aspirin's COX inhibition permanently eliminates thromboxane A2 synthesis in platelets for 8 to 10 days?","choices":{"A":"Aspirin is the only NSAID small enough to cross the platelet plasma membrane and reach intracellular COX-1 stores; other NSAIDs are excluded by the platelet membrane lipid bilayer and can only inhibit surface-associated COX","B":"Aspirin selectively inhibits COX-2 at low doses while simultaneously acetylating a different serine residue on COX-1 that produces 15-epi-lipoxin A4, an endogenous anti-aggregatory mediator that suppresses platelet function for days","C":"Aspirin covalently acetylates serine-530 on the COX-1 active site, permanently inactivating the enzyme; since platelets are anucleate and cannot transcribe or translate new COX-1 protein, the inhibition lasts the entire platelet lifespan","D":"Aspirin is metabolized to salicylate in the liver, and it is the salicylate metabolite that permanently binds the ADP receptor P2Y12 on platelet surfaces, preventing aggregation signal transduction independently of the COX pathway","E":"Aspirin irreversibly inhibits both COX-1 and COX-2 with equal potency at any dose; other NSAIDs selectively target COX-2 at clinical doses and therefore preserve thromboxane A2 synthesis from platelet COX-1"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Aspirin uniquely acetylates serine-530 on COX-1 (and serine-516 on COX-2), forming a covalent bond that permanently inactivates the enzyme. In nucleated cells, new COX protein can be synthesized within hours, restoring function. Platelets, being anucleate, cannot replace inactivated COX-1 for the rest of their 8-10 day lifespan. All other NSAIDs are reversible competitive inhibitors, so their antiplatelet effect resolves as drug is cleared.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Aspirin = irreversible (covalent) COX acetylation; all other NSAIDs = reversible competitive inhibition. Platelets anucleate = no new COX synthesis = lasting antiplatelet effect. COMLEX: Preoperative aspirin cessation timing is based on platelet lifespan (~10 days for full recovery).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — Knee ligament injuries: perioperative antiplatelet management requires understanding aspirin irreversible COX-1 acetylation and the timeline for platelet recovery"},{"id":"sdl22_q12","sdlNumber":22,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe other secondary mechanisms of action common for NSAIDs.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with osteoarthritis of both knees asks his rheumatologist whether long-term NSAID use might offer any cancer prevention benefits. The rheumatologist mentions epidemiological data suggesting reduced colorectal cancer incidence with regular NSAID use. Which secondary mechanism best explains this potential protective effect?","choices":{"A":"NSAIDs upregulate p53 tumor suppressor expression in colonic epithelial cells by reducing prostaglandin E2-mediated ubiquitination of p53 protein, enhancing apoptotic elimination of genetically damaged pre-malignant cells","B":"NSAIDs reduce vascular endothelial growth factor expression in colonic stroma by blocking COX-2-derived 12-hydroxyeicosatetraenoic acid signaling, impairing angiogenesis in early adenoma microenvironments","C":"NSAIDs stimulate Toll-like receptor 4 signaling in colonic macrophages, increasing local interferon-gamma production that activates cytotoxic T-lymphocytes against dysplastic epithelial cells expressing altered surface antigens","D":"COX-2 inhibition in colonic epithelium reduces prostaglandin E2-driven cellular proliferation and suppression of apoptosis in pre-malignant cells; epidemiological studies show up to 50 percent reduction in colorectal cancer risk with five or more years of NSAID use","E":"NSAIDs inhibit the Wnt signaling pathway in intestinal stem cells by reducing beta-catenin phosphorylation downstream of prostaglandin receptor activation, decreasing stem cell renewal in the colon crypt epithelium"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A well-documented secondary benefit of NSAIDs — particularly aspirin and indomethacin — is colorectal cancer risk reduction. COX-2 is overexpressed in colonic adenomas and carcinomas; its product PGE2 promotes cell proliferation and suppresses apoptosis. Regular NSAID use inhibits this pathway, with epidemiological data showing ~50% reduction in colorectal cancer incidence with long-term use. This is distinct from COX primary anti-inflammatory mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: COX-2 overexpressed in colorectal adenomas/carcinomas — NSAIDs reduce cancer risk via PGE2 reduction. COMLEX: Secondary NSAID mechanisms include chemopreventive properties; indomethacin is particularly well-studied in this regard.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — Exercise and joint aging: secondary NSAID mechanisms reduce oxidative and proliferative tissue injury in chronically inflamed tissues beyond primary COX inhibition"},{"id":"sdl22_q13","sdlNumber":22,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Discuss adverse effects of NSAIDs by body system.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with NYHA Class II heart failure and knee osteoarthritis is prescribed ibuprofen by his orthopedic surgeon. One week later, his serum creatinine rises from 1.1 to 2.4 mg/dL. His urine output has decreased. Which mechanism best explains this NSAID-induced acute kidney injury?","choices":{"A":"Renal prostaglandins normally maintain afferent arteriolar vasodilation to preserve glomerular filtration rate in states of reduced renal perfusion; NSAID-mediated COX inhibition removes this protective mechanism, precipitating acute prerenal azotemia in patients with compromised renal hemodynamics","B":"Ibuprofen directly inhibits aquaporin-2 water channels in collecting duct principal cells, reducing free water reabsorption, causing medullary washout, and impairing the concentrating ability needed to maintain adequate GFR","C":"NSAID metabolites compete with creatinine for proximal tubular secretion transporters, pharmacologically blocking creatinine excretion and artificially elevating serum creatinine without truly reducing the glomerular filtration rate","D":"Ibuprofen activates renal thromboxane A2 receptors on afferent arterioles, directly causing vasoconstriction and reducing single-nephron GFR through a prostaglandin-independent mechanism that is not reversed by stopping the drug","E":"Chronic ibuprofen use triggers autoimmune tubulointerstitial nephritis through T-cell sensitization to NSAID-protein adducts formed in renal proximal tubular epithelial cells during phase I hepatic biotransformation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In states of reduced effective circulating volume (heart failure, cirrhosis, hypovolemia), the kidney becomes prostaglandin-dependent to maintain afferent arteriolar tone and GFR. COX inhibition removes this compensatory prostaglandin vasodilation, leading to afferent arteriolar constriction and acute reduction in GFR. This is a hemodynamically mediated, reversible form of acute kidney injury that resolves with NSAID discontinuation. NSAIDs are contraindicated in patients with decompensated heart failure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Prostaglandins maintain RBF/GFR when perfusion is compromised (heart failure, cirrhosis, dehydration) — NSAIDs most dangerous in these settings. COMLEX: NSAIDs contraindicated in decompensated heart failure; renal PG-dependence underlies the mechanism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — Hip OA: NSAID pharmacokinetics and renal hemodynamic adverse effects are critical in elderly patients with underlying cardiovascular and renal comorbidities"},{"id":"sdl22_q14","sdlNumber":22,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss adverse effects of NSAIDs by category (salicylates, COX-2 selective inhibitors, nonselective COX inhibitors).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis requires long-term NSAID therapy. She has a documented history of peptic ulcer disease but no cardiovascular disease history and a normal echocardiogram. She needs adequate anti-inflammatory coverage. Which NSAID class best minimizes her GI complication risk?","choices":{"A":"High-dose nonselective COX inhibitors such as ibuprofen are preferred because their potent COX-2 inhibition reduces joint inflammation more rapidly than selective agents and their GI risk can be neutralized with routine proton pump inhibitor co-prescription","B":"High-dose aspirin is preferred because irreversible COX-1 inhibition paradoxically protects the gastric mucosa by suppressing thromboxane A2-mediated mast cell degranulation and reducing local histamine-driven acid secretion","C":"Selective COX-2 inhibitors such as celecoxib are preferred because sparing gastric COX-1 preserves prostaglandin E2-mediated mucus and bicarbonate secretion, significantly reducing peptic ulcer risk compared to nonselective NSAIDs","D":"Nonacetylated salicylates such as salsalate are the best choice because they provide full anti-inflammatory efficacy equivalent to celecoxib without any COX inhibition and therefore carry no GI ulcer risk whatsoever","E":"Nonselective COX inhibitors combined with misoprostol fully eliminate peptic ulcer risk by replacing the gastroprotective prostaglandins lost to COX-1 inhibition and should be preferred over selective agents for their superior anti-inflammatory potency"},"correct":"C","explanation":"COX-2 selective inhibitors (celecoxib) spare gastric COX-1, preserving PGE2-mediated mucosal protection (mucus, bicarbonate, blood flow). This results in significantly fewer gastric ulcers compared to nonselective NSAIDs. In this patient with no CV history, the increased CV risk of celecoxib is less concerning than her peptic ulcer history. Nonacetylated salicylates have reduced but not absent GI risk and may have less anti-inflammatory potency for RA. Misoprostol co-prescription reduces but does not eliminate risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: COX-2 selective = fewer GI ulcers (spares COX-1 gastric protection) but greater CV risk (reduces endothelial prostacyclin). Weigh GI vs. CV risk per patient profile. COMLEX: Patient-specific risk stratification determines NSAID category selection in the clinical management framework.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — Hip OA: NSAID category selection balances GI and cardiovascular risk in elderly patients with competing comorbidities requiring chronic anti-inflammatory therapy"},{"id":"sdl22_q15","sdlNumber":22,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the mechanism of action for other analgesics including acetaminophen, ketorolac, and tramadol.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman with chronic low back pain and major depressive disorder is taking sertraline 100 mg daily. Her orthopedic specialist prescribes tramadol 50 mg every 6 hours. Three days later she presents with agitation, clonus, diaphoresis, and hyperthermia. Which mechanism best explains her presentation?","choices":{"A":"Tramadol is a potent inhibitor of CYP2D6, blocking sertraline metabolism and increasing sertraline plasma concentrations to toxic levels that directly stimulate 5-HT2A receptors in the brainstem independent of reuptake inhibition","B":"Tramadol activates mu-opioid receptors in the dorsal raphe nucleus, paradoxically increasing serotonin synthesis and release into synaptic clefts where sertraline prevents reuptake, creating a purely pharmacodynamic interaction","C":"Tramadol is metabolized to O-desmethyltramadol, which acts as a potent direct 5-HT2C receptor agonist and produces serotonergic toxicity independently of reuptake inhibition or pharmacokinetic interactions with sertraline","D":"Tramadol inhibits both serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake transporters; combined with sertraline serotonin reuptake inhibition, synaptic serotonin accumulates to toxic levels that activate 5-HT1A and 5-HT2A receptors, producing the classic triad of serotonin syndrome","E":"Tramadol and sertraline both inhibit dopamine reuptake in striatal circuits, producing dopaminergic overstimulation that mimics serotonin syndrome clinically but is mechanistically mediated through D2 receptor hyperstimulation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Tramadol has dual mechanisms: weak mu-opioid agonism and inhibition of both serotonin (SERT) and norepinephrine (NET) reuptake transporters. Sertraline independently blocks SERT. The combination creates marked excess synaptic serotonin, activating 5-HT1A and 5-HT2A receptors and producing serotonin syndrome (agitation, clonus, diaphoresis, hyperthermia, tachycardia). Tramadol is contraindicated with SSRIs, SNRIs, MAOIs, and other serotonergic medications.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Serotonin syndrome triad = altered mental status + autonomic instability + neuromuscular abnormalities (clonus). Tramadol + SSRI is the classic precipitant. COMLEX: Serotonin syndrome is distinguished from NMS by clonus and rapid onset; treatment = cyproheptadine, stop offending agents.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — Mechanical back pain: tramadol serotonin syndrome risk mandates careful screening for concurrent serotonergic medications before prescribing"},{"id":"sdl22_q16","sdlNumber":22,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the mechanism of action of opioid medications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A first-year medical student asks their neuroscience tutor to explain how opioids reduce pain transmission at the level of the spinal dorsal horn. The tutor describes both presynaptic and postsynaptic mechanisms. Which of the following correctly describes both components of opioid-mediated pain inhibition?","choices":{"A":"Presynaptically, opioids activate adenylyl cyclase to increase cAMP, which phosphorylates potassium channels and hyperpolarizes the presynaptic terminal; postsynaptically, opioids stimulate IP3 production to sequester intracellular calcium and reduce AMPA receptor sensitivity","B":"Presynaptically, opioids block voltage-gated sodium channels to prevent action potential propagation into the synaptic terminal; postsynaptically, opioids activate chloride channels to hyperpolarize the secondary neuron and suppress ascending pain signal transmission","C":"Presynaptically, opioids activate phospholipase C to produce diacylglycerol that directly closes N-type calcium channels; postsynaptically, opioids increase cyclic GMP through nitric oxide synthase activation, reducing glutamate receptor phosphorylation and sensitivity","D":"Presynaptically, opioids stimulate large-conductance potassium channels to prolong repolarization and reduce firing frequency; postsynaptically, opioids block NMDA receptors through an open-channel mechanism to prevent calcium influx and central sensitization","E":"Presynaptically, opioids close voltage-gated calcium channels on primary afferent terminals, reducing Ca2+ influx and decreasing release of glutamate and substance P; postsynaptically, opioids open inwardly rectifying potassium channels on dorsal horn neurons, causing hyperpolarization and reducing firing"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Mu (and other opioid) receptor activation via Gi/o coupling produces two key synaptic effects: (1) presynaptically — closure of N-type (and P/Q-type) voltage-gated Ca2+ channels reduces intracellular Ca2+ and decreases vesicular release of glutamate and substance P from primary afferent C-fiber and A-delta terminals; (2) postsynaptically — opening of GIRK (G-protein-coupled inwardly rectifying K+) channels hyperpolarizes the secondary neuron, raising its threshold for firing. Together these reduce ascending pain transmission in the dorsal horn.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pre = close Ca2+ channels (less neurotransmitter release); Post = open K+ channels (hyperpolarization). Both mediated by Gi/o. COMLEX: Same mechanisms operate in supraspinal sites (PAG, RVM) to activate descending inhibitory pathways.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 4 — Hip dislocations: opioid presynaptic and postsynaptic mechanisms explain the rapid analgesic onset needed for procedural pain control in acute joint reductions"},{"id":"sdl22_q17","sdlNumber":22,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Outline acceptable uses for opioid medications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 31-year-old man with opioid use disorder and a history of intravenous heroin use is started on buprenorphine plus naloxone (Suboxone) for maintenance therapy. His provider explains the specific pharmacological rationale for this combination. Which statement best explains the therapeutic design of this regimen?","choices":{"A":"Buprenorphine is a full mu-opioid agonist with a very long plasma half-life exceeding 72 hours; naloxone is added as a competitive antagonist that partially occupies mu receptors and creates a pharmacological ceiling preventing dose escalation-related euphoria","B":"Buprenorphine is a partial mu-opioid agonist with very high receptor affinity and slow dissociation, producing a ceiling effect on respiratory depression; naloxone is added to deter intravenous misuse, as sublingual naloxone has poor oral bioavailability but would precipitate acute withdrawal if the combination were injected","C":"Buprenorphine is a kappa-opioid receptor antagonist that blocks the dysphoria and paranoia produced by endogenous dynorphin signaling; naloxone functions as a partial mu agonist to suppress opioid withdrawal symptoms without producing significant euphoria","D":"Buprenorphine undergoes extensive hepatic first-pass metabolism that converts it to norbuprenorphine, a full mu-opioid agonist; naloxone competitively blocks this active metabolite from binding mu receptors in mesolimbic reward circuits to eliminate the addiction potential","E":"Buprenorphine and naloxone target different opioid receptor populations in the same brain region; buprenorphine selectively activates spinal cord mu receptors for analgesia while naloxone selectively blocks limbic mu receptors to eliminate the rewarding properties of opioids"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Buprenorphine is a partial mu-opioid agonist with exceptionally high receptor affinity (displaces full agonists) and slow receptor dissociation. Its partial agonism produces analgesia and suppresses withdrawal while creating a ceiling on euphoria and respiratory depression. Sublingual naloxone is poorly absorbed (first-pass destroyed), contributing minimally to the clinical effect when taken properly. However, if the tablet is dissolved and injected, naloxone reaches systemic circulation and precipitates withdrawal, deterring intravenous misuse.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Buprenorphine = partial mu agonist (ceiling effect on respiratory depression); high affinity displaces other opioids. Naloxone in Suboxone = abuse deterrent (poor sublingual bioavailability but active IV). COMLEX: Opioid use disorder treatment with buprenorphine is a COMLEX-emphasized clinical presentation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 4 — Hip dislocations: buprenorphine partial agonism and naloxone deterrence inform management decisions when opioid-maintained patients require acute pain control"},{"id":"sdl22_q18","sdlNumber":22,"batch":2,"objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Discuss adverse effects of opioid medication.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 79-year-old man with stage 3 chronic kidney disease (GFR 35 mL/min) undergoes elective total hip arthroplasty. The anesthesiologist orders meperidine 50 mg IV every 4 hours for postoperative pain. On postoperative day 3, the patient develops myoclonus and then a generalized tonic-clonic seizure. Naloxone administration fails to terminate the seizure. Which mechanism is responsible?","choices":{"A":"Meperidine is hepatically metabolized to normeperidine, an active metabolite with a half-life of 15 to 20 hours that is renally excreted; accumulation in renal insufficiency lowers the seizure threshold through central nervous system excitatory mechanisms that are not reversed by naloxone because normeperidine does not act at opioid receptors","B":"Meperidine itself directly activates NMDA glutamate receptors in the hippocampal CA3 region after accumulating to toxic plasma concentrations in renal failure, triggering excitotoxic calcium influx and seizure activity independent of opioid receptor binding","C":"Chronic meperidine administration upregulates mu-opioid receptor expression in motor cortex interneurons, paradoxically increasing cortical excitability and producing myoclonus through receptor sensitization and increased Gi-mediated disinhibition of glutamatergic circuits","D":"Meperidine undergoes O-demethylation to meperidinic acid in hepatic microsomes, and this metabolite competes with GABA at ionotropic GABA-A receptors, reducing inhibitory chloride conductance in cortical motor networks and triggering focal seizure activity","E":"Renal failure impairs meperidine excretion and forces accumulation of the parent compound in central nervous system tissue where it directly blocks glycine receptors in spinal cord and brainstem interneurons, causing disinhibitory myoclonus through loss of glycinergic inhibition"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Meperidine is demethylated in the liver to normeperidine, an active metabolite that is proconvulsant. Normeperidine has a half-life of 15-20 h and is renally excreted; in patients with renal insufficiency it accumulates rapidly. Normeperidine lowers the seizure threshold through non-opioid excitatory CNS mechanisms and therefore does not respond to naloxone. Meperidine is absolutely contraindicated in renal insufficiency and in elderly patients. Preferred alternatives include morphine, hydromorphone, or fentanyl.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meperidine contraindicated in renal failure (normeperidine accumulation) and MAOIs (serotonin syndrome/hyperpyrexic crisis). Seizures from normeperidine are naloxone-unresponsive — classic board trap. COMLEX: Meperidine is also contraindicated with MAOIs; alternative opioids must be selected in elderly patients with CKD.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — Hip fracture: normeperidine accumulation in renal-impaired elderly patients produces non-naloxone-reversible seizures, mandating alternative opioid analgesic selection in the perioperative setting"}]},{"sdlNumber":23,"title":"Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation","questions":[{"id":"sdl23_q01","sdlNumber":23,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe common therapeutic exercises: closed kinetic chain, concentric, core stability, eccentric, isometric, isotonic, open kinetic chain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which therapeutic exercise type fixes the distal segment so the proximal segment moves, and is specifically recommended for shoulder and knee rehabilitation and dynamic stability?","choices":{"A":"Open kinetic chain exercise fixes the proximal segment while the distal segment moves freely, as in long arc quadriceps extension and most arm weight-lifting, making it ideal for functional improvement in activities of daily living","B":"Concentric exercise involves the distal segment being fixed during muscle shortening, generating compression forces that make it specifically suited for early post-operative knee and shoulder rehabilitation programs","C":"Eccentric exercise braces the distal segment against a fixed surface while lengthening contraction produces controlled deceleration forces, which is why it is prescribed for shoulder and knee sport-specific strengthening protocols","D":"Isometric exercise involves the distal segment being fixed against an immovable surface while the muscle contracts without joint movement, and is specifically prescribed for shoulder and knee rehabilitation after joint replacement surgery","E":"Closed kinetic chain exercise fixes the distal segment while the proximal segment moves, as in leg presses, squats, and elliptical walking, making it ideal for shoulder and knee rehabilitation and dynamic stability training"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In closed kinetic chain (CKC) exercise, the distal segment is fixed and the proximal segment moves — e.g., leg press, squat, elliptical walker. This creates multi-joint co-contraction and is ideal for shoulder and knee rehab and dynamic stability. Open kinetic chain (OKC) is the opposite: distal segment moves freely (e.g., long arc quad extension), used for ADL functional improvement. Concentric = shortening contraction; eccentric = lengthening contraction; isometric = no joint movement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know CKC vs OKC — CKC has distal segment fixed (leg press, squat), OKC has distal segment free (quad extension). COMLEX: CKC is used in ACL/post-op knee rehab due to joint stability from co-contraction; OMM complements CKC by addressing somatic dysfunction that limits kinetic chain mechanics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — ACL/PCL/MCL injuries: therapeutic exercise types are central to conservative management and structured rehabilitation protocols"},{"id":"sdl23_q02","sdlNumber":23,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe common therapeutic exercises: closed kinetic chain, concentric, core stability, eccentric, isometric, isotonic, open kinetic chain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A sports medicine physician prescribes eccentric heel drops for a patient with Achilles tendinopathy. Which statement correctly defines eccentric exercise and its primary therapeutic application?","choices":{"A":"Eccentric exercise occurs when a muscle contracts while simultaneously shortening, generating maximum force at the shortest fiber length, which is why it is the primary mechanism for increasing muscle mass and strength","B":"Eccentric exercise occurs when a muscle contracts while simultaneously lengthening, such as during the extension phase of a biceps or hamstring curl, and is prescribed for sport-specific strengthening and injury prevention","C":"Eccentric exercise maintains constant muscle length during contraction against an immovable surface, making it ideal for joint-protective strengthening when joint mobility is restricted after injury or surgery","D":"Eccentric exercise applies constant resistance through the complete joint range of motion in both directions using free weights or cable machines, and is primarily prescribed for general muscle conditioning","E":"Eccentric exercise specifically targets deep trunk and abdominal stabilizers through whole-body lengthening movements such as Pilates and back extension, making it the preferred choice for lumbar spine rehabilitation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Eccentric contraction = muscle contracts while lengthening (e.g., extension phase of a biceps curl, lowering phase of a squat). Key therapeutic use: sport-specific strengthening and injury prevention — especially Achilles and patellar tendinopathy. Concentric (shortening) = increases mass/strength. Isometric = no length change. Isotonic = constant resistance through ROM. Core stability targets low back/trunk/abdomen.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Eccentric exercises are first-line for tendinopathy rehabilitation — Alfredson protocol (eccentric heel drops) is the classic example for Achilles tendinopathy. COMLEX: Eccentric loading promotes collagen remodeling in tendons; failure to prescribe appropriately leads to chronic tendinopathy that OMM alone cannot address.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — ACL/PCL/MCL injuries: therapeutic exercise types are central to conservative management and structured rehabilitation protocols"},{"id":"sdl23_q03","sdlNumber":23,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe common therapeutic exercises: closed kinetic chain, concentric, core stability, eccentric, isometric, isotonic, open kinetic chain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which therapeutic exercise type is most specifically indicated for a patient with patellofemoral pain syndrome when joint range of motion is limited or contraindicated?","choices":{"A":"Isotonic exercise using constant resistance through full range of motion is most appropriate when joint mobility must be preserved and the goal is general muscle conditioning for the entire lower extremity","B":"Open kinetic chain exercise is most appropriate when joint mobility is limited because the free distal segment reduces compressive forces at the patellofemoral interface and allows strengthening without joint stress","C":"Isometric exercise involves muscle contraction at constant length without joint movement, making it ideal for strengthening when joint mobility is restricted; quadriceps isometrics are specifically recommended for patellofemoral pain syndrome","D":"Core stability exercise is most appropriate for patients with patellofemoral joint mobility limitations because abdominal bracing reduces compressive forces on peripheral joints and enables safe supervised exercise","E":"Closed kinetic chain exercise is specifically recommended for patellofemoral syndrome because weight-bearing joint co-contraction reduces shear forces and maintains mobility through progressive loading of the articular surface"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Isometric exercise = muscle contracts without changing length or moving the joint (e.g., holding a weight in a stationary position, quad sets). Specifically indicated when joint mobility is contraindicated. Quadriceps isometrics are first-line for patellofemoral pain syndrome. Isotonic = constant resistance through ROM (general conditioning). OKC = distal segment free (ADL function). CKC = dynamic stability for shoulder/knee rehab.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Isometric quad exercises are specifically recommended for patellofemoral pain syndrome — the lack of joint movement avoids provocative arc loading. COMLEX: When prescribing PT for patellofemoral syndrome, isometrics bridge the gap between immobility and full loading; OMM addressing patellar tracking and hip somatic dysfunction complements isometric strengthening.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — ACL/PCL/MCL injuries: therapeutic exercise types are central to conservative management and structured rehabilitation protocols"},{"id":"sdl23_q04","sdlNumber":23,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe commonly used physical therapy modalities: ultrasound, phonophoresis, iontophoresis, electrical stimulation, low level laser therapy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the primary mechanism by which pulsed (nonthermal) therapeutic ultrasound promotes tissue healing, as distinguished from the thermal effects of continuous ultrasound?","choices":{"A":"Pulsed ultrasound heats superficial soft tissues more efficiently than continuous ultrasound because intermittent energy delivery allows deeper penetration before thermal energy dissipates into surrounding structures","B":"Pulsed ultrasound stimulates type I collagen synthesis by directly activating fibroblast gene expression through nuclear membrane deformation, a mechanism unique to intermittent acoustic radiation not seen with continuous delivery","C":"Pulsed ultrasound generates stable cavitation bubbles that mechanically disrupt collagen cross-links in scar tissue, enabling remodeling that continuous ultrasound cannot achieve due to its lower average power output","D":"Pulsed ultrasound activates the gate control mechanism in peripheral nociceptors by creating rhythmic pressure waves that override pain signal transmission to the dorsal horn, providing analgesia without tissue healing effects","E":"Pulsed ultrasound produces nonthermal effects by altering membrane permeability, ionic concentration gradients, and cellular biochemical activity at the cellular level, accelerating tissue healing without generating significant heat"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Continuous ultrasound = thermal effect (heats tissue). Pulsed ultrasound = nonthermal effect (membrane permeability, ionic gradients, cellular biochemical activity) → accelerates healing at the cellular level. High-frequency US treats superficial tissue (patellar tendons); lower frequencies penetrate deeper (up to 5 cm) for deep muscle bruising/spasms/strains. Can cause burns or endothelial damage if applied incorrectly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know thermal vs nonthermal US — continuous = heat, pulsed = cellular healing. Ultrasound CI include pacemakers, malignant tumors, epiphyseal plates, thrombophlebitis, acute inflammation, and reproductive organs. COMLEX: Ultrasound is an adjunctive modality; it does not replace OMM or therapeutic exercise but complements soft tissue treatment in PT protocols.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — Mechanical back pain: PT modalities including ultrasound, TENS, and iontophoresis are widely applied in LBP management"},{"id":"sdl23_q05","sdlNumber":23,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe commonly used physical therapy modalities: ultrasound, phonophoresis, iontophoresis, electrical stimulation, low level laser therapy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Phonophoresis uses ultrasound to deliver topical medications through the skin to deeper tissues. Which medication is most commonly used with this modality, and for which condition is it primarily applied?","choices":{"A":"Dexamethasone 0.4% solution is the most commonly prescribed medication for phonophoresis, applied at 1-2 watts per cm2 in a 10% concentration base to treat tendinopathies and inflammatory conditions such as tendonitis, arthritis, and bursitis","B":"Lidocaine 4% solution is the most commonly used medication in phonophoresis, applied in gel form to provide local anesthesia in superficial soft tissues and reduce procedural pain associated with minor musculoskeletal interventions","C":"Topical diclofenac 1% gel is the most commonly used medication in phonophoresis, delivered into synovial tissue to inhibit COX-2 in the joint space and provide anti-inflammatory effects equivalent to intra-articular corticosteroid injection","D":"Hyaluronic acid 0.5% solution is the most commonly used medication in phonophoresis, delivered into periarticular tissues to augment synovial fluid viscosity and provide viscoelastic cushioning comparable to intra-articular hyaluronate injection","E":"Capsaicin 0.025% cream is the most commonly used medication in phonophoresis, delivered through the dermis to deplete substance P in peripheral nociceptors and provide long-lasting analgesia for chronic musculoskeletal pain syndromes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Phonophoresis = ultrasound used to drive topical medications through the skin. Dexamethasone 0.4% is the most commonly prescribed drug, used for tendinopathies and inflammatory conditions (tendonitis, arthritis, bursitis). It is typically mixed in a 10% concentration at 1-2 W/cm2. Risk of thermal injury increases with energy. Same contraindications as ultrasound apply. Ketoprofen is also studied.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Phonophoresis = ultrasound + topical drug (dexamethasone 0.4% most common). Uses: tendonitis, arthritis, bursitis. Contraindications same as ultrasound. COMLEX: Phonophoresis is a common adjunct in PT for inflammatory tendinopathies that may also benefit from OMM soft-tissue techniques targeting the same regions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — Mechanical back pain: PT modalities including ultrasound, TENS, and iontophoresis are widely applied in LBP management"},{"id":"sdl23_q06","sdlNumber":23,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe commonly used physical therapy modalities: ultrasound, phonophoresis, iontophoresis, electrical stimulation, low level laser therapy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"In which patient group is electrical stimulation absolutely contraindicated, and what is the physiological rationale for this restriction?","choices":{"A":"Electrical stimulation is contraindicated in patients with any metal implant anywhere in the body because electrical current concentrates at all metallic surfaces and causes resistive heating that leads to thermal injury of surrounding bone and soft tissue","B":"Electrical stimulation is contraindicated in patients with cardiac pacemakers and known cardiac arrhythmias because the externally generated electrical current can interfere with pacemaker sensing circuits, trigger arrhythmias, or compete with pacemaker impulse generation","C":"Electrical stimulation is contraindicated in patients with peripheral neuropathy because absent protective sensation prevents patients from reporting excessive current intensity, leading to inadvertent burns and electrode-related skin breakdown","D":"Electrical stimulation is contraindicated in patients with osteoporosis because the muscle contractions generated by neuromuscular electrical stimulation create excessive compressive loads on fragile trabecular bone and may cause pathologic fractures","E":"Electrical stimulation is contraindicated in patients with inflammatory arthritis because the electrical current activates synovial mast cells and fibroblasts, increasing prostaglandin and cytokine production and worsening joint inflammation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Electrical stimulation absolute CI: cardiac pacemakers, known cardiac arrhythmias, thrombophlebitis, and abdomen/pelvis of pregnant patients. Near metallic implants is a precaution (not absolute CI for all metals — CI specifically near metallic implants/wires/staples per iontophoresis; electrical stimulation is used with caution in patients with cardiac disease). Mechanism of ES analgesia: gate control + endogenous opioid/cortisol release.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Electrical stimulation CI = cardiac pacemakers, arrhythmias, thrombophlebitis, pregnant abdomen. TENS mechanism: gate control theory + endogenous opioid release. COMLEX: TENS for LBP and neuropathic pain is commonly tested; OMM is often combined with TENS in multimodal pain management protocols for chronic low back pain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — Mechanical back pain: PT modalities including ultrasound, TENS, and iontophoresis are widely applied in LBP management"},{"id":"sdl23_q07","sdlNumber":23,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the components of a physical therapy prescription to include diagnosis, goal, frequency and duration, specific protocols, exercises or treatments, safety requirements or limitations, and a signature and date.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following is a required component of a valid physical therapy prescription according to standard prescribing guidelines?","choices":{"A":"A completed insurance pre-authorization form signed by both the ordering physician and the physical therapist is required before initiating any PT intervention because payers mandate prospective coverage review for all outpatient rehabilitation services","B":"An objective functional assessment score such as the Oswestry Disability Index or DASH questionnaire is required on the PT prescription to establish a documented baseline for measuring therapeutic progress and justifying continuation of care","C":"A list of all current medications including dosage, frequency, and prescribing physician is required on the PT prescription so the therapist can screen for pharmacological agents that may alter pain perception, muscle tone, or cardiovascular response","D":"A radiology report confirming musculoskeletal pathology via X-ray or MRI is required before a PT prescription is valid, ensuring the therapist treats a documented structural abnormality rather than subjective symptoms without imaging confirmation","E":"A physician signature and date are required for a physical therapist to perform requested services; the prescription must also include the diagnosis, frequency and duration, specific protocols or treatments, and safety precautions to enable appropriate and reimbursable care"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Required components of a PT prescription (Table 3 from SDL): 1. Diagnosis (with proper coding for insurance billing), 2. Frequency and duration of therapy, 3. Specific protocols or treatments, 4. Safety precautions (ROM limitations, weight-bearing limits, comorbidities), 5. Physician signature and date (required for therapist to perform services). Goals are also a standard part. Functional scores, pre-auth forms, imaging, and medication lists are not required prescription components.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PT prescription components = Diagnosis, Goals, Frequency/Duration, Specific treatments, Safety precautions, Physician signature/date. Missing any required component invalidates the prescription. COMLEX: Prescribing PT is a core competency — COMLEX tests whether students can write complete PT orders that include all mandated elements for musculoskeletal conditions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — Shoulder diagnostic workflow: PT prescription is a core management step integrating diagnosis, frequency, and safety precautions"},{"id":"sdl23_q08","sdlNumber":23,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the components of a physical therapy prescription to include diagnosis, goal, frequency and duration, specific protocols, exercises or treatments, safety requirements or limitations, and a signature and date.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physician writes a PT prescription for knee osteoarthritis that includes the diagnosis, frequency, specific exercises, and physician signature. The physical therapist returns it as incomplete. Which required element is most likely missing?","choices":{"A":"Safety precautions are a required prescription component including weight-bearing limitations, range-of-motion restrictions, and comorbidities affecting therapy; without these, the therapist cannot safely determine appropriate exercise intensity and loading parameters for this patient","B":"The patient's insurance policy number and prior authorization code are the missing required elements, without which the physical therapy clinic cannot bill for services or begin treatment under federal outpatient therapy billing regulations","C":"Documentation of prior conservative treatment attempts is the missing required element because payers require evidence that home-based exercise was tried and failed before approving supervised physical therapy for knee osteoarthritis management","D":"The specific brand name of exercise equipment to be used is the missing required element because physical therapists are legally required to use physician-specified equipment for all therapeutic exercise prescriptions for musculoskeletal conditions","E":"The expected discharge functional status stated in measurable terms such as anticipated walking distance or stair-climbing ability is the missing required element required by federal guidelines for establishing an end-point for care"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A complete PT prescription includes: diagnosis, frequency/duration, specific protocols/treatments, safety precautions, and physician signature/date. In this case the prescription includes diagnosis, frequency, exercises, and signature — so the most likely missing element is safety precautions (weight-bearing limits, ROM restrictions, comorbidities affecting therapy decisions). These are critical for post-surgical, elderly, or high-risk patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Safety precautions are a required PT prescription element — especially critical for post-operative and high-risk patients. They inform the therapist about weight-bearing status, ROM restrictions, and clinical conditions affecting exercise prescription. COMLEX: Incomplete prescriptions are a legal liability; learn all required components to write a valid, actionable PT order.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — Shoulder diagnostic workflow: PT prescription is a core management step integrating diagnosis, frequency, and safety precautions"},{"id":"sdl23_q09","sdlNumber":23,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the components of a physical therapy prescription to include diagnosis, goal, frequency and duration, specific protocols, exercises or treatments, safety requirements or limitations, and a signature and date.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Based on the standard algorithm for selecting physical therapy interventions by phase of injury, which combination of modality, exercise, and frequency is most appropriate for a subacute musculoskeletal injury at 10 days post-injury?","choices":{"A":"In the subacute phase, cold application, pulsed ultrasound, and electrical stimulation are the preferred modalities combined with isometric and gentle active range-of-motion exercises with gentle massage, prescribed daily for five days","B":"In the subacute phase, transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation and continuous ultrasound are the preferred modalities combined with progressive strengthening and stabilization exercises with myofascial release, prescribed twice per week for four weeks","C":"In the subacute phase, heat application, low-level laser therapy, and iontophoresis are preferred modalities combined with isotonic and active range-of-motion exercises and joint mobilization, prescribed three times per week for two weeks","D":"In the subacute phase, cryotherapy and compression with neuromuscular electrical stimulation are the preferred modalities combined with eccentric loading and concentric strengthening with soft tissue mobilization, prescribed four times per week for one week","E":"In the subacute phase, phonophoresis and interferential electrical stimulation are the preferred modalities combined with core stability and closed kinetic chain exercises with passive stretching only, prescribed five times per week for two weeks"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Injury phase algorithm: Acute (<72h) = cold, electrical stimulation, pulsed ultrasound; isometric, gentle ROM; gentle massage; daily x5 days. Subacute (3-14 days) = heat, electrical stimulation, low-level laser, iontophoresis; isotonic, active ROM, stretching; massage, joint mobilization; 3x/week x2 weeks. Chronic (>2 weeks) = TENS, continuous ultrasound; strengthening, stabilization; myofascial release; 2x/week x4 weeks.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the three injury phases and their corresponding modalities, exercises, and dosing: acute = cold/isometric/daily; subacute = heat/isotonic/3x/week; chronic = TENS/strengthening/2x/week. COMLEX: Prescribing PT by injury phase is a core clinical skill — COMLEX tests appropriate modality and exercise selection based on the timeline of musculoskeletal injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — Shoulder diagnostic workflow: PT prescription is a core management step integrating diagnosis, frequency, and safety precautions"},{"id":"sdl23_q10","sdlNumber":23,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe common therapeutic exercises: closed kinetic chain, concentric, core stability, eccentric, isometric, isotonic, open kinetic chain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old female competitive runner presents with 4 months of posterior heel pain. Imaging confirms Achilles tendinopathy without tear. She has failed rest and passive stretching. Which type of therapeutic exercise has the strongest evidence for promoting tendon collagen remodeling and should anchor her rehabilitation program?","choices":{"A":"Isometric exercise, in which muscle contraction occurs without any change in length, has the strongest evidence for tendinopathy because static loading at therapeutic angles reduces cortical pain inhibition without creating the shear forces associated with dynamic loading","B":"Open kinetic chain exercise, in which the distal segment is free, has the strongest evidence for tendinopathy because the absence of weight-bearing compressive forces on the inflamed tendon allows strengthening while maintaining cardiovascular fitness","C":"Core stability exercise targeting the lumbopelvic region has the strongest evidence for Achilles tendinopathy because improving proximal control reduces excessive ground reaction force transmission and indirectly offloads the distal tendon","D":"Eccentric exercise, in which the muscle contracts while lengthening, has the strongest evidence for treating tendinopathy; eccentric heel drops load the Achilles tendon during the lengthening phase, stimulating collagen synthesis and tendon remodeling as first-line exercise therapy","E":"Isotonic exercise applying constant resistance through full range of motion in both concentric and eccentric phases has the strongest evidence for tendinopathy because it stimulates the highest absolute rate of tendon collagen cross-linking per session"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Eccentric exercise is the exercise type with the strongest evidence for tendinopathy rehabilitation. The controlled lengthening contraction (e.g., Alfredson protocol: eccentric heel drops for Achilles) stimulates collagen synthesis and remodeling. Isometric exercise may reduce acute pain but does not drive tendon structural change as effectively. OKC reduces load but lacks the mechanotransduction signal needed for tendon healing. Core stability is complementary, not primary.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Eccentric exercise = first-line for tendinopathy; Alfredson protocol (eccentric heel drops) is classic for Achilles. COMLEX: Tendinopathy rehabilitation relies on controlled progressive loading — eccentric protocols are complemented by OMM soft-tissue techniques to address fascial restrictions contributing to mechanical load on the tendon.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: eccentric heel drops are first-line exercise therapy and the cornerstone of conservative management"},{"id":"sdl23_q11","sdlNumber":23,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe common therapeutic exercises: closed kinetic chain, concentric, core stability, eccentric, isometric, isotonic, open kinetic chain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old male undergoes ACL reconstruction with a patellar tendon autograft. The physical therapist plans to use closed kinetic chain (CKC) exercises preferentially over open kinetic chain (OKC) exercises in the early post-operative period. Which biomechanical feature of CKC exercise justifies this preference?","choices":{"A":"In closed kinetic chain exercise, the fixed distal segment promotes co-contraction of agonist and antagonist muscle groups, which enhances joint stability and reduces anterior tibial shear forces on the healing ACL graft compared to open kinetic chain exercises that isolate quadriceps contraction","B":"Closed kinetic chain exercise is preferred post-ACL because it isolates the quadriceps without engaging the hamstrings, which reduces compressive load from antagonist co-contraction and prevents anterior drawer forces at the tibiofemoral joint","C":"Closed kinetic chain exercise is preferred post-ACL because it allows high-velocity ballistic movements that accelerate neuromuscular re-education and restore proprioception faster than the slower controlled movements characteristic of open kinetic chain protocols","D":"Closed kinetic chain exercise is preferred post-ACL because it loads the graft exclusively through concentric contraction, avoiding eccentric lengthening forces that cause repetitive microtrauma and graft elongation during early healing","E":"Closed kinetic chain exercise is preferred post-ACL because the distal segment moves freely about the proximal segment, which reduces the bending moment at the knee and distributes load evenly across the healing graft and native structures"},"correct":"A","explanation":"CKC = distal segment fixed, proximal moves (leg press, squat, lunges). Co-contraction of quads + hamstrings in CKC reduces anterior tibial shear (and therefore graft stress) compared to OKC quad-isolated exercises (e.g., leg extension). This is why CKC is the foundation of early ACL rehab. OKC exercises are reintroduced later in rehab when the graft is more mature. Wrong choice E incorrectly describes CKC as having a free distal segment — that is OKC.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CKC = distal fixed, proximal moves; co-contraction protects ACL graft. OKC = distal free (leg extension) — delayed in ACL rehab due to anterior tibial shear. COMLEX: Post-surgical PT prescriptions should specify CKC vs OKC progression — COMLEX tests appropriate exercise selection in post-operative rehabilitation scenarios.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — ACL/PCL/MCL injuries: CKC exercises are the foundation of post-operative ACL rehabilitation due to co-contraction-mediated joint stability"},{"id":"sdl23_q12","sdlNumber":23,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe common therapeutic exercises: closed kinetic chain, concentric, core stability, eccentric, isometric, isotonic, open kinetic chain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old sedentary office worker presents with chronic non-specific low back pain without neurological deficits or red flags. Her physician wants to prescribe a therapeutic exercise category specifically shown to reduce low back pain symptoms. Which exercise classification is most directly targeted at her primary needs?","choices":{"A":"Closed kinetic chain exercises in weight-bearing positions such as squats and leg presses strengthen the paraspinal muscles that support the lumbar vertebral column and are most specifically indicated for non-specific low back pain in sedentary adults","B":"Core stability exercises targeting the low back, trunk, and abdominal muscles through sit-ups, back extension, abdominal crunches, and Pilates are specifically indicated for the relief of low back pain and pregnancy-related pelvic pain","C":"Eccentric exercises involving controlled lengthening contractions of the erector spinae and multifidus during forward bending are the most efficient mechanism for achieving lumbar stabilization in deconditioned sedentary patients with chronic low back pain","D":"Isometric paraspinal contractions without joint movement are the safest approach for deconditioned sedentary adults and produce the highest absolute strength gains in the lumbar extensors per session without risk of exacerbation","E":"Open kinetic chain exercises activating free trunk movement in space are most specifically indicated because they engage the deep multifidus stabilizers more effectively than weight-bearing exercises that primarily recruit superficial global movers of the spine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Core stability exercises (sit-ups, back extension, abdominal crunch, Pilates) specifically target the low back, trunk, and abdominal muscles and are the exercise category indicated for low back pain and pregnancy-related pelvic pain per Table 2. CKC is for shoulder/knee rehab and dynamic stability, not specifically LBP. Eccentric is for tendinopathy/sport strengthening. Isometric is for when joint mobility is restricted. OKC is for ADL functional improvement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Core stability = specific indication for low back pain and pregnancy-related pelvic pain (sit-up, back extension, Pilates). CKC = shoulder/knee rehab. Isometric = patellofemoral or restricted mobility. COMLEX: Core stability exercises are prescribed alongside OMM for low back pain — both address the deep stabilizing musculature that is often inhibited in chronic LBP.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — Mechanical back pain: core stability exercises are the primary therapeutic exercise intervention for non-specific low back pain management"},{"id":"sdl23_q13","sdlNumber":23,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe commonly used physical therapy modalities: ultrasound, phonophoresis, iontophoresis, electrical stimulation, low level laser therapy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physical therapist plans to treat a patient's calcific shoulder tendinopathy with iontophoresis using acetic acid. A medical student asks how iontophoresis differs from phonophoresis as a drug delivery mechanism. Which comparison is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Phonophoresis uses a direct electric current to deliver charged anti-inflammatory molecules into the joint, while iontophoresis uses high-frequency sound waves to heat the medication and increase its thermal diffusion rate into periarticular tissues","B":"Phonophoresis and iontophoresis use identical delivery mechanisms based on sonic vibration, differing only in the frequency range: phonophoresis operates at audible frequencies while iontophoresis operates at ultrasonic frequencies above 20 kHz","C":"Phonophoresis uses ultrasound waves to push topical medications through the skin into deeper tissue, while iontophoresis uses a direct electric current to drive ionically charged substances through the skin, with cathodes repelling negatively charged drugs and anodes repelling positively charged drugs","D":"Phonophoresis delivers medications exclusively into the superficial epidermis, while iontophoresis penetrates all the way to bone and joint capsule because the electrical gradient overcomes resistance of all tissue layers simultaneously and without limitation","E":"Phonophoresis and iontophoresis are equally effective for any musculoskeletal condition, and the clinical distinction is only whether the medication is formulated as a topical gel or as an aqueous ionic solution compatible with the delivery device"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Phonophoresis = ultrasound used to drive topical meds (dexamethasone 0.4%) through skin. Iontophoresis = direct electric current used to drive ionically charged substances through skin to deeper tissue. Cathode delivers negatively charged substances; anode delivers positively charged substances. Iontophoresis uses: calcific tendinopathy, inflammatory conditions, hyperhidrosis. CI for iontophoresis: allergy to applied substance, open wounds, decreased sensation, near metallic implants/wires/staples.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Phonophoresis = ultrasound + drug (dexamethasone). Iontophoresis = electric current + ionic drug (acetic acid for calcific tendinopathy, dexamethasone). Cathode = negatively charged; anode = positively charged. COMLEX: Both modalities deliver anti-inflammatory agents; iontophoresis is specifically indicated for calcific tendinopathy and hyperhidrosis in addition to general inflammatory conditions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — De Quervain's tenosynovitis: phonophoresis and iontophoresis are commonly applied PT modalities for tenosynovitis and inflammatory tendon conditions"},{"id":"sdl23_q14","sdlNumber":23,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe commonly used physical therapy modalities: ultrasound, phonophoresis, iontophoresis, electrical stimulation, low level laser therapy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old male with chronic low back pain reports significant pain relief from transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation (TENS) during his PT sessions. His physician asks the therapist to explain the mechanism of TENS analgesia. Which explanation is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"TENS reduces pain by generating microcurrents that directly reverse depolarization of peripheral nociceptor membranes, restoring resting membrane potential and preventing action potential generation in C-fibers and A-delta fibers","B":"TENS reduces pain by delivering an electrical field that disrupts substance P binding to NK-1 receptors in the dorsal horn, preventing central sensitization without engaging the descending inhibitory system or any endogenous analgesic pathway","C":"TENS reduces pain by activating sympathetic efferents that produce local vasoconstriction, reducing edema and lowering the concentration of algogenic chemicals such as bradykinin and prostaglandins in the interstitial fluid around nociceptors","D":"TENS reduces pain by activating the gate control mechanism in which electrical stimulation of large-diameter sensory fibers inhibits pain signal transmission from small nociceptive C and A-delta fibers at the dorsal horn, and by stimulating endogenous opioid peptide and cortisol release","E":"TENS reduces pain by generating a therapeutic electrical field that directly inactivates COX-2 in injured tissue, reducing local prostaglandin E2 concentration through the same pathway as NSAIDs but without systemic adverse effects"},"correct":"D","explanation":"TENS analgesia mechanisms: 1) Gate control theory — large A-beta fiber stimulation inhibits C-fiber and A-delta nociceptive transmission at the dorsal horn interneurons; 2) Stimulates endogenous opioid and cortisol release. Electrical stimulation also reduces inflammation and edema and facilitates chronic wound healing. This is distinct from phonophoresis (drug delivery) or ultrasound (tissue heating/healing).","boardPrep":"Step 1: TENS mechanism = gate control (large fiber inhibits small fiber at dorsal horn) + endogenous opioid/cortisol release. TENS is used for chronic LBP, neuropathic pain, and TENS is applied in the chronic phase of injury. COMLEX: TENS is used alongside OMM for chronic musculoskeletal pain; understanding the gate control mechanism is testable for both Step 1 and COMLEX-1.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 8 — Lumbosacral spinal stenosis: electrical stimulation including TENS is a standard PT modality for managing chronic lumbar pain and neurogenic claudication"},{"id":"sdl23_q15","sdlNumber":23,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe commonly used physical therapy modalities: ultrasound, phonophoresis, iontophoresis, electrical stimulation, low level laser therapy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with bilateral hand numbness and weakness, consistent with carpal tunnel syndrome, asks her physician whether low-level laser therapy is an option for her condition. Which statement most accurately characterizes the evidence base and regulatory status of LLLT?","choices":{"A":"LLLT is FDA approved for all musculoskeletal conditions including tendinopathy, osteoarthritis, and rheumatoid arthritis based on consistent level-1 randomized controlled trial evidence demonstrating clinically significant improvements in pain and joint function","B":"LLLT is FDA approved for carpal tunnel syndrome and minor musculoskeletal pain; Cochrane reviews show limited but inconsistent benefit for osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis, and LLLT has not been shown to cause adverse effects in standard use","C":"LLLT has no FDA approval for any musculoskeletal condition because randomized controlled trials have consistently demonstrated no benefit beyond sham therapy, and its use in clinical practice is considered experimental and generally discouraged","D":"LLLT is FDA approved specifically for rheumatoid arthritis because its mechanism of decreasing prostaglandin E2 production mimics the anti-inflammatory effect of NSAIDs without the associated gastrointestinal and cardiovascular adverse effects","E":"LLLT is contraindicated in all patients with musculoskeletal pain because photon radiation causes cumulative DNA damage in connective tissue cells, accelerating cartilage degeneration and ultimately worsening the conditions it is intended to treat"},"correct":"B","explanation":"LLLT mechanism: absorption of photon radiation → alters cellular oxidative metabolism (adenosine triphosphate production via cytochrome oxidase) → decreases prostaglandin E2 concentration in peritendinous fluid. FDA approved for carpal tunnel syndrome and minor MSK pain. Evidence for OA and RA is limited and conflicting per Cochrane reviews. Safety goggles are required; caution in patients with malignant tumors, anticoagulants, corticosteroids, immunosuppressives, and pregnant patients (not on uterus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: LLLT FDA approved for CTS and minor MSK pain. Mechanism: cytochrome oxidase activation → increased ATP, decreased PGE2. Evidence for OA/RA is limited and inconsistent. COMLEX: LLLT is a low-risk modality used in chronic MSK pain management — cautions include malignant tumors and anticoagulant therapy; safety goggles protect therapists and patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — Carpal tunnel syndrome: low-level laser therapy is FDA approved for CTS management and is one of the PT modalities applied for this condition"},{"id":"sdl23_q16","sdlNumber":23,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the components of a physical therapy prescription to include diagnosis, goal, frequency and duration, specific protocols, exercises or treatments, safety requirements or limitations, and a signature and date.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient is being discharged after a lumbar microdiscectomy. The attending writes a PT referral that includes the diagnosis, exercise protocols, and physician signature. The physical therapist calls the office to say the prescription is incomplete before treatment can begin. Which required element is most likely missing?","choices":{"A":"A measurable functional outcome goal stated as expected walking distance or stair-climbing ability is the missing required element; federal outpatient therapy guidelines mandate that every PT prescription specify the anticipated level of functional recovery","B":"The specific brand of lumbar brace or assistive device to be worn during therapy sessions is the missing required element; surgeons are required to specify all post-operative orthotic requirements on the PT referral for the therapist to follow","C":"Safety precautions are a required prescription component including post-surgical weight-bearing limitations, range-of-motion restrictions, lifting restrictions, and comorbidities affecting therapy decisions; for any post-operative patient, these are essential to guide safe exercise intensity and loading parameters","D":"A list of contraindicated physical therapy modalities is the missing required element; for any post-operative patient the referring physician must explicitly document prohibited PT interventions to avoid liability for modality-related complications","E":"Documentation of the patient's pre-operative Oswestry Disability Index score is the missing required element as federal guidelines require a validated baseline disability score on the prescription to establish the starting point for measuring progress"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Required PT prescription components: diagnosis, frequency/duration, specific protocols/treatments, safety precautions, and physician signature/date. This prescription has diagnosis, exercise protocols, and signature — the most likely missing element is safety precautions. Post-surgical patients require explicit notation of weight-bearing status, ROM restrictions, lifting limits, and relevant comorbidities so the therapist can safely advance the rehabilitation protocol.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Safety precautions are especially critical post-operatively — weight-bearing status, ROM restrictions, and lifting limits must be specified. Missing safety precautions creates clinical risk and legal liability. COMLEX: Complete PT prescriptions prevent PT-related complications after surgery; COMLEX tests the ability to write complete and appropriate post-operative rehabilitation orders.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 7 — Lumbosacral radiculopathy: post-surgical PT prescriptions for lumbar conditions require explicit safety precautions to guide progressive loading and protect surgical repair"},{"id":"sdl23_q17","sdlNumber":23,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the components of a physical therapy prescription to include diagnosis, goal, frequency and duration, specific protocols, exercises or treatments, safety requirements or limitations, and a signature and date.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A family physician writes the following PT referral: \"Dx: right shoulder rotator cuff tendinopathy. Tx: therapeutic exercises and ultrasound. Signed, Dr. Smith, 4/28/26.\" The physical therapist calls requesting a critical addition before scheduling can begin. What required element is most likely missing from this prescription?","choices":{"A":"Frequency and duration of therapy are missing; without specifying how many sessions per week and the total treatment length the therapist cannot create a schedule, and the clinic cannot obtain payer authorization for a defined episode of care","B":"The patient's insurance co-payment information and referral number are missing; without prior verification of coverage details the physical therapy clinic cannot register the patient or confirm that the payer will reimburse for the prescribed services","C":"An MRI report confirming rotator cuff tendinopathy is missing; physical therapists are legally required to verify structural pathology via imaging before initiating therapeutic exercise for suspected rotator cuff conditions in any patient over 40","D":"Specific exercise names and repetition counts for each planned therapeutic intervention are missing; physical therapists are required to obtain physician approval for exact exercises rather than using clinical judgment to select appropriate interventions","E":"The patient's numeric pain rating at rest and with overhead activity is missing; federal billing guidelines require an objective pain score to be documented on every PT prescription before payers will authorize outpatient musculoskeletal rehabilitation services"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This prescription has: diagnosis (rotator cuff tendinopathy), specific treatments (exercises, ultrasound), and physician signature/date. The missing required component is frequency and duration of therapy (e.g., \"3x/week for 6 weeks\"). Without this, the therapist cannot schedule sessions appropriately or submit for insurance authorization. The other prescription elements present are adequate; insurance details, imaging, exact exercise names, and pain scores are not required prescription components.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PT prescription must include frequency and duration — this is distinct from just specifying the type of treatment. A complete prescription enables scheduling, insurance authorization, and a defined treatment episode. COMLEX: A common COMLEX stem presents an incomplete PT order and asks what is missing — frequency/duration and safety precautions are the most commonly omitted elements.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — Shoulder rotator cuff injuries: PT prescription with complete frequency, duration, and safety precautions is the standard management step following rotator cuff tendinopathy diagnosis"},{"id":"sdl23_q18","sdlNumber":23,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the components of a physical therapy prescription to include diagnosis, goal, frequency and duration, specific protocols, exercises or treatments, safety requirements or limitations, and a signature and date.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A first-year resident is writing a PT prescription for a patient with a medial collateral ligament sprain at 10 days post-injury. The attending asks which combination of modality, exercise type, and session frequency is most consistent with evidence-based guidelines for this phase of injury.","choices":{"A":"Subacute injuries are managed with cold application, pulsed ultrasound, and electrical stimulation combined with isometric exercises and gentle active range-of-motion with gentle massage, prescribed daily for five days to control ongoing edema and pain","B":"Subacute injuries are managed with transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation and continuous ultrasound combined with progressive strengthening and stabilization exercises with myofascial release, prescribed twice per week for four weeks","C":"Subacute injuries are managed with cryotherapy and compression combined with eccentric loading and concentric strengthening with soft tissue mobilization, prescribed four times per week for one week to maximize early collagen deposition","D":"Subacute injuries at 3-14 days are managed with heat application, iontophoresis, and low-level laser therapy combined with isotonic exercises, active range-of-motion, and joint mobilization, prescribed three times per week for two weeks","E":"Subacute injuries are managed with phonophoresis and interferential electrical stimulation combined with core stability and closed kinetic chain exercises with passive stretching only, prescribed five times per week for two weeks"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Phase algorithm: Acute (<72h) = cold/electrical stimulation/pulsed US; isometric/gentle ROM; daily x5 days. Subacute (3-14 days) = heat/electrical stimulation/LLLT/iontophoresis; isotonic/active ROM/stretching; massage/joint mobilization; 3x/week x2 weeks. Chronic (>2 weeks) = TENS/continuous US; strengthening/stabilization; myofascial release; 2x/week x4 weeks. This patient is at day 10 = subacute phase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Injury phase algorithm must be memorized — the transition from acute to subacute changes modalities from cold to heat, from isometric to isotonic, and from daily to 3x/week. COMLEX: Prescribing PT by phase of injury is a testable clinical skill — expect scenarios that require identifying whether a patient is acute, subacute, or chronic and selecting the corresponding intervention package.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — ACL/PCL/MCL injuries: subacute phase PT protocol with heat, isotonic exercises, and joint mobilization is the standard approach for ligamentous sprains in the 3-14 day window"}]},{"sdlNumber":25,"title":"Joint Dislocations","questions":[{"id":"sdl25_q01","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the presentation and management of interphalangeal joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which structure is primarily disrupted in a dorsal proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joint dislocation, producing posterior displacement of the middle phalanx on the proximal phalanx?","choices":{"A":"Collateral ligament rupture alone without disruption of the palmar restraint structures, producing lateral instability rather than dorsal displacement of the middle phalanx","B":"Central extensor slip avulsion from the base of the middle phalanx, producing volar rather than dorsal displacement of the middle phalanx at the PIP joint","C":"Volar plate rupture from its distal attachment, allowing dorsal translation of the middle phalanx through the resulting gap in the palmar restraint system","D":"Flexor digitorum profundus tendon rupture from its distal phalanx insertion, producing DIP extension deformity rather than PIP joint displacement","E":"Dorsal capsule rupture and posterior ligamentous disruption, which would produce anterior rather than posterior displacement of the middle phalanx"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dorsal PIP dislocation is the most common finger dislocation. The mechanism is axial load combined with hyperextension, which ruptures the volar plate from its distal attachment to the middle phalanx, allowing dorsal translation. Collateral ligament rupture alone causes lateral instability. Central slip injury causes volar PIP dislocation (rare) and boutonnière risk. FDP rupture creates a jersey finger deformity at the DIP.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Volar plate rupture = dorsal PIP dislocation (hyperextension); central slip injury = volar PIP dislocation → boutonnière if untreated. COMLEX: Post-reduction splint at 30° flexion for 3 weeks; somatic dysfunction of the hand may accompany high-energy digit injuries.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — Joint stabilizers: volar plate and collateral ligament integrity at interphalangeal joints underlies dislocation pattern and deformity risk"},{"id":"sdl25_q02","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the presentation and management of interphalangeal joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"After closed reduction of a dorsal PIP joint dislocation with a confirmed volar plate avulsion on post-reduction X-ray, what is the appropriate immobilization position and duration?","choices":{"A":"Splint the PIP joint in full extension for 6 weeks, which places maximum tension on the healing volar plate and is appropriate only for central slip injuries requiring boutonnière prevention","B":"Splint the PIP joint at 30 degrees of flexion for 3 weeks, which maintains reduction while allowing the volar plate to heal in a shortened position and prevents swan neck deformity","C":"Buddy tape only to the adjacent finger with immediate active range of motion exercises, which does not adequately immobilize the ruptured volar plate during the critical healing period","D":"Immobilize in 90 degrees of PIP flexion for 4 weeks, which over-shortens the volar plate and produces a flexion contracture of the PIP joint as the primary complication","E":"Apply a dynamic extension splint immediately to restore full range of motion within 48 hours, bypassing the initial immobilization phase required for volar plate healing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Post-reduction management of dorsal PIP dislocation requires splinting at 30° flexion for 3 weeks to allow volar plate healing. Full extension splinting is reserved for central slip injuries (boutonnière prevention). Immediate motion without immobilization risks chronic volar plate laxity. 90° flexion causes a flexion contracture. The goal is healing without deformity — inadequate treatment leads to swan neck deformity from chronic volar plate laxity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dorsal PIP → volar plate → splint at 30° flexion 3 weeks; volar PIP → central slip → splint in full extension. COMLEX: Untreated volar plate laxity → swan neck deformity (PIP hyperextension, DIP flexion); somatic dysfunction of MCP/PIP may accompany ligamentous injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — Joint stabilizers: volar plate and collateral ligament integrity at interphalangeal joints underlies dislocation pattern and deformity risk"},{"id":"sdl25_q03","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the presentation and management of interphalangeal joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient with a previously untreated dorsal PIP dislocation presents 6 weeks later with PIP joint hyperextension and DIP joint flexion. Which deformity has developed from the untreated volar plate tear?","choices":{"A":"Swan neck deformity — chronic volar plate laxity at the PIP allows hyperextension, which increases tension on the oblique retinacular ligament and produces reciprocal DIP flexion","B":"Boutonnière deformity — central slip rupture at the PIP joint allows lateral bands to migrate volarly, producing PIP flexion and DIP hyperextension as the characteristic pattern","C":"Mallet finger deformity — terminal extensor tendon rupture at the DIP joint produces DIP flexion and cannot be extended actively, with no associated PIP deformity","D":"Trigger finger deformity — stenosing tenosynovitis at the A1 pulley produces a catching or locking finger in flexion that snaps into extension with passive manipulation","E":"Dupuytren contracture — palmar fascia fibrosis producing progressive flexion deformity at the MCP and PIP joints bilaterally, unrelated to acute ligamentous injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Swan neck deformity (PIP hyperextension + DIP flexion) develops from chronic volar plate laxity after untreated dorsal PIP dislocation. Boutonnière is the opposite pattern (PIP flexion + DIP hyperextension) from central slip rupture. Mallet finger is isolated DIP flexion from terminal extensor tendon avulsion. Trigger finger is A1 pulley stenosis. Dupuytren is palmar fascia fibrosis — not post-traumatic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Swan neck = PIP hyperextension + DIP flexion (volar plate laxity or FDS rupture); boutonnière = PIP flexion + DIP hyperextension (central slip rupture). COMLEX: Both deformities benefit from orthotic management and hand rehabilitation; OMM of the wrist/finger may address secondary somatic dysfunction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — Joint stabilizers: volar plate and collateral ligament integrity at interphalangeal joints underlies dislocation pattern and deformity risk"},{"id":"sdl25_q04","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the presentation and management of elbow joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Elbow dislocations are the second most common major joint dislocation in adults. Which direction of dislocation is most common, and how is the elbow typically positioned by the patient?","choices":{"A":"Anterior dislocation is most common; the patient holds the elbow in full extension with the arm appearing elongated and the antecubital fossa showing a palpable humeral mass anteriorly","B":"Medial dislocation is most common; the patient holds the elbow at 90 degrees with the forearm deviated medially and the radial head palpable laterally through the skin","C":"Posterior or posterolateral dislocation is most common; the patient holds the elbow in partial flexion with the forearm appearing shortened and the olecranon prominent posteriorly","D":"Lateral dislocation is most common; the patient holds the elbow in supination with the forearm deviated laterally and the medial epicondyle palpable on the medial arm","E":"Rotatory dislocation is most common; the patient holds the elbow in a neutral rotation with clicking and locking that prevents full flexion or extension of the forearm"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Posterior and posterolateral dislocations account for the majority of elbow dislocations. The mechanism is a FOOSH (fall on outstretched hand) with the elbow in extension and a valgus stress. The olecranon is prominent posteriorly and the elbow is held in partial flexion. Anterior dislocations are rare and result from a direct posterior blow. Medial and lateral dislocations are uncommon isolated injuries.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Elbow dislocation = second most common major joint dislocation (shoulder is first); posterior/posterolateral most common; terrible triad = dislocation + radial head fracture + coronoid fracture. COMLEX: Neurovascular check before and after reduction; somatic dysfunction of the elbow and forearm common after dislocation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve at elbow: posterior medial epicondyle course makes this nerve vulnerable in elbow dislocations and the terrible triad"},{"id":"sdl25_q05","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the presentation and management of elbow joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The \"terrible triad\" of the elbow describes a combination of injuries that produces severe instability. Which combination of findings defines this injury pattern?","choices":{"A":"Elbow dislocation combined with olecranon fracture and avulsion of the medial epicondyle, creating a pattern that is best managed with immediate cast immobilization in 90 degrees of flexion","B":"Elbow dislocation combined with radial head fracture and coronoid process fracture, creating a triplanar instability that typically requires surgical fixation to restore elbow stability","C":"Elbow dislocation combined with distal humerus fracture and median nerve laceration, representing the classic high-energy pattern seen in pediatric supracondylar fractures with dislocation","D":"Elbow dislocation combined with radial shaft fracture and complete ulnar nerve transection, creating the typical low-energy injury seen in elderly patients with osteoporosis","E":"Elbow dislocation combined with lateral epicondyle avulsion and biceps tendon rupture, producing an anterolateral rotatory instability pattern requiring ligament reconstruction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The terrible triad consists of elbow dislocation + radial head fracture + coronoid process fracture. The coronoid provides the anterior buttress against posterior ulnar translation, and the radial head provides lateral column support. Loss of both creates triplanar instability that typically requires surgical fixation of both the coronoid and radial head, plus ligamentous repair. This combination is notoriously unstable.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Terrible triad = elbow dislocation + radial head fracture + coronoid fracture; coronoid is the key stabilizer against posterior translation — even small fragments matter. COMLEX: Ulnar nerve monitoring essential post-reduction; elbow somatic dysfunction and restricted ROM are common sequelae requiring OMM and PT.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve at elbow: posterior medial epicondyle course makes this nerve vulnerable in elbow dislocations and the terrible triad"},{"id":"sdl25_q06","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the presentation and management of elbow joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which mechanism most commonly produces a posterior elbow dislocation in an adult?","choices":{"A":"Direct medial blow to the flexed elbow with the forearm in pronation, which applies a varus moment that drives the radial head medially and the olecranon anteriorly under the trochlea","B":"Forced hyperflexion of the elbow against an axial compressive load, which fails the posterior capsule and drives the humeral trochlea anteriorly through the coronoid fossa into the brachialis","C":"Repetitive valgus stress from overhead throwing mechanics, which gradually attenuates the ulnar collateral ligament and eventually allows posterior subluxation of the elbow with throwing","D":"Posterior blow to the olecranon with the elbow in 90 degrees of flexion, which is the typical mechanism of anterior elbow dislocation rather than the posterior pattern","E":"Fall on an outstretched hand with the elbow in extension and a valgus stress, which drives the olecranon posteriorly over the distal humerus as the anterior capsule fails under traction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Posterior elbow dislocation results from a FOOSH with the elbow in extension plus valgus stress. This combination axially loads the elbow while forcing the olecranon posteriorly, failing the anterior capsule and lateral collateral ligament complex. Hyperflexion with axial load would more likely fracture the olecranon. Repetitive valgus stress causes UCL attenuation (thrower's elbow) without dislocation. A direct posterior blow to the olecranon produces anterior dislocation (rare).","boardPrep":"Step 1: FOOSH + extension + valgus → posterior elbow dislocation; reduction by longitudinal traction on forearm with supination, then flexion under conscious sedation. COMLEX: Post-reduction early mobilization to prevent elbow stiffness (most common complication); assess for heterotopic ossification in high-energy injuries.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve at elbow: posterior medial epicondyle course makes this nerve vulnerable in elbow dislocations and the terrible triad"},{"id":"sdl25_q07","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the presentation and management of glenohumeral joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 22-year-old athlete sustains recurrent anterior glenohumeral dislocations. MRI shows an impaction fracture on the posterolateral humeral head. What is this lesion, and what causes it?","choices":{"A":"Hill-Sachs lesion — a compression fracture of the posterolateral humeral head created by the sharp anteroinferior glenoid rim as the humeral head dislocates anteriorly over it during dislocation","B":"Bankart lesion — an anteroinferior capsular tear with avulsion of the glenoid labrum from the anteroinferior glenoid rim, representing the soft-tissue restraint failure rather than a bony humeral lesion","C":"Reverse Hill-Sachs lesion — a compression fracture of the anterior humeral head created in posterior dislocations when the posterior glenoid rim impacts the humeral head from behind","D":"Greater tuberosity avulsion — traction failure of the supraspinatus and infraspinatus insertions from the greater tuberosity during reduction maneuvers in elderly patients over 40 years of age","E":"SLAP lesion — a tear of the superior labrum extending from anterior to posterior at the biceps anchor, resulting from repetitive overhead traction rather than acute traumatic dislocation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Hill-Sachs lesion is a posterolateral humeral head impaction fracture caused by the anterior glenoid rim during anterior dislocation. The Bankart lesion is the glenoid labrum tear — the soft-tissue equivalent. Reverse Hill-Sachs (McLaughlin lesion) occurs in posterior dislocations — anterior humeral head impaction on the posterior glenoid. Greater tuberosity avulsion occurs in patients >40 years from rotator cuff traction. SLAP lesions are overhead athlete injuries.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hill-Sachs = posterolateral humeral head (anterior dislocation); reverse Hill-Sachs = anterior humeral head (posterior dislocation); both Hill-Sachs and Bankart predispose to recurrent dislocation when large. COMLEX: Apprehension test detects anterior instability; relocation test (posterior pressure) relieves symptoms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — Rotator cuff integrity: disrupted in anterior shoulder dislocation in patients over 40, contributing to glenohumeral instability"},{"id":"sdl25_q08","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the presentation and management of glenohumeral joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which nerve is most commonly injured in anterior glenohumeral dislocation, and what is the classic sensory finding used to assess its function?","choices":{"A":"Radial nerve — sensory loss over the dorsal forearm and hand with wrist drop confirms injury; the radial nerve is the most vulnerable structure during reduction maneuvers at the elbow","B":"Musculocutaneous nerve — sensory loss over the lateral forearm (lateral antebrachial cutaneous) with loss of elbow flexion and forearm supination confirms injury after dislocation","C":"Median nerve — sensory loss over the thenar eminence and radial 3.5 digits with hand of benediction deformity on attempted fist closure confirms median nerve injury at the shoulder","D":"Axillary nerve — sensory loss over the proximal lateral aspect of the arm (regimental badge area) confirms injury; the deltoid extension lag sign assesses motor function","E":"Suprascapular nerve — motor loss of supraspinatus and infraspinatus with empty can test weakness and external rotation deficit confirms suprascapular nerve injury after dislocation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The axillary nerve courses around the surgical neck of the humerus and through the quadrilateral space, making it the nerve most vulnerable in anterior glenohumeral dislocation. Sensory loss over the lateral deltoid (regimental badge area) is the classic finding. The deltoid extension lag test assesses motor function — the shoulder is passively extended and the patient cannot hold the position if the axillary nerve is injured. Radial nerve injury occurs with midshaft humerus fractures, not shoulder dislocations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Axillary nerve = most common nerve injured in anterior shoulder dislocation; sensory = lateral deltoid (regimental badge); motor = deltoid (abduction) and teres minor (external rotation). COMLEX: Assess axillary nerve BEFORE and AFTER reduction; dead arm syndrome is a transient variant from brief axillary nerve compression.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — Rotator cuff integrity: disrupted in anterior shoulder dislocation in patients over 40, contributing to glenohumeral instability"},{"id":"sdl25_q09","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the presentation and management of glenohumeral joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient sustains a posterior glenohumeral dislocation and undergoes successful closed reduction. In what position should the shoulder be immobilized, and why?","choices":{"A":"Internal rotation in a standard sling for 2 to 6 weeks, which is appropriate for anterior dislocations where external rotation is the apprehension position but would recreate the vulnerable position for posterior dislocations","B":"Full abduction at 90 degrees in a shoulder spica cast for 4 weeks, which is an outdated approach previously used for all shoulder dislocations but not specific to directional instability patterns","C":"External rotation with a small amount of abduction for 2 to 6 weeks, because this position tightens the posterior capsule and prevents the arm from returning to the internally rotated vulnerable position that caused the dislocation","D":"Neutral rotation with the elbow at 90 degrees in a sling for 3 weeks, which provides no directional preference and fails to tighten the posterior capsule against re-dislocation in the posterior direction","E":"Flexion at 90 degrees with internal rotation in a body harness for 6 weeks, which is the position used for acromioclavicular separations and is inappropriate for posterior glenohumeral instability"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Posterior dislocation is immobilized in external rotation (+ slight abduction) for 2-6 weeks. This is opposite to anterior dislocation management (sling in internal rotation). External rotation tightens the posterior capsule and subscapularis, preventing re-dislocation into the vulnerable position of internal rotation and forward flexion. Immobilizing in internal rotation recreates the injury position. Posterior dislocation is classically associated with seizures, lightning strike, and electrocution.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior dislocation → arm in internal rotation, cannot externally rotate; AP x-ray may look normal; axillary lateral view confirms; immobilize in EXTERNAL rotation. COMLEX: Reverse Hill-Sachs (anterior humeral head) is the bony lesion; suspect posterior dislocation in any patient after seizure with shoulder pain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — Rotator cuff integrity: disrupted in anterior shoulder dislocation in patients over 40, contributing to glenohumeral instability"},{"id":"sdl25_q10","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the presentation and management of femoroacetabular joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Traumatic femoroacetabular dislocations most commonly occur in which direction, and in what characteristic position is the limb held?","choices":{"A":"Posterior dislocation accounts for approximately 85% of cases; the hip is held in flexion, adduction, and internal rotation with apparent limb shortening","B":"Anterior dislocation is most common at 65% of cases; the hip is held in extension, abduction, and external rotation with the femoral head palpable in the inguinal region or over the obturator foramen","C":"Central dislocation is the most common pattern; the femoral head is driven medially through the acetabular floor by axial loading, with no characteristic limb position as the femur remains in neutral","D":"Superior dislocation occurs most commonly in high-energy trauma; the limb is held in abduction with the greater trochanter riding superiorly above the acetabular rim on plain radiograph","E":"Inferior dislocation is most common in lower extremity compression injuries; the limb is held in abduction with the femoral head palpable in the perineum and the sciatic nerve stretched superiorly"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Posterior hip dislocation accounts for approximately 85% of cases. The classic mechanism is a posteriorly directed force on the knee with the hip flexed — the \"dashboard injury\" in MVCs. The limb is held in flexion, adduction, and internal rotation. Anterior dislocation (15%) produces the opposite — extension, abduction, external rotation — with the femoral head near the obturator foramen or femoral triangle. Central dislocation involves acetabular fracture with medial femoral head displacement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior hip dislocation = flexion/adduction/internal rotation; MVC dashboard mechanism; reduce within 6 hours to minimize AVN risk. COMLEX: Posterior hip dislocation = CP8 named condition; sciatic nerve injury (10-20%); somatic dysfunction of the pelvis and sacrum common after high-energy hip trauma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — Hip dislocation: femoroacetabular instability, AVN risk, and sciatic nerve proximity established in hip trauma framework"},{"id":"sdl25_q11","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the presentation and management of femoroacetabular joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which factor most significantly increases the risk of avascular necrosis (AVN) of the femoral head following posterior hip dislocation?","choices":{"A":"Anterior direction of hip dislocation increases AVN risk more than posterior direction, because the medial circumflex femoral artery is more directly compressed during anterior displacement of the femoral head","B":"Patient age under 30 years is the strongest predictor of AVN because the femoral head blood supply is more dependent on the ligamentum teres in skeletally immature patients with open physes","C":"Delay in reduction beyond 6 to 12 hours dramatically increases AVN risk — delays exceeding 6 hours increase AVN risk approximately 5.6-fold by prolonging ischemia of the retinacular vessels","D":"Use of closed rather than open reduction increases AVN risk significantly, because open reduction with direct visualization prevents additional vascular traction injury during the reduction maneuver","E":"Absence of an associated acetabular fracture paradoxically increases AVN risk because pure ligamentous dislocations require greater force to dislocate and cause more vascular stretch injury"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Time to reduction is the most critical modifiable factor for AVN prevention after hip dislocation. Delays beyond 6 hours increase AVN risk approximately 5.6-fold by prolonging ischemia of the retinacular vessels (branches of the medial circumflex femoral artery) supplying the femoral head. Posterior dislocation has higher AVN risk than anterior. Closed reduction is preferred and does not increase AVN risk. Age and fracture pattern affect prognosis but are not the primary AVN determinant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AVN after hip dislocation = medial circumflex femoral artery injury; reduce within 6 hours; AVN may not manifest clinically for 2 years post-injury — follow with serial MRI. COMLEX: Hip dislocation = CP8 named condition; post-reduction PT and protected weight-bearing protocols are essential rehabilitation components.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — Hip dislocation: femoroacetabular instability, AVN risk, and sciatic nerve proximity established in hip trauma framework"},{"id":"sdl25_q12","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the presentation and management of femoroacetabular joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which nerve is most commonly injured in posterior hip dislocation, and what clinical findings indicate its involvement?","choices":{"A":"Femoral nerve injury is most common with all types of hip dislocation; findings include loss of knee extension and quadriceps weakness with sensory loss over the anterior thigh and medial leg","B":"Obturator nerve injury is the signature complication of posterior hip dislocation; findings include medial thigh sensory loss and weakness of hip adduction with a positive obturator sign on exam","C":"Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve injury is most common with posterior hip dislocation; findings include paresthesias over the anterolateral thigh without motor deficit in a pattern identical to meralgia paresthetica","D":"Superior gluteal nerve injury is the most common neurologic complication; findings include Trendelenburg gait and weakness of gluteus medius and minimus with preserved sensory function throughout the leg","E":"Sciatic nerve injury occurs in 10 to 20% of posterior hip dislocations; findings include foot drop, weakness of hamstrings and all muscles below the knee, and sensory loss in the posterior thigh and below"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The sciatic nerve runs directly posterior to the hip joint and is injured in 10-20% of posterior hip dislocations. Clinical findings include foot drop (loss of dorsiflexion from peroneal division), hamstring weakness, and sensory loss below the knee posterolaterally. The peroneal division is most vulnerable due to its more lateral course. Femoral nerve injury occurs with anterior dislocation. The lateral femoral cutaneous nerve is compressed at the inguinal ligament (meralgia paresthetica), not from posterior hip dislocation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior hip dislocation + sciatic nerve injury → foot drop; peroneal division most vulnerable (lateral position in sciatic trunk); assess dorsiflexion and eversion strength. COMLEX: CP8 named condition; piriformis somatic dysfunction can mimic sciatic nerve irritation; OMM of the pelvis/sacrum post-reduction aids rehabilitation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — Hip dislocation: femoroacetabular instability, AVN risk, and sciatic nerve proximity established in hip trauma framework"},{"id":"sdl25_q13","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the presentation and management of knee joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"After reduction of a traumatic knee dislocation, which ankle-brachial index (ABI) threshold mandates CT angiography to evaluate for popliteal artery injury?","choices":{"A":"An ABI below 0.5 is the only threshold requiring CT angiography, because ABIs between 0.5 and 0.9 represent mild flow reduction that can be managed with serial pulse checks every 4 hours","B":"An ABI below 1.2 compared to the contralateral limb requires CT angiography, because any reduction from the expected normal value of 1.0 to 1.2 indicates arterial injury requiring investigation","C":"An ABI below 0.9 is considered abnormal and mandates CT angiography, as even hard signs of vascular injury may be absent despite significant popliteal artery damage requiring surgical repair","D":"An ABI below 0.7 requires CT angiography, while ABIs between 0.7 and 0.9 can be managed conservatively with Doppler monitoring and repeat examination at 24 and 48 hours post-reduction","E":"Any ABI below 1.0 in the absolute sense requires CT angiography regardless of the degree of reduction, even though ABIs of 0.9 to 1.0 are commonly seen in normal limbs without vascular injury"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After knee dislocation, vascular assessment is critical because popliteal artery injury occurs in a significant proportion of cases. An ABI below 0.9 is abnormal and mandates CT angiography. Vascular status should be monitored up to 72 hours post-reduction because intimal injuries can cause delayed thrombosis even with initially normal pulses. Ischemia lasting more than 4 hours is associated with poor outcomes and amputation risk requiring urgent fasciotomy plus vascular repair.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Knee dislocation = popliteal artery injury (most important vascular complication); ABI <0.9 → CTA; monitor vascular status for 72 hours. COMLEX: Knee dislocation = CP8 named condition; fasciotomy + vascular repair for ischemia; multiligamentous injury requires surgical reconstruction in young active patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — Knee ligamentous injury: ACL/PCL/collateral disruption all occur in traumatic knee dislocations requiring extensive soft-tissue tearing"},{"id":"sdl25_q14","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the presentation and management of knee joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"How are traumatic tibiofemoral dislocations classified, and why is this classification clinically important?","choices":{"A":"Classified by the direction of femoral displacement relative to the tibia, which determines whether the knee extensors or flexors are primarily involved and whether weight-bearing can begin immediately","B":"Classified by the degree of ligamentous tear on MRI (grade I through III), which directly determines surgical versus conservative management independent of the direction of dislocation","C":"Classified by the mechanism of injury (contact versus non-contact), which predicts the pattern of associated neurovascular injury and determines timing of surgical ligamentous reconstruction","D":"Classified by the direction of tibial displacement relative to the femur (anterior, posterior, lateral, medial, or rotatory), which guides ligamentous injury pattern and management strategy","E":"Classified by the associated meniscal injury pattern on arthroscopic examination, which is the primary determinant of long-term functional outcome and return to sport timeline after knee dislocation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Knee dislocations are classified by the direction of tibial displacement relative to the femur. This is clinically important because the direction predicts the ligamentous injury pattern: anterior dislocation predominantly injures the PCL, posterior injures the ACL, and lateral/medial/rotatory dislocations involve the collateral complexes. Complete dislocation requires extensive tearing of multiple supporting ligaments and soft tissues. Rotatory dislocations may be irreducible by closed means due to soft tissue interposition.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Knee dislocation = classified by tibial displacement direction; all knee dislocations injure multiple ligaments; spontaneous reduction can occur before ED arrival — maintain high suspicion in multiligamentous instability. COMLEX: Knee dislocation = CP8 named condition; popliteal artery and common peroneal nerve at greatest risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — Knee ligamentous injury: ACL/PCL/collateral disruption all occur in traumatic knee dislocations requiring extensive soft-tissue tearing"},{"id":"sdl25_q15","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the presentation and management of knee joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Following reduction of a traumatic knee dislocation, at what threshold of ischemia time is prognosis considered poor and amputation risk high?","choices":{"A":"Ischemia lasting more than 12 hours is the threshold for poor prognosis; the first 12 hours represent a safe window during which vascular repair consistently restores limb viability without fasciotomy","B":"Ischemia lasting more than 4 hours is associated with poor prognosis; irreversible muscle and nerve damage accelerates after this threshold, requiring urgent vascular repair with fasciotomy","C":"Ischemia lasting more than 8 hours is the definitive threshold; outcomes remain acceptable within 8 hours of injury regardless of the degree of vascular occlusion in the popliteal artery","D":"Any period of ischemia beyond 30 minutes universally results in amputation regardless of subsequent vascular repair, making fasciotomy and immediate OR the only acceptable management","E":"Ischemia lasting more than 24 hours is the clinical threshold used in current guidelines, as modern vascular and microsurgical techniques reliably restore perfusion within this window after knee dislocation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The 4-hour ischemia threshold is critical for knee dislocations. After 4 hours of ischemia, irreversible muscle and nerve damage accelerates sharply, dramatically worsening amputation risk. Management requires immediate surgical vascular repair plus fasciotomy to address compartment syndrome from reperfusion. The 4-hour threshold emphasizes the urgency of vascular evaluation after knee dislocation — even spontaneously reduced dislocations require emergent vascular assessment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Popliteal artery injury after knee dislocation + ischemia >4 hours = poor prognosis; vascular repair + fasciotomy are emergent; monitor vascular status for 72 hours post-reduction for delayed intimal tear thrombosis. COMLEX: Physical exam findings of progressive pallor, pulselessness, paresthesias, and pain with passive stretch signal compartment syndrome post-repair.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — Knee ligamentous injury: ACL/PCL/collateral disruption all occur in traumatic knee dislocations requiring extensive soft-tissue tearing"},{"id":"sdl25_q16","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the presentation and management of patellar dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Patellar dislocations almost always occur in which direction, and which anatomical feature explains this directional predilection?","choices":{"A":"Medial dislocation is most common because the lateral retinaculum is thicker and stronger than the medial retinaculum, making medial displacement the path of least resistance under forceful contraction","B":"Superior dislocation is most common from quadriceps tendon avulsion injuries, because the superior pole of the patella loses its restraining structure during forced extension against resistance","C":"Inferior dislocation is most common from patellar tendon rupture, because inferior pole displacement follows loss of the patellar tendon anchor during sudden deceleration or landing injuries","D":"Lateral dislocation is nearly universal because the quadriceps pull angle (Q-angle) creates a net lateral force vector on the patella, and the medial patellofemoral ligament is the primary soft-tissue restraint resisting this force","E":"Posterior dislocation into the intercondylar notch is most common in young athletes, because the thin posterior patellar cartilage fails under compressive loading from direct impact during pivoting"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lateral patellar dislocation is almost universal due to the lateral force vector of the quadriceps pull angle (Q-angle). The medial patellofemoral ligament (MPFL) is the primary restraint against lateral displacement, and it tears in >50% of acute dislocations, most often at its femoral attachment. Predisposing factors include trochlear dysplasia (reduced bony constraint), patella alta, and excessive femoral anteversion. Spontaneous reduction occurs as the knee is extended, tightening the quadriceps.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Patellar dislocation = almost always lateral; MPFL is primary restraint; predisposing factors = trochlear dysplasia, patella alta, increased Q-angle. COMLEX: CP8 named condition; first-time dislocation → brace in extension 2-3 weeks + VMO strengthening; recurrent → MPFL reconstruction ± tibial tubercle transfer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — Patellofemoral mechanics: trochlear dysplasia and quadriceps vector abnormalities predispose to lateral patellar instability and dislocation"},{"id":"sdl25_q17","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the presentation and management of patellar dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which ligament is torn in more than 50% of acute patellar dislocations, and at which attachment site does failure most commonly occur?","choices":{"A":"The lateral retinaculum is torn in more than 50% of cases; failure occurs at the patellar attachment and is the primary structure addressed surgically during lateral retinacular release procedures","B":"The medial patellofemoral ligament (MPFL) is torn in more than 50% of cases; failure most commonly occurs at the femoral attachment on the medial femoral condyle near the adductor tubercle","C":"The medial collateral ligament of the knee is torn in more than 50% of patellar dislocations; failure occurs at the tibial attachment and produces combined valgus and patellar instability","D":"The anterior cruciate ligament is torn in more than 50% of patellar dislocations because the same valgus mechanism that dislocates the patella also applies an anterior tibial shear force to the ACL","E":"The patellar tendon is disrupted in more than 50% of cases; failure occurs at the tibial tubercle insertion and produces superior patellar migration rather than lateral displacement at the trochlea"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The MPFL is the primary restraint against lateral patellar displacement and is torn in >50% of acute lateral patellar dislocations. The femoral attachment near the adductor tubercle is the most common failure site. MPFL reconstruction targeting this anatomical footprint is the basis of surgical treatment for recurrent instability. Lateral retinaculum release alone is no longer recommended as an isolated procedure. MCL and ACL injuries are associated with different mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MPFL = primary medial restraint of the patella; femoral attachment most commonly torn; MRI confirms injury location prior to reconstruction planning. COMLEX: Hemarthrosis after patellar dislocation suggests osteochondral injury; apprehension test (lateral patellar force) + medial patellar tenderness = classic exam findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — Patellofemoral mechanics: trochlear dysplasia and quadriceps vector abnormalities predispose to lateral patellar instability and dislocation"},{"id":"sdl25_q18","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the presentation and management of patellar dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 17-year-old athlete sustains a first-time lateral patellar dislocation that reduces spontaneously. X-ray shows no fracture. What is the initial management?","choices":{"A":"Brace or cylinder cast with the knee in extension for 2 to 3 weeks, followed by quadriceps strengthening focusing on the VMO, with operative management reserved for chronic or recurrent instability","B":"Immediate surgical MPFL repair is indicated for all first-time patellar dislocations in athletes to prevent recurrence, as conservative management has unacceptably high re-dislocation rates in young patients","C":"Closed reduction with splinting in 90 degrees of knee flexion for 6 weeks, which relaxes the quadriceps and allows the MPFL to heal in a shortened position suitable for return to sport","D":"Arthroscopic washout alone without immobilization, because hemarthrosis drainage is the primary treatment goal and early range of motion prevents arthrofibrosis after patellar dislocation","E":"Open patellar realignment surgery with tibial tubercle osteotomy for all first-time dislocations in patients with patella alta, regardless of TT-TG distance or degree of trochlear dysplasia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"First-time lateral patellar dislocation without fracture is managed conservatively: brace/cylinder cast in extension for 2-3 weeks, then quadriceps and VMO strengthening, and gradual return to activity. Operative management (MPFL reconstruction, tibial tubercle transfer) is reserved for recurrent dislocations or when structural risk factors (elevated TT-TG >20 mm, severe trochlear dysplasia) warrant early surgical correction. Immobilization in flexion is incorrect — extension maintains patellar alignment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First-time patellar dislocation → conservative management (brace in extension, PT); recurrent → MPFL reconstruction; TT-TG >20 mm → consider tibial tubercle anteromedialization. COMLEX: VMO strengthening is the cornerstone of patellar rehabilitation; assess for somatic dysfunction at the knee and hip affecting quadriceps mechanics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — Patellofemoral mechanics: trochlear dysplasia and quadriceps vector abnormalities predispose to lateral patellar instability and dislocation"},{"id":"sdl25_q19","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the presentation and management of ankle joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the clinical significance of a 1 mm lateral talar shift within the ankle mortise, and why is precise mortise congruency critical?","choices":{"A":"A 1 mm lateral talar shift is within the normal radiographic tolerance of 2 mm and is clinically insignificant, requiring no intervention as the peroneal muscles compensate for this degree of displacement","B":"A 1 mm lateral talar shift decreases tibiotalar contact area by approximately 10%, which is mild but acceptable in low-demand patients who are managed with functional bracing and activity modification","C":"A 1 mm lateral talar shift decreases tibiotalar contact area by approximately 20%, which is significant only in combination with medial clear space widening greater than 4 mm on the mortise view","D":"A 1 mm lateral talar shift is only relevant in pediatric patients with open physes, as adult ankle cartilage and subchondral bone tolerate mild talar displacement without accelerated arthritis","E":"A 1 mm lateral talar shift decreases the tibiotalar contact area by approximately 40%, dramatically increasing contact stress and accelerating post-traumatic arthritis if left unreduced"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The ankle mortise functions as a uniplanar hinge joint and requires precise congruency. Even a 1 mm lateral talar shift decreases tibiotalar contact area by approximately 40%, dramatically increasing point contact stress and accelerating post-traumatic arthritis. This is why ankle dislocations should never be left unreduced and why precise anatomic restoration is essential. Post-reduction imaging confirms mortise congruency, and persistent instability requires CRIF or ORIF.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ankle mortise = uniplanar hinge; 1 mm lateral talar shift = 40% contact area reduction; syndesmotic injury (tibiofibular clear space >6 mm) = most reliable indicator of mortise instability. COMLEX: Ankle dislocation = CP8 named condition; reduce immediately, splint, elevate; assess for subtalar dislocation in high-energy ankle injuries.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: lateral ligamentous laxity and mortise stability principles bridge ankle sprain to talar dislocation pathomechanics"},{"id":"sdl25_q20","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the presentation and management of ankle joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"On a post-reduction mortise view following ankle dislocation, which radiographic finding is the most reliable indicator of syndesmotic injury requiring fixation?","choices":{"A":"Medial clear space greater than 4 mm indicates isolated deltoid ligament rupture and is the most reliable indicator of syndesmotic injury requiring operative repair of the medial ankle structures","B":"Tibiofibular clear space greater than 6 mm on the mortise view is the most reliable indicator of syndesmotic disruption and indicates instability requiring syndesmotic fixation","C":"Fibular shortening greater than 2 mm on the AP view is the primary indicator of syndesmotic injury because fibular length is always maintained when the syndesmotic ligaments are intact","D":"Loss of talar dome cartilage on MRI is the most reliable indicator of syndesmotic disruption, as osteochondral injury consistently accompanies complete syndesmotic ligament failure in ankle dislocations","E":"Tibiofibular overlap less than 10 mm on the AP view is the standard threshold for syndesmotic injury, while tibiofibular overlap measurement on the mortise view is unreliable due to projection variation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Tibiofibular clear space >6 mm on the mortise view is the most reliable radiographic indicator of syndesmotic injury. Medial clear space >4 mm indicates deltoid ligament disruption, which is a separate finding. Fibular shortening suggests a Maisonneuve or Weber C pattern. When the syndesmosis is disrupted, the mortise widens and the talus laterally shifts, causing the characteristic contact area reduction. Syndesmotic fixation (screws or flexible suture-button device) restores mortise anatomy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Syndesmotic injury = tibiofibular clear space >6 mm (mortise view); medial clear space >4 mm = deltoid rupture; assess both views after ankle dislocation reduction. COMLEX: Ankle dislocation never left unreduced — skin necrosis risk; subtalar dislocation (talocalcaneal + talonavicular) is a separate entity from tibiotalar dislocation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: lateral ligamentous laxity and mortise stability principles bridge ankle sprain to talar dislocation pathomechanics"},{"id":"sdl25_q21","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the presentation and management of ankle joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the highest-priority management step immediately upon recognizing an ankle dislocation in the emergency setting, before advanced imaging is obtained?","choices":{"A":"Obtain a full MRI of the ankle before any reduction attempt to characterize all associated soft-tissue injuries and plan the definitive surgical approach for ligamentous reconstruction","B":"Apply a posterior splint in the dislocated position and admit for observation, because premature reduction attempts without orthopedic consultation risk conversion to an open dislocation","C":"Administer IV antibiotics prophylactically before any reduction attempt, because all ankle dislocations are considered contaminated injuries requiring antibiotic coverage prior to manipulation","D":"Prompt closed reduction must be performed immediately because the ankle should never be left in a dislocated position — skin tenting risks pressure necrosis, and neurovascular compromise worsens with delay","E":"Obtain CT angiography of the foot and ankle before reduction to document vascular anatomy, as reduction without vascular imaging risks embolism from disrupted ankle vessels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ankle dislocations must be reduced immediately — the ankle should never be left in a dislocated position. Skin tenting risks pressure necrosis and conversion to an open injury. Neurovascular compromise worsens with prolonged dislocation. Reduction is performed with adequate analgesia/sedation, followed by splinting, elevation, and post-reduction imaging. MRI is not a pre-reduction study. Antibiotics are indicated for open fractures/dislocations but not routinely before closed reduction. The reduction priority parallels hip and knee dislocation urgency.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ankle dislocation = immediate reduction; never leave dislocated; post-reduction: splint, elevate, neurovascular check, AP/mortise/lateral x-rays; persistent instability → CRIF or ORIF. COMLEX: Subtalar dislocation (medial 85%, lateral 15%) is distinct — inversion produces medial subtalar dislocation; reduction under sedation, NWB cast 6 weeks.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: lateral ligamentous laxity and mortise stability principles bridge ankle sprain to talar dislocation pathomechanics"},{"id":"sdl25_q22","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the presentation and management of interphalangeal joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old male basketball player catches a fast-pass awkwardly, jamming the ring finger. He presents with swelling, point tenderness at the PIP joint, and dorsal displacement of the middle phalanx. Post-reduction X-ray shows a small volar plate avulsion fragment at the base of the middle phalanx with concentric joint reduction. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Digital nerve block followed by splinting the PIP joint at 30 degrees of flexion for 3 weeks, protecting the volar plate during healing while allowing early DIP and MCP range of motion","B":"Closed reduction under general anesthesia in the operating room, because all avulsion fractures at the base of the middle phalanx require intraoperative fluoroscopy to confirm fragment position before immobilization","C":"Immediate open reduction and internal fixation of the volar plate avulsion fragment regardless of fragment size, because any bony avulsion at the PIP joint destabilizes the flexor tendon sheath mechanism","D":"Hyperextend the PIP joint and apply a dorsal blocking splint in 90 degrees of flexion for 6 weeks, which is the correct technique for dorsal DIP dislocations but not for PIP volar plate avulsions","E":"Buddy tape the ring finger to the middle finger and allow immediate full active range of motion, because concentric reduction confirmed on X-ray means the joint is inherently stable without immobilization"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A concentric dorsal PIP dislocation with a small volar plate avulsion is managed with digital block, reduction, and splinting at 30° flexion for 3 weeks. This protects the healing volar plate while allowing adjacent joint motion. ORIF is reserved for large fragments (>30-40% of the articular surface) or irreducible dislocations. Buddy taping alone is insufficient for acute volar plate injuries. 90° flexion immobilization would cause flexion contracture. The avulsion fragment does not destabilize the flexor mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Small volar plate avulsion + concentric PIP reduction = splint at 30° flexion 3 weeks; large fragment (>30-40% articular surface) or irreducible = operative. COMLEX: Irreducible PIP dislocation = interposed volar plate or FDP tendon blocking reduction; assess for associated osteochondral injury with X-ray.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — Connective tissue physiology: volar plate composition and attachment determine IP joint dislocation pattern and healing capacity after avulsion injury"},{"id":"sdl25_q23","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the presentation and management of interphalangeal joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old female volleyball player sustains a dorsal PIP dislocation of the middle finger with a confirmed volar plate tear. She declines immobilization and returns to play. She presents 8 weeks later with the PIP joint hyperextended and the DIP joint flexed, unable to fully flex the PIP joint. Which deformity has developed, and what is the underlying biomechanical explanation?","choices":{"A":"Boutonnière deformity — volar plate laxity allows the lateral extensor bands to migrate volarly past the PIP axis of rotation, producing PIP flexion and DIP hyperextension with loss of active PIP extension","B":"Mallet finger deformity — chronic volar plate insufficiency transmits abnormal force through the extensor mechanism to the terminal tendon, producing isolated DIP flexion deformity with preserved PIP motion","C":"Trigger finger deformity — post-traumatic adhesions between the volar plate and the flexor tendon sheath at the A2 pulley produce intermittent catching at the PIP joint during flexion and extension","D":"Swan neck deformity — chronic volar plate laxity at the PIP allows hyperextension, which increases oblique retinacular ligament tension and reciprocally flexes the DIP joint through a tenodesis mechanism","E":"Intrinsic plus deformity — volar plate laxity at the PIP causes the intrinsic muscles to overpower the extrinsics, producing MCP flexion with PIP and DIP extension across all digits simultaneously"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Swan neck deformity (PIP hyperextension + DIP flexion) develops from chronic volar plate laxity. The volar plate normally prevents PIP hyperextension; when lax, the PIP hyperextends, which tightens the oblique retinacular ligament (running volar to the PIP axis but dorsal to the DIP axis), pulling the DIP into flexion. Boutonnière is the opposite pattern — PIP flexion + DIP hyperextension from central slip failure. Mallet finger is isolated DIP drop from terminal extensor tendon rupture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Swan neck = PIP hyperextension + DIP flexion (volar plate laxity); boutonnière = PIP flexion + DIP hyperextension (central slip rupture). COMLEX: Both deformities are preventable with proper initial immobilization; silver ring splints can manage flexible swan neck deformity non-operatively in mild cases.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — Connective tissue physiology: volar plate and oblique retinacular ligament form an integrated tenodesis system that couples PIP and DIP joint motion"},{"id":"sdl25_q24","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the presentation and management of interphalangeal joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 31-year-old male construction worker sustains a volar PIP dislocation — the middle phalanx is displaced volarly on the proximal phalanx. This injury is rare and requires careful evaluation. Which additional structure injury must be specifically assessed, and what deformity results if it is missed?","choices":{"A":"Flexor digitorum profundus avulsion must be assessed — volar PIP dislocation is caused by FDP rupture at the distal phalanx, and if missed leads to jersey finger deformity with DIP flexion deficit and a palpable mass in the palm","B":"Volar plate rupture must be assessed as the primary associated injury — in volar PIP dislocations the volar plate avulses volarly, and if untreated leads to swan neck deformity from the resulting PIP hyperextension","C":"Central extensor slip rupture must be assessed — volar PIP dislocation is associated with central slip injury, and if missed and left unsplinted in extension, progressive boutonnière deformity develops as the lateral bands migrate volarly","D":"Radial collateral ligament rupture must be assessed — radial collateral ligament is six times more likely to rupture than the ulnar collateral, and if missed leads to permanent lateral instability at the PIP joint","E":"Terminal extensor tendon avulsion must be assessed — volar displacement of the middle phalanx stretches the terminal tendon at the DIP, and if missed leads to mallet finger deformity with isolated DIP flexion deficit"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Volar PIP dislocation (middle phalanx displaced volarly) is the rare counterpart to the common dorsal dislocation. It is associated with central slip rupture — the extensor mechanism's middle slip that inserts on the dorsal base of the middle phalanx. If the central slip is disrupted and not splinted in full extension for 4-6 weeks, the lateral bands migrate volarly past the PIP axis, producing boutonnière deformity (PIP flexion + DIP hyperextension). This distinction from dorsal PIP dislocation management is critical.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Volar PIP dislocation → central slip rupture → boutonnière if untreated; splint PIP in full EXTENSION for 4-6 weeks (opposite of dorsal PIP); allow active DIP flexion during immobilization to prevent DIP stiffness. COMLEX: Elson test (DIP extension with PIP held at 90° flexion) detects central slip integrity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — Connective tissue physiology: central slip extensor mechanism integrity at PIP joint determines whether volar dislocation produces boutonnière deformity on healing"},{"id":"sdl25_q25","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the presentation and management of elbow joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old male falls from a ladder, landing on an outstretched right hand. He presents with elbow pain, deformity, and a shortened forearm with the olecranon prominent posteriorly. X-ray confirms a posterior elbow dislocation without fracture. After successful closed reduction under conscious sedation, which of the following is the most appropriate immediate post-reduction management?","choices":{"A":"Apply a long arm cast in full extension for 6 weeks to allow complete capsuloligamentous healing before initiating any range of motion, which is the standard protocol for all elbow dislocations without fracture","B":"Perform immediate ORIF of the collateral ligaments and anterior capsule under general anesthesia, because all elbow dislocations result in complete ligamentous disruption requiring operative repair","C":"Obtain MRI arthrogram before allowing any range of motion to document the completeness of capsuloligamentous healing and determine if any occult osteochondral injuries require arthroscopic treatment","D":"Apply a circular cast in 30 degrees of elbow flexion for 8 weeks, because immobilization in flexion prevents posterior subluxation but is maintained at less than 90 degrees to avoid neurovascular compromise","E":"Assess neurovascular status and full range of motion immediately post-reduction, then splint at 90 degrees of flexion and begin early mobilization within the first week to prevent elbow stiffness, the most common complication"},"correct":"E","explanation":"After reduction of a simple posterior elbow dislocation (no fracture), the priority is neurovascular reassessment and range of motion testing to confirm stability. The elbow is splinted at ~90° flexion. Early mobilization within the first week is emphasized because elbow stiffness is the most common complication of elbow dislocation, and prolonged immobilization dramatically worsens outcomes. Long arm casting in extension is contraindicated. ORIF is reserved for unstable reductions or terrible triad injuries.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Simple elbow dislocation → closed reduction → splint at 90° → early ROM within 1 week; stiffness = most common complication. COMLEX: Assess brachial artery and median/ulnar/radial nerves pre- and post-reduction; heterotopic ossification is a delayed complication of high-energy elbow dislocations treated with prolonged immobilization.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at elbow: posterior medial epicondyle anatomy makes the ulnar nerve vulnerable during elbow dislocation and a priority for post-reduction neurologic assessment"},{"id":"sdl25_q26","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the presentation and management of elbow joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 27-year-old female rugby player sustains a posterior elbow dislocation during a match. After closed reduction, she reports paresthesias along the medial aspect of the forearm and the medial one and a half digits, along with weakness of her grip and finger abduction. Which nerve is most likely injured, and what is the anatomic explanation?","choices":{"A":"Radial nerve — it spirals around the radial groove of the humerus and is injured during dislocation, producing wrist drop and loss of dorsal forearm and hand sensation from the posterior interosseous nerve branch","B":"Ulnar nerve — it courses in a superficial groove posterior to the medial epicondyle and is stretched or contused during dislocation, producing medial forearm and ring/small finger paresthesias with intrinsic hand weakness","C":"Median nerve — it runs anteromedially through the cubital fossa and is the most commonly injured nerve in elbow dislocation, producing thenar atrophy and radial 3.5 digit sensory loss as the primary deficit","D":"Medial antebrachial cutaneous nerve — it provides sensory innervation to the medial forearm and is the most commonly injured structure during elbow reduction maneuvers rather than the dislocation itself","E":"Anterior interosseous nerve — it branches from the median nerve just below the elbow and produces an isolated motor deficit of the FPL and index FDP without any sensory loss or intrinsic hand weakness"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The ulnar nerve lies in the cubital tunnel posterior to the medial epicondyle and is the nerve most vulnerable during elbow dislocation due to this superficial medial position. Injury produces medial forearm paresthesias (dorsal and palmar medial forearm) and medial 1.5 digit numbness (ring and small finger), plus intrinsic hand weakness (interossei — finger abduction/adduction; hypothenar muscles). The radial nerve injury causes wrist drop. Median nerve injury produces thenar and lateral finger sensory loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ulnar nerve = medial epicondyle groove; elbow dislocation → ulnar nerve stretch; medial 1.5 digits + medial forearm sensory loss + intrinsic weakness. COMLEX: Cubital tunnel syndrome (SDL 14 Obj 3) involves same nerve at same location; elbow dislocation acutely recreates this anatomy with traction rather than compression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at elbow: post-medial epicondyle course creates identical vulnerability in both chronic cubital tunnel syndrome and acute elbow dislocation traction injury"},{"id":"sdl25_q27","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the presentation and management of elbow joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 42-year-old male sustains a terrible triad elbow injury — posterior dislocation with radial head fracture and coronoid process fracture confirmed on CT. During surgical planning, the surgeon emphasizes that a specific fracture component is the most critical destabilizer of the elbow joint, even when the fragment appears small on imaging. Which structure is this?","choices":{"A":"Lateral epicondyle fracture — lateral epicondyle avulsion disrupts the common extensor origin and lateral ulnar collateral ligament origin, which is the primary restraint against posterolateral rotatory instability of the elbow","B":"Capitellum fracture — an associated capitellum fracture indicates a high-energy pattern that disrupts the radiocapitellar articulation, which is the primary lateral column stabilizer against valgus and axial loading","C":"Olecranon fracture — olecranon comminution is the defining feature of terrible triad injury and always requires plate fixation first because the triceps pull directly destabilizes the ulnohumeral articulation","D":"Coronoid process fracture — the coronoid is the primary anterior buttress preventing posterior ulnar translation, and even a small coronoid fragment removal destabilizes the elbow against posterior dislocation forces","E":"Radial styloid fracture — the radial styloid is the key lateral stabilizer of the forearm and its fracture predisposes to proximal radioulnar joint instability rather than distal radius shortening"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The coronoid process is the critical anterior buttress of the ulna that prevents posterior translation of the ulnohumeral joint. In the terrible triad, even a small (Regan-Morrey Type I or II) coronoid fragment, if excised or not repaired, allows recurrent posterior elbow instability. Surgical management prioritizes coronoid fixation or reconstruction first, then radial head fixation or arthroplasty, then lateral collateral ligament repair. The radial head provides lateral column support but the coronoid is the key to preventing re-dislocation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Terrible triad = dislocation + radial head fracture + coronoid fracture; coronoid = anterior buttress; surgical priority = coronoid → radial head → LCL repair. COMLEX: Even small coronoid fragments (Regan-Morrey Type I) have clinical significance in the setting of elbow instability; stiffness remains the most common post-operative complication.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve at elbow: the medial anatomy of the elbow including the coronoid and medial epicondyle shares surgical planes with the ulnar nerve requiring careful protection during terrible triad repair"},{"id":"sdl25_q28","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the presentation and management of glenohumeral joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 20-year-old male college football player sustains his first anterior glenohumeral dislocation and undergoes successful closed reduction. He is immobilized in a sling for 4 weeks and completes physical therapy. Over the next year he has two more dislocations with progressively less force. MRI confirms a Bankart lesion and a Hill-Sachs lesion. What is the most appropriate next management step?","choices":{"A":"Repeat conservative management with a longer immobilization period of 8 to 12 weeks in internal rotation to allow the Bankart lesion to heal without surgery, as recurrence after two dislocations does not yet meet operative criteria","B":"Surgical stabilization including Bankart repair with labral reattachment to the anteroinferior glenoid rim and anteroinferior capsular tightening, given failure of conservative treatment and high recurrence risk in young active patients","C":"Physical therapy exclusively focusing on posterior capsule and internal rotator strengthening for 6 additional months before considering surgery, because strengthening the dynamic stabilizers eliminates most recurrent dislocation risk","D":"Glenohumeral corticosteroid injection to reduce capsular inflammation and restore normal joint mechanics, which is the recommended intervention for recurrent instability in young athletes before surgical consultation","E":"Reverse total shoulder arthroplasty to replace the damaged glenohumeral joint surfaces, which is the definitive surgical treatment for young patients with recurrent anterior instability and associated Hill-Sachs lesions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Young patients (<25 years) with recurrent anterior glenohumeral dislocation have a high risk of re-dislocation (up to 90% conservatively) and should be counseled about surgical stabilization after failed conservative management. Arthroscopic Bankart repair (labral reattachment + anteroinferior capsular tightening) is the standard surgical approach. Large Hill-Sachs lesions may require remplissage or Latarjet procedure. Corticosteroids and additional PT are not appropriate for recurrent instability. Reverse shoulder arthroplasty is for rotator cuff arthropathy in elderly patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Young patient + recurrent anterior dislocation → Bankart repair; Hill-Sachs + Bankart lesions = classic pair in recurrent anterior instability; glenoid bone loss >20% → Latarjet coracoid transfer. COMLEX: TUBS = Traumatic, Unidirectional, Bankart lesion, Surgical treatment; AMBRI = Atraumatic, Multidirectional, Bilateral, Rehabilitation, Inferior capsular shift.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — Rotator cuff injuries: rotator cuff tear occurs in >40% of patients over 40 with anterior shoulder dislocation, requiring MRI evaluation concurrent with instability workup before definitive surgical planning"},{"id":"sdl25_q29","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the presentation and management of glenohumeral joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old male with a history of alcohol-use disorder is brought to the emergency department after a witnessed generalized tonic-clonic seizure. He complains of right shoulder pain and cannot lift his arm. His arm is held with the hand on his abdomen and he is unable to externally rotate the shoulder. The AP radiograph appears nearly normal. What is the diagnosis and what imaging confirms it?","choices":{"A":"Frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis) — the insidious onset and limited external rotation in this older patient with a comorbid condition is most consistent with adhesive capsulitis, confirmed by MRI arthrogram showing capsular thickening","B":"Anterior glenohumeral dislocation — the mechanism of seizure causes anterior capsule failure, and the AP radiograph typically confirms anterior displacement clearly without need for additional imaging views","C":"Acromioclavicular joint separation — the seizure causes a direct downward force on the shoulder girdle, and the Zanca view best demonstrates the degree of coracoclavicular ligament disruption and clavicle displacement","D":"Rotator cuff tear with pseudoparalysis — seizure-related muscle contractions avulse the supraspinatus from the greater tuberosity, and ultrasound of the subacromial space confirms the full-thickness tear causing apparent weakness","E":"Posterior glenohumeral dislocation — classically associated with seizures and electrocution; the AP may appear normal or show only subtle findings; axillary lateral or Velpeau view confirms the diagnosis and reveals a reverse Hill-Sachs lesion on the anterior humeral head"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Posterior glenohumeral dislocation is classically missed because the AP radiograph may appear nearly normal or show only a \"light bulb sign\" (internal rotation of the humeral head). The arm is held in internal rotation (hand on belly) and external rotation is impossible — the classic locked shoulder of posterior dislocation. Seizures, lightning strikes, and electrocution are the classic atraumatic mechanisms (bilateral symmetric muscle contractions). The axillary lateral view confirms the diagnosis and shows the reverse Hill-Sachs (anterior humeral head compression). Reduction requires longitudinal traction + anterior-directed force, then immobilize in external rotation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior dislocation = seizure, lightning, electrocution; arm in internal rotation, cannot externally rotate; AP may look normal (lightbulb sign); axillary lateral confirms; immobilize in EXTERNAL rotation. COMLEX: Posterior dislocation is the \"great masquerader\"; always obtain axillary lateral view in any shoulder with locked internal rotation after seizure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — Rotator cuff injuries: posterior dislocation can avulse the lesser tuberosity via subscapularis traction, just as anterior dislocation avulses the greater tuberosity via supraspinatus/infraspinatus traction in patients over 40"},{"id":"sdl25_q30","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the presentation and management of glenohumeral joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 23-year-old female volleyball player sustains a first-time anterior shoulder dislocation reduced in the emergency department. After reduction, she develops numbness over her lateral deltoid and cannot maintain her arm in extension when the shoulder is passively extended and released. Which nerve is injured and what specific test confirms the motor deficit?","choices":{"A":"Axillary nerve injury — the deltoid extension lag test confirms motor deficit: the shoulder is passively extended and the patient cannot hold the position because the deltoid (primary extensor at this range) is denervated","B":"Suprascapular nerve injury — confirmed by the empty can test showing weakness of supraspinatus-mediated abduction and the internal rotation lag sign showing infraspinatus deficit in external rotation arc","C":"Radial nerve injury — confirmed by wrist drop and loss of active extension at the wrist, thumb, and fingers, with sensory loss over the dorsal forearm and hand rather than the lateral deltoid region","D":"Musculocutaneous nerve injury — confirmed by loss of elbow flexion and forearm supination with sensory loss over the lateral forearm (lateral antebrachial cutaneous nerve distribution) rather than the shoulder","E":"Long thoracic nerve injury — confirmed by scapular winging on the wall push-up test due to serratus anterior denervation, which presents with lateral winging when the arm is forward flexed against resistance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Axillary nerve injury is confirmed by the deltoid extension lag sign: the shoulder is passively extended to end range and the patient cannot actively hold the position. This tests deltoid function (specifically the posterior deltoid for extension) while avoiding supraspinatus compensation. Sensory loss over the lateral deltoid (regimental badge area) confirms the diagnosis. The suprascapular nerve supplies supraspinatus and infraspinatus (not deltoid). The radial nerve injury produces wrist drop. The long thoracic nerve serves serratus anterior.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Axillary nerve = surgical neck humerus/shoulder dislocation; deltoid extension lag test = most specific motor test; sensory = lateral deltoid. COMLEX: Assess axillary nerve before AND after reduction; dead arm syndrome = transient axillary nerve compression resolving within minutes from brief subluxation during athletic activity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — Rotator cuff injuries: axillary nerve injury post-dislocation must be distinguished from rotator cuff tear as a cause of deltoid weakness, as both present with limited shoulder function but require different rehabilitation approaches"},{"id":"sdl25_q31","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the presentation and management of femoroacetabular joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 30-year-old male unrestrained front-seat passenger is brought in after a head-on MVC. He has right hip pain with the limb held in flexion, adduction, and internal rotation. X-ray confirms a posterior hip dislocation without associated fracture. He is hemodynamically stable. What is the most appropriate immediate management?","choices":{"A":"Emergent open reduction in the operating room for all posterior hip dislocations regardless of associated fractures, because closed reduction risks sciatic nerve traction injury and inadequate acetabular visualization","B":"CT scan of the pelvis before any reduction attempt to rule out associated acetabular fractures, because unrecognized intra-articular fragments during closed reduction cause iatrogenic femoral head cartilage damage","C":"Apply femoral skeletal traction overnight and schedule reduction in the morning operating room to allow muscle relaxation, which reduces the force required for reduction and minimizes sciatic nerve traction risk","D":"Urgent closed reduction under conscious sedation within 6 hours using the Allis method (axial traction with hip and knee at 90 degrees), because delay beyond 6 hours increases AVN risk approximately 5.6-fold by prolonging ischemia of the retinacular vessels","E":"Discharge with non-weight-bearing crutches and outpatient orthopedic referral within 3 to 5 days, because isolated posterior hip dislocation without fracture can be safely managed as an elective reduction procedure"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Posterior hip dislocation requires urgent closed reduction within 6 hours — the most critical determinant of AVN risk. The Allis method: patient supine, assistant stabilizes the pelvis, surgeon applies axial traction with hip and knee at 90° flexion, then gentle internal→external rotation to reduce. A post-reduction CT is obtained after reduction (not before, unless the patient is going directly to OR) to check for intra-articular fragments and associated fractures. Delaying for CT or OR scheduling dramatically increases AVN risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip dislocation → reduce within 6 hours (Allis or Stimson methods); post-reduction CT to assess for intra-articular fragments; AVN follow-up with MRI at 4-6 weeks. COMLEX: Posterior hip dislocation = CP8 named condition; 10-20% sciatic nerve injury rate; assess foot drop pre- and post-reduction; pelvis/sacral somatic dysfunction common after high-energy hip trauma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — Avascular necrosis of the hip: medial circumflex femoral artery disruption in posterior hip dislocation directly bridges the mechanism of AVN and explains the 6-hour reduction urgency window"},{"id":"sdl25_q32","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the presentation and management of femoroacetabular joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old male undergoes successful closed reduction of a posterior hip dislocation sustained in an MVC. Post-reduction CT confirms concentric reduction with a small posterior acetabular wall fragment and no intra-articular loose bodies. Hip stability is confirmed through full range of motion. Over the next 2 years, which complication remains the greatest concern for this patient?","choices":{"A":"Avascular necrosis (AVN) of the femoral head — disruption of the posterior capsule and retinacular vessels (branches of the medial circumflex femoral artery) during dislocation can cause ischemia that manifests as AVN months to 2 years after injury","B":"Sciatic nerve palsy that is permanent and will not recover — sciatic nerve injuries from hip dislocation uniformly progress to complete motor and sensory loss that fails to recover regardless of reduction timing or decompression","C":"Heterotopic ossification at the posterior acetabulum requiring prophylaxis — indomethacin is required for all posterior hip dislocations because heterotopic ossification predictably limits hip flexion to less than 90 degrees within 6 months","D":"Post-traumatic hip osteoarthritis developing within 6 months — cartilage damage from the dislocation produces symptomatic OA within 6 months requiring total hip arthroplasty in all patients under 35 years of age","E":"Deep vein thrombosis from enforced bed rest — immobility after hip dislocation causes DVT in more than 80% of patients, making anticoagulation with therapeutic heparin the most urgent management priority post-reduction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AVN of the femoral head is the most feared long-term complication after hip dislocation. The medial circumflex femoral artery supplies the femoral head retinacular vessels, which are disrupted during posterior dislocation. AVN may not manifest clinically or radiographically for months to 2 years, requiring serial MRI surveillance. Sciatic nerve injuries from hip dislocation can recover partially or fully. Heterotopic ossification occurs but is not universal. Post-traumatic OA develops over years, not months.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AVN after hip dislocation = medial circumflex femoral artery (retinacular branches); onset 1-2 years after injury; follow with MRI; core decompression or THA if symptomatic. COMLEX: Protected weight-bearing post-reduction; serial follow-up for 2 years; hip rehabilitation OMM can address surrounding soft-tissue dysfunction without loading the compromised femoral head.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — Avascular necrosis of the hip: femoral head blood supply via medial circumflex femoral artery is the shared anatomic basis for AVN in both hip dislocation and femoral neck fracture contexts"},{"id":"sdl25_q33","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the presentation and management of femoroacetabular joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old female driver sustains a posterior hip dislocation in a head-on collision where her knees were braced against the dashboard. After reduction, the orthopedic team performs a focused neurologic examination. Which nerve injury should be specifically assessed, and what physical examination findings indicate its involvement?","choices":{"A":"Femoral nerve — anterior to the hip joint and most at risk in anterior hip dislocations; findings include loss of knee extension and quadriceps weakness with sensory loss over the anterior thigh and medial leg from saphenous nerve involvement","B":"Obturator nerve — medial to the hip joint and most vulnerable during medial and central hip dislocations; findings include medial thigh sensory loss and hip adductor weakness producing a characteristic obturator nerve palsy gait","C":"Sciatic nerve — posterior to the hip joint and at risk in 10-20% of posterior dislocations; findings include foot drop (loss of ankle dorsiflexion from peroneal division), hamstring weakness, and sensory loss over the posterior thigh, lateral leg, and dorsum of the foot","D":"Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve — compressed at the inguinal ligament in prone-position patients; findings include anterolateral thigh paresthesias and burning without motor deficit in a distribution identical to meralgia paresthetica","E":"Superior gluteal nerve — injured when the femoral head displaces superiorly during dislocation; findings include Trendelenburg sign and weakness of gluteus medius and minimus without sensory loss throughout the lower extremity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The sciatic nerve passes directly posterior to the hip joint through the greater sciatic foramen and is the nerve most at risk in posterior hip dislocation (10-20% incidence). The dashboard mechanism (posterior directed force on a flexed, adducted knee → hip) causes posterior femoral head displacement that stretches or compresses the sciatic nerve. The peroneal division (more lateral in the sciatic trunk) is most vulnerable, producing foot drop. Femoral nerve is at risk in anterior dislocations. The lateral femoral cutaneous nerve is compressed at the inguinal ligament, not from hip dislocation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sciatic nerve = posterior hip dislocation; peroneal division most vulnerable → foot drop; assess dorsiflexion strength specifically. COMLEX: Post-reduction sciatic nerve exam; piriformis muscle syndrome can mimic sciatic nerve irritation; OMM of the piriformis and posterior hip capsule can complement rehabilitation after posterior dislocation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — Hip fracture and dislocation: sciatic nerve anatomy posterior to the hip joint creates identical vulnerability in both posterior hip dislocation and posterior acetabular wall fractures requiring surgical repair"},{"id":"sdl25_q34","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the presentation and management of knee joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old obese male (BMI 44) trips stepping off a curb and presents with a grossly unstable knee. X-ray confirms a tibiofemoral dislocation. He is hemodynamically stable and the dislocation is reduced. Post-reduction ABI on the affected limb is 0.71. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Close observation with serial pulse checks every 4 hours for 72 hours, because an ABI between 0.7 and 0.9 represents mild flow reduction from post-reduction edema and does not require immediate vascular imaging","B":"Immediate surgical exploration of the popliteal fossa without vascular imaging, because any ABI below 1.0 mandates emergent operative vascular repair to prevent limb-threatening ischemia after knee dislocation","C":"Apply a knee immobilizer and obtain MRI of the knee ligaments before vascular evaluation, because multiligamentous injury documentation is required for surgical planning before addressing vascular status","D":"Repeat the ABI measurement in 6 hours after the swelling resolves from initial reduction, because edema-related ABI reductions routinely normalize within 6 hours and do not require immediate CTA","E":"CT angiography of the lower extremity to evaluate for popliteal artery injury, because an ABI below 0.9 is the threshold mandating vascular imaging regardless of the presence or absence of distal pulses"},"correct":"E","explanation":"An ABI below 0.9 after knee dislocation mandates CT angiography regardless of pulse presence. The popliteal artery is tethered at the knee and commonly injured in knee dislocations, sometimes with initially preserved distal pulses from collateral flow. Intimal injuries can cause delayed thrombosis. Vascular status must be reassessed for 72 hours post-reduction. Low-energy dislocations in obese patients are increasingly recognized, and the same vascular urgency applies. MRI evaluation waits until vascular status is confirmed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Knee dislocation + ABI <0.9 → CT angiography; popliteal artery injury can present with normal pulses initially (intimal tear); monitor vascular status 72 hours. COMLEX: Low-energy knee dislocation in morbidly obese patients = emerging clinical pattern; same ABI threshold and vascular urgency as high-energy dislocations applies.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — ACL/PCL injuries: multiligamentous knee instability after dislocation involves the same ligamentous structures as isolated ACL/PCL injuries but with greater severity and obligate vascular risk assessment requirement"},{"id":"sdl25_q35","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the presentation and management of knee joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 26-year-old male motorcycle rider is brought to the emergency department after a high-speed crash with a traumatic knee dislocation that spontaneously reduced before EMS arrival. CTA is normal. Physical examination reveals gross anteroposterior and mediolateral instability consistent with multiligamentous disruption including the ACL, PCL, and posterolateral complex. He is a competitive cyclist. What is the appropriate surgical management recommendation?","choices":{"A":"Conservative management with hinged knee bracing and physical therapy is equivalent to surgery for all knee dislocations and is the preferred approach because surgical complication rates exceed re-dislocation risk in braced patients","B":"Surgical ligamentous reconstruction of all injured structures is recommended for young, active patients with knee dislocation, given that complete dislocation requires extensive tearing of supporting ligaments and full stability cannot be restored by bracing alone","C":"Only the ACL and PCL require reconstruction after complete knee dislocation, because the collateral ligaments predictably heal with conservative management and do not contribute meaningfully to long-term instability","D":"Surgical reconstruction should be deferred for at least 6 months to allow spontaneous ligamentous healing and scar formation, which often restores sufficient stability without requiring operative intervention in young patients","E":"Only elderly or sedentary patients benefit from surgical reconstruction of knee dislocation; young athletes universally recover full function with an accelerated physical therapy protocol without operative intervention"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Young, active patients with complete knee dislocation and multiligamentous injury should undergo surgical reconstruction of all injured structures. The knee cannot be stabilized adequately by bracing alone when the ACL, PCL, and lateral complex are all disrupted. Early surgical reconstruction (typically staged with initial spanning external fixation if needed) optimizes functional outcomes and return to sport. The spontaneous reduction before ED arrival does not diminish the severity — multiligamentous instability defines the injury pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Knee dislocation = multiligamentous injury requiring reconstruction in young active patients; staged approach common; neurovascular assessment before any surgical intervention. COMLEX: Knee dislocation = CP8 named condition; peroneal nerve injury (foot drop) assessed separately from vascular injury; CPM and early PT post-operatively.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — ACL/PCL injuries: isolated ligament reconstruction techniques from ACL/PCL repair are combined in knee dislocation surgery, which requires multiligamentous reconstruction in a single or staged operative setting"},{"id":"sdl25_q36","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the presentation and management of knee joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman sustains a traumatic knee dislocation in a motor vehicle collision. The dislocation is reduced and her initial ABI is 0.95. Four hours after reduction, her leg becomes progressively pale, cool, and painful with passive stretch of her toes. What do these findings indicate and what is the immediate management?","choices":{"A":"Progressive pallor and coolness are expected post-reduction findings from reactive edema compressing superficial veins, and the appropriate management is elevation and compression dressing with reassessment in 24 hours","B":"Cold extremity and pain with passive stretch after knee dislocation indicate peroneal nerve injury causing loss of normal pain signaling, requiring only splinting in neutral ankle position to prevent foot drop contracture","C":"These findings indicate normal post-reduction inflammation and should be observed for 72 hours without intervention, as current guidelines recommend conservative vascular monitoring before any surgical exploration after knee dislocation","D":"Ischemia time exceeding 4 hours is associated with poor prognosis and amputation risk; these findings indicate evolving vascular occlusion or compartment syndrome requiring emergent vascular repair plus fasciotomy to prevent irreversible limb ischemia","E":"Pale and cool extremity after knee dislocation reduction indicates venous obstruction from hemarthrosis, which responds to aspiration of the joint effusion and repeat Doppler ultrasound to confirm venous patency"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Progressive pallor, coolness, and pain with passive stretch 4 hours after reduction represent a vascular emergency. Ischemia beyond 4 hours after knee dislocation dramatically worsens prognosis and amputation risk. Pain with passive stretch of the toes is also an early compartment syndrome sign. This pattern requires immediate surgical vascular repair (typically popliteal artery bypass or interposition graft) plus four-compartment fasciotomy. An initial ABI of 0.95 can normalize transiently due to vasospasm, then worsen from intimal tear thrombosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ischemia >4 hours after knee dislocation = poor prognosis; emergent vascular repair + fasciotomy; pain with passive stretch = compartment syndrome sign. COMLEX: Monitor for 72 hours even with initially normal vascular exam; compartment syndrome of the leg is a named complication of knee dislocation vascular repair requiring clinical vigilance.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — ACL/PCL injuries: the popliteal fossa neurovascular bundle passes posterior to both the ACL and PCL attachment regions, explaining the proximity that makes it vulnerable in complete knee dislocation with multiligamentous disruption"},{"id":"sdl25_q37","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the presentation and management of patellar dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 17-year-old female gymnast sustains a lateral patellar dislocation that spontaneously reduces when she extends her knee. MRI confirms a complete MPFL tear at the femoral attachment, severe trochlear dysplasia (flat trochlear groove), and patella alta. Which factor most significantly increases her risk of recurrent dislocation?","choices":{"A":"Patient age over 40 years is the strongest predictor of recurrent patellar dislocation, because older patients have greater quadriceps atrophy and VMO weakness that reduces dynamic patellar stabilization during activity","B":"Prior ACL reconstruction is a major risk factor for recurrent patellar dislocation because the graft harvest creates a lateral retinacular imbalance that shifts the patellar tracking vector toward the lateral compartment","C":"Obesity with BMI over 30 is the primary structural risk factor for recurrent lateral patellar dislocation because increased weight increases the Q-angle and the lateral force vector on the patella with every step","D":"Isolated lateral retinaculum tightness without any bony dysplasia is the primary anatomic risk factor for recurrence, because lateral retinacular tightness is the single most important structure preventing medial patellar reduction","E":"Trochlear dysplasia — a flat or convex trochlear groove provides minimal bony constraint to lateral patellar displacement, making the patella dependent entirely on soft-tissue restraints that are already disrupted, creating the highest risk for recurrence"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Trochlear dysplasia is the strongest anatomic risk factor for recurrent patellar dislocation. A flat or convex trochlear groove fails to provide the bony constraint that normally stabilizes the patella during flexion. Combined with MPFL disruption and patella alta, trochlear dysplasia creates the highest-risk triad for recurrence. Surgical management in this patient may include MPFL reconstruction and trochleoplasty. Young age (not older age) is actually a risk factor for recurrence. Prior ACL reconstruction and obesity are not classic risk factors for patellar instability.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Patellar instability risk triad = trochlear dysplasia + patella alta + MPFL tear; TT-TG distance >20 mm = indication for tibial tubercle medialization. COMLEX: CP8 named condition; Dejour classification grades trochlear dysplasia A-D; Grade B-D dysplasia most likely to require trochleoplasty in addition to MPFL reconstruction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — Patellofemoral syndrome: trochlear morphology is the key bony determinant of both patellofemoral syndrome (contact stress) and patellar dislocation (bony constraint deficit), sharing the same anatomic framework"},{"id":"sdl25_q38","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the presentation and management of patellar dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old male soccer player plants his foot and pivots, feeling his patella \"pop out\" and then immediately reduce. On examination he has medial patellar pole tenderness, a moderate hemarthrosis, and positive apprehension sign with lateral patellar pressure. What is the biomechanical explanation for why the patella spontaneously reduced when he extended his knee?","choices":{"A":"Extension tightens the quadriceps mechanism and pulls the patella proximally and centrally into the trochlear groove; as the knee extends from flexion, the increasing patellar tendon tension realigns the patella medially within the groove without manual intervention","B":"Medial retinaculum contraction actively generates sufficient force to pull the displaced patella back into the trochlear groove during knee extension, providing the primary active stabilization of the medially displaced patella","C":"Gravity acts on the laterally displaced patella when the patient stands upright, producing a medially directed force vector that passively returns the patella to the midline position of the trochlear groove on weight-bearing","D":"Hamstring relaxation during knee extension releases the posterolateral tightening that maintained the lateral dislocation, allowing the medial retinaculum tension alone to centralize the patella into the trochlear groove","E":"VMO spasm following lateral dislocation generates an acute medially directed contractile force that pulls the patella rapidly back across the trochlear groove, explaining the audible clunk heard at the moment of spontaneous reduction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Spontaneous patellar reduction with knee extension occurs because quadriceps tension increases as the knee extends. This proximal and central traction pulls the patella back into the trochlear groove. As the knee approaches full extension, the tightened quadriceps vector becomes increasingly perpendicular to the patella, providing the centralization force. This is why lateral patellar dislocations almost always reduce spontaneously with extension — the quadriceps mechanism acts as the reduction force without manual assistance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Patellar dislocation spontaneously reduces with knee extension via quadriceps centralization; hemarthrosis + medial patellar pole tenderness + positive apprehension = classic post-dislocation triad; X-ray after spontaneous reduction confirms osteochondral fragment risk. COMLEX: Medial patellar pole tenderness = MPFL femoral attachment site; check sunrise view for osteochondral injury.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — Patellofemoral syndrome: quadriceps force vector and Q-angle mechanics that produce patellofemoral stress in PFS are the same biomechanical framework that drives lateral patellar dislocation and spontaneous reduction with extension"},{"id":"sdl25_q39","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the presentation and management of patellar dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 29-year-old male has sustained three lateral patellar dislocations over 18 months despite completing a structured physical therapy program including VMO strengthening, taping, and bracing. MRI shows a complete MPFL tear at the femoral attachment. His TT-TG distance measures 24 mm. What is the most appropriate surgical intervention?","choices":{"A":"Isolated lateral retinacular release alone, which reduces the lateral tethering force on the patella and corrects the imbalanced Q-angle effect without requiring MPFL reconstruction or bony realignment surgery","B":"MPFL reconstruction combined with tibial tubercle anteromedialization (Fulkerson osteotomy) to address the elevated TT-TG distance above the 20 mm threshold, correcting both the soft-tissue restraint deficiency and the abnormal lateral quadriceps vector","C":"Total knee replacement for end-stage patellofemoral arthritis, which is the definitive treatment for recurrent patellar instability in young patients who have failed conservative management and MPFL reconstruction","D":"Patellectomy to eliminate the unstable patella entirely, which resolves recurrent lateral dislocation by removing the dislocating structure and has acceptable long-term functional outcomes in young active patients","E":"Quadriceps tendon repair with medial retinaculum reefing alone, which provides sufficient medial stabilization in all patients with TT-TG distances below 30 mm regardless of the number of prior dislocations"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A TT-TG distance >20 mm indicates an excessively lateral quadriceps vector that places the patella at risk regardless of soft-tissue reconstruction alone. MPFL reconstruction addresses the soft-tissue deficiency, while tibial tubercle anteromedialization (Fulkerson osteotomy — anteromedialized tibial tubercle transfer) corrects the bony malalignment by reducing the TT-TG distance and decreasing lateral patellofemoral joint contact stress. Isolated lateral release is no longer recommended as the sole procedure. Patellectomy and TKA are inappropriate in a young active patient.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TT-TG >20 mm = tibial tubercle medialization indicated; MPFL reconstruction = primary soft-tissue procedure for recurrent instability; combined MPFL + Fulkerson = standard for elevated TT-TG. COMLEX: TT-TG measured on MRI or CT; anteromedialization unloads medial facet and corrects lateral tracking simultaneously.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — Patellofemoral syndrome: TT-TG distance is the same measurement used to evaluate patellofemoral syndrome malalignment, bridging PFS diagnosis to surgical planning for recurrent lateral patellar instability"},{"id":"sdl25_q40","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the presentation and management of ankle joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 29-year-old male basketball player lands awkwardly from a jump, sustaining obvious ankle deformity. X-ray confirms talar dislocation from the mortise. The skin over the lateral malleolus is tented and beginning to blanch. What is the most appropriate immediate management?","choices":{"A":"Obtain MRI before any reduction attempt to fully characterize all soft-tissue and osteochondral injuries prior to deciding between closed and open reduction techniques in the operating room","B":"Open reduction and internal fixation in the operating room is required before any closed reduction is attempted, because tented skin indicates soft-tissue interposition that will block closed reduction","C":"Immediate closed reduction without delay — tented, blanching skin indicates imminent pressure necrosis and potential open dislocation; reduce under procedural sedation, splint, elevate, and obtain post-reduction imaging to confirm mortise congruency","D":"Apply a compression dressing and elevate the ankle overnight while awaiting orthopedic consultation in the morning, because the overlying skin blanching reflects normal post-injury inflammatory vasospasm rather than ischemia","E":"Perform bedside reduction only after general anesthesia is established in the operating room, because adequate muscle relaxation is required for all ankle dislocations and procedural sedation is insufficient"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ankle dislocations are orthopedic emergencies requiring immediate reduction. The ankle should never be left in a dislocated position — skin tenting with blanching signals imminent pressure necrosis and risk of skin breakdown/open dislocation. Procedural sedation is appropriate for ED reduction. MRI is not a pre-reduction study in this emergency. The post-reduction priority is confirming mortise congruency on X-ray, assessing neurovascular status, and determining if persistent instability requires operative stabilization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ankle dislocation = immediate reduction; never leave dislocated; skin tenting = emergency; post-reduction: splint, elevate, mortise/AP/lateral views, neurovascular check. COMLEX: Ankle dislocation = CP8 named condition; persistent instability after reduction → CRIF or ORIF; assess for associated distal fibula fracture on post-reduction films.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: the same anterolateral ligamentous complex (ATFL, CFL) that fails in lateral ankle sprains forms the primary restraint against talar dislocation, with mortise stability dependent on intact syndesmosis and medial structures"},{"id":"sdl25_q41","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the presentation and management of ankle joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old male sustains an ankle dislocation that is successfully reduced in the ED. Post-reduction mortise view shows a tibiofibular clear space of 8 mm, a medial clear space of 3 mm, and lateral talar shift of 1.5 mm. The ankle is unstable to stress testing. Which finding is the most reliable indicator of the primary instability source?","choices":{"A":"Tibiofibular clear space greater than 6 mm is the most reliable indicator of syndesmotic disruption; syndesmotic injury allows the fibula to displace laterally, widening the mortise and producing the lateral talar shift and persistent instability","B":"Medial clear space of 3 mm indicates complete deltoid ligament rupture as the primary instability source, because a medial clear space above the 2 mm normal threshold is the most sensitive indicator of ankle instability","C":"Lateral talar shift of 1.5 mm indicates isolated anterolateral ligamentous disruption as the primary instability source, because talar shift below 2 mm is exclusively caused by ATFL and CFL rupture without syndesmotic involvement","D":"The combination of lateral talar shift and medial clear space widening indicates bimalleolar fracture as the primary instability pattern, with ligamentous instability being a secondary consideration after fracture exclusion","E":"Fibular shortening greater than 2 mm on the AP view is the most reliable indicator of syndesmotic instability, while tibiofibular clear space measurement on the mortise view has high false-positive rates due to projection variation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Tibiofibular clear space >6 mm on the mortise view is the most reliable radiographic indicator of syndesmotic disruption. The medial clear space of 3 mm is borderline normal (normal <4 mm), so it does not indicate deltoid ligament rupture in isolation. The 1 mm lateral talar shift (reducing contact area by 40%) combined with a widened tibiofibular clear space points to syndesmotic failure as the primary instability source. Syndesmotic fixation (screw or suture-button device) is required to restore mortise anatomy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Syndesmotic injury = tibiofibular clear space >6 mm (mortise view); medial clear space >4 mm = deltoid rupture; 1 mm lateral talar shift = 40% contact area loss. COMLEX: Syndesmotic injuries (\"high ankle sprains\") are often underdiagnosed; stress mortise view or external rotation stress test under fluoroscopy confirms instability.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: syndesmotic sprain (high ankle sprain) involves the same tibiofibular ligament complex disrupted in ankle dislocation, with mortise radiographic thresholds applicable to both injury patterns"},{"id":"sdl25_q42","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the presentation and management of ankle joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 40-year-old male sustains a severe ankle inversion injury in a trail-running fall. He presents with the foot inverted and internally rotated in a locked position that will not correct with gentle manipulation. X-ray shows simultaneous dislocation of both the talocalcaneal and talonavicular joints without a talar neck fracture. What is the most likely diagnosis and predominant direction?","choices":{"A":"Lateral subtalar dislocation from an eversion mechanism is the most common pattern, accounting for 70% of subtalar dislocations, where the calcaneus and forefoot displace laterally while the talus remains in the ankle mortise","B":"Superior subtalar dislocation from an axial compression mechanism is the most common pattern, where the calcaneus is driven superiorly against the talar body during a fall from height onto the heel with foot in neutral position","C":"Anterior subtalar dislocation from a plantarflexion mechanism is the most frequent presentation, where the calcaneus displaces anteriorly on the talus while the talonavicular joint simultaneously subluxes dorsally","D":"Rotatory subtalar dislocation from a combined axial load and twisting mechanism is the most common pattern, where the calcaneus rotates on the talar axis producing a spiral dissociation of both subtalar and midtarsal joints simultaneously","E":"Medial subtalar dislocation — simultaneous dislocation of the talocalcaneal and talonavicular joints without talar neck fracture; inversion mechanism produces medial displacement of the calcaneus and forefoot in approximately 85% of subtalar dislocations"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Subtalar dislocation involves simultaneous dislocation of the talocalcaneal (subtalar) and talonavicular joints with an intact talar neck — distinct from tibiotalar dislocation. Medial subtalar dislocation (foot inverted and internally rotated) accounts for approximately 85% of cases from inversion injury. Lateral subtalar dislocation (eversion mechanism) accounts for 15% and has worse prognosis due to associated open injury risk. Management is closed reduction under sedation, NWB short leg cast for 6 weeks. Open subtalar dislocations have high infection risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Subtalar dislocation = talocalcaneal + talonavicular simultaneous dislocation with intact talar neck; medial 85% (inversion), lateral 15% (eversion); reduce under sedation, NWB cast 6 weeks. COMLEX: Distinguish from tibiotalar dislocation on X-ray by noting talar position within mortise; subtalar joint is a CP8-adjacent structure in foot and ankle OMM.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: severe inversion mechanism from ankle sprain can progress to subtalar dislocation when ligamentous disruption extends from the lateral ankle ligaments to involve the talocalcaneal interosseous ligament and talonavicular capsule"}]}]}]};
